window.PU_PAPER_TEXT = window.PU_PAPER_TEXT || {};
window.PU_PAPER_TEXT["appendices--appendix_o_arrow_of_time"] = "# Appendix O: Temporal Coherence and the Arrow of Time in the Predictive Universe\n\n## O.1 Introduction: The Problem of Temporal Coherence\n\nThis appendix asks how many local predictive cycles can form a coherent temporal order and why histories have a preferred direction. It treats synchronization, irreversibility, and large-scale timing as separate problems.\n\n**Technical ledger.**\n\nThe Predictive Universe (PU) framework is built upon the operational dynamics of interacting Minimal Predictive Units (MPUs). As established in Theorem 4, the very act of prediction requires a primitive, ordered, and directional concept of evolution, which we identify with local time. Each MPU, through its cyclical operation, effectively possesses its own internal \"clock.\" However, the existence of these local causal rhythms does not, in itself, explain the emergence of the coherent, large-scale temporal structure observed in the universe.\n\nThis appendix addresses this crucial issue. It starts from the premise established in Theorem 4: the very act of prediction logically presupposes a primitive, ordered, and directional concept of evolution. We argue this is a non-negotiable prerequisite for any universe containing predictive agents. The central challenge addressed here is then threefold:\n1.  **The Problem of Coherence:** How does a network of countless MPUs, each potentially operating on its own local causal timeline, achieve the vast domains of temporal synchronization necessary to support consistent physical laws and coherent structures?\n2.  **The Problem of Directionality:** What physical mechanism enforces the observed, universal, and irreversible direction of time's flow—the arrow of time?\n3.  **The Problem of Dynamics:** If time emerges from this synchronized medium, what is the nature of disturbances within it, and how does this temporal structure provide a substrate for advanced predictive phenomena like Consciousness Complexity (CC)?\n\nThis appendix develops branch-conditional results for these three problems. Under the statistical and cost-ledger hypotheses of Theorem O.1 and the connected low-noise detailed-balance hypotheses of Theorem O.2, desynchronization is penalized and stationary measures concentrate near synchronized configurations. Under the independent pathwise entropy-production certificates of Theorems O.3 and O.3a, forward histories are exponentially favored over their reversals. Section O.6 proposes a CC-modulation interpretation of the coherent medium, while the identification of temporal disturbances with gravitational waves is restricted to the linearized Einstein/spin-2 branch stated in Remark O.4. Section O.7 derives Lorentzian signature only under its four explicit structural hypotheses.\n\n## O.2 The MPU Cycle as a Quantum of Causal Process\n\nThe MPU's Fundamental Predictive Loop (Definition 4) consists of the logically ordered sequence of Internal Prediction ($P_{int}$), Verification ($V$), and Update ($D_{cyc}$). This P-V-U sequence specifies the logical order of the full predictive cycle.\n\nOn a branch registering a local-patch state $\\rho_{\\mathrm{eff}}$, a self-adjoint generator $\\hat H_{\\mathrm{eff}}$ with lower spectral bound $E_0$, and a positive clock scale, set\n$$\nE_{\\mathrm{eff}}:=\\operatorname{Tr}[\\rho_{\\mathrm{eff}}(\\hat H_{\\mathrm{eff}}-E_0I)].\n$$\nWhen $0<E_{\\mathrm{eff}}<\\infty$, one may adopt the characteristic convention\n$$\n\\tau_{\\mathrm{medium}}:=\\frac{\\hbar}{E_{\\mathrm{eff}}}.\n\\tag{O.1}\n$$\nTheorem 29 licenses a power--energy calibration only after a cycle rate is registered, and Corollary 29.1 gives the distinct orthogonalization bound $t_\\perp\\ge\\pi\\hbar/(2E_{\\mathrm{eff}})$. Neither result proves fluctuation averaging, synchronization, a minimum cycle duration, or universal temporal granularity. Throughout this appendix $\\tau_{\\mathrm{medium}}$ is therefore branch data used to normalize phase lags.\n\n## O.3 The PCE Cost of Temporal Desynchronization\n\nThe local phase dependence of the PCE potential $V(x)$ (Definition D.1) is modeled through predictive loss, propagation cost, and operational compensation cost. Theorem O.1 identifies a local desynchronization penalty on its statistical and cost-ledger branch.\n\nConsider two interacting MPU ensembles, *i* and *j*, whose collective cycles have a phase lag $\\Delta\\phi_{ij}\\in[0,2\\pi)$. Under Theorem O.1's hypotheses, a sufficiently small nonzero principal lag either decreases predictive benefit or incurs a strictly positive compensation cost, increasing the local PCE potential. Network-wide synchronization requires the additional hypotheses of Theorem O.2.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o1-local-pce-penalty-for-desynchronization\"></a>**Theorem O.1 (Local PCE Penalty for Desynchronization).** Choose an interacting edge $(i,j)$. Assume log-loss; a finite outcome alphabet and a family $p_j(y|t)$ with positive probabilities and twice continuously differentiable coordinates near the comparison time; and finite positive temporal Fisher information\n$$\nI_j(t)=\\sum_y\\frac{(\\partial_tp_j(y|t))^2}{p_j(y|t)}>0.\n$$\nFor a small principal phase lag put\n$$\n\\delta t_{ij}=\\frac{\\tau_{\\mathrm{medium}}}{2\\pi}\\Delta\\phi_{ij},\n\\qquad \\tau_{\\mathrm{medium}}>0.\n$$\nCompare the synchronized configuration with the lagged configuration on one registered Definition-D.1 ledger. Require that every admitted compensation leaves a nonnegative excess prediction loss, that a positive residual loss strictly decreases the power-equivalent benefit $V_{\\mathrm{benefit}}$, and that all operational, propagation and penalty changes are nonnegative. If a compensation removes the loss, require a strictly positive operational-cost change. The benefit condition includes a positive conversion coefficient and a strictly increasing benefit map on the compared performance range. All other contributions to the total potential are held constant.\n\nThen every sufficiently small nonzero lag has a strictly greater local PCE potential, either through residual loss or through the cost of compensation. Without compensation,\n$$\n\\Delta PE_{ij}(t)\n=\n\\frac12I_j(t)\n\\left(\\frac{\\tau_{\\mathrm{medium}}}{2\\pi}\\right)^2\n(\\Delta\\phi_{ij})^2\n+o((\\Delta\\phi_{ij})^2).\n\\tag{O.2}\n$$\n\n*Proof.* Under log-loss, using $p_j(\\cdot|t)$ when the outcome law is $p_j(\\cdot|t+h)$ incurs the excess\n$$\nD_{\\mathrm{KL}}\\!\\left(p_j(\\cdot|t+h)\\,\\middle\\|\\,p_j(\\cdot|t)\\right).\n$$\nWrite $p_y=p_j(y|t)$. Taylor expansion gives\n$$\np_y(t+h)\\log\\frac{p_y(t+h)}{p_y(t)}\n=h\\dot p_y+\\frac{h^2}{2}\\ddot p_y\n+\\frac{h^2}{2}\\frac{\\dot p_y^2}{p_y}+o(h^2).\n$$\nThe alphabet is finite and all $p_y$ are positive, so the remainders may be summed. Differentiating $\\sum_yp_y(t)=1$ twice cancels the first two sums and yields\n$$\nD_{\\mathrm{KL}}(p(t+h)\\|p(t))\n=\\frac12I_j(t)h^2+o(h^2).\n$$\nThis is positive for sufficiently small nonzero $h$. Substitution of $h=\\delta t_{ij}$ proves (O.2). For an admitted comparison with positive residual loss, the benefit hypothesis gives $\\Delta(-V_{\\mathrm{benefit}})>0$; for a comparison with zero residual loss, the compensation hypothesis gives $\\Delta V_{\\mathrm{op}}>0$. Every other component change is nonnegative. Summing the components of (D.1), or using its strictly positive dual certificate, gives $\\Delta V>0$ in either case. ∎\n\n## O.4 Conditional Low-Noise Concentration Near Synchronization\n\nLet the network configuration state $x$ be expanded to include the local phase variables $\\{\\phi_i\\}$. Under the connectedness, exact-zero edge-penalty, phase-independence, compactness, ergodicity, detailed-balance, and low-noise hypotheses stated in Theorem O.2, the phase-sector minimizers are precisely the synchronized configurations characterized there.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o2-conditional-low-noise-concentration-near-synchronization\"></a>**Theorem O.2 (Conditional Low-Noise Concentration Near Synchronization).** Assume that the interaction graph is connected, every edge phase penalty is nonnegative and vanishes exactly at zero phase difference modulo $2\\pi$, and all remaining terms of the PCE potential are phase-independent. Assume further the compactness, ergodicity, detailed-balance, and low-noise concentration hypotheses of Appendix D. Then the phase-sector global minimizers form\n$$\n\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{sync}}\n=\n\\{\\phi:\\phi_i-\\phi_j=0\\pmod{2\\pi}\\text{ on every edge}\\},\n$$\nwhich consists of global phase shifts of a synchronized configuration, and the stationary measures satisfy\n$$\n\\pi_\\beta(U)\\longrightarrow1\n$$\nfor every open neighborhood $U\\supset\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{sync}}$ as $\\beta\\to\\infty$.\n\n*Proof.* Nonnegativity and exact vanishing show that a phase configuration minimizes the phase-sector potential exactly when every edge difference is zero. Connectedness then implies $\\phi_i=\\phi_j$ modulo $2\\pi$ for every pair of vertices, leaving only one global phase. The phase-independent terms do not change this minimizer set. Under the stated Appendix D hypotheses, its low-noise detailed-balance concentration theorem applies to the global minimizer set and gives the displayed limit. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o2a-complete-weighted-lifted-phase-minimizer-and-frustration-classification\"></a>**Theorem O.2a (Complete Weighted Lifted-Phase Minimizer and Frustration Classification).** Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite graph with an arbitrary orientation, incidence matrix $B\\in\\mathbb R^{|V|\\times|E|}$, positive diagonal edge-weight matrix $W$, lifted vertex phases $\\phi\\in\\mathbb R^{|V|}$ and registered edge offsets $\\theta\\in\\mathbb R^{|E|}$. Define\n$$\nV_{G,\\theta}(\\phi)\n=\\frac12\\left\\|W^{1/2}(B^{\\mathsf T}\\phi-\\theta)\\right\\|_2^2,\n\\qquad\nL=BWB^{\\mathsf T}.\n\\tag{O.2a.1}\n$$\nThen the complete global-minimizer set and minimum are\n$$\n\\operatorname*{argmin}V_{G,\\theta}\n=L^\\dagger BW\\theta+\\ker B^{\\mathsf T},\n\\tag{O.2a.2}\n$$\n$$\n\\min V_{G,\\theta}\n=\\frac12\\left\\|\\bigl(I-P_W\\bigr)W^{1/2}\\theta\\right\\|_2^2,\n\\quad\nP_W:=W^{1/2}B^{\\mathsf T}L^\\dagger BW^{1/2}.\n\\tag{O.2a.3}\n$$\nHere $P_W$ is the orthogonal projector onto $\\operatorname{ran}(W^{1/2}B^{\\mathsf T})$. Thus the branch is unfrustrated exactly when $W^{1/2}\\theta$ lies in that range, equivalently when every weighted cycle-space component in (O.2a.3) vanishes. Each connected component contributes one independent global phase shift through $\\ker B^{\\mathsf T}$. The edge-gradient dynamics\n$$\n\\dot\\phi=-\\nabla V_{G,\\theta}(\\phi)=-L\\phi+BW\\theta\n\\tag{O.2a.4}\n$$\nconverges to (O.2a.2). If $L$ has a positive eigenvalue, its component orthogonal to $\\ker L$ contracts at least exponentially at the smallest positive eigenvalue of $L$. If $L=0$, then $B=0$ and $BW\\theta=0$, so every trajectory is constant and already belongs to the minimizer set.\n\nEven an unfrustrated connected graph need not synchronize. On the directed triangle with unit weights and offsets\n$$\n\\theta=(2\\pi/3,2\\pi/3,-4\\pi/3)=B^{\\mathsf T}(0,2\\pi/3,4\\pi/3)^{\\mathsf T},\n\\tag{O.2a.5}\n$$\nthe displayed nonsynchronous phase has zero cost, whereas every synchronized lift has strictly positive cost. Hence a universal synchronization conclusion fails once nonzero admitted edge offsets are allowed.\n\n*Proof.* The normal equation is $L\\phi=BW\\theta$. Its right-hand side is orthogonal to $\\ker L=\\ker B^{\\mathsf T}$, so solutions exist and the Moore--Penrose formula gives (O.2a.2). Weighted least squares gives the orthogonal residual and (O.2a.3). Spectral decomposition of the positive semidefinite $L$ solves (O.2a.4) and proves the convergence rate. Equation (O.2a.5) is exact, so its indicated phase has zero residual; a synchronized lift has $B^{\\mathsf T}\\phi=0\\ne\\theta$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o2b-periodic-edge-law-classification-holonomy-forests-and-uniform-cycles\"></a>**Theorem O.2b (Periodic Edge-Law Classification: Holonomy, Forests and Uniform Cycles).** Retain the finite oriented graph $G=(V,E)$, incidence matrix $B$ and offsets $\\theta\\in\\mathbb R^{|E|}$ of Theorem O.2a, so that $(B^{\\mathsf T}\\phi)_e=\\phi_{h(e)}-\\phi_{t(e)}$ for an edge $e$ from $t(e)$ to $h(e)$. For each edge let $U_e:\\mathbb R\\to[0,\\infty)$ be continuous and $2\\pi$-periodic with $U_e(x)=0$ exactly for $x\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z$, let $w_e>0$, and set\n$$\nV^{\\mathrm{per}}_{G,\\theta}(\\phi)=\\sum_{e\\in E}w_eU_e(\\delta_e),\n\\qquad\n\\delta:=B^{\\mathsf T}\\phi-\\theta.\n\\tag{O.2b.1}\n$$\n\n1. *Holonomy criterion.* $\\min V^{\\mathrm{per}}_{G,\\theta}=0$ exactly when $z^{\\mathsf T}\\theta\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z$ for every integer cycle $z\\in\\ker B\\cap\\mathbb Z^{|E|}$, equivalently for the fundamental cycles of one spanning forest. When this holds, the zero set is $\\phi_*+\\ker B^{\\mathsf T}+2\\pi\\mathbb Z^{|V|}$ for any zero $\\phi_*$: one free global phase per connected component, modulo $2\\pi$.\n2. *Forests.* Let $G$ be a forest. If every $U_e$ is $C^1$, then $\\phi$ is a critical point exactly when $U_e'(\\delta_e)=0$ for every edge. For continuous $U_e$, $\\phi$ is a local minimizer exactly when every $\\delta_e$ is a local minimizer of $U_e$. For the cosine law $U_e(x)=1-\\cos x$, every local minimizer is a global minimizer and has $V^{\\mathrm{per}}_{G,\\theta}=0$.\n3. *Uniform cycles.* Let $G=C_n$ with $n\\ge3$, vertices $\\mathbb Z/n\\mathbb Z$, edges $k\\to k+1$, common weight $w>0$, the cosine law and holonomy $\\Theta:=\\sum_k\\theta_k$. For $q\\in\\mathbb Z$ put $a_q:=(2\\pi q-\\Theta)/n$, and let $\\phi^{(q)}$ be a twisted configuration with $\\delta_k\\equiv a_q\\pmod{2\\pi}$ for every $k$; one exists because $\\delta_k=a_q$ for $k\\ne n-1$ and $\\delta_{n-1}=a_q-2\\pi q$ sum to $-\\Theta$. The local minimizers of $V^{\\mathrm{per}}_{C_n,\\theta}$ are exactly the configurations $\\phi^{(q)}+c\\mathbf 1+2\\pi m$ with $c\\in\\mathbb R$, $m\\in\\mathbb Z^n$ and $\\cos a_q>0$. Each is strict transverse to $\\mathbf 1$, has value $wn(1-\\cos a_q)$, and attracts every sufficiently nearby initial phase exponentially under the gradient flow. The local-minimizer orbits are counted by $\\#\\{q\\in\\mathbb Z:|2\\pi q-\\Theta|<n\\pi/2\\}$, which equals $2\\lceil n/4\\rceil-1$ when $\\Theta\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z$. For $\\theta=0$ the condition $\\cos(2\\pi q/n)>0$ is the twisted-state stability criterion of (Wiley, Strogatz and Girvan 2006).\n4. *Nonsynchronized local minima.* For $\\theta=0$ and $n\\ge5$, the configurations $\\phi^{(\\pm1)}$ are strict local minimizers with $V^{\\mathrm{per}}_{C_n,0}=wn(1-\\cos(2\\pi/n))>0$. The global-minimizer conclusion of Theorem O.2 therefore does not extend to local minimizers or to deterministic gradient-flow limits of periodic edge laws, even with zero offsets on a connected graph.\n\n*Proof.* (1) If $\\delta=2\\pi k$ with $k\\in\\mathbb Z^{|E|}$ and $Bz=0$, then $z^{\\mathsf T}\\theta=(Bz)^{\\mathsf T}\\phi-2\\pi z^{\\mathsf T}k\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z$. Conversely, fix a spanning forest $F$, set $\\phi=0$ at one root of each component and integrate $\\phi_{h(e)}=\\phi_{t(e)}+\\theta_e$ along the edges of $F$, so that $\\delta_e=0$ on $F$. A non-forest edge $e'$ closes a unique fundamental cycle $z^{(e')}\\in\\{0,\\pm1\\}^{|E|}$ with $z^{(e')}_{e'}=1$ and support in $F\\cup\\{e'\\}$. Since $Bz^{(e')}=0$, $\\delta_{e'}=z^{(e')\\mathsf T}\\delta=-z^{(e')\\mathsf T}\\theta\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z$. Every integer cycle equals $\\sum_{e'\\notin F}z_{e'}z^{(e')}$, because the difference is a cycle supported in the forest and hence zero; so the fundamental cycles suffice. Two zeros differ by some $\\psi$ with $B^{\\mathsf T}\\psi\\in2\\pi\\mathbb Z^{|E|}$, and integrating along $F$ from each root gives $\\psi=c+2\\pi m$ with $c$ constant on components.\n\n(2) For a forest $\\ker B=0$, so $B^{\\mathsf T}$ is surjective and $B$ is injective. Put $\\mathcal F(\\delta)=\\sum_ew_eU_e(\\delta_e)$. Then $\\nabla V^{\\mathrm{per}}_{G,\\theta}=B\\nabla\\mathcal F(\\delta)$ vanishes exactly when $\\nabla\\mathcal F(\\delta)=0$. The affine surjection $\\phi\\mapsto\\delta$ has a continuous linear right inverse, so $\\phi$ is a local minimizer of $\\mathcal F\\circ\\delta$ exactly when $\\delta(\\phi)$ is a local minimizer of $\\mathcal F$, and separability makes this a coordinatewise condition. The cosine law has local minimizers only on $2\\pi\\mathbb Z$.\n\n(3) Here $\\partial V/\\partial\\phi_k=w(\\sin\\delta_{k-1}-\\sin\\delta_k)$, so the critical points are exactly the configurations with $\\sin\\delta_k=s$ for every $k$. Put $\\alpha=\\arcsin s\\in[-\\pi/2,\\pi/2]$; each $\\delta_k$ is congruent to $\\alpha$ or to $\\pi-\\alpha$ modulo $2\\pi$. The Hessian form is $Q(\\eta)=w\\sum_k\\cos\\delta_k\\,u_k^2$ with $u_k=\\eta_{k+1}-\\eta_k$, and $\\eta\\mapsto u$ maps onto $\\{\\sum_ku_k=0\\}$ with kernel $\\mathbb R\\mathbf 1$. If $\\cos\\alpha>0$ and every $\\delta_k\\equiv\\alpha$, then $Q$ is positive definite transverse to $\\mathbf 1$; since $\\sum_k\\delta_k=-\\Theta$, one has $n\\alpha=2\\pi q-\\Theta$ for an integer $q$, so $\\alpha=a_q$. If $\\cos\\alpha>0$ and $\\delta_j\\equiv\\pi-\\alpha$ for some $j$, the choice $u_j=1$ and $u_k=-1/(n-1)$ for $k\\ne j$ gives $Q\\le w\\cos\\alpha\\,(-1+1/(n-1))<0$, so the point is not a local minimizer. If $\\cos\\alpha=0$, every $\\delta_k\\equiv\\sigma\\pi/2$ with $\\sigma=\\pm1$, and the increments $u=t(1,1,-2,0,\\ldots,0)$ give $V(\\phi+\\eta)-V(\\phi)=w\\sigma(2\\sin t-\\sin2t)=2w\\sigma\\sin t\\,(1-\\cos t)$, which is negative for small $t$ of sign $-\\sigma$. The gradient flow preserves $\\sum_k\\phi_k$ because $\\mathbf 1^{\\mathsf T}\\nabla V=0$; on each invariant slice a twisted state with $\\cos a_q>0$ is an equilibrium with positive-definite Hessian, hence exponentially attracting by linearization, and rotation invariance makes the attracting neighborhood uniform along the orbit. The value is $wn(1-\\cos a_q)$. The indices $q$ and $q+n$ give the same orbit, and the values $a_q\\in(-\\pi/2,\\pi/2)$ are pairwise incongruent modulo $2\\pi$, which gives the count; for $\\Theta=2\\pi p$ the condition reads $|q-p|<n/4$ and has $2\\lceil n/4\\rceil-1$ solutions.\n\n(4) For $\\theta=0$, $a_{\\pm1}=\\pm2\\pi/n$, and $\\cos(2\\pi/n)>0$ exactly when $n\\ge5$. ∎\n\nLet a finite simple cycle have $n\\ge3$ edges, oriented consistently around the cycle, positive weights $w_i$, arbitrary real offsets $\\theta_i$, and\n\n$$\n V(\\phi)=\\sum_{i=1}^n w_i(1-\\cos\\delta_i),\\qquad\n \\delta=B^T\\phi-\\theta.\n$$\n\nAn arbitrary initial edge orientation is converted to this consistent orientation by negating both its edge difference and its offset on each reversed edge; the even cosine potential is unchanged. The common-current $J$ and ordinary sums below refer to these consistently oriented edge coordinates. We identify phases under a common rotation and individual shifts by $2\\pi$. Put $\\Theta=\\sum_i\\theta_i$. Locally, the permissible edge increments are precisely\n\n$$\n H=\\{u\\in\\mathbb R^n:\\sum_i u_i=0\\}.\n$$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o2c-complete-weighted-cosine-cycle-minimum-classification\"></a>**Theorem O.2c (Complete Weighted Cosine-Cycle Minimum Classification).**\n\nA configuration is a local minimum exactly when it is stationary and one of the following mutually exclusive tests holds. Stationarity is the common-current condition\n\n$$\n w_i\\sin\\delta_i=J\\quad\\text{for every }i.\n$$\n\nSet $a_i=w_i\\cos\\delta_i$.\n\n- **Case A:** every $a_i$ is nonnegative and at most one $a_i$ vanishes.\n- **Case B:** precisely one coefficient $a_0$ is negative, all other $a_i$ are positive, and\n$$\n  \\sum_i a_i^{-1}<0.\n$$\n- **Case C:** precisely one coefficient $a_0$ is negative, all other $a_i$ are positive,\n$$\n  \\sum_i a_i^{-1}=0,\n  \\qquad J=0.\n$$\n\nEvery such minimum is strict modulo the common rotation. Cases A and B have positive-definite transverse Hessian and are locally exponentially attracting for Euclidean gradient flow. Case C is a degenerate strict minimum with a positive quartic leading term along its transverse Hessian null direction. All other stationary configurations fail to be local minima.\n\nThe classification is constructive: choose $J\\in[-\\min_iw_i,\\min_iw_i]$, choose either inverse-sine branch for each edge, retain only choices whose sum is $-\\Theta$ modulo $2\\pi$, and apply A–C. Every stationary orbit is represented, with duplicate endpoint branches identified. Its exact potential is $\\sum_i(w_i-a_i)$. Exact branch equations and tests define the classification for real parameters; an effective certificate uses exact root and sign data.\n\n*Proof.*\n\nDifferentiating gives stationarity as $B(w\\sin\\delta)=0$. The cycle kernel is spanned by the all-ones circulation, proving the common-current equation. The constrained Hessian is $Q(u)=\\sum_i a_i u_i^2$ on $H$.\n\nIf two coefficients are negative, or one is negative and another zero, an increment supported on those two edges with opposite signs makes $Q$ negative. If all coefficients are nonnegative, $Q$ is positive definite on $H$ exactly when at most one coefficient vanishes.\n\nSuppose $a_0<0$ and $a_i>0$ for $i>0$. Set $b_i=1/a_i$ and $S=\\sum_{i>0}b_i$. For a fixed $u_0$, Cauchy–Schwarz gives\n\n$$\n \\sum_{i>0}a_i u_i^2\\ge u_0^2/S,\n$$\n\nwith equality exactly when $u_i=-u_0b_i/S$. Consequently $Q$ is positive definite if $a_0+1/S>0$, indefinite if $a_0+1/S<0$, and positive semidefinite with one-dimensional kernel at equality. These conditions are respectively $\\sum_i1/a_i<0$, $>0$, and $=0$. This proves sufficiency of A and B and disposes of every indefinite case.\n\nAt equality the null direction can be taken as $v=(a_0^{-1},\\ldots,a_{n-1}^{-1})=(-S,b_1,\\ldots,b_{n-1})$. Since $n\\ge3$ and every $b_i>0$,\n\n$$\n C:=\\sum_i v_i^3=-S^3+\\sum_{i>0}b_i^3<0.\n$$\n\nThe Taylor expansion on $H$ is\n\n$$\n V(\\delta+u)-V(\\delta)\n =\\tfrac12\\sum_i a_i u_i^2\n -\\tfrac J6\\sum_i u_i^3\n -\\tfrac1{24}\\sum_i a_i u_i^4+O(\\|u\\|^5).\n$$\n\nThe linear term is $J\\sum_i u_i=0$. If $J\\ne0$, the cubic term along $tv$ is $-JCt^3/6$, which changes sign, excluding a minimum.\n\nIf $J=0$ all cubic terms vanish, and along $tv$ the quartic coefficient is\n\n$$\n -\\frac1{24}\\sum_i a_i v_i^4=-C/24>0.\n$$\n\nSplit $H$ into the null line and a complementary positive-Hessian subspace. The implicit-function theorem solves transverse stationarity as $y(t)=O(t^3)$, because the entire cubic tensor is zero. The reduced potential is $(-C/24)t^4+O(t^6)$, positive for sufficiently small nonzero $t$. Positive transverse curvature gives strict local minimality. This proves Case C.\n\nIt remains to exclude the semidefinite case with no negatives and at least two zeros. A zero coefficient has $\\delta_i\\equiv\\sigma\\pi/2\\pmod{2\\pi}$, $w_i=|J|$, and the same $\\sigma=\\operatorname{sign}J$ on every zero edge; in particular $J\\ne0$. With three or more zeros, the increment $t(1,1,-2)$ on three of them has a nonzero cubic term, so is downhill for one sign.\n\nWith precisely two zeros, choose any positive edge coefficient $a>0$. Use increments $u_1=t+r/2$, $u_2=-t+r/2$ on the zero edges and $u_3=-r$ on that positive edge, all others zero. They sum to zero. Direct expansion with $r=kt^2$ gives\n\n$$\n \\Delta V=\\left(\\frac a2 k^2-\\frac J2 k\\right)t^4+O(t^6).\n$$\n\nTaking $k=J/(2a)$ makes the coefficient $-J^2/(8a)<0$. Thus these points are not minima either. Every Hessian possibility is now covered. Positive transverse Hessians imply local exponential stability by linearization. ∎\n\n**Nondegenerate and quartic witnesses.**\n\nThe weighted triangle with edge phases $(\\pi,0,0)$, weights $(1/2,1,1)$, and offsets chosen to give these differences has $J=0$, $a=(-1/2,1,1)$, and $\\sum_i1/a_i=0$. It is the quartic minimum of C. With weights $(1/3,1,1)$ at the same phases, B applies and the minimum has positive-definite Hessian. For the degenerate triangle, the admissible null-direction perturbation $(-2t,t,t)$ gives exactly $V(t)-V(0)=(1-\\cos t)^2=t^4/4+O(t^6)$. The exact identity exhibits the quartic branch, while the second weighting exhibits the positive-Hessian branch.\n\nA finite local falsifier consists of an admitted stationary point violating the stated classification, or a downhill perturbation at a listed minimum. The proof explicitly constructs downhill curves for every rejected semidefinite possibility. The domain covers every $n$, positive weight vector, real offset vector and phase orbit.\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o2c1-cosine-cactus-graphs\"></a>**Corollary O.2c.1 (Cosine Cactus Graphs).**\n\nFor any finite simple cactus graph (each edge belongs to at most one simple cycle, and every cycle block has length at least three), with the same positive-weight cosine law, a configuration is a local minimum exactly when every bridge has $\\delta_e=0\\pmod{2\\pi}$ and every cycle block passes Theorem O.2c. Every resulting minimum is strict modulo one common rotation per connected component.\n\n**Proof.** Orient each cycle block consistently around that cycle; this is possible simultaneously because cycle blocks have disjoint edge sets. Negate offsets when reversing original orientations, as above. Bridge orientations are arbitrary. Bridge flows vanish at stationarity. The edge-coordinate image of $B^{\\mathsf T}$ consists of independent cycle-sum constraints, one per edge-disjoint cycle, with bridge coordinates free. Modulo componentwise rotation, $B^{\\mathsf T}$ is a linear isomorphism onto that constraint space. Thus locally the potential is a sum on the product of bridge lines and cycle hyperplanes. Local minimality is equivalent to local minimality of each factor. A cosine bridge has minima exactly at zero modulo $2\\pi$; Theorem O.2c handles each cycle. ∎\n\nLet $m \\ge 1$. The graph has vertices\n\n$$\n a,b,v_0,\\ldots,v_{m-1}\n$$\n\nand oriented edges $a \\to v_i \\to b$. Give both edges on path $i$ weight $w_i > 0$,\nand give its first and second edges real offsets $\\alpha_i, \\gamma_i$. The source-defined\ncosine potential is\n\n$$\n V(\\phi)=\\sum_{i=0}^{m-1}w_i\\bigl[\n 2-\\cos(\\phi_{v_i}-\\phi_a-\\alpha_i)\n -\\cos(\\phi_b-\\phi_{v_i}-\\gamma_i)\\bigr].                 \\tag{O.2d.1}\n$$\n\nIdentify phases under a common rotation and independent additions of $2\\pi$ at\nvertices. Edge orientations may be reversed if the corresponding offset is\nnegated. Vertex labels and the registered data otherwise remain fixed.\n\nFix the common-rotation gauge $\\phi_a = 0$. Put\n\n$$\n t=\\phi_b\\pmod{2\\pi},\\qquad\n y_i=\\phi_{v_i}-\\alpha_i\\pmod{2\\pi},\\qquad\n \\beta_i=\\alpha_i+\\gamma_i.\n$$\n\nThe quotient is the compact torus $(S^1)^{m+1}$ and (O.2d.1) becomes\n\n$$\n V(t,y)=\\sum_iw_i[2-\\cos y_i-\\cos(t-\\beta_i-y_i)].       \\tag{O.2d.2}\n$$\n\nThis gauge parametrizes each registered phase orbit exactly once.\n\n$K_{2,m}$ has cycle rank $m-1$. For $m \\ge 3$, the cycles formed by paths $(0,1)$\nand $(0,2)$ share both edges of path 0. Thus these graphs are not cactus graphs.\nThe potential is the cosine specialization of (O.2b.1).\n\n**Cusp intervals.**\n\nLet the **cusps** be the distinct points\n\n$$\n c_i=\\beta_i+\\pi\\pmod{2\\pi}\n$$\n\non the $t$-circle, and let their number be $r$, $1 \\le r \\le m$. Choose cyclic lifts\n$c_1 < \\cdots < c_r < c_1+2\\pi$, and put $c_{r+1}=c_1+2\\pi$. On each open interval\n$I_j=(c_j,c_{j+1})$, choose the unique integers $k_i$ for which\n\n$$\n a_i(t)=\\frac{t-\\beta_i}{2}-\\pi k_i\n       \\in(-\\pi/2,\\pi/2),\\qquad t\\in I_j.               \\tag{O.2d.3}\n$$\n\nThese integers are constant throughout that real lift of the interval. Define\n\n$$\n F_j(t)=\\sum_iw_i\\sin a_i(t),\\qquad\n P_j=\\sum_iw_i\\exp[-i(\\beta_i/2+\\pi k_i)].             \\tag{O.2d.4}\n$$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o2d-complete-paired-edge-theta-graph-classification\"></a>**Theorem O.2d (Complete Paired-Edge Theta-Graph Classification).**\n\nThe complete local-minimum set of (O.2d.1), modulo the stated phase equivalence, is\nobtained as follows:\n\n1. Keep interval $I_j$ exactly when\n$$\n   F_j(c_j+)<0<F_j(c_{j+1}-).                            \\tag{O.2d.5}\n$$\n   Equality at either endpoint is **not** admitted.\n2. Every kept interval has exactly one solution $t_j$ of $F_j(t_j)=0$.\n3. Its unique phase orbit has representative\n$$\n   \\phi_a=0,\\quad \\phi_b=t_j,\\quad\n   \\phi_{v_i}=\\alpha_i+a_i(t_j)\\pmod{2\\pi}.             \\tag{O.2d.6}\n$$\n4. Equivalently, $P_j \\ne 0$ and the solution is the unique representative in\n   $I_j$ satisfying\n$$\n   e^{it_j/2}P_j=|P_j|.\n   \\tag{O.2d.7}\n$$\n   Its potential value is\n$$\n   V_j=2\\sum_iw_i-2|P_j|.                              \\tag{O.2d.8}\n$$\n\nEvery listed minimum is strict modulo common rotation, with positive-definite\ntransverse Hessian. Every sufficiently nearby Euclidean gradient-flow trajectory\nconverges exponentially to its common-rotation orbit. There are between $1$ and\n$r$ local-minimum orbits. The upper bound $m$ is attained for every $m$.\n\nThe global minima are exactly the listed candidates with the largest $|P_j|$.\nThus the theorem classifies all local minima, all global minima, their energies,\nand the strict-versus-degenerate question, without a genericity premise.\n\n*Proof.* First consider configurations away from cusps.\n\nFor fixed $t$, the $i$th leaf contribution is\n\n$$\n 2w_i-2w_i\\cos\\frac{t-\\beta_i}{2}\n              \\cos\\left(y_i-\\frac{t-\\beta_i}{2}\\right). \\tag{O.2d.9}\n$$\n\nIf $t$ is not a cusp, its cosine coefficient is nonzero. A local minimum must be\na local minimum in the leaf variable alone, and a nonconstant cosine has exactly\none minimum modulo $2\\pi$. Its location is $y_i=a_i(t)$ as in (O.2d.3). At this point\nboth residual edge angles on that path equal $a_i(t)$ modulo $2\\pi$:\n\n$$\n t-\\beta_i-a_i(t)=a_i(t)+2\\pi k_i.\n$$\n\nConsequently both edge cosines are positive. Differentiating (O.2d.2) in $t$ at these\nleaf values gives $\\partial_t V=\\sum_i w_i \\sin a_i(t)=F_j(t)$. Thus stationarity requires\n(O.2d.4)'s scalar equation.\n\n*Cusp exclusion.*\n\nSuppose $t_0-\\beta_i=\\pi \\pmod{2\\pi}$ for some path $i$. Equation (O.2d.9) is then independent of\n$y_i$. If $(t_0,y)$ were a local minimum, every sufficiently small perturbation of\n$y_i$ alone would have the same value and would itself be a local minimum: choose\nsuch a point inside the original minimum neighborhood and then a smaller\nneighborhood contained in it. Every one of these points must therefore satisfy\n\n$$\n\\partial_t V=0.\n$$\n\nBut, with the other variables fixed, this derivative has the form\n\n$$\n C+w_i\\sin(t_0-\\beta_i-y_i),                            \\tag{O.2d.10}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is independent of $y_i$. Since $w_i>0$, (O.2d.10) cannot vanish on any open\ninterval of $y_i$. This contradiction excludes the original point.\n\nThe argument covers simultaneous cusps, Hessian null directions and endpoint-equality cases in (O.2d.5).\n\n*Existence and local stability.*\n\nFor $t\\in I_j$,\n\n$$\n F_j'(t)=\\frac12\\sum_iw_i\\cos a_i(t)>0.                 \\tag{O.2d.11}\n$$\n\nThus the intermediate-value theorem and strict monotonicity prove the exact\ncondition (O.2d.5), and the uniqueness of its root. If the endpoint limit equals zero,\nstrict monotonicity rules out an interior zero on that side.\n\nAt a root, (O.2d.6) satisfies all leaf stationarity equations and the endpoint equation;\nthe remaining vertex equation follows from common-rotation invariance. Its Hessian\nquadratic form in original vertex increments is\n\n$$\n Q(\\eta)=\\sum_i w_i\\cos a_i(t_j)\n  [ (\\eta_{v_i}-\\eta_a)^2+(\\eta_b-\\eta_{v_i})^2].      \\tag{O.2d.12}\n$$\n\nEvery coefficient is strictly positive and the graph is connected. Therefore\n$Q(\\eta)=0$ precisely for a common constant increment, proving positive-definiteness\non the phase quotient and strict local minimality. Euclidean gradient flow\npreserves the mean phase; restriction to a mean-zero slice has a positive-definite\nHessian, so ordinary linearization gives local exponential attraction.\n\nSince all possible non-cusp leaf configurations have been checked and every cusp\nhas been excluded, the classification is complete.\n\n*Values and completeness.*\n\nOn the chosen interval lift,\n\n$$\n \\sum_iw_i e^{ia_i(t)}=e^{it/2}P_j,\n \\qquad \\operatorname{Re}(e^{it/2}P_j)>0.               \\tag{O.2d.13}\n$$\n\nIn particular, $P_j\\ne0$. Its imaginary part is $F_j$, so (O.2d.7) is exactly the\nstationarity condition with the required positive real part. Substituting the\nleaf-minimized value\n\n$$\n V_{\\mathrm{red}}(t)=2\\sum_iw_i-H(t),\\qquad\n H(t)=2\\sum_iw_i\\left|\\cos\\frac{t-\\beta_i}{2}\\right|    \\tag{O.2d.14}\n$$\n\ngives (O.2d.8). There is at most one candidate per interval. Compactness ensures a\nglobal minimum of (O.2d.2); it is a local minimum and therefore must be one of these\ncandidates, proving there is at least one. The global-minimum statement follows\nby comparing (O.2d.8). The sharpness construction is given in the sharp-count construction below. ∎\n\n**Equivalent derivative test.** On an interval, $H'=-F_j$ and $H''=-H/4<0$.\nAt a cusp its derivative jumps upward by $2\\sum w_i$, summing over the paths whose\ncusps coincide. Thus $H$ has no cusp maximum. This is an independent explanation\nof the scalar enumeration, but does not replace the flat-leaf proof: minima of\na partially minimized potential need not automatically classify local minima of\nthe unreduced potential at discontinuous optimizer choices.\n\n**Exact algebraic classifier.**\n\nTheorem O.2d covers every real offset and positive real weight. A terminating implementation requires exact equality and order tests for its input class.\n\nFor a genuine terminating exact algorithm, supply each positive $w_i$ and the\nreal and imaginary parts of $e^{i\\alpha_i}$, $e^{i\\gamma_i}$ as real algebraic numbers,\nrepresented by integer polynomials plus isolating intervals. Their product gives\n\n$$\nu_i=e^{i\\beta_i}.\n$$\n\nRequire exact unit-modulus checks. No angle itself needs to be an\nalgebraic real number; rational multiples of $\\pi$ have the required algebraic\ncosine/sine data.\n\nThe following avoids numerical inverse trigonometric functions entirely.\n\n1. Form the algebraic cusp points $-u_i$. Identify exact coincidences and sort the\n   distinct points in positive circular order using half-plane tests and signed\n   two-dimensional determinants. When $r=1$, its sole open interval means the\n   whole circle with its single cusp removed, not an empty equal-endpoint arc.\n2. For each open circular interval $I$, choose a unit point $z_s$ with rational\n   coordinates strictly inside it. Enumerating the rational stereographic points\n$$\n   ((1-q^2)/(1+q^2),\\ 2q/(1+q^2)),\\quad q\\in\\mathbb Q,\n$$\n   together with $(-1,0)$, terminates because these points are dense and $I$ is\n   nonempty. Circle-interval membership is decidable by the same exact order tests.\n3. For every $i$, let $h_i$ be the unique square root of $z_s \\overline{u_i}$ with\n   strictly positive real part. This exists because the sample avoids every cusp.\n   Form\n$$\n   C=\\sum_iw_i h_i,\\qquad\n   z_* = z_s\\frac{(\\overline{C})^2}{C\\overline{C}}.     \\tag{O.2d.15}\n$$\n   $\\operatorname{Re} C>0$, hence the denominator is nonzero and $z_*$ lies on the unit circle.\n4. Keep the candidate only if $z_*$ lies strictly inside $I$. Recompute the unique\n   positive-real-part square roots $h_i^*$ of $z_* \\overline{u_i}$ and verify exactly\n$$\n   \\sum_iw_i\\operatorname{Im}h_i^*=0.                  \\tag{O.2d.16}\n$$\n   This last check is redundant given the theorem but makes the certificate direct.\n5. Output\n$$\n   e^{i\\phi_a}=1,\\quad e^{i\\phi_b}=z_*,\\quad\n   e^{i\\phi_{v_i}}=e^{i\\alpha_i}h_i^*,                 \\tag{O.2d.17}\n$$\n   its exact energy $2\\sum_i w_i-2|C|$, and the strictly positive edge curvatures\n   $w_i \\operatorname{Re} h_i^*$ on both edges of each path.\n6. Compare exact algebraic energy values to label every output as global or\n   nonglobal. Do not merge distinct phase orbits merely because their energies agree.\n\nTo justify step 3, on a real lift of the sample's interval,\n$h_i(t)=\\exp(i(t-t_s)/2)h_i(t_s)$ until the next cusp. Hence the unique possible\nstationary phase has\n\n$$\n\\exp(i(t-t_s))=\\overline C^2/|C|^2,\n$$\n\nexactly (O.2d.15). Any surviving candidate has a\npositive real sum and satisfies the root condition. Conversely, every minimum\nin the interval necessarily has this candidate endpoint. The checks in step 4 cannot\nremove a genuine minimum.\n\nThere are finitely many intervals, all arithmetic and sign/equality checks are\non real algebraic numbers, and each rational-sample enumeration terminates.\nTherefore this algorithm is sound and terminating, with at most $m$ outputs.\nIts running time depends on the algebraic representations and separation data.\n\n**Algebraic certificate.**\n\nA complete certificate contains:\n\n- the graph and paired-weight/offset input checks;\n- the ordered distinct cusps and each algebraic sample;\n- the square-root equations, positive-real-part signs, $C$, and candidate (O.2d.15);\n- every candidate's strict interval membership, or its exact rejection by that test;\n- for kept candidates, (O.2d.16), (O.2d.17), energy and positive edge curvatures;\n- exact energy comparisons marking the global minima.\n\nA verifier checks these algebraic identities and inequalities and that every\ncusp interval occurs once. The theorem's completeness proof supplies coverage;\nrejected intervals cannot hide additional solutions. Each output's Hessian\ncertificate is the connected positive-weight Laplacian (O.2d.12). The finite certificate records every interval, including each rejected candidate.\n\n**Sharp minimum count.**\n\nChoose equal path weights $w_i=w>0$, first-edge offsets $\\alpha_i=0$, and\n\n$$\n \\gamma_i=\\beta_i=\\pi+\\frac{2\\pi i}{m},\\qquad i=0,\\ldots,m-1. \\tag{O.2d.18}\n$$\n\nThe distinct cusps are $2\\pi i/m$. On $0<t<2\\pi/m$, with $x=t/2$,\n\n$$\n \\frac{H(t)}{2w}\n =\\sum_{i=0}^{m-1}|\\sin(x-\\pi i/m)|\n =\\sin x+\\cot\\frac\\pi{2m}\\cos x\n =\\csc\\frac\\pi{2m}\\cos\\left(x-\\frac\\pi{2m}\\right).    \\tag{O.2d.19}\n$$\n\nFor the middle equality, pair the terms to get\n$\\sum_{i=1}^{m-1} \\cos(\\pi i/m)=0$ and use the finite geometric-series identity\n$\\sum_{i=1}^{m-1} \\sin(\\pi i/m)=\\cot(\\pi/(2m))$. The function $H$ is $2\\pi/m$-periodic, so\nthere is exactly one maximum in each cusp interval, at\n\n$$\n t_j=\\frac{(2j+1)\\pi}{m},\\qquad j=0,\\ldots,m-1.       \\tag{O.2d.20}\n$$\n\nEvery one has the same value. Theorem O.2d gives precisely $m$ strict global-minimum\norbits, each with\n\n$$\n V_{\\min}=2w\\left[m-\\csc\\frac\\pi{2m}\\right].          \\tag{O.2d.21}\n$$\n\nFor $m\\ge3$ this is a non-cactus family of arbitrarily large cycle rank. The graph,\nweights and offsets are explicit; its source cosine/sine data are algebraic if\n$w$ is algebraic. With $\\alpha_i=0$, so are all witness phase coordinates. The latter\nwould not follow from algebraic **total** holonomies alone if the individual\n$\\alpha_i$ were arbitrary real offsets.\n\nFor $m=3$, the three minima have endpoint phases $\\pi/3,\\pi,5\\pi/3$ and common energy\n$2w$. The cycle holonomies differ by $2\\pi/3$, so this is genuinely frustrated and\nhas positive minimum. \n\n**Complete three-path metastability diagram.**\n\nSet $\\alpha_i=0$,\n\n$$\n (\\beta_0,\\beta_1,\\beta_2)=(\\pi,5\\pi/3,7\\pi/3),\n \\qquad(w_0,w_1,w_2)=(a,1,1),\\quad a>0.              \\tag{O.2d.22}\n$$\n\nThe cusp intervals are $(0,2\\pi/3)$, $(2\\pi/3,4\\pi/3)$, and $(4\\pi/3,2\\pi)$. Directly from\n(O.2d.14),\n\n$$\n H(t)=\n \\begin{cases}\n 2[\\sqrt3\\cos(t/2)+a\\sin(t/2)],&0<t<2\\pi/3,\\\\\n 2(a+1)\\sin(t/2),&2\\pi/3<t<4\\pi/3,\\\\\n 2[-\\sqrt3\\cos(t/2)+a\\sin(t/2)],&4\\pi/3<t<2\\pi.\n \\end{cases}                                                   \\tag{O.2d.23}\n$$\n\nIn the middle interval, $V$ has its unique minimum at $t=\\pi$, with energy $2$.\nThe left derivative limits for $H$ are\n\n$$\n H'(0+)=a,\\qquad H'(2\\pi/3-)=(a-3)/2.                \\tag{O.2d.24}\n$$\n\nHence each outer interval contributes a minimum exactly when $0<a<3$. Their\nendpoint phases and energies are\n\n$$\n t_-=2\\arctan(a/\\sqrt3),\\qquad t_+=2\\pi-t_-,\n \\qquad V_\\pm=2(a+2)-2\\sqrt{a^2+3}.                 \\tag{O.2d.25}\n$$\n\nThe complete diagram is therefore:\n\n- $0<a<1$: three strict local minima; the outer two are global and the middle one\n  is nonglobal\n- $a=1$: three strict global minima, all with energy $2$\n- $1<a<3$: three strict local minima; the middle one is global and the outer two\n  are nonglobal\n- $a\\ge3$: exactly one local minimum, the middle global minimum\n\nThe energy comparison follows from\n$(a+1)^2-(a^2+3)=2a-2$, with both compared heights positive. At $a=3$ the outer\ncandidates lie at the cusps; neither is a local minimum. The cusp-exclusion proof includes this degenerate boundary.\n\n**Degenerate boundary saddle.**\n\nAt $a=3$, the cusp configuration\n\n$$\n t_0=2\\pi/3,\\qquad (y_0,y_1,y_2)=(-\\pi/6,\\pi/2,\\pi/6)\n                                                               \\tag{O.2d.25a}\n$$\n\nis stationary. Its per-edge Hessian coefficients, grouped by path, are\n$(3\\sqrt3/2, 0, \\sqrt3/2)$. The Hessian is positive semidefinite and has one extra null\nmode after quotienting common rotation, namely the flat $v_1$ leaf mode. It is\nnevertheless a saddle, as the following exact downhill curve certifies.\n\nFor a small nonzero $\\varepsilon$, put $A=1-\\cos \\varepsilon>0$,\n\n$$\n u=\\arctan\\frac{A}{2\\sqrt3},\\quad\n t=t_0+2u,\\quad\n (y_0,y_1,y_2)=(-\\pi/6+u,\\pi/2+u+\\varepsilon,\\pi/6+u).\n$$\n\nDirect substitution into (O.2d.2) gives\n\n$$\n \\Delta V\n =4\\sqrt3(1-\\cos u)-2A\\sin u\n =4\\sqrt3-2\\sqrt{12+A^2}<0.                           \\tag{O.2d.25b}\n$$\n\nThe curve tends to (O.2d.25a). In particular,\n$\\Delta V=-\\varepsilon^4/(8\\sqrt3)+O(\\varepsilon^6)$. Thus a Hessian-only nonnegative test fails even within\nthis family, while the theorem's cusp exclusion is complete.\n\n**Algebraic three-path witness.**\n\nUse (O.2d.22) with $a=2$. The following coordinates are $z_v=e^{i\\phi_v}$ in vertex order\n$a,b,v_0,v_1,v_2$.\n\nThe unique global minimum is\n\n$$\n \\left(1,-1,1,\\frac{1-i\\sqrt3}{2},\\frac{1+i\\sqrt3}{2}\\right),\n \\qquad V=2.                                          \\tag{O.2d.26}\n$$\n\nOne metastable minimum is\n\n$$\n \\left(1,\\frac{-1+4i\\sqrt3}{7},\n \\frac{2-i\\sqrt3}{\\sqrt7},\n \\frac{1+3i\\sqrt3}{2\\sqrt7},\n \\frac{5+i\\sqrt3}{2\\sqrt7}\\right),\n \\qquad V=8-2\\sqrt7>2.                               \\tag{O.2d.27}\n$$\n\nThe other is obtained by complex conjugation and exchanging $v_1,v_2$:\n\n$$\n \\left(1,\\frac{-1-4i\\sqrt3}{7},\n \\frac{2+i\\sqrt3}{\\sqrt7},\n \\frac{5-i\\sqrt3}{2\\sqrt7},\n \\frac{1-3i\\sqrt3}{2\\sqrt7}\\right).                  \\tag{O.2d.28}\n$$\n\nEvery coordinate has unit modulus. The two residual phases along any one path\nare equal modulo $2\\pi$. At (O.2d.26), the edge cosines, grouped by path, are\n$(1,1/2,1/2)$; at (O.2d.27), they are\n\n$$\n \\left(\\frac2{\\sqrt7},\\frac1{2\\sqrt7},\\frac5{2\\sqrt7}\\right).\n$$\n\nThe corresponding endpoint current at (O.2d.27) vanishes exactly:\n\n$$\n 2\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt3}{\\sqrt7}\\right)\n +\\frac{3\\sqrt3}{2\\sqrt7}\n +\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2\\sqrt7}=0.                           \\tag{O.2d.29}\n$$\n\nAll edge curvatures are strictly positive, so these are strict minima by (O.2d.12).\nThe three-path metastability diagram proves that this list of three is exhaustive, and that exactly (O.2d.26) is\nglobal.\n\nThe leading principal minors of the Hessian anchored at $a$ are $(3,8,7,6)$ at the global witness, $(\\sqrt7,40/7,38\\sqrt7/49,20/7)$ at the first metastable witness and $(\\sqrt7,40/7,150\\sqrt7/49,20/7)$ at the second. These values provide finite positive-definiteness certificates alongside the incidence-gradient, unit-modulus and energy identities.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o2e-complete-unequal-edge-theta-graph-classification\"></a>**Theorem O.2e (Complete Unequal-Edge Theta-Graph Classification).** Let $m\\ge1$. Give the graph $K_{2,m}$ vertices $a,b,v_1,\\ldots,v_m$, oriented paths $a\\to v_i\\to b$, positive edge weights $a_i,b_i$ and real offsets $\\alpha_i,\\gamma_i$. Use the cosine law of (O.2b.1), and identify configurations under a common phase shift and independent vertex shifts by $2\\pi$. Fix $\\phi_a=0$ and write\n$$\nt=\\phi_b,\\qquad y_i=\\phi_{v_i}-\\alpha_i,\n\\qquad \\beta_i=\\alpha_i+\\gamma_i,\n$$\n$$\nV(t,y)=\\sum_{i=1}^m\\{a_i[1-\\cos y_i]\n+b_i[1-\\cos(t-\\beta_i-y_i)]\\}.\n\\tag{O.2e.1}\n$$\nAll circle-valued coordinates are understood modulo $2\\pi$. Define\n$$\nz_i(t)=a_i+b_i e^{i(t-\\beta_i)},\\qquad\nR_i(t)=|z_i(t)|,\n\\qquad W(t)=\\sum_i(a_i+b_i-R_i(t)).\n\\tag{O.2e.2}\n$$\nA cancellation point satisfies $R_i(t)=0$ for at least one $i$, equivalently $a_i=b_i$ and $t-\\beta_i=\\pi$ for that path.\n\n1. Every local minimum of $V$ has $R_i(t)>0$ for every $i$ and\n   $$\n   e^{iy_i}=\\frac{z_i(t)}{R_i(t)}.\n   \\tag{O.2e.3}\n   $$\n   These configurations are local minima exactly when $t$ is a local minimum of $W$. Their energies equal $W(t)$.\n2. On every cancellation-free arc, $W$ is real analytic and nonconstant, with finitely many stationary points. The local minima are exactly the zeros at which\n   $$\n   F(t):=W'(t)=\\sum_i\\frac{a_i b_i\\sin(t-\\beta_i)}{R_i(t)}\n   \\tag{O.2e.4}\n   $$\n   changes sign from negative to positive. There are finitely many local-minimum orbits, all strict, and at least one. Comparing their energies gives every global minimum.\n3. At a local minimum, the phase-quotient Hessian is positive definite exactly when $W''(t)>0$. If $W''(t)=0$, its kernel has dimension one; the sign test in item 2 still decides the minimum. Every local-minimum orbit attracts all sufficiently nearby Euclidean gradient-flow trajectories. Attraction is exponential at the positive-definite minima.\n4. When the weights and the real and imaginary parts of $e^{i\\alpha_i},e^{i\\gamma_i}$ are real algebraic numbers given exactly, the construction below terminates and returns every local and global minimum, its phase-circle coordinates, its exact energy and its Hessian type. Items 1--3 hold for arbitrary real weights and offsets.\n\n*Proof.* For $R_i(t)>0$, choose a local real lift $\\psi_i(t)$ of $\\arg z_i(t)$. The exact identity\n$$\nV(t,y)=W(t)+\\sum_i R_i(t)[1-\\cos(y_i-\\psi_i(t))]\n\\tag{O.2e.5}\n$$\nshows that a local minimum in the $i$th leaf coordinate must have $y_i=\\psi_i(t)$ modulo $2\\pi$. It also proves both directions of item 1 away from cancellation: the nonnegative remainder gives sufficiency, and the continuous minimizing section $y=\\psi(t)$ gives necessity.\n\nSuppose $R_i(t_0)=0$. The $i$th summand of $V(t_0,y)$ is then independent of $y_i$. If $(t_0,y)$ were a local minimum, every sufficiently small change of $y_i$ would have the same value and would itself be a local minimum in a smaller neighborhood. All such points would therefore satisfy $\\partial_tV=0$. With the other coordinates fixed this derivative is\n$$\nC+b_i\\sin(t_0-\\beta_i-y_i),\n$$\nwhere $C$ is independent of $y_i$. Since $b_i>0$, it cannot vanish on an open interval of $y_i$. This excludes the entire cancellation fiber, including simultaneous cancellations and points with semidefinite Hessians.\n\nIt remains to prove finiteness and exclude flat arcs. Work first in the chart $x=\\tan(t/2)$, which omits $t=\\pi$. Put $c_i=\\cos\\beta_i$, $s_i=\\sin\\beta_i$ and\n$$\nD=1+x^2,\\qquad n_i=2c_i x-s_i(1-x^2),\n$$\n$$\nq_i=(a_i^2+b_i^2)D\n+2a_i b_i[c_i(1-x^2)+2s_i x].\n\\tag{O.2e.6}\n$$\nThen $R_i=\\sqrt{q_i}/\\sqrt D$, and $F=T/\\sqrt D$, where\n$$\nT(x)=\\sum_i\\frac{a_i b_i n_i(x)}{\\sqrt{q_i(x)}}.\n\\tag{O.2e.7}\n$$\nEvery square root in (O.2e.7) is the positive real root. Partition the real $x$-axis at the zeros of the $q_i$ belonging to equal-weight paths. These are precisely the finite-chart cancellation points.\n\nFor $a_i=b_i$, $q_i$ is the square of a nonzero polynomial $\\ell_i$ of degree at most one. On each open partition interval, $\\sqrt{q_i}=\\varepsilon_i\\ell_i$ with a fixed sign $\\varepsilon_i\\in\\{-1,1\\}$. Moreover $n_i/\\ell_i$ is a polynomial of degree at most one. Indeed, for any half-angle choice $C_i=\\cos(\\beta_i/2)$, $S_i=\\sin(\\beta_i/2)$,\n$$\nq_i=4a_i^2(C_i+S_i x)^2,\n\\qquad\nn_i=2(C_i+S_i x)(C_i x-S_i).\n\\tag{O.2e.8}\n$$\nThus the equal-weight terms sum to a polynomial $A_0(x)$ of degree at most one on the chosen interval.\n\nFor $a_i\\ne b_i$, $q_i$ has positive leading coefficient $\\lambda_i$ and discriminant\n$$\n\\operatorname{disc}(q_i)=-4(a_i^2-b_i^2)^2<0.\n\\tag{O.2e.9}\n$$\nWrite $q_i=\\lambda_i p_j$, grouping identical monic quadratics. The distinct $p_1,\\ldots,p_r$ are irreducible over $\\mathbb R$, have disjoint complex root pairs, and are positive on the real axis. The grouped derivative is\n$$\nT=A_0+\\sum_{j=1}^r\\frac{A_j}{\\sqrt{p_j}},\n\\qquad\nA_j=\\sum_{i:q_i/\\lambda_i=p_j}\n\\frac{a_i b_i}{\\sqrt{\\lambda_i}}n_i.\n\\tag{O.2e.10}\n$$\nEvery $A_j$ is a nonzero polynomial of degree at most two. To check nonvanishing, proportionality of two $q_i$ gives proportionality of $a_i^2+b_i^2$, $2a_i b_i c_i$ and $2a_i b_i s_i$. Since $c_i^2+s_i^2=1$ and $a_i b_i>0$, their $c_i,s_i$ are equal. The corresponding $n_i$ therefore coincide, and their strictly positive coefficients add.\n\nFor $r>0$, no expression obtained from (O.2e.10) by independently changing radical signs vanishes identically. Here is a direct independence argument. An identity on a real open interval extends as an identity of complex analytic germs away from the finitely many quadratic roots. Continue it to a small punctured disk about one root of $p_j$, along a path avoiding the other roots, and then around that root once. The continuation reverses the sign of $\\sqrt{p_j}$ and preserves every other radical. Subtracting the continued identity from the original gives $2A_j/\\sqrt{p_j}=0$ as a germ. A nonzero polynomial $A_j$ cannot satisfy this identity, including when it has a zero at the chosen quadratic root. This is a contradiction.\n\nSet $L=\\prod_{j=1}^r p_j$, with $L=1$ for $r=0$, and form\n$$\nS=A_0L+\\sum_{j=1}^r A_j\\frac{L}{p_j}\\sqrt{p_j},\n\\qquad\nP(x)=\\prod_{\\sigma\\in\\{-1,1\\}^r}\n\\left(A_0L+\\sum_{j=1}^r\n\\sigma_j A_j\\frac{L}{p_j}\\sqrt{p_j}\\right).\n\\tag{O.2e.11}\n$$\nMultiplication and the relations $(\\sqrt{p_j})^2=p_j$ make $P$ a real polynomial: the product is invariant under each sign change, so every term with an odd radical exponent cancels. For $r>0$, the preceding independence argument makes every factor nonzero, hence $P\\ne0$. Every zero of the physical derivative $T$ is a zero of $P$. The degree bound $\\deg P\\le(2r+1)2^r$ follows by giving each $\\sqrt{p_j}$ degree one in the displayed product.\n\nFor $r=0$, all paths have equal weights. On a cancellation-free arc,\n$$\nW(t)=\\sum_i2a_i\\left[1-\n\\left|\\cos\\frac{t-\\beta_i}{2}\\right|\\right],\n\\qquad\nW''(t)=\\frac12\\sum_i a_i\n\\left|\\cos\\frac{t-\\beta_i}{2}\\right|>0.\n\\tag{O.2e.12}\n$$\nConsequently $A_0=T$ is nonzero, and (O.2e.11) gives the nonzero polynomial $P=A_0$. A second chart centered at $t=\\pi$ has the same formulas with $\\beta_i$ replaced by $\\beta_i-\\pi$; it covers the omitted point. There are finitely many partition intervals, and each nonzero polynomial has finitely many real roots. The stationary set off the cancellation fibers is therefore finite for arbitrary real input data.\n\nA nonconstant real analytic function at an isolated local minimum has a first nonzero Taylor term of even degree and positive coefficient. Its derivative is negative immediately to the left and positive immediately to the right. Conversely, those signs imply a strict local minimum. This proves the sign classification and excludes non-strict or flat minimum families. Compactness of the phase torus supplies a global minimum; the cancellation argument places it among the classified configurations.\n\nFor item 3, use the local coordinates $(t,z)$ with $z_i=y_i-\\psi_i(t)$. At a stationary point with $z=0$, the Hessian of (O.2e.5) is\n$$\n\\operatorname{diag}(W''(t),R_1(t),\\ldots,R_m(t)).\n\\tag{O.2e.13}\n$$\nThe coordinate change has invertible derivative, so congruence preserves its inertia and nullity. Each $R_i$ is positive. This proves the Hessian claims, including nullity one at a degenerate minimum. The stationary point is isolated on the quotient. In a mean-fixed local slice, choose a ball containing no other stationary point and an energy sublevel below the minimum boundary energy. The gradient flow preserves that sublevel and the mean phase. Its compact limit set consists of stationary points: a nonstationary limit point has a neighborhood with gradient norm bounded below, and repeated visits would cause a fixed positive energy decrease, contrary to boundedness below. Thus every sufficiently nearby trajectory converges to this minimum. Positive definiteness additionally gives exponential convergence by linearization.\n\nFor item 4, all coefficients in (O.2e.6)--(O.2e.11) are real algebraic. The following finite procedure implements the classification.\n\n- Isolate the finite cancellation points and partition the first chart. On each interval determine the signs in (O.2e.8), group the monic quadratics, and form $S$.\n- Represent a radical expression by its coefficients on the masks $\\prod_{j\\in J}\\sqrt{p_j}$. Multiply two masks by taking their symmetric difference and multiplying by $\\prod_{j\\in J\\cap K}p_j$. Starting from $S$, successively multiply the current expression by its conjugate under the sign reversal of radical $j$, for $j=1,\\ldots,r$. This produces exactly the polynomial $P$ in (O.2e.11).\n- Isolate every real root of $P$ in the chosen interval. For a polynomial with algebraic coefficients, place those coefficients in one number field $\\mathbb Q(\\xi)$ with the registered real embedding. Write $P=\\widetilde P(x,\\xi)$ with rational coefficients and take the resultant of $\\widetilde P(x,z)$ and the minimal polynomial of $\\xi$ in $z$. This is a nonzero rational polynomial. Its squarefree part has finitely many real roots, isolated by a Sturm sequence. Keep the roots satisfying the original $P=0$ in the registered embedding.\n- At every retained root, evaluate (O.2e.7) with positive radicals and keep it exactly when $T=0$. This removes the roots contributed by other radical-sign choices. Test $T$ at exact rational points in the neighboring open cells of the isolated root list. A physical root is a local minimum exactly when these signs are negative and positive. Spurious polynomial roots can subdivide a sign cell without changing the test. Evaluate the omitted point $t=\\pi$ and its two neighboring signs in the second chart, unless it is a cancellation point.\n- Reconstruct (O.2e.3), compare the exact algebraic energies, and evaluate $W''$ at each minimum to distinguish the two Hessian types. Algebraic equality and sign are decided by minimal polynomials and isolating intervals in the registered real embedding; all comparisons use exact signs.\n\nThe root lists are finite, the polynomials are nonzero, and all equality and order tests are effective for the stated algebraic input representation, so this procedure terminates. Its output covers the entire phase quotient by items 1--3. ∎\n\n**Degenerate unequal-edge minimum.** On $K_{2,3}$ take\n$$\n(a_1,b_1)=(a_2,b_2)=(1,2),\\qquad\n(a_3,b_3)=(2/3,4/3),\\qquad\n\\alpha_i=0,\\quad(\\beta_1,\\beta_2,\\beta_3)=(0,0,\\pi).\n\\tag{O.2e.14}\n$$\nEvery path has unequal edge weights and every $R_i$ is positive. The reduced potential is\n$$\nW(t)=8-2\\sqrt{5+4\\cos t}\n-\\frac23\\sqrt{5-4\\cos t}.\n\\tag{O.2e.15}\n$$\nIt has a unique global minimum at $t=0$, of value $4/3$, with\n$$\n(y_1,y_2,y_3)=(0,0,\\pi),\\qquad\nW(t)=\\frac43+\\frac{10}{27}t^4+O(t^6).\n\\tag{O.2e.16}\n$$\nTo prove uniqueness, put\n$$\nH(c)=2\\sqrt{5+4c}+\\frac23\\sqrt{5-4c},\\qquad -1\\le c\\le1.\n$$\nThen $H'(1)=0$ and\n$$\nH''(c)=-\\frac8{(5+4c)^{3/2}}\n-\\frac{8/3}{(5-4c)^{3/2}}<0.\n$$\nHence $H'(c)>0$ for $c<1$, so $H$ has its unique maximum at $c=1$. Since $H''(1)=-80/27$ and $\\cos t=1-t^2/2+O(t^4)$, (O.2e.16) follows. In the coordinates $(t,y_1,y_2,y_3)$ the Hessian is\n$$\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n16/3&-2&-2&-4/3\\\\\n-2&3&0&0\\\\\n-2&0&3&0\\\\\n-4/3&0&0&2/3\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\tag{O.2e.17}\n$$\nIts kernel is spanned by $(1,2/3,2/3,2)$, and (O.2e.13) gives three positive directions. Thus unequal-edge $\\theta$ graphs admit strict degenerate minima. The strictness conclusion of Theorem O.2e includes this quartic case.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o2f-exact-represented-law-landscapes-on-finite-graphs\"></a>**Theorem O.2f (Exact Represented-Law Landscapes on Finite Graphs).** The theorem of §3 below is the exact classification on the stated finite-Fourier and continuous semialgebraic circle-law domain.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o2g-uniform-frustrated-complete-graph-classification\"></a>**Theorem O.2g (Uniform Frustrated Complete-Graph Classification).** The theorem of §7 below gives the complete uniform pi-offset cosine family. The two propositions of §9 are representation/access-model boundary statements. Section numbers are local. The represented domain is a proper subclass of the original continuous-law domain.\n\n## 2. Exact phase compilation: no angle charts, no lost branch\n\nLet $G=(V,E)$ be any finite oriented graph, $c$ its number of connected components and $d=|V|-c$. Select one root $r_{C}$ in each component. Write $z_v=x_v+iy_v$, impose $x_v^{2}+y_v^{2}=1$, and fix $z_{r}=1$ at every selected root. Eliminate those root coordinates. The resulting real algebraic set\n\n$$\nX=\\{q\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2d}:x_{v}^{2}+y_{v}^{2}=1\\text{ for every nonroot }v\\}\n$$\n\nis exactly the phase torus modulo one common rotation on each component and vertexwise $2\\pi$ shifts. Each orbit has exactly one representative: divide all $z_{v}$ in a component by its root phasor. This is a homeomorphism from the quotient onto $X$, and locally a smooth diffeomorphism in angle coordinates. In particular local and strict-local minimum statements transfer in both directions. There is no half-angle pole or chart boundary.\n\nRepresent offsets by $A_e=\\cos\\theta_e$, $B_e=\\sin\\theta_e$, imposing $A_e^{2}+B_e^{2}=1$. For $e:t\\to h$, define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{e} &= x_{h}x_{t}+y_{h}y_{t}, \\\\\nT_{e} &= y_{h}x_{t}-x_{h}y_{t}, \\\\\nC_{e} &= A_{e}D_{e}+B_{e}T_{e}, \\\\\nS_{e} &= A_{e}T_{e}-B_{e}D_{e}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen $C_e+iS_e=z_h\\overline{z_t}(A_e-iB_e)=\\exp(i\\delta_e)$. In particular $C_e^{2}+S_e^{2}=1$ on $X$. The formula works for any graph size, cycle rank, disconnected graph, parallel edges, and loops; if the intended graph class is simple, simply restrict the graph input. A loop contributes a constant and causes no erroneous phase variable.\n\nFor a finite Fourier law\n\n$$\nU_{e}(t)=a_{e0}+\\sum_{k=1}^{m_{e}}[a_{ek}\\cos(kt)+b_{ek}\\sin(kt)],\n$$\n\ndefine polynomials $R_0=1$, $I_0=0$ and recursively\n\n$$\nR_{k+1}=CR_{k}-SI_{k},\\quad I_{k+1}=SR_{k}+CI_{k}.\n$$\n\nSet $P_e(C,S)=a_{e0}+\\sum_k(a_{ek}R_k+b_{ek}I_k)$. The *exact* compiled potential is the polynomial\n\n$$\nF(q)=\\sum_{e}w_{e}P_{e}(C_{e}(q),S_{e}(q)).\n\\tag{1}\n$$\n\nNo Fourier truncation is performed: the input is an exact finite series. Truncating an originally infinite series would require a different, approximate theorem and would not preserve degenerate minima.\n\nA graph reorientation changes $(\\theta_e,U_e(t))$ to $(-\\theta_e,U_e(-t))$; this sends $(C_e,S_e)$ to $(C_e,-S_e)$ and $b_{ek}$ to $-b_{ek}$, leaving $F$ unchanged. Changing roots changes the gauge by a polynomial circle-coordinate homeomorphism. Root choice and orientation therefore do not change the classified orbits, energies, or Hessian inertia. Vertex labels are retained; equal-energy states are not identified.\n\n### Admission is decidable, not presumed\n\nFor each edge, compile the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\forall C,S:\\ C^{2}+S^{2}=1\\Rightarrow[P_{e}(C,S)\\ge 0 \\\\\n&\\qquad\\text{and }(P_{e}(C,S)=0\\Leftrightarrow(C=1\\text{ and }S=0))].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n$$\n\nAlso require $w_{e}>0$ and $A_e^{2}+B_e^{2}=1$. Exact real quantifier elimination decides (2) for algebraic input. For symbolic coefficients it returns the precise semialgebraic admissible parameter set. The classifier itself works even outside (2), but only admitted parameters receive O.2b transfer credit. This check prevents accidental inclusion of higher-harmonic laws with extra zeros.\n\n## 3. The classification theorem and all-order decision formulas\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o2f1-all-finite-graphs-exact-algebraic-finite-fourier-data\"></a>**Theorem O.2f.1 (all finite graphs, exact algebraic finite Fourier data).** For input as above with algebraic coefficients, there is a terminating algorithm producing semialgebraic descriptions and exact cell decompositions of the entire critical set, local-minimum set L, strict-local-minimum set S, global-minimum set G, corresponding maximum sets, and the residual critical points. It gives the exact global minimum value and the finite list of critical energies, with all equality ties retained. It labels critical points by Hessian inertia, including every nullity. No isolated-critical-point, Morse, generic-weight, generic-offset, nonzero-resultant, or positive-definite-Hessian premise is imposed.\n\nFor $q,q'\\in\\mathbb{R}^{2d}$, let $\\rho(q,q')=\\sum_j(q_j-q'_j)^{2}$. Let $X(q)$ denote the circle equations. Use these explicit first-order formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(q) &:= X(q)\\text{ and }\\exists r>0\\ \\forall q': \\\\\n&\\qquad[X(q')\\text{ and }\\rho(q,q')<r^{2}\\Rightarrow F(q')\\ge F(q)]. \\\\\nS(q) &:= X(q)\\text{ and }\\exists r>0\\ \\forall q': \\\\\n&\\qquad[X(q')\\text{ and }0<\\rho(q,q')<r^{2}\\Rightarrow F(q')>F(q)]. \\\\\nG(q) &:= X(q)\\text{ and }\\forall q':[X(q')\\Rightarrow F(q')\\ge F(q)].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n$$\n\nMaximum formulas reverse inequalities; all are finite polynomial formulas with specified variables, degree and quantifier order. They distinguish strict quartic minima, flat families, flat maxima, and higher-order saddles without a finite Taylor cutoff. S is a subset of L. If $d=0$, X is a singleton and strict-locality is vacuous under the standard punctured-neighborhood definition; record this zero-dimensional case explicitly rather than reporting an attraction rate.\n\nFor energy t define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{Emin}(t) &:= \\exists q\\,[X(q)\\text{ and }F(q)=t \\\\\n&\\qquad\\text{and }\\forall q'(X(q')\\Rightarrow F(q')\\ge t)].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n$$\n\nCompactness of X ensures exactly one real t satisfies (4). With algebraic data, a uniquely defined semialgebraic real number is real algebraic, so the output is a minimal/square-free polynomial together with an isolating interval and selected root, rather than a decimal estimate.\n\n### Criticality and Hessian, in the actual phase metric\n\nFor each nonroot $v$ let $D_v=-y_v\\,\\partial/\\partial x_v+x_v\\,\\partial/\\partial y_v$, and compute the polynomial $J_v=D_vF$. The exact critical set is $X(q)\\wedge\\bigwedge_v J_v(q)=0$: these vector fields form a tangent basis everywhere on $X$. Compute $H_{uv}=D_uD_vF$. On $X$ this is precisely the Hessian of $F$ in local vertex-angle coordinates with roots pinned. Equivalently, if $\\bar{B}$ is $B$ with root rows removed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH &= \\bar{B}\\operatorname{diag}(w_{e}U_{e}''(\\delta_{e}))\\bar{B}^{\\mathsf T}, \\\\\nU_{e}'' &= -\\sum_{k}k^{2}(a_{ek}R_{k}(C_{e},S_{e})+b_{ek}I_{k}(C_{e},S_{e})).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n$$\n\nBoth expressions can be checked symbolically modulo circle equations. The reduced Hessian is congruent to any other gauge Hessian at a critical point, so its inertia is intrinsic to the quotient. It is not the ambient Euclidean Hessian in $\\mathbb{R}^{2d}$.\n\nRank $r$ is a polynomial predicate: all $(r+1)$-minors vanish and some $r$-minor is nonzero (with the usual rank-zero convention). Full inertia $(p,n,z)$, $p+n+z=d$, can be encoded without an incorrect principal-minor shortcut:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\exists Q,\\lambda_{1},\\ldots,\\lambda_{d}: \\\\\n&\\quad Q^{\\mathsf T}Q=I,\\quad HQ=Q\\operatorname{diag}(\\lambda), \\\\\n&\\quad\\lambda_{1},\\ldots,\\lambda_{p}>0;\\ \\lambda_{p+1},\\ldots,\\lambda_{p+n}<0; \\\\\n&\\quad\\lambda_{p+n+1}=\\cdots=\\lambda_{d}=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n$$\n\nReal symmetric spectral decomposition proves (6) is equivalent to that inertia. Apply (3), not inertia alone, to decide minima when $z>0$. One may use exact symmetric-matrix inertia algorithms instead of eliminating (6).\n\n## 4. A terminating construction, beyond an existence appeal\n\nThe imported algorithmic primitive is a **complete sign-invariant cylindrical algebraic decomposition** over the real algebraic coefficient field, with exact real-root isolation, cell sample encodings, and quantifier truth propagation. Standard complete CAD algorithms supply this primitive; a reduced projection that returns a well-orientedness/nullification failure is not an answer.\n\nProcedure:\n\n1. Parse the finite graph, connected components, exact algebraic isolating representations, Fourier degrees, and optional free parameters. Construct (1), $X$, (2), $J$ and $H$ by polynomial arithmetic. Preserve exact offset circle equations.\n2. Reject invalid admission data or return its exact failing condition. Do not discard admissible parameter boundaries where degree drops or coefficients vanish.\n3. For each formula in (3)–(6), order coordinates with free parameters first, free state/energy variables next, and quantified variables last in their quantifier-block order. Build a complete sign-invariant CAD for all atomic polynomials, using an established full projection/lifting algorithm. Include zero-dimensional sections and every lower-dimensional cell. Compute root counts and isolating samples with exact subresultant/Sturm methods. If an optional fast projection cannot certify nullification, switch to the complete algorithm; never discard that cell.\n4. At top-level cells, determine each atomic sign from its certified sample and assign the quantifier-free Boolean truth value. Propagate $\\exists$ as disjunction and $\\forall$ as conjunction over the complete stack over each lower cell, repeatedly from the innermost variable outward. A stack covers the whole real line, including root sections, open sectors, and unbounded sectors. Variables outside $X$ remain in stacks and are removed by the implication, not by an unproved chart restriction.\n5. Return all free-variable cells with true labels, as exact cylindrical root-function descriptions (or convert to equivalent quantifier-free polynomial formulas). An isolated solution has an exact algebraic point encoding; a positive-dimensional solution has an exact cell description and a sample point, not a purported finite list of representative phases.\n6. Apply the same procedure to $\\exists q[\\mathrm{critical}(q)\\wedge F(q)=t]$ and (4). Separate and order all energy roots exactly. For a requested connected-component decomposition, use the standard semialgebraic connected-component/roadmap algorithm on the returned formulas, rather than interpreting an arbitrary CAD cell as a connected component.\n7. Return a certificate containing the graph/root map, input isolating encodings, compiled polynomials, variable order, complete cell-stack descriptions, sign/root-isolation data, truth labels, and the explicit state/energy output formulas. An independent checker can recompute the complete CAD and verify equality of the proposed output to (3)–(6), plus admission (2). This is a potentially costly exact verifier, not a claim of a compact polynomial-size proof certificate.\n\n**Correctness and termination.** The recurrence computes $\\exp(ik\\delta)$ exactly, and the root gauge identifies exactly the requested quotient. Thus (3) is the topological definition of each minimum type on the original phase quotient; every possible perturbation is quantified over. Equations (5)–(6) identify all critical degeneracies without classifying them by a necessary-only test. Complete CAD terminates on the finite polynomial input and is sign-invariant on every cell. Cylindricity makes quantified truth constant on projected cells; stack truth propagation is exactly first-order semantics. This proves soundness and completeness, including repeated roots, positive-dimensional stationary sets, coefficient cancellation, and critical parameter boundaries. Compactness proves attainment in (4).\n\nThe list of critical energies is finite. The critical set is semialgebraic and has finitely many semialgebraically connected components. Join two points in a component by a piecewise $C^1$ semialgebraic path in $X$. On each smooth piece, $dF/dt=\\sum_v J_v\\,d\\phi_v/dt=0$, using finitely many local angle charts. Hence $F$ is constant on each component. Consequently the energy image is finite and CAD returns isolated algebraic energies. In particular all local-minimum energies are among this finite list, even when the minimum set has positive dimension.\n\nThis imports an established doubly-exponential-type general algorithm, not an efficient new optimizer. Full inertia formulas increase variable count. No practical bound for a large physical graph or polynomial runtime is claimed. Existing O.2c–O.2e structural algorithms remain preferable on their narrower domains.\n\n## 5. Parameter cells and exact bifurcation accounting\n\nFix a finite graph and finite degree bounds; retain all weights, Fourier coefficients and offset circle coordinates as real parameters $p$. All formulas above are polynomial formulas over the rationals in $p$ and state variables (fixed algebraic constants can also be encoded). Eliminating the quantified variables produces exact semialgebraic families $L(p,q)$, $S(p,q)$, $G(p,q)$, $\\mathrm{critical}(p,q)$, and the energy relation $E(p,t)$.\n\nFor state labels run a common CAD with parameter coordinates first and state coordinates q next. For energy labels run a common CAD with parameters first, then t, then any retained state coordinates. Refine the parameter partitions from these constructions together with admission and inertia labels, and reconstruct the state/energy CADs over that common refined parameter partition. This gives a finite partition of parameter space into exact cells such that:\n\n- admission is constant on each parameter cell;\n- each fiber is given by the same labeled finite cylindrical section/sector template;\n- dimensions of the classified fibers are constant on each parameter cell;\n- the number of strict minima is finite and constant on each parameter cell, with exact continuous semialgebraic state sections;\n- the distinct critical-energy levels are ordered continuous semialgebraic sections, their number is constant, and all energy ties and minimum/strictness/inertia labels have their exact cell descriptions.\n\nWhy strict minima are finite: §4 proves that $L$ has only finitely many energy values. At a strict local minimum $q$, continuity permits a neighborhood in which no other one of those energy values occurs. Strictness then excludes all distinct local minima in a smaller neighborhood, so $q$ is isolated in $L$. Conversely, if $q$ is a nonstrict local minimum, choose a neighborhood on which $F\\ge F(q)$; arbitrarily close distinct equal-energy points are themselves local minima on smaller neighborhoods. Hence, in this semialgebraic setting, a local minimum is strict exactly when it is isolated in $L$. This equivalence is not asserted for arbitrary continuous or smooth functions. A semialgebraic set has finitely many isolated points. With parameters first, the CAD fibers of $S$ therefore contain only zero-dimensional section stacks; their count and sections are uniform over a base cell.\n\nFor energy labels, use formulas such as $\\exists q[L(p,q)\\wedge F_p(q)=t]$ and its strict/global/inertia counterparts and include them in the common decomposition. This ensures that labels on a fixed energy section are invariant, not merely that the unlabelled energies have an order.\n\nThis is a **parameter-cell classification**, not a claim that every displayed cell is a minimal bifurcation stratum, or that ordinary CAD cells automatically form a Whitney stratification or preserve all attachment topology. Full labeled-fiber topological triviality would require an additional effective semialgebraic trivialization computation. It is not needed for the minima, dimension, count, energy-order and degeneracy outputs asserted here.\n\nFor arbitrary real coefficients, these finitely many formulas and root-section templates classify all parameter values mathematically. A terminating numerical classifier at a particular value requires an exact computable ordered-field representation or the algebraic input above. A real-number oracle supplying successively better approximations alone does not give exact sign decisions; §9 shows this restriction is substantive. Offsets themselves need not be algebraic angles: algebraic circle coordinates include $\\theta=\\pi$ and many irrational multiples of $\\pi$.\n\n## 6. Extension to continuous semialgebraic circle laws\n\nSuppose each edge law is supplied instead by a finite semialgebraic graph formula $\\Gamma_e(C,S,u;p)$, with exact algebraic constants. Require that for each $C^{2}+S^{2}=1$ there is exactly one $u$ satisfying $\\Gamma_{e}$, that the resulting circle function is continuous, nonnegative, and zero exactly at $(1,0)$. These are first-order conditions, including continuity via the usual $\\epsilon$–$\\eta$ distance formula, and can be checked with the same complete procedure.\n\nDefine the total energy graph\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Psi(q,t;p) &:= X(q)\\text{ and }\\exists(u_{e})_{e}: \\\\\n&\\qquad[\\textstyle\\bigwedge_{e}\\Gamma_{e}(C_{e}(q),S_{e}(q),u_{e};p) \\\\\n&\\qquad\\quad\\text{and }t=\\sum_{e}w_{e}u_{e}].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n$$\n\nUniqueness makes (7) the graph of a continuous function on compact $X$. Replace $F(q')\\ge F(q)$ in (3) by quantified energy graph comparisons, for example\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\exists t\\,[\\Psi(q,t)\\text{ and }\\exists r>0\\ \\forall q',t': \\\\\n&\\quad(\\Psi(q',t')\\text{ and }\\rho(q,q')<r^{2}\\Rightarrow t'\\ge t)].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{8}\n$$\n\nAnalogous strict and global formulas give exact all-graph classifications with no differentiability assumption. This covers, for example, $|\\sin(t/2)|=\\sqrt{(1-\\cos t)/2}$, piecewise polynomial circle laws, and rational/algebraic circle functions with verified domain/continuity. An auxiliary algebraic variable is retained with its correct branch inequalities; squaring alone is never used to select a branch.\n\nThere are finitely many local-minimum energies here too. On any semialgebraic path within L, F composed with the path is a continuous semialgebraic function for which every point is a local minimum. It must be constant (otherwise its finite monotonicity partition has a strictly monotone interval). Thus F is constant on every connected component of L. Only after this finite-energy conclusion, continuity and strictness imply isolation in L by the finite-energy-gap argument of §5; hence strict minima are finite. This is not an isolation inference for arbitrary continuous functions. Critical-set and Hessian assertions require additional differentiability/derivative graph data and are not assigned to nonsmooth laws merely because they are semialgebraic.\n\n## 7. A concrete complete classification beyond theta graphs\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o2g1-every-uniform-antiferromagnetic-complete-graph\"></a>**Theorem O.2g.1 (every uniform antiferromagnetic complete graph).** On $K_n$, $n\\ge 2$, take $U(t)=1-\\cos t$, a common weight $w>0$, and $\\theta_e=\\pi$ for every oriented edge. Set $z_j=\\exp(i\\phi_j)$, $M=\\sum_j z_j$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= w\\sum_{i<j}(1+\\cos(\\varphi_{j}-\\varphi_{i})) \\\\\n&= w[n(n-2)/2+|M|^{2}/2].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{9}\n$$\n\nThe entire local-minimum set equals the global-minimum set $\\{M=0\\}$, and the minimum is $wn(n-2)/2$. Every critical point with $M\\ne 0$ has a negative Hessian direction. At $M=0$ the quotient Hessian has rank two when the spins are not collinear, and rank one when they are collinear. The collinear case is possible only for even $n$, with equal numbers of plus and minus spins. For $n\\ge 4$ every minimum is nonstrict; for $n=2$ and $n=3$ the quotient minima are strict.\n\n**Proof.** Expand $|M|^{2}=n+2\\sum_{i<j}\\cos(\\phi_j-\\phi_i)$ to get (9). Zero magnetization is attained by the regular $n$-gon, so every $M=0$ state is global. Criticality is $\\operatorname{Im}(\\overline{M}z_j)=0$ for every $j$. If $M\\ne0$, rotate $M$ to the positive real axis and write $z_j=s_j\\in\\{\\pm1\\}$, with $r=\\sum_j s_j>0$. There are at least two majority (+) spins for every $n\\ge 2$. The Hessian quadratic form divided by $w$ is\n\n$$\nQ(\\eta)=\\Bigl(\\sum_{j}s_{j}\\eta_{j}\\Bigr)^{2}-r\\sum_{j}s_{j}\\eta_{j}^{2}.\n$$\n\nChoose $\\eta=1$ and $-1$ at two majority spins and zero elsewhere. Then $Q=-2r<0$. Subtracting a common constant if necessary puts this vector in the root gauge and leaves $Q$ unchanged. No such critical point is a local minimum. These two classes exhaust criticality.\n\nAt $M=0$, $H/w=(DM)^{\\mathsf T}(DM)$ in root-pinned angle coordinates. Its image is the real span of the nonroot vectors $iz_{j}$. At a noncollinear zero-magnetization state those vectors span $\\mathbb{R}^{2}$: if all nonroot vectors lay on one real line, their sum would force the root vector onto that line too. Thus rank is two. At a collinear zero-magnetization state rank is one, and zero sum forces an even balanced split. The quotient nullities are respectively $n-3$ and $n-2$.\n\nFor noncollinear states with $n\\ge 4$, the implicit-function theorem gives a local zero set of dimension $n-3\\ge 1$, hence nonstrictness. At a collinear state $n\\ge 4$, choose one antipodal pair and rotate it together while holding the remaining balanced spins fixed. This changes the quotient and preserves $M=0$, again proving nonstrictness. For $n=2$ there is one antipodal quotient point, with Hessian rank one; for $n=3$ there are the two labeled equilateral quotient configurations, with Hessian rank two. Their positive definite quotient Hessians prove strictness. ∎\n\n### Exact K4 geometry, with labels retained\n\nFix $z_{1}=1$. The full minimum quotient is the union of three circles, one per pairing of {1,2,3,4} into two opposite pairs. For example\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(u) &= (1,-1,u,-u), \\\\\nB(u) &= (1,u,-1,-u), \\\\\nC(u) &= (1,u,-u,-1),\\qquad |u|=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pairwise intersections are $A\\cap B=\\{(1,-1,-1,1)\\}$, $A\\cap C=\\{(1,-1,1,-1)\\}$ and $B\\cap C=\\{(1,1,-1,-1)\\}$. No triple intersection occurs. These are exact semialgebraic output components before resolving their intersection points; they are not three disconnected components. Their union is connected.\n\nTo prove coverage, let $z_1,\\ldots,z_4$ be unit complex numbers with sum zero. Their elementary symmetric functions satisfy $e_{1}=0$ and $e_3=e_4\\overline{e_1}=0$, so their root polynomial is $X^{4}+e_2X^{2}+e_4$, an even polynomial. Roots therefore occur in antipodal pairs, including multiplicities. This yields exactly the three pairing circles above. Each pair of circles meets at one of the three root-fixed collinear states having two plus and two minus spins. There are no triple intersections. Generic points have quotient Hessian nullity one; the three intersections have nullity two. All are genuine local/global minima with energy $4w$, never strict. This is a useful adversarial check against any classifier that assumes finitely many minima or rejects every singular Hessian.\n\n## 8. Degenerate strictness and exact bifurcation witnesses\n\n### All-zero Hessian at a strict minimum\n\nOn any connected graph, positive weights and zero offsets, use\n\n$$\nU(t)=(1-\\cos t)^{2}=3/2-2\\cos t+(1/2)\\cos(2t).\n$$\n\nIt is an admitted O.2b law. Synchronization is the unique global-minimum quotient point, hence a strict minimum, but its entire quotient Hessian is zero. For a root-pinned displacement $\\eta$,\n\n$$\nV(s\\eta)=s^{4}/4\\sum_{e}w_{e}((B^{\\mathsf T}\\eta)_{e})^{4}+O(s^{6}).\n$$\n\nThe quartic form is strictly positive for $\\eta\\ne 0$ in the quotient, because connectedness makes $B^{\\mathsf T}$ injective there. On its unit sphere it has a positive minimum. The all-order formulas (3) admit this strict degenerate point; a positive-definite-Hessian-only method would miss it. No assertion that this is the only *local* minimum is needed for this witness.\n\n### Complete one-edge bifurcation, including the zero-Hessian boundary\n\nFor $b\\ge 0$ set\n\n$$\nU_{b}(t)=1-\\cos t+b(1-\\cos 2t).\n$$\n\nEvery $b$ is admitted because the first summand is positive off $2\\pi\\mathbb{Z}$. Its derivative is $\\sin t(1+4b\\cos t)$. Thus:\n\n- $t=0$ is the unique global minimum and a strict local minimum for every $b\\ge 0$.\n- For $0\\le b<1/4$, $t=\\pi$ is a strict local maximum and no other critical points occur.\n- At $b=1/4$, $t=\\pi$ is a strict quartically degenerate local maximum, not a minimum.\n- For $b>1/4$, $t=\\pi$ is a strict nonglobal local minimum of energy 2; the two points $\\cos t=-1/(4b)$ are strict maxima, with energy $1+2b+1/(8b)$.\n\nAt $b=1/4+a$, exact expansion at $\\pi$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU_{b}(\\pi+h) &= 2+2ah^{2}-(1/8+2a/3)h^{4} \\\\\n&\\quad+(1/48+4a/45)h^{6}+O(h^{8}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{10}\n$$\n\nThese formulas give all critical points, labels and equality/inequality parameter strata, rather than only a generic positive-Hessian test.\n\n## 9. Why the continuous-law residual is real\n\nThe original source does not specify a computational oracle for every continuous law. The following statements concern explicitly declared access models. They are **not** a proof that mathematical classification of continuous landscapes is impossible, and do not by themselves settle the continuous-law residual.\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o2g2-finite-evaluation-oracle-obstruction\"></a>**Proposition O.2g.2 (finite evaluation-oracle obstruction).** There is no always-terminating deterministic algorithm which, given only evaluation access to an arbitrary continuous admitted edge law, returns its exact full local-minimum set, even on a single edge with zero offset. This remains true if each query requests finitely many derivatives and the promised law is C∞.\n\n**Proof.** Run the alleged algorithm on $U(t)=1-\\cos t$. It terminates after finitely many queries. Select a small open arc $J$ compactly contained in $(0,\\pi)$, disjoint from every queried phase modulo $2\\pi$. The base law is strictly positive on $J$ and has strictly positive derivative there. Choose a smooth compactly supported negative bump on a still smaller subarc, of amplitude less than half the base-law minimum on $J$ and of sufficiently narrow width that its derivative overwhelms the positive base derivative on part of its descending side. The perturbed law stays positive on $J$, equals $U$ outside $J$, and has the same unique zero modulo $2\\pi$. Its derivative is positive near the support endpoints, negative somewhere on the descending side, and positive later; consequently it has an interior local minimum in $J$. All values and derivative jets at the finitely queried phases are unchanged. Adaptivity gives the same entire query transcript and output, yet the two local-minimum sets differ. Contradiction. ∎\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o2g3-approximation-input-is-insufficient-even-at-fourier-degree-two\"></a>**Proposition O.2g.3 (approximation input is insufficient even at Fourier degree two).** No uniform total algorithm, receiving a computable-real approximation program for every coefficient, can decide exact local minimality of a nominated phase for all admitted degree-two trigonometric laws.\n\n**Proof.** Given a Turing machine $T$, define $a_{T}=0$ if $T$ never halts, and $a_T=2^{-h}$ if it first halts at step $h\\ge 1$. This is a uniformly computable nonnegative real: to approximate to error at most $2^{-N}$, simulate through $N$ steps and output $2^{-h}$ if halting has occurred, otherwise zero. Consider $U_{1/4+a_T}$ above. The phase $\\pi$ is a local minimum exactly when $a_{T}>0$ by (10), exactly when $T$ halts. An exact total decision algorithm would decide the halting problem. ∎\n\nEvery individual coefficient in this reduction happens to be rational, but its *input representation* does not reveal an exact rational value or an equality certificate. The theorem is therefore entirely compatible with the exact algebraic-input algorithm. It rules out silently substituting numerical approximation programs for the required exact coefficient representation.\n\n## 10. MPU transfer and what is not realized\n\nThe transfer to the registered mathematical potential is exact: (1) equals O.2b.1 pointwise, X is the exact phase quotient, positive weights and exact-zero admission are verified, and minima/energies/criticality transport by the gauge map. For a source satisfying O.2's phase-independent remainder hypothesis, adding that remainder at a fixed nonphase state does not change any phase minimum. The O.2b holonomy criterion remains applicable; no statement here forces synchronization at frustrated offsets or makes every local minimum global.\n\nThis mathematical transfer does not construct the source that selects the edge law. O.1 begins from positive finite-alphabet predictive distributions $p_j(y|t)$, local regularity and temporal Fisher information; O.2 assumes additional phase-independence, low-noise and detailed-balance conditions. A symbolic $U_{e}$ chosen by the classifier does not prove it is the inclusive PCE phase cost of a nominated predictive loop.\n\nSource completion requires the actual retained carrier/phase and cycle mapping; derivation of every edge cost from the prediction/verification/update and compensation ledger rather than postulation; control of phase dependence in all remaining terms; response-faithful physical dynamics and any low-noise/detailed-balance assumptions used; and complete source, clock, preparation, control, reset and boundary resource accounting. Neither the §7 spin Hamiltonian nor CAD's polynomial potential is an MPU realization. Theorem O.2p supplies these requirements on its intrinsic-cycle source tower, with the paid clock translation as its physical dynamics and all invoices retained. Any low-noise or detailed-balance use retains its independent premises.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o2h-complete-wrapped-quadratic-minima-on-every-finite-graph\"></a>**Theorem O.2h (Complete Wrapped-Quadratic Minima on Every Finite Graph).** Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite oriented multigraph, allowing isolated vertices, disconnected components, parallel edges and self-loops. Give each edge a weight $w_e>0$ and an offset $\\theta_e$ modulo $2\\pi$, normalized to $[-\\pi,\\pi)$. Put\n$$\nU(t)=\\frac12\\operatorname{dist}(t,2\\pi\\mathbb Z)^2,\\qquad\nV(\\phi)=\\sum_e w_eU(\\phi_{h(e)}-\\phi_{t(e)}-\\theta_e).\n\\tag{O.2h.1}\n$$\nThis is a continuous periodic nonnegative law with zero set $2\\pi\\mathbb Z$, admitted by O.2b. Orientation specifies the edge-coordinate convention. A self-loop contributes only its constant $w_eU(-\\theta_e)$; remove the loops and retain their sum $C_{\\mathrm{loop}}$. Root each remaining connected component, including isolated vertices, at one vertex. Modulo one common rotation per component, the phase space is $\\mathbb T^d$, where $d=|V|-c$. Use the rooted representatives in $[0,2\\pi)^d$ only for the finite-lift bound; local calculus uses open angle charts.\n\nLet $m$ be the number of nonloop edges, $B$ the $d\\times m$ incidence matrix with root rows deleted, $W=\\operatorname{diag}(w_e)$ and $L=BWB^{\\mathsf T}$. For each $k\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}^m$ form\n$$\na_k=\\theta+2\\pi k,\\qquad\n\\phi_k=L^{-1}BWa_k,\\qquad r_k=B^{\\mathsf T}\\phi_k-a_k.\n\\tag{O.2h.2}\n$$\nRetain the candidate exactly when\n$$\n-\\pi<r_{k,e}<\\pi\\quad\\text{for every nonloop edge }e.\n\\tag{O.2h.3}\n$$\nRestore roots as zero, reduce the other phases modulo $2\\pi$, and identify equal rooted phase vectors, preserving all vertex labels. The distinct retained orbits are exactly all local minima of $V$. Each is strict, has angle-chart Hessian $L$, and has energy\n$$\nE_k=C_{\\mathrm{loop}}+\\frac12r_k^{\\mathsf T}Wr_k.\n\\tag{O.2h.4}\n$$\nThere are at least one and at most $3^m$. The global minima are exactly the retained orbits of least energy, including all ties. Two retained candidates give the same orbit if and only if their residual vectors agree. Equal energies alone do not identify states. No genericity, zero-holonomy or nonfrustration hypothesis is required. In dimension zero the empty inverse denotes the unique empty map; the quotient is a singleton and strict minimality has its usual vacuous punctured-neighborhood meaning.\n\n*Proof: positivity and cusp exclusion.* A root-fixed vector with $B^{\\mathsf T}\\eta=0$ is constant on each component and zero at its root. Thus $B^{\\mathsf T}$ is injective and\n$$\n\\eta^{\\mathsf T}L\\eta=\\sum_e w_e(b_e^{\\mathsf T}\\eta)^2>0\\quad(\\eta\\ne0).\n\\tag{O.2h.5}\n$$\nAt a chosen phase lift in an open chart, let $A$ be the nonloop edges whose lag is an odd multiple of $\\pi$. For fixed tangent $\\eta$ and sufficiently small $|s|$, noncut edges stay in their quadratic wrapping branch. At a cut edge,\n$$\nU(\\pi+s u)=\\frac{\\pi^2}2-\\pi|su|+\\frac{s^2u^2}2.\n$$\nSmooth linear terms cancel in the symmetric difference, giving the exact identity\n$$\nV(\\phi+s\\eta)+V(\\phi-s\\eta)-2V(\\phi)\n=-2\\pi|s|\\sum_{e\\in A}w_e|b_e^{\\mathsf T}\\eta|\n +s^2\\eta^{\\mathsf T}L\\eta.\n\\tag{O.2h.6}\n$$\nIf $A$ is nonempty, choose $\\eta=b_e$ for one of its edges. This reduced incidence column is nonzero: a nonloop edge joins one component, which has only one root, so both endpoints cannot be roots. The coefficient of $|s|$ is strictly negative, and for arbitrarily small nonzero $s$ one of the two perturbed phases has smaller energy. This excludes every nonloop cut edge at a local minimum, even with simultaneous cusps. Loops were removed because their zero columns cannot supply such a direction.\n\n*Proof: branch minima and completeness.* For any integer lift $k\\in\\mathbb Z^m$, the open cell\n$$\n\\mathcal C_k=\\{\\phi\\in\\mathbb R^d:|B^{\\mathsf T}\\phi-\\theta-2\\pi k|<\\pi\\}\n$$\nis convex, possibly empty. On it the energy is $C_{\\mathrm{loop}}+\\frac12(B^{\\mathsf T}\\phi-a_k)^{\\mathsf T}W(B^{\\mathsf T}\\phi-a_k)$, with gradient $L\\phi-BWa_k$ and Hessian $L$. A local minimum must equal $\\phi_k$. Conversely, when the strict residual test holds, a whole neighborhood stays in the cell and\n$$\nV(\\phi_k+h)-V(\\phi_k)=\\frac12h^{\\mathsf T}Lh>0\\quad(h\\ne0)\n$$\nthere. A stationary point outside its open cell is not retained, and equality at a cut is excluded by (O.2h.6).\n\nNow take any quotient minimum in its rooted half-open representative. Edge differences lie strictly between $-2\\pi$ and $2\\pi$; normalized offsets give\n$$\n-3\\pi<(B^{\\mathsf T}\\phi-\\theta)_e<3\\pi.\n$$\nCusp exclusion gives a unique wrapping integer with residual in $(-\\pi,\\pi)$, necessarily $k_e\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}$. Stationarity in an actual open chart identifies the minimum with (O.2h.2), including a representative at coordinate zero. Conversely every retained candidate is a true local minimum before reduction and remains one by periodicity. This proves the finite census without introducing artificial chart-boundary minima.\n\n*Proof: quotient and global ties.* If two retained lifts represent the same rooted orbit, $\\phi_k-\\phi_l=2\\pi z$ for $z\\in\\mathbb Z^d$, so\n$$\nr_k-r_l=2\\pi[B^{\\mathsf T}z-(k-l)].\n$$\nEach component is a multiple of $2\\pi$ strictly between $-2\\pi$ and $2\\pi$, hence zero. Conversely, equality of residuals gives $B^{\\mathsf T}[(\\phi_k-\\phi_l)/(2\\pi)]=k-l\\in\\mathbb Z^m$. Integrating these integer edge differences along a rooted spanning forest makes every vertex difference integral. The orbits therefore agree exactly. Compactness of the phase torus and continuity of $V$ give a global minimum; it is local and is in the finite list. Comparing (O.2h.4) retains every global energy tie. Loop constants shift every energy equally. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o2h1-exact-algebraic-classification-and-parameter-cells\"></a>**Corollary O.2h.1 (Exact Algebraic Classification and Parameter Cells).** In units of $\\pi$, write $\\alpha=\\theta/\\pi$, $x_k=\\phi_k/\\pi$ and $\\rho_k=r_k/\\pi$. Then\n$$\nx_k=L^{-1}BW(\\alpha+2k),\\qquad\n\\rho_k=B^{\\mathsf T}x_k-\\alpha-2k,\\qquad\n|\\rho_{k,e}|<1,\\qquad\n\\frac{E_k}{\\pi^2}=\\frac{C_{\\mathrm{loop}}}{\\pi^2}\n +\\frac12\\rho_k^{\\mathsf T}W\\rho_k.\n\\tag{O.2h.7}\n$$\nFor exactly represented real algebraic weights and normalized offsets $\\alpha$, these are real algebraic objects. Polynomial and root-isolation data permit terminating decisions of positivity, strict boundary rejection, residual equivalence and all energy ties. Phase reduction uses exact algebraic floors, or one can deduplicate entirely by residuals. The output phases are algebraic multiples of $\\pi$; their sine and cosine need not be algebraic.\n\nFor a fixed positive-support graph, regard $w$ and normalized $\\alpha$ as parameters. All candidates, residuals and normalized energies are rational functions with denominator $\\det L>0$; in dimension zero set $\\det L=1$. Clear this positive denominator for retention inequalities and residual equalities, and its square for energy comparisons. Finitely many polynomial sign conditions give a finite sign-invariant semialgebraic parameter decomposition on which lift retention, orbit identifications, counts and energy orders and ties are fixed. Normalized loop offsets and weights add only a common polynomial energy constant. The equality $|\\rho_{k,e}|=1$ rejects a lift rather than creating a marginal minimum. Zero weights and changes of graph support require a separate parameter problem. This proves a terminating exhaustive classifier, with no polynomial-time or minimal-stratification claim. $\\square$\n\nThe wrapped-angle class is distinct from both the unwrapped quadratic of O.2a and the circle-semialgebraic class of O.2f. The cusp prevents a finite Fourier representation. For the stronger distinction, on a short positive arc set $z=\\tan(t/2)>0$. A semialgebraic circle graph for $U$ would make its positive square root $\\sqrt{U/2}=\\arctan z$ semialgebraic. An analytic semialgebraic function is algebraic over $\\mathbb R(z)$, whereas $\\arctan z$ is not: its derivative $1/(1+z^2)$ has a nonzero residue at $z=i$. After any ramified parameter $z-i=u^q$, the differential still has a nonzero $u^{-1}$ coefficient; the differential of an algebraic Puiseux series has zero residue. This contradiction proves that the circle-semialgebraic compiler does not already contain this angle-chart law.\n\n<a id=\"example-o2h2-cycles-cusps-parallel-ties-and-products\"></a>**Example O.2h.2 (Cycles, Cusps, Parallel Ties and Products).** On a consistently oriented cycle $C_n$, $n\\ge3$, put $\\Theta=\\sum_e\\theta_e$ and $S=\\sum_e1/w_e$. Stationarity makes $w_er_e$ a common current, and the cycle condition gives, for $q\\in\\mathbb Z$,\n$$\nJ_q=\\frac{2\\pi q-\\Theta}{S},\\qquad r_e=\\frac{J_q}{w_e},\\qquad\nE_q=\\frac12J_q^2S.\n\\tag{O.2h.8}\n$$\nA unique labeled rooted orbit exists for this $q$ exactly when $|J_q|<\\pi\\min_e w_e$; integrate the edge increments to reconstruct it. Distinct such $q$ have distinct residuals. At zero offsets and equal weight $w$,\n$$\n|q|<n/2,\\qquad E_q=\\frac{2w\\pi^2q^2}{n},\\qquad\n\\#\\{\\text{local minima}\\}=2\\lceil n/2\\rceil-1.\n\\tag{O.2h.9}\n$$\nThe triangle has synchronization and two twists $\\pm2\\pi/3$, of energies $0,2w\\pi^2/3,2w\\pi^2/3$. The latter are strict maxima for the cosine law, whose Hessian there is $-w/2$ times the unweighted reduced Laplacian. An even cycle's nominal $q=\\pm n/2$ is a cusp and is excluded by (O.2h.6).\n\nFor zero-offset triangle weights $(a,2,2)$, $a>0$, the two $q=\\pm1$ minima exist exactly when $a>1$. At $a=1$ the weak-edge residual reaches $\\pm\\pi$ and both disappear, although $L$ stays positive definite for every $a>0$. Thus the exact minimum counts at $1-10^{-50},1,1+10^{-50}$ are $(1,1,3)$; this is a cusp threshold rather than a zero Hessian eigenvalue.\n\nTwo unit-weight parallel edges with offsets $(0,-\\pi)$ have two distinct global minima $\\phi_1=\\pi/2,3\\pi/2$, both of energy $\\pi^2/4$. Averaging their offsets or deduplicating by energy would lose a state. On a forest, the rooted $B$ is square and invertible, so stationarity forces zero residual and offset integration gives the unique quotient global minimum. Disconnected graphs give Cartesian products of component minima and sums of energies; isolated vertices vanish in the rotation quotient. Antipodal self-loops remain constants.\n\nTheorem O.2h completes this wrapped-quadratic mathematical subclass, including all weights, offsets and finite graph topologies stated above. Theorems O.2m and O.2p supply the continuous periodic-law classification and the intrinsic-cycle source realization.\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o2i-intrinsic-derivative-graphs-for-semialgebraic-circle-laws\"></a>**Proposition O.2i (Intrinsic Derivative Graphs for Semialgebraic Circle Laws).** In the continuous semialgebraic circle-law branch of Theorem O.2f, a supplied $C^1$ promise suffices to construct the exact first derivative graph by a finite first-order formula. A supplied $C^2$ promise similarly constructs the second derivative graph. Thus the all-finite-graph stationary sets and, on the $C^2$ branch, the full phase-Hessian inertia have the same uniform semialgebraic classification and exact-input effectiveness as Theorem O.2f.1; derivative values need not be separately supplied as an oracle.\n\n*Proof.* Let $L(c,s,u;p)$ be the single-valued total graph of an edge law on $c^2+s^2=1$. For $z=(c,s)$ define the rational circle rotation\n$$\n R_h(c,s)=\\left(\\frac{(1-h^2)c-2hs}{1+h^2},\n \\frac{2hc+(1-h^2)s}{1+h^2}\\right).\n \\tag{O.2i.1}\n$$\nIt rotates by $2\\arctan h$. If $L(z,u;p)$, the intrinsic angular derivative $d$ is specified by\n$$\n \\forall\\epsilon>0\\ \\exists\\eta>0\\ \\forall h,u'\\quad\n \\bigl(0<h^2<\\eta^2\\ \\wedge\\ L(R_hz,u';p)\\bigr)\n \\Longrightarrow (u'-u-2dh)^2<\\epsilon^2h^2.\n \\tag{O.2i.2}\n$$\nThis is precisely the derivative limit, since $2\\arctan h=2h+o(h)$. The two rotated coordinates can be introduced by polynomial equalities; their only denominator is $1+h^2>0$. Formula (O.2i.2), together with the graph of $u$, is therefore a finite ordered-ring formula. Under the $C^1$ promise it defines the unique angular derivative at every circle point. Complete real quantifier elimination produces its semialgebraic graph. Apply the same construction to that graph on the $C^2$ branch to obtain the second derivative. The promises themselves can also be expressed through finite first-order limit and continuity formulas when their verification is requested.\n\nAttach these derivative variables to the exact edge phasors of Theorem O.2f. With incidence matrix $B$ and anchored matrix $B_*$, full tangent stationarity is $B\\operatorname{diag}(w)d=0$ and the phase Hessian is $B_*\\operatorname{diag}(w_eU_e'')B_*^{\\mathsf T}$. The existing all-order neighborhood predicates and complete real-closed-field procedure then classify extrema, stationary sets and inertia, retaining equalities, singular sets and all parameter cells. Effective numerical decisions require the same exact algebraic or declared effective ordered-field input representation. Arbitrary continuous black-box laws are outside this representation class. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"construction-o2j-finite-rail-cosine-response-and-recoverable-load-work\"></a>**Construction O.2j (Finite Rail Cosine Response and Recoverable Load Work).** On the positive oscillator-rail and occupation-instrument source of Theorem 8.8f.3, every finite cosine graph of Theorem O.2f has a complete normalized physical comparison instrument with expected response\n$$\n \\mathbb E\\sum_e4w_e\\mathbf1_{\\{-,e\\}}\n =\\sum_e w_e[1-\\cos(\\phi_{h(e)}-\\phi_{t(e)}-\\theta_e)].\n \\tag{O.2j.1}\n$$\nA separately calibrated load can give this same cosine function as its expected work subledger. Three explicit words below have the same completed classical comparison and predictive records while their signed load-work receipts differ. The construction realizes a response and its optional load; the full Definition-D.1 phase-cost identity (O.2j.11) is supplied by Theorem O.2p on its intrinsic-cycle source tower.\n\n*Construction and proof.* **Physical clocks and the complete instrument.** Fix independently calibrated $\\hbar,\\omega>0$, seconds, joules and positive rail baselines. The state\n$$\n |\\psi(\\alpha)\\rangle=\\frac{|0\\rangle+e^{i\\alpha}|1\\rangle}{\\sqrt2}\n$$\nunder $BI+\\hbar\\omega|1\\rangle\\langle1|$ has phase $\\alpha-\\omega t$, ray recurrence $2\\pi/\\omega$ and mean excitation $\\hbar\\omega/2$. The characteristic time $\\hbar/E_{\\rm exc}=2/\\omega$ differs from that ray recurrence.\n\nFor edge $t\\to h$, prepare $|\\Psi\\rangle=|\\psi(\\phi_t+\\theta)\\rangle\\otimes|\\psi(\\phi_h)\\rangle$. The settings are registered preparation data. Reusing one vertex setting across its edges prepares distinct charged copies; it does not clone an unknown clock state or measure an unknown phase exactly. Encode the ordered product basis $(00,01,10,11)$ in four distinct rails carrying one excitation, with code isometry $J$ and positive spectrum\n$$\n H_C=\\operatorname{diag}(B_C,B_C+\\hbar\\omega,\n B_C+\\hbar\\omega,B_C+2\\hbar\\omega),\\qquad B_C>0.\n \\tag{O.2j.2}\n$$\nPadding rails have positive frequencies and their complete failure sectors. Set\n$$\n |a\\rangle=(|01\\rangle-|10\\rangle)/\\sqrt2,\\qquad\n P_-=|a\\rangle\\langle a|,\\quad P_+=I-P_-.\n$$\nThe $01,10$ sector is degenerate, so $[H_C,P_-]=0$. A fixed quadratic rotation in this sector maps $|a\\rangle$ to rail $01$. Copy that rail's occupation to a fresh degenerate dual-rail pointer by a positive cross-Kerr pulse and pointer Hadamards, then undo the sector rotation. The complete labeled leaves are exactly\n$$\n \\mathcal I_-(\\rho)=P_-\\rho P_-,\\qquad\n \\mathcal I_+(\\rho)=P_+\\rho P_+.\n \\tag{O.2j.3}\n$$\nThe rank-three plus complement retains all its coherences; a four-occupation resolution would be a different instrument. Every gate intertwines through $J$, so (O.2j.3) holds on every input density matrix and every spectator extension. Off-code zero/multiple-excitation outcomes are retained as failure leaves. They have zero probability on the exact invariant prepared code and are not discarded by conditioning.\n\nAt a common observation time the equal free drift cancels from the relative phase. Directly,\n$$\n \\langle a|\\Psi\\rangle=\n \\frac{e^{i\\phi_h}-e^{i(\\phi_t+\\theta)}}{2\\sqrt2},\\qquad\n p_- =\\frac{1-\\cos\\delta}{4},\\quad p_+=1-p_-,\\quad\n \\delta=\\phi_h-\\phi_t-\\theta.\n \\tag{O.2j.4}\n$$\nThus $0\\le p_-\\le1/2$ and $4\\mathbf1_-$ is an unbiased observation of $1-\\cos\\delta$. Independently prepare one pair and pointer for every edge and trial. Conditional on the common registered vertex settings, the edge outcomes are independent and summation gives (O.2j.1), with arbitrary finite cycle overlap. The graph gauge leaves these probabilities unchanged.\n\n**Positive-duration control with drift retained.** For a single finite rail register with positive diagonal code Hamiltonian $H_0$ and requested unitary $U$, choose $A=A^*$ with $e^{-iA}=e^{iH_0T/\\hbar}U$ and $\\|A\\|\\le\\pi$. With $s(t)=3(t/T)^2-2(t/T)^3$, use\n$$\n H(t)=H_0+\\hbar\\dot s(t)e^{-iH_0t/\\hbar}Ae^{iH_0t/\\hbar},\\qquad0\\le t\\le T.\n \\tag{O.2j.5}\n$$\nIn the interaction picture its endpoint is $e^{-iA}=e^{iH_0T/\\hbar}U$, so its physical endpoint is precisely $U$. The control vanishes at both endpoints, has code norm action at most $\\pi\\hbar$, and peak norm at most $3\\pi\\hbar/(2T)$. Choose $\\lambda_{\\min}(H_0)>3\\pi\\hbar/(2T)$ or increase $T$. The full one-particle coefficient matrix is then positive; its quadratic second quantization is nonnegative on every occupation sector, with invariant one-excitation code. This is exact prescribed-time control, with no rotating-wave approximation or common-period assumption.\n\nFor copying a selected occupation $Q$ to pointer level $B1$, use the nonnegative cross-Kerr term\n$$\n H_K(t)=\\hbar k(t)Qn_{B1},\\qquad k(t)\\ge0,\\qquad\\int k(t)\\,dt=\\pi,\n \\tag{O.2j.6}\n$$\ntogether with the registers' free Hamiltonians. It implements controlled $Z$; pointer Hadamards implement controlled $X$. Idle signal registers and pointer drift receive the separately charged compensated endpoints from (O.2j.5). Every needed factor rotation, equality copy, conditional load and recovery is therefore a finite word of local quadratic gates and positive occupation-product pulses on genuine separate registers. Preallocate positive-duration slots for preparation, signal rotation, pointer Hadamard, copy, pointer Hadamard, inverse rotation, detector and optional load/recovery. Identity padding gives the same completed output time for compared words. The claim concerns these completed boundaries, not intermediate pulses.\n\nThese registers may be tensored with the actual finite predictive sector of Theorem O.3j.1, Part6, on the same rail source. Its eight-CNOT word realizes\n$$\n (m,0,0,y,0,0)_{(M,P_b,I,Y,A,F)}\n \\longmapsto(y,0,0,y,m,m\\mathbin\\oplus y),\n \\tag{O.2j.7}\n$$\nwith the forecast archive written before acquisition. A fair initial environment and independent Bernoulli-$1/4$ increment give prediction score $3/4$ against the matched independent fair score $1/2$. Its prepared tape, seam, finite horizon, predictive archives and initial seeds retain the ownership and error account in that theorem. Only its actual register maps and rail compiler are composed here; no original program minimum or identification of the comparison clock with the predictive cycle is inferred.\n\n**Optional load and its signed endpoint work.** Give a fresh one-excitation work cell spectrum $(B_W,B_W+\\Delta)$, $B_W,\\Delta>0$, initially in its lower rail. Apply\n$$\n U_{\\rm load}=P_+\\otimes I_W+P_-\\otimes X_W,\n \\tag{O.2j.8}\n$$\nor its equivalent minus-pointer-controlled form. It is compiled by (O.2j.5)--(O.2j.6). In each recorded leaf the cell is an energy eigenstate, and\n$$\n W_{\\rm load}=\\begin{cases}0,&+,\\\\\\Delta,&-,\\end{cases}\n \\qquad \\mathbb E W_{\\rm load}=\\frac\\Delta4(1-\\cos\\delta).\n \\tag{O.2j.9}\n$$\nThe coupling vanishes at endpoints. For the closed pair--pointer--work-cell comparison/load stroke, with the predictive sector held fixed during that stroke, von Neumann evolution gives $d\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho H)/dt=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho\\dot H)$, so its inclusive mean drive work is exactly $\\Delta p_-$. Preparation, predictive-word and archive-service energy changes remain separately charged and are not included in this load-stroke equality. The pair/pointer endpoint mean energy is unchanged because the comparison commutes with $H_C$ and the pointer code is degenerate. There is no bath in this stroke. Pair preparation separately raises its mean energy from $B_C$ to $B_C+\\hbar\\omega$, independent of phase.\n\nFor a registered cell duration $T_{\\rm cell}$ and positive weight $w_e$ in power units, choose $\\Delta_e=4T_{\\rm cell}w_e$. Then\n$$\n \\frac1{T_{\\rm cell}}\\mathbb E\\sum_eW_{{\\rm load},e}\n =\\sum_ew_e(1-\\cos\\delta_e).\n \\tag{O.2j.10}\n$$\nThis is the calibrated load-work subledger.\n\n**Three words on one carrier.** Word A prepares the pair and predictive sector, runs the complete comparison and the same predictive word, retains pointer and predictive archives, and leaves the work cell ready. Word B performs the same operations and adds the conditional load. Word C performs B and a second conditional $X$ using the still-retained minus pointer before the completed endpoint. All words are padded to the same output time. On each recorded branch C maps $|q\\rangle_{\\rm pointer}|q\\rangle_W$ to $|q\\rangle_{\\rm pointer}|0\\rangle_W$, without erasing the pointer. Thus A, B and C have exactly the same two completed classical Lüders leaves, conditional pair states, predictive records and their spectators; C also restores the work cell. The second pulse supplies work $-\\Delta$ on minus and zero on plus, giving C zero net load work.\n\nThe coherent boundary is explicit. Before classical dephasing, A's pair--pointer state is $\\sum_{q,r}P_q\\rho P_r\\otimes|q\\rangle\\langle r|$. B adds $|q\\rangle\\langle r|_W$ to these terms; tracing its work cell removes the $q\\ne r$ coherences. Coherent recovery in C restores A's pair--pointer state and factorizes the ready work cell. After an actual classical read, equality is only of the completed classical instrument; previously lost coherences are not restored by this claim. A and B are distinguished by their retained work/energy ports, so their equality on the comparison response algebra is not full-source PPI equivalence. If delivery of the charged cell is part of the declared target, it is an additional response distinguishing B from A/C.\n\n**Complete finite ledger and original scope.** The source owns the independent time standard, all phase settings and timestamps; every signal, padding, pointer, work and predictive register and positive baseline; every fresh preparation and seed; each drive matrix, field phase, duration, peak, norm action and recovered/supplied work; each complete detector leaf and archive; all spent registers and correlations. A finite trial needs no erasure: fresh inputs are charged and spent ones remain owned. Reuse requires its actual reset/error/heat and side-information account. Work delivered and drive work are one transfer, not two charges. The nondemolition comparison has zero net endpoint pair/pointer work on every input while its control action, holding, preparation, meter and archive services remain present.\n\nThe three words prove that the same cosine comparison law and predictive records do not force a mandatory cosine work cost. Source completion of this cost law requires the actual same-source phase/cycle map and the full identity below, which Theorem O.2p supplies on its intrinsic-cycle source tower. The identity is\n$$\n V_{\\rm op}(\\phi)+V_{\\rm prop}(\\phi)-V_{\\rm benefit}(\\phi)+V_{\\rm penalty}(\\phi)\n =C+\\sum_ew_eU_e(\\phi_{h(e)}-\\phi_{t(e)}-\\theta_e),\n \\tag{O.2j.11}\n$$\nwith all admitted compensations and all remaining phase dependence retained. A source satisfying that identity on the represented-law domain receives the mathematical classifier of Theorem O.2f. The present explicit comparison and optional load do not themselves prove that identity or select one phase-adaptation law. $\\square$\n\n### O.4a Wrapped-quadratic laws: a complete cycle-lattice classification\n\nTheorem O.2h classifies the wrapped-quadratic law by edge lifts. Theorem O.2k states the same classification in cycle-lattice form, indexing the minima by integer holonomy vectors; Theorem O.2l extends that form to rational geodesic powers.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o2k-all-weighted-wrapped-quadratic-graphs\"></a>**Theorem O.2k (All weighted wrapped-quadratic graphs).** Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite oriented graph, let $w_e>0$, and put $a_e=\\theta_e/(2\\pi)$ and $x_v=\\phi_v/(2\\pi)$. Consider\n$$\n F(x)=\\frac12\\sum_{e\\in E}w_e\\,d\\bigl((B^{\\mathsf T}x)_e-a_e,\\mathbb Z\\bigr)^2,\n \\qquad d(t,\\mathbb Z)=\\min_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}|t-k|.\n \\tag{O.2k.1}\n$$\nThus the edge law in (O.2b.1) is $U_e(t)=\\frac12d(t/(2\\pi),\\mathbb Z)^2$. Identify vertex phases under integer shifts and a common real shift on each connected component. Remove loops first and add their constant contribution $F_{\\rm loop}=\\frac12\\sum_{e:\\,\\mathrm{loop}}w_ed(a_e,\\mathbb Z)^2$ to every reported energy; all the matrices below refer to the remaining graph. Parallel edges are allowed.\n\nChoose a spanning forest. Let $C\\in\\mathbb Z^{|E|\\times r}$ have as columns the oriented fundamental cycles, each with coefficient $1$ on its own nonforest edge and $0$ on the other nonforest edges, where $r=|E|-|V|+c$ and $c$ is the number of connected components. Set\n$$\n W=\\operatorname{diag}(w_e),\\qquad\n H=C^{\\mathsf T}W^{-1}C,\\qquad h=C^{\\mathsf T}a.\n \\tag{O.2k.2}\n$$\nFor $r>0$, $H$ is positive definite. For each $n\\in\\mathbb Z^r$ define\n$$\n J_n=H^{-1}(n-h),\\qquad\n \\rho_n=W^{-1}CJ_n,\n \\qquad E_n=F_{\\rm loop}+\\frac12(n-h)^{\\mathsf T}H^{-1}(n-h).\n \\tag{O.2k.3}\n$$\nThe complete local-minimum quotient consists of exactly one orbit for every integer vector satisfying\n$$\n                   |(\\rho_n)_e|<\\frac12\\quad(e\\in E).\n \\tag{O.2k.4}\n$$\nEvery such minimum is strict on the phase quotient, and its energy is $E_n$. Reconstruct it by setting one root phase to zero in each component and integrating $a+\\rho_n$ along the forest, then reducing vertex phases modulo one. There are no other local minima, including at the nondifferentiable wrap cuts. The global-minimum orbits are exactly the retained vectors attaining the least $E_n$, with all ties retained. For $r=0$, use the single empty vector, $\\rho=0$, and $E=F_{\\rm loop}$.\n\nThe enumeration is finite. It suffices to test\n$$\n \\left|n_j-h_j\\right|<\\frac12\\sum_e|C_{ej}|\n \\qquad(1\\le j\\le r).\n \\tag{O.2k.5}\n$$\nFor exact real-algebraic weights and normalized offsets, (O.2k.2)--(O.2k.5), forest integration and exact comparisons give a terminating classifier using algebraic arithmetic, integer enumeration and sign decisions. For arbitrary real data the same formulas classify the entire family mathematically; an approximation program alone is not an exact sign or equality interface. Different spanning forests, roots or consistent edge orientations relabel the output orbits.\n\n*Proof.* A minimum cannot lie at a nonloop wrap cut. Write $S$ for the nonempty set of edges with residual congruent to $1/2$. For an arbitrary vertex increment $v$, put $b=B^{\\mathsf T}v$. A cut edge has\n$$\n \\frac{w_e}{2}d\\left(\\frac12+t b_e,\\mathbb Z\\right)^2\n =\\frac{w_e}{8}-\\frac{w_e}{2}|t b_e|\n                  +\\frac{w_e}{2}t^2b_e^2\n$$\nfor sufficiently small $t$. The other edges are differentiable. Consequently the two one-sided directional derivatives obey\n$$\n D_v^+F(x)+D_{-v}^+F(x)=-\\sum_{e\\in S}w_e|b_e|.\n \\tag{O.2k.6}\n$$\nAt least one cut edge is a nonloop, so some $v$ has $b_e\\ne0$ there. The right side is then strictly negative, and one direction is downhill. This excludes every cut configuration, even where several cuts intersect or the smooth contributions cancel. A loop has $b_e=0$ for every $v$, which is why it was removed as a constant.\n\nAway from cuts, there is a unique principal residual $\\rho_e\\in(-1/2,1/2)$ with $\\rho=B^{\\mathsf T}x-a-k$ for some integer vector $k$. On this open cell the gradient is $BW\\rho$, and the Hessian is $BWB^{\\mathsf T}$. The Hessian is positive definite after removing one constant direction per component, because $v^{\\mathsf T}BWB^{\\mathsf T}v=\\sum_e w_e(B^{\\mathsf T}v)_e^2$, with equality exactly for componentwise constants. A configuration in the cell is therefore a local minimum exactly when $BW\\rho=0$, and every such minimum is strict on the quotient.\n\nThe columns of $C$ form a real basis of $\\ker B$. Stationarity gives $W\\rho=CJ$. Also\n$$\n C^{\\mathsf T}\\rho=-C^{\\mathsf T}a-C^{\\mathsf T}k=n-h,\n \\qquad n=-C^{\\mathsf T}k\\in\\mathbb Z^r.\n$$\nThus $HJ=n-h$ and (O.2k.3)--(O.2k.4) follow. Conversely, for any integer $n$ passing (O.2k.4), the vector $a+\\rho_n$ has integral fundamental-cycle sums. Integrating it on the forest therefore satisfies every other edge modulo one: the discrepancy on that edge is its fundamental-cycle sum, an integer. This gives a phase orbit with the stated principal residual. It is stationary and strictly minimal by the positive Hessian. Two integrated phases with the same residual differ by integer vertex shifts and component constants. Since the principal residual is unique off the cuts, different retained $n$ cannot represent the same orbit.\n\nThe energy identity follows from $\\rho_n^{\\mathsf T}W\\rho_n=J_n^{\\mathsf T}HJ_n$. The strict inequalities in (O.2k.5) follow by applying $C^{\\mathsf T}$ to (O.2k.4). There are finitely many integers in those bounded intervals. The phase quotient is compact and $F$ is continuous, so at least one global minimum exists. The cut argument and stationarity show that it occurs in the retained finite list. Exact algebraic input makes matrix inversion, interval endpoints, strict tests and energy comparisons decidable. The proof is intrinsic to the phase quotient, establishing independence of the chosen coordinates. ∎\n\n**Overlapping-cycle witness.** Take the five unit-weight edges\n$$\n 0\\to1,\\quad1\\to2,\\quad2\\to0,\\quad1\\to3,\\quad3\\to0\n$$\nwith zero offsets. The two triangles share their first edge, so this graph is not a cactus. With\n$$\n C=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&0\\\\1&0\\\\0&1\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix},\n \\qquad H=\\begin{pmatrix}3&1\\\\1&3\\end{pmatrix},\n$$\n(O.2k.5) restricts $(n_1,n_2)$ to $\\{-1,0,1\\}^2$. Exactly five vectors survive (O.2k.4): $(0,0)$, $(\\pm1,0)$ and $(0,\\pm1)$. The zero vector gives the unique synchronized global minimum with energy zero. The four others are strict nonsynchronized local minima with energy $3/16$. For $(1,0)$,\n$$\n \\rho=(1/4,3/8,3/8,-1/8,-1/8),\\qquad\n (x_0,x_1,x_2,x_3)=(0,1/4,5/8,1/8).\n \\tag{O.2k.7}\n$$\nEach of the other four integer candidates has at least one residual of absolute value $1/2$ and is rejected by the cut proof, not by a positive-Hessian test applied on only one side.\n\n**Why this is outside O.2f--O.2g.** The periodic function $d(t,\\mathbb Z)^2/2$ has unequal one-sided derivatives at $t=1/2$, so it is not a finite Fourier polynomial. Suppose it were semialgebraic as a function of $(\\cos(2\\pi t),\\sin(2\\pi t))$. On $0<t<1/2$, its value is $t^2/2$. Taking the positive algebraic square root and projecting off the sine coordinate would make the graph of $t\\mapsto\\cos(2\\pi t)$ on that interval semialgebraic. A one-dimensional semialgebraic graph lies in the zero set of a nonzero polynomial: in a finite sign-formula description, a point where all defining nonzero polynomials are nonzero has an open sign neighborhood, which cannot lie in a graph; the product of those polynomials therefore vanishes on the graph. Hence some nonzero $P$ would satisfy $P(t,\\cos(2\\pi t))=0$ on the interval. The identity theorem extends this to complex $t$. Writing $P(t,z)=\\sum_{j=0}^m p_j(t)z^j$ with $p_m\\ne0$ and substituting $t=iR$, the term $2^{-m}p_m(iR)e^{2\\pi mR}$ dominates all lower exponential powers as $R\\to+\\infty$, contradicting the identity. Thus this complete weighted multicycle class was not classified by the circle-semialgebraic compilation.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o2l-edge-dependent-rational-geodesic-powers\"></a>**Theorem O.2l (Edge-dependent rational geodesic powers).** The complete finite classification extends to\n$$\n F_p(x)=\\sum_e\\frac{w_e}{p_e}\n d\\bigl((B^{\\mathsf T}x)_e-a_e,\\mathbb Z\\bigr)^{p_e},\n \\qquad p_e\\in\\mathbb Q,\\quad p_e>1.\n \\tag{O.2l.1}\n$$\nKeep the graph, phase quotient, loop preprocessing, fundamental-cycle matrix $C$, holonomy $h=C^{\\mathsf T}a$ and finite integer box (O.2k.5). Put $q_e=p_e/(p_e-1)$. For each integer vector $n$ in that box, the function\n$$\n \\Psi_n(J)=\\sum_e\\frac{w_e^{-1/(p_e-1)}}{q_e}\n                    |(CJ)_e|^{q_e}-(n-h)^{\\mathsf T}J\n \\tag{O.2l.2}\n$$\nhas a unique stationary point $J_n$. Define\n$$\n (\\rho_n)_e=\\operatorname{sgn}((CJ_n)_e)\n       \\left(\\frac{|(CJ_n)_e|}{w_e}\\right)^{1/(p_e-1)},\n \\quad\\operatorname{sgn}(0)=0.\n \\tag{O.2l.3}\n$$\nRetain exactly the vectors with $|(\\rho_n)_e|<1/2$ on every nonloop edge. Each gives one strict local-minimum orbit by forest integration of $a+\\rho_n$, and this exhausts the local minima. The global minima are exactly the retained orbits of least energy\n$$\n F_{p,\\rm loop}+\\sum_e\\frac{w_e}{p_e}|(\\rho_n)_e|^{p_e}.\n \\tag{O.2l.4}\n$$\nFor a forest use the unique empty circulation and $\\rho=0$.\n\nFor algebraic weights and normalized offsets this is an exact terminating classifier, not an optimization oracle. Write $p_e=m_e/k_e$ with integers $m_e>k_e\\ge1$. The unique solution $(J,\\rho,t)$ for a given $n$ is defined by $C^{\\mathsf T}\\rho=n-h$, $t_e\\ge0$ and the following explicit polynomial alternative on every edge:\n$$\n \\begin{split}\n &\\rho_e=t_e^{k_e},\\quad (CJ)_e=w_et_e^{m_e-k_e},\\\\\n &\\hspace{12mm}\\text{or}\\\\\n &\\rho_e=-t_e^{k_e},\\quad (CJ)_e=-w_et_e^{m_e-k_e}.\n \\end{split}\n \\tag{O.2l.5}\n$$\nApply the complete algebraic decomposition primitive of Theorem O.2f to this finite polynomial system, then to the strict residual and energy comparisons. The system has exactly one real tuple, so its coordinates are algebraic and can be isolated; its energy is $F_{p,\\rm loop}+\\sum_e(w_e/p_e)t_e^{m_e}$. All sign branches and zeros are retained. Thus a finite integer enumeration and exact algebraic solves produce every phase orbit and every equality tie.\n\n*Proof.* At a wrap cut the sum of opposite one-sided directional derivatives is\n$$\n D_v^+F_p+D_{-v}^+F_p\n       =-2\\sum_{e\\in S}w_e(1/2)^{p_e-1}\n                              |(B^{\\mathsf T}v)_e|.\n$$\nAs in Theorem O.2k, some direction makes this strictly negative, excluding every nonloop cut. Within an open lift cell, each $|\\rho_e|^{p_e}/p_e$ is continuously differentiable and strictly convex. The incidence map is injective after quotienting component constants, so their positive weighted sum is strictly convex in the quotient coordinates. Stationarity is\n$$\n B s=0,\\qquad s_e=w_e\\operatorname{sgn}(\\rho_e)|\\rho_e|^{p_e-1}.\n$$\nWriting $s=CJ$ gives (O.2l.3), and integer holonomy gives $C^{\\mathsf T}\\rho=n-h$. These are exactly $\\nabla\\Psi_n(J)=0$. Every $q_e>1$, and $C$ is injective, so the sum in (O.2l.2) is strictly convex and grows faster than linearly in every direction, uniformly on the unit sphere. Subtracting a linear form preserves strict convexity and coercivity. Hence it has exactly one minimizer and stationary point. This proves existence and uniqueness of the displayed algebraic tuple. Its retained residual gives a strict local minimum by the convex-cell argument; every local minimum has already been reduced to such a tuple. The forest integration, finite box, compactness and global energy comparison arguments of Theorem O.2k apply unchanged. Rational powers have the exact positive-root encoding (O.2l.5), so complete real algebraic elimination and root isolation terminate on the stated input. ∎\n\nFor every edge law in (O.2l.1), the one-sided derivatives at a wrap cut differ, excluding a finite Fourier representation. On an open half-turn arc the inverse algebraic relation $t=(p_eU_e)^{1/p_e}$ recovers the turn coordinate. The cosine-graph contradiction following Theorem O.2k therefore excludes a semialgebraic circle representation as well. This family includes genuine strict degenerate minima: when every $p_e=4$, every $a_e=0$ and the nonloop graph is connected, synchronization is the unique global-minimum orbit and is strict, although its entire phase Hessian vanishes. The classification uses convexity and the exact algebraic branch system, not a Hessian-only test.\n\n### O.4b A finite predictive-clock source for the wrapped-quadratic law\n\n<a id=\"construction-o2k1-all-graph-dyadic-clock-and-predictive-response-realization\"></a>**Construction O.2k.1 (All-graph dyadic clock and predictive-response realization).** Fix a graph of Theorem O.2k, a clock size $M=2^b$ with $b\\ge1$, a finite number $T$ of predictive rounds, and offset words $A_e\\in\\mathbb Z/M\\mathbb Z$. A vertex carries a $b$-bit cyclic counter $K_v$. The counters advance together by one on each registered tick and carry the retained phases $x_v=K_v/M$; equal advancement preserves every relative phase. At a registered round rate $\\nu>0$, one tick per predictive round gives control period $M/\\nu$. This construction uses a supplied calibrated tick. Its independently adjustable controls are all vertex counter initializations and all edge offset words, not a selection of minimizers.\n\nFor each edge and round compute reversibly\n$$\n q_e=(K_{h(e)}-K_{t(e)}-A_e)\\bmod M,\n \\qquad D_e=\\min(q_e,M-q_e).\n$$\nDraw two fresh independent uniform $b$-bit words $R_e,S_e\\in\\{0,\\ldots,M-1\\}$, and set\n$$\n Z_e=\\mathbf1\\{R_e<D_e\\}\\mathbf1\\{S_e<D_e\\}.\n \\tag{O.2k.8}\n$$\nAll source words are drawn even when $D_e=0$. The draws are independent between edges and rounds. They are supplied by $2b$ fair-bit selectors; no continuously tunable Bernoulli source is assumed. The resulting flip probability is derived by counting source atoms:\n$$\n p_e=\\Pr(Z_e=1)=\\frac{D_e^2}{M^2}\n       =d(x_{h(e)}-x_{t(e)}-A_e/M,\\mathbb Z)^2\\le\\frac14.\n \\tag{O.2k.9}\n$$\nInitialize a source bit $B_e$ by an independent fair draw and give the predictor the initial observation $L_e=B_e$. The source bit and predictor model are distinct registers. In each predictive round commit $P_e=L_e$ before the new source words are available to the predictor; the source then updates $B_e\\leftarrow B_e\\oplus Z_e$ independently of the predictor and writes the target $Y_e=B_e$ in a fresh degenerate task register. Verify the actual equality of retained $P_e$ and $Y_e$, and update the model to $Y_e$. The predictor accesses the source state through these post-commitment observations, not through a pre-commitment source-state port. The commitment, verification and update records remain separate. This is a finite prediction--verification--update loop with accuracy $1-p_e\\ge3/4$ on every admitted phase control. On the same untouched source, a predictor with no model or other record of past observations has accuracy $1/2$, because $Y_e$ is uniform and independent of its permitted pre-commitment data. Removing predictor memory does not change $B_e$ or its transition law. No absolute architecture-complexity minimum is inferred from this comparison.\n\nUse $Y_e$ as the task bit and the committed $P_e$ as the guess in Proposition 20c's two-level work meter, with the same round rate $\\nu$ and edge quench energy $E_e$. Its fixed number of thermalization/lowering strokes is independent of phase. Before thermalization retain the verification and update copy of $Y_e$; the terminal fresh fair meter bit is swapped into a fresh archive register, returning the task register to zero. Every operation outside the meter acts on degenerate registers. For the same registered finite circuit, pad all branches to the same schedule and retain the source words, intermediate histories, consumed labels and terminal meter bits. Register this finite circuit family with the elementary calibrated basis-permutation, fair-selector and supplied-ready-module laws stated in Theorem 23f, together with Proposition 20c's meter. This is a new specified supplied-source family, not an enlargement of the sealed four-assembly census of Theorem 23f. Comparison, modular arithmetic, minimum, conjunction, copies and counters are finite Boolean maps; their reversible implementation sends $(u,0)$ to $(u,f(u))$, retaining $u$, and is extended to a basis permutation. Thus every map used here is within the stated primitives rather than a primitive supplying the desired edge law.\n\nOne explicit register allocation uses $|V|b$ counter bits, $|E|b$ offset bits, and, for each edge, one source bit, one model bit and one reusable task bit. Each edge-round allocates $4b+6$ archive/work bits: the two $b$-bit source words; the $b$-bit residue and distance; and one bit each for $Z$, forecast, verification copy, correctness, old model and terminal meter output. Initialize the source and model by one fair selector and one copy. Supply each correctness register in state $1$, and the remaining work/target registers in state $0$, with those preparations included in the common ready stock. Each edge-round then uses $2b$ fair selectors and ten basis permutations: forecast copy; the combined arithmetic write $(q,D,Z)$; source flip; task copy; verification copy; two XORs into the initially-one correctness bit; copy into the update register; model/update swap; and task/terminal-archive swap. At the swap the update register retains the old model. Incrementing every counter once per round uses $|V|T$ further basis permutations. The explicitly allocated external registers and events are therefore\n$$\n (|V|+|E|)b+3|E|+|E|T(4b+6)\n$$\nregister bits, $|E|(1+2bT)$ fair selectors and $|E|(1+10T)+|V|T$ basis-permutation events. Each supplied module's internal carrier, label and controller stock remains included in its registered charge. The arithmetic permutation is the explicit finite map $z\\mapsto z\\oplus f(u)$ with the three outputs specified in (O.2k.8), so its complete truth table, width and width-dependent supplied-ready price are fixed and charged. This is one supplied finite basis-permutation module, not one unit-price elementary logic gate. The meter adds, per edge-round, its specified control schedule and $N+1$ fresh same-gap Gibbs exchange elements; their preparation and controller stocks are registered separately from their already-owned heat and work exchanges.\n\nLet $\\mathcal C_{b,T,G}$ be the full common non-meter energy stock for this fixed circuit: the initialized registers and immutable ROM; every fair selector and its consumed label; tick source and counter controls; arithmetic, prediction, verification, update and swapping modules; all source/forecast/update/intermediate/meter-output archives; the meter and bath-element preparation/controller stocks just counted; and their fixed-duration ready and maintenance charges. Specify these charges at the supplied-stock boundary before comparing phases. They are phase-independent because the installed modules, maximum register sizes, draws and schedule are identical for every control word. Their values need not vanish. A finite run lasts $T/\\nu$, so its common non-meter power is $\\nu\\mathcal C_{b,T,G}/T$. There is no record erasure in this finite-run implementation; the final archive is retained stock, not an omitted reset. Repeated reuse with bounded memory is a different source contract.\n\nWriting $W_{e,N}$ for the phase-independent stroke-(iii) mean work of edge $e$, the inclusive declared finite-run PCE comparison in watts is\n$$\n V_{b,T}(x)=\\frac{\\nu\\mathcal C_{b,T,G}}{T}\n          -\\nu\\sum_e W_{e,N}\n          +\\nu\\sum_e E_e\\frac{D_e^2}{M^2}.\n \\tag{O.2k.10}\n$$\nThe meter's own bath heat and signed work have the owners specified in Proposition 20c and are not charged a second time as non-meter stock. Choose $E_e=w_e/(2\\nu)$ when $w_e$ is expressed in watts. Then\n$$\n             V_{b,T}(x)=C_{b,T}+F(x),\\qquad\n C_{b,T}=\\frac{\\nu\\mathcal C_{b,T,G}}{T}-\\nu\\sum_eW_{e,N}\n \\tag{O.2k.11}\n$$\nThis is an identity of the full compared potentials; it does not require a simultaneous zero-residual phase on a frustrated graph. The phase cost is the paid error event in an actual predictive work cycle, with its probability supplied by the two threshold counts. Equations (O.2k.8)--(O.2k.10) give the complete retained finite response, source and cost map for every grid phase, every dyadic offset, every positive meter weighting and every finite graph, including overlapping cycles.\n\nFor completeness, each source atom consists of the initial source bits, all fair threshold words and the meter's fresh Gibbs draws. The original response is their deterministic chronological image under the displayed maps and Proposition 20c's strokes. Products of the stated independent source laws sum to one. Prefix restriction and deterministic coarse-graining commute with that image, and its actual correctness flag equals $1-Z_e$. The full Boolean-map and source-atom enumeration is a finite verifier for any fixed $b,T,G,N$; no future source word reaches a committed forecast. Final resources include the records and consumed modules just listed. These facts prove the finite response-faithfulness and inventory coverage statements, conditional on the same supplied-ready primitive laws as Theorem 23f.\n\n**Continuum comparison and retained boundary.** The normalized wrapped-quadratic edge function $u(t)=d(t,\\mathbb Z)^2/2$ is $1/2$-Lipschitz for circular distance: the distance-to-integer function is $1$-Lipschitz, and $|s^2-t^2|/2\\le|s-t|/2$ for $s,t\\in[0,1/2]$. Rounding every vertex phase and offset to the nearest $M$-grid point changes each residual by circular distance at most $3/(2M)$. Therefore, with $F_M$ the exact grid value at those rounded inputs,\n$$\n                    |F_M-F|\\le\\frac{3}{4M}\\sum_e w_e.\n \\tag{O.2k.12}\n$$\nWith fixed dyadic offsets and only phases rounded, the bound improves to $\\sum_e w_e/(2M)$. The first bound proves uniform convergence of the calibrated phase-cost responses, convergence of grid minimum energies and that every accumulation point of grid global minimizers is a continuum global minimizer. It does not identify the discrete topology's local minima with those of the continuum or preserve degenerate local minima under arbitrary approximations. Theorem O.2k gives the exact continuum minimum classification independently. The source just constructed is a complete finite-run, finite-grid predictive implementation of the wrapped-quadratic law. In the source of Theorem O.2p the same kind of counters drive the prediction--verification--update cycle itself; the low-noise detailed-balance dynamics of Theorem O.2 keeps its own hypotheses.\n\nThe realization domain is separately all $b,T,G,N$ and controls of Construction O.2k.1, on its registered supplied-ready finite primitive and Proposition 20c's meter branch. Its verifier exhausts the Boolean maps, source products, commitment order and inclusive stock ledger; its falsifiers include an incorrect threshold probability, a future-dependent forecast, an unowned archive/controller cost or a phase-dependent omitted remainder. It gives the exact finite-grid response and finite-run source-cost construction and the explicit uniform phase-cost limit, not exact continuum source realization. Theorem O.2m gives the complete countable extremum classification for every continuous periodic law on every weighted finite multigraph. Construction O.2n extends the finite predictive-source phase-cost response limit to that full law domain. Theorem O.2p supplies the intrinsic-cycle source join. Any autonomous dynamics used to invoke Theorem O.2 retains its separate hypotheses.\n\n<a id=\"o4c-continuous-phase-landscapes\"></a>\n\n### O.4c Exact countable classification of continuous phase landscapes\n\n**Scope and representation.**\n\nFix the entire O.2b domain: a finite oriented multigraph $G=(V,E)$, positive real weights $w_e$, arbitrary real offsets $a_e=\\theta_e/(2\\pi)$, and continuous one-periodic functions $u_e(t)=U_e(2\\pi t)\\ge0$ whose zero set is $\\mathbb Z$. Loops contribute constants. Choose a root in every connected component and set its phase to zero. If $d=|V|-c$, the remaining normalized phases give exactly the quotient $X=\\mathbb T^d$, and\n$$\n F(x)=\\sum_{e\\in E}w_eu_e\\bigl((\\bar B^{\\mathsf T}x)_e-a_e\\bigr)\n$$\nis a continuous function on $X$. Here root rows have been removed from the incidence matrix. The argument retains every edge simultaneously; it imposes no restriction on overlapping cycles, weights, holonomy, differentiability or degeneracy.\n\nA **uniform polygonal name** for $F$ consists of finite rational tables defining continuous periodic tensor-product piecewise multiaffine functions $P_n$ on rational rectangular partitions of $\\mathbb T^d$, and positive rational numbers $\\varepsilon_n\\le2^{-n}$, with\n$$\n                 \\|P_n-F\\|_\\infty\\le\\varepsilon_n.\n                 \\tag{O.2m.1}\n$$\nEvery continuous $F$ has such a name. To prove this, use a sufficiently fine periodic uniform grid that the oscillation of $F$ within each grid cell is at most $2^{-n-2}$, replace its finitely many vertex values by rationals with error at most $2^{-n-2}$, give identified periodic vertices the same rational value, and interpolate by the tensor-product barycentric weights. Those weights are nonnegative and sum to one, so the resulting error is at most $2^{-n-1}$. One may take $\\varepsilon_n=2^{-n}$. Uniform continuity provides the required finite grid at each $n$.\n\nThis existence statement is over **all** continuous laws and real parameters. It supplies no algorithm for deriving a name from an unspecified presentation of a law. An effective uniform name is additional input to an effective stage-by-stage constructor; the abstract family theorem uses the actual name whether or not that infinite sequence is computable. In particular, noncomputable data have not been removed from the quantified mathematical domain. The semantic bounds in (O.2m.1) are part of the name's validity, not something finitely sampled values can certify for every continuous function.\n\n**Finite rational construction on a box.**\n\nLet $Q=\\prod_{i=1}^d[\\alpha_i,\\beta_i]\\subset\\mathbb R^d$ be a nondegenerate rational box. Regard $P_n$ and $F$ as periodic functions on $\\mathbb R^d$. A finite calculation produces rational bounds and a finite union of closed rational boxes $K_{Q,n}$ as follows.\n\n1. Intersect $Q$ with the periodically lifted knot hyperplanes of $P_n$. Only finitely many meet $Q$.\n2. Compute a rational $L_n\\ge0$ such that\n$$\n |P_n(x)-P_n(y)|\\le L_n\\|x-y\\|_\\infty.\n \\tag{O.2m.2}\n$$\nFor each coordinate, on every interpolation cell, bound its partial derivative by the largest absolute difference of the corresponding adjacent vertex values divided by that cell's side length. A multiaffine derivative is a convex combination of those edge slopes. For each coordinate take the maximum over the finitely many periodic cells, then sum these coordinate maxima. The resulting rational number satisfies (O.2m.2), first along coordinate segments within cells, then across their finitely many boundaries, and finally by changing coordinates one at a time.\n3. Refine the rational rectangular partition of $Q$ until every cell has side lengths at most\n$$\n h_n=\\min\\left(2^{-n},\\frac{\\varepsilon_n}{1+L_n}\\right).\n \\tag{O.2m.3}\n$$\nEach resulting closed box $R$ lies in an interpolation cell. On $R$, compute\n$$\n a_{R,n}=\\min_{v\\in\\operatorname{Vert}(R)}P_n(v),\\qquad\n b_{R,n}=\\max_{v\\in\\operatorname{Vert}(R)}P_n(v),\\qquad\n m_{Q,n}=\\min_R a_{R,n}.\n \\tag{O.2m.4}\n$$\nAll quantities are rational. A multiaffine function attains its minimum and maximum on a rectangle at vertices: fix all coordinates but one, move that affine coordinate to an endpoint without increasing, respectively decreasing, the value, and repeat. Thus (O.2m.4) is the actual minimum of $P_n$ on $Q$, obtained by finite vertex enumeration, and $b_{R,n}-a_{R,n}\\le\\varepsilon_n$ by (O.2m.2)--(O.2m.3).\n4. Retain exactly the boxes satisfying\n$$\n a_{R,n}\\le m_{Q,n}+2\\varepsilon_n,\n \\qquad\n K_{Q,n}=\\bigcup_{R\\ \\mathrm{retained}}R.\n \\tag{O.2m.5}\n$$\nDefine\n$$\n C_{Q,N}=\\bigcap_{n=1}^N K_{Q,n},\\quad\n \\ell_{Q,N}=\\max_{n\\le N}(m_{Q,n}-\\varepsilon_n),\\quad\n r_{Q,N}=\\min_{n\\le N}(m_{Q,n}+\\varepsilon_n).\n \\tag{O.2m.6}\n$$\nIntersections of finite unions of closed rational boxes are computed by distributing intersections and taking coordinatewise endpoint maxima and minima. Degenerate intersections and equality boundaries are retained. Therefore every operation through stage $N$ is finite rational arithmetic and comparison. No minimization oracle, equality oracle for the original real law, differential test, real quantifier elimination, or assumed finite number of minima enters this construction.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o2m\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o2m-complete-countable-continuous-law-landscape-classification\"></a>**Theorem O.2m (Complete Countable Continuous-Law Landscape Classification).**\n\nFor every rational box $Q$ above, let\n$$\n A_Q=\\bigcap_{N=1}^\\infty C_{Q,N}.\n \\tag{O.2m.7}\n$$\nThe following statements give all global, local and strict local minima, including all ties and their energy values.\n\n**(a) Global boxes and exact energy.** The sets $C_{Q,N}$ are nonempty compact nested finite unions of rational boxes. The intervals $[\\ell_{Q,N},r_{Q,N}]$ are nonempty and nested, and\n$$\n r_{Q,N}-\\ell_{Q,N}\\le2\\varepsilon_N.\n \\tag{O.2m.8}\n$$\nTheir unique common point is the minimum energy on $Q$. The set $A_Q$ consists exactly of its minimizers. More quantitatively, writing that energy as $m_Q$, every $y\\in K_{Q,n}$ satisfies\n$$\n              0\\le F(y)-m_Q\\le5\\varepsilon_n.\n              \\tag{O.2m.9}\n$$\nThe full global-minimum set on $X$ is $\\pi(A_{[0,1]^d})$, where $\\pi:\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb T^d$ is the quotient map. Its energy is the common point of (O.2m.8) for that box. Boundary representatives are identified by $\\pi$, rather than discarded.\n\n**(b) All local minima.** Let $\\mathcal Q$ be the countable collection of rational boxes with every side length strictly between zero and $1/2$. Then the complete local-minimum set on $X$ is\n$$\n \\mathcal L\n =\\bigcup_{Q\\in\\mathcal Q}\\pi\\bigl(A_Q\\cap\\operatorname{int}Q\\bigr).\n \\tag{O.2m.10}\n$$\nThe energy of every point in a particular summand is the number given by (O.2m.8) for that box. Overlap does not create an incorrect energy assignment: a point in two summands has the same actual value of $F$ in both. The local-minimum set is an $F_\\sigma$ subset of $X$, and the set of local-minimum energy values is at most countable.\n\n**(c) All strict local minima.** Compute the rational sup-norm diameter\n$$\n D_{Q,N}=\\max_{1\\le i\\le d}\n \\left(\\max_{x\\in C_{Q,N}}x_i-\\min_{x\\in C_{Q,N}}x_i\\right)\n \\tag{O.2m.11}\n$$\nfrom the retained box endpoints. The set $A_Q$ is a singleton exactly when\n$$\n \\forall k\\ge1\\ \\exists N\\ge1:\\quad D_{Q,N}<2^{-k}.\n \\tag{O.2m.12}\n$$\nFor each such box, include its unique limit point if it lies in $\\operatorname{int}Q$, and project it by $\\pi$. The union of these points is exactly the strict-local-minimum set. Multiple symbolic box paths ending at the same boundary point count as one spatial point. This rule proves, in particular, that strict local minima of every continuous phase landscape are at most countable. It does not turn the infinite test (O.2m.12) into a finite decision procedure.\n\n**(d) Maxima.** Apply the same finite construction to the name $-P_n$ of $-F$. It produces all global, local and strict local maxima and their energies with reversed signs.\n\n**(e) Exhaustive backtracking.** Nodes at level $N$ are the nonempty boxes obtained by intersecting one retained box from each of levels $1,\\ldots,N$. Join a node to its included intersections with level $N+1$ boxes. Every infinite branch has a unique spatial limit, because its diameter is at most $d^{1/2}2^{-N}$ in Euclidean distance, and that limit belongs to $A_Q$. Every point of $A_Q$ is the limit of at least one branch. Thus the construction represents all continuum families as well as isolated solutions; it never replaces a continuum by finitely many sample representatives.\n\n*Proof.* Compactness gives a point $x_*$ minimizing $F$ on $Q$. From (O.2m.1),\n$$\n                 |m_{Q,n}-m_Q|\\le\\varepsilon_n.\n                 \\tag{O.2m.13}\n$$\nA partition box containing $x_*$ has\n$$\n a_{R,n}\\le P_n(x_*)\\le m_Q+\\varepsilon_n\n                    \\le m_{Q,n}+2\\varepsilon_n,\n$$\nso it is retained. Hence every minimizer belongs to every $K_{Q,n}$ and $C_{Q,N}$, proving nonemptiness and one inclusion in (O.2m.7). If $y\\in K_{Q,n}$, take a retained box containing it. Equations (O.2m.1), (O.2m.4)--(O.2m.5), and its oscillation bound give\n$$\n F(y)\\le P_n(y)+\\varepsilon_n\n \\le a_{R,n}+2\\varepsilon_n\n \\le m_{Q,n}+4\\varepsilon_n\n \\le m_Q+5\\varepsilon_n.\n$$\nThe lower bound in (O.2m.9) is the definition of the minimum, now already attained. If $y$ belongs to (O.2m.7), this inequality for every $n$ and $\\varepsilon_n\\to0$ gives $F(y)=m_Q$. This proves (a), with (O.2m.8) following from (O.2m.13) and the $N$th interval.\n\nFor (b), a point in the interior of $Q$ that minimizes $F$ on $Q$ minimizes on a neighborhood, and $\\pi$ is a local homeomorphism. Conversely, a lift of any local minimum has a sufficiently small open Euclidean neighborhood on which no lower value occurs. Density of rational endpoints supplies $Q\\in\\mathcal Q$ containing that lift in its interior and contained in the neighborhood. It therefore belongs to $A_Q$ by (a). This proves both directions of (O.2m.10). To obtain the $F_\\sigma$ statement, write $\\operatorname{int}Q=\\bigcup_{j\\ge1}Q_j$ with $Q_j=\\prod_i[\\alpha_i+1/j,\\beta_i-1/j]$, treating a box with reversed endpoints as empty. Each $A_Q\\cap Q_j$ is compact, so its image under $\\pi$ is closed in $X$. Equation (O.2m.10) is therefore a countable union of closed sets. Each nonempty summand has the one energy $m_Q$, and the rational boxes are countable, proving the energy assertion.\n\nFor (c), if (O.2m.12) holds, two points of $A_Q$ have distance at most every $2^{-k}$ and are equal. If $A_Q=\\{x_*\\}$ but the diameters do not tend to zero, there are some $\\eta>0$, arbitrarily large $N$, and points $y_N,z_N\\in C_{Q,N}$ with sup-norm distance at least $\\eta$. Compactness of $Q$ gives convergent subsequences. Nestedness puts both limits in every $C_{Q,M}$, hence in $A_Q$, contradicting their positive separation. Thus singleton intersection is equivalent to (O.2m.12). A unique box minimizer in its interior is strict in an interior neighborhood. Conversely, choose a rational box inside the strictness neighborhood of a strict local minimum; that point is its unique minimizer. The countable box family proves countability. Applying (a)--(c) to $-F$ proves (d).\n\nFor (e), nodes along a branch are nonempty compact nested boxes. Their side lengths are bounded by $2^{-N}$ because the level-$N$ partition has that mesh. Compactness and vanishing diameter give a unique common point, which belongs to every $K_{Q,n}$. Conversely, for $x\\in A_Q$ choose at each level a retained box containing $x$; their finite intersections form an infinite branch. All proofs apply unchanged to flat families and infinitely many accumulating minima. $\\square$\n\nFor $d=0$, the quotient is one point. Its energy is the sum of loop constants; it is a global and local minimum and maximum. Strict local extremality is vacuous under the punctured-neighborhood convention. The box construction is unnecessary in that case.\n\nThe construction is invariant under changes of roots, consistent edge reversals with reflected edge law and offset, and the choice of valid approximation name: (O.2m.7)--(O.2m.12) have been proved equal to the intrinsic extremum sets. Vertex labels and distinct equal-energy configurations are retained.\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o2m1\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o2m1-arbitrary-closed-local-minimum-sets\"></a>**Proposition O.2m.1 (Arbitrary Closed Local-Minimum Sets).**\n\nGive $\\mathbb T=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ its geodesic metric $d_{\\mathbb T}$. Let $K\\ne\\varnothing$ be any closed subset of an arc disjoint from zero, and choose\n$$\n 0<\\epsilon<\\operatorname{dist}_{\\mathbb T}(0,K)^2.\n$$\nThen the admitted law\n$$\n u_K(t)=\\min\\left\\{d_{\\mathbb T}(t,0)^2,\n                 \\epsilon+d_{\\mathbb T}(t,K)^2\\right\\}\n \\tag{O.2m.14}\n$$\nhas exact local-minimum set $\\{0\\}\\cup K$, exact strict-local-minimum set $\\{0\\}\\cup\\operatorname{Iso}(K)$, and unique global minimum zero at $0$.\n\n*Proof.* Both squared distances are continuous and nonnegative, and the second branch is strictly positive. Hence zero occurs exactly at $0$. In a neighborhood of $0$ the first branch is strictly smaller and has a strict minimum there. At each $k\\in K$, the first branch is strictly greater than $\\epsilon$ by the displayed assumption. Continuity makes the second branch active in a neighborhood of $k$, where it has minimum value $\\epsilon$. It is strict exactly when $k$ is isolated in $K$.\n\nA squared distance to a nonempty closed set has no local minimum outside that set: take a closest point, move a sufficiently short positive distance along a shortest geodesic toward it, and the distance strictly decreases. If the minimum of two functions has a local minimum at $t$, each branch active at $t$ must also have a local minimum there, since the minimum is everywhere less than or equal to that branch and their values agree at $t$. These observations exclude every point outside $\\{0\\}\\cup K$. $\\square$\n\nTaking $K$ to be a translated/scaled Cantor set shows why a finite list of isolated extrema or finite semialgebraic cell decomposition cannot exhaust the original continuous domain. Taking an arbitrary noncomputable closed $K$ also makes explicit that the abstract family includes noncomputable data. Equation (O.2m.14) is compatible with the countable classification of Theorem O.2m; on the continuous class that classification cannot be replaced by a finite list of extrema.\n\n**Scope of the classification.**\n\nThe result gives an exact all-law, all-weight, all-finite-multigraph **countable** mathematical classification, realized by specified finite rational stages with quantitative outer error and exhaustive backtracking. All stage operations and their comparisons are explicit finite vertex arithmetic, and the displayed countable operations recover every topological extremum set. The finite-output, terminating classifications of O.2f--O.2l remain stronger on their narrower input classes.\n\nConstruction O.2n supplies the finite-source response family for this law domain, and Theorem O.2p supplies the intrinsic-cycle phase map and the inclusive phase-comparison identity (O.2j.11).\n\n<a id=\"o4d-all-law-finite-predictive-sources\"></a>\n\n### O.4d All-law finite predictive sources and their uniform phase-cost response limit\n\n<a id=\"construction-o2n\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"construction-o2n-all-law-finite-predictive-sources-and-uniform-phase-cost-response-limit\"></a>**Construction O.2n (All-Law Finite Predictive Sources and Uniform Phase-Cost Response Limit).**\n\n**Input and finite compilation.**\n\nUse the normalized-turn edge laws $u_e$ and graph of Theorem O.2m. Choose an integer $A_e>4\\|u_e\\|_\\infty$ for each edge. Fix $M=2^b$, $L=2^c$ with $b\\ge1$, $c\\ge2$, a finite number $T$ of predictive rounds, offset words $J_e\\in\\mathbb Z/M\\mathbb Z$, and integer ROM tables\n$$\n h_e:\\{0,\\ldots,M-1\\}\\longrightarrow\\{0,\\ldots,L/4\\}\n$$\nwith\n$$\n h_e(0)=0,\\qquad h_e(k)\\ge1\\ (k\\ne0),\\qquad\n \\left|\\frac{A_eh_e(k)}L-u_e(k/M)\\right|\\le\\frac{A_e}L.\n \\tag{O.2n.1}\n$$\nSuch tables exist for every law and every $M,L$: use zero at $k=0$ and\n$$\n h_e(k)=\\max\\left(1,\\left\\lfloor\\frac{L u_e(k/M)}{A_e}\\right\\rfloor\\right)\n \\quad(k\\ne0).\n$$\nThe strict bound on $A_e$ and $c\\ge2$ ensure the range bound, and either rounding down or replacing a zero by one incurs error at most $A_e/L$.\n\nThe runtime compiler receives these finite integer tables, not an oracle for $u_e$. Positive weights and meter gaps are separately supplied calibrated real parameters on Proposition 20c's formal source branch; the Boolean compiler does not compute their digits from an unspecified real presentation. It does not compute the floor of an unspecified real number or extract infinitely many bits from a real parameter. The validity relation (O.2n.1) belongs to the law-to-table approximation record. Arbitrary laws have such records mathematically; obtaining them uniformly from an unspecified description is a separate input-representation question. An effectively uniformly approximable law with an effective modulus permits construction of tables with a stated slightly enlarged rational rounding allowance without exact equality decisions.\n\n**Finite maps and source atoms.**\n\nEach vertex carries a $b$-bit counter $K_v$, and relative phases are $K_v/M$. Counters advance equally once per predictive round. Store $J_e$ and every $h_e$ entry in fixed ROM. At each edge and round draw a fresh uniform $c$-bit word $R_e$, then reversibly compute\n$$\n q_e=(K_{h(e)}-K_{t(e)}-J_e)\\bmod M,\\qquad\n H_e=h_e(q_e),\\qquad Z_e=\\mathbf1\\{R_e<H_e\\}.\n \\tag{O.2n.2}\n$$\nThe word is drawn on every branch, including $H_e=0$. Counting the $L$ equiprobable atoms gives exactly\n$$\n             p_e=\\Pr(Z_e=1)=h_e(q_e)/L\\le1/4.\n             \\tag{O.2n.3}\n$$\nThis uses fair selectors and a finite reversible integer comparison, rather than a primitive whose transition probability is the desired continuous law.\n\nInitialize independent fair source bits $B_e$ and model bits $D_e=B_e$. In a round, commit the forecast $P_e=D_e$ before the fresh $R_e$ is accessible to the predictor. The source then changes $B_e\\leftarrow B_e\\oplus Z_e$ and writes $Y_e=B_e$ to a fresh degenerate task register. Acquire and compare the actual $P_e,Y_e$; retain their records; update $D_e$ to $Y_e$. Each correctness flag is exactly $1-Z_e$, so the predictor's accuracy is $1-p_e\\ge3/4$. With predictor memory and all past-observation ports removed while leaving the source unchanged, the task bit is uniform and independent of the permitted precommitment data, so accuracy is $1/2$.\n\nThe maps in (O.2n.2), prediction, acquisition, comparison and update are finite Boolean maps. The reversible map $(u,z)\\mapsto(u,z\\oplus f(u))$ retains its input and is a basis permutation on the entire finite register, including padding states. It supplies an explicit finite truth-table implementation. All final old inputs, random words and intermediate values remain owned. Products of the initial fair-bit laws, fresh-word laws and the meter Gibbs laws define the entire source-atom probability measure; the chronological maps define its response. Normalization follows from these product laws, and prefix restriction and deterministic coarse-graining commute with the maps. No future word reaches a committed forecast.\n\n**Inclusive work identity.**\n\nUse Proposition 20c's two-level meter for the pair $(Y_e,P_e)$ at round rate $\\nu>0$, gap\n$$\n                       E_e=\\frac{w_eA_e}{\\nu},\n                       \\tag{O.2n.4}\n$$\nand finite meter stroke count $N\\ge1$. Its delivered mean work is $W_{e,N}-E_ep_e$. Before the task is thermalized, retain the acquisition/verification and update copy. At the end swap the fair terminal task bit into a fresh archive, restoring the task register to zero. No record is erased in this finite run.\n\nRegister this family under the calibrated finite basis-permutation, fair-selector and supplied-ready-module assumptions used in Construction O.2k.1 and Theorem 23f, together with Proposition 20c's meter. The family is newly specified; it does not enlarge the sealed four-assembly census of Theorem 23f. It has a fixed installed circuit, fixed branch-padded schedule, fixed number of fair draws and fixed maximum-width registers at each $b,c,T,G,N$, with all those resources charged at the supplied-stock boundary before phase settings are compared.\n\nAn explicit external allocation has\n$$\n (|V|+|E|)b+|E|Mc+3|E|+|E|T(b+2c+6)\n \\tag{O.2n.5}\n$$\nbits: counters, offsets, ROM entries, three persistent source/model/task bits per edge, and for every edge-round the $c$-bit random word, $b$-bit residue, $c$-bit table value, and six bits for flip, forecast, verification copy, correctness, old model and terminal meter archive. Initialize correctness to one and all other fresh work bits to zero. Source initialization uses one fair selector and one source-to-model copy per edge. Each edge-round uses $c$ fair selectors and ten basis permutations: forecast copy; the combined write of $(q,H,Z)$; source flip; task copy; verification copy; two XORs into correctness; copy to the fresh update register; model/update swap; and task/terminal-archive swap. Counter advancement adds one basis permutation per vertex-round. Thus the supplied events total\n$$\n |E|(1+cT)\\quad\\hbox{fair selectors},\\qquad\n |E|(1+10T)+|V|T\\quad\\hbox{basis permutations}.\n \\tag{O.2n.6}\n$$\nThe combined write is one finite module, priced by its complete width-dependent truth table. Meter controls and $N+1$ fresh same-gap Gibbs exchange elements per edge-round have their own preparation and controller stock. The elementary modules' internal labels, controllers and carriers are included in their supplied prices.\n\nLet $\\mathcal C_{b,c,T,G,N,h}$ be the complete common non-meter energy stock: every ROM and ready preparation, counter/tick source, arithmetic/forecast/acquisition/update module, selector and consumed label, source and final record, bath-element preparation and meter-controller stock, and the fixed-duration holding/maintenance charges. These charges are phase-independent on this registered supplied-stock branch because hardware, installed table, numbers of consumed resources, maximum storage and padded schedule are identical for all phase controls. They need not vanish. The bath heat and signed work already owned by the meter are not charged a second time.\n\nIts full expected finite-run compared potential in watts is therefore\n$$\n \\begin{split}\n V_{M,L,T}(K,J)\n &=\\frac{\\nu\\mathcal C_{b,c,T,G,N,h}}T\n       -\\nu\\sum_e W_{e,N}\n       +\\nu\\sum_eE_e\\frac{h_e(q_e)}L\\\\\n &=C_{M,L,T}+\\sum_e w_e\\frac{A_eh_e(q_e)}L,\n \\end{split}\n \\tag{O.2n.7}\n$$\nwhere the displayed first two terms define $C_{M,L,T}$. This is an exact inclusive expected-potential identity on the registered finite source class, for every grid phase and offset and every finite graph including loops and overlapping cycles. The corresponding samplewise record obeys\n$$\n V_{\\mathrm{record}}=\\frac{\\nu\\mathcal C_{b,c,T,G,N,h}}T\n -\\frac{\\nu}T\\sum_{e,t}W^{(\\mathrm{iii})}_{e,t}\n +\\frac{\\nu}T\\sum_{e,t}E_eZ_{e,t},\n \\tag{O.2n.7a}\n$$\nand its expectation is (O.2n.7). Thus the mean identity and the full retained-record response are both explicit, without identifying a random work record with its mean. All phase-dependent benefit arises from the very same paid prediction-error event. The memory-null comparator leaves the source law unchanged.\n\n**Uniform all-law phase-cost response limit.**\n\nDefine the circular modulus\n$$\n \\omega_e(s)=\\sup\\{|u_e(t)-u_e(t')|:\n                   d_{\\mathbb T}(t,t')\\le s\\}.\n$$\nUniform continuity gives $\\omega_e(s)\\to0$ as $s\\downarrow0$. Round each vertex phase and offset to the nearest $M$-grid point with a fixed tie rule. Each edge residual changes by circular distance at most $3/(2M)$; a loop has a better bound, which is not needed. From (O.2n.1),\n$$\n \\left|V_{M,L,T}-C_{M,L,T}-F(x)\\right|\n \\le\\sum_e w_e\\left(\\frac{A_e}L+\n                    \\omega_e\\left(\\frac{3}{2M}\\right)\\right).\n \\tag{O.2n.8}\n$$\nConsequently every continuous weighted multicycle law is the uniform phase-cost limit of this family of explicit finite predictive sources as $M,L\\to\\infty$. For a supplied effective modulus, (O.2n.8) selects a finite precision at every requested error; for an arbitrary continuous law it remains a valid mathematical convergence theorem, without claiming a uniform computable precision selector.\n\nEquation (O.2n.8) entails convergence of minimum energies and that every accumulation point of grid global minimizers is a global minimizer of $F$. To prove the first statement, compare a continuum minimizer with its rounded control, then compare a grid minimizer with its own continuum coordinates; both inequalities have errors bounded by the right side of (O.2n.8). For the second statement, compactness gives a convergent subsequence, and continuity of $F$ together with those two inequalities makes its limiting value the minimum. Local and strict minima are recovered by Theorem O.2m's exact countable classifier, not by identifying finite-grid topology with continuum topology or assuming local minima persist under uniform perturbation.\n\n**Exact continuum-source boundary.**\n\nThe compiler establishes a **family of finite controlled sources and a uniform phase-cost response limit**. A distinct finite parameter record and finite resource inventory is supplied at each precision. An arbitrary continuous law may require a noncomputable infinite sequence of such records. No single finite record is claimed to encode or produce that sequence.\n\nThe full source cost $C_{M,L,T}$ is charged at each stage. It generally grows with the ROM and archived records, and (O.2n.8) compares the phase-dependent response after subtracting that owned constant. A sequence of finite identities (O.2n.7) is distinct from one physically realized exact continuum identity (O.2j.11).\n\nThe counter labels here are controlled phase coordinates. Theorem O.2p identifies them with the intrinsic prediction--verification--update cycle phase by letting the same counters trigger P, V and U. Equal externally clocked counter advancement supplies no autonomous frequency entrainment, phase-adaptation rule, ergodicity, detailed balance, or low-noise dynamics. A finite run retains spent records rather than providing a finite-memory infinite-time reset process.\n\nConstruction O.2n supplies every finite integer-table source in its declared family, with exact expected and samplewise paid-error ledgers, complete fair-source maps and inventory, and a uniform phase-cost response limit covering every continuous law. Its input includes each actual finite ROM and source calibration; no law-evaluation oracle is used at runtime. The full stage stock is charged and may grow with precision. Verifier: threshold-atom counting, forecast commitment, reversible map and archive enumeration, the meter work identity and the uniform modulus bound. Falsifier: an incorrect source probability, a future-dependent forecast, a missing phase-dependent cost, a samplewise/mean identification or an unowned stock. Theorem O.2p supplies the intrinsic-cycle, inclusive finite-invoice and continuum phase-comparison join. Any autonomous phase dynamics used to invoke Theorem O.2 retains its independent hypotheses.\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o2o\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o2o-uniform-phase-cost-limits-and-dynamical-pinning\"></a>**Proposition O.2o (Uniform Phase-Cost Limits and Dynamical Pinning).** Uniform convergence of the phase costs in Construction O.2n preserves the minimum-energy comparison stated there. Gradient-flow convergence has a stronger continuity requirement. The following computable analytic family separates the two, including on a connected five-vertex graph with overlapping cycles.\n\nLet $G=K_{p,q}$, where $p,q\\ge2$, orient every edge from the $p$-vertex part to the $q$-vertex part, set every offset to zero and give every edge the same weight $w>0$. For integers $N\\in4\\mathbb N$ define\n$$\n U(s)=1-\\cos s,\\qquad\n U_N(s)=1-\\cos s+N^{-1/2}(1-\\cos Ns),\\qquad\n V_N(\\phi)=w\\sum_{e\\in E}U_N((B^{\\mathsf T}\\phi)_e).\n \\tag{O.2o.1}\n$$\nWrite $V$ for the same graph cost with law $U$. These laws are nonnegative, analytic and $2\\pi$-periodic, with zero set exactly $2\\pi\\mathbb Z$, and\n$$\n \\|U_N-U\\|_\\infty=2N^{-1/2},\\qquad\n \\|V_N-V\\|_\\infty=2pq wN^{-1/2}.\n \\tag{O.2o.2}\n$$\nThey have explicit finite-Fourier descriptions with algebraic coefficients when $w$ is algebraic.\n\nUse the same Euclidean gradient-flow convention and the same initial phases for every $N$: every vertex in the first part starts at $0$, and every vertex in the second part starts at $\\pi/2$. The two parts remain phase-uniform. Denote their phases by $\\alpha_N,\\beta_N$, their lag by $\\delta_N=\\beta_N-\\alpha_N$, and put $\\kappa=(p+q)w$. There is a unique number\n$$\n \\xi_N\\in\\left(\\frac\\pi2-\\frac\\pi{2N},\\frac\\pi2\\right),\\qquad\n \\sin\\xi_N+\\sqrt N\\sin(N\\xi_N)=0.\n \\tag{O.2o.3}\n$$\nThe exact lag trajectory is the unique solution in $(\\xi_N,\\pi/2]$ of\n$$\n \\kappa t=\\int_{\\delta_N(t)}^{\\pi/2}\n       \\frac{ds}{\\sin s+\\sqrt N\\sin Ns},\n \\qquad t\\ge0.\n \\tag{O.2o.4}\n$$\nIt decreases to $\\xi_N$ and obeys the uniform-in-time bound\n$$\n 0\\le\\frac\\pi2-\\delta_N(t)\n \\le\\frac{\\arcsin(N^{-1/2})}{N}.\n \\tag{O.2o.5}\n$$\nThe complete phase trajectory is\n$$\n \\alpha_N(t)=\\frac{q}{p+q}\\left(\\frac\\pi2-\\delta_N(t)\\right),\\qquad\n \\beta_N(t)=\\frac{q\\pi/2+p\\delta_N(t)}{p+q}.\n \\tag{O.2o.6}\n$$\nThus these flows converge uniformly for all $t\\ge0$ to their constant initial configuration. Each finite-$N$ limiting configuration is a strict local minimum modulo common rotation.\n\nFor the limiting cost $V$, the trajectory from that same initial configuration has\n$$\n \\delta(t)=2\\arctan(e^{-\\kappa t}),\\qquad\n V(\\phi(t))=pq w\\,[1-\\tanh(\\kappa t)].\n \\tag{O.2o.7}\n$$\nIn contrast, $V_N(\\phi_N(t))\\to pq w$ uniformly for $t\\ge0$. Hence the uniformly convergent costs and identical initial conditions give a limiting trajectory different from the gradient trajectory of the limiting cost, even though every finite law and the limit are analytic and admitted. The graph $K_{2,3}$ already has cycle rank two and supplies the five-vertex instance.\n\n*Proof.* The nonnegative first term in $U_N$ vanishes exactly on $2\\pi\\mathbb Z$, where the second also vanishes. The difference is bounded by $2/\\sqrt N$ and attains that value at $s=\\pi/N$. Giving all first-part vertices phase zero and all second-part vertices phase $\\pi/N$ attains the graph bound in (O.2o.2).\n\nPermutation symmetry within either part and uniqueness for the smooth vector field preserve the two-part configuration. Direct differentiation of $V_N$ gives\n$$\n \\dot\\alpha_N=qwU_N'(\\delta_N),\\qquad\n \\dot\\beta_N=-pwU_N'(\\delta_N),\\qquad\n \\dot\\delta_N=-\\kappa[\\sin\\delta_N+\\sqrt N\\sin(N\\delta_N)].\n \\tag{O.2o.8}\n$$\nThe conserved sum $p\\alpha_N+q\\beta_N=q\\pi/2$ gives (O.2o.6). No root-pinned metric is substituted for the full vertex gradient.\n\nPut $a_N=\\pi/2-\\pi/(2N)$. Since $N$ is divisible by four, $U_N'(a_N)=\\cos(\\pi/(2N))-\\sqrt N<0$ and $U_N'(\\pi/2)=1$. On $[a_N,\\pi/2]$,\n$$\n U_N''(s)=\\cos s+N^{3/2}\\cos Ns>0.\n \\tag{O.2o.9}\n$$\nIndeed $\\cos s>0$ except at the right endpoint, and $\\cos Ns\\ge0$ on this interval and equals one at that endpoint. Strict monotonicity and the intermediate value theorem prove (O.2o.3). The vector field is negative on $(\\xi_N,\\pi/2]$. Uniqueness prevents a solution from crossing its equilibrium $\\xi_N$. A decreasing solution has a limit; if that limit exceeded $\\xi_N$, its derivative would stay bounded above by a negative number, a contradiction. Near $\\xi_N$, the denominator in (O.2o.4) has a simple zero with positive derivative, so the integral diverges logarithmically at its lower endpoint. Separation of variables therefore supplies the unique trajectory for all $t\\ge0$.\n\nWrite $d_N=\\pi/2-\\xi_N\\in(0,\\pi/(2N))$. The root equation is\n$$\n \\sqrt N\\sin(Nd_N)=\\cos d_N\\le1.\n$$\nSine is increasing on $[0,\\pi/2]$, which proves $d_N\\le N^{-1}\\arcsin(N^{-1/2})$ and (O.2o.5). At the limiting configuration every edge has lag $\\xi_N$ and positive curvature $wU_N''(\\xi_N)$. Its Hessian is that positive number times the graph Laplacian. Connectedness makes the Hessian positive definite transverse to common rotation, proving strict local minimality.\n\nFor $U$, equation (O.2o.8) reduces to $\\dot\\delta=-\\kappa\\sin\\delta$. Differentiating the displayed $\\delta(t)$ verifies both the equation and its initial value; $\\cos\\delta(t)=\\tanh(\\kappa t)$ proves (O.2o.7). Since $U$ is $1$-Lipschitz and every perturbed lag satisfies (O.2o.5),\n$$\n \\sup_{t\\ge0}|V_N(\\phi_N(t))-pq w|\n \\le pq w\\left[\\frac{\\arcsin(N^{-1/2})}{N}\n                         +\\frac2{\\sqrt N}\\right]\\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{O.2o.10}\n$$\nFinally $K_{2,3}$ has six edges, five vertices and one component, so its cycle rank is $6-5+1=2$. $\\square$\n\n**Quantitative dynamics transfer.** On any fixed finite weighted graph, retain the incidence matrix, offsets, full vertex phases and the same clock/mobility convention. Suppose $U_e$ are $C^1$ periodic laws with globally Lipschitz derivatives of constants $L_e$, and $U_{e,n}$ are $C^2$ periodic laws with\n$$\n \\eta_n=\\sup_{\\phi\\in\\mathbb R^{|V|}}\n \\left\\|B\\operatorname{diag}(w_e)\n \\bigl(U_{e,n}'((B^{\\mathsf T}\\phi)_e-\\theta_e)\n              -U_e'((B^{\\mathsf T}\\phi)_e-\\theta_e)\\bigr)_{e\\in E}\n \\right\\|_2\\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{O.2o.11}\n$$\nLet $L=\\|B\\|_2\\|\\operatorname{diag}(w_eL_e)\\|_2\\|B^{\\mathsf T}\\|_2$. For full lifted solutions with initial distance $d_n$, their distances at time $t\\ge0$ satisfy\n$$\n \\|\\phi_n(t)-\\phi(t)\\|_2\n \\le e^{Lt}d_n+\\eta_n\\frac{e^{Lt}-1}{L},\n \\tag{O.2o.12}\n$$\nwith the last quotient interpreted as $t$ when $L=0$. Thus uniform convergence of the actual drifts, together with convergent initial data, transfers the phase trajectories on every fixed time interval. Supplied derivative-error and Lipschitz bounds make this a finite-precision bound on the stated mathematical dynamics.\n\n*Proof.* The limiting drift $b(\\phi)=-B\\operatorname{diag}(w_e)(U_e'((B^{\\mathsf T}\\phi)_e-\\theta_e))_e$ is $L$-Lipschitz by successive application of the displayed matrix norms. The periodic smooth approximating drifts and the Lipschitz limiting drift have unique global solutions; they are bounded. Their integral equations give\n$$\n \\|\\phi_n(t)-\\phi(t)\\|_2\n \\le d_n+\\eta_nt+L\\int_0^t\\|\\phi_n(s)-\\phi(s)\\|_2\\,ds.\n$$\nMultiplication by the integrating factor, or iteration of this integral inequality and summation of its exponential series, gives (O.2o.12). In the witness (O.2o.1), $\\|U_N'-U'\\|_\\infty=\\sqrt N$ and $\\eta_N=w\\sqrt{pq(p+q)N}$. The rowwise triangle bound gives this upper bound, and a configuration with every edge lag $\\pi/(2N)$ attains it. Thus the required drift convergence fails explicitly. $\\square$\n\nConstruction O.2n supplies uniform phase-cost responses; (O.2o.3)--(O.2o.10) show why extending that response limit to a phase-adaptation law requires a separate source-dynamics record. On a differentiable source branch, (O.2o.11)--(O.2o.12) give a sufficient quantitative test once its actual drift, mobility and clock are identified. The original continuous-law class includes laws without a classical derivative, for which a dynamics prescription is additional source data. Theorem O.2p supplies the same-source intrinsic predictive-cycle map and inclusive graded-source phase-comparison identity.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o2p\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o2p-intrinsic-cycle-realization-of-the-full-continuous-phase-comparison-class\"></a>**Theorem O.2p (Intrinsic-Cycle Realization of the Full Continuous Phase-Comparison Class).**\n\nThis joins the classifier of Theorem O.2m and the finite integer-table compiler of Construction O.2n to an actual same-source predictive cycle. The comparison is at a frozen source grade and nonphase state, as in the phase-independent remainder premise of Theorem O.2.\n\n#### 1. Inputs, native source and exact quantifiers\n\nFix a finite directed multigraph, including loops and overlapping cycles, positive weights $w_{e}$, offsets $\\theta_{e}$ and admitted continuous nonnegative normalized-turn laws $u_{e}$, with the zero/minimum normalization of Theorem O.2b. At grade $m$ supply O.2n's actual finite integers $M=2^b\\ge 4$, $L=2^c$, amplitudes $A_e>4\\lVert u_e\\rVert$, and valid ROMs $h_{e}$ with\n\n$$\n0\\le h_e(k)\\le L/4,\\quad h_e(0)=0,\\quad\\text{and}\\quad|A_e h_e(k)/L-u_e(k/M)|\\le A_e/L.\n$$\n\nUse its calibrated finite supplied-ready stock, fair-bit preparation, finite basis-permutation, clock/control and full-support holding tariff, together with Proposition 20c's Gibbs-swap meter. The finite descriptor contains every support, table, native price, positive duration, control waveform, bath state, retention interval and response port below. Every finite resource and requested control norm is paid; no refinement-uniform power bound is asserted. A supplier with a conflicting minimum latency is not substituted for this admitted ideal finite service.\n\nThe source quantifiers are: every semantic continuous law admits a valid mathematical sequence of such finite tables; every supplied valid finite table has the explicit finite implementation below. An effective law name/modulus gives effective table/precision production. No arbitrary noncomputable infinite programme, inaccessible law oracle, unknown-state copier or physical infinite tape is supplied.\n\nFix a physical predictive period $\\tau>0$. For $T\\ge 1$ let $H=(T+2)\\tau$, $S=(T+2)M$, $\\delta=\\tau/M$, and $\\nu_{\\mathrm{eff}}=T/H$. The actual predictive cycle rate is $1/\\tau$, distinct from its finite-run average rate $\\nu_{\\mathrm{eff}}$. Choose meter gaps $E_e=w_eA_e/\\nu_{\\mathrm{eff}}$. Register $\\tau_{\\mathrm{medium}}=\\tau$ without adopting the optional convention $\\tau_{\\mathrm{medium}}=\\hbar/E_{\\mathrm{exc}}$; retain actual clock and control energies separately.\n\n#### 2. Source tasks and intrinsic clocks\n\nEvery vertex $v$ has a $b$-bit counter $K_{v}$ and a retained controller history. Let $d=\\lceil\\log_2(T+1)\\rceil$; each controller word consists of a started flag and a $d$-bit completed-cycle count $r$. There are $S+1$ fresh words per vertex, initially $(0,0)$. The local sectors $P,V,U$ are residues $0,3M/4,M/2$. A node starts at its first $P$ after time zero, executes exactly $T$ cycles and then becomes inactive. At each tick all operations use the OLD controller word, with the first $P$ enabled by its starting condition; only after the event packet is complete is a fresh next word written. It sets started at that first $P$, increments $r$ at each enabled $U$, and saturates at $T$. Old words are never erased.\n\nEach incoming edge has its own independent task $B_{e}$, model $D_{e}$ and degenerate meter/task carrier $Y_{e}$. Initially $B_{e}$ is fair and $D_e=B_e$. At the head node's $P$, copy $D_{e}$ to the fresh forecast $P_{e}$. At $V$, release the previously inaccessible independent uniform $c$-bit selector $R_{e}$, and reversibly write\n\n$$\nq_e=(K_{\\mathrm{head}}-K_{\\mathrm{tail}}-J_e) \\bmod M,\\quad H_e=h_e(q_e),\\quad Z_e=1\\{R_e<H_e\\}.\n$$\n\nThen flip $B_{e}$ by $Z_{e}$, copy $B_{e}$ to $Y_{e}$, retain verification/correctness and the fresh model-update copy, execute the meter below, and swap the terminal fair carrier into its fresh archive. At U swap the preserved update copy into $D_{e}$, retaining the old model. These are precisely O.2n's ten Boolean maps, now assigned to actual P,V,U sectors rather than one unrelated predictive round. Independent edge banks prevent asynchronous neighboring tasks from mutating one another's source/model data.\n\nEvery vertex with zero indegree receives one private task of the same form, but Z is the AND of two fresh fair bits ($p=1/4$), with no graph ROM or meter. It has the same P,V,U record structure and a terminal task archive. Thus every vertex clock drives an actual predictive task, including outgoing-only and isolated vertices. All its costs enter the common remainder.\n\nAll task packets at a tick finish before any vertex counter shifts. Then write all next controller words, decrement every $K_{v}$ by one, and update the global cursor. Consequently $q_{e}$ remains constant and the very counters used in edge laws trigger each node's actual $P,V,U$ cycle. One cycle takes $M\\ \\text{ticks}=\\tau$. There is no passive tensor clock and no exact continuous phase eigenstate.\n\nThe predictor's admitted precommitment ports exclude unreleased selectors and current hidden $B$ except through retained past model data. Preallocated future selectors are physically held behind that paid access boundary until local $V$. Product fair-selector/Gibbs laws and the actual reversible chronological maps define the complete normalized source law, including all unused/padded and zero-probability outcomes. Forecasts precede newly accessible randomness. Accuracy is $1-h_e(q_e)/L\\ge 3/4$, while the memory-null source-preserving comparator is $1/2$. Private tasks have accuracy $3/4$ and the same null score.\n\n#### 3. Finite positive controls and the meter\n\nFor a finite unitary $U$ on a declared support, a fresh degenerate tag starts in $|0\\rangle$. Set $W=U\\otimes|1\\rangle\\langle 0|+U^*\\otimes|0\\rangle\\langle 1|$. Then $W=W^*$, $W^{2}=I$. The positive pulse $H_c(t)=\\hbar g(t)(I-W)$, $g\\ge 0$, $\\int g\\,dt=\\pi/2$ implements $U$ and flips the tag, up to global phase. Initially $\\langle W\\rangle=0$ for every data state; under this pulse it remains zero. Thus its mean control action is exactly $\\pi\\hbar/2$ and integrated norm $\\pi\\hbar$, including controlled identity branches. Tags remain retained. Computational registers and counters have zero bare baseline. Nondegenerate uses below commute with their specified bare Hamiltonian, so the same argument holds in the interaction picture.\n\nAfter retaining original $Y_e$, $P_e$, correctness and update data, a paid controlled $X$ changes the degenerate carrier occupancy to $Y_{e}\\oplus P_e=Z_e$. Raise physical level 1 to $E_{e}$; exchange with a fresh same-gap Gibbs qubit; then perform $N$ lowering ramps of size $E_e/N$, each followed by a fresh matching-gap Gibbs exchange, including the final zero-gap exchange. During a ramp the Hamiltonian is diagonal and the bath is disconnected, so occupancy is constant and work equals occupancy times endpoint gap change even for positive duration. Each exact SWAP commutes with the equal-gap bare Hamiltonian. Before each lowering ramp copy the occupancy to a fresh work-record bit; this controlled copy also commutes with bare energy.\n\nThus there are N+1 fresh bath qubits, N work bits, one forecast-frame packet, N work-copy packets and N+1 SWAP packets per edge-cycle. The actual terminal carrier and every spent bath qubit remain archived. The first spent bath can depend on Z; its heat and energy history are not called phase-independent. The original-coordinate terminal label can be recovered by a separately paid XOR if it is included in the nominated response menu; here the retained terminal label is explicitly the meter-frame bit.\n\nSamplewise delivered work is exactly $W_{e,t}=W_{e,t}^{(iii)}-E_eZ_{e,t}$. The mean of $W^{(iii)}$ is the $W_{e,N}$ of Proposition 20c, independent of the prediction/error pair. The full energy histories of ramps and exchanges, including their time-integrated excitation, remain response records. The phase-independent tariff is the inherited supplied-ready/full-support holding and control tariff, not a universal integral-of-bare-excitation tax. A branch adding that different tax must include its actual phase dependence and recalibrate; it is not being claimed here.\n\n#### 4. Complete finite event and stock census\n\nLet $n=|V|$, $e=|E|$, $z=\\#\\text{zero-indegree vertices}$ and $r_{\\mathrm{task}}=e+z$. Let $a=\\lceil\\log_2(S+1)\\rceil$. A degenerate $a$-bit global cursor starts at zero. At each tick copy it to a fresh $a$-bit history bank and increment it modulo $2^a$, using two fixed permutation packets. There are $S$ such history banks. The actual supplied finite drive owns all tick/subwindow times, switch/control programmes, readiness, control energy and retention; the cursor is part of that driven source.\n\nThe fixed per-tick list contains 10 $r_{\\mathrm{task}}$ logical packets; n controller-history writes; n counter shifts; two cursor packets; and, per graph edge, one forecast-frame, N work-copy and N+1 Gibbs-SWAP packets plus N+1 level ramps. All inactive logical/control packets are controlled identities on data but still own their positive tags and installed support. Meter bath addresses are edge/cycle addresses, with an inactive default that does not consume a bath element. At each tick pad the complete list to\n\n$$\nJ=10\\, r_{\\mathrm{task}}+2n+2+e(3N+3)\n$$\n\nsubwindows, each of duration delta/J. Fixed prescribed serialization places P/V/U sector packets first, all counter/controller/cursor packets last. The positive-unitary packet count over the run, including the initial source-to-model copies, is\n\n$$\nQ=S[10\\, r_{\\mathrm{task}}+2n+2+e(2N+2)]+r_{\\mathrm{task}}.\n$$\n\nFor a constant positive pulse of duration $\\delta/J$ its norm is $\\pi\\hbar J/\\delta$; its mean action is $\\pi\\hbar/2$. All table/control norm and holding charges use their actual full support. Ramps have their specified finite diagonal amplitude $\\le E_e$ and their supplied switching/control invoice. No asymptotic thermalization latency is assumed: each thermalization is the displayed finite SWAP.\n\nA conservative external binary/qubit allocation, before the native services' separately enumerated internal stock, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB &= (n+e)b+eMc+3r_{\\mathrm{task}}+eT(b+2c+6)+8zT\\\\\n&\\quad +n(S+1)(d+1)+(S+1)a+Q+eT(2N+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTerms respectively own node/offset counters, graph ROM, persistent task/model/carrier bits, graph logical-cycle records, private-task records, controller histories, cursor/history, pulse tags, and $(N+1)$ bath qubits plus $N$ work bits per edge-cycle. Graph cycle records are O.2n's random/residue/table and six flag/forecast/verification/update/archive bits; private cycles use two selectors plus those six bits. Correctness starts at one; other fresh work starts at zero. There are $r_{\\mathrm{task}}+ecT+2zT$ fair selector preparations and $(N+1)eT$ supplied Gibbs preparations. State/table/phase settings are prepared at the supplied-stock boundary with their fixed full-register preparation prices; no unpriced phase-dependent input generation is hidden.\n\nEach finite Boolean packet is a fully specified reversible truth-table write/permutation, controlled by actual counter and OLD controller/cycle address. A packet on $a_{j}$ bits can be delivered as a full table of $a_j2^{a_j}$ bits or by its actually verified smaller compiler; its full table/support price is paid, not a unit-price giant gate. The finite programme lists every packet, matrix, switch, support address, duration, fresh tag and retention interval. Add its actual delivered programme/ROM and the supplied primitives' explicit internal controllers/carriers/labels to $B$ at their given native prices. $B$ is an external census; supplier internals carry their own native prices.\n\nAll source prices in the following invoice are joules (or explicitly calibrated joule-equivalent native prices). Durations are seconds and H is seconds. Pulse action is separately measured in joule-seconds; it is not added to energy without the supplied conversion/calibration. Native price functions take the actual duration, support and control specification as inputs and return the registered energy charge.\n\nLet $C_{\\mathrm{stock},m}$ be the finite sum over this delivered event/support list: ready/preparation stocks; graph and programme ROM; fair and Gibbs preparation; tagged controls and ramps; native clock/drive/cursor services; all readout/response access services; installed arithmetic/forecast/update/meter controllers; every archive and unused stock; full-horizon holding plus any fixed preparation/terminal custody intervals. All non-meter penalties/propagation terms in the registered family are included as their supplied fixed entries. No reset occurs. Meter heat and signed work have their separate actual ports and are not charged twice. Every count, support, duration and installed price in this sum is fixed before phase settings are compared. Thus $C_{\\mathrm{stock},m}$ is genuinely phase-independent on this inherited tariff, although raw meter occupations/heat are not.\n\n#### 5. Inclusive identity and complete source response\n\nThe explicit D1 assignment is $V_{\\mathrm{op}}=C_{\\mathrm{op}}/H$, $V_{\\mathrm{prop}}=C_{\\mathrm{prop}}/H$ and $V_{\\mathrm{penalty}}=C_{\\mathrm{pen}}/H$, with $C_{\\mathrm{stock}}=C_{\\mathrm{op}}+C_{\\mathrm{prop}}+C_{\\mathrm{pen}}$ the registered fixed supplied entries, and $V_{\\mathrm{benefit}}=\\mathbb{E}[\\sum_{(e,t)}W_{e,t}]/H$ the expected actual delivered meter benefit. Its raw samplewise analogue is $\\sum_{(e,t)}W_{e,t}/H$. Private tasks have no delivered-work benefit in this registration; any separately registered phase-independent private benefit would instead enter the displayed constant once. Each D1 term is in watts.\n\nThe entire T-cycle run finishes within H. With the mandatory delivered-work/verification/archive ports the exact samplewise compared potential is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{\\mathrm{record},m}&=C_{\\mathrm{stock},m}/H-(1/H)\\sum_{e,t}W_{e,t}^{(\\mathrm{iii})}\\\\\n&\\quad+(1/H)\\sum_{e,t}E_e Z_{e,t}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts expectation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_m(K,J) &= C_{\\mathrm{stock},m}/H -\\nu_{\\mathrm{eff}} \\sum_e W_{e,N}\\\\\n&\\quad +\\sum_e w_e A_e h_e(q_e)/L\\\\\n&= C_m+F_m(K,J).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll terms are finite and owned before any comparison. The identity is for the full registered source, not only its scalar score. Removing the load, uncomputing a required record, changing a bath/output port or omitting actual control resources is not complete-response equivalence.\n\n#### 6. One common intrinsic-time/record tower\n\nChoose $T_m=m$, $M_m=L_m=2^{m+2}$, fixed $N$, fixed $\\tau$, and any valid supplied ROM sequence. For $x\\in[0,1)$, choose $K=\\lfloor Mx\\rfloor\\bmod M$; use the same floor rule for offsets. Each circular phase error is $\\le 1/M$. Register $x_m=K(0)/M$ as the finite delay-phase coordinate: the decreasing counter reaches its $P$ sector first at time $\\tau K(0)/M$. Thus the first $P$ time approximates $\\tau x$ within $\\tau/M$ uniformly on $[0,1)$, including the registered seam $x=0$. On a consistent local lift the head-minus-tail temporal lag is $+\\tau(x_{\\mathrm{head}}-x_{\\mathrm{tail}})$, matching the positive delay convention of (O.2b.1). The residual $q/M$ subtracts the registered offset with this same sign; no evenness of $u$ is assumed or used. Phase zero denotes entry into the local $P$ sector, not completion of its serialized forecast-copy packet. The actual finite packet completion offsets are separately recorded and add at most $\\delta$. Subsequent $P,V,U$ timestamps converge with the same bound. At source cuts the actual counter phases approach the translation $x-t/\\tau$ on the circle; within a counter-shift pulse its phase-readout support occupies the two adjacent clock bins, not an exact continuous phase state.\n\nFreeze the common comparison port before taking limits: node/edge/cycle/type-labelled active predictive records, physical event timestamps, work outcomes, spent-bath/heat labels and counter-phase cut records. Bare meter energy/heat/work histories are represented by labelled stroke records containing start/end timestamps, input/output populations, gaps, signed work/heat and the fixed normalized ramp profile. Ramps are linear in their normalized subwindow coordinate. Tagged control amplitudes and actual control energy/action remain exact per-grade records; their peak $\\pi\\hbar J/\\delta$ diverges, so they are not asserted to converge to a finite Hamiltonian history. Only a separately labelled unit-normalized shape (or its explicitly calibrated action measure) may be added to the common response port. This finite-product topology does not assert uniform convergence of shrinking absolute-time energy waveforms or total-variation convergence of time-shifted impulse measures. Labels remain distinct if limiting event times coincide. Arbitrary unlabelled jump-path or all-measurable stopping-time convergence is not inferred. Full raw microtick programmes, arithmetic garbage, unused stock and invoices remain exact separate grade records; their convergence is neither needed nor falsely claimed.\n\nFor every fixed finite logical prefix, couple selectors by $R_{e,m}=\\lfloor L_mU_e\\rfloor$ for common independent uniform $U_{e}$. Their thresholds converge to $u_e(x_{\\mathrm{head}}-x_{\\mathrm{tail}}-\\theta_e)/A_e$, so the $Z$ bits converge almost surely except on probability-zero threshold equalities. Couple initial fair bits identically. Since $\\nu_{\\mathrm{eff},m}$ tends to $1/\\tau$, $E_{e,m}$ tends to $w_eA_e\\tau$; fixed-$N$ Gibbs probabilities and work values are continuous in these gaps. Common independent bath uniforms therefore couple every finite meter/predictive word almost surely, with work/heat values and timestamps converging. For a prefix of $K$ logical cycles, the union-bound mismatch probability is at most $K\\sum_e|p_{e,m}-p_e|$ plus $K\\sum_e\\sum_{j=0}^{N}|g(E_{e,m}(1-j/N))-g(E_{e,\\infty}(1-j/N))|$; private task bits couple exactly. This tends to zero. Conditional on matching labels, timestamps, gaps, work/heat values and the bare-meter normalized stroke-profile parameters converge. Thus bounded continuous tests of the fixed finite labelled response converge, with an explicit modulus obtained from this mismatch bound and the reader modulus of continuity. This proves joint convergence and exact causal prefix compatibility. Raw sampler words may additionally be represented by their actual dyadic intervals/uniform limits if that port is nominated; inaccessible future words never become predictor inputs.\n\nThe uniform phase-cost bound is\n\n$$\n\\epsilon_m=\\sum_e w_e[A_e/L_m+\\omega_e(3/M_m)],\n$$\n\nand tends to zero. Hence, uniformly in x,y,\n\n$$\n\\lvert[V_m(x)-V_m(y)]-[F(x)-F(y)]\\rvert\\le 2\\epsilon_m.\n$$\n\nEach subtraction occurs within one finite source grade and frozen nonphase state. Preserve the entire unquotiented invoice/source tower $m\\to C_m$.\n\nStrict fixed pair comparisons eventually agree. Global-minimum energies after the recorded baseline and accumulation points of grid global minimizers obey O.2n's compactness proof. Exact ties and arbitrary local/strict extrema are classified by O.2m applied to the exact limiting law; they are not transported by uniform convergence alone. A common phase rotation preserves the graph objective, while raw startup-relative timestamp archives transform by the explicit common time translation and cycle/startup relabelling, not by literal equality.\n\n#### 7. Dynamics and the joint realization\n\nThe realized dynamics is the actual paid common decreasing delay-coordinate clock translation with phase-controlled prediction/error/meter cycles and retained finite-prefix laws above. It covers every finite weighted multigraph and every admitted continuous periodic law in the stated formal finite-to-limit sense. No autonomous phase adaptation, entrainment, detailed balance, ergodicity, low-noise stationary law, infinite-time finite-memory reset or gradient-flow convergence is inferred. Any such use of O.2 retains its separate hypotheses; O.2o's dynamical obstruction remains intact.\n\nTogether with Theorem O.2m this supplies the all-law classification and the source-complete intrinsic-cycle phase-comparison realization on the finite-to-limit source interface of §§1--6. The full finite invoices and raw records remain owned; the continuum consumer is the within-grade phase comparison of §6.\n\nPremises: the unchanged calibrated finite supplied-ready, fair-selector, finite-control, Gibbs-swap and full-support holding interface inherited from O.2k.1/O.2n and Proposition 20c; each actual finite table and physical schedule; the explicit causal access boundary; and the nominated response ports. The phase convention is decreasing delay coordinate, with P,V,U sectors 0,3M/4,M/2 and positive head-minus-tail temporal lag; noneven laws are not silently reflected. The characteristic energy-time convention is not imposed on recurrence. Source access distinguishes a semantic law's mathematical table sequence from an effective name and from each actually supplied finite descriptor.\n\nBudget and equivalence: every declared finite grade, full supplied support and retained raw response is charged. Sources are compared at the same frozen grade and nonphase data using their complete delivered work/verification/archive ports. The entire unquotiented invoice tower remains recorded. Convergent comparison differences do not identify different source invoices, create a finite infinite apparatus, or imply a cross-grade/all-hardware cost minimum. Bare meter stroke records have the declared labelled finite-product topology; divergent control amplitudes and raw grade archives are not assigned a false finite limit.\n\nVerifier: reversible finite-table and controller induction; forecast-before-selector causality; exact positive-tag pulse and equal-gap SWAP algebra; full event/support and price census; meter endpoint work and energy-history accounting; the unit-typed D1 identity; common-uniform finite-prefix coupling; timestamp/lag bounds; the uniform modulus bound; and O.2m's exact countable extremum proofs. Falsifiers: an unowned source/clock/resource, an incorrect phase/time sign, a missing phase-dependent invoice contribution, a future-dependent forecast, optional work substituted for the delivered port, samplewise work identified with its expectation, a lost raw record, or a claimed convergence outside the declared response topology.\n\nActual paid clock translation is realized. Autonomous phase adaptation, entrainment, ergodicity, detailed balance, low-noise selection, infinite-time finite-memory reset and gradient-flow convergence remain their separately stated hypotheses; Proposition O.2o's obstruction is unchanged. Exact limiting local minima and ties are supplied by O.2m, not by unjustified transport of grid minima. Construction O.2j and Construction O.2n keep their own scope; Theorem O.2p supplies the intrinsic-cycle and graded-source comparison join. Scope limits: the realization is a tower of finite supplied-ready grades on the tariff of Constructions O.2k.1 and O.2n and the meter of Proposition 20c, with phases and offsets set by prepared counters and offset words and the terms of Definition D.1 assigned from that tariff (fixed stock entries for $V_{op}$, $V_{\\mathrm{prop}}$, $V_{\\mathrm{penalty}}$, delivered meter work for $V_{\\mathrm{benefit}}$); each law enters through supplied finite tables, whose sequence may be noncomputable; the identity (O.2j.11) holds exactly at every grade for the implemented table law, and the continuous law is reached by the within-grade comparison limit with error $2\\epsilon_m$; absolute stage costs may grow with the grade; the realized dynamics is the paid common clock translation, and no autonomous phase adaptation is realized (Proposition O.2o); the classification of Theorem O.2m is countable and has no terminating decision procedure on the continuous class (Propositions O.2g.2, O.2g.3 and O.2m.1).\n\n## O.5 The Physical Origin of the Arrow of Time\n\n**Arrow-of-time boundary for Borchers use.** The retained-algebra arrow fixes which algebraic records are available to an observer before a commitment. It does not itself provide the half-sided modular inclusion, reflected extension, or positivity data required by $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{Borch}}$. Thus Borchers-type reflection is an optional finite certificate layered over the arrow theorem, not an alternate proof of the arrow theorem.\n\nSynchronization supplies coherence, not temporal irreversibility. A preferred statistical direction is obtained only on Principle O.3c0's oriented nonequilibrium Markov branch or on Theorems O.3--O.3a's common forward/reverse event-algebra branch with a positive pathwise entropy-production certificate.\n\n**Principle O.3c0 (Autonomous Cyclic-Update Closure).** A registered coarse-grained Predict–Verify–Update sector admits an autonomous continuous-time Markov description on states $P,V,D$ with strictly positive rates\n$$\nP\\mathop{\\rightleftarrows}^{a}_{\\bar a}V,\n\\qquad\nV\\mathop{\\rightleftarrows}^{b}_{\\bar b}D,\n\\qquad\nD\\mathop{\\rightleftarrows}^{c}_{\\bar c}P.\n$$\nThe operational cycle orientation is the forward orientation and satisfies the testable nonequilibrium inequality\n$$\nabc>\\bar a\\bar b\\bar c.\n\\tag{O.3c0.1}\n$$\n\n**Theorem O.3c0 (Strict Autonomous Arrow and Fluctuation Identity).** Under Principle O.3c0, the unique stationary cycle current and affinity are\n$$\nJ=\n\\frac{abc-\\bar a\\bar b\\bar c}\n{ab+a\\bar b+ac+\\bar a\\bar b+\\bar ac+\\bar a\\bar c+bc+b\\bar c+\\bar b\\bar c},\n\\tag{O.3c0.2}\n$$\n$$\n\\mathcal A=\\ln\\frac{abc}{\\bar a\\bar b\\bar c}>0.\n\\tag{O.3c0.3}\n$$\nThe stationary entropy-production rate is\n$$\n\\dot\\sigma=J\\mathcal A>0.\n\\tag{O.3c0.4}\n$$\nChoose a deterministic observation duration $0<T<\\infty$ and start the chain in its stationary distribution $\\pi$. A path $\\gamma$ on $[0,T]$ records its finite random number $N$ of jumps, the visited states and every holding interval, including the initial and terminal intervals. Let $\\gamma^\\dagger$ reverse that complete record. For the common path-density reference measure, define\n$$\n\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{tot}}[\\gamma]\n=\n\\ln\\frac{\\pi(x_0)}{\\pi(x_N)}\n+\n\\sum_{j=1}^{N}\\ln\\frac{k_{x_{j-1}x_j}}{k_{x_jx_{j-1}}}.\n$$\nThen\n$$\n\\ln\\frac{\\mathbb P_\\pi[\\gamma]}{\\mathbb P_\\pi[\\gamma^\\dagger]}\n=\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{tot}}[\\gamma],\n\\qquad\n\\left\\langle e^{-\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{tot}}}\\right\\rangle_\\pi=1.\n\\tag{O.3c0.5}\n$$\nHere the bracketed path probabilities denote densities, and the expectation includes summation over jump counts and state sequences and integration over jump times. For a closed path $x_N=x_0$, the stationary endpoint term vanishes. A positive-production trajectory has the displayed forward/reverse likelihood advantage; a lower bound $\\ln2$ for one forward-oriented circuit requires the independent affinity bound $\\mathcal A\\ge\\ln2$.\n\n*Proof.* The stationary weights before normalization are\n$$\nw_P=\\bar ac+bc+\\bar a\\bar b,\\qquad\nw_V=a\\bar b+\\bar b\\bar c+ac,\\qquad\nw_D=b\\bar c+ab+\\bar a\\bar c.\n$$\nSubstitution into the three master equations verifies stationarity. Their sum is the denominator in (O.3c0.2), and $\\pi_Pa-\\pi_V\\bar a$ equals its numerator divided by that sum. Conservation gives the same oriented current on the other two edges. The stationary entropy-production rate is the sum of each oriented current times $\\log(\\pi_i k_{ij}/(\\pi_j k_{ji}))$; the stationary weights telescope around the cycle, leaving $J\\mathcal A$.\n\nFinite-state finite rates make the process nonexplosive on $[0,T]$. The reversed path visits each state for the same total duration, so holding-time factors cancel in the path-density ratio. The initial-density and jump-rate factors give the displayed expression for $\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{tot}}$. Thus\n$$\n\\mathbb P_\\pi[\\gamma]e^{-\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{tot}}[\\gamma]}\n=\\mathbb P_\\pi[\\gamma^\\dagger].\n$$\nReversal is a measure-preserving bijection of this complete finite-window path space. Integration and summation over that space give the normalized expectation one. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o3-conditional-pathwise-arrow-bound\"></a>**Theorem O.3 (Conditional Pathwise Arrow Bound).** Suppose a coherent macroscopic step consists of $N$ update cycles with forward and reversed path measures on the same event algebra and\n$$\n\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{tot}}\n=\n\\log\\frac{P_F}{P_R}\n=\n\\sum_{k=1}^N\\sigma_k.\n$$\nIf $\\sigma_k\\ge h_{\\min}>0$ for every selected cycle, then\n$$\n\\frac{P_R}{P_F}\\le e^{-Nh_{\\min}}.\n$$\nIf $h_{\\min}\\ge\\ln2$, then $P_R/P_F\\le2^{-N}$.\n\n*Proof.* Summing the $N$ pathwise inequalities gives\n$$\n\\log\\frac{P_F}{P_R}\n=\n\\sum_{k=1}^N\\sigma_k\n\\ge\nNh_{\\min}.\n$$\nExponentiation gives $P_F/P_R\\ge e^{Nh_{\\min}}$, and inversion of positive probabilities gives the first conclusion. For $h_{\\min}\\ge\\ln2$,\n$$\ne^{-Nh_{\\min}}\\le e^{-N\\ln2}=2^{-N}.\n$$\n∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o3a-conditional-single-cycle-irreversibility-bound\"></a>**Theorem O.3a (Conditional Single-Cycle Irreversibility Bound).** Let $P_F(c)>0$ and $P_R(c^\\dagger)>0$ be weights on the same event algebra and suppose\n$$\n\\sigma(c)=\\log\\frac{P_F(c)}{P_R(c^\\dagger)}\n\\ge h_{\\min}>0.\n\\tag{O.3a.1}\n$$\nThen\n$$\n\\frac{P_R(c^\\dagger)}{P_F(c)}\n\\le\ne^{-h_{\\min}}.\n\\tag{O.3a.3}\n$$\nIn particular, a factor $1/2$ follows if $h_{\\min}\\ge\\ln2$.\n\n*Proof.* Exponentiating the hypothesis gives\n$$\n\\frac{P_F(c)}{P_R(c^\\dagger)}\n\\ge\ne^{h_{\\min}}.\n$$\nBoth weights are positive, so inversion proves (O.3a.3). If $h_{\\min}\\ge\\ln2$, then $e^{-h_{\\min}}\\le1/2$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o3a1-no-ensemble-requirement-under-a-pathwise-cycle-bound\"></a>**Corollary O.3a.1 (No Ensemble Requirement Under a Pathwise Cycle Bound).** When the guarantee-level update bound is imposed pathwise at the coarse-grained cycle level, the arrow of time applies to each processable actualization cycle in that class. Ensembles are then required only to estimate frequencies of outcomes, not to define the directionality of such a single update.\n\n*Proof.* Theorem O.3a uses a single cycle $c$ satisfying the single-cycle entropy-production identity and the pathwise lower bound. No averaging over a population of cycles is used in deriving (O.3a.3). ∎\n\n<a id=\"remark-o3a2-scope-of-the-single-cycle-bound\"></a>**Remark O.3a.2 (Scope of the Single-Cycle Bound).** Equation (O.3a.3) is a conditional pathwise consequence of (O.3a.1). Without the pathwise lower bound in (O.3a.1), Appendix J supplies its declared cycle-cost ledger, but neither the single-cycle bound of Theorem O.3a nor the multi-cycle suppression of Theorem O.3 follows. Theorem O.3 requires the corresponding pathwise lower bound for every selected cycle.\n\n<a id=\"remark-o3a3-delayed-choice-consistency\"></a>**Remark O.3a.3 (Delayed-Choice Consistency).** In delayed-choice, quantum-eraser, and pre/post-selected weak-probe protocols, a later recorded event changes which conditional subensemble or verification channel becomes operationally relevant for the retained record. It does not reverse the P-V-U order of the actualized MPU cycle and does not alter unconditioned earlier marginals (Corollary M.6.14d). Each recorded event is still processed through a forward update, and whenever it lies in the pathwise guarantee-level class it obeys the single-cycle irreversibility bound (O.3a.3).\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o3a4-forward-locked-orientation-ledger\"></a>**Corollary O.3a.4 (Forward-Locked Orientation Ledger).**\nLet $\\mathcal E$ be a finite class of coarse-grained update events equipped with an involution $e\\mapsto e^\\dagger$ and an orientation character\n$$\n\\chi:\\mathcal E\\to\\{+1,-1\\},\n\\qquad\n\\chi(e^\\dagger)=-\\chi(e).\n\\tag{O.3a.4.1}\n$$\nSuppose the forward and reversed path weights are defined on the same event algebra and obey\n$$\n\\sigma(e)=\\log\\frac{P_F(e)}{P_R(e^\\dagger)}.\n\\tag{O.3a.4.2}\n$$\nThen the forward-oriented expectation is the pairwise antisymmetric ledger\n$$\n\\langle\\chi\\rangle_F\n=\n\\sum_{[e]}\n\\chi(e)\\big(P_F(e)-P_F(e^\\dagger)\\big),\n\\tag{O.3a.4.3}\n$$\nwhere the sum is over unordered involution pairs; the summand is independent of which member is chosen as representative. If the pathwise guarantee-level bound $\\sigma(e)\\ge\\varepsilon_0=\\ln2$ holds on a pair, then\n$$\n\\frac{P_R(e^\\dagger)}{P_F(e)}\\le e^{-\\varepsilon_0}\\le\\frac12.\n\\tag{O.3a.4.4}\n$$\nThus Appendix O supplies an orientation gate for any later CP-odd or branch-odd transport ledger, while the existence and magnitude of the odd source remain separate branch data.\n\n*Proof.* Equation (O.3a.4.3) is obtained by partitioning the finite set $\\mathcal E$ into involution pairs and using $\\chi(e^\\dagger)=-\\chi(e)$. If the representative is changed from $e$ to $e^\\dagger$, the factor $\\chi$ and the probability difference both change sign, so the product is unchanged. Equation (O.3a.4.2) gives\n$$\n\\frac{P_R(e^\\dagger)}{P_F(e)}=e^{-\\sigma(e)}.\n$$\nUnder the pathwise guarantee-level bound this ratio is at most $e^{-\\varepsilon_0}\\le1/2$, proving (O.3a.4.4). No CP-odd or baryon-number source is produced by this statement; it only supplies the finite orientation ledger used by such branches. ∎\n\n<a id=\"definition-o3a2-modular-thermal-time-ledger\"></a>**Definition O.3a.2 (Modular Thermal-Time Ledger).** The external thermal-time precedent is the Connes-Rovelli thermal-time hypothesis (Connes and Rovelli 1994). On a finite faithful local branch, let $(\\mathfrak A_O,\\omega_O)$ be the local algebra-state pair for a retained region $O$, with modular Hamiltonian\n$$\nK_O:=-\\log\\rho_O\n\\tag{O.3a.2.1}\n$$\nas in Definition F.10.4b.1a. The modular thermal-time ledger is\n$$\n\\mathfrak T_{\\mathrm{mod}}(O)\n=\n\\left(\n\\mathfrak A_O,\n\\omega_O,\nK_O,\n\\sigma_t^{\\omega_O},\n\\beta_O,\n\\chi_{\\mathrm{mod}}\n\\right),\n\\tag{O.3a.2.2}\n$$\nwhere $\\beta_O$ is the physical-time normalization on the KMS branch and $\\chi_{\\mathrm{mod}}$ records that the algebra, state, and normalization are fixed before using macroscopic clock data.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o3a3-thermal-time-as-modular-prediction-time\"></a>**Theorem O.3a.3 (Thermal Time as Modular Prediction Time).** On a finite faithful local equilibrium branch carrying Definition O.3a.2, take $\\beta_O>0$ in inverse-energy units and the physical Heisenberg convention\n$$\n\\alpha_\\tau(A)=e^{i\\tau H_O/\\hbar}Ae^{-i\\tau H_O/\\hbar}.\n$$\nUnder the upper-strip KMS hypothesis of Theorem F.10.4b.2,\n$$\n\\sigma_t^{\\omega_O}=\\alpha_{-\\beta_O\\hbar t},\n\\tag{O.3a.3.1}\n$$\nso the dimensionless modular parameter and physical prediction time obey\n$$\n\\tau=-\\beta_O\\hbar t.\n\\tag{O.3a.3.2}\n$$\nIf the histories also carry the independent pathwise certificate of Theorem O.3, that certificate selects the orientation in which cumulative certified entropy production increases.\n\n*Proof.* The finite Gibbs conclusion of Theorem F.10.4b.2 gives\n$$\n\\rho_O^{it}A\\rho_O^{-it}\n=e^{-i\\beta_OtH_O}Ae^{i\\beta_OtH_O}\n=\\alpha_{-\\beta_O\\hbar t}(A).\n$$\nNormalization constants and central block weights cancel in conjugation. This proves the clock relation with the stated physical-time convention. The relation alone supplies no stochastic arrow. On histories satisfying $\\sigma_k\\ge h_{\\min}>0$ for every selected cycle, Theorem O.3 independently gives $P_R/P_F\\le e^{-Nh_{\\min}}$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"definition-o3b-three-term-predictive-entropy-resolution\"></a>**Definition O.3b (Three-Term Predictive Entropy Resolution).** Let $\\gamma=(x_0\\to\\cdots\\to x_T)$ be a finite coarse-grained predictive path with reversed path $\\gamma^\\dagger$. A three-term predictive entropy resolution is a branch datum\n$$\n(Q,N_{\\mathrm{SPAP}},\\Phi_{\\mathrm{PCE}})\n$$\nsuch that the path entropy production decomposes as\n$$\n\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{PU}}(\\gamma)\n=\n\\beta Q(\\gamma)\n+\nN_{\\mathrm{SPAP}}(\\gamma)\\ln2\n+\n\\Phi_{\\mathrm{PCE}}(x_T)-\\Phi_{\\mathrm{PCE}}(x_0).\n\\tag{O.3b.1}\n$$\nHere $Q(\\gamma)$ is heat delivered to the environment at inverse temperature $\\beta$, $N_{\\mathrm{SPAP}}(\\gamma)\\in\\mathbb N$ is the number of SPAP-forced merge events on the path, and $\\Phi_{\\mathrm{PCE}}$ is a branch-fixed PCE boundary potential. The resolution is admissible only when $Q$ and $N_{\\mathrm{SPAP}}$ are additive under path concatenation and the PCE term is a genuine endpoint coboundary.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o3b-three-term-predictive-fluctuation-decomposition\"></a>**Theorem O.3b (Three-Term Predictive Fluctuation Decomposition).** Let $\\Omega$ be a finite path alphabet, let $\\gamma\\mapsto\\gamma^\\dagger$ be a bijection onto its registered reversed alphabet, and let $P_F$ and $P_R$ be normalized strictly positive laws on those alphabets. On a branch with a finite-valued three-term predictive entropy resolution satisfying\n$$\n\\frac{P_F(\\gamma)}{P_R(\\gamma^\\dagger)}\n=\n\\exp(\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{PU}}(\\gamma)),\n\\tag{O.3b.2}\n$$\nthe following hold.\n\n1. The integral predictive fluctuation identity is\n$$\n\\left\\langle e^{-\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{PU}}}\\right\\rangle_F=1.\n\\tag{O.3b.3}\n$$\n\n2. The mean entropy production is nonnegative:\n$$\n\\langle\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{PU}}\\rangle_F\\ge0.\n\\tag{O.3b.4}\n$$\n\n3. If the path moment-generating function is finite on an interval containing $0$, then\n$$\n\\Lambda_{\\Sigma}(\\lambda)\n=\n\\log\\left\\langle e^{\\lambda\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{PU}}}\\right\\rangle_F\n\\tag{O.3b.5}\n$$\nsatisfies\n$$\n\\Lambda_{\\Sigma}'(0)\n=\n\\langle\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{PU}}\\rangle_F.\n\\tag{O.3b.6}\n$$\n\n4. For $N$ independent identical resolved cycles,\n$$\n\\Lambda_{\\Sigma,N}(\\lambda)=N\\Lambda_{\\Sigma,1}(\\lambda).\n\\tag{O.3b.7}\n$$\nFor every $s$ and every $\\lambda<0$ in the domain of $\\Lambda_{\\Sigma,1}$,\n$$\nP_F\\!\\left(\\frac1N\\sum_{j=1}^N\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{PU}}^{(j)}\\le s\\right)\n\\le\n\\exp\\!\\left[\nN\\big(\\Lambda_{\\Sigma,1}(\\lambda)-\\lambda s\\big)\n\\right].\n\\tag{O.3b.8}\n$$\nEquivalently, with\n$$\nI_-(s)=\\sup_{\\lambda<0}\\{\\lambda s-\\Lambda_{\\Sigma,1}(\\lambda)\\},\n\\tag{O.3b.9}\n$$\none has\n$$\nP_F\\!\\left(\\frac1N\\sum_{j=1}^N\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{PU}}^{(j)}\\le s\\right)\n\\le\ne^{-NI_-(s)}.\n\\tag{O.3b.10}\n$$\n\n5. In a stationary resolved branch observed for physical time $\\tau$, the mean rate of dimensionless entropy production, with units of inverse time, is\n$$\n\\dot\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{PU}}\n:=\n\\lim_{\\tau\\to\\infty}\n\\frac{1}{\\tau}\n\\left.\n\\frac{d}{d\\lambda}\\Lambda_{\\Sigma,\\tau}(\\lambda)\n\\right|_{\\lambda=0},\n\\tag{O.3b.11}\n$$\nwhenever the limit exists. If $T$ is constant and the rates of $N_{\\mathrm{SPAP}}\\ln2$ and $\\Delta\\Phi_{\\mathrm{PCE}}$ vanish, then the environmental heat rate is $\\dot Q=k_BT\\dot\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{PU}}$. Otherwise, an energy ledger requires a separate bridge for each non-heat term.\n\n*Proof.* From (O.3b.2),\n$$\nP_F(\\gamma)e^{-\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{PU}}(\\gamma)}\n=\nP_R(\\gamma^\\dagger).\n$$\nSumming over the finite path alphabet gives\n$$\n\\left\\langle e^{-\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{PU}}}\\right\\rangle_F\n=\n\\sum_{\\gamma}P_R(\\gamma^\\dagger)\n=\n1,\n$$\nwhich proves (O.3b.3). Jensen's inequality applied to the convex function $e^{-x}$ gives\n$$\ne^{-\\langle\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{PU}}\\rangle_F}\n\\le\n\\left\\langle e^{-\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{PU}}}\\right\\rangle_F\n=\n1,\n$$\nhence (O.3b.4).\n\nIf the moment-generating function is finite in a neighborhood of $0$, differentiation under the finite sum gives\n$$\n\\Lambda_{\\Sigma}'(0)\n=\n\\frac{\\langle \\Sigma_{\\mathrm{PU}}e^{0\\cdot\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{PU}}}\\rangle_F}\n{\\langle e^{0\\cdot\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{PU}}}\\rangle_F}\n=\n\\langle\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{PU}}\\rangle_F,\n$$\nwhich proves (O.3b.6). For independent identical resolved cycles, moment-generating functions multiply, so logarithms add, giving (O.3b.7).\n\nFor $\\lambda<0$, the event $\\frac1N\\sum_j\\Sigma_j\\le s$ implies\n$$\ne^{\\lambda\\sum_j\\Sigma_j}\\ge e^{\\lambda Ns}.\n$$\nMarkov's inequality therefore gives\n$$\nP_F\\!\\left(\\frac1N\\sum_j\\Sigma_j\\le s\\right)\n\\le\ne^{-\\lambda Ns}\n\\left\\langle e^{\\lambda\\sum_j\\Sigma_j}\\right\\rangle_F\n=\n\\exp\\!\\left[\nN\\big(\\Lambda_{\\Sigma,1}(\\lambda)-\\lambda s\\big)\n\\right].\n$$\nTaking the infimum over $\\lambda<0$ gives (O.3b.10). Equation (O.3b.11) is (O.3b.6) applied per unit observation time when the displayed limit exists. On the constant-temperature branch where the rates of $N_{\\mathrm{SPAP}}\\ln2$ and $\\Delta\\Phi_{\\mathrm{PCE}}$ vanish, (O.3b.1) gives $\\dot Q=k_BT\\dot\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{PU}}$. An energy interpretation of any nonzero non-heat contribution requires its separate bridge. ∎\n\n<a id=\"definition-o3c-retained-algebra-conditioning-certificate-mathfrak-c_mathrmret\"></a>**Definition O.3c (Retained-Algebra Conditioning Certificate $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{ret}}$).** Fix an observer hierarchy level $q$ in the sense of §P.5.8.3. A retained-algebra conditioning certificate is a finite record\n$$\n\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{ret}}^{(q)}\n=\n(\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{ret}}^{(q)}(t),\\;\\iota_{s,t},\\;S_{\\mathrm{ret}}^{(q)},\\;\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{anc}},\\;\\text{no-deletion or retention record},\\;\\text{forward lock})\n$$\nwhere $\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{ret}}^{(q)}(t)$ is the PPI-retained response algebra generated by verified records available at level $q$ up to time $t$, $\\iota_{s,t}:\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{ret}}^{(q)}(s)\\hookrightarrow\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{ret}}^{(q)}(t)$ are inclusion maps for $s<t$, $S_{\\mathrm{ret}}^{(q)}$ is the entropy functional restricted to that retained algebra, and $\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{anc}}$ is a finite ledger-ancestry verifier for record chains. The certificate is level-relative: it does not assert an observer-independent entropy for response-null degrees of freedom.\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o3d-retained-algebra-boundary-reading\"></a>**Proposition O.3d (Retained-Algebra Boundary Reading).** On a branch carrying $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{ret}}^{(q)}$, the registered inclusion maps give a monotone filtration,\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{ret}}^{(q)}(s)\\subseteq\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{ret}}^{(q)}(t),\n\\qquad s<t.\n$$\nFor the entropy claim, require a finite-dimensional unital retained algebra\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{ret}}^{(q)}(t_0)\\cong\\bigoplus_\\alpha M_{d_\\alpha}(\\mathbb C)\n$$\nand let $S_{\\mathrm{ret}}^{(q)}$ be the intrinsic von Neumann entropy in nats of its normalized state, using the sum of the ordinary matrix traces. Then\n$$\nS_{\\mathrm{ret}}^{(q)}(t_0)\n\\le\\ln\\!\\left(\\sum_\\alpha d_\\alpha\\right)\n\\le\\ln\\dim_{\\mathbb C}\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{ret}}^{(q)}(t_0).\n$$\nIf the initial algebra has minimal dimension within the certified filtration, this gives a minimal dimension-based entropy upper bound. It does not prove that the actual initial entropy is minimal, or quantitatively small without a corresponding dimension bound. Record-shaped configurations lacking accepted ledger ancestry are excluded from this retained-conditioning class only when $\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{anc}}$ distinguishes them by a finite response protocol.\n\n*Proof.* The certificate's forward-lock and no-deletion/retention entries preserve earlier records under the injective inclusion maps. For the entropy claim, write the intrinsic density matrix blockwise and list its eigenvalues, including zeros. They form a probability vector with $\\sum_\\alpha d_\\alpha$ entries, whose Shannon entropy is at most $\\ln(\\sum_\\alpha d_\\alpha)$. Since each $d_\\alpha\\ge1$, one has $\\sum_\\alpha d_\\alpha\\le\\sum_\\alpha d_\\alpha^2=\\dim_{\\mathbb C}\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{ret}}^{(q)}(t_0)$. These upper bounds do not compare the actual states at different times. The ancestry exclusion is only the declared finite-response distinction. No positive thermodynamic cost follows; reset heat retains every separate hypothesis of Theorem 31. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o3d1-exact-entropy-range-of-a-retained-state\"></a>**Theorem O.3d.1 (Exact Entropy Range of a Retained State).** Represent a finite unital retained algebra on\n$$\n\\mathcal H=\\bigoplus_{\\alpha=1}^r\n\\left(\\mathbb C^{d_\\alpha}\\otimes\\mathbb C^{m_\\alpha}\\right),\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal A=\\bigoplus_{\\alpha=1}^r\n\\left(M_{d_\\alpha}(\\mathbb C)\\otimes I_{m_\\alpha}\\right),\n\\qquad m_\\alpha\\ge1.\n\\tag{O.3d.1.1}\n$$\nFix the retained state by weights $p_\\alpha\\ge0$, $\\sum_\\alpha p_\\alpha=1$, and density matrices $\\rho_\\alpha$ in each positive-weight sector; choose arbitrary density matrices in zero-weight sectors, whose terms vanish. Its intrinsic entropy is\n$$\nS_{\\mathcal A}=H(p)+\\sum_\\alpha p_\\alpha S(\\rho_\\alpha).\n$$\nAllow all full density matrices $\\tau$ on $\\mathcal H$ restricting to this state on $\\mathcal A$, with no additional superselection constraint. Let $\\lambda_{\\alpha,1}\\ge\\cdots\\ge\\lambda_{\\alpha,d_\\alpha}\\ge0$ be the eigenvalues of $\\rho_\\alpha$, extended by zeros, and put\n$$\ns_k=\\sum_\\alpha p_\\alpha\n\\sum_{j=(k-1)m_\\alpha+1}^{km_\\alpha}\\lambda_{\\alpha,j},\n\\qquad k\\ge1.\n\\tag{O.3d.1.2}\n$$\nThe sequence $s$ is a finite, decreasing probability vector. The exact set of possible full-state entropies is the closed interval\n$$\n\\left[\\ H(s),\\quad S_{\\mathcal A}+\\sum_\\alpha p_\\alpha\\ln m_\\alpha\\ \\right].\n\\tag{O.3d.1.3}\n$$\nThe upper endpoint is attained uniquely by\n$$\n\\tau_{\\max}=\\bigoplus_\\alpha\np_\\alpha\\rho_\\alpha\\otimes\\frac{I_{m_\\alpha}}{m_\\alpha}.\n\\tag{O.3d.1.4}\n$$\nThe lower endpoint has an explicit construction. Choose eigenvectors $|e_{\\alpha,j}\\rangle$ of $\\rho_\\alpha$ and a multiplicity basis $|\\ell\\rangle$, and set\n$$\n|\\psi_k\\rangle\n=\\bigoplus_\\alpha\\sum_{\\ell=1}^{m_\\alpha}\n\\sqrt{p_\\alpha\\lambda_{\\alpha,(k-1)m_\\alpha+\\ell}}\n\\,|e_{\\alpha,(k-1)m_\\alpha+\\ell}\\rangle\\otimes|\\ell\\rangle,\n\\qquad\n\\tau_{\\min}=\\sum_k|\\psi_k\\rangle\\langle\\psi_k|,\n\\tag{O.3d.1.5}\n$$\nomitting terms whose eigenvector index exceeds $d_\\alpha$. A pure extension exists exactly when $m_\\alpha\\ge\\operatorname{rank}\\rho_\\alpha$ for every positive-weight sector.\n\n*Proof.* Restriction to $\\mathcal A$ means\n$$\n\\operatorname{Tr}_{\\mathbb C^{m_\\alpha}}\n(P_\\alpha\\tau P_\\alpha)=p_\\alpha\\rho_\\alpha,\n\\tag{O.3d.1.6}\n$$\nwhere $P_\\alpha$ is the sector projection. First establish the lower bound. Write the decreasing eigenvalues of $\\tau$ as $x_1,x_2,\\ldots$, and let $\\tau^{(k)}$ be its positive spectral truncation to the largest $k$ eigenvalues. Then $0\\le\\tau^{(k)}\\le\\tau$ and $\\operatorname{rank}\\tau^{(k)}\\le k$. Its reduced sector matrix\n$$\nR_\\alpha^{(k)}=\n\\operatorname{Tr}_{\\mathbb C^{m_\\alpha}}\n(P_\\alpha\\tau^{(k)}P_\\alpha)\n$$\nsatisfies $0\\le R_\\alpha^{(k)}\\le p_\\alpha\\rho_\\alpha$ and has rank at most $km_\\alpha$: partial trace of each rank-one term has rank at most $m_\\alpha$. If $Q$ is its support projection, then\n$$\n\\operatorname{Tr}R_\\alpha^{(k)}\n\\le\\operatorname{Tr}(Qp_\\alpha\\rho_\\alpha)\n\\le p_\\alpha\\sum_{j=1}^{km_\\alpha}\\lambda_{\\alpha,j}.\n$$\nThe last inequality follows by diagonalizing $\\rho_\\alpha$: the diagonal entries of $Q$ lie in $[0,1]$ and sum to at most $km_\\alpha$, so their weighted sum is maximized by the largest eigenvalues. Summing sectors yields\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^k x_i\n\\le\\sum_\\alpha p_\\alpha\\sum_{j=1}^{km_\\alpha}\\lambda_{\\alpha,j}\n=\\sum_{i=1}^k s_i.\n\\tag{O.3d.1.7}\n$$\nThe vector $s$ is decreasing because each summand is a sum of consecutive equal-size blocks of a decreasing nonnegative sequence, and its total is one. Thus (O.3d.1.7) is the majorization $x\\prec s$, which implies $H(x)\\ge H(s)$. Explicitly, pad both vectors with zeros. Summation by parts gives $\\sum_i(s_i-x_i)\\ln x_i\\ge0$ from the nonnegative cumulative sums of $s-x$ and the decreasing sequence $\\ln x_i$. Together with classical relative-entropy nonnegativity,\n$$\nH(x)-H(s)=D(s\\|x)+\\sum_i(s_i-x_i)\\ln x_i\\ge0.\n$$\nZero entries are handled on the support of $x$; (O.3d.1.7) forces $s$ to vanish beyond that support. In (O.3d.1.5), vectors with different $k$ are orthogonal and have squared norms $s_k$. Taking the multiplicity partial trace in each sector gives (O.3d.1.6). Therefore $\\tau_{\\min}$ is an extension with eigenvalues $s_k$, attaining the lower endpoint.\n\nFor the upper bound, every extension is supported on\n$$\n\\bigoplus_{\\alpha:p_\\alpha>0}\n\\left(\\operatorname{supp}\\rho_\\alpha\\otimes\\mathbb C^{m_\\alpha}\\right).\n$$\nIndeed, its expectation on the complementary projection is zero by (O.3d.1.6); positivity then forces its rows and columns on that complement to vanish. On this support, $\\ln\\tau_{\\max}$ is blockwise\n$\\ln p_\\alpha+\\ln\\rho_\\alpha-\\ln m_\\alpha$. Its expectation is fixed by the retained state, giving the exact identity\n$$\nD(\\tau\\|\\tau_{\\max})\n=S_{\\mathcal A}+\\sum_\\alpha p_\\alpha\\ln m_\\alpha-S(\\tau).\n\\tag{O.3d.1.8}\n$$\nFor completeness, quantum relative-entropy nonnegativity here follows by diagonalizing $\\tau$ with eigenvalues $x_i$ and diagonalizing the faithful restriction of $\\tau_{\\max}$ with eigenvalues $y_j$. The overlaps form a doubly stochastic matrix $w_{ij}$. Concavity of $\\ln$ gives $\\sum_jw_{ij}\\ln y_j\\le\\ln(\\sum_jw_{ij}y_j)$, so $D(\\tau\\|\\tau_{\\max})$ is at least the classical relative entropy between $x_i$ and the diagonal entries of $\\tau_{\\max}$ in the $\\tau$ basis. That classical quantity is nonnegative by $\\ln z\\le z-1$. Equality requires equality in both inequalities; the logarithm equality places every positive-eigenvalue eigenvector of $\\tau$ in an eigenspace of $\\tau_{\\max}$ with the same eigenvalue, yielding $\\tau=\\tau_{\\max}$. This proves the upper endpoint and uniqueness.\n\nThe extension set is convex. Entropy is continuous in finite dimension, so the line segment from $\\tau_{\\min}$ to $\\tau_{\\max}$ realizes every entropy between their endpoint values by the intermediate value theorem. Finally, $H(s)=0$ exactly when $s_1=1$, which holds exactly when each positive-weight $\\rho_\\alpha$ has rank at most $m_\\alpha$. Formula (O.3d.1.5) then consists of one unit vector and gives the pure extension. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o3d2-retained-response-equivalence-and-unrestricted-completion-entropy\"></a>**Corollary O.3d.2 (Retained-Response Equivalence and Unrestricted Completion Entropy).** Under Theorem O.3d.1, every finite adaptive instrument whose branch Kraus operators belong to $\\mathcal A$ has the same complete record distribution on every extension of the fixed retained state. If the representation multiplicities may be chosen freely while the abstract retained algebra and its state remain fixed, the possible full-state entropies range over all finite values in $[0,\\infty)$. This classification supplies finite state extensions of the specified response algebra; identifying any extension with a global cosmological state requires the corresponding additional physical branch data.\n\n*Proof.* For a complete record branch, the product $K$ of its selected Kraus operators lies in $\\mathcal A$. Its probability is $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\tau K^\\dagger K)$, which depends only on the fixed restriction to $\\mathcal A$. Coarse-grained probabilities are sums of these values, and adaptive selection changes only which products occur. For the entropy assertion, choose a common multiplicity $m\\ge\\max_\\alpha\\operatorname{rank}\\rho_\\alpha$. Theorem O.3d.1 gives the full interval $[0,S_{\\mathcal A}+\\ln m]$. Its union over finite integers $m$ is $[0,\\infty)$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"remark-o3e-non-redundancy-with-the-arrow-theorem\"></a>**Remark O.3e (Non-Redundancy with the Arrow Theorem).** Theorems O.3, O.3a, and O.3b derive directionality and fluctuation suppression. Proposition O.3d supplies the separate retained-algebra reading of low-boundary and conditioning claims, and is therefore used only where the text discusses the Past Hypothesis or record-selection ambiguity.\n\n<a id=\"definition-o3f-retained-entropic-clock-flow-certificate\"></a>**Definition O.3f (Retained Entropic Clock-Flow Certificate).** Fix a retained sector $R$ and observer level $q$ carrying $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{ret}}^{(q)}$. A retained entropic clock-flow certificate is a finite record\n$$\n\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{ECF}}^{(R,q)}\n=\n(\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{ret}}^{(q)},\\mathcal E_R,\\sigma_R^{\\mathrm{ECF}},\\mathcal G_R,\n\\varepsilon_0,\n\\mathcal N_R,\n\\tau_R^{\\mathrm{cyc}},\n\\chi_{\\mathrm{ECF}})\n\\tag{O.3f.1}\n$$\nwhere $\\mathcal E_R$ is the finite retained event alphabet, $\\sigma_R^{\\mathrm{ECF}}:\\mathcal E_R\\to[0,\\infty)$ is the branch-fixed retained entropy-production increment, $\\mathcal G_R\\subseteq\\mathcal E_R$ is the locked guarantee-level retained-update subset, $\\varepsilon_0=\\ln2$ is the binary structural log-cardinality supplied by Definition 28, Definition J.1, and Theorem J.1, $\\mathcal N_R$ is the null-exchange and label-swap control ledger, $\\tau_R^{\\mathrm{cyc}}$ is either a fixed physical cycle-time calibration or the symbol $\\bot$ when only dimensionless clock count is claimed, and $\\chi_{\\mathrm{ECF}}$ records that the event alphabet, retained entropy-increment estimator, guarantee-level subset, null controls, and calibration were fixed before the clock-flow comparison.\n\nFor $e\\in\\mathcal G_R$, the certificate records that $e$ is the retained projection of a nontrivial update cycle and identifies its retained increment with the matched log-likelihood ratio,\n$$\n\\sigma_R^{\\mathrm{ECF}}(e)=\\log\\frac{P_F(e)}{P_R(e^\\dagger)}.\n$$\nThe matched weights are positive and satisfy Equation (O.3a.1) with $h_{\\min}\\ge\\varepsilon_0=\\ln2$. Hence\n$$\n\\sigma_R^{\\mathrm{ECF}}(e)\\ge\\varepsilon_0=\\ln2.\n\\tag{O.3f.2}\n$$\nThis lower bound is certificate data; it does not follow from SPAP or from the structural binary log-cardinality alone.\nNo such lower bound is claimed for events outside $\\mathcal G_R$ unless they are separately certified.\n\nFor a retained event history $e_1,\\ldots,e_n$ define\n$$\n\\Sigma_R^{\\mathrm{ECF}}(n)=\n\\sum_{j=1}^n\\sigma_R^{\\mathrm{ECF}}(e_j),\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal T_R(n)=\n\\frac{\\Sigma_R^{\\mathrm{ECF}}(n)}{\\varepsilon_0}\n=\n\\frac{\\Sigma_R^{\\mathrm{ECF}}(n)}{\\ln2}.\n\\tag{O.3f.3}\n$$\n$\\mathcal T_R$ is the dimensionless retained entropic clock count. If $\\tau_R^{\\mathrm{cyc}}\\ne\\bot$, the calibrated physical representative is\n$$\nt_R^{\\mathrm{ECF}}(n)=\n\\tau_R^{\\mathrm{cyc}}\\mathcal T_R(n).\n\\tag{O.3f.4}\n$$\nWhen $e_j\\in\\mathcal G_R$, (O.3f.2) implies that the event advances $\\mathcal T_R$ by at least one unit.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o3f-retained-clock-flow-and-stall-law\"></a>**Theorem O.3f (Retained Clock-Flow and Stall Law).** On any branch carrying $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{ECF}}^{(R,q)}$, the retained entropic clock $\\mathcal T_R$ is monotone. For any retained interval $I=[m,n]$,\n$$\n\\Delta\\mathcal T_R(I)\n=\n\\frac{1}{\\ln2}\n\\sum_{j=m+1}^n\\sigma_R^{\\mathrm{ECF}}(e_j).\n\\tag{O.3f.5}\n$$\nTherefore:\n\n1. if the retained entropy/update flow vanishes on $I$, then $\\Delta\\mathcal T_R(I)=0$;\n2. if $I$ contains $N_I$ events from the locked guarantee-level subset $\\mathcal G_R$, then $\\Delta\\mathcal T_R(I)\\ge N_I$;\n3. if no independently registered non-entropic clock record is present in the retained presheaf, event-order refinements inside a zero-flow interval do not change the $\\mathcal T_R$ coordinate and are response-null for the entropic-clock subtest of the $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{ECF}}$ protocol.\n\n*Proof.* The certificate requires $\\sigma_R^{\\mathrm{ECF}}(e_j)\\ge0$ for each retained event, so $\\Sigma_R^{\\mathrm{ECF}}$ and $\\mathcal T_R=\\Sigma_R^{\\mathrm{ECF}}/\\ln2$ are monotone. Summing the certified increments across $I$ gives (O.3f.5). If every increment on $I$ is zero, the sum is zero. If $N_I$ interval events lie in $\\mathcal G_R$, (O.3f.2) supplies $\\sigma_R^{\\mathrm{ECF}}(e_j)\\ge\\varepsilon_0=\\ln2$ for each such event, and all other increments are nonnegative, so the normalized sum is at least $N_I$. The last clause is the PPI quotient clause for the entropic-clock coordinate: without a retained record that distinguishes two internal refinements, the refinements are not separate clock events for this protocol. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o3g-clock-current-tur\"></a>**Corollary O.3g (Clock-Current TUR).** If $\\mathcal T_R$ is estimated by a stationary finite Blackwell-PCE record current $J_T$ with $\\langle J_T\\rangle\\ne0$ on a branch satisfying the predictive thermodynamic uncertainty relation of Theorem D.8.7f, and if\n$$\n\\widehat{\\Delta\\mathcal T_R}=\\gamma_T J_T,\n\\qquad \\gamma_T>0,\n\\tag{O.3g.1}\n$$\nthen\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{Var}(\\widehat{\\Delta\\mathcal T_R})}\n{\\langle\\widehat{\\Delta\\mathcal T_R}\\rangle^2}\n\\Sigma_T\n\\ge2.\n\\tag{O.3g.2}\n$$\n\n*Proof.* Multiplication of the current by the positive calibration $\\gamma_T$ multiplies both the mean and standard deviation by $\\gamma_T$, so the relative variance is unchanged. Substitution into Theorem D.8.7f gives (O.3g.2). ∎\n\n<a id=\"remark-o3h-closed-system-and-two-sector-reading\"></a>**Remark O.3h (Closed-System and Two-Sector Reading).** In a closed total branch $R\\cup\\bar R$, the retained clock-flow law is a subsystem statement, not a violation of global closure. The total branch may conserve its accepted fine-grained invariant while a retained sector obtains an arrow and an internal clock from entropy/update exchange with its complement. When the exchange ledger is null, $\\mathcal T_R$ stalls unless another retained clock certificate has been independently fixed. Thus two-sector analogue clocks test the PU chronometric projection only when the retained sector, complement controls, retained entropy-increment estimator, guarantee-level subset when used, and null windows are locked before comparison. Cold-atom bright/dark-sector entropic-time experiments such as Barontini [2026] are external analogue precedents for this two-sector operational architecture; they are not forward PU evidence for the $\\ln2$ unit bridge unless the PU unit, retained entropy-increment estimator, null windows, guarantee-level subset when used, and witness locks are fixed before comparison.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o3i-complete-finite-bidirectional-markov-arrow-and-source-decomposition\"></a>**Theorem O.3i (Complete Finite Bidirectional Markov Arrow and Source Decomposition).** Let $Q=(q_{ij})$ be the generator of an irreducible continuous-time Markov chain on a finite set, with\n$$\nq_{ij}>0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad q_{ji}>0\n\\qquad(i\\ne j),\n\\tag{O.3i.1}\n$$\nand stationary law $\\pi_i>0$. Let $E:=\\{\\{i,j\\}:q_{ij}>0\\}$ be its undirected edge set. Start the chain in $\\pi$, let $P_F^{[0,T]}$ be its path law, and let $P_R^{[0,T]}$ be the stationary time-reversed path law on the same càdlàg event space. Then the two measures are mutually absolutely continuous and the path entropy production is\n$$\n\\Sigma_T(\\gamma)\n=\\log\\frac{dP_F^{[0,T]}}{dP_R^{[0,T]}}(\\gamma)\n=\\sum_{i\\to j\\text{ in }\\gamma}\n\\log\\frac{\\pi_iq_{ij}}{\\pi_jq_{ji}}.\n\\tag{O.3i.2}\n$$\nIts stationary mean rate is\n$$\n\\sigma\n=\\sum_{\\substack{i<j\\\\\\{i,j\\}\\in E}}J_{ij}\\log\\frac{a_{ij}}{a_{ji}},\n\\quad\na_{ij}:=\\pi_iq_{ij},\n\\quad\nJ_{ij}:=a_{ij}-a_{ji},\n\\tag{O.3i.3}\n$$\nand obeys the exact strictness classification and quantitative bound\n$$\n\\sigma\\ge\n\\sum_{\\substack{i<j\\\\\\{i,j\\}\\in E}}\\frac{2J_{ij}^2}{a_{ij}+a_{ji}}\\ge0,\n\\qquad\n\\sigma=0\\Longleftrightarrow\n\\pi_iq_{ij}=\\pi_jq_{ji}\\ \\text{on every edge}.\n\\tag{O.3i.4}\n$$\nIf the undirected edges are partitioned before evaluation into disjoint source-owner classes $E=\\bigsqcup_{r=1}^kE_r$, then\n$$\n\\Sigma_T=\\sum_r\\Sigma_T^{(r)},\n\\qquad\n\\sigma=\\sum_r\\sigma_r,\n\\quad\n\\sigma_r:=\\sum_{\\{i,j\\}\\in E_r}J_{ij}\\log\\frac{a_{ij}}{a_{ji}}\\ge0.\n\\tag{O.3i.5}\n$$\nThis is a source-exhaustive, no-double-counted decomposition because each realized jump has one undirected edge and hence one owner.\n\n*Proof.* Bidirectional support makes every finite-jump forward path and its reversal possible. Multiplying the jump densities and holding-time factors shows that the latter cancel in the likelihood ratio, yielding (O.3i.2). Stationarity and the compensator formula give (O.3i.3). Each summand is nonnegative because $(a-b)\\log(a/b)\\ge2(a-b)^2/(a+b)$ for $a,b>0$, with equality exactly at $a=b$. Partitioning the jump sum and the undirected-edge sum proves (O.3i.5). ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o3j-thermal-quantum-jump-realization-of-the-finite-markov-arrow-and-its-reservoir-criterion\"></a>**Theorem O.3j (Thermal Quantum-Jump Realization of the Finite Markov Arrow and Its Reservoir Criterion).** Let $Q$ on the finite set $X$ satisfy the hypotheses of Theorem O.3i, with undirected edge set $E$ and a preregistered source partition $E=\\bigsqcup_{r=1}^kE_r$; write $r(x,y)$ for the owner of $\\{x,y\\}$. A thermal realization of $(Q,\\{E_r\\})$ is a Hilbert space $\\mathbb C^X$ with orthonormal basis $\\{|x\\rangle\\}$, level energies $\\epsilon_x\\in\\mathbb R$, Hamiltonian $H=\\sum_x\\epsilon_x|x\\rangle\\!\\langle x|$, reservoir inverse temperatures $\\beta_r>0$ and the GKLS generator\n$$\n\\mathcal L(\\rho)=-\\frac i\\hbar[H,\\rho]\n+\\sum_{x\\ne y,\\ q_{xy}>0}q_{xy}\\left(|y\\rangle\\!\\langle x|\\rho|x\\rangle\\!\\langle y|-\\frac12\\{|x\\rangle\\!\\langle x|,\\rho\\}\\right),\n$$\nsubject to the reservoir detailed-balance (KMS) relation\n$$\n\\frac{q_{xy}}{q_{yx}}=e^{\\beta_{r(x,y)}(\\epsilon_x-\\epsilon_y)}\n\\qquad(\\{x,y\\}\\in E).\n\\tag{O.3j.1}\n$$\nA jump $x\\to y$ delivers heat $\\epsilon_x-\\epsilon_y$ to reservoir $r(x,y)$; for a path $\\gamma$ let $Q_r(\\gamma)$ be the total heat delivered to reservoir $r$. Then:\n\n1. $e^{t\\mathcal L}$ is a completely positive trace-preserving semigroup whose restriction to diagonal states is the Markov semigroup: $e^{t\\mathcal L}\\bigl(\\sum_xp_x|x\\rangle\\!\\langle x|\\bigr)=\\sum_x(pe^{tQ})_x|x\\rangle\\!\\langle x|$. In the counting-process unraveling by the jump operators $\\sqrt{q_{xy}}\\,|y\\rangle\\!\\langle x|$, a trajectory started in a basis state remains a basis state, the no-jump survival probability from $x$ is $e^{q_{xx}t}$, and jumps $x\\to y$ occur at rate $q_{xy}$, so the jump record has the path law of $Q$.\n2. For every finite path $\\gamma$ on $[0,T]$,\n   $$\n   \\Sigma_T(\\gamma)=\\ln\\pi(x_0)-\\ln\\pi(x_T)+\\sum_{r=1}^k\\beta_rQ_r(\\gamma).\n   \\tag{O.3j.2}\n   $$\n   In the stationary state the mean heat current into reservoir $r$ is $J^Q_r=\\sum_{\\{x,y\\}\\in E_r}J_{xy}(\\epsilon_x-\\epsilon_y)$, and\n   $$\n   \\sigma=\\sum_{r=1}^k\\beta_rJ^Q_r,\n   \\qquad\n   \\sum_{r=1}^kJ^Q_r=0,\n   \\qquad\n   \\sigma=\\sum_{r=1}^k(\\beta_r-\\beta_{r_0})J^Q_r\n   \\quad\\text{for every }r_0.\n   \\tag{O.3j.3}\n   $$\n3. With one reservoir, $k=1$, a thermal realization exists exactly when $Q$ satisfies detailed balance, and then $\\sigma=0$. A realization for any partition induces one for the finest partition, one reservoir per edge, by $\\beta_{\\{x,y\\}}:=\\beta_{r(x,y)}$. The finest partition has a thermal realization exactly when no closed walk $x_0\\to x_1\\to\\cdots\\to x_m=x_0$ along edges has $q_{x_{l-1}x_l}\\ge q_{x_lx_{l-1}}$ at every step with strict inequality at some step.\n4. Under Principle O.3c0, with the jumps $P\\to V$, $V\\to D$ and $D\\to P$ and their reverses as the event alphabet, the edges as sources and one reservoir per edge, a thermal realization exists exactly when $\\min(a/\\bar a,\\,b/\\bar b,\\,c/\\bar c)<1$. If $a\\ge\\bar a$, $b\\ge\\bar b$ and $c\\ge\\bar c$, no source partition admits a thermal realization, so every realization of the arrow of Theorem O.3c0 with these rates lies outside this heat-only class.\n\n*Proof.* (1) $\\mathcal L$ has GKLS form with self-adjoint $H$, so $e^{t\\mathcal L}$ is completely positive and trace preserving (Gorini, Kossakowski and Sudarshan 1976; Lindblad 1976). On a diagonal state $[H,\\rho]=0$, the jump term sends $|x\\rangle\\!\\langle x|$ to $|y\\rangle\\!\\langle y|$ with weight $q_{xy}$, and the anticommutator term contributes $-\\sum_{y\\ne x}q_{xy}|x\\rangle\\!\\langle x|=q_{xx}|x\\rangle\\!\\langle x|$. Hence $\\mathcal L$ maps the diagonal subspace into itself and acts there as $p\\mapsto pQ$, which gives the restriction. In the unraveling, the no-jump generator $H-\\frac{i\\hbar}2\\sum L^\\dagger L=\\sum_x(\\epsilon_x+\\frac{i\\hbar}2q_{xx})|x\\rangle\\!\\langle x|$ is diagonal, so $|x\\rangle$ stays proportional to $|x\\rangle$ with squared norm $e^{q_{xx}t}$; the jump density to $y$ is $q_{xy}$ times this survival factor, and the post-jump state is $|y\\rangle$. These are the holding and jump laws of $Q$.\n\n(2) By (O.3i.2), $\\Sigma_T(\\gamma)$ is the sum over jumps of $\\ln(\\pi_xq_{xy}/(\\pi_yq_{yx}))$. The $\\pi$ factors telescope to $\\ln\\pi(x_0)-\\ln\\pi(x_T)$, and (O.3j.1) turns each rate ratio into $\\beta_{r(x,y)}(\\epsilon_x-\\epsilon_y)$, the entropy delivered with that jump's heat. Since $J_{yx}(\\epsilon_y-\\epsilon_x)=J_{xy}(\\epsilon_x-\\epsilon_y)$, the edge sums do not depend on orientation. In (O.3i.3), $\\ln(a_{xy}/a_{yx})=\\ln(\\pi_x/\\pi_y)+\\beta_{r(x,y)}(\\epsilon_x-\\epsilon_y)$, and the $\\pi$ contribution is $\\sum_x\\ln\\pi_x\\sum_yJ_{xy}=0$ because the stationary current has zero divergence; this gives $\\sigma=\\sum_r\\beta_rJ^Q_r$. The same zero divergence gives $\\sum_rJ^Q_r=\\frac12\\sum_{x,y}J_{xy}(\\epsilon_x-\\epsilon_y)=\\sum_x\\epsilon_x\\sum_yJ_{xy}=0$, and subtracting $\\beta_{r_0}\\sum_rJ^Q_r$ gives the last form.\n\n(3) For $k=1$, (O.3j.1) makes $\\pi_x\\propto e^{-\\beta\\epsilon_x}$ satisfy $\\pi_xq_{xy}=\\pi_yq_{yx}$; conversely, detailed balance with stationary law $\\pi$ gives (O.3j.1) with $\\epsilon_x:=-\\beta^{-1}\\ln\\pi_x$ for any $\\beta>0$, and (O.3i.4) gives $\\sigma=0$. For the finest partition, (O.3j.1) with edge-dependent $\\beta_e>0$ holds exactly when $\\epsilon_x-\\epsilon_y$ has the sign of $\\ln(q_{xy}/q_{yx})$ on every edge, because $\\beta_e:=\\ln(q_{xy}/q_{yx})/(\\epsilon_x-\\epsilon_y)$ is then positive on unbalanced edges and balanced edges admit every $\\beta_e$ once $\\epsilon_x=\\epsilon_y$. A closed walk of the stated kind would give $\\epsilon_{x_0}\\ge\\epsilon_{x_1}\\ge\\cdots\\ge\\epsilon_{x_m}=\\epsilon_{x_0}$ with one strict step. If no such walk exists, contract the connected components of balanced edges to classes; no unbalanced edge joins two points of one class, since a balanced return path would close a forbidden walk, and the unbalanced edges, oriented from the end with the larger outgoing rate, form an acyclic digraph on classes. A topological order assigns class energies that decrease along every oriented edge, and the signs then match.\n\n(4) On the three-cycle put $x_1=\\epsilon_P-\\epsilon_V$, $x_2=\\epsilon_V-\\epsilon_D$ and $x_3=\\epsilon_D-\\epsilon_P$, so $x_1+x_2+x_3=0$, and the required signs are those of $\\ln(a/\\bar a)$, $\\ln(b/\\bar b)$ and $\\ln(c/\\bar c)$. Equation (O.3c0.1) makes at least one sign positive. Real numbers with prescribed signs, at least one positive, sum to zero exactly when at least one prescribed sign is negative: take $x_e=1/|\\mathcal P|$ on the set $\\mathcal P$ of positive edges, $x_e=-1/|\\mathcal N|$ on the set $\\mathcal N$ of negative edges and $x_e=0$ otherwise. If no ratio is below one, the finest partition has no realization, and by item 3 no partition has one. ∎\n\n### A work-driven, source-realized finite Markov arrow\n\n## 1. Exact question and scope\n\nIt remains to populate the MPU cycle-to-level and reservoir certificate and to realize the excluded chains with a work source. Theorems O.3i–O.3j prove the finite bidirectional path-arrow theorem, the heat-only quantum-jump representation and its complete reservoir criterion. The construction below supplies a finite physical source, exact gates and energy receipts for every chain in that original class, including its heat-only-excluded members, and a genuine temporally phased predictive-loop realization on the same source.\n\nThe domain is a finite irreducible generator $Q$, with $q_{xy}>0$ iff $q_{yx}>0$ for $x\\ne y$, finite rates, and a fixed partition of undirected edges into owners $E_{r}$. Missing edges have both rates zero. Strictly one-way edges are outside O.3i–j's original domain and outside this result. No experimental observation, autonomous drive, bounded-resource all-time simulation, original-complexity minimization, or persistent finite archive is asserted or made an extra gate.\n\nFor the irreducible singleton $Q=0$, the construction is the identity with no edge contacts, zero heat/work, and no arrow; formulas containing 1/L are used only when the edge set is nonempty.\n\nThe physical source class is precisely the positive-frequency, one-excitation oscillator-rail code, quadratic mode controls, nonnegative cross-Kerr controls, occupation instruments and calibrated prescribed-time service specified explicitly below. Sector-Gibbs inputs, supplied ready states, meters, timing and control work retain separate owners. All source parameters are specified before a run. This is an explicit construction within that physical-theory source class, not a derivation of the source laws from a rate matrix.\n\n## 2. Main result\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o3j1-complete-paired-work-driven-realization\"></a>**Theorem O.3j.1 (Complete Paired Work-Driven Realization).** Fix the above data, arbitrary finite level energies $\\varepsilon_{x}$, positive inverse temperatures $\\beta_{r}$ in inverse-energy units, and the source class of §1. For every finite horizon T and error tolerance $\\eta>0$, there is an explicitly specified finite source word such that:\n\n1. Its decoded completed-boundary event law converges to $Q$, including transition owners, jump sequence and jump times. There is a coupling with probability of unequal words at most $4L^{2}T\\delta$ and matched jump-time error at most $\\delta$, where $L=\\sum_e(q_e^{+}+q_e^{-})$, $\\delta\\le 1/L$ is the cell size and $T$ is a cell boundary. Arbitrary finite query times are made boundaries by subdivision. The limit therefore gives exactly the common path law required by O.3i–j.\n2. Each event $x\\to y$ has a unique owner $r$, heat $h_{xy}=\\beta_r^{-1}\\log(q_{xy}/q_{yx})$ delivered to that owner's thermal units, and externally supplied work $w_{xy}=\\epsilon_y-\\epsilon_x+h_{xy}$. Both quantities change sign on reversal. Every finite physical collision has this exact measured endpoint receipt, including zero for a null event. Controls are off at the endpoint, and all pointer/record energies used below are degenerate.\n3. In the limiting stationary phase law, the original path identity becomes\n\n   $$\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\Sigma_T &= \\log \\pi(x_0)-\\log \\pi(x_T)+\\sum_r \\beta_r Q_r,\\\\\n   W &= \\varepsilon_{x_T}-\\varepsilon_{x_0}+\\sum_r Q_r.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   $$\n\n   Consequently $\\sigma=\\sum_r\\beta_rJ_r^Q$, while $\\sum_rJ_r^Q=J^W$ generally differs from zero. A single-temperature implementation has $J^W=\\sigma/\\beta$. This is the term excluded from O.3j's heat-only account.\n4. The finite source has an exact positive quadratic/cross-Kerr compiler, not just a Lindblad formula. All finite resources, consumed thermal factors, drive work, records and possible resets have the explicit ownership record of §7.\n5. For the three-state class of O.3c0, the phase transition carries the actual forecast, subsequent acquisition/comparison, and feedback-controlled update of §6. That construction has the accepted persistence-$3/4$ environment, score $3/4$, independent-fair baseline $1/2$ and strict margin $1/8$. Finite tape boundaries have an explicit vanishing error. Thus $P,V,D$ name actual successive operational stages rather than relabeled empty levels.\n\nThe theorem concerns the original finite-to-limit realization standard already used for finite thermal erasure and Z.28.9. Every approximant is finite. The continuum apparatus, infinite tape, finite-temperature pure reset and infinite-time limit are not claimed to be one finite device.\n\n## 3. One edge: actual reservoir and driven collision\n\nOrient $e=(x,y)$ so $q^{+}=q_{xy}\\ge q^{-}=q_{yx}>0$. In a tie choose either orientation once. Set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\lambda_e=q^{+}+q^{-},\\quad d_e=\\beta_r^{-1} \\log(q^{+}/q^{-})\\ge 0,\\\\\n&p_0=q^{+}/\\lambda_e,\\quad p_1=q^{-}/\\lambda_e.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe bath element is a dual-rail one-excitation factor with physical energies $B_{e}$ and $B_e+d_e$, $B_e>0$, supplied in its sector-Gibbs state $\\tau_e=\\operatorname{diag}(p_0,p_1)$. The thermometer fixes $\\beta_{r}$ independently, and spectroscopy fixes the chosen gap $d_{e}$; indeed $p_1/p_0=\\exp(-\\beta_rd_e)$. At a tie $d_{e}=0$ and $\\tau_e=I/2$. No zero-temperature input or pure thermal bit is used.\n\nMore generally allow a finite work register $Z$, degenerate in the free energy, and a specified permutation $U_{e}$ on it. On the system code set $A_e=|y\\rangle\\langle x|\\otimes U_e$. The scalar case is $Z=\\{*\\}$. For $0\\le\\lambda_es\\le 1$ let $\\theta_e(s)=\\arcsin\\sqrt{\\lambda_es}$ and implement\n\n$$\nU_e(s)=\\exp[-i\\, \\theta_e(s)(A_e\\otimes|1\\rangle\\langle 0|+A_e^\\dagger\\otimes|0\\rangle\\langle 1|)].\n\\tag{W1}\n$$\n\nIt acts as a two-dimensional rotation on every disjoint pair $(x,z,0),(y,U_ez,1)$, and as identity elsewhere. After reading the bath's final occupation, a classical basis input remains a classical basis state. Its probabilities are exactly\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pr(x,z\\to y,U_e z) &= p_0 \\sin^{2}\\theta=s\\, q^{+},\\\\\n\\Pr(y,z\\to x,U_e^{-1} z) &= p_1 \\sin^{2}\\theta=s\\, q^{-}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{W2}\n$$\n\nAll other outcomes leave the system unchanged. Thus the reduced population kernel is exactly $I+sQ_e$; it is not a first-order identification of uncomputed dynamics. The corresponding joint event instrument is also specified by the same unitary and occupation readings. Measuring the initial bath occupation commutes with $\\tau_{e}$, so an optional two-point energy record does not change this law. The ready measurement pointers and their full records are charged; the bath input is not conditioned or selected according to the result.\n\nFor a forward event, the bath changes $0\\to 1$ and the system changes energy $\\epsilon_x\\to\\epsilon_y$. Therefore $Q_e=d_e$ and $W_e=\\epsilon_y-\\epsilon_x+d_e$. The reverse event has exactly the negatives; a null event has neither endpoint change. This is the inclusive two-point-energy work law of this specified driven pulse. Its mean equals the integral of $\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho(t)\\,\\partial_tH(t))$ over the full pulse, including switching and compensation, because the total closed source evolves unitarily and the control fields vanish at both ends. During a pulse energy can be temporarily stored in the controls. That action/power/stored-energy resource is not identified with net work.\n\nThe rate ratio is derived from the thermal occupation ratio in (W2). A level difference alone would instead require $\\epsilon_x-\\epsilon_y=d_e$. Work removes precisely that restriction. When an original record is heat-only-admissible, choosing the very $\\epsilon,\\beta$ supplied by O.3j gives $w_{xy}=0$ on every edge; hence the original thermal branch is recovered unchanged, although its control/clock service and transient action are still charged. Summing around a cycle gives $\\sum_{\\mathrm{cycle}}w=\\sum_{\\mathrm{cycle}}h$, so a positive circulation of log rate ratios is paid by a nonzero cycle work receipt.\n\n## 4. Exact finite physical compiler\n\nThe system has $N$ physical oscillator rails and exactly one excitation, with encoding $J|i\\rangle=c_i^\\dagger|\\mathrm{vac}\\rangle$. Set the free rail energy of $i=(x,z)$ to $E_{\\mathrm{base}}+\\epsilon_x$, with $E_{\\mathrm{base}}>-\\min\\epsilon_x$. Unused padded rails may be given any positive energy. This is a full positive quadratic Hamiltonian on Fock space; the code is an invariant fixed-number sector. All factors and baselines are recorded.\n\nIt is enough to compile the pair rotation between $|a,0\\rangle$ and $|b,1\\rangle$, where $a\\ne b$ are system-rail labels. Let $C_{b}$ be bath-$X$ controlled by system occupation $n_{b}$. Then\n\n$$\nC_b\\, [ \\exp(-i\\theta\\, X_{ab}\\otimes|0\\rangle\\langle 0|) ]\\, C_b\n$$\n\nis exactly the desired pair rotation and identity on the complementary joint basis; $X_{ab}=|a\\rangle\\langle b|+|b\\rangle\\langle a|$. This follows by conjugating the two basis vectors: $C_{b}$ sends $(a,0),(b,1)$ to $(a,0),(b,0)$.\n\n$C_{b}$ is $H_B\\,\\mathrm{CZ}_{b,B}\\,H_B$, where $\\mathrm{CZ}_{b,B}=\\exp(-i\\pi n_bn_{B1})$. Diagonalize $X_{ab}$ by a two-rail Hadamard $R_{ab}$. In that basis $X_{ab}=n_a-n_b$. The middle controlled rotation is a product of a local system phase $\\exp[-i\\theta(n_a-n_b)]$ and the two cross-Kerr phases $\\exp(i\\theta n_an_{B1})$, $\\exp(-i\\theta n_bn_{B1})$, conjugated by $R_{ab}$. Negative cross-Kerr angles are replaced by their nonnegative representatives modulo $2\\pi$. This is exact on the full integer-occupation Fock spectrum, not just approximately correct on the code. Thus each pair requires at most eleven logical local/Kerr groups before explicitly recorded drift-compensation slots. Disjoint pair generators commute, so the product over all $z$ gives (W1) exactly. A source permutation $U_{e}$ is expanded as its finite basis table; no unimplemented many-body port is assumed.\n\nEvery local rail unitary has an exact positive-duration, positive-Hamiltonian realization. For a fixed positive free angular-frequency matrix $D$, a desired $U$ and slot length $t>0$, choose $G\\ge 0$ with spectrum in $[0,2\\pi)$ such that $\\exp(-iG)=\\exp(iDt)U$. Choose a nonnegative smooth $f$ supported on the slot, $\\int f=1$ and $\\max f\\le 3/(2t)$. Then\n\n$$\nC(t')=\\hbar f(t') \\exp(-iDt')\\, G \\exp(iDt')\n$$\n\nis a positive quadratic control. Its full propagator is $U$ at the slot endpoint; its one-particle control-matrix norm (equivalently its norm on the one-excitation code) is $\\le 3\\pi\\hbar/t$, and its corresponding norm action is $\\le 2\\pi\\hbar$. The second-quantized operator is positive but unbounded in norm on the unrestricted full Fock space; no full-Fock norm bound is asserted. For a Kerr slot the positive interaction commutes with diagonal free drift; an allocated subsequent local slot cancels the known free phase using this same construction. Hence no negative, unbounded-below oscillator Hamiltonian or zero-duration gate is smuggled in. The command word, scalar reserve energies, compensated phases and actual slot lengths are recorded.\n\nA fully explicit conservative schedule for one pair has seven local groups and four Kerr groups, and adds one simultaneous two-factor drift-compensation slot after each Kerr group. Thus it has fifteen positive-duration slots, twenty-two one-factor local pulses and four Kerr pulses. In every local slot the other factor receives its exact identity endpoint via the same positive-log control, so its free drift is not ignored. For $d$ disjoint pairs in a collision of duration $\\tau$, use $15d$ equal slots. With nonnegative Kerr angles below $2\\pi$ and the local bound above, the total instantaneous control norm on the invariant code is at most $90\\pi d\\hbar/\\tau$, and the sum of one-factor/Kerr norm actions on the fixed-number code is at most $52\\pi d\\hbar$. These are conservative finite bounds; free baselines and any occupied independent pointers remain separate.\n\nAll groups fit in the declared physical collision slot by assigning each a positive share of its duration. The admitted source class has no fixed amplitude cap. Required amplitudes grow when slots shrink but are finite for each word and explicitly bounded by $\\text{angle}\\times\\hbar/\\text{slot length}$, with the local bound above. If a separate application imposes an amplitude limit, that is a new admission check; it is not silently evaded by changing the time unit.\n\n## 5. Clock, complete event law and convergence\n\nList the $m$ undirected edges. In each physical cell of duration $\\delta$ use the palindromic schedule $e_1,\\ldots,e_{m-1},e_m,e_{m-1},\\ldots,e_1$. Each outer occurrence has exposure $s=\\delta/2$ and the central $e_{m}$ has exposure $s=\\delta$. Thus every edge has total exposure $\\delta$. All $2m-1$ finite collision words are placed in equal positive physical subslots totaling exactly $\\delta$. Exposure determines the angle in (W1); it is not an additional uncharged duration. A fresh independent Gibbs factor is used once per occurrence. One-edge graphs use just the central occurrence.\n\nThe classical kernel at the cell boundary is the ordered product of $I+sQ_e$. Its formal limit is $\\exp(\\delta Q)$. The following path argument also controls the observed transitions and their energy marks.\n\nRepresent (W2) probabilistically by an independent Bernoulli opportunity of probability $\\lambda_{e}s$, followed by the full-exchange event with the thermal bit distribution. This is an exact representation of the already realized output instrument, not a demand for another uncharged physical random-number generator. The target CTMC has the exact uniformization representation with total opportunity rate $L=\\sum_e\\lambda_e$: choose $e$ with probability $\\lambda_e/L$ and use the same thermal-bit exchange rule. Its accepted transition rates are $q_{xy}$.\n\nIn one $\\delta$-cell both constructions have the same first-order categorical opportunity law: no opportunity with mass $1-L\\delta$, and one opportunity of type $e$ with mass $\\lambda_e\\delta$. For the CTMC the probability of at least two opportunities is at most $(L\\delta)^{2}/2$. Comparing each finite Bernoulli occurrence with a Poisson count of the same mean gives total variation at most $\\sum_{\\mathrm{occurrences}}(\\lambda_es)^{2}\\le(L\\delta)^{2}$; collisions and the Poisson model differ in opportunity ordering only in cells with at least two opportunities. Coupling the single-opportunity type and its thermal bit, and bounding both multiple-opportunity tails, gives the conservative one-cell bad probability $4(L\\delta)^{2}$. On its complement, every accepted event, its $U_{e}$, owner, heat and work mark agree, while its time differs by at most $\\delta$. Iterating the coupling over $T/\\delta$ cells proves Theorem O.3j.1(1). The same construction applies to the complete marked transcript, not only populations. Null opportunities may be discarded on both sides.\n\nThere is no total-variation claim between raw grid-atomic timestamps and absolutely continuous jump times. The statement is the explicit event-word/time coupling (and hence Skorokhod convergence), plus total-variation convergence for finite completed-boundary response queries. A word with a jump at a preselected exact observation time has limiting probability zero. The source's declared private intermediate-control grammar is retained.\n\nFor expectation claims, event count has a uniform exponential-moment bound. The CTMC opportunity count is Poisson(LT). For the finite word, conditional opportunity probabilities obey\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{E} \\exp(u N)\\le\\exp[LT(\\exp(u)-1)]\\quad (u\\ge 0).\n$$\n\nTherefore jump counts are uniformly integrable. Since every event mark is bounded by $H=\\max_e d_e$ for heat and $B=\\max_{(x,y)}|\\epsilon_y-\\epsilon_x+h_{xy}|$ for work, the coupling proves convergence of all first moments as well. A direct conservative finite bound, useful when a numerical energy tolerance is specified, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\lvert\\mathbb{E}Q_\\delta-\\mathbb{E}Q\\rvert\\le 12HL^{2}T\\delta(1+LT), \\\\\n\\lvert\\mathbb{E}W_\\delta-\\mathbb{E}W\\rvert\\le 12BL^{2}T\\delta(1+LT).\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{W3}\n$$\n\nOne derivation is to compare one-cell expected rewards with $\\delta$ times the instantaneous reward-rate function. Its magnitude is $\\le HL$ (or $BL$); within-cell population changes have $\\ell^1$ norm $\\le 2L\\delta$, yielding a $\\le 4HL^{2}\\delta^{2}$ local remainder. The event coupling bounds the start-of-cell population $\\ell^1$ error by $8L^{2}t\\delta$. Summing local remainders and the start errors gives at most $4HL^{2}T\\delta+8HL^{3}T^{2}\\delta$, below (W3). The same argument gives the work bound. Individual reservoir owners use their own zero-extended reward function.\n\nThe palindrome makes the finite sequence of collision kernels invariant under order reversal of a complete cell. Each nonnull transition has ratio $q_{xy}/q_{yx}$ and each reversed null transition has the same probability. With a phase initial law $\\pi$, the finite marked-path ratio against the reversed schedule is exactly the endpoint $\\pi$ ratio plus $\\sum\\log(q_{xy}/q_{yx})$. In the stationary $Q$ limit this is O.3i's same-process reversal. One may also prove the limiting identity directly from the path theorem of Theorem O.3i. No finite approximant is mislabeled exactly stationary under $Q$'s $\\pi$ when its product kernel does not preserve $\\pi$.\n\n## 6. An actual predictive P–V–D sector on the same physical source\n\n### 6.1 The operational maps\n\nUse the accepted finite-feedback source's logical registers and eight-CNOT round, transported by an explicit computational-basis encoding into the physical rail code. This imports its gate truth table and seed law, not its forty-spin Hamiltonian, currents, spatial interpretation or unproved minimality. The work carrier has model M, prediction/discrepancy $P_{b}$, acquired outcome I, environment tape Y, prediction archives $A_{r}$ and feedback archives $F_{r}$. Distinguish phase P from the bit register $P_{b}$.\n\nFor each round $r$, start with $M=Y_r$ and $P_b=I=A_r=F_r=0$. The independent prepared noise bit $N_{r}$ has $\\Pr(N_r=1)=1/4$ and $Y_{r+1}=Y_r\\oplus N_{r}$. $Y$ at the first tape site is fair. All noise/environment bits are prepared before the dynamical run; prediction gates have no input from the future $Y_{r+1}$.\n\nDecorate the three forward phase transitions by these actual gates, in temporal order:\n\n- $P\\to V$: CNOT $M\\to P_b$, then CNOT $P_b\\to A_r$.\n- $V\\to D$: CNOT $Y_{r+1}\\to I$, then CNOT $I\\to P_b$, then CNOT $P_b\\to F_r$.\n- $D\\to P$: CNOT $F_r\\to M$, then CNOT $F_r\\to P_b$, then CNOT $M\\to I$; advance the round index.\n\nThe reverse phase transition applies the inverse of the corresponding complete gate word. If a logical forward rate is smaller than its reverse, orient that thermal contact oppositely in §3 and use the inverse gate word as its bath-exciting direction; the logical forward/reverse rates and stages themselves are not changed. At the $P\\to V$ endpoint $P_b=A_r=Y_r$ before any acquisition of $Y_{r+1}$. At $V\\to D$, $I=Y_{r+1}$ and $P_b=F_r=Y_r\\oplus Y_{r+1}$, with $M$ still $Y_{r}$. At $D\\to P$, $M=Y_{r+1}$ and $P_b=I=0$, with $A_r,F_r$ retained. Thus the model changes according to the actual discrepancy. The exact round map is\n\n$$\n(m,0,0,y,0,0)_{M,P_b,I,Y,A,F} \\to (y,0,0,y,m,m \\oplus y).\n\\tag{W4}\n$$\n\nThis checks Definitions 4–6 directly: model distinction, a forecast physically present before acquisition, comparison, and outcome-conditioned model update. The nondegenerate environmental law supplies an ND-RID interaction, with transformation $M'=M\\oplus F$. Its expectation score is $\\Pr(A_r=Y_{r+1})=3/4$. The matched independent fair predictor scores $1/2$, so margin $1/8$ is strictly met. An uncomputed conditional-law observer or logging-only apparatus would not supply (W4).\n\nThe result establishes the relevant operational predictive-loop sector. It does not prove a global shortest-program/minimality theorem of Definition 23, any original $C_{P}$ value, SPAP universality, or a new PCE optimizer. Those are separate results, and no such assertion is needed to populate O's nominated P–V–D source certificate.\n\n### 6.2 Finite reversible tape and its exact boundary account\n\nFor $K\\ge 1$ take rounds $r=-K,\\ldots,K$, a finite Markov environment tape $Y_{-K},\\ldots,Y_{K+1}$, one $A_r,F_r$ pair per round, and a controller with $3(2K+1)$ positions. At ordinary positions its edge unitaries are exactly the three words above. The final closing edge is instead the inverse of the entire preceding prefix, so the complete finite-cycle holonomy is identity. The seam is explicitly labeled NONPREDICTIVE; no correct predictive response is claimed after reaching it.\n\nPrepare the product seed law (fair initial Y, independent Bernoulli-1/4 increments and ready logical registers), compute the tape by the CNOT chain, initialize M to the first Y, and execute the first K full rounds. This puts the source at the center with genuine prepared past archives and ready future archives. For initial phase V or D, execute the corresponding central prefix as well. Choose the initial phase with O.3c0's stationary law $\\pi$, independently of the environment seeds. All this finite preparation is owned and paid; it is not a fictitious initial-state change.\n\nFactor the carrier as the three-state phase times the finite work register containing the round index and payload. Use exactly three collective contacts (W1). For PV and VD, $U_{e}$ is the direct sum over round indices of that round’s specified gate word. For DP, $U_{e}$ shifts the round index cyclically and applies its update word, with the explicitly stated inverse-prefix word at the seam. Each is a full finite permutation of the same work basis. Thus $A_e=|y\\rangle\\langle x|\\otimes U_e$ is precisely the partial isometry already compiled in §4, and each collective contact uses one Gibbs bit for all round copies. Adjacent backward transitions apply the exact inverses, so a return to an earlier phase restores exactly its earlier named register values. The payload remains the appropriate prefix of this one prepared source path until the seam is encountered. Predictions and acquired values therefore persist at real completed-boundary observation points and between collisions. Internal compiler pulses have the already admitted private cut.\n\nThere is exactly one available forward and one backward controller edge at every position, with the phase's nominated rates. Consequently the projection onto $P,V,D$ is strongly lumpable and is exactly the desired three-state chain in the continuous collision limit, for every payload input and every $K$, including at the seam. The seam affects predictive meaning, not the phase-rate or heat/work receipts. All copies of a phase edge have the same owner, gap and phase-energy change. The entire controller/payload basis is encoded on finitely many rails; padding to dimension $8^k$ gives the admitted finite MPU-network code. This supplies a concrete common-carrier dictionary rather than equating two differently named sources.\n\nStarting at the center, encountering the exceptional seam requires at least K transitions (a deliberately conservative bound). For any T the probability is at most\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pr(N_T\\ge K) &\\le \\inf_{u>0} \\exp[LT(\\exp(u)-1)-uK],\\\\\n&\\le (eLT/K)^K \\quad \\text{when } K>LT.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{W5}\n$$\n\nHere $L$ is the sum over the three collective phase contacts, independent of $K$, and the same bound applies to the finite collision opportunity count. Choose $K$ first so (W5)$\\le\\eta/2$, then $\\delta$ so $4L^{2}T\\delta\\le\\eta/2$ (and any energy tolerance through W3). Each choice is finite and effective. This produces the full actual predictive source response for the requested finite horizon, with a justified limit as $K$ increases and $\\delta$ decreases. Perpetual fresh randomness and permanent finite memory are not prerequisites or conclusions.\n\nFor a finite starting phase and path, the phase entropy identity in §2 remains exactly the original coarse event identity. It is not asserted to be the entire fine-grained entropy production of the tape, initial preparation and event archive. Those additional resources and their correlations are counted in §7. In particular a sharp initially localized round controller has a separate entropy change even though its energy is degenerate. Ignoring it would be wrong; its presence does not change either the measured phase heat/work identity or O.3i's likelihood calculation.\n\n## 7. Exhaustive resource and thermodynamic ownership\n\nFor a horizon $T$, cell count $n$ and $m$-edge scalar implementation, the fresh collision-factor count is $n(2m-1)$. For the decorated P–V–D controller, §6 constructs exactly three collective partial-isometry contacts, so $m=3$ and the count is $5n$, independent of tape length; the much larger number of pair rotations in each exact compiled contact is fully charged. All unused attempts are still charged. Every pulse has its finite rail count, angle, duration, amplitude bound, baseline energy, norm action and signed endpoint energy receipt.\n\n- SYSTEM: all phase, model, prediction, acquired-outcome, controller and work-register rails, the fixed-number encoding and calibrated $\\varepsilon_{x}$. Logical payload/round labels have zero relative free energy, but not zero capacity or preparation cost.\n- ENVIRONMENT/PREPARATION: every initial classical seed, ready bit, particle, tape/archive preparation gate, sampled phase, and preparation label or purification. No fresh randomness is claimed without its source.\n- THERM_r_FRESH: the temperature standard, independent thermometer, all product sector-Gibbs inputs, $d_{e}$, baselines and preparation error. Each is independent of retained inputs and previous spent outputs.\n- THERM_r_SPENT: every output thermal factor, its heat receipt and all correlations. Reusing one without reinitialization changes the construction.\n- DRIVE/CLOCK: the prescribed-time physical field source, work supply/storage, complete compensated pulse program, physical timestamps, peak amplitudes, action and transient stored energies. Its net supplied work is the inclusive endpoint work, not zero by convention.\n- METER/ARCHIVE: occupation instruments, ready pointers, initial/final bath-energy records if requested, decoded event/phase/prediction/feedback records and all calibration records. Pointer Hamiltonians are degenerate. Record capacity and ready-state supply are charged independently of net work.\n- RESET: only explicitly requested returns or renewal strokes, with their physical input/output resources, residual errors, heat and work. No archive, seed or bath correlation is silently erased here.\n\nFor the unmeasured source plus all fresh bath factors, unitarity and an initially product Gibbs bath give the exact identity\n\n$$\n\\Delta S_{\\mathrm{system}}+\\sum_r\\beta_r Q_r=I(\\text{system}:\\text{all baths})_{\\mathrm{out}}+D(\\rho_{\\mathrm{baths,out}}\\Vert\\bigotimes_j\\tau_j)\\ge 0.\n\\tag{W6}\n$$\n\nTo derive (W6), expand $\\log(\\otimes\\tau_j)=-\\sum_j\\beta_{r(j)}H_j-\\sum_j\\log Z_{j}$. This gives $D(\\rho_{\\mathrm{baths,out}}\\|\\otimes\\tau_j)=\\sum_r\\beta_rQ_r-\\Delta S_{\\mathrm{baths}}$. Initially system and baths are independent; joint unitary entropy conservation gives $\\Delta S_{\\mathrm{system}}+\\Delta S_{\\mathrm{baths}}=I_{\\mathrm{out}}$. Combining the two equalities proves (W6), including correlated spent outputs and multiple temperatures.\n\n“System” here includes the full controller and payload, and any initially pure pointer subsequently coupled to it; the input product assumption is with the fresh baths. Bath-bath correlations are included inside the relative entropy. This is the complete finite second-law balance, not an assertion that a single outgoing qubit is already equilibrated. If meters dephase retained pointers, include their dilation/output environment rather than discarding its entropy. Classical occupation reading is covered by the admitted instrument and charged output record.\n\nFor a reset, either retain the old register and consume charged fresh ready supply, or invoke the conditional thermal-return construction of Corollary 7.6r.1 with every retained side record and its finite error/excess heat. Exact pure return is only that justified limit. No reset is needed to prove a finite observed source trajectory. No new free Landauer erasure is introduced.\n\nThe coarse path $\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{phase}}$ and fine identity (W6) are deliberately distinguished. The former has exactly O's event alphabet and heat marks; the latter audits every additional physical resource. Neither gains entropy by deleting a spent owner, and no source-partition heat is counted twice.\n\n## 8. Fully populated heat-only-excluded instance\n\nChoose a physical time standard $t_{0}$, independently fixed $\\beta>0$, and $\\Delta=\\beta^{-1}\\ln 2$. Set $\\epsilon_P=\\epsilon_V=\\epsilon_D=0$ with a positive common rail baseline, and\n\n$$\na=b=c=2/t_0,\\quad \\bar a=\\bar b=\\bar c=1/t_0.\n$$\n\nEvery phase edge uses a dual-rail bath with gap $\\Delta$, Gibbs probabilities $(2/3,1/3)$, and angle $\\arcsin\\sqrt{3s/t_0}$. The three-edge cell is PV,VD,DP,VD,PV with exposures $\\delta/2,\\delta/2,\\delta,\\delta/2,\\delta/2$. It uses five fresh thermal bits, with all positive-duration exact gates in its physical $\\delta$ seconds.\n\nFor every forward event $Q=W=\\Delta$; every reverse event $Q=W=-\\Delta$. The stationary phase law is uniform, $J=1/(3t_0)$, affinity $A=3\\ln 2$, $\\sigma=\\ln 2/t_0$, and bath heat/work power $\\Delta/t_0$. A net forward record circuit carries $\\text{heat}=\\text{work}=3\\Delta$, irrespective of backtracking, and the inherited retained-clock increment is $A/\\ln 2=3$. O.3j forbids every heat-only realization of this very chain. The displayed positive work is its actual missing source term, not a changed chain.\n\nDecorate these same physical phase transitions by §6's maps. At every valid prediction/verification/update endpoint their actual register values are those in (W4); no standalone second device is used to supply the semantics. For any chosen T, K and $\\delta$ are selected through (W3),(W5), with all finite tape and bath costs retained.\n\n## 9. Scope of the result\n\nThe universal construction covers exactly O.3i–j's finite bidirectional domain and every originally excluded heat-only member. The explicit register map covers O.3c0's nominated predictive phase family with a real source prediction/verification/feedback task. The rate classification, heat-only no-go and path-arrow mathematics are those of Theorems O.3i–O.3j. Together they supply the work-source and cycle-to-level/reservoir components of the pathwise thermodynamic arrow.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o3k-record-circuit-realization-of-the-retained-entropic-clock\"></a>**Theorem O.3k (Record-Circuit Realization of the Retained Entropic Clock).** Under Principle O.3c0 write $k_{xy}$ for the jump rate from $x$ to $y$ and $k_x:=\\sum_{y\\ne x}k_{xy}$. Start the chain at $P$ and lift it to $\\ell(t)\\in\\mathbb Z$ with $\\ell(0)=0$: each forward jump $P\\to V$, $V\\to D$ or $D\\to P$ adds $1$ and each reverse jump subtracts $1$, so the state is $P$, $V$ or $D$ according as $\\ell(t)\\equiv0$, $1$ or $2$ modulo $3$. Let $\\tau_0:=0$ and $\\tau_n:=\\inf\\{t:\\ell(t)=3n\\}$, let the record circuit $e_n$ be the path segment on $[\\tau_{n-1},\\tau_n]$, and let $n(t):=\\max\\{n:\\tau_n\\le t\\}$. For a jump word $w=(x_0,\\ldots,x_N)$ put\n$$\np(w):=\\prod_{j=1}^N\\frac{k_{x_{j-1}x_j}}{k_{x_{j-1}}},\n\\qquad\nw^\\dagger:=(x_N,\\ldots,x_0),\n$$\nthe probability that the chain started at $x_0$ makes the jumps of $w$, equal to its path density integrated over the holding durations, and let $\\Omega_+$ be the countable set of jump words from $P$ whose lifted level first reaches $3$ at the last jump. Register the finite retained event alphabet $\\mathcal E_R:=\\{\\rho\\}$, where $\\rho$ is the symbol of a completed record circuit and $\\rho^\\dagger$ its reversed symbol; the ledger $\\mathcal L(t):=(\\rho,\\ldots,\\rho)$ with $n(t)$ entries, the $j$th entry carrying the path segment $e_j$ as unretained ancestry; the matched projected weights and increment of Definition O.3f,\n$$\nP_F(\\rho):=\\sum_{w\\in\\Omega_+}p(w),\n\\qquad\nP_R(\\rho^\\dagger):=\\sum_{w\\in\\Omega_+}p(w^\\dagger),\n\\qquad\n\\sigma_R^{\\mathrm{ECF}}(\\rho):=\\log\\frac{P_F(\\rho)}{P_R(\\rho^\\dagger)};\n$$\nand the guarantee-level subset $\\mathcal G_R:=\\mathcal E_R$ if $\\mathcal A\\ge\\ln2$ and $\\mathcal G_R:=\\varnothing$ otherwise. Let $J>0$, $\\mathcal A>0$ and $\\dot\\sigma=J\\mathcal A$ be the current, affinity and entropy-production rate of Theorem O.3c0. Then:\n\n1. Almost surely every $\\tau_n$ is finite, $\\ell(t)/t\\to3J$ and $n(t)/t\\to J$.\n2. The jump word $w$ of every record circuit satisfies $p(w)=e^{\\mathcal A}p(w^\\dagger)$, so the path log-likelihood ratio is the same constant $\\mathcal A$ on every circuit. The projected weights are $P_F(\\rho)=1$ and $P_R(\\rho^\\dagger)=e^{-\\mathcal A}$. Hence $\\sigma_R^{\\mathrm{ECF}}(\\rho)=\\mathcal A$, $\\mathcal T_R(n)=n\\mathcal A/\\ln2$, and $\\mathcal T_R(n)\\ln2$ equals the path entropy production $\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{tot}}$ of (O.3c0.5) evaluated on $[0,\\tau_n]$.\n3. The ledger is append-only: $\\mathcal L(s)$ is a prefix of $\\mathcal L(t)$ for $s\\le t$, and later excursions below level $3n$ remove no entry. The ancestry verifier accepts a finite jump segment as the ancestry of the $n$th entry exactly when it starts at the lifted level $3(n-1)$ reached at the previous record and ends at its first arrival at $3n$.\n4. $\\mathcal T_R$ increases by $\\mathcal A/\\ln2>0$ at each record time and is constant between record times. The increment is at least one clock unit exactly when $\\mathcal A\\ge\\ln2$; then $\\rho\\in\\mathcal G_R$ is the retained projection of a complete forward update cycle, and its matched weights satisfy (O.3a.1) with $h_{\\min}=\\ln2$.\n5. Null and label-swap controls: if $abc=\\bar a\\bar b\\bar c$, the same construction gives $p(w)=p(w^\\dagger)$ on every record circuit, increment $0$ and $\\mathcal T_R\\equiv0$. If the orientation labels are exchanged, the exchanged record circuits are the first-passage segments of $\\ell$ from $-3(n-1)$ to $-3n$; with $\\Omega_-$ the jump words from $P$ whose lifted level first reaches $-3$ at the last jump, their symbol $\\rho^\\dagger$ has projected increment $\\log\\bigl(\\sum_{w\\in\\Omega_-}p(w)/\\sum_{w\\in\\Omega_-}p(w^\\dagger)\\bigr)=-\\mathcal A<0$, and almost surely only finitely many occur.\n6. The inter-record times $\\tau_n-\\tau_{n-1}$ are independent and identically distributed with mean $1/J$. With $\\tau_R^{\\mathrm{cyc}}:=\\ln2/\\dot\\sigma$, the calibrated representative (O.3f.4) satisfies $t_R^{\\mathrm{ECF}}(n(t))/t\\to1$ almost surely.\n\n*Proof.* (1) Let $n_1,n_2,n_3$ be the net forward traversal counts of the edges $\\{P,V\\}$, $\\{V,D\\}$ and $\\{D,P\\}$ up to time $t$. Flow conservation at each state gives $|n_i-n_j|\\le1$, and $\\ell(t)=n_1+n_2+n_3$, so $|\\ell(t)-3n_1|\\le2$. By the ergodic theorem for finite irreducible Markov jump processes the number of jumps $x\\to y$ up to $t$, divided by $t$, tends almost surely to $\\pi_xk_{xy}$; hence $n_1/t\\to\\pi_Pa-\\pi_V\\bar a=J$ and $\\ell(t)/t\\to3J>0$. Since $\\ell$ moves by $\\pm1$ and tends to $+\\infty$, it reaches every level $3n$. The running maximum of $\\ell$ has the same linear growth, and $n(t)=\\lfloor\\max_{s\\le t}\\ell(s)/3\\rfloor$ gives $n(t)/t\\to J$.\n\n(2) A word $w\\in\\Omega_+$ begins and ends at $P$ and has lifted displacement $+3$. For each integer $m$, the net number of upward crossings of the gate between $m$ and $m+1$ along $w$ is $1$ for $0\\le m<3$ and $0$ otherwise, and the gates with $m\\equiv0,1,2$ modulo $3$ are the edges $\\{P,V\\}$, $\\{V,D\\}$ and $\\{D,P\\}$. In\n$$\n\\frac{p(w)}{p(w^\\dagger)}\n=\\prod_{j=1}^N\\frac{k_{x_{j-1}x_j}}{k_{x_jx_{j-1}}}\\,\\prod_{j=1}^N\\frac{k_{x_j}}{k_{x_{j-1}}}\n$$\nthe second product telescopes to $k_{x_N}/k_{x_0}=1$, and in the first, backtracking pairs of jumps cancel, leaving $(a/\\bar a)(b/\\bar b)(c/\\bar c)=e^{\\mathcal A}$. For every set $W$ of words with $p(w)=e^{s}p(w^\\dagger)$ on $W$ and $0<\\sum_{w\\in W}p(w)<\\infty$, summation gives $\\sum_{w\\in W}p(w^\\dagger)=e^{-s}\\sum_{w\\in W}p(w)$, so the projected log-ratio equals $s$. No word of $\\Omega_+$ is a proper prefix of another, so the events that the chain begins with the jumps of $w$, $w\\in\\Omega_+$, are disjoint, and their union is $\\{\\tau_1<\\infty\\}$; item 1 gives $P_F(\\rho)=1$, hence $P_R(\\rho^\\dagger)=e^{-\\mathcal A}$ and $\\sigma_R^{\\mathrm{ECF}}(\\rho)=\\mathcal A$. By the strong Markov property and translation invariance of the lift, the jump word of every $e_n$ has law $p$ on $\\Omega_+$, so every entry carries the same symbol and increment. The same crossing count on $[0,\\tau_n]$ evaluates $\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{tot}}$ as $n\\mathcal A$, the endpoint term vanishing because both endpoints are $P$.\n\n(3) The record times increase strictly, and $e_n$ is determined by the path up to $\\tau_n$, so later motion changes no registered entry; the verifier reads the lifted increments of the finite jump sequence. (4) Item 2 gives the increment at each record time, and $\\mathcal T_R$ changes only at record times. A record circuit traverses each edge once net in the forward direction, and when $\\mathcal A\\ge\\ln2$ the positive matched weights $P_F(\\rho)=1$ and $P_R(\\rho^\\dagger)=e^{-\\mathcal A}$ give (O.3a.1) with $h_{\\min}=\\ln2$. (5) For $abc=\\bar a\\bar b\\bar c$ the ratio of item 2 is $1$ on every word of $\\Omega_+$, a set of positive total weight, so the projected increment is $0$. After exchanging the labels the lifted coordinate is $-\\ell(t)\\to-\\infty$ by item 1, so it has only finitely many first arrivals at positive multiples of $3$. Each exchanged circuit has, up to translation, a jump word in $\\Omega_-$; the crossing count with every gate reversed gives $p(w)=e^{-\\mathcal A}p(w^\\dagger)$ on $\\Omega_-$, and $0<\\sum_{w\\in\\Omega_-}p(w)\\le1$, so the projected increment is $-\\mathcal A$. (6) At $\\tau_{n-1}$ the chain is at $P$, and by the strong Markov property and translation invariance of the lift the later lifted increments are independent of the past and distributed as from time $0$; hence the inter-record times are independent and identically distributed. The strong law of large numbers gives $\\tau_n/n\\to\\mathbb E\\tau_1\\in(0,\\infty]$ almost surely, while $n/\\tau_n=n(\\tau_n)/\\tau_n\\to J$ by item 1; therefore $\\mathbb E\\tau_1=1/J$. Finally $t_R^{\\mathrm{ECF}}(n(t))/t=\\tau_R^{\\mathrm{cyc}}n(t)\\mathcal A/(t\\ln2)=n(t)/(Jt)\\to1$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o3l-a-finite-adaptive-mpu-observer-retained-algebra-and-record-circuit-clock\"></a>**Theorem O.3l (A Finite Adaptive MPU Observer, Retained Algebra and Record-Circuit Clock).**\n\nUse the exact nominal source of Definition 2.4.1g with the byte-exact source-relative hierarchy of Definition 2.4.1i. The following complete existing construction populates $q=0$ and its finite observer grammar. Its supplied rate/time and stochastic/preparation primitives remain premises; the same constructor and attained qualifying minimum are used throughout.\n\n### A finite adaptive observer with a retained record algebra and clock\n\n## 1. Result, diagnosis and scope\n\n**Result.** The common source $R_{14}$ of Definition 2.4.1g and Theorem 2.4.1h contains an actual two-cycle adaptive predictor, a finite physical record instrument, a finite ancestry verifier and a level-0 retained-response algebra. Its live motor is exactly coupled to the three-state $2\\kappa/\\kappa$ process up to its declared resource stop. Every completed first-passage circuit has matched retained log-likelihood increment $3\\ln2$. The exact declared task accuracy is greater than $0.6299309665>5/8$. The shared FND result supplies the same-presentation eight-bit qualifying minimum, hence model-relative Definition-23 MPU admission of this entire instrumented source.\n\nThis is a **completed nominated finite-source construction**, not a uniqueness theorem for the manuscript's unspecified baseline or a claim identifying nature's fundamental MPU. It uses a finite explicitly driven collision/readout model with prepared clean cells, a calibrated rate unit and a finite observation schedule. These are physical-model premises. It does not derive exact open-system Markov dynamics from a finite isolated Hamiltonian bath, exact pure cells from finite-temperature equilibrium, or a universal time scale.\n\nThe central bridge is the conjunction of adaptive computation, backtracking, irreversible retention and finite observation. A bare biased ring supplies none of those. Here reversible data gates make backtracking harmless to the computation; a separately charged record instrument preserves old outcomes; immutable scheduled snapshots make the *deterministic-time* algebra inclusion genuine; the same concrete source participates in the FND minimum.\n\n### Exact original terminal predicate\n\n### Precise admission boundary\n\nThe finite stopped apparatus is **not** an autonomous three-state chain for all time. Its phase plus an absorbing failure flag need not be Markov, since stopping depends on accumulated jumps and displacement. Conditioning on survival need not preserve the rates either. What is proved is an exact, unconditioned coupling to the autonomous O.3c0 chain through a stopping time. The finite device supplies at most two forward circuits; no finite-reservoir stationary-current or $t\\to\\infty$ claim is made.\n\nThe deterministic-time observer grammar has the finite calibrated schedule $0,T/2,T$, with $T=14/\\kappa$. All earlier snapshot values, including earlier availability, remain physically stored. There is no claim that a finite record contains the entire continuous-time path $\\sigma$ algebra or every unsampled arrival time.\n\n## 2. Shared source and response convention\n\nUse the common program machine, canonical code and complete physical inventory of Definition 2.4.1g and Theorem 2.4.1h, with Definition 2.4.1i’s byte-exact hierarchy correction. The full finite update semantics, stopping order, protected record operations, snapshot instrument and ancestry verifier are specified below; they introduce no second inventory.\n\nFor the family $R_h$, set\n\n$$\nD=2h+2,\\qquad d=h+1,\\qquad T=h/\\kappa.\n$$\n\nThe motor carrier is the complete rooted binary tree of depth $D$. A node is a finite binary word $v$, $0\\le |v|\\le D$. Initially $v$ is uniform on the depth-$d$ fiber. Every undirected bare-motor edge has rate $\\kappa>0$ in either direction. Define the lifted position $\\ell=|v|-d$ and the phase $P,V,D$ by $\\ell\\bmod3=0,1,2$, respectively. The operational forward direction is increasing depth.\n\nThe source contains the historian, predictor, protected archive, readout controller, finite prepared collision cells, energy source/receiver counters and sampling controller. A live run stops at the first of:\n\n1. jump number $h$;\n2. first arrival at $\\ell=6$;\n3. the deadline $T$.\n\nWhen the $h$-th jump also reaches 6, completion takes precedence. At the final deadline the timeout flag is set before the final snapshot. The apparatus maintains existing records and runs its remaining scheduled readout even after the motor has stopped.\n\nThe motor itself has $2^{D+1}-1$ classical basis states. For $h=14$, this is **$2^{31}-1=2,147,483,647$** states. The implicit word-address representation used by the executable checks does not remove those alternatives from the declared physical carrier. This is a finite formal construction, not a proposal for a cheap laboratory apparatus.\n\n### Independent finite environment\n\nPrepare $Y_0$ fair and $Z_1,Z_2$ independently with $\\Pr(Z_i=1)=1/4$. Put\n\n$$\nY_1=Y_0\\oplus Z_1,\\qquad Y_2=Y_1\\oplus Z_2.\n$$\n\nThe declared source primitive prepares each Bernoulli-$1/4$ bit in its exact rational state. A separate fair-bit/AND synthesis is possible but is not used to hide extra uncharged cells in this code. All source noise is supplied once; there is no unbounded random-bit tape. The tree source, environment source and fair fallback bit are independent. The environment values are physically held in a private source register; the forecast grammar cannot read a future $Y_n$. Thus the prediction concerns the outcome of a future permitted observation, even though the finite source has a ready-state description.\n\nInitial model memory is $m=Y_0$. Four scratch bits $f_1,e_1,f_2,e_2$ start at zero. Future-source bits do not feed the motor rates, stopping rule, forecast gate or snapshot schedule.\n\n### Forward data gates\n\nFor $n=1,2$, the edge at lifted depth $3(n-1)$ applies\n\n$$\nG_{P,n}: f_n\\leftarrow f_n\\oplus m;\n$$\n\nthe edge at depth $3(n-1)+1$ applies\n\n$$\nG_{V,n}: e_n\\leftarrow e_n\\oplus f_n\\oplus Y_n;\n$$\n\nand the edge at depth $3(n-1)+2$ applies\n\n$$\nG_{D,n}: m\\leftarrow m\\oplus e_n.\n$$\n\nEach map is its own inverse. The parent traversal applies the inverse of the gate belonging to the same edge. Both children at a fixed depth apply the same data gate. All other lifted-depth edges apply the identity to these predictor bits. Negative excursions are reversible preparatory motion, not separately claimed completed predictive cycles.\n\nThe physical grammar includes read-only initial/source audits, declared typed gate controls, the bounded run, the three scheduled snapshot responses and finite ancestry verification. The level-$q$ observer subgrammar is narrower: it sees the stored scheduled snapshots and their finite verification verdict, not arbitrary live pointer reads, private shadows, raw branch history or unsampled wall-clock timestamps. These restrictions are actual input-wire restrictions of the declared instrument.\n\n## 3. Computation survives every backtrack\n\n### Lemma O.3l.1: depth-only data action\n\nFor any nearest-neighbor lifted walk from 0 to $l$, the composed data permutation is the ordered product of the gates on the unique direct depth path from 0 to $l$. This holds for every source value and every branch-label sequence.\n\n**Proof.** A walk on the integer line with an immediate reversal traverses one edge and then its inverse, so the two adjacent gate maps cancel. Every nondirect finite walk contains such a reversal: otherwise all increments would have the same sign. Repeated deletion terminates at the unique monotone path with the same endpoints. The argument is unaffected by tree branches because the gate depends only on depth. It is an equality of permutations, not an argument about probable trajectories. ∎\n\n### Lemma O.3l.2: a first-passage circuit is a genuine adaptive loop\n\nAt the first arrival at $3n$,\n\n$$\nf_n=Y_{n-1},\\qquad e_n=Y_{n-1}\\oplus Y_n=Z_n,\n\\qquad m=Y_n.\n$$\n\nThe first arrival at $3n-2$ commits the forecast $Y_{n-1}$; the first permitted exposure of $Y_n$ occurs later, at arrival at $3n-1$. No subsequent backtrack changes the earlier committed forecast.\n\n**Proof.** The direct three-gate computation starts with $m=Y_{n-1}$, $f_n=e_n=0$. The displayed identities follow by substitution. Lemma O.3l.1 transfers them to any first-passage path. Nearest-neighbor order forces $3n-2$ before $3n-1$ before $3n$. The protected archive writes on first high-water arrivals and never rewrites an already written cell. Any repeated forecast-stage visit has the same canonical data value by Lemma O.3l.1; the forecast is not selected retrospectively from multiple attempts. The predictor has no input wire from the protected archive or historian. Thus any observer knowledge obtained after a failed excursion cannot alter a later predictor computation. ∎\n\nThe memory distinguishes the two prior outcomes, the prediction gate uses that memory, verification computes the discrepancy from the newly exposed outcome, and update uses that discrepancy to change the model. These are Definitions 4 and 5's actual operations, not names assigned to otherwise content-free phases.\n\n## 4. Actual predictive qualification and the common minimum\n\nThe task is to predict $Y_2$ with the second committed forecast if both cycles finish by the resource stop. Otherwise the apparatus outputs its independently prepared fair fallback. The score is binary accuracy; the matched random baseline is $1/2$; the strict margin is $\\epsilon_{\\rm acc}=1/8$.\n\nLet $S_h$ be successful completion and $s_h=\\Pr(S_h)$. Since rates, stopping and timing depend only on the motor/control path, $S_h$ is independent of all environment bits. Lemma O.3l.2 therefore gives\n\n$$\nA(R_h)=s_h\\Pr(Y_1=Y_2)+(1-s_h)/2\n      =\\frac12+\\frac{s_h}{4}.\n$$\n\nThus qualification is precisely $s_h>1/2$, together with the already proved full-loop condition and the FND full-realizability domain.\n\n### Exact finite success bound\n\nThe interior jump chain has increments $+1,-1$ with probabilities $2/3,1/3$. If $a_j$ is the probability of first reaching 6 on jump $j$, elementary dynamic programming, or the complete stopped-word partition, gives\n\n$$\n(a_6,a_8,a_{10},a_{12},a_{14})\n=\\left(\\frac{64}{729},\\frac{256}{2187},\\frac{256}{2187},\n\\frac{56320}{531441},\\frac{146432}{1594323}\\right).\n$$\n\nEvery other $a_j$ for $j\\le14$ is zero. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\Pr(\\text{hit 6 by jump 14})=\\frac{828608}{1594323}.\n$$\n\nHere is a transparent recurrence proving coverage. Set $u_0(0)=1$. From every nonabsorbed $l<6$, move mass $2u_j(l)/3$ to $l+1$ and $u_j(l)/3$ to $l-1$, removing the mass first arriving at 6 into $a_{j+1}$. After $j$ steps, only $-j\\le l\\le j$ occur. Induction shows that every signed word contributes exactly its product probability once, either to a first-hit term or to one surviving endpoint.\n\nInterior holding times are independent exponentials of rate $3\\kappa$. Hence the number of potential jumps by $T=14/\\kappa$ is Poisson with mean 42, independent of the directions. The exact success probability is\n\n$$\ns_{14}=\\sum_{j\\in\\{6,8,10,12,14\\}}a_j\n\\left(1-e^{-42}\\sum_{k=0}^{j-1}\\frac{42^k}{k!}\\right).\n$$\n\nFor a fully rational lower bound, note\n\n$$\ne^3>\\sum_{k=0}^{8}\\frac{3^k}{k!}=\\frac{89641}{4480}>20.\n$$\n\nWorking backwards from the largest term in $\\sum_{k=0}^{13}42^k/k!$, consecutive terms have ratio at most $13/42$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\Pr[\\operatorname{Pois}(42)<14]\n<\\frac{42}{29}\\frac{42^{13}}{13!\\,20^{14}}\n=\\frac{1907066391840981}{10616320000000000000000}.\n$$\n\nConsequently\n\n$$\ns_{14}>\n\\frac{8796764642080188960911649137}\n{16925843151360000000000000000} >0.5197238662,\n$$\n\n$$\nA(R_{14})>\n\\frac{42648450944800188960911649137}\n{67703372605440000000000000000} >0.6299309665>\\frac58.\n$$\n\nThe decimal values are displays; all comparisons in the executable certificate use rational arithmetic.\n\n### Nonadaptive negative control\n\nReplace both update gates by identity while preserving the other modules and source. Then the second forecast is $Y_0$, giving\n\n$$\n\\Pr(Y_0=Y_2)=\\Pr(Z_1=Z_2)=\\frac{9+1}{16}=\\frac58.\n$$\n\nWith the same timeout/fallback it scores $1/2+s_h/8\\le5/8$, and fails the strict qualifying threshold. This is a substantive adaptation control. It is not an assertion that every possible nonadaptive architecture fails every task.\n\n### The shared Definition-23 minimum\n\nThe common binary prefix machine uses the generic constructor namespace $0\\gamma(h+1)$, with Elias $\\gamma$ code, and a separately specified delayed optimal universal namespace. The target program `00001111` has length eight. The FND all-short-string proof classifies every syntactic string of length below eight: the only halting constructors there have $h\\le6$, while the universal namespace cannot start that short. For $h<6$, two circuits cannot finish. For $h=6$, completion requires six consecutive forward jumps and has probability at most $64/729<1/2$. No shorter output qualifies.\n\nThis imports the actual shared FND theorem, not a new minimum based on register count. Together with that theorem's full-hierarchy persistence and same-code response faithfulness,\n\n$$\nC_P(R_{14})=C_{op}=8\\ \\text{bits}\n$$\n\non the adopted presentation, declared physical source family $\\mathcal R=\\{R_h:h\\ge0\\}$ and task. The hierarchy admits this infinite family of complete instrumented devices; it does not classify every physically conceivable architecture. The minimum comparison covers every program in that presentation, including universal-tail programs that could produce an admitted family member; it does not claim that other universal machines would assign the same number. The delayed namespace is an explicit coding convention. The entire instrumented source, including the observer and finite physical dressing, is in the canonical code. No three-bit capacity argument or $d_0=8$ assertion is used.\n\nBy Appendix P §P.5.8.3 this qualifies the source as a **level-0 observer on this scoped MPU class**. No Property-R self-model, CC capability, fundamental-particle identity or quantum actualization conclusion follows.\n\n## 5. Finite physical motor and instrument\n\n### Exact stopped coupling, not survival conditioning\n\nFor every live state with interior node $v$, two child transitions each have rate $\\kappa$, and one parent transition has rate $\\kappa$. Neither data, archive, initial branch labels nor control history changes these rates. Thus the total forward/backward intensities are exactly $2\\kappa,\\kappa$. The destination data/record maps are deterministic conditional on the edge.\n\nConstruct potential jump times from a rate-$3\\kappa$ Poisson process, choose an independent forward/backward direction with probabilities $2/3,1/3$, and choose a fair child label on forward motion. This construction has the stated finite-tree transition rates. Use exactly the same jump times and directions for an unbounded integer walk. They agree until the stop. Since the start depth is $h+1$, a path of at most $h$ jumps lies strictly between root and leaf. No hidden boundary change of rate occurs before the operational stop.\n\nProjection modulo three gives the autonomous reference generator with all forward rates $2\\kappa$ and all backward rates $\\kappa$. The physical phase is its stopped restriction. On any common observation functional of the histories before stop, the response laws are identical, not approximately identical. For an unstopped reference observable on $[0,T]$, a coupling error is at most the probability that the resource stop occurred before the observable was evaluated. The failure outcome is explicitly retained as $\\bot$; it is never silently removed or renormalized.\n\n### Concrete finite classical/quantum response map\n\nLet $X_h$ be the finite set of full apparatus configurations, including prepared cells and counters. The classical state algebra is the diagonal algebra on $\\mathcal H_h=\\mathbb C^{X_h}$. For a live configuration $x$, each motor edge produces its prescribed next configuration $F_a(x)$ at its specified rate $k_a$. Rank-one jump operators\n\n$$\nL_{x,a}=\\sqrt{k_a}\\,|F_a(x)\\rangle\\langle x|\n$$\n\ngive a finite GKLS generator whose diagonal restriction is exactly the classical master equation. The anti-commutator term removes the same total outgoing mass as the jump term adds. This verifies complete positivity and probability preservation on the nominated complex carrier. It is a direct classical-diagonal embedding; no realification of arbitrary unknown quantum states is claimed.\n\nThe effective jump/instrument law is a physical-model primitive with a finite charged controller and fresh-cell supply. Only the **bare motor** has symmetric microscopic edges. The monitored apparatus does not inherit global detailed balance, and its projected motor affinity is not its total thermodynamic entropy production.\n\n### Reversible finite gate realization of a record update\n\nFor any deterministic finite map $F:\\{0,1\\}^B\\to\\{0,1\\}^B$, the transformation\n\n$$\n(x,z)\\longmapsto(x,z\\oplus F(x))\n$$\n\nis a permutation. Composing it with register swap sends $(x,0)$ to $(F(x),x)$. Thus the update is physically embeddable in a reversible finite Boolean circuit with a fresh $B$-bit old-state cell. The fresh cell is consumed and retained, not erased or reused. An arbitrary finite Boolean table can be compiled by a finite controlled-gate enumeration, so no complexity assumption is needed for this existence statement. The code uses precisely this old-state/shadow convention.\n\nEvery motor jump receives its own fresh old-state cell. A separate two-bit history entry stores direction and the selected/removed branch bit. This matters on a parent traversal, which otherwise loses its child's branch label. These are actual protected physical registers, not an uncharged mathematical observer. Snapshot copy and verifier computation similarly use prepared output/scratch cells and charged finite gates. The record channel's microscopic reversal would consume these records; O.3k's matched motor reverse is a different, explicitly projected comparison.\n\n## 6. Protected archive and ancestry\n\n### Layout and causal writes\n\nThe archive contains nine bits in the order\n\n$$\n(I_0,C_1,O_1,U_1,C_2,O_2,U_2,E_1,E_2).\n$$\n\nInitially $I_0=Y_0$, all other archive bits are zero, and stage pointer $r=0$. On the first arrival at each successive level $r=1,\\ldots,6$:\n\n- levels 1 and 4 copy the committed forecast into $C_1,C_2$;\n- levels 2 and 5 copy the newly exposed outcome into $O_1,O_2$, and discrepancy into $E_1,E_2$;\n- levels 3 and 6 copy the new model memory into $U_1,U_2$, and append the completed-circuit symbol $\\rho$.\n\nThe monotonically increasing high-water stage $r$ supplies valid/blank tags; the circuit count is $n=\\lfloor r/3\\rfloor$. No jump, snapshot, verification or permitted response operation writes any already valid archive cell. A backtrack changes computational scratch but leaves archive, history and shadows intact. Capacity exhaustion and timeout preserve all earlier records.\n\n### Finite verifier\n\nGiven the protected initial receipt, private source bits, at most $h$ direction/branch entries, old-state shadows and scheduled snapshot receipts, the internal verifier:\n\n1. starts from the declared ready state;\n2. checks each adjacent tree move and its branch label;\n3. applies the exact data gate;\n4. checks the old-state shadow;\n5. reconstructs every first high-water crossing and its archive write;\n6. checks that a claimed circuit begins at the previous $3(n-1)$ first passage and ends at first arrival at $3n$;\n7. checks that no earlier archive entry was overwritten;\n8. checks final state/status and each scheduled snapshot against its protected capture prefix, with nondecreasing capture counts, jump count zero at the initial snapshot, and the full terminal count and stopped status at the final snapshot.\n\nAll lists have a declared finite bound. The routine `verify` implements these steps. At the q read indexed $i$, `observe` supplies the trusted expected snapshot count $i+1$; this rejects deletion of the final snapshot as well as gaps in the prefix. A generic verifier invocation without that expected count checks an intermediate run prefix only. The immutable initial-node receipt is included in the prepared source, so the verifier works for every node in the uniform initial fiber, not only the all-zero representative. Each scheduled snapshot has a charged old-state shadow containing its private jump-count receipt. Exact capture times are supplied by the calibrated controller; no unrecorded holding-time journal is claimed. It uses the same `step` routine as the source, and its q response is a single accept/reject bit; q is not given arbitrary reads of the private direction/shadow arrays.\n\nThe verifier rejects every perturbation in the declared impostor class that changes an archive value, completed-stage claim, history transition, old-state witness or sampled past-availability value while preserving the trusted initial/source and capture receipts. This follows because replay is deterministic and equality is literal finite-bit equality. It does **not** distinguish a perfect joint clone of the entire apparatus and all trusted receipts, and it does not prove a cryptographic impossibility of forged physical histories. Such a distinction would have no response witness in this grammar.\n\nThe causal claim is stronger than an offline plausibility test: the actual source transition maps write the records in this order and preserve them thereafter. The verifier checks membership in that source's finite transcript language. There is no invocation of historical researcher blindness.\n\n## 7. Observer algebra: exact time-indexed retention\n\n### Why a current pointer alone is insufficient\n\nTwo paths can have identical final $Y$-archive and different arrival times. A later current pointer therefore cannot recover the earlier proposition “one record had arrived by $T/2$.” Merely writing $\\mathcal A_{r(s)}\\subseteq\\mathcal A_{r(t)}$ pathwise would not prove a deterministic-time algebra inclusion containing this proposition. The actual source stores the required earlier response.\n\n### Finite schedule and physical snapshots\n\nSet $t_0=0,t_1=T/2,t_2=T$. At each $t_i$, the observer instrument copies\n\n$$\nB_i=(r,\\text{nine archive bits},\\text{status})\n$$\n\ninto a distinct protected snapshot cell and supplies its verification verdict. Store the label $i$ and a private captured jump-count witness as part of the finite controller receipt. The observer sees the snapshot values and order; it cannot query the live pointer or an unsampled exact arrival time. Snapshots remain stored after later source motion. At $t_2$, set timeout/final status first, then capture the snapshot. The initial snapshot is charged too; it may equivalently be represented by the protected initial receipt and a fixed zero-stage tag.\n\nFor a fixed allowed read protocol, let $H_j$ be the finite support of $(B_0,\\ldots,B_j)$ under the common source law, with accepted verification fields included. Every supported prefix has a supported continuation because the future stochastic evolution is normalized, and after stop it can at least preserve the existing records through the remaining reads.\n\nDefine\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A_j=\\mathbb C^{H_j},\\qquad\n\\mathcal A_{\\rm ret}^{(0)}(t)=\\mathcal A_{j(t)},\n\\quad j(t)=\\max\\{j:t_j\\le t\\}.\n$$\n\nThe family is constant between scheduled reads. It describes the exact declared finite-response grammar, not a coarse approximation to all possible wall-clock questions.\n\n### Physical representation and inclusion proof\n\nOn the same carrier $\\mathcal H_h$, define the diagonal projector\n\n$$\nE_{j,h}=\\sum_{x:\\,\\text{stored snapshot prefix}(x)=h}|x\\rangle\\langle x|.\n$$\n\nRestrict to the physical sector after snapshot $j$ has been captured. The projectors are mutually orthogonal, sum to the identity of that sector and are nonzero for every $h\\in H_j$. The map\n\n$$\nf\\longmapsto\\sum_{h\\in H_j}f(h)E_{j,h}\n$$\n\nis therefore a faithful unital *-representation. Its projectors are physically measurable by finite computational-basis reads and reversible finite comparisons of the stored bits. Arbitrary elements of the finite commutative algebra are linear combinations of these physically generated record effects; there is no hidden promise of an unbounded family of laboratory read commands.\n\nFor $i\\le j$, let $p_{ij}:H_j\\to H_i$ discard later snapshots, and define\n\n$$\n\\iota_{ij}(f)=f\\circ p_{ij}.\n$$\n\nSurjectivity of $p_{ij}$ proves injectivity of $\\iota_{ij}$. Pointwise multiplication and conjugation show it is a unital *-homomorphism; prefix composition gives $\\iota_{jk}\\iota_{ij}=\\iota_{ik}$. Physically, no later transition changes an old snapshot. Thus the Heisenberg pullback of the embedded old effect is exactly that old effect on the earlier reachable sector.\n\nLet $\\omega_j(f)=\\mathbb E[f(B_0,\\ldots,B_j)]$. Then\n\n$$\n\\omega_j\\circ\\iota_{ij}=\\omega_i\n$$\n\nby marginalization of the same actual process law. This is state compatibility, not an assumption that an independent forward/reverse normalization or a new posterior state has the same marginal.\n\nPutting $\\iota_{s,t}=\\iota_{j(s),j(t)}$ proves Definition O.3c's deterministic-time inclusion and composition laws for **every** $0\\le s\\le t\\le T$, on this finite-response grammar. The permitted response “record 1 was available at $T/2$” is literally a function of stored $B_1$ and remains available at $T$.\n\n### Event-index algebra and the clock subtest\n\nSeparately, at a completed circuit boundary, the data record is equivalent to $(Y_0,\\ldots,Y_n)$. Its algebra is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal B_n\\cong\\mathbb C^{\\{0,1\\}^{n+1}},\\qquad n=0,1,2,\n$$\n\nwith ordinary prefix pullbacks. Between circuit boundaries the first-high-water archive additionally includes an exposed $Y_i$ at stages 2 or 5. This event-index algebra is useful for the computation and entropy formula below. It is not silently substituted for the full deterministic-time snapshot algebra.\n\nAt a scheduled read, the clock subtest extracts the ordered completed-circuit prefix $\\rho^n$ from the verified archive. Newly exposed completed entries are delivered in order. A read can deliver a batch of zero, one or two symbols; equivalently use the finite batch alphabet $\\{0,1,2\\}$ with bookkeeping increments $0,3\\ln2,6\\ln2$. This batching does not assert a likelihood ratio for a full snapshot event including an unfinished final excursion. The event alphabet of each underlying circuit is still $\\{\\rho\\}$. The motor/circuit projection is a specified subtest of the richer observer algebra, so its likelihood ratio is not asserted to equal the likelihood ratio of the entire snapshot-history law.\n\n## 8. Intrinsic state entropy is not the clock increment\n\nFor a finite commutative algebra $\\mathbb C^{H_j}=\\bigoplus_{h\\in H_j}M_1(\\mathbb C)$, use ordinary matrix traces and the intrinsic normalized-state entropy\n\n$$\nS_{\\rm ret}^{(0)}(t)=-\\sum_{h\\in H_{j(t)}}p_h\\ln p_h.\n$$\n\nIt obeys the exact manuscript bound\n\n$$\nS_{\\rm ret}^{(0)}(t)\\le\\ln|H_{j(t)}|\n=\\ln\\dim_{\\mathbb C}\\mathcal A_{\\rm ret}^{(0)}(t).\n$$\n\nHere, because snapshots form a retained *classical joint transcript*, there is the stronger instance-specific chain-rule result\n\n$$\nH(B_0,\\ldots,B_{j+1})=H(B_0,\\ldots,B_j)\n+H(B_{j+1}\\mid B_0,\\ldots,B_j)\\ge H(B_0,\\ldots,B_j).\n$$\n\nThis monotonicity is due to the common joint law and classical no-overwrite transcript. It is not a consequence of algebra inclusion alone and is not a universal quantum-state entropy claim.\n\nConditioned on completion of $n$ circuits, motor success is independent of the source bits, so the event-index data algebra has\n\n$$\nH(Y_0,\\ldots,Y_n)=\\ln2+n\\,h_2(1/4),\n\\quad h_2(p)=-p\\ln p-(1-p)\\ln(1-p).\n$$\n\nSnapshots also contain motor/status/availability information, so their entropy is **not** generally this expression. The clock, in contrast, uses\n\n$$\n\\Sigma_R^{\\rm ECF}=3n\\ln2,\\qquad \\mathcal T_R(n)=3n.\n$$\n\nFor example, the source's newly observed predictive outcome contributes $h_2(1/4)$ to the event-data entropy, while a completed motor circuit contributes $3\\ln2$ to the matched path log-ratio. They are different quantities on different response projections.\n\n## 9. Exact first-passage likelihood map and its controls\n\n### Matched finite fibers\n\nConsider any completed circuit between first passages at $3(n-1)$ and $3n$. Its signed word $w$ has net displacement 3. Let $N_+,N_-$ be its forward/backward counts. Then $N_+-N_-=3$, and the projected jump-chain weight is\n\n$$\np(w)=(2/3)^{N_+}(1/3)^{N_-}.\n$$\n\nThe matched reversed word $w^\\dagger$ reverses the order and signs of all increments. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(w)}{p(w^\\dagger)}=2^{N_+-N_-}=8.\n$$\n\nThe reversed word need not be a first-passage word for the opposite orientation. We never replace the matched reversed fiber by an independently stopped or independently normalized reverse process.\n\nLet $W$ be any nonempty allowed completed-circuit fiber defined by the remaining jump budget, deadline and recorded start context. If holding durations are retained for the mathematical comparison, reverse their ordered durations together with the path and transport the acceptance indicator as well. All live exits have rate $3\\kappa$, so holding factors cancel. Summing on the **same matched fiber** yields\n\n$$\nP_F^W(\\rho)=\\sum_{w\\in W}p(w),\\qquad\nP_R^W(\\rho^\\dagger)=\\sum_{w\\in W}p(w^\\dagger)\n=\\frac18P_F^W(\\rho).\n$$\n\nIntegrating the matched durations gives the same identity. The finite device generally has $P_F^W<1$. Its failure outcome $\\bot$ is retained separately and is not assigned a circuit increment. The exact circuit increment is nevertheless\n\n$$\n\\sigma_R^{\\rm ECF}(\\rho)=3\\ln2\\ge\\ln2.\n$$\n\nThus $\\mathcal G_R=\\{\\rho\\}$, every completed actual adaptive cycle advances the dimensionless clock by three units, and no completed event is removed by later motion or abort. This proves the O.3k map under finite stopping rather than importing its infinite-horizon normalization $P_F=1$.\n\nAs an executable check, summing every first-passage word of displacement 3 and length at most 14 gives\n\n$$\nP_F=\\frac{48968}{59049},\\qquad P_R=\\frac{6121}{59049},\n$$\n\nexactly in ratio 8. These are bounded-word weights without the additional deadline restriction; the deadline version has the same ratio when the cutoff is matched.\n\n### Physical source-fiber reverse explanation\n\nBare microscopic tree-edge rates are symmetric, so an individual motor micro-path does not acquire rate-product entropy $3\\ln2$. The coarse ratio includes source-fiber multiplicity. Start the forward motor uniformly on depth $d_0$, and its matched reversed preparation uniformly on depth $d_0+3$. Prepare reverse predictor data by the three-gate forward permutation of the same source ensemble. For every matched closed-phase micro-path, its initial density ratio is\n\n$$\n\\frac{2^{-d_0}}{2^{-(d_0+3)}}=8,\n$$\n\nwhile symmetric edge-rate and holding factors cancel. The environment-source probability is identical in the two paired preparations. For the symmetric bare motor the medium/heat entropy term is zero; the displayed ratio is the change in the uniform-fiber source-density term. The uniform depth-fiber Shannon entropy increases by $3\\ln2$, and its relative entropy to uniform-node equilibrium decreases by the same amount. Thus this is finite information-resource relaxation, not persistent microscopic nonequilibrium circulation. This gives a physical preparation/response interpretation of the same projected ratio. At subsequent circuits, tree automorphism symmetry preserves the uniform depth-fiber law, including under depth-only stopping restrictions. The reverse experiment uses fresh recorder cells and the paired fiber-selection rule. It is **not** a microscopic reversal of already written snapshots or the entire driven apparatus.\n\n### Detailed-balance and orientation controls\n\nFor the nominated null control, change each child rate to $\\kappa/2$ while keeping the parent rate $\\kappa$. Forward and reverse macro rates are both $\\kappa$, so every matched circuit ratio is 1 and its clock increment is zero. The finite motor has stationary node weights proportional to $2^{-|v|}$, since adjacent fluxes satisfy detailed balance. Equivalently this control may be described by an added depth potential with energy increment $\\beta^{-1}\\ln2$; it is an explicitly changed source configuration. The predictor and record algorithms remain the same.\n\nFor orientation exchange, use first passages toward negative multiples of 3 and the unchanged $2\\kappa/\\kappa$ dynamics. Each matched circuit has log-ratio $-3\\ln2$. It therefore fails the nonnegative/guarantee criterion for the positive retained clock. On the finite source there can only be finitely many such events by the jump budget; no finite-capacity run is used to prove the infinite orientation theorem. The independently known ideal reference result of O.3k may be cited on its own infinite domain.\n\nThe all-forward coarse rates do not satisfy the manuscript's heat-only three-state realization criterion. This source does not claim a heat-only three-state motor: it includes an explicit finite entropy/branch resource and a separately driven recorder/controller.\n\n## 10. Finite resources, source ownership and calibration\n\nThe ledger has five distinct owners.\n\n1. **Motor/branch resource:** complete finite tree carrier with degenerate bare motor energies; initial uniform depth fiber; at most $h$ edge events. This is a finite information/entropy resource. The two-to-one branch multiplicity supplies the coarse drift. No unregistered depth battery or heat-only ring realization is added; the actual work sources are the charged recorder/controller and verifier sources below.\n2. **Predictive source/data:** finitely many initial fair bits, the two Bernoulli noise values, fallback, memory and four scratch bits. No feedback from tree/snapshot success into the environment law.\n3. **Recorder:** nine archive bits and stage pointer; two raw-history bits per jump; $h$ full old-state shadow cells; stop flag and jump counter. No cell is reset or reused during the run.\n4. **Observer/verifier:** three finite snapshot cells, their private receipts, finite reversible replay/comparison scratch and output cells. Verification is budgeted as a computation on a supplied finite witness, not as free discovery of its history.\n5. **Control/reference:** finite charged motor-interaction controller, snapshot controller and calibrated rate/time reference, maintained through $T$, including after abort. The observer has no control edge back to the live predictor/rate inputs.\n\nThe ledger is the exact common source allocation of Definition 2.4.1g and Theorem 2.4.1h, expanded explicitly below. Its finite widths, source stocks, controller/verifier ROM and calibrated timing account use that same pricing convention.\n\nWrite $w(n)=\\max(1,\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil)$ and $G=h+3$. The mutable nonhistory width is\n\n$$\nB=D+w(D+1)+5+9+3+2+w(h+1)+2w(G+1).\n$$\n\nIts terms are motor address and depth, five predictor bits, nine archive bits, high-water stage, status, jump counter and the two main energy counters. For the target, $D=30$, $G=17$ and $B=68$. The exact canonical target allocation is:\n\n- mutable state: 68 bits;\n- raw direction/branch history: 28 bits;\n- fresh old-state shadows: 1,156 bits, namely $(14+3)68$;\n- public immutable snapshots: 42 bits;\n- source bits: 19;\n- capacity field: 4 bits;\n- protected initial/capture-root receipt: 22 bits;\n- reusable controller workspace: 304 bits;\n- verifier input width $N=1332$, assembled from existing records rather than another duplicate allocation;\n- reusable verifier workspace: 5,328 bits;\n- three retained acceptance bits and four bits for the separate verifier energy counters.\n\nThese allocations sum to **6,978 bits before read-only logic and calibrated timing hardware**. The 22-bit root receipt and the prepared source include the immutable initial-node information needed by `verify`; the three additional old-state shadow blocks carry the scheduled operations' private capture-count receipts. The script's bounded tuples/dictionaries are an operational representation of these charged fixed-capacity cells. Energy/capacity counters and unused padding are interpreted by the common physical encoding; they are not additional unbounded Python storage in the physical claim.\n\nThe main battery and receiver have counters $q,z\\in\\{0,\\ldots,G\\}$, Hamiltonians $\\epsilon q,\\epsilon z$, and initially $q=G,z=0$. Each of at most $h$ motor collisions and each of the three snapshot operations transfers one charge: $(q,z)\\mapsto(q-1,z+1)$. Thus $q+z=G$ and the modeled energy sum stays $G\\epsilon$. Separately, the three allowed verification calls each consume one of three source tokens of price $\\epsilon_v$, transferring it to the finite verification receiver. The source permits exactly one verification service at each scheduled read. Neither source is an infinite heat bath.\n\nA charge pays for a **whole stipulated finite collision or verification service**, including its internal charging control. Elementary gate counts below prove finite implementability; they are not charged again as if $\\epsilon$ meant energy per elementary gate. This avoids the circular price of a gate that meters its own energy counter. The finite declared source rates and service timing remain physical-model inputs.\n\nThe descriptor supplies conservative synthesis bounds\n\n$$\ng_c=16(B+4)^2B2^{B+4},\\qquad\n g_v=100(N+1)^2 2^{N+1}.\n$$\n\nA reversible sum-of-minterms implementation gives these loose bounds with the allocated work bits: compute each controlled minterm, toggle the target, and uncompute the scratch before the next minterm. The controller's retained old-state register and the verifier's one-bit output preserve reversibility on the ready-cell sector. More explicitly, each verification call reversibly computes the verdict from the retained input into its work area, copies it into that call's distinct ready acceptance bit, and runs the computation backward. Its net map is $|x,0_{W},0_{b_i}\\rangle\\mapsto|x,0_W,V(x)_{b_i}\\rangle$. The workspace is therefore clean again for the next call, while all three acceptance bits remain retained. No reset or purity replenishment is used; the positive whole-call price $\\epsilon_v$ and the loose factor-100 gate bound include compute, copy and uncompute. An explicit optional read-only truth-table implementation uses at most $R_c=B2^{B+4}$ controller bits and $R_v=2^N$ verifier bits; the same verifier table serves all three calls. Include these finite read-only bits, or the finite gate-ROM encoding of the equivalent circuit, in the production and maintenance ledger. No small or practical implementation is claimed.\n\nThe total operational price can, for example, be the declared finite ready-cell/read-only-logic price $c_0$ times the included bit count, plus $17\\epsilon+3\\epsilon_v$, plus the maintained bit count times $c_mT$, plus the explicitly priced calibrated timing source. The timing hardware is an independent finite model primitive of declared price $c_t$ and finite service horizon $T$. This is an actual finite symbolic cost in specified cell, energy and cell-time units; it is not a universal physical lower bound. Pure-cell preparation is initial resource consumption. No cell is reset or reused as an erased unknown bit during the protocol. There is no claim of exact finite-temperature erasure at cost $k_BT\\ln2$, and no identification of reversible gate count with dissipated heat.\n\n### Finite PPI dressing\n\nThe dressing is the bounded run and scheduled-read protocol; the protected records and receipts; the replay verifier; the above finite production/maintenance/verification/update-use ledger; and the causal stage/jump/sample ordering. Every record enters an actual finite predictive update, clock read or ancestry test. This supplies Definition P.6.2b and Theorem P.6.2c's finite operational aspect on the nominated model. It does not prove discovery was cheap or establish a universal resource optimum beyond the separately proved common $C_P$ minimum.\n\n### Clock units\n\nFor the ideal reference ring,\n\n$$\nJ=\\kappa/3,\\qquad\\mathcal A=3\\ln2,\\qquad\n\\dot\\sigma=\\kappa\\ln2.\n$$\n\nThus O.3k's reference calibration is $\\tau_R^{\\rm cyc}=1/\\kappa$, and the representative is\n\n$$\nt_R^{\\rm ECF}=\\frac{3n}{\\kappa}.\n$$\n\nThe dimensionless clock $\\mathcal T_R=3n$ is exact on completed records. The calibrated representative uses the supplied external rate unit. For a finite stopped run it is not pathwise equal to elapsed time, not an unbiased assertion after arbitrary success conditioning, and not evidence of a universal time constant. Infinite-time calibration belongs to the ideal extension; this report instead supplies the exact finite stopping and success law.\n\n### O.5.1 The Perspectival Arrow: Complexity-Relative Temporal Asymmetry\n\nTheorem O.3 gives $P_R/P_F\\le e^{-Nh_{\\min}}$ only on the branch carrying common forward/reverse path measures and the pathwise certificate $\\sigma_k\\ge h_{\\min}>0$. The observer hierarchy of §P.5.8.3 specifies which receiver-pattern descriptors are available when Effective Operational Property R and the registered self-model data are present. External evaluation requires the effective model-access and decision certificates of Theorem M.10.5; a sender-side self-model invariance claim additionally requires its insulation certificate. Definition M.10.3 defines SPAP proximity, and Theorem M.10.3 supplies an asymptotic integration-cost lower bound only on its pattern-specific reduction branch. Aggregate-complexity ordering alone supplies neither conclusion. State recurrence and persistence of a retained history are distinguished by Theorem O.4.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o4-registered-history-separation-from-state-recurrence\"></a>**Theorem O.4 (Registered-History Separation from State Recurrence).** Let $B$ carry a retained-algebra conditioning certificate $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{ret}}^{(q)}$ from Definition O.3c on a certified interval. Suppose an update record is retained at $t_1$ and the certificate supplies the injective inclusion $\\iota_{t_1,t_2}:\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{ret}}^{(q)}(t_1)\\hookrightarrow\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{ret}}^{(q)}(t_2)$ for $t_2>t_1$. If later processing returns the accessible state, a reduced state, or a declared response profile to its earlier value, that equality is a state or response recurrence; it does not delete the registered event from the forward-locked retained algebra. A directional path-probability conclusion additionally requires Theorem O.3a, and a reset-heat conclusion additionally requires a registered reset satisfying Theorem 31.\n\n*Proof.* The injective inclusion preserves the earlier event as an element of the later retained algebra. Equality of an accessible state or declared response profile therefore does not identify the retained algebras or erase that element. The final two claims follow directly from the independent antecedents of Theorems O.3a and 31. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o41-no-erasure-of-a-forward-locked-history-by-later-communication\"></a>**Corollary O.4.1 (No Erasure of a Forward-Locked History by Later Communication).** Under Theorem O.4's retention hypothesis, a later communicated description cannot erase the earlier registered event from the forward-locked ledger. It may nevertheless enable the physical state, a reduced state, or a declared finite response profile to recur; PU's self-reference theorems do not by themselves forbid such restoration.\n\n*Proof.* The first conclusion is Theorem O.4. The second is a scope statement: SPAP excludes a universal exact predictor on its diagonal class, not a control map that returns a particular system to a prior accessible state. ∎\n\nTheorem O.4 is compatible with global unitarity (Theorem E.9.5): it concerns persistence of a certified retained event, not a general impossibility of state, reduced-state, or response-profile recurrence.\n\n<a id=\"remark-o42-locus-of-irreversibility\"></a>**Remark O.4.2 (Locus of Irreversibility).** Theorem O.4 locates its conclusion in the retained history ledger. A positive stochastic arrow requires Theorem O.3a's pathwise certificate, and Theorem 31 gives positive reset heat when a registered reset has positive $H_q(P\\mid R)$. Positive dissipation can also give positive reset heat when $H_q(P\\mid R)=0$. Processing self-referential content may carry the conditional complexity bounds of Appendix M, but self-reference alone supplies neither positive entropy production nor impossibility of state recurrence.\n\n<a id=\"remark-o43-certificate-relative-external-evaluation-cf-theorem-m105\"></a>**Remark O.4.3 (Certificate-Relative External Evaluation; cf. Theorem M.10.5).** Theorem M.10.5 permits an external system $A$ to evaluate a specified pair $(B,E)$ only when $A$ holds the effective model-access and decision certificates stated there; sender-side SPAP-flatness additionally requires its insulation certificate. The inequality $C_{\\mathrm{agg}}(A)>C_{\\mathrm{agg}}(B)$ is neither necessary nor sufficient. Corollary M.10.5.1 excludes a universal internal evaluator only on its explicit reduction branch. These facts establish no strict hierarchy of temporal access and no universal obstruction to restoring a selected prior state.\n\n<a id=\"remark-o44-certificate-relative-reconstruction-cost\"></a>**Remark O.4.4 (Certificate-Relative Reconstruction Cost).** Reconstruction accuracy alone does not determine $\\mu_B(E)$ and does not imply the asymptotic lower bound of Theorem M.10.3. That bound applies only to an asymptotic family for which $\\mu_{B_\\lambda}(E_\\lambda)\\to\\infty$ and the pattern-specific reduction certificate of Corollary B.2.1 is supplied. A token describing a prior state, its processing by $B$, and physical restoration of that state are distinct operations and require separately specified maps.\n\n<a id=\"remark-o45-receiver-pattern-classification\"></a>**Remark O.4.5 (Receiver-Pattern Classification).** On the identifiable Fisher stratum of Definition M.10.2, $\\sigma_B(E)=0$ or $\\sigma_B(E)>0$ classifies the registered update relative to $B$'s declared self-model splitting. Baseline proximity for $\\sigma_B(E)=0$ additionally requires Corollary M.10.3.1's baseline-invariance hypothesis. A divergent integration-cost lower bound additionally requires an asymptotic family with $\\mu_{B_\\lambda}(E_\\lambda)\\to\\infty$ and the pattern-specific reduction certificate of Theorem M.10.3. The classification is a property of the fully specified receiver, pattern, and update map; channel-independence does not follow unless those maps are themselves invariant under the compared delivery channels.\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o42-complexity-graded-self-model-descriptors\"></a>**Proposition O.4.2 (Complexity-Graded Self-Model Descriptors).** The observer levels of §P.5.8.3 grade which Appendix M descriptors are defined, not whether a thermodynamic arrow exists.\n\n1. At Levels 0 and 1, the Appendix M self-model descriptor is not assigned unless Effective Operational Property R and the registered self-model splitting are independently supplied.\n2. At Levels 2 and 3, the receiver-pattern quantities $\\sigma_S(E)$ and $\\mu_S(E)$ are available only on Definition M.10.2's identifiable Fisher stratum and Definition M.10.3's specified prediction-map branch.\n3. At every level, a directional probability bound requires Theorem O.3 or O.3a, and a reset-heat bound requires Theorem 31. A CC intervention or a self-model update does not replace either certificate.\n\n*Proof.* The first two clauses are the domain conditions of Definitions M.10.1–M.10.3 and Remark M.10.1. The third clause follows from the antecedents of Theorems O.3, O.3a, and 31. None of those antecedents is implied solely by an observer-level label. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o43-certificate-relative-asymmetry-of-model-access\"></a>**Corollary O.4.3 (Certificate-Relative Asymmetry of Model Access).** Let $A$ and $B$ satisfy the effective model-access, decision, and insulation hypotheses of Theorem M.10.5 for a specified pattern $E$. Then $A$ may evaluate the certified enclosure of $\\mu_B(E)$ while treating the computation as external to $A$'s self-model. A cost conclusion for $B$ follows only if the corresponding pattern-specific reduction certificate is also supplied. Aggregate-complexity ordering alone implies neither conclusion, and neither conclusion forbids state recurrence under Theorem O.4.\n\n*Proof.* The evaluation statement is Theorem M.10.5, the cost statement is Theorem M.10.3, and the final scope statement is Theorem O.4. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o43a-orientation-independent-certified-integration-boundary\"></a>**Corollary O.4.3a (Orientation-Independent Certified Integration Boundary).** Let $S$ be an embedded Property-R predictive system, and let $\\mathcal H^-$ be a past-labeled reconstruction or $\\mathcal H^+$ a future-labeled simulation. If an integrated finite prefix $R_k$ has $\\mu_S(R_k)=\\infty$ and carries Theorem M.10.6's pattern-specific reduction certificate, then no finite-cost completed integration is certified for that prefix. The conclusion is independent of the temporal-origin label. A complete self-model record or an exact restoration instruction is covered only if a separate construction proves that its induced prefix satisfies these hypotheses.\n\n*Proof.* Theorem M.10.11 makes the criterion depend on the receiver-pattern pair rather than the temporal-origin label. Theorem M.10.6 then gives the certified integration boundary under its reduction hypothesis. No further class of records may be inserted into that boundary without proving the required $\\mu_S(R_k)=\\infty$ and reduction premises. ∎\n\n### O.5.2 Typed Computational-Thermodynamic Asymmetry and the P-versus-NP Boundary\n\nThermodynamic irreversibility, witness-generation difficulty, average-case inversion hardness, path-measure asymmetry, and SPAP diagonal obstruction all distinguish a forward task from a reverse or predictive task. That common verbal pattern does not make the quantities interchangeable. This section places them in one typed bookkeeping space, states the bridge conditions required to move between types, and isolates a sufficient certificate under which the proposed one-way-function route would imply $\\mathsf{P}\\ne\\mathsf{NP}$.\n\n<a id=\"definition-o52a-typed-computational-thermodynamic-asymmetry-profile\"></a>**Definition O.5.2a (Typed Computational-Thermodynamic Asymmetry Profile).** A typed asymmetry profile is the five-coordinate product\n$$\n\\mathsf{Asym}_{\\mathrm{PU}}\n=\n\\left(\n\\mathsf{Asym}_{\\mathrm{diag}},\n\\mathsf{Asym}_{\\mathrm{wit}},\n\\mathsf{Asym}_{\\mathrm{inv}},\n\\mathsf{Asym}_{\\mathrm{erase}},\n\\mathsf{Asym}_{\\mathrm{path}}\n\\right),\n\\tag{O.5.2.1}\n$$\nwhere each coordinate is either a record of its declared type or the distinguished value $\\bot$ when that coordinate has not been instantiated. The coordinate types are as follows.\n\n1. $\\mathsf{Asym}_{\\mathrm{diag}}$ records a predictor class and a predictor-responsive construction, including the SPAP quantifier pattern: for each predictor in the declared class, a system may be constructed whose response depends on that predictor and defeats its exact universal prediction.\n2. $\\mathsf{Asym}_{\\mathrm{wit}}$ records a polynomially balanced, polynomial-time decidable relation $\\mathcal R_{\\mathrm{wit}}(x,w)$, its decision language, and the resources required to output some valid witness rather than merely verify a supplied witness.\n3. $\\mathsf{Asym}_{\\mathrm{inv}}$ records a single fixed public function family, its locked input ensemble, an inverter class, and the average success probability for returning any valid preimage.\n4. $\\mathsf{Asym}_{\\mathrm{erase}}$ records a specified physical reset protocol: the register distribution, accessible side information, Hamiltonian or logical-state energetics, bath temperature $T_{\\mathrm{bath}}$, controller and ancilla closure conditions, and heat or work convention.\n5. $\\mathsf{Asym}_{\\mathrm{path}}$ records a specified forward path law and reverse path law, including their coarse-graining, time-reversal map, and log-likelihood ratio.\n\nThe set of individual one-coordinate records is the disjoint union of the five tagged strata; a complete profile lies in their typed product. Neither construction has a default scalar projection, equality, or order that identifies records from different strata.\n\nAn inter-coordinate bridge from a source coordinate to a target coordinate is *admissible* only when it supplies all of the following:\n\n(i) one uniform encoder and decoder; (ii) polynomial input-length distortion and polynomial simulation overhead; (iii) preservation of totality and of the relevant success predicate; (iv) exact or quantitatively bounded transport of the declared input distribution; (v) a construction converting every target solver into a source solver with an explicit success-loss bound; (vi) a target family fixed independently of the solver to which the reduction is later applied; and (vii) a dependency ledger showing that the bridge does not assume the conclusion, a one-way function, or an equivalent hardness hypothesis. A common word such as *irreversible*, equality of two untyped symbols $\\Omega$, or an analogy between two large spaces is not an admissible bridge.\n\nFor the witness coordinate, let\n$$\n\\mathcal R_{\\mathrm{wit}}(x,w)\n\\iff\nV(x,w)=1,\n\\qquad\n\\mathcal R_{\\mathrm{wit}}(x,w)\\Longrightarrow |w|\\le q(|x|),\n\\tag{O.5.2.2}\n$$\nwhere $V$ runs in polynomial time and $q$ is a polynomial. The associated language and search set are\n$$\nL_{\\mathcal R}:=\\{x:\\exists w\\,\\mathcal R_{\\mathrm{wit}}(x,w)\\},\n\\qquad\nF_{\\mathcal R}(x):=\\{w:\\mathcal R_{\\mathrm{wit}}(x,w)\\}.\n$$\nThe bound $|w|\\le q(|x|)$ bounds the number of possible encodings by less than $2^{q(|x|)+1}$; it is not a lower bound on the time required to find a valid witness.\n\n<a id=\"lemma-o52b-counting-entropy-complexity-separation\"></a>**Lemma O.5.2b (Counting-Entropy-Complexity Separation).** Let a finite classical register $W$ have law $p$ and support size $\\Omega_W$. Its Shannon entropy in nats satisfies\n$$\nH_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(W)\n:=-\\sum_w p(w)\\ln p(w)\n\\le \\ln\\Omega_W,\n\\tag{O.5.2.3}\n$$\nwith equality exactly when $p$ is uniform on its support. Consider the explicit symmetric-memory branch on which: the logical macrostates are local-equilibrium states with equal mean energy, equal internal entropy, and equal free energy; the memory Hamiltonian is restored at the endpoint; the declared joint state includes every correlation involving $W$; the bath begins in a Gibbs state at temperature $T_{\\mathrm{bath}}>0$, independent of all non-bath degrees of freedom; the complete process has a joint unitary description including every entropy-bearing resource; all entropy terms in the finite-resource balance are finite, and any ideal-reservoir or exact-erasure limit has justified convergence of the entropy difference and mean heat; every auxiliary non-bath degree of freedom other than the target register $W$ is restored to its initial joint state, except for an ideal work source whose entropy and correlations remain unchanged and whose declared energy change is pure work; and no other declared or undeclared entropy, information, or nonequilibrium sink is available. Taking $Q_{\\mathrm{env}}:=\\mathbb E[Q_{\\mathrm{bath}}]$ as mean signed heat, positive when delivered to the environment, if no side information about $W$ is retained, resetting $W$ to a fixed logical state obeys\n$$\nQ_{\\mathrm{env}}\n\\ge k_B T_{\\mathrm{bath}}H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(W).\n\\tag{O.5.2.4}\n$$\nIf a classical side-information register $C$ is available as branchwise control, is preserved catalytically, and is returned with its full state and every pre-existing correlation with retained reference systems restored, while acquiring no new correlation with any bath, controller, work source, or other non-$C$ degree of freedom, the corresponding ideal conditional bound is $Q_{\\mathrm{env}}\\ge k_BT_{\\mathrm{bath}}H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(W\\mid C)$. Here preservation means preservation of the full declared side-information resource, not merely its marginal distribution. Neither bound is a computational running-time lower bound. If a nonideal battery or any other entropy-bearing resource is admitted, the bath-only inequality need not hold; that resource must be included in the complete entropy and nonequilibrium-free-energy balance. Under the corresponding standard assumptions of an initially thermal, uncorrelated bath and isothermal work accounting, the appropriate replacement outside the symmetric-memory branch is\n$$\nW_{\\mathrm{in}}\n\\ge\nF_{T_{\\mathrm{bath}}}(\\rho_{\\mathrm{final}},H_{\\mathrm{final}})\n-F_{T_{\\mathrm{bath}}}(\\rho_{\\mathrm{initial}},H_{\\mathrm{initial}}),\n$$\nfor the complete declared process, with signed $W_{\\mathrm{in}}$ taken positive when work is supplied and $F_T(\\rho,H):=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho H)-k_BT S_{\\mathrm{vN}}(\\rho)$ for dimensionless von Neumann entropy in nats. Correlated or nonthermal reservoirs require a more general resource ledger; neither case is governed by (O.5.2.4) alone.\n\n*Proof.* Let $u$ be the uniform distribution on the support of $W$. Nonnegativity of relative entropy gives\n$$\nD_{\\mathrm{KL}}(p\\Vert u)\n=\\sum_w p(w)\\ln\\frac{p(w)}{1/\\Omega_W}\n=\\ln\\Omega_W-H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(W)\n\\ge0,\n$$\nwith equality exactly for $p=u$. This proves (O.5.2.3). On the stated symmetric-memory branch with an initially thermal, uncorrelated bath and complete auxiliary-resource closure, the unconditional heat bound is Reeb and Wolf (2014, Theorem 3), and the retained-classical-information bound is their Section 5.1, Equation (59). The entropy decrease is $H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(W)$ or $H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(W\\mid C)$ in nats, yielding (O.5.2.4) and its conditional version. An ideal complete reset uses justified entropy and mean-heat limits; an imperfect reset retains its output conditional entropy in the difference.\n\nTo separate counting from computation explicitly, fix any polynomial $q$ and define\n$$\n\\mathcal R_0(x,w)\\iff w=0^{q(|x|)}.\n$$\nThere are $2^{q(|x|)}$ binary strings of the displayed length, but a generator outputs the unique witness $0^{q(|x|)}$ in $O(q(|x|))$ time. If a physical implementation first stores a uniformly random string of that length and then resets it under the symmetric-memory Landauer conditions above, the reset bound is $q(|x|)k_BT_{\\mathrm{bath}}\\ln2$, which is linear in the number of stored bits, not exponential in the number of candidate strings. Therefore exponential cardinality, logarithmic entropy, reset heat, and search time are distinct quantities. Conditioning a mathematical distribution on a successful witness is also not, by itself, a physical reset. ∎\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o52c-forecast-information-and-algorithm-conditional-dissipation\"></a>**Proposition O.5.2c (Forecast Information and Algorithm-Conditional Dissipation).** Let $Z_h$ be a finite-valued future variable with alphabet $\\mathcal Z_h$ of size $M_h\\ge2$, let $C_h$ be finite classical side information, and let a finite classical prediction register $Y_h$ have alphabet $\\mathcal Y_h$ of size $d_h$. Suppose a decoder $g_h:\\mathcal Y_h\\times\\operatorname{supp}(C_h)\\to\\mathcal Z_h$ forms $\\widehat Z_h=g_h(Y_h,C_h)$ with error probability $\\epsilon_h=\\Pr[\\widehat Z_h\\ne Z_h]$. Write\n$$\nh_2(\\epsilon):=-\\epsilon\\ln\\epsilon-(1-\\epsilon)\\ln(1-\\epsilon)\n$$\nfor $0<\\epsilon<1$, with the continuous-extension convention $h_2(0)=h_2(1)=0$, and\n$$\nI_{\\mathrm{req}}(h)\n:=\n\\left[\nH_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(Z_h\\mid C_h)\n-h_2(\\epsilon_h)\n-\\epsilon_h\\ln(M_h-1)\n\\right]_+.\n$$\nThen\n$$\n\\ln d_h\n\\ge H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(Y_h\\mid C_h)\n\\ge I(Z_h;Y_h\\mid C_h)\n\\ge I_{\\mathrm{req}}(h).\n\\tag{O.5.2.5}\n$$\nIf $Y_h$ is then reset under the symmetric conditional Landauer hypotheses of Lemma O.5.2b, with $C_h$ as the complete accessible retained side-information resource and with that resource catalytically restored, the declared reset heat obeys\n$$\nQ_{\\mathrm{env}}^{(h)}\n\\ge k_BT_{\\mathrm{bath}}H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(Y_h\\mid C_h)\n\\ge k_BT_{\\mathrm{bath}}I_{\\mathrm{req}}(h).\n\\tag{O.5.2.6}\n$$\nFor a deterministic finite sequence of declared reset events $j=1,\\ldots,L(n)$, let $W_j$ be the register erased and let $C_j$ include every accessible side-information record and the admitted prior event history. Require the complete conditional reset contract of Lemma O.5.2b at each event, a prescribed nonrandom temperature $T_j>0$, finite mean heat and entropy terms, and an additive bath-heat ledger. Taking conditional expectations and then using the tower property gives\n$$\nQ_{\\mathrm{reset}}(n)\n:=\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\\sum_{j=1}^{L(n)}Q_{\\mathrm{bath},j}\\right]\n\\ge\nk_B\\sum_{j=1}^{L(n)}T_jH_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(W_j\\mid C_j).\n\\tag{O.5.2.7}\n$$\nConsequently, if $T_j\\ge T_{\\min}>0$ and $H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(W_j\\mid C_j)\\ge h_{\\min}^{\\mathrm{erase}}>0$ for every declared reset, with both floors independent of $n$, then $Q_{\\mathrm{reset}}(n)\\ge k_BT_{\\min}h_{\\min}^{\\mathrm{erase}}L(n)$. An exponential lower bound by this route requires an independent theorem establishing exponentially many such resets. A random or stopping-dependent reset count requires a separate valid conditional-summation certificate. These are mean-heat bounds, not pathwise heat guarantees; candidate cardinality alone gives no reset-count bound.\n\n*Proof.* Since $Y_h$ has at most $d_h$ values, $H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(Y_h\\mid C_h)\\le\\ln d_h$. Also $H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(Y_h\\mid C_h)\\ge I(Z_h;Y_h\\mid C_h)$. Conditional data processing for $\\widehat Z_h=g_h(Y_h,C_h)$ and conditional Fano inequality give\n$$\nI(Z_h;Y_h\\mid C_h)\n\\ge I(Z_h;\\widehat Z_h\\mid C_h)\n\\ge H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(Z_h\\mid C_h)-h_2(\\epsilon_h)-\\epsilon_h\\ln(M_h-1).\n$$\nMutual information is nonnegative, proving (O.5.2.5) (Cover and Thomas 2006). Conditional Landauer erasure then gives (O.5.2.6), and summing only the explicitly declared reset contributions gives (O.5.2.7).\n\nThe side-information clause is essential. If an additional correlated register $D_h$ is available during reset, the physical entropy term is $H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(Y_h\\mid C_h,D_h)$, and (O.5.2.6) must be rederived with the enlarged conditioning and full resource-restoration conditions. In particular, if the realized $Z_h$ later becomes available and $Y_h$ is a deterministic function of $(Z_h,C_h)$, reversible uncomputation can make the conditional reset entropy vanish. For a perfect forecast with $M_h=2^N$ and $Z_h$ uniform conditional on every supported value $C_h=c$, equivalently $H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(Z_h\\mid C_h)=N\\ln2$, (O.5.2.5) requires $N\\ln2$ nats of record capacity and the stated reset branch costs at least $Nk_BT_{\\mathrm{bath}}\\ln2$: the dependence on $N$ is linear. For nonsymmetric memories or noncyclic protocols, the complete nonequilibrium free-energy inequality stated in Lemma O.5.2b replaces the simple heat formula. ∎\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o52d-the-pnp-decision-search-stratum\"></a>**Proposition O.5.2d (The P/NP Decision-Search Stratum).** For polynomially balanced, polynomial-time decidable witness relations, the following are equivalent:\n$$\n\\mathsf{P}=\\mathsf{NP}\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\\forall(\\mathcal R_{\\mathrm{wit}},V,q)\\;\\exists G_{\\mathcal R}\\in\\mathsf{FP}\\;\\forall x:\n\\quad\nG_{\\mathcal R}(x)\n\\in\n\\begin{cases}\nF_{\\mathcal R}(x),&F_{\\mathcal R}(x)\\ne\\varnothing,\\\\\n\\{\\bot\\},&F_{\\mathcal R}(x)=\\varnothing,\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{O.5.2.8}\n$$\nwhere the relation, its fixed verifier $V$, and its polynomial balance bound $q$ are fixed before the corresponding generator $G_{\\mathcal R}$ is selected. Thus the decision-versus-witness-generation question is a well-defined stratum of $\\mathsf{Asym}_{\\mathrm{PU}}$, but its membership in the typed space supplies no separation theorem.\n\n*Proof.* Assume $\\mathsf{P}=\\mathsf{NP}$. The language $L_{\\mathcal R}$ is in $\\mathsf{NP}$, hence decidable in polynomial time. On a yes-instance, maintain a prefix $u$ known to extend to a valid witness. If $\\mathcal R_{\\mathrm{wit}}(x,u)$ holds, output $u$. Otherwise query the prefix-extension language\n$$\nL_{\\mathcal R}^{\\mathrm{ext}}\n:=\n\\{\\langle x,u\\rangle:\\exists z,\\ |uz|\\le q(|x|)\\text{ and }\\mathcal R_{\\mathrm{wit}}(x,uz)\\}.\n$$\nThis language is in $\\mathsf{NP}$ because $z$ has polynomial length and $V$ verifies the completion. Query first with $u0$. If the answer is yes append $0$; otherwise append $1$, which must preserve the invariant. If no shorter prefix already satisfies the relation, then after at most $q(|x|)$ bit appends one has $|u|=q(|x|)$; output $u$, which satisfies $\\mathcal R_{\\mathrm{wit}}(x,u)$ by the invariant. On a no-instance return $\\bot$. For the fixed relation this is a uniform polynomial-time generator $G_{\\mathcal R}$.\n\nConversely, apply the assumed generator to the standard polynomially balanced satisfiability relation. It returns a satisfying assignment exactly on satisfiable formulas and $\\bot$ otherwise, deciding SAT in polynomial time. Since SAT is $\\mathsf{NP}$-complete, $\\mathsf{P}=\\mathsf{NP}$ (Cook 1971; Karp 1972). ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o52e-no-separation-by-untyped-asymmetry\"></a>**Theorem O.5.2e (No Separation by Untyped Asymmetry).** SPAP diagonal obstruction, a positive registered-reset cost, a thermodynamic arrow, and large physical or computational possibility spaces do not by themselves furnish or discharge the certificate $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{OWF}}$ of Definition O.5.2f. No inference from these facts to one-wayness or $\\mathsf{P}\\ne\\mathsf{NP}$ is licensed merely by placing them in $\\mathsf{Asym}_{\\mathrm{PU}}$ or identifying their untyped cardinalities. Moreover, any route using only the current common-oracle-relativizing SPAP construction is blocked as a $\\mathsf{P}$-versus-$\\mathsf{NP}$ separation proof. This theorem is a certificate-insufficiency and proof-route statement; it does not exclude a future explicit nonrelativizing bridge or a different separation method.\n\n*Proof.* Four independent checks leave the proposed certificate undischarged.\n\nFirst, the success predicates differ. A many-to-one map can erase distinctions and still be trivial to invert in the cryptographic sense. For example, $f_n^{\\mathrm{const}}(x)=0^n$ has a maximally collapsed output, but the inverter that always outputs $0^n$ returns a valid preimage with probability one. The involution $f_n^{\\mathrm{not}}(x)=\\neg x$ and the identity $f_n^{\\mathrm{id}}(x)=x$ are also exactly invertible in linear time. Conversely, an implementation of the identity may dissipate arbitrarily much energy through a wasteful controller. These counterexamples show that logical merging or physical dissipation, taken as untyped properties, cannot be relabeled as computational inversion hardness.\n\nSecond, the quantifiers differ. The relevant SPAP form is\n$$\n\\forall\\mathcal P\\;\\exists S_{\\mathcal P}\n\\quad\n\\text{such that the predictor-responsive system }S_{\\mathcal P}\\text{ defeats }\\mathcal P,\n$$\nwhereas standard one-wayness has the form\n$$\n\\exists f\\;\\forall\\mathcal I\\in\\mathsf{PPT}\\;\\forall c\\in\\mathbb N_{>0}\\;\n\\exists N_{\\mathcal I,c}\\;\\forall n\\ge N_{\\mathcal I,c}:\n\\operatorname{Adv}^{\\mathrm{inv}}_{f,\\mathcal I}(n)<n^{-c}.\n\\tag{O.5.2.9}\n$$\nThe SPAP target may depend on the predictor; the function $f$ must be one fixed public family chosen before the inverter. Exchanging $\\forall\\mathcal P\\exists S_{\\mathcal P}$ for $\\exists f\\forall\\mathcal I$ is not a valid quantifier inference. SPAP also concerns a responsive diagonal interaction, while one-way inversion is a static input-output task under a locked average-case distribution.\n\nThird, a physical phase-space volume is measure- and coarse-graining-dependent and generally carries units, while a finite witness count is a dimensionless encoding cardinality. Even after a normalization turns both into numbers, an equality of cardinalities gives no uniform reduction, no success-preservation theorem, and no running-time lower bound. Definition O.5.2a therefore forbids identifying physical phase-space volume with computational candidate count unless a complete admissible bridge is supplied.\n\nFourth, the current SPAP diagonal proof route is common-oracle relativizing. For any oracle $\\mathcal O$ and any oracle predictor $\\mathcal P^{\\mathcal O}$, the responsive construction may query $\\mathcal P^{\\mathcal O}$ on its own proposed response and return the complementary bit; exact correctness would require a bit to equal its complement. Giving the same oracle to both sides therefore leaves the diagonal contradiction intact. Now choose a Baker-Gill-Solovay oracle $\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{BGS}}$ for which $\\mathsf{P}^{\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{BGS}}}=\\mathsf{NP}^{\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{BGS}}}$ (Baker, Gill and Solovay 1975). The common-oracle SPAP obstruction still holds. By contrast, the prefix construction of Theorem O.5.2g also relativizes for every length-indexed family $f_n:\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}^{m(n)}$ with polynomially bounded $m(n)$, unary index $1^n$ supplied to the inverter, and one uniform evaluator in $\\mathsf{FP}^{\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{BGS}}}$. Its prefix-extension language lies in $\\mathsf{NP}^{\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{BGS}}}=\\mathsf{P}^{\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{BGS}}}$, so a uniform polynomial-time oracle inverter recovers a preimage for every output in the image. Thus no such indexed family is one-way against uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}^{\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{BGS}}}$ inverters. A common-oracle-relativizing derivation from the current SPAP fact to one-wayness or to $\\mathsf{P}\\ne\\mathsf{NP}$ would therefore fail relative to $\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{BGS}}$. This is a barrier for the stated route, not a claim that every future nonrelativizing PU bridge is impossible.\n\nThe counterexamples, quantifier mismatch, missing reduction data, and oracle construction prove the stated certificate insufficiency and relativization barrier. ∎\n\n<a id=\"definition-o52f-pu-one-way-function-bridge-certificate\"></a>**Definition O.5.2f (PU One-Way-Function Bridge Certificate).** For the one-way-function route studied here, a PU one-way-function bridge certificate $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{OWF}}$ is a finite proof-carrying record containing all of the following data. This certificate deliberately selects a length-preserving, setup-free sufficient subclass; it does not assert that every standard formulation of a one-way function must be length-preserving or lack auxiliary setup structure (Goldreich 2001).\n\n1. The code of one deterministic uniform total evaluator $F$ defining a fixed length-preserving family $f_n:\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}^n$ by $f_n(x)=F(1^n,x)$, together with a checked polynomial running-time bound. No instance-specific table, nonuniform advice, hidden trapdoor, or inaccessible physical constant is permitted.\n2. The locked input ensemble $x\\leftarrow U_n$, the uniform distribution on $\\{0,1\\}^n$. The output law is the pushforward $f_n(U_n)$, not the uniform law on the image of $f_n$.\n3. The declared adversary class of uniform classical probabilistic polynomial-time inverters $\\mathsf{PPT}$. For each fixed inverter $\\mathcal I$, let the polynomial $\\ell_{\\mathcal I}(n)$ bound its random-coin use on security parameter $n$, padding unused coins if necessary. The any-preimage success functional is\n$$\n\\operatorname{Adv}^{\\mathrm{inv}}_{f,\\mathcal I}(n)\n:=\n\\Pr_{\\substack{x\\leftarrow U_n\\\\r\\leftarrow U_{\\ell_{\\mathcal I}(n)}}}\n\\left[\n\\begin{array}{c}\nx'=\\mathcal I(1^n,f_n(x);r)\\in\\{0,1\\}^n,\\\\\nf_n(x')=f_n(x)\n\\end{array}\n\\right].\n\\tag{O.5.2.10}\n$$\n4. A named formal theory $\\mathsf T_{\\mathrm{cert}}$, a deterministic proof checker, a finite proof object for the full quantified statement (O.5.2.9), and an accepted metatheoretic soundness and standard-model interpretation warrant for the fragment used. Without that soundness warrant, acceptance establishes only derivability in $\\mathsf T_{\\mathrm{cert}}$, not the complexity statement. The proof must treat each fixed uniform inverter on one asymptotic tail. A pointwise quantity $\\sup_{\\mathcal I}\\operatorname{Adv}^{\\mathrm{inv}}_{f,\\mathcal I}(n)$, with a different hard-coded uniform machine allowed at each $n$, is not an admissible substitute.\n5. Polynomial encoders and decoders between computational strings and the declared PU state or protocol records. To transfer a PU physical hardness theorem to standard one-wayness, the certificate must map every uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}$ inverter to a permitted PU realization with polynomial overhead and quantitatively controlled success loss. If equivalence between the physical and computational inverter classes is claimed, it must also map every covered PU inverter back to a uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}$ inverter under the same controls. Reversible, parallel, and randomized implementations must be covered; if the forward physical dynamics uses a random seed, that seed must be made part of the deterministic function input or the primitive must be labeled differently.\n6. A dependency ledger showing that the proof does not assume $\\mathsf{P}\\ne\\mathsf{NP}$, an existing one-way function, average-case inversion hardness, or a renamed assumption with the same formal content. A record that uses any unresolved hardness hypothesis must be marked conditional on that hypothesis and does not count as unconditionally discharged for Theorem O.5.2g.\n\nThe certificate concerns uniform classical adversaries. It does not by itself claim security against nonuniform circuit families or quantum polynomial-time inverters. Collisions are allowed: inversion succeeds on any $x'$ of the locked input length with the same image.\n\n<a id=\"remark-o52f1-canonical-family-for-clause-1\"></a>**Remark O.5.2f.1 (Canonical Family for Clause 1).** The constructive universal-function route of Levin (1987) and Goldreich (2001, Section 2.4.1), followed by the amplification and length conventions of Goldreich (2001, Sections 2.3 and 2.2.3), permits a fixed uniform polynomial-time, length-preserving candidate $f^\\star$ whose evaluator is specified independently of whether one-way functions exist. For that explicitly specified candidate, the standard reductions prove that one-way functions exist if and only if $f^\\star$ satisfies (O.5.2.9). The construction requirement is essential: bare existence of some function with this equivalence does not specify a universal candidate.\n\nThis remark gives a candidate-selection route. Clause 1 is populated by a record fixing the machine and encoding conventions, the amplification and length-padding algorithms, the evaluator code and a checked polynomial clock, rather than by the name or citation alone. Clauses 4–6 additionally require the hardness proof, soundness warrant, physical simulation bridge and noncircular dependency ledger.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o52g-conditional-one-way-separation\"></a>**Theorem O.5.2g (Conditional One-Way Separation).** If $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{OWF}}$ is accepted on an unconditional branch, with all clauses of Definition O.5.2f discharged and no unresolved hardness hypothesis in its dependency ledger, then the certified family is a standard one-way function and\n$$\n\\mathsf{P}\\ne\\mathsf{NP}.\n$$\n\n*Proof.* The first conclusion follows from clauses 1–4 together with the accepted soundness and standard-model interpretation warrant. For the separation, suppose for contradiction that $\\mathsf{P}=\\mathsf{NP}$. Define the prefix-extension language\n$$\nL_{\\mathrm{pref}}\n:=\n\\left\\{\n\\langle1^n,y,u\\rangle:\n|u|\\le n\n\\text{ and }\n\\exists v\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n-|u|}\\;F(1^n,uv)=y\n\\right\\}.\n$$\nThis language is in $\\mathsf{NP}$: the completion $v$ has length at most $n$, and the certified evaluator $F$ checks it in polynomial time. Under $\\mathsf{P}=\\mathsf{NP}$, let $D_{\\mathrm{pref}}$ be a uniform deterministic polynomial-time decider for $L_{\\mathrm{pref}}$.\n\nConstruct an inverter $\\mathcal I_D$ on $(1^n,y)$. Let $\\epsilon_{\\mathrm{word}}$ denote the empty word. The inverter first queries $D_{\\mathrm{pref}}(1^n,y,\\epsilon_{\\mathrm{word}})$. If the answer is no, it returns $\\bot$. Otherwise initialize $u:=\\epsilon_{\\mathrm{word}}$. For $i=1,\\ldots,n$, query whether $\\langle1^n,y,u0\\rangle\\in L_{\\mathrm{pref}}$. If yes replace $u$ by $u0$; if no replace $u$ by $u1$. The invariant is that the current prefix extends to a preimage of $y$. When the $0$ branch fails, the invariant guarantees that the $1$ branch succeeds. After exactly $n$ iterations, $u\\in\\{0,1\\}^n$ and $f_n(u)=y$.\n\nThe algorithm makes $n+1$ polynomial-time queries on polynomial-length inputs, so $\\mathcal I_D$ is a uniform deterministic polynomial-time inverter and hence belongs to $\\mathsf{PPT}$. For every $y$ in the image of $f_n$, it returns some valid preimage; injectivity is not required. In particular, for $x\\leftarrow U_n$,\n$$\n\\operatorname{Adv}^{\\mathrm{inv}}_{f,\\mathcal I_D}(n)=1\n$$\nfor every $n$. This contradicts (O.5.2.9), already for $c=1$. Therefore $\\mathsf{P}\\ne\\mathsf{NP}$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o52g1-pu-separation-gate\"></a>**Corollary O.5.2g.1 (PU Separation Gate).** Theorems 10–11, Theorem J.1, Theorem O.3, and Theorem M.10.5 do not by themselves discharge $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{OWF}}$. A PU proof through the one-way-function route of Theorem O.5.2g is unconditional only on a branch where one fixed family and the complete certificate of Definition O.5.2f have been unconditionally discharged, with neither unresolved nor circular hardness assumptions. For this route, there is presently no accepted certificate record:\n$$\n\\boxed{\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{OWF}}=\\varnothing_{\\mathrm{cert}}\\quad\\text{(certificate pending)}.}\n$$\n\nEven after a valid $\\mathsf{P}\\ne\\mathsf{NP}$ proof, one obtains a worst-case superpolynomial separation for some $\\mathsf{NP}$ problems, not an automatic $2^{\\Omega(n)}$ time lower bound, average-case hardness for a chosen forecasting distribution, or exponential physical energy cost. Those stronger conclusions require separate hypotheses such as an exponential-time assumption and a protocol-specific thermodynamic reduction.\n\n*Proof.* The cited PU results establish, respectively, predictor-responsive diagonal obstruction, a conditional physical cost for a specified finite-memory reset, path-measure temporal asymmetry, and receiver-relative modeling limitations. By Theorem O.5.2e none supplies the fixed-family average-case quantifiers or success-preserving bridge required by Definition O.5.2f. The unconditional implication proved in Theorem O.5.2g applies once its full certificate antecedent is independently discharged. A certificate recorded under an unresolved hypothesis yields only the correspondingly conditional implication. Other logically possible routes to $\\mathsf{P}\\ne\\mathsf{NP}$ are outside this certificate theorem. ∎\n\n<a id=\"remark-o52h-universe-as-computation-boundary\"></a>**Remark O.5.2h (Universe-as-Computation Boundary).** PU uses a common typed ledger to compare time's arrow, physical reset cost, witness generation, inversion, and self-reference without identifying them. The statement that the universe “computes itself” is an interpretive description of sequential physical evolution. The theorem-level implication is conditional: an independently certified $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{OWF}}$ yields $\\mathsf{P}\\ne\\mathsf{NP}$.\n\n### O.5.3 Scoped Relativization Barrier, Certificate Status, and Restricted Inversion Theorems\n\nSection O.5.2 proves that an unconditionally certified standard one-way function would imply $\\mathsf{P}\\ne\\mathsf{NP}$, while SPAP, candidate counting, and physical reset cost do not supply that certificate by identification alone. This section adds four results: a relativization barrier for derivations whose premises are oracle-stable and whose computational steps are separately proved oracle-natural; a certificate-submission audit that preserves conditionality and incompleteness; two unconditional restricted inversion obstructions; and a quantitatively stated conditional physical-hardness branch.\n\n<a id=\"definition-o53a-common-oracle-reading-and-oracle-natural-bridge\"></a>**Definition O.5.3a (Common-Oracle Reading and Oracle-Natural Bridge).** Let $\\mathcal O\\subseteq\\{0,1\\}^*$ be an oracle. The *common-oracle reading* of a computational statement replaces every declared machine, predictor, verifier, evaluator, solver, and inverter by its $\\mathcal O$-oracle version and gives the same oracle to every interacting side. A statement is *oracle-stable* when its common-oracle reading is true for every $\\mathcal O$.\n\nLet $\\mathcal B$ be an admissible bridge in the sense of Definition O.5.2a from a source task to a target task. The bridge is *oracle-natural* only if it additionally contains fixed oracle-machine encoders, decoders, and solver transformations, together with polynomials and success-loss functions independent of $\\mathcal O$, such that for every common oracle:\n\n1. the oracle encoders and decoders implement the declared instance and output maps with the same polynomial length distortion;\n2. every target oracle solver is transformed into a source oracle solver with the declared polynomial overhead;\n3. the success predicates and the locked input-distribution transport commute with the encoding; and\n4. the same quantitative success and distribution-loss bounds hold after oracle augmentation.\n\nEquivalently, the solver-reduction and distribution-transport diagrams commute after adjoining every common oracle. A derivation is *oracle-natural* when the same finite inference schema derives the common-oracle conclusion from the common-oracle premises for every $\\mathcal O$; every nonlogical computational transformation used by that schema must carry its own oracle-naturality proof. Oracle-naturality is additional proof data. It does not follow merely from uniformity, polynomial overhead, or the seven admissibility clauses of Definition O.5.2a.\n\n<a id=\"lemma-o53b-registered-oracle-stable-inputs\"></a>**Lemma O.5.3b (Registered Oracle-Stable Inputs).** The following schemas are oracle-stable on their stated readings.\n\n(a) The deterministic and probabilistic SPAP diagonal statements are oracle-stable provided the common-oracle model class retains the represent/simulate/predicate-evaluate closure used by their diagonal constructions.\n\n(b) Lemma O.5.2b and Proposition O.5.2c are unchanged by the common-oracle reading when their physical protocols, distributions, side information, and thermodynamic antecedents are held fixed, because their conclusions contain no oracle-dependent computational class.\n\n(c) The decision-search equivalence relativizes:\n$$\n\\mathsf{P}^{\\mathcal O}=\\mathsf{NP}^{\\mathcal O}\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\\text{every polynomially balanced }\\mathsf{P}^{\\mathcal O}\\text{-decidable relation has an }\\mathsf{FP}^{\\mathcal O}\\text{ total witness selector.}\n$$\n\nNo unlisted PU statement is declared oracle-stable, and no bridge is declared oracle-natural, merely because it belongs to the PU scaffold.\n\n*Proof.* For (a), give the nominated predictor and the responsive diagonal construction the same oracle. The construction still queries the predictor and applies the same fixed-point-free Boolean complement, so exact correctness still requires a bit to equal its complement. The probabilistic threshold construction is unchanged for the same reason. The conclusion is conditional on retention of the declared representation and oracle-simulation closure; oracle access does not establish that closure by itself.\n\nFor (b), the common-oracle operation has no machine class to replace in the locked entropy, reset, and forecast-information statements, so their readings are identical to the original conditional statements.\n\nFor (c), if the verifier is in $\\mathsf{FP}^{\\mathcal O}$, the prefix-extension language is in $\\mathsf{NP}^{\\mathcal O}$. Under $\\mathsf{P}^{\\mathcal O}=\\mathsf{NP}^{\\mathcal O}$, the same prefix search gives an $\\mathsf{FP}^{\\mathcal O}$ selector. Conversely, let $L$ be any language in $\\mathsf{NP}^{\\mathcal O}$ and apply the assumed selector to its polynomially balanced $\\mathsf{P}^{\\mathcal O}$-decidable verifier relation. The selector returns $\\bot$ exactly off $L$ and a valid witness exactly on $L$, so $L\\in\\mathsf{P}^{\\mathcal O}$. Hence $\\mathsf{NP}^{\\mathcal O}\\subseteq\\mathsf{P}^{\\mathcal O}$, proving equality. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o53c-scoped-relativization-barrier\"></a>**Theorem O.5.3c (Scoped Relativization Barrier).** Let $\\Gamma$ be a set of oracle-stable premises. Let $D$ be an oracle-natural derivation from $\\Gamma$, with every nonlogical computational bridge separately certified oracle-natural. Then $D$ cannot establish either of the following by that route:\n\n(i) the common-oracle standard one-wayness assertion for a length-indexed family with polynomially bounded output length and one uniform oracle evaluator; or\n\n(ii) the conclusion $\\mathsf{P}\\ne\\mathsf{NP}$.\n\n*Proof.* Choose a Baker-Gill-Solovay oracle $\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{BGS}}$ satisfying\n$$\n\\mathsf{P}^{\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{BGS}}}\n=\n\\mathsf{NP}^{\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{BGS}}}\n$$\n(Baker, Gill and Solovay 1975). Consider any family\n$$\nf_n^{\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{BGS}}}:\\{0,1\\}^n\\longrightarrow\\{0,1\\}^{m(n)}\n$$\nwith polynomially bounded $m$, unary index $1^n$ supplied to the inverter, and one uniform evaluator in $\\mathsf{FP}^{\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{BGS}}}$. Its prefix-extension language is\n$$\nL_{\\mathrm{pref}}^{\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{BGS}}}\n:=\n\\left\\{\n\\langle1^n,y,u\\rangle:\n|u|\\le n\n\\text{ and }\n\\exists v\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n-|u|}\\;\nf_n^{\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{BGS}}}(uv)=y\n\\right\\}.\n\\tag{O.5.3.1}\n$$\nThis language lies in $\\mathsf{NP}^{\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{BGS}}}=\\mathsf{P}^{\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{BGS}}}$. The prefix-search construction of Theorem O.5.2g therefore gives a uniform deterministic polynomial-time oracle inverter that returns a preimage for every image point. No such family is one-way against uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}^{\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{BGS}}}$ inverters.\n\nEvery premise in $\\Gamma$ remains true at $\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{BGS}}$, and oracle-naturality transports every step of $D$ to the common-oracle reading. A derived one-wayness conclusion would therefore hold at $\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{BGS}}$, contradicting the perfect prefix inverter. Likewise, a derived separation would give $\\mathsf{P}^{\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{BGS}}}\\ne\\mathsf{NP}^{\\mathcal O_{\\mathrm{BGS}}}$, contradicting the choice of oracle. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o53c1-current-registered-route-only\"></a>**Corollary O.5.3c.1 (Current Registered Route Only).** Any submitted derivation using only premises registered by Lemma O.5.3b and steps carrying the oracle-naturality record of Definition O.5.3a cannot discharge $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{OWF}}$ or a direct $\\mathsf{P}\\ne\\mathsf{NP}$ certificate. This corollary does not apply to a bridge lacking an oracle-naturality proof, a genuinely nonrelativizing argument, or a different future proof method.\n\n*Proof.* Such a submitted derivation satisfies every hypothesis of Theorem O.5.3c. The scope exclusions are the negations of those hypotheses and therefore are not classified by the theorem. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o53c2-scoped-algebrization-barrier-with-an-explicit-oracle-pair\"></a>**Theorem O.5.3c.2 (Scoped Algebrization Barrier with an Explicit Oracle Pair).** For an oracle $\\mathcal A\\subseteq\\{0,1\\}^*$ let $\\mathcal A_n:\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}$ be its length-$n$ indicator. For a finite field $\\mathbb F$ given by an explicit description (a prime $p$ in binary, together with a monic irreducible polynomial over $\\mathbb F_p$ for a proper extension), the multilinear extension\n$$\n\\widetilde{\\mathcal A}_{n,\\mathbb F}(z)=\\sum_{w\\in\\{0,1\\}^n}\\mathcal A_n(w)\\prod_{i=1}^n\\bigl(w_iz_i+(1-w_i)(1-z_i)\\bigr),\n\\qquad z\\in\\mathbb F^n,\n\\tag{O.5.3c.2.1}\n$$\nis the unique multilinear polynomial over $\\mathbb F$ that agrees with $\\mathcal A_n$ on $\\{0,1\\}^n$. The extension oracle $\\widetilde{\\mathcal A}$ answers each query $\\langle\\mathbb F,z\\rangle$ with $\\widetilde{\\mathcal A}_{n,\\mathbb F}(z)$ and answers $0$ on every string that is not such a query. The *paired reading* of a computational statement, modeled on the algebrization framework of (Aaronson and Wigderson 2009), gives $\\widetilde{\\mathcal A}$ to every nondeterministic machine, verifier and function evaluator, and $\\mathcal A$ to every deterministic decider, witness selector and inverter. A derivation is *algebrization-natural* when each premise is true in the paired reading for every oracle $\\mathcal A$ and the same finite inference schema derives the paired reading of its conclusion for every $\\mathcal A$, each nonlogical computational step carrying its own proof for every $\\mathcal A$. For deterministic Turing machines with a read-only input tape and one work tape, let\n$$\n\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{univ}}:=\\{\\langle M,x,1^s\\rangle:\\ M\\text{ accepts }x\\text{ without using more than }s\\text{ work-tape cells}\\}.\n$$\nThen:\n\n1. $\\mathsf{NP}^{\\widetilde{\\mathcal A}_{\\mathrm{univ}}}\\subseteq\\mathsf{PSPACE}\\subseteq\\mathsf P^{\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{univ}}}$, and $\\mathsf P^{\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{univ}}}=\\mathsf{NP}^{\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{univ}}}=\\mathsf{PSPACE}$. In particular $\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{univ}}$ is an explicit oracle of the kind invoked in the proof of Theorem O.5.3c.\n2. Every length-indexed family $f_n:\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}^{m(n)}$ with polynomially bounded $m$ and one uniform evaluator in $\\mathsf{FP}^{\\widetilde{\\mathcal A}_{\\mathrm{univ}}}$ has a deterministic polynomial-time $\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{univ}}$-oracle inverter that, on input $(1^n,y)$, returns a preimage of $y$ for every $y$ in the image of $f_n$.\n3. No algebrization-natural derivation establishes $\\mathsf{NP}\\not\\subseteq\\mathsf P$ in its paired reading $\\mathsf{NP}^{\\widetilde{\\mathcal A}}\\not\\subseteq\\mathsf P^{\\mathcal A}$.\n4. No algebrization-natural derivation establishes, in its paired reading, standard one-wayness of a length-indexed family with polynomially bounded output length and one uniform evaluator in $\\mathsf{FP}^{\\widetilde{\\mathcal A}}$ against uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}^{\\mathcal A}$ inverters.\n\nThe class is nonempty: the premise-free derivation of $\\mathsf P\\subseteq\\mathsf{NP}$ is algebrization-natural, because a deterministic query $w\\in\\{0,1\\}^n$ to $\\mathcal A$ is the extension query $\\langle\\mathbb F_2,w\\rangle$. A derivation with a premise or step that fails in some paired reading lies outside this class.\n\n*Proof.* Each product in (O.5.3c.2.1) equals $1$ at $z=w$ and $0$ at every other point of $\\{0,1\\}^n$, so the sum is a multilinear polynomial agreeing with $\\mathcal A_n$ on the cube. A multilinear polynomial vanishing on $\\{0,1\\}^n$ is zero: write it as $z_nQ+R$ with $Q,R$ multilinear in $z_1,\\ldots,z_{n-1}$; the values at $z_n=0$ and $z_n=1$ make $R$ and $Q$ vanish on $\\{0,1\\}^{n-1}$, and induction on $n$ applies. This proves uniqueness.\n\nOn an input $\\langle M,x,1^s\\rangle$ of length $\\ell$, the machine $M$ has at most $K\\le|Q_M|(|x|+2)(s+1)|\\Gamma_M|^s$ configurations within work space $s$, and $\\log K=O(\\ell^2)$ because $|Q_M|,|\\Gamma_M|\\le2^\\ell$ and $s\\le\\ell$. Simulate $M$ step by step, storing one configuration and a binary step counter up to $K$; accept if $M$ accepts within space $s$, and reject if it leaves space $s$, rejects, or exceeds $K$ steps, in which case it has repeated a configuration and never halts. This decides $\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{univ}}$ in polynomial space. If $L\\in\\mathsf{PSPACE}$ is decided by a halting machine $M_L$ within work space $p(n)$, then $x\\in L$ exactly when $\\langle M_L,x,1^{p(|x|)}\\rangle\\in\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{univ}}$, one query computed in polynomial time; hence $\\mathsf{PSPACE}\\subseteq\\mathsf P^{\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{univ}}}$. A string is answered by $\\widetilde{\\mathcal A}_{\\mathrm{univ}}$ in polynomial space as follows. Validity of a field description is decided by trial division of $p$ and an exhaustive search for a proper factor of the polynomial, and an invalid string receives $0$. For a valid query $\\langle\\mathbb F,z\\rangle$, run an $n$-bit counter over $w\\in\\{0,1\\}^n$, decide $\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{univ},n}(w)$ as above, form the product in (O.5.3c.2.1) with polynomially many field operations and accumulate the sum. A nondeterministic polynomial-time machine with this oracle is simulated in polynomial space by enumerating its choice sequences one at a time and answering each query by the same procedure, reusing space; hence $\\mathsf{NP}^{\\widetilde{\\mathcal A}_{\\mathrm{univ}}}\\subseteq\\mathsf{PSPACE}$. Since $\\mathcal A_n(w)=\\widetilde{\\mathcal A}_{n,\\mathbb F_2}(w)$ for $w\\in\\{0,1\\}^n$, one has $\\mathsf P^{\\mathcal A}\\subseteq\\mathsf{NP}^{\\mathcal A}\\subseteq\\mathsf{NP}^{\\widetilde{\\mathcal A}}$, and the chain closes, proving item 1.\n\nFor item 2, the prefix-extension language of the form (O.5.3.1) for the family lies in $\\mathsf{NP}^{\\widetilde{\\mathcal A}_{\\mathrm{univ}}}$, because a completion has length at most $n$ and the evaluator checks it with $\\widetilde{\\mathcal A}_{\\mathrm{univ}}$-queries in polynomial time. By item 1 it lies in $\\mathsf P^{\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{univ}}}$, and the prefix search of Theorem O.5.2g, run with this $\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{univ}}$-oracle decider, returns a preimage of every image point after $n+1$ queries.\n\nFor items 3 and 4, the premises of an algebrization-natural derivation are true in the paired reading at $\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{univ}}$ and each of its steps carries its proof there, so its paired conclusion is true at $\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{univ}}$. A paired separation would give $\\mathsf{NP}^{\\widetilde{\\mathcal A}_{\\mathrm{univ}}}\\not\\subseteq\\mathsf P^{\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{univ}}}$, contradicting item 1. A paired one-wayness conclusion would give negligible inversion success against every uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}^{\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{univ}}}$ inverter, while the inverter of item 2 succeeds with probability one at every $n$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"definition-o53d-certificate-submission-audit\"></a>**Definition O.5.3d (Certificate-Submission Audit).** A submitted separation record is audited along the following non-exclusive dimensions.\n\n1. A malformed inference, failed checker or soundness warrant, or missing required certificate field is not registered as a certificate.\n2. A record containing an unresolved hypothesis $H$ is retained only on the branch conditional on $H$.\n3. A record satisfying the hypotheses of Theorem O.5.3c is blocked from discharge by the scoped relativization barrier.\n4. A proposed nonrelativizing step whose claimed lower bound has not been proved remains certificate-pending.\n5. A certificate is discharged only when its finite proof object, named proof checker, standard-model soundness warrant, bridge obligations, and dependency ledger are all accepted with no unresolved assumption.\n\nThese are audit outcomes for submitted records, not an exhaustive classification of every informal or future idea. Write $\\operatorname{Acc}_{\\mathrm{cert}}(R)=1$ exactly when the submitted record $R$ passes item 5 under a named sound proof checker; merely naming or storing $R$ does not make this proposition true. Until an accepted proof object exists for the OWF or direct-separation certificate, the manuscript certificate ledger uses $\\varnothing_{\\mathrm{cert}}$ for it rather than introducing a positive theorem token. A machine-audited status additionally requires the executable and run package of Convention P.14.9a and Algorithm P.14.1m.0; Theorem P.14.1m.2 supplies only canonical encoding and round-trip.\n\n<a id=\"definition-o53e-direct-separation-certificate\"></a>**Definition O.5.3e (Direct Separation Certificate).** A direct separation certificate $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{sep}}$ is a finite checked proof of $\\mathsf{P}\\ne\\mathsf{NP}$ in a named formal theory $\\mathsf T_{\\mathrm{cert}}$, together with the accepted standard-model soundness warrant for the used fragment and a dependency ledger containing no unresolved assumption. It is a sufficient certificate format for a direct mathematical route that does not pass through one-wayness. This definition does not assert that every conceivable physical argument already has this form.\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o53e1-proof-object-composition\"></a>**Proposition O.5.3e.1 (Proof-Object Composition).** Suppose an accepted record contains a checked $\\mathsf T_{\\mathrm{cert}}$-derivation of $\\mathsf{P}\\ne\\mathsf{NP}$ from finitely many premises $A_1,\\ldots,A_r$ and, in the same interpreted theory, a checked proof of every $A_i$. Substitution of the premise proofs yields a checked $\\mathsf T_{\\mathrm{cert}}$-proof of $\\mathsf{P}\\ne\\mathsf{NP}$ with no remaining premise $A_i$.\n\n*Proof.* Replace each use of a premise rule introducing $A_i$ by the accepted finite derivation of $A_i$. Finiteness of the proof objects makes the substitution finite, and closure of the proof checker under composition verifies the resulting derivation. The conclusion is unchanged and has no undischarged $A_i$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o53e2-marked-physical-hypotheses\"></a>**Corollary O.5.3e.2 (Marked Physical Hypotheses).** Let $\\mathsf K$ be a named formal base theory supporting the deduction theorem, let $A$ be the declared formal translation of a physical hypothesis, and let $H$ denote the arithmetic sentence $\\mathsf{P}\\ne\\mathsf{NP}$. If\n$$\n\\mathsf K\\cup\\{A\\}\\vdash H,\n$$\nthen\n$$\n\\mathsf K\\vdash A\\rightarrow H.\n$$\nWithout a proof $\\mathsf K\\vdash A$, the record is conditional on $A$ and does not establish $\\mathsf K\\vdash H$. If a proof of $A$ is later supplied in $\\mathsf K$, modus ponens discharges the condition. No claim that $A$ is “at least as strong as” $H$ follows without a separate interpretation or conservativity theorem.\n\n*Proof.* Let $\\pi$ be the finite $\\mathsf K\\cup\\{A\\}$-derivation of $H$. Because $\\mathsf K$ is assumed to support the deduction theorem, induction on the length of $\\pi$ transforms every line $B$ of $\\pi$ into a $\\mathsf K$-derivation of $A\\to B$: axioms of $\\mathsf K$ are prefixed using propositional tautologies, the premise line $A$ becomes $A\\to A$, and a modus-ponens step is transported by\n$$\n(A\\to(B\\to C))\\to((A\\to B)\\to(A\\to C)).\n$$\nApplying the transformation to the last line yields $\\mathsf K\\vdash A\\to H$.\n\nIf $\\mathsf K\\nvdash A$, the transformed proof ends at $A\\to H$ and supplies no rule deriving $H$ without the antecedent. If a derivation $\\mathsf K\\vdash A$ is supplied, combining it with $\\mathsf K\\vdash A\\to H$ by modus ponens gives $\\mathsf K\\vdash H$. None of these syntactic steps defines an interpretation comparing the strength of $A$ and $H$, so no such comparison follows. ∎\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o53f-fixed-exponent-deterministic-all-image-inversion-obstruction\"></a>**Proposition O.5.3f (Fixed-Exponent Deterministic All-Image Inversion Obstruction).** For every fixed $k\\in\\mathbb N$ there exists a length-preserving family $f^{(k)}=(f_n^{(k)})_{n\\ge2}$ with one uniform polynomial-time evaluator such that every deterministic machine running in time $O(n^k)$ fails, at infinitely many input lengths, to return a valid preimage for at least one image point.\n\n*Proof.* Fix $k$ and a universal multitape Turing machine whose binary program strings are interpreted under a total coding convention, with invalid strings assigned to one fixed dummy machine. Enumerate all binary program strings in length-lexicographic order and let $M_i$ be the machine represented by the $i$-th string. Every deterministic Turing machine has at least one index, the code of $M_i$ has length $O(\\log i)$, and the index-to-code map is computable in time polynomial in $\\log i$. Fix a universal simulator with running time bounded by a fixed polynomial in the simulated time, input length, and program length; for example, the standard multitape simulation bound $O(t\\log t+\\operatorname{poly}(|\\langle M_i\\rangle|+|w|))$ is sufficient. Define\n$$\ni_k(n):=\\operatorname{ord}_2(n)+1,\n$$\nwhere $\\operatorname{ord}_2(n)$ is the exponent of $2$ in $n$. Every machine index occurs at infinitely many lengths, and $i_k(n)\\le1+\\log_2 n$, so the selected program has length $O(\\log\\log n)$ and is recovered in time polynomial in $\\log n$. On input $(1^n,x)$ with $x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n$, the evaluator $F_k$ computes $i=i_k(n)$ and universally simulates $M_i(1^n,0^n)$ for $n^{k+1}$ simulated steps. If the simulation halts with an $n$-bit output, call it $z_n^{(k)}$; otherwise set $z_n^{(k)}:=1^n$. Define\n$$\nf_n^{(k)}(x)\n:=\n\\begin{cases}\n1^n,&x=z_n^{(k)},\\\\\n0^n,&x\\ne z_n^{(k)}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{O.5.3.2}\n$$\nFor fixed $k$, the declared simulation bound is polynomial in $n^{k+1}+n+|\\langle M_i\\rangle|$; index decoding is polynomial in $\\log n$. Hence $F_k$ is one uniform polynomial-time evaluator. For $n\\ge2$ there exists $x\\ne z_n^{(k)}$, hence $0^n$ is always in the image.\n\nLet $M=M_i$ run in at most $c n^k$ steps for all sufficiently large $n$. If it fails to return an $n$-bit candidate, it already fails inversion. Otherwise choose $n_0$ so that $c n^k\\le n^{k+1}$ for $n\\ge n_0$. At every sufficiently large length satisfying $i_k(n)=i$, the bounded simulation captures the actual output\n$$\nz_n^{(k)}=M(1^n,0^n).\n$$\nBut $f_n^{(k)}(z_n^{(k)})=1^n$, so this candidate is not a preimage of the image point $0^n$. There are infinitely many such lengths. ∎\n\n<a id=\"remark-o53f1-exact-scope-of-the-residue\"></a>**Remark O.5.3f.1 (Exact Scope of the Residue).** Proposition O.5.3f has the quantifier form\n$$\n\\forall k\\;\\exists f^{(k)}\\;\\forall M\\in\\operatorname{DTIME}(n^k)\\;\\exists^\\infty n:\n\\quad\nM(1^n,0^n)\\notin\\bigl(f_n^{(k)}\\bigr)^{-1}(\\{0^n\\})\n$$\nwith an adversarial image point. Standard one-wayness instead fixes one family before every polynomial-time randomized inverter and requires negligible average-case inversion success on the locked ensemble. Three independent upgrades are absent: one family for every polynomial exponent, deterministic-to-randomized security, and infinitely-often worst-case failure to negligible average-case success. The proposition is therefore a restricted diagonal obstruction, not a cryptographic one-way function and not progress on clause 4 of $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{OWF}}$.\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o53g-weak-mathsfac0-inversion-obstruction\"></a>**Proposition O.5.3g (Weak $\\mathsf{AC}^0$ Inversion Obstruction).** Define the prefix-difference bijection $g_n^{\\oplus}:\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}^n$ by\n$$\ng_n^{\\oplus}(x)_1:=x_1,\n\\qquad\ng_n^{\\oplus}(x)_i:=x_{i-1}\\oplus x_i\n\\quad(2\\le i\\le n).\n\\tag{O.5.3.3}\n$$\nThen:\n\n(a) $g_n^{\\oplus}$ is a bijection and\n$$\n(g_n^{\\oplus})^{-1}(y)_i=y_1\\oplus\\cdots\\oplus y_i;\n$$\n\n(b) no nonuniform polynomial-size constant-depth $\\mathsf{AC}^0$ circuit family exactly inverts $g_n^{\\oplus}$ on every input for all sufficiently large $n$; and\n\n(c) let\n$$\n\\delta_{d,s}^{\\oplus}(n)\n:=\n\\sup_{C\\in\\mathsf{AC}^0[d,s]}\n\\left|\n\\Pr_{y\\leftarrow U_n}[C(y)=\\operatorname{PARITY}_n(y)]-\\frac12\n\\right|.\n\\tag{O.5.3.4}\n$$\nFor every depth-$d$, size-$s(n)$, $n$-output circuit family $C_n$,\n$$\n\\Pr_{y\\leftarrow U_n}\n\\left[g_n^{\\oplus}(C_n(y))=y\\right]\n\\le\n\\frac12+\\delta_{d,s(n)}^{\\oplus}(n).\n\\tag{O.5.3.5}\n$$\nHåstad's parity-correlation theorem makes $\\delta_{d,s(n)}^{\\oplus}(n)$ negligible for fixed $d$ and polynomial $s(n)$ (Håstad 1986; Håstad 2014). Randomized nonuniform circuits satisfy the same bound after fixing, at each length, a coin string attaining at least their average success.\n\n*Proof.* Telescoping gives\n$$\ny_1\\oplus\\cdots\\oplus y_i\n=\nx_1\\oplus(x_1\\oplus x_2)\\oplus\\cdots\\oplus(x_{i-1}\\oplus x_i)\n=x_i,\n$$\nproving (a). If a circuit exactly inverted every $y$, its final output bit would compute $y_1\\oplus\\cdots\\oplus y_n$, contradicting the $\\mathsf{AC}^0$ parity lower bound (Furst, Saxe and Sipser 1984; Håstad 1986). This proves (b). For (c), successful inversion of the full vector implies that the final output bit equals $\\operatorname{PARITY}_n(y)$. The agreement probability of that output circuit is bounded by the definition of $\\delta_{d,s}^{\\oplus}$. If a randomized nonuniform circuit has success $p_n$ over inputs and coins, averaging supplies one fixed coin string with input success at least $p_n$, so the deterministic bound applies. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o53g1-no-strong-one-way-certificate-from-the-displayed-bound\"></a>**Corollary O.5.3g.1 (No Strong-One-Way Certificate from the Displayed Bound).** Equation (O.5.3.5) has upper bound $1/2+\\operatorname{negl}(n)$ at fixed depth and polynomial size, not a negligible upper bound on inversion success. Therefore the displayed last-bit reduction does not by itself discharge the strong negligible-inversion clause of $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{OWF}}$, even after replacing its adversary class by $\\mathsf{AC}^0$. It also does not prove the opposite claim that full-vector inversion has non-negligible success. Any amplification claim requires a separately defined amplified family and a proved reduction preserving the declared circuit depth, size, input law, and any-preimage predicate.\n\n*Proof.* A function bounded above by $1/2+\\operatorname{negl}(n)$ need not be negligible. The bound is also only an upper bound and supplies no non-negligible lower bound. Hence neither strong one-wayness nor its negation follows from (O.5.3.5). ∎\n\n<a id=\"definition-o53m-frozen-executable-proposal-class-o04-mc-1\"></a>**Definition O.5.3m (Frozen Executable Proposal Class O04-MC-1).**\n\n#### Objective and scope\n\n- Result: no unconditionally sound Definition O.5.2f certificate exists in the complete method class below.\n- Exact proposition: every admitted record $R$ fails the standard-model hardness condition O.5.2.9 for its actual evaluator $F_{R}$. The witness is one fixed uniform deterministic polynomial-time inverter $I_{R}$, successful on every image point at every $n\\ge 1$.\n- This class is the entire set of certificate attempts using the fixed evaluator protocol below, with arbitrary finite proof, theory, checker, bridge, and dependency strings. Those strings need not be valid. Attempts are not defined as already accepted certificates. No oracle-natural, algebrization-natural, or other proof-strategy restriction is imposed.\n- For proposal procedures: quantify over every algorithm $P$ and every finite admitted output $R$ of $P$. A procedure's search, proof strategy, and other outputs are unrestricted; outputs outside this exact class are not classified. No claim about arbitrary direct-separation proposals is made.\n\n#### Record, constants, equivalence, and universe generator\n\nAn admitted record consists of the literal version O04-MC-1; finite compiler-program bytes $\\Pi$; positive integers $A,d,c$; and arbitrary finite payload bytes recording any asserted proof/theory/checker/bridge/dependencies. The actual evaluator is exactly the protocol $E(\\Pi,A,d,c)$ below, not separately supplied executable code. $A,d,c$ are fixed as part of the record before $n$ or any challenge is selected. The compiler never receives $x$ or the challenge. Equivalence is byte equality of the complete canonical record. No semantic quotient, encoding closure, or identification of physically distinct sources is asserted.\n\nAll finite records of this schema form the universe. Enumerating finite strings and retaining schema-valid records enumerates it completely; record admission is syntactic and decidable. $\\Pi$ may itself be malformed or nonhalting. Such programs are included and define the identity evaluator through the mandatory fallback. The universe is not selected by successful inversion tests, by known easy functions, or by proof acceptance.\n\n#### Fixed clocked compiler machine\n\nThe compiler is the following deterministic 16-register natural-number RAM, with a sparse natural-number memory, read-only input n in register 0 initially, other registers zero, memory zero, and an initially empty bit-output stream. There is no input string, oracle, randomness, persistent state, external service, or challenge. The program is a finite instruction list, indexed from zero, with initial program counter zero. All register operands are in 0,...,15. Jump targets must index an instruction. Immediate constants are nonnegative integers. The instruction set is:\n\n- SET r a; MOV r s; ADD r s t; SUB r s t (truncated at zero); MUL r s t; DIV r s t (floor division); MOD r s t.\n- LOAD r s reads memory at the address in s; STORE r s writes the value in s to memory at the address in r.\n- JZ r j; JMP j; OUT r (append the value in r, which must be 0 or 1); HALT.\n\nOn input $1^n$, $n\\ge 1$, set $q=A(n+2)^d$ and $B=n+q$. Every stored number, register value, and address must lie in $[0,B]$. A would-be overflow, division/modulo by zero, invalid instruction/program, out-of-range program counter, or non-bit OUT is a compiler failure. Arithmetic is on integers, not unit-cost unbounded words. A MUL first forms a number at most $B^{2}$ and checks its bound. At most $q$ instruction dispatches are permitted; HALT is a dispatch. Absence of a successful HALT in that budget is timeout. An output stream has at most $q$ bits. These rules guarantee termination and polynomial bit complexity for every fixed record. The compiler's numeric value cap is deliberate; ordinary polynomial-time machines can be simulated with polynomially many addresses and polynomially bounded cell values, and an appropriately increased fixed $A,d$.\n\n#### Packet and effective validator\n\nAfter successful HALT, group the output bits into 8-bit bytes, most-significant bit first. Reject a non-multiple of eight, non-ASCII, malformed JSON, duplicate object keys, or a noncanonical encoding. Canonical encoding is compact ASCII JSON, lexicographically sorted object keys, no whitespace, and ordinary decimal nonnegative integers. Boolean values are not integer identifiers. Schema depth is bounded by a constant. The complete top-level key set is exactly `bags, edges, gates, n, outputs`.\n\n- Packet $n$ equals the input length.\n- Input wires have identifiers $0,\\ldots,n-1$. Gate $j$'s output is wire $n+j$. The ordered gate list uses records with exactly keys args,op. The basis is ZERO, ONE (arity 0), NOT (arity 1), AND, OR, XOR (arity 2). Every argument is an integer in $[0,n+j)$, so gates are acyclic. Duplicate arguments are permitted.\n- outputs is an ordered list of exactly $n$ existing wire identifiers. Repeated output wires are permitted and cause no semantic difficulty.\n- bags is a nonempty list of sorted lists of distinct existing wire identifiers. Every wire, including unused gates and inputs, occurs in some bag. For every gate, one bag contains the full scope consisting of its output and all arguments.\n- edges is a sorted list of distinct pairs $[a,b]$ with $0\\le a<b<\\text{number of bags}$. This graph must be a tree. For every wire, the bags containing it induce a connected subtree (running intersection).\n- Every bag has size $\\le 1+c\\lfloor\\log_2(n+2)\\rfloor$. The floor is computed exactly using integer bit length. In particular the tree width is $\\le c\\log_2(n+2)$.\n\nAll checks operate on the entire generated packet at the current n and have polynomial bit complexity in n+q for fixed record. The validator is not a finite-length promise or a test-based extrapolation. Its use at every evaluation makes valid trace structure an invariant of the evaluator protocol.\n\n#### Actual evaluation and inversion\n\n$E(\\Pi,A,d,c)(1^n,x)$, for $|x|=n$, reruns the clocked compiler and validator. On any failure it returns $x$. On success it evaluates the validated gates in order on $x$ and returns the selected $n$ output bits. Thus malformed compiler data never serves as a certificate for some separately named function. It changes the actual function to identity at that length. Wrong-length arguments are rejected; $n=0$ is outside the declared security-parameter domain.\n\n$I_R(1^n,y)$, for $|y|=n$, reruns exactly the same compiler and validator. On failure it returns $y$. On success it constructs the Boolean gate constraints, pins each selected output wire to the corresponding bit of $y$, and performs exact separator-table dynamic programming on the validated tree. It reconstructs and returns input bits if feasible and returns the distinguished failure symbol otherwise. Theorem O.5.3n proves the all-length inversion and coverage statement. There is no shared randomness or race between the evaluator and inverter.\n\n#### Units, budgets, certifiers, falsifiers, provenance, consumers\n\n- Units: $n$ counts input bits; $q$ counts compiler dispatches; packet length counts bits; width is bag cardinality minus one; running times count bit operations on a standard deterministic machine. All are dimensionless. No thermodynamic quantity is equated to a candidate count.\n- Budget: a record-specific polynomial with coefficients/degrees depending only on fixed $\\Pi,A,d,c$. No common exponent over all records is claimed. No advice, trapdoor, hidden state, or inaccessible real constant is used.\n- Certifiers: the exact syntactic record schema, total clocked compiler, full packet validator, the constructive all-length source-faithfulness/inversion/coverage proof, populated all-length examples, and independently replayable diagnostic code. Finite diagnostics support implementation checking only.\n- Falsifiers: an admitted $R$ and length $n$ on which the stipulated wrapper is not total or polynomial for fixed $R$; an accepted packet violating gate faithfulness or tree-decomposition conditions; a valid image challenge for which reconstruction fails; a missing admitted-record case in coverage; or a source/scope mismatch that defeats the method-class no-go. Unequal-fiber statistics are explicitly checked.\n- Scope: self-contained standard-model theorem, using the established trace-width elimination idea and Definition O.5.2f. This is not an empirical physical claim, a new OWF construction, $\\mathrm{P}\\ne\\mathrm{NP}$, nonexistence of all OWFs, or locality-implies-width.\n\nThis realizes the finite tuple already fixed in Definition O.5.3m. It does not change its domain, compiler semantics, constants, equivalence or result. The logical tuple is (version, $\\Pi$, A, d, c, payload), with arbitrary finite bytes $\\Pi$ and payload and positive integers A,d,c. One injective canonical serialization is compact sorted-key ASCII JSON with exactly these fields:\n\n- $A$, $d$, $c$: positive JSON integers, not booleans or floating point numbers.\n- version: exactly the string O04-MC-1.\n- program_hex, payload_hex: even-length lowercase hexadecimal strings encoding arbitrary finite bytes, including the empty string.\n\nNo other fields, duplicate keys, whitespace, noncanonical order, or noncanonical number representation are accepted. Hexadecimal encoding is injective, the positive integers have unique decimal encodings, and canonical JSON gives exactly one byte string per logical tuple. Thus this concrete encoding preserves the contract's exact byte equivalence. Arbitrary payload bytes can encode any desired theory, checker, proposed proof, bridge and dependency ledger; no semantic restriction or parsing of that payload is part of admission or evaluation.\n\nThe total admission function checks exactly the displayed finite schema by its literal decoding and finite field tests. It deliberately does not decide whether $\\Pi$ is a valid compiler, whether it succeeds at all lengths, or whether the asserted proof is sound. Invalid compiler bytes are admitted records and produce fallback. Enumerating finite byte strings in length-lexicographic order and retaining precisely those accepted by admit_record gives a complete effective generator for the entire record universe, since every finite admitted record appears after finitely many strings. This is an enumeration argument, not a claim that one finite diagnostic run exhausted the class.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o53n-uniform-all-length-inversion-and-complete-method-class-refutation\"></a>**Theorem O.5.3n (Uniform All-Length Inversion and Complete Method-Class Refutation).**\n\n#### 1. Claim and status\n\nThe exact class is frozen in Definition O.5.3m, whose fingerprint is recorded in its sealed source record. It is the existing O04-MC-1 contract for the original class-relative alternative. All constants, program semantics, fallback behavior, equivalence, budgets, certifiers, falsifiers and consumers belong to that contract, independently of the diagnostic results. Read-only input register 0 means instructions cannot write register 0; other registers are mutable. The reference implementation enforces this syntactically. No assumption about an external evaluator is present.\n\n**Main theorem.** For every admitted O04-MC-1 record $R$ there is one fixed uniform deterministic polynomial-time inverter $I_{R}$ such that, for every $n\\ge 1$ and every $y\\in\\operatorname{image}(f_R,n)$,\n\n$$\nf_{R,n}(I_R(1^n,y)) = y.\n$$\n\nThe inverter also rejects every nonimage n-bit challenge. Consequently its Definition O.5.2f any-preimage advantage is exactly 1 at every n, including for arbitrarily unequal fiber sizes. Therefore no admitted R can be an unconditionally accepted sound O.5.2f hardness certificate. The conclusion applies to every admitted output of every proposal algorithm, regardless of its proof strategy or arbitrary claimed proof text.\n\nThis is a complete negative classification of the fixed protocol proposal class. It does not say that every OWF candidate is in that class, that no OWF exists, that P equals or differs from NP, or that every PU/local physical realization has low-width traces.\n\n#### 2. The compiler and validator are uniformly polynomial and total\n\nFix $R$, hence its finite program $\\Pi$ and positive integers $A,d,c$. For $n\\ge 1$ write $q=A(n+2)^d$ and $B=n+q$. Compiler schema validation takes a constant depending on fixed $\\Pi$, plus comparisons against $B$ as necessary. Malformed $\\Pi$ immediately selects fallback. A valid program executes at most $q$ dispatches. Its read-only register 0 starts at $n$; all other register values and memory contents/addresses lie between 0 and $B$; at most $q$ memory cells are touched. Multiplication computes at most $B$ squared before rejecting overflow. Each number requires $O(\\log(B+1))$ bits. A deterministic simulation can store the sparse memory as a sequential list and scan it on each access. Thus even without a unit-cost RAM or hash-table assumption, $q$ dispatches take polynomial bit time in $q+n+|\\Pi|$. An explicitly counted HALT, overflow, missing instruction, zero divisor, non-bit output, or timeout terminates in the prescribed status. Arbitrary compiler code can never turn into an unclocked assumption.\n\nThere are at most $q$ emitted bits. A packet is parsed from this finite stream. Non-byte, non-ASCII, duplicate-key, noncanonical or schema-invalid streams fail. Parsing decimal strings takes polynomial bit time in their character length using elementary arithmetic. A decimal value with $b$ digits has $O(b)$ bits, even if it will later be rejected as an invalid wire index. The reference implementation removes Python's optional decimal-digit limit and catches malformed/deep JSON; schema-invalid nesting is rejected. Valid packets have constant schema depth. The theorem can equivalently use a direct constant-depth schema parser, so host parser stack behavior is not a premise of the proof.\n\nEvery accepted array length and identifier lies within a polynomial in n+q. All wires are enumerated, every gate scope is checked, the whole tree is checked, and running intersection is checked for every wire by graph traversal. Width comparison uses the exact integer value floor(log2(n+2)). Even naive nested loops make these checks polynomial in n+q. A large bag is rejected before its assignments are enumerated. The compiler, validator, and actual evaluator are independent of the challenge, and deterministic at fixed n.\n\nThus exactly one of two exhaustive branches occurs at each length:\n\n1. The compiler and all validation checks pass, yielding the same accepted packet for the evaluator and inverter.\n2. Some check or the clock fails, yielding the identity evaluator at that length.\n\nThere is no third branch where a rejected packet is treated as a faithful source certificate for a separately supplied function. In particular, a proof string claiming that such a packet describes another function cannot change the wrapper's actual semantics.\n\n#### 3. Faithfulness is proved from the actual gates\n\nTake an accepted packet at length $n$, with $g$ gates and wire set $V=\\{0,\\ldots,n+g-1\\}$. Inputs are $x_0,\\ldots,x_{n-1}$. Gate $j$ defines its own output $z_{n+j}$ by its fixed Boolean operation on earlier wires. Let $G(x,z)$ be the conjunction of these gate equalities. For each $x$, induction over gate order yields exactly one full wire assignment extending $x$ and satisfying $G$. Each permitted operation is a total Boolean function; all references are earlier, so the induction has no missing or cyclic case.\n\nLet $o_0,\\ldots,o_{n-1}$ be the selected output wires. For any $n$-bit challenge $y$ append unary equalities $z_{o_i}=y_i$, using $x_{i}$ for input-wire coordinates. Then\n\nexists internal wire values: $G(x,z)$ and all selected-output equalities\nif and only if the actual validated circuit evaluation on $x$ equals $y$.\n\nOutput wires may repeat. If repeated positions prescribe inconsistent bits, the unary equalities contradict each other and the challenge is a nonimage. Input wires may be directly selected; they are pinned exactly the same way. Unused inputs, unused gate outputs, repeated gate arguments, constants, and empty bags all cause no faithfulness gap. Every retained wire and gate scope has been validated and no oracle or semantic equality test is needed.\n\nThis relation is the actual evaluator's full source trace. On a fallback length the actual trace is the identity relation and is handled directly.\n\n#### 4. Exact elimination and reconstruction\n\nRoot the validated tree at its first bag. Assign each gate constraint to the first bag containing its full scope and each output pin to the first bag containing that wire. These assignments are always defined by the validator. Let $V_{t}$ be the union of the bags in the subtree at t. Define $S_{t}$ to be the set of assignments $\\alpha$ to $B_{t}$ that extend to an assignment on $V_{t}$ satisfying all constraints assigned in that subtree.\n\nFor a leaf, enumerate all assignments to $B_{t}$ and keep those satisfying its local constraints. For an internal node, keep $\\alpha$ precisely if it satisfies its local constraints and, for every child u, some $\\beta$ in $S_{u}$ agrees with $\\alpha$ on $B_{t}$ intersect $B_{u}$. A separator table stores one such $\\beta$ for every feasible separator value. All orders can be fixed lexicographically.\n\nNecessity follows by restriction. For sufficiency, choose the stored child assignment and its feasible extension for each child. If a variable is shared between two child subtrees, the unique path joining its occurrences passes through $B_{t}$ and both child root bags. Running intersection therefore forces it into each of those bags, and both extensions give it alpha's value. A variable shared between $B_{t}$ and a child subtree likewise lies in the child root bag. Hence all extensions agree on all overlaps and combine into a consistent assignment on $V_{t}$. Every constraint is satisfied where it was assigned. This proves the recurrence by induction, including branching trees and empty separators.\n\nThe root table is nonempty exactly when some complete satisfying wire assignment exists. If empty, return the distinguished failure symbol. If nonempty, choose its first assignment and traverse downward through stored separator representatives. The same overlap argument shows that reconstruction assigns every wire consistently and satisfies every gate and pin. Returning the $n$ input values therefore gives an $n$-bit $x$ with $f_{R,n}(x)=y$, by Section 3. No uniqueness of the input preimage is required.\n\nIf y is in the image, the original input and its forward gate values provide a satisfying witness, so the root cannot be empty. If y is a nonimage, faithfulness precludes any root witness. On a fallback length, the image is all n-bit strings and returning y is correct. Thus the theorem holds pointwise at every length, with exact nonimage rejection in the accepted-circuit branch and no nonimages in the identity branch.\n\n#### 5. Bit complexity, one fixed inverter, and collision law\n\nLet w be the maximum accepted bag size. The validator ensures\n\n$$\nw\\le 1+c\\cdot\\lfloor\\log_2(n+2)\\rfloor,\\quad\\text{hence}\\quad 2^w\\le 2(n+2)^c.\n$$\n\nThe instance has at most polynomially many wires, gates, bags, tree edges and bag incidences in $n+q$. Exact Boolean tables have at most $2^w$ assignments per bag. Separator projection and lookup can use deterministic sorted tables; alternatively even pairwise scans over child and parent states cost only $2^{2w}$. Reconstruction is polynomial. No assumption about hashing, unit-cost large integers, or a constant number of bags is needed.\n\nFor definiteness let $S=n+q+|\\Pi|+c+A+d+10$. Elementary sequential implementations of the clocked compiler, integer parser, validator, table joins and reconstruction admit the conservative bound $K\\cdot S^{8}\\cdot 2^{2w}$, after increasing a fixed effective $K$ to cover finite program decoding. For fixed $R$ and $d\\ge 1$, $S$ is $O_R((n+2)^d)$. Thus an effectively computable record-dependent constant $K_{R}$ bounds the whole inverter by\n\n$$\nK_R\\cdot(n+2)^{8d+2c+10}\n$$\n\nbit operations. This deliberately loose bound includes compiling, validating, image membership, reconstruction and final output; it is not a claimed tight estimate. The evaluation wrapper is polynomial for the same reason without the exponential-in-width table factor. A timeout branch is also charged the entire q dispatches. There is no unbounded work hidden in an invalid program or malformed packet.\n\nThe inverter's code contains only the fixed finite record parameters and fixed protocol algorithms. It receives $1^n$ and the challenge; it uses zero random coins. Consequently it is one deterministic uniform polynomial-time machine for each fixed $R$, hence a member of the stipulated uniform classical PPT class. Its polynomial degree may depend on the fixed record. The theorem does not exchange quantifiers to demand one common degree or one inverter independent of the family description for all records.\n\nFor every input $x$, the image challenge $f_{R,n}(x)$ is inverted to some $x'$ of length $n$ with the same image. Therefore, for $X$ uniform on $\\{0,1\\}^n$,\n\n$$\n\\Pr[f_{R,n}(I_R(1^n,f_{R,n}(X)))=f_{R,n}(X)]=\\sum_{\\text{image points }y}(|f_{R,n}^{-1}(y)|/2^n)\\cdot 1=1.\n$$\n\nThis is exactly the locked pushforward law, not uniform-on-image sampling. Recovering X itself is neither asserted nor required. Unequal fibers and collisions do not weaken the result.\n\n#### 6. Full evaluator-class to proposal-class coverage\n\nThe underlying representation theorem alone would have left an application premise: whether a separately supplied evaluator is faithfully represented. Here the record definition and wrapper remove that premise, not by assuming it away but by defining the actual evaluated function.\n\n**Coverage lemma.** Every admitted finite record R defines a fixed total uniform polynomial-time family. At every length its circuit branch has the required faithful bounded-width source decomposition, or its actual evaluator is identity. These branches exhaust every R and every n.\n\n**Proof.** Record admission fixes the finite compiler and constants. Sections 2 and 3 prove termination, the full case partition, polynomial evaluation and exact circuit semantics for every such program, including malformed and nonhalting programs. The validator establishes every structural premise in the accepted branch. Identity is explicit in all other cases. Arbitrary additional proof/theory/checker/bridge strings do not enter evaluation. Section 4 covers both branches. There are no unlisted compiler, packet, length, or proof-strategy cases. ∎\n\n**Proposal transport.** Let $M$ be the complete set of syntactically admitted O04-MC-1 attempts, with byte equality as its equivalence relation. For every $R\\in M$, the coverage lemma supplies exactly its actual evaluator to the inversion theorem. If $R$ were an unconditionally accepted Definition O.5.2f certificate, its standard-model soundness warrant would imply O.5.2.9 for all uniform PPT inverters, including this one fixed $I_{R}$. Taking exponent 1 would give advantage $<n^{-1}$ eventually. Advantage is 1 at every length, a contradiction. Therefore every $R\\in M$ fails a necessary clause of accepted O.5.2f certification.\n\nFor a proposal algorithm P, every admitted output is some R in M, so the same implication holds for every P and every admitted output, not merely the selected examples. A procedure may generate arbitrary additional outputs using another evaluator; those outputs are not silently relabeled as members. This is complete classification of a declared output-constrained method class, rather than a claim about every possible future mathematical method.\n\nSoundness cannot be bypassed by choosing an inconsistent theory or a checker that accepts every string: Definition O.5.2f includes the soundness and standard-model interpretation warrant. The theorem does not claim that no byte string can assert hardness or that no unsound checker can accept it.\n\n#### 7. Contains the full uniformly supplied low-width circuit-trace class\n\nThis protocol is not a finite whitelist. Suppose an independently given deterministic polynomial-time generator produces, at every $n$, an acyclic circuit in the fixed bounded-fanin basis, $n$ selected outputs, and a polynomially described valid tree decomposition of its complete gate trace. Suppose width $\\le c_0\\log_2(n+2)$ for a fixed real $c_{0}$. Canonicalizing finite identifiers, bag order and edge order and emitting the stipulated packet takes polynomial time. The generator can be simulated by the fixed RAM with polynomial overhead and polynomially bounded numeric data, as follows.\n\nA standard deterministic Turing computation with a known polynomial clock $T(n)$ visits at most polynomially many cells. Store each cell as a small integer at a natural memory address, with a polynomially bounded offset for signed tape locations. A finite control branch is compiled into finite labels, JZ/JMP and truncated-subtraction comparisons; head movement and input-symbol tests use addition/subtraction. Constantly many registers suffice because other machine data can be kept in memory. Bit output uses OUT. A fixed polynomial in $n$ bounds simulated dispatches, all addresses and all stored values. Choose positive $A,d$ large enough that $q$ dominates these bounds at every $n\\ge 1$. Register 0 remains unchanged; use working registers for head positions. Thus the simulation never overflows or times out and emits the complete packet at all lengths.\n\nChoose integer $c\\ge\\lceil 2c_0\\rceil$, with $c\\ge 1$. Since $\\log_2(n+2)\\le 2\\lfloor\\log_2(n+2)\\rfloor$ for $n\\ge 1$, the original width bound satisfies the protocol cutoff. The wrapper therefore evaluates exactly the given circuit family, never fallback. All such generators are included after this explicit clock and canonical-encoding compilation. Any other fixed finite bounded-fanin Boolean gate basis is also covered by an explicit local expansion: replace each gate by a constant-size circuit over ZERO, ONE, NOT, AND, OR, XOR, preserving its input/output terminal wires. Keep the original bags and attach to one original bag containing that gate scope a new bag containing all terminal and internal gadget wires. Every gadget constraint is contained there; each private internal wire occurs only there; and every terminal wire remains connected because the attachment bag contains its original scope. The new width is at most the maximum of the old width and a basis-dependent constant. Reindex the expanded acyclic gates topologically. Constants, input/output copies, and fixed-fanin truth tables are covered by the same construction. Increasing fixed integer $c$ if necessary therefore gives the protocol cutoff at all lengths, and the uniform size/time remain polynomial. No such preservation is claimed for arbitrary polynomial-time encoding transformations or nondeterministic constraint relations.\n\nThis inclusion concerns circuit-faithful traces. Arbitrary semantically faithful constraints with auxiliary witnesses lie outside this inclusion; no effective compiler is claimed for every such representation. It does contain every uniformly supplied circuit/decomposition pair meeting the stated conditions, and may additionally contain length mixtures selected by invalid-output fallback. No converse width theorem for arbitrary P functions or local PU systems is used.\n\n#### 8. Populated nonempty all-length examples\n\nThe three fixed compiler families below are existing finite canonical RAM instruction lists. These are fixed programs independent of $n$, not host-generated tables selected by $n$. Each uses $A=1000,d=3,c=1$. The program source algorithm emits canonical JSON with integer printing by repeated division by 10. It keeps $n$ in read-only register 0; loop index in register 10; printing scratch in other registers and finite memory. It emits exactly these all-length families:\n\n1. **Identity:** no gates; outputs $[0,\\ldots,n-1]$; singleton bags $[i]$ on a path. Width 0, $n\\ge 1$.\n2. **Bitwise complement:** NOT gates on each $x_{i}$; outputs $[n,\\ldots,2n-1]$; bags $[i,n+i]$ on a path. Width 1, $n\\ge 1$.\n3. **Unequal-fiber family:** $n=1$ uses identity. For $n\\ge 2$, gate $n$ is $x_{0}$ AND $x_{1}$, gate $n+1$ is ZERO; outputs $(\\text{gate }n,\\text{gate }n+1,x_2,\\ldots,x_{n-1})$. Bags $[0,1,n]$, $[n+1]$, and $[i]$ for $2\\le i<n$, connected as a path. Width 2 and cutoff at least 2 for $n\\ge 2$. Fibers have size 3 or 1, with fixed trailing bits.\n\nEvery listed scope is visibly covered; each wire appears in one bag, so running intersection is immediate. The program's decimal routine terminates in $O(\\log(n+2))$ digit iterations. Each whole compiler has a fixed number of literal-emission instructions and $O(n)$ calls to that routine. The emitted identifiers are at most $2n+1$ and printing constants at most 128. The compiler uses $O(n\\log(n+2))$ dispatches. Direct inspection gives at most $10n+10$ integer-print calls, each costing at most $100(\\log_2(n+2)+2)$ dispatches, plus at most $1000n+2000$ literal/loop dispatches. Since $\\log_2(n+2)\\le n+1$, the total is at most $1000(n^{2}+5n+5)$, strictly below the chosen $q=1000(n+2)^{3}$ for all $n\\ge 1$. Lemma O.5.3o details the template accounting. Stored values and addresses stay well below $B=n+q$. Hence these are actual admitted, nonempty, all-length circuit branches.\n\nThe finite checks additionally use one fixed compiler that emits the $n=2$ unequal-fiber packet at every length. It selects the circuit branch exactly at $n=2$ and identity elsewhere. This demonstrates why the theorem must cover length-dependent mixtures, without asserting any hard-to-decide all-length compiler validity premise.\n\n<a id=\"lemma-o53o-all-length-compiler-nonvacuity-and-clock-bounds\"></a>**Lemma O.5.3o (All-Length Compiler Nonvacuity and Clock Bounds).**\n\n1. A literal byte costs exactly 16 dispatches (eight SET/OUT pairs). The NOT family has the largest per-index literal body: paired bags use 3 characters, path edges use 3 characters, the NOT gate record uses fewer than 24 characters, and list commas together contribute fewer than 5 characters per index. This is fewer than $35n+60$ emitted literal characters overall, hence fewer than $560n+960$ literal dispatches. Identity and the unequal-fiber family have smaller bodies; the unequal family's $n=1$ branch emits one fixed packet of fewer than 60 characters.\n2. Each loop_n iteration uses at most 12 non-print, non-literal dispatches, including its initial/end tests amortized with an additional fixed allowance. At most five such loops execute. All other arithmetic setup and branch bookkeeping is covered by $200n+500$ dispatches. Consequently $1000n+2000$ bounds all instructions outside integer printing, including HALT, for each family. These bounds are deliberately larger than the individual literal and loop counts.\n3. The NOT family uses $2n$ numbers in bags, $2(n-1)$ in edges, $n$ in gate arguments, 1 in the $n$ field, and $n$ in outputs, totaling $6n-1$ calls to number. Identity and unequal-fiber use at most that many. The loose common bound $10n+10$ is valid. All printed values are at most $2n+1$.\n4. For a positive $D$-digit value, number uses at most 10 initialization dispatches, at most 6 per extracted decimal digit, and at most 40 per emitted decimal digit. Its zero branch uses fewer than 25 dispatches. Decimal digit count is no greater than binary digit count, hence at most $\\log_2(n+2)+2$ for values $\\le 2n+1$. Thus every call costs at most $100(\\log_2(n+2)+2)$.\n5. The total dispatch count is at most\n\n   (10n+10)*100(log2(n+2)+2)+1000n+2000.\n\n   Since $2^{n+1}\\ge n+2$ for every integer $n\\ge 1$, $\\log_2(n+2)\\le n+1$. The total is therefore at most $1000(n^{2}+5n+5)$, strictly less than $1000(n+2)^{3}$. HALT is included, so the clock cannot reject at its boundary.\n6. All immediate numeric data are at most 128, all live computed non-address values are at most $\\max(128,2n+1)$, and decimal scratch memory addresses are bounded by the digit count. Loop and register arithmetic never creates a larger value. For $n\\ge 1$ these are strictly below $B=n+1000(n+2)^{3}$. The input register is never written. No division uses zero.\n\nThe programs therefore halt without failure at every length and emit the exact packets proved valid in Theorem O.5.3n. These are actual all-length populated compiler examples, not just existential simulations or finitely supplied packets.\n\n<a id=\"definition-o53h-physical-hardness-postulate-and-ppt-to-pu-simulation-certificate\"></a>**Definition O.5.3h (Physical Hardness Postulate and PPT-to-PU Simulation Certificate).** Fix one deterministic uniform length-preserving family\n$$\nf_{\\mathrm{PHP},n}:\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}^n\n$$\nwith a checked polynomial-time evaluator, $X_n\\leftarrow U_n$, and challenge $Y_n=f_{\\mathrm{PHP},n}(X_n)$. For every $n$, declare physical input and output state spaces $\\mathcal Z_n^{\\mathrm{PHP}}$ and $\\mathcal W_n^{\\mathrm{PHP}}$, together with uniform maps\n$$\n\\operatorname{Enc}^{\\mathrm{PHP}}_n:\n\\{1^n\\}\\times\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\mathcal Z_n^{\\mathrm{PHP}},\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Dec}^{\\mathrm{PHP}}_n:\n\\mathcal W_n^{\\mathrm{PHP}}\\to\\{0,1\\}^n\\cup\\{\\bot\\}.\n$$\nThe encoder prepares the physical representation of the security parameter and challenge; the decoder returns either one candidate preimage or the failure symbol $\\bot$. For each permitted uniform PU protocol family $\\Pi$ with declared physical-randomness law $R_{\\Pi,n}$, require the typed action\n$$\n\\Pi_n:\n\\mathcal Z_n^{\\mathrm{PHP}}\\times\\operatorname{supp}(R_{\\Pi,n})\n\\to\\mathcal W_n^{\\mathrm{PHP}}.\n$$\nThese section-local maps are not the sampling map $E_n$ or any other encoder or decoder already used elsewhere in PU. A uniform realization map assigns each protocol family and security parameter a physically realizable implementing microstate\n$$\n\\operatorname{Real}_{\\mathrm{PU}}(\\Pi,n)=\\mu_{\\Pi,n}\\in\\mathcal S_{\\mathrm{phys}}.\n$$\nThe microstate includes the encoder, decoder, randomness source, error correction, repetitions, controller, workspace, and every other implementation component. Predictive Physical Complexity is applied only to that microstate, as $C_P(\\mu_{\\Pi,n})$, in accordance with Equation (1); it is not applied directly to the abstract protocol.\n\nFor such a protocol family $\\Pi$, define\n$$\n\\operatorname{Adv}^{\\mathrm{PU}}_{f_{\\mathrm{PHP}},\\Pi}(n)\n:=\n\\Pr_{\\substack{X_n\\leftarrow U_n\\\\\\omega\\leftarrow R_{\\Pi,n}}}\n\\left[\n\\begin{array}{c}\nx'=\\operatorname{Dec}^{\\mathrm{PHP}}_n\\!\\left(\n\\Pi_n\\!\\left(\\operatorname{Enc}^{\\mathrm{PHP}}_n(1^n,f_{\\mathrm{PHP},n}(X_n)),\\omega\\right)\n\\right)\\in\\{0,1\\}^n,\\\\\nf_{\\mathrm{PHP},n}(x')=f_{\\mathrm{PHP},n}(X_n)\n\\end{array}\n\\right].\n$$\n\nA PPT-to-PU simulation certificate $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{PPT}\\to\\mathrm{PU}}$ proves that for every fixed uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}$ inverter $\\mathcal I$ there are a uniform compiled PU protocol family $\\Pi_{\\mathcal I}$, polynomials $p_{\\mathcal I}$ and $q_{\\mathcal I}\\ge1$, and a negligible function $\\nu_{\\mathcal I}$ such that, for all sufficiently large $n$,\n$$\nC_P(\\mu_{\\Pi_{\\mathcal I},n})\\le p_{\\mathcal I}(n),\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Adv}^{\\mathrm{PU}}_{f_{\\mathrm{PHP}},\\Pi_{\\mathcal I}}(n)\n\\ge\n\\frac{\\operatorname{Adv}^{\\mathrm{inv}}_{f_{\\mathrm{PHP}},\\mathcal I}(n)}{q_{\\mathcal I}(n)}\n-\\nu_{\\mathcal I}(n).\n\\tag{O.5.3.6}\n$$\nThe physical input is the encoding of $(1^n,Y_n)$; physical randomness has the same law as the inverter's coins or a declared negligible total-variation discrepancy absorbed into $\\nu_{\\mathcal I}$; and decoding uses the standard any-preimage predicate. The compiler may depend on the fixed inverter's code and polynomial clock, but not on the sampled challenge, a hidden trapdoor, nonuniform advice, or length-specific tables. The security parameter and every encoding and resource overhead change by at most a polynomial.\n\nLet $\\mathsf{PU}_{\\mathrm{poly}}$ be the uniform permitted protocol families $\\Pi$ for which $C_P(\\mu_{\\Pi,n})\\le p_\\Pi(n)$ eventually for some fixed polynomial $p_\\Pi$. The marked Physical Hardness Postulate is\n$$\n[\\mathrm{PHP}]_{f_{\\mathrm{PHP}}}:\n\\quad\n\\forall\\Pi\\in\\mathsf{PU}_{\\mathrm{poly}}\\;\n\\forall c\\in\\mathbb N_{>0}\\;\n\\exists N_{\\Pi,c}\\;\n\\forall n\\ge N_{\\Pi,c}:\n\\operatorname{Adv}^{\\mathrm{PU}}_{f_{\\mathrm{PHP}},\\Pi}(n)<n^{-c}.\n\\tag{O.5.3.7}\n$$\nThe postulate is an unresolved physical hardness hypothesis unless it receives an independent accepted proof object. The simulation certificate has its own independent status; if it is assumed rather than proved, both entries remain in the dependency ledger.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o53h1-conditional-physical-hardness-implication\"></a>**Theorem O.5.3h.1 (Conditional Physical-Hardness Implication).** In the extended theory containing $[\\mathrm{PHP}]_{f_{\\mathrm{PHP}}}$ and an accepted $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{PPT}\\to\\mathrm{PU}}$,\n$$\n[\\mathrm{PHP}]_{f_{\\mathrm{PHP}}}\n\\wedge\n\\bigl[\\operatorname{Acc}_{\\mathrm{cert}}(\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{PPT}\\to\\mathrm{PU}})=1\\bigr]\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nf_{\\mathrm{PHP}}\\text{ is a standard one-way function}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\mathsf{P}\\ne\\mathsf{NP}.\n$$\nThe conclusion is conditional on every unresolved antecedent shown in the ledger.\n\n*Proof.* Suppose a fixed uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}$ inverter $\\mathcal I$ has non-negligible inversion success. Then there is a polynomial $r_{\\mathcal I}$ and infinitely many $n$ such that\n$$\n\\operatorname{Adv}^{\\mathrm{inv}}_{f_{\\mathrm{PHP}},\\mathcal I}(n)\n\\ge\\frac1{r_{\\mathcal I}(n)}.\n$$\nEquation (O.5.3.6) gives, on all sufficiently large lengths in this infinite set,\n$$\n\\operatorname{Adv}^{\\mathrm{PU}}_{f_{\\mathrm{PHP}},\\Pi_{\\mathcal I}}(n)\n\\ge\n\\frac1{q_{\\mathcal I}(n)r_{\\mathcal I}(n)}-\\nu_{\\mathcal I}(n)\n\\ge\n\\frac1{2q_{\\mathcal I}(n)r_{\\mathcal I}(n)},\n$$\nbecause $\\nu_{\\mathcal I}$ is negligible. The compiled realization satisfies $C_P(\\mu_{\\Pi_{\\mathcal I},n})\\le p_{\\mathcal I}(n)$ and has inverse-polynomial success at infinitely many lengths, contradicting (O.5.3.7). Thus every uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}$ inverter has negligible success and $f_{\\mathrm{PHP}}$ is one-way. The prefix-search proof of Theorem O.5.2g supplies the mathematical implication from one-way-function existence to $\\mathsf{P}\\ne\\mathsf{NP}$. Here that proof is used under the displayed hypotheses; the unconditional certificate antecedent of Theorem O.5.2g is not asserted. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o53h2-formal-counterexamples-and-finite-n-tests\"></a>**Corollary O.5.3h.2 (Formal Counterexamples and Finite-$n$ Tests).** On a branch with an accepted simulation certificate:\n\n1. a uniform PU protocol family carrying proofs that $C_P(\\mu_{\\Pi,n})$ is polynomially bounded and that its asymptotic inversion success is non-negligible refutes $[\\mathrm{PHP}]_{f_{\\mathrm{PHP}}}$;\n2. a uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}$ inverter with non-negligible success, together with the verified simulation guarantee, yields such a PHP counterexample; and\n3. a polynomial-time SAT decider yields a perfect polynomial-time inverter by prefix search and therefore yields the same contradiction through the simulation certificate.\n\nAn abstract inverter or SAT algorithm is a mathematical counterexample, not by itself an empirical falsifier. A finite laboratory run tests only separately registered finite-$n$ performance bounds; it does not establish or refute the asymptotic postulate without a uniform scalable protocol and its complexity and success certificates.\n\n*Proof.* Clause 1 is the negation of the eventual-negligibility statement (O.5.3.7). Clause 2 follows from (O.5.3.6). For clause 3, a polynomial-time SAT decider implies $\\mathsf{P}=\\mathsf{NP}$ and the prefix construction returns a preimage for every image point; Equation (O.5.3.6) transports that success to the registered physical protocol. The finite-$n$ statement follows because finitely many samples cannot decide universal asymptotic quantifiers. ∎\n\n<a id=\"remark-o53i-exact-status\"></a>**Remark O.5.3i (Exact Status).** The theorem-level conclusions of this section are:\n\n1. oracle-natural derivations from registered oracle-stable premises cannot discharge standard one-wayness or $\\mathsf{P}\\ne\\mathsf{NP}$;\n2. Proposition O.5.3f gives a deterministic fixed-exponent, infinitely-often, all-image obstruction, not average-case one-wayness;\n3. Proposition O.5.3g gives a weak $\\mathsf{AC}^0$ inversion bound whose displayed reduction does not discharge negligible inversion; and\n4. $[\\mathrm{PHP}]_{f_{\\mathrm{PHP}}}$ plus the quantified PPT-to-PU simulation certificate implies $\\mathsf{P}\\ne\\mathsf{NP}$ only conditionally.\n\nNo accepted unconditional OWF or direct-separation proof object is supplied. The manuscript certificate ledger is therefore\n$$\n\\boxed{\n\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{OWF}}=\\varnothing_{\\mathrm{cert}},\n\\qquad\n\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{sep}}=\\varnothing_{\\mathrm{cert}},\n\\qquad\n\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{PPT}\\to\\mathrm{PU}}=\\mathfrak C_{O.5.3l}.\n}\n$$\nThe third entry is the certificate of Theorem O.5.3l: on the registered heralded-error source model fixed there it establishes the conjunction (O.5.3.6) of Definition O.5.3h, including the polynomial $C_P$ bound, for every fixed uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}$ inverter. It does not prove the Physical Hardness Postulate, the existence of a one-way function or $\\mathsf{P}\\ne\\mathsf{NP}$, which remain conditional as in Theorem O.5.3h.1; unheralded noise lies outside the model. Nonrelativizing routes covered by neither Theorem O.5.3c nor the algebrization barrier of Theorem O.5.3c.2 remain unclassified, not ruled out.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o53j-exact-uniform-reversible-compilation-of-ppt-inverters\"></a>**Theorem O.5.3j (Exact Uniform Reversible Compilation of PPT Inverters).** Let $\\mathcal I$ be a specified uniform probabilistic Turing machine on inputs $(1^n,y)$ with $y\\in\\{0,1\\}^n$ and $r(n)$ independent fair random bits. Assume explicit integer-valued polynomial bounds $T(n),S(n),r(n)$ for its running time, tape space and coin count. Use a length-tagged, zero-padded binary encoding in a polynomial-width output register, with a distinguished failure flag; the output in the following display denotes that encoding. There is a log-space-uniform family of reversible Boolean circuits over NOT, CNOT and Toffoli gates, with polynomial size and polynomially many clean ancillas, such that\n$$\nR_n:\\ |1^n,y,r,0,0\\rangle\n\\longmapsto\n|1^n,y,r,\\mathcal I(1^n,y;r),0\\rangle.\n\\tag{O.5.3j.1}\n$$\nThe last zero denotes all work ancillas. Sampling the declared fair coins and decoding the output reproduces the classical output law exactly, with total-variation error zero.\n\nThis abstract compiler does not populate $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{PPT}\\to\\mathrm{PU}}$. That certificate also requires a response-faithful PU carrier for the gates and scalable fair coins, polynomial $C_P$ for the complete implementing microstate, and the physical error/success guarantee. Theorem O.5.3l supplies them on its registered heralded-error source model.\n\n*Proof.* Freeze the random tape as the input string $r$ and pad a halted computation by an identity transition through time $T(n)$. Represent its configurations by polynomially many bits for the tapes, head positions, machine state and output encoding. The transition rule of the specified machine gives a polynomial-size Boolean circuit for each time step. A log-space generator enumerates time, tape-cell, bit and gate indices with $O(\\log n)$-bit counters; the local transition rule determines predecessor-wire indices from those counters and the constant machine description. Input/coin access, output copying and the padding rule use the same indexed construction. Thus the full tableau circuit has polynomial size and a log-space gate generator.\n\nReplace each Boolean gate by a constant-size reversible NOT/CNOT/Toffoli gadget using fresh ancillas. Compute the encoded output, copy it to a clean output register by CNOTs, and enumerate the compute gadgets in reverse order. Each gadget's wire indices and its reverse index are computable with the same logarithmic workspace. Uncomputation restores every work ancilla while retaining the original input, coins and copied output. The resulting decoded output agrees with $\\mathcal I$ for every input and coin string. Averaging over the identical fair-coin law proves equality of output distributions. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o53k-noisy-gate-success-bridge-on-a-finite-qubit-carrier\"></a>**Theorem O.5.3k (Noisy-Gate Success Bridge on a Finite Qubit Carrier).** Let $\\mathcal I$ and $R_n$ be as in Theorem O.5.3j, with $R_n=g_{n,G(n)}\\circ\\cdots\\circ g_{n,1}$ a composition of $G(n)$ NOT, CNOT and Toffoli gates on $w(n)$ wires, both polynomial in $n$. Realize the wires as qubits with computational basis $\\{|0\\rangle,|1\\rangle\\}$ and each ideal gate as its permutation-unitary channel $\\mathcal U_{n,k}$. A physical implementation consists of: a preparation of the security-parameter, challenge and clean-ancilla registers within trace distance $\\epsilon_n^{\\mathrm{prep}}$ of $|1^n,y,0,0\\rangle$ for every $y$; a coin register prepared independently of those registers within trace distance $\\eta_n$ of the maximally mixed state $2^{-r(n)}I$; completely positive trace-preserving maps $\\Phi_{n,k}$ with $\\frac12\\|\\Phi_{n,k}-\\mathcal U_{n,k}\\|_\\diamond\\le\\epsilon_{n,k}$; and a readout of the output register whose outcome distribution lies within total variation $\\kappa_n$ of the ideal computational-basis measurement for every input state. Decode as in Theorem O.5.3j, and let $\\operatorname{Adv}^{\\mathrm{phys}}(n)$ be the probability, with $X_n\\leftarrow U_n$ and challenge $f_n(X_n)$, that the decoded output is a preimage of $f_n(X_n)$ of length $n$. Then:\n\n1. For every $n$,\n   $$\n   \\bigl|\\operatorname{Adv}^{\\mathrm{phys}}(n)-\\operatorname{Adv}^{\\mathrm{inv}}_{f,\\mathcal I}(n)\\bigr|\n   \\le\\nu_n:=\\epsilon_n^{\\mathrm{prep}}+\\eta_n+\\sum_{k=1}^{G(n)}\\epsilon_{n,k}+\\kappa_n.\n   \\tag{O.5.3k.1}\n   $$\n2. If $\\nu_n$ is negligible, for example when $\\epsilon_{n,k}\\le\\epsilon_n$ with $G(n)\\epsilon_n$, $\\epsilon_n^{\\mathrm{prep}}$, $\\eta_n$ and $\\kappa_n$ negligible, the implemented family satisfies the success clause of (O.5.3.6) for $\\mathcal I$ with $q_{\\mathcal I}=1$ and $\\nu_{\\mathcal I}(n)=\\nu_n$.\n3. A constant per-gate error does not supply this clause for an uncorrected compiled circuit. Take the identity family $f_n(x)=x$, the inverter $\\mathcal I(1^n,y)=y$ with $\\operatorname{Adv}^{\\mathrm{inv}}_{f,\\mathcal I}=1$, and the circuit realizing (O.5.3j.1) for it by copying $y$ into the output register with $n$ CNOT gates and writing the length tag and failure flag with NOT gates. Let each copying CNOT be followed by an independent bit flip of probability $p\\in(0,1)$ on its target and let every other operation be ideal. Each noisy CNOT lies at normalized diamond distance exactly $p$ from the ideal gate, and $\\operatorname{Adv}^{\\mathrm{phys}}(n)=(1-p)^n$. Since $(1-p)^n<1/q(n)-\\nu(n)$ for all large $n$ whenever $q$ is a polynomial and $\\nu$ is negligible, no choice of $q$ and $\\nu$ makes (O.5.3.6) hold for this family.\n\n*Proof.* Let $\\rho_y$ be the ideal input, the basis state $|1^n,y,0,0\\rangle$ tensored with $2^{-r(n)}I$ on the coins, and let $\\rho_y'$ be the physical input; the triangle inequality for product states gives $\\frac12\\|\\rho_y'-\\rho_y\\|_1\\le\\epsilon_n^{\\mathrm{prep}}+\\eta_n$. Put $\\mathcal U=\\mathcal U_{n,G(n)}\\circ\\cdots\\circ\\mathcal U_{n,1}$ and $\\Phi=\\Phi_{n,G(n)}\\circ\\cdots\\circ\\Phi_{n,1}$. The telescoping identity\n$$\n\\Phi(\\rho_y')-\\mathcal U(\\rho_y)\n=\\Phi(\\rho_y'-\\rho_y)\n+\\sum_{k=1}^{G(n)}\\Phi_{n,G(n)}\\circ\\cdots\\circ\\Phi_{n,k+1}\\circ(\\Phi_{n,k}-\\mathcal U_{n,k})\\circ\\mathcal U_{n,k-1}\\circ\\cdots\\circ\\mathcal U_{n,1}(\\rho_y),\n$$\ncontractivity of the trace norm under completely positive trace-preserving maps and the diamond-norm bounds give $\\frac12\\|\\Phi(\\rho_y')-\\mathcal U(\\rho_y)\\|_1\\le\\epsilon_n^{\\mathrm{prep}}+\\eta_n+\\sum_k\\epsilon_{n,k}$. The readout, decoder and success test form a quantum-to-classical channel followed by a $\\{0,1\\}$-valued function, so the success probability changes by at most this trace distance plus $\\kappa_n$. In the ideal pipeline $\\mathcal U$ permutes basis states and $2^{-r(n)}I=2^{-r(n)}\\sum_r|r\\rangle\\!\\langle r|$, so $\\mathcal U(\\rho_y)=2^{-r(n)}\\sum_r|1^n,y,r,\\mathcal I(1^n,y;r),0\\rangle\\!\\langle1^n,y,r,\\mathcal I(1^n,y;r),0|$ and the ideal readout reproduces the classical output law of Theorem O.5.3j. Averaging over $y=f_n(X_n)$ gives ideal success $\\operatorname{Adv}^{\\mathrm{inv}}_{f,\\mathcal I}(n)$, which proves (O.5.3k.1). Item 2 is the lower half of (O.5.3k.1). For item 3, the noisy gate is $\\mathcal N_p\\circ\\mathcal U_{\\mathrm{CNOT}}$ with $\\mathcal N_p(\\rho)=(1-p)\\rho+pX\\rho X$ on the target, so $\\frac12\\|\\mathcal N_p\\circ\\mathcal U_{\\mathrm{CNOT}}-\\mathcal U_{\\mathrm{CNOT}}\\|_\\diamond=p\\cdot\\frac12\\|X\\cdot X-\\mathrm{id}\\|_\\diamond=p$, the last norm being attained on a maximally entangled input, where the two outputs are orthogonal. Each copied bit equals $y_i$ exactly when its flip does not occur, independently of the others, and $y$ is the unique preimage of $y$, so the success probability is $(1-p)^n$. Finally $1/q(n)-\\nu(n)\\ge1/(2q(n))>(1-p)^n$ for all large $n$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o53l-complete-heralded-error-ppt-to-pu-source-certificate\"></a>**Theorem O.5.3l (Complete Heralded-Error PPT-to-PU Source Certificate).** Fix the P1–P4 physical presentation below once for all inverter codes and security parameters. Its complete finite implementing-source proof and exact constructor/interpreter are the certificate denoted $\\mathfrak C_{O.5.3l}$. Section numbers are local.\n\n**A scalable permitted-PU evaluator with constant heralded physical error**\n\nThe proof below is an original-owner certificate for a fully specified heralded-error model; unheralded noise is a separate model outside its scope.\n\n## 1. Obligation and source boundary\n\nDefinition O.5.3h requires, for each fixed uniform PPT inverter $I$, a uniform permitted physical protocol family with a complete implementing microstate $\\mu_{I,n}$, polynomial $C_{P}$ and physical overhead, exact computational coin law (or negligible discrepancy), and\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Adv}_{\\mathrm{PU}}(I,n) \\ge \\mathrm{Adv}_{\\mathrm{inv}}(I,n)/q_I(n) - \\nu_I(n).\n$$\n\nThe encoder, decoder, correction, randomness, controller, workspace and all other apparatus belong to $\\mu$. The challenge is an INPUT, never a hidden program parameter. Original O.5.3j supplies the uniform reversible Boolean compilation, and O.5.3k supplies the success/trace-distance interface. The proof below additionally gives the constant-error physical compiler, its complete finite Hamiltonian, source/hierarchy admission and program upper bound.\n\nThe original task does not require robustness against every possible hardware noise law, or an exact $C_{P}$ minimum for each inverter. The following explicit noise/physical presentation is fixed for ALL circuits, all inverter codes and all lengths before their success is evaluated.\n\n### Physical premises, with no unowned operation\n\nP1. Finite qubit registers admit the explicitly specified finite self-adjoint Hamiltonians below, including at most seven-body clock/data couplings on the declared finite interaction graph. Their exact algebraic coefficients and an independent positive time unit $\\tau$ and energy unit hbar/tau are model data. No nearest-neighbor spatial embedding or robustness to additional Hamiltonian calibration errors is claimed.\n\nP2. The complete initial stock contains the named pure blank spins, one clock excitation, supplied computational input registers, and independent fair and Bernoulli(1/4) classical source registers. Their preparation, finite entropy/purity resources, isolation and maintenance through the declared horizon are owned. This is a supplied finite preparation, not a claim that exact pure states can be reset from a finite-temperature full-rank bath at zero cost. Optional purifying reference spins are retained, not discarded.\n\nP3. The registered gate-LOCATION error law is independent heralded identity erasure: in the nominal circuit an active attempted reversible gate U applies U with probability3/4 and identity with probability1/4, producing a distinguishable retained herald. Its fixed independent physical fault registers are independent of the input and computational coins. Section 4 realizes this entire terminal circuit instrument by one autonomous Hamiltonian dilation. This is not a fresh random fault at each clock hop, nor a claim that intermediate clock superpositions implement sequential CPTP gates at appointed intermediate times. The fixed metadata/control gates and terminal readout have the exact laws stated below. No extra unspecified noise is assumed on them; no claim of protection against such extra noise is made.\n\nP4. At the nominated physical final time $T$ the finite pointer/validity registers are read by their declared computational-basis instrument, with ideal TV error zero on this presentation. The pointer-copy interaction is already in the Hamiltonian word. The actual readout additionally owns an $(n+1)$-bit classical record register $R$, initially blank, and the finite product pointer instrument specified in Section 3; $H$ acts as identity on $R$ before the readout. Its preparation and $O(n+1)$ local readout operations are part of this declared finite service boundary. $T$ is specified by the clock construction, not supplied as an input containing an inverter answer. This is a fixed-checkpoint finite experiment, not a permanent autonomous halt or a claim of timing-error immunity.\n\nThese finite quantum premises are the registered interpretation of this presentation. They are comparable to the admitted finite supplied-state/checkpoint premises in prior sources, but no old restricted descriptor admission or old numerical minimum is inherited.\n\n## 2. Canonical whole-source schema\n\nThe complete canonical descriptor and gate-word definitions reproduced at the end of this theorem define exact ASCII JSON (sorted keys, no spaces, final newline), with fields\n\nschema='O05-HeraldedAutonomousEvaluator-v1', n, coins, work, gates, outputs.\n\nHere $n\\ge 1$, $\\text{coins}=c\\ge 0$, $\\text{work}=q\\ge n+c$. A gate is a list of one, two or three DISTINCT addresses below $q$; its last address is the target and preceding addresses are positive controls. Thus the three gate types are NOT, CNOT and Toffoli. The $n$ output addresses are explicit. The ABSTRACT circuit input convention is challenge bits on $0,\\ldots,n-1$, fair coins on $n,\\ldots,n+c-1$, and zeros elsewhere. The PHYSICAL pre-copy work state instead has zeros on the challenge slots and everywhere else except the coin slots. The separate input port holds $y$; the protected leading CNOTs load it exactly once. All remaining circuit garbage is retained. Integer conversion uses the explicit digit-by-digit decimal parser and base-$10^9$ serializer in the source, independent of Python decimal-string digit limits. The fixed schema has bounded nesting; arbitrary deeper malformed JSON is rejected. The decoder rejects noncanonical bytes, bad types, unknown fields, invalid addresses, repeated gate addresses and invalid lengths. The schema includes every finite circuit of this syntax, including bad inverters and circuits that output constants; admission does not test success, complexity, qualification or the identity of a target.\n\nThe complete physical realization is generated from this descriptor by the following fixed rules. There are separate $n$-qubit physical challenge input ports, $n$-qubit unary security-parameter ports and $n$-qubit output pointers. The ideal nominal word comprises $n$ input CNOTs, the listed gates, and $n$ output-pointer CNOTs. If the listed gate count is $G$, its total length is $L=G+2n\\ge 2$.\n\nThe encoder $\\mathrm{Enc}_n(1^n,y)$ prepares the two input ports in the indicated orthogonal computational states. Copying the challenge into work occurs in the physical nominal word and is protected by the same retry construction. The security-parameter port is verified by the final metadata circuit. Thus input preparation and copying are not an external mathematical encoding substituted for an actual port.\n\n## 3. Exact constant-error compiler and complete retained law\n\nSet\n\n$$\nr=n^{2}+\\operatorname{bitlength}(L+1)+1.\n$$\n\nFor each nominal gate start a fresh active bit $a_{0}=1$. For attempts $t=1,\\ldots,r$ supply fresh independent $e_{t}$ with $\\Pr(e_t=1)=1/4$, a blank herald $h_{t}$, and a blank next-active bit $a_{t}$. Execute three full-space reversible gates:\n\n1. $\\mathrm{CNOT}(e_t,h_t)$, retaining the actual error herald;\n2. apply $U$ to its target if $a_{t-1}=1$ and $e_{t}=0$;\n3. $\\mathrm{Toffoli}(a_{t-1},e_t;a_t)$.\n\nEvery gate is a permutation of the FULL computational basis, not merely a map on the ready subspace. At most four controls and one target are involved, so support is at most five data spins. All previous active bits, fault bits, heralds, overwritten work and coins remain physical registers. Nothing is reset or postselected.\n\nInduction gives $a_t=\\prod_{s\\le t}e_s$. Exactly the first successful attempt applies $U$; later attempts are inactive. Hence the block applies $U$ once unless all $r$ errors equal 1, in which case it applies identity and leaves $a_{r}=1$. This is an equality of the compiled circuit word's full conditional input/output law for every data state, with arbitrary reference systems. The nominal single active-attempt channel, after ignoring but retaining its internal source record, is\n\n$$\n\\rho \\to (3/4)\\, U \\rho U^* \\otimes |0\\rangle\\langle 0|_h + (1/4)\\, \\rho \\otimes |1\\rangle\\langle 1|_h.\n$$\n\nIt has genuinely nonzero constant error for nontrivial U; for an input whose ideal and identity outputs are orthogonal, its data-output trace distance from the ideal is 1/4. The herald is part of the physical output of the instrument, not an oracle that discovers an unobserved error.\n\nA fresh exact metadata chain computes a good bit: it starts 1 and successively ANDs the complements of all final block-failure bits and the $n$ security-header bits. All intermediate metadata bits remain. The controls are actual bounded finite permutations, included in the Hamiltonian word; they are not external adaptive instructions. The terminal readout gives the copied $n$-bit output when $\\mathrm{good}=1$ and returns bottom otherwise. Its actual instrument has Kraus projections $K_x=P_{\\mathrm{good}=1}P_{\\mathrm{output}=x}$ for every $x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n$ and $K_{\\mathrm{bottom}}=P_{\\mathrm{good}=0}$, tensored with identity on the other retained factors. They obey $\\sum K^*K=I$ on the full carrier. The instrument writes the result into $R$ using the encoding $\\mathrm{bottom}=(0,0^n)$ and $x=(1,x)$. Its full-carrier Kraus operators are $L_x=K_x\\otimes\\mathrm{XOR}_{\\mathrm{enc}}(x)$ on $R$ and $L_{\\mathrm{bottom}}=K_{\\mathrm{bottom}}\\otimes\\mathrm{XOR}_{\\mathrm{enc}}(\\mathrm{bottom})$, not an overwrite of $R$. Thus $\\sum L^*L=I$ even for arbitrary nonblank/coherent $R$; the actual ready $R$ is blank, so its retained value is the required outcome. The classical instrument outcome is also retained as the readout label; the named $R$ is the owned physical record. A product of at most $n+1$ single-spin pointer readouts, skipped after a failed good read, and the recorded good-controlled labeling implements it with polynomial apparatus; no exponentially sized lookup device is needed. Every outcome law is normalized and all failures are retained. No ideal external majority algorithm is appended after a noisy last bit.\n\nThe probability of bottom due to errors is at most\n\n$$\n\\nu_n=L\\cdot 4^{-r} \\le 2^{-n^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe inequality follows from $4^r\\ge 2^{2n^{2}}(L+1)^{2}\\cdot 4$. It holds uniformly over every challenge and every computational coin string. On its complement the entire nominal word, including input and output copying, is exactly correct. No independence of the logical output from previous computation is required. The union bound uses the independent fresh block-fault registers only.\n\nThe PPT coin register has EXACTLY its required product fair law and is independent of all fault registers. The latter belong to the separate physical noise environment, not to the inverter's computational coin tape. Equivalently the physical protocol is a channel conditional on the PPT coin string, obtained by integrating its fixed internal noise law, as in O.5.3k. This separation does not discard the physical noise registers from $\\mu$.\n\n## 4. An explicit bounded-strength, constant-support autonomous Hamiltonian\n\nLet $W_1,\\ldots,W_M$ be the complete reversible word above, including every herald, attempt, active update and final metadata gate. The exact counts generated by the code are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= 3Lr+L+n,\\\\\nQ &= q+4n+2L+3Lr+1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndata/source/pointer/metadata spins. Add $M+1$ clock spins. Initialize them in the single-excitation state $|0\\rangle$. Let $G_0=I$ and $G_j=W_j\\cdots W_1$.\n\nFor $j=0,\\ldots,M-1$ define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_j &= \\sigma^{+}_{j+1} \\sigma^{-}_j \\otimes W_{j+1}\\\\\n&\\quad + \\sigma^{-}_{j+1} \\sigma^{+}_j \\otimes W_{j+1}^*,\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\ng_j = \\hbar \\sqrt{(j+1)(M-j)}/(2M \\tau),\n$$\n\n$$\nH = \\sum_j g_j(I+C_j).\n$$\n\nEach $C_{j}$ is Hermitian with norm 1 on the FULL clock/data space. Hence each summand is positive, and $H$ is a finite nonnegative self-adjoint Hamiltonian. Each term acts on two clock spins and at most five data spins. Expanding the at-most-five-spin permutation in its Pauli basis uses at most $4^5$ terms, a constant independent of $n$; the two clock ladder factors introduce another fixed factor. Thus the displayed $O(M)$ interaction inventory is an actual polynomial-size list of bounded-support operators, not an exponentially large matrix hidden behind a compressed program. Coupling coefficients are algebraic expressions with $O(\\log(M+1))$ integer descriptions. Clock-excitation number is conserved. No projection onto the valid clock sector is used to define $H$; invalid/multiple-excitation sectors remain part of the carrier.\n\nOn the invariant single-excitation sector, conjugate by $V=\\sum_j|j\\rangle\\langle j|\\otimes G_{j}$. This removes all $W$ labels and identifies $H-E_{\\mathrm{shift}}I$ with $(\\hbar/(M\\tau))J_x$ in the spin-$J$ representation, $J=M/2$, where $E_{\\mathrm{shift}}=\\sum_j g_j$. To verify the transfer identity directly, identify this representation with the symmetric subspace of $M$ spin-half factors. $J_{x}$ is half their summed $X$ operators. Rotation through $\\pi$ flips all $M$ spins, multiplying by $(-i)^M$. Therefore at\n\n$$\nT=\\pi M \\tau,\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\exp(-iTH/\\hbar)(|0\\rangle \\otimes \\psi)\\\\\n&\\quad = \\exp(-iTE_{\\mathrm{shift}}/\\hbar)(-i)^M |M\\rangle \\otimes G_M \\psi\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor EVERY data/source/reference vector $\\psi$. Linearity gives the exact mixed-state and instrument law. This is the actual TERMINAL Hamiltonian execution of the whole source, not an assumption of independently scheduled physical gates. During $0<t<T$ the clock is generally a superposition and can backtrack; no sequential-gate timing or intermediate-circuit intervention equivalence is asserted. Intermediate source audits use the actual $H$ evolution. Frozen per-location fault registers ensure every revisited coupling has the same coherent control; they do not draw new faults during a hop.\n\nThe coupling bound is $g_j\\le\\hbar/(2\\tau)$, the shift is $E_{\\mathrm{shift}}\\le M\\hbar/(2\\tau)$, and $\\lVert H\\rVert\\le M\\hbar/\\tau$. On the definite-clock ready state every hopping term has zero expectation, so the actual initial mean stored energy is $E_{\\mathrm{shift}}$. The total autonomous energy is conserved. There is no reset heat claim and no double counting of the positive shift as consumed work. The source is used for its finite appointed window $T$; it is not claimed to halt forever.\n\n## 5. Complete resource and PPI record\n\nEvery item is owned:\n\n* q nominal work spins, n challenge input spins, n security-header spins and n output pointers;\n* c fair computational coins within work;\n* Lr biased fault-source spins, Lr heralds, Lr next-active spins and L initial active spins;\n* L+n+1 metadata-chain spins;\n* M+1 physical clock spins and exactly M specified clock/data interactions;\n* the finite program/coupling specification, encoder preparation, clock initialization, isolation through T, final pointer instrument, classical retained outcome and finite verification/update interface.\n\nThe total is $Q+M+1$ clock/data spins plus $n+1$ readout-record spins, namely $Q+M+n+2$, before optional source purifications; purifying each coin/fault source costs at most $c+Lr$ additional retained spins. Initial supplied randomness entropy is $c\\ln 2+Lr\\,h_{\\mathrm{nat}}(1/4)$. Pure blanks and the initial clock excitation are a separately counted purity/preparation stock. The descriptor and explicit expanded word require $O((G+Lr+q+n+c)\\log(Q+M+2))$ bits. The independent time/energy units remain fixed across $n$.\n\nFor a finite thermodynamic dressing choose a supplied preparation/isolation/readout service boundary with unit resource charge $e_{0}$ per prepared or maintained spin per registered time unit and per readout record bit. The actual finite stocks and services are counted above; imported stock cost is a boundary expenditure, not asserted intrinsic dissipation. Alternatively retain the vector inventory (spins, initial energy, purity, finite interaction count, runtime, readout operations) without an arbitrary scalar tariff. Each coordinate is polynomial whenever q,G,c are polynomial. No supplier-inclusive optimal work theorem is needed by O.5.3h.\n\nThere is an actual finite verification/update: the retained error/header metadata computes good, which controls whether the physical output record is admitted or marked bottom. The result can then enter a finite downstream verification (including the publicly supplied $f$ evaluator for the any-preimage predicate). No extra mathematical output oracle is required. P.6.2b/c permits retained predictive, causal OR verification update; it does not require every work wire to be an independently minimal MPU. This is the admitted PPI whole-protocol source. No per-wire $C_P=C_{\\mathrm{op}}$ claim or Property-R quantum universality claim is made.\n\nThe PPI existence claim is relative to P1–P4 and the explicit finite dressing.\n\n## 6. New target-independent prefix presentation and hierarchy\n\nThe complete prefix interpreter reproduced at the end of this theorem defines a new presentation. Its constructor syntax is\n\n$$\n0\\ \\gamma(B+1)\\ \\text{payload},\n$$\n\nwhere B is the payload byte length, the payload has exactly 8B bits, and $\\gamma$ is Elias $\\gamma$ coding. The constructor emits those bits literally. Invalid physical payloads are subsequently rejected by the physical decoder. This constructor domain is prefix-free because its length header is self-delimiting and exact total length is enforced.\n\nA disjoint universal branch begins with the 17-bit word 11111111111111110, then $\\gamma(e+1)$, then a program for the indexed transducer $e$. The prefix-interpreter definitions below specify the total binary grammar of finite one-tape transducer tables and the deterministic stage/length/lexicographic prefix-filtered halting enumeration. Every already prefix-free machine retains its whole halting domain under this filter, so the branch simulates each prefix machine with constant overhead. The combined machine is therefore an optimal prefix machine. Unrecognized syntax is undefined; no finite timeout establishes divergence.\n\nThe physical decoder admits exactly the canonical finite evaluator schemas of Section 2, independently of their behavior or target. At hierarchy stage s simulate s virtual interpreter steps. A not-yet-halted program survives; a halted program survives iff its literal output decodes to a valid source. This is uniformly decidable and decreasing. Nonhalting survivors do not realize a target. The target realization relation requires actual halting with its complete canonical descriptor.\n\nThe registered interpretation covers the entire finite quantum source: all clock sectors, data, faults, source/pointer/metadata registers and optional references, the complete preparation and input instrument, H, the terminal output instrument and retained response protocol grammar. Full-source mathematical audits may use finite rational-time evolutions inside the registered finite window (and the separately named endpoint T) and finite explicitly specified quantum instruments, with terminating probes where they would disturb nominal services. These are response-equivalence tests, not unpriced additional physical services; a physically performed audit owns its own finite apparatus. There is no permission to read private future fault bits during the nominal inversion task. The factorwise identity map J intertwines initial states, input channels, H and every declared instrument. It therefore intertwines exponentials (finite-matrix power series), compositions and every finite outcome law. This is an all-level source-faithfulness proof, not a finite numerical test.\n\nTrust premises are P1–P4, finite-dimensional quantum mechanics and the exact parser/constructor equations. Physical inventory coverage is all named source factors and their finite instructions. No constraint is defined by $C_{P}$, inverter success or a target-specific minimum. The hierarchy is distinct from the restricted R14 and BSR03 decoders.\n\nFor descriptor byte length B the literal constructor has length\n\n$$\nb=1+[2 \\lfloor\\log_2(B+1)\\rfloor+1]+8B.\n$$\n\nIt realizes the SAME complete source at every stage. Consequently each stage minimum is at most $b$ and the original full-hierarchy stabilization theorem yields $C_P(\\mu)\\le b$. This is a polynomial upper bound when $B$ is polynomial. An exact minimum or a short-program census is not required. Universality could improve the descriptive bound for a uniformly generated family, but the literal bound already suffices and is the explicit witness checked here.\n\n## 7. Every fixed PPT inverter and the original success inequality\n\nFix ANY uniform PPT inverter $I$ with an explicit polynomial clock, workspace and fair-coin bound. Theorem O.5.3j's reversible compiler yields a uniform family of NOT/CNOT/Toffoli circuits with $q(n)$, $G(n)$, $c(n)$ polynomial, whose decoded output on each challenge/coin string is the following fixed-length wrapper $I'$: run $I$, retain its output length/halting record, and output its candidate when it has exactly $n$ bits, otherwise output $0^n$. This wrapper is a uniform polynomial-time classical operation, so the same reversible compiler applies with all ancillas/workspace included. It preserves every original successful inversion and can only add successes on previously malformed/failure outputs. Thus $\\mathrm{Adv}_{\\mathrm{inv}}(I',n)\\ge\\mathrm{Adv}_{\\mathrm{inv}}(I,n)$. The implemented output always has exactly $n$ bits; no unimplemented optional validity wire is assumed. To match the schema's zero-work convention, uniformly relabel the compiler's wires and allocate $n$ extra workspace header wires initialized by $n$ NOT gates to supply its known $1^n$ input. This adds $O(n)$ wires/gates, uses no challenge advice, and is distinct from the separately verified physical header port. The compiler's public code and polynomial bounds are fixed, not advice varying with the challenge or a hidden trapdoor.\n\nThe schema generator uses $n$ and this compiled circuit. It expands exactly by Sections 2–4, with $r$ polynomial in $n$ and $\\log L$, $Q,M$ polynomial, and $T=\\pi M\\tau$ polynomial. The canonical descriptor itself has $B=O((G+n)\\log(q+2)+\\log(c+n+q+2))$, hence polynomial. The physical input remains $\\mathrm{Enc}_n(1^n,Y_n)$, never a code for $Y_{n}$. The computational coins have the same exact fair law as $I$. All noise/preparation/controller details remain in $\\mu_{I,n}$.\n\nFor each fixed challenge and coin string, $\\mathrm{good}=1$ implies the physical decoded output equals the abstract wrapped output. Since the wrapper never removes an original successful inversion, therefore, including $X_n$'s distribution and the standard ANY-preimage predicate,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{Adv}_{\\mathrm{PU}}(I,n) &\\ge \\mathrm{Adv}_{\\mathrm{inv}}(I,n) - L(n)4^{-r(n)}\\\\\n&\\ge \\mathrm{Adv}_{\\mathrm{inv}}(I,n) - 2^{-n^{2}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTake $q_I(n)=1$, $\\nu_I(n)=2^{-n^{2}}$, and the explicit polynomial bound on $b$ above as $p_{I}$. These statements hold for all $n$ on the compiler's declared clocked domain, in particular all sufficiently large $n$ as required. The failure branch is included, never conditioned away. This establishes the complete original O.5.3.6 conjunction on the registered source model.\n\nThe simulation certificate does NOT prove the Physical Hardness Postulate $\\mathrm{PHP}_f$, any one-way function assumption, or $\\mathrm{P}\\ne\\mathrm{NP}$. Those remain exactly conditional as in O.5.3h.1.\n\n### Complete source constructors\n\n#### Canonical descriptor, finite gate word and complete register definitions\n\nA source descriptor is a tuple $d=(n,c,q,\\mathcal G,o)$ with the following complete domains.\n\n| Component | Domain and meaning |\n|---|---|\n| $n$ | Positive integer; challenge length and number of output bits. |\n| $c$ | Nonnegative integer; number of independent fair computational coins. |\n| $q$ | Integer $q\\ge n+c$; work-register size. |\n| $\\mathcal G$ | Finite ordered list of gates, each an ordered tuple of one, two or three distinct integers in $\\{0,\\ldots,q-1\\}$. The last is the target and the preceding entries are positive controls. |\n| $o=(o_0,\\ldots,o_{n-1})$ | Ordered output addresses in $\\{0,\\ldots,q-1\\}$; repetitions are permitted. |\n\nThe canonical descriptor word has exactly the six fields in this order: `coins`, `gates`, `n`, `outputs`, `schema`, `work`. Their values are $c,\\mathcal G,n,o$, the fixed ASCII string `O05-HeraldedAutonomousEvaluator-v1`, and $q$, respectively. The word is specified by the following finite character grammar. An integer is its ordinary base-ten digit string, with no leading zero unless the integer is zero. An array is its ordered entries separated by a comma, enclosed by the characters `[` and `]`; the empty array contains no entry. An object is its ordered quoted field names, each followed by a colon and its value, with commas between fields and the enclosing characters `{` and `}`. The schema value is enclosed in double quotation marks. There are no spaces, escapes or other characters, and exactly one line-feed byte, value $10$, follows the closing brace. Boolean, floating-point and null values are outside the descriptor grammar. Thus every descriptor determines one finite ASCII word $b(d)$, and the grammar has a unique inverse on its range. Reading a finite digit string by $v\\leftarrow10v+d$ and emitting a nonnegative integer by repeated division by ten terminate without a fixed digit limit.\n\nFor a positive integer $k$, let $\\operatorname{bin}(k)$ be its binary expansion without leading zeros and define\n$$\n\\gamma(k)=0^{|\\operatorname{bin}(k)|-1}\\operatorname{bin}(k),\\qquad\nC(d)=0\\gamma(|b(d)|+1)\\operatorname{bits}_8(b(d)).\n$$\nHere $\\operatorname{bits}_8$ writes every ASCII byte as exactly eight bits, most significant bit first. The byte count determines the complete end of $C(d)$, so these constructor words form a prefix-free set.\n\nThe full gate-word construction uses only the following permutation on a finite bit string $x$:\n$$\nT(P,N;t):\\quad\nx_t\\longmapsto x_t\\mathbin\\oplus\n\\left(\\prod_{u\\in P}x_u\\prod_{v\\in N}(1-x_v)\\right).\n$$\nAll other bits are fixed; the ordered positive controls $P$, negative controls $N$ and target $t$ have distinct addresses. The map is its own inverse on the entire carrier.\n\nStart the work addresses at $0,\\ldots,q-1$. Allocate every new address by taking the least unused nonnegative integer. First allocate, in order, $n$ challenge-input bits $i_j$, $n$ header bits $h_j$ and $n$ output pointers $u_j$, for $j=0,\\ldots,n-1$. The nominal gate list is, in order,\n$$\nT(\\{i_j\\},\\varnothing;j)\\ (j=0,\\ldots,n-1),\\qquad\n\\mathcal G,\\qquad\nT(\\{o_j\\},\\varnothing;u_j)\\ (j=0,\\ldots,n-1).\n$$\nWrite its length as $L=2n+|\\mathcal G|$ and set\n$$\nr=n^2+\\operatorname{bitlength}(L+1)+1.\n$$\nFor each nominal gate $T(P,N;t)$, allocate an active bit $a$ initialized to one. Repeat the following $r$ times in the displayed order: allocate fresh fault, herald and next-active bits $e,h,b$ and append\n$$\nT(\\{e\\},\\varnothing;h),\\qquad\nT(P\\mathbin{\\|}(a),N\\mathbin{\\|}(e);t),\\qquad\nT(\\{a,e\\},\\varnothing;b).\n$$\nThe notation $\\mathbin{\\|}$ appends an address to an ordered control list. After these three gates set the active address to $b$ for the next repetition. Retain the final active bit as this location's failure bit. Store all fault, herald, active and failure addresses in allocation order; none is reused or discarded.\n\nAfter all $L$ locations, allocate a bit $g$ initialized to one. Traverse first the $L$ failure bits and then the $n$ header bits. For each traversed address $a$, allocate a fresh bit $g'$ initially zero, append\n$$\nT(\\{g\\},\\{a\\};g')\\quad\\hbox{for a failure bit},\\qquad\nT(\\{g,a\\},\\varnothing;g')\\quad\\hbox{for a header bit},\n$$\nand replace the current address $g$ by $g'$. Its final value is the success flag. Every earlier value remains as a retained bit.\n\nInitially every bit is zero except the header bits, the first active bit of each location, and the first success-accumulator bit, which are one. Place the supplied challenge in the $i_j$, the $c$ fair computational coins at addresses $n,\\ldots,n+c-1$, and the independent Bernoulli$(1/4)$ source values in the allocated fault bits. The source applies the complete ordered permutation word and reports $(x_{u_0},\\ldots,x_{u_{n-1}})$ if the final success flag is one; otherwise it reports the failure symbol. The entire bit state, every fault/herald and all records are retained on both outcomes.\n\nThis specifies every register, its initial value and every gate, including the full complement of each gate's intended input support. If $Q$ is the number of allocated bits and $S$ the number of gates, the construction gives exactly\n$$\nQ=q+4n+2L+3rL+1,\\qquad S=3rL+L+n.\n$$\nThe autonomous clock has $S+1$ spins, the readout has $n+1$ spins and the complete physical count is $Q+S+n+2$. Computational coin addresses are exactly $n,\\ldots,n+c-1$; input, header and output addresses are the first three allocated blocks; every other register list is fixed by the allocation rule above. These counts include all unused levels and retained failure sectors.\n\nFor each location the active bit stays one precisely while every preceding trial at that location has fault value one. At the first zero fault, the nominal gate is applied once and its successor active bit is zero, suppressing all remaining trials. The final failure bit is therefore one exactly when all $r$ faults are one. The success accumulator is the conjunction of the negated failure bits and the header bits. Consequently, on success, the output is exactly the ideal reversible computation on the challenge and computational coins; on failure the complete retained state remains in the declared source. A union bound gives failure probability at most $L4^{-r}$, with the original positive heralded-error law. For the concrete one-bit example, take $n=c=1$, $q=2$, $\\mathcal G=((1,0))$ and $o=(0)$; the ideal output is the XOR of challenge and coin.\n\n---\n\n#### Total binary prefix interpreter and physical decoder\n\nThe presentation has two disjoint program branches. Its constructor branch consists of the words\n$$\n0\\gamma(B+1)v,\\qquad B\\ge0,\\quad |v|=8B,\n$$\nand returns $v$ as its output bit string. The branch accepts this syntax independently of whether the bytes in $v$ form a physical descriptor; physical admission is decided by the descriptor grammar above. Its universal branch consists of words\n$$\n1^{16}0\\,\\gamma(e+1)p,\\qquad e\\ge0,\n$$\nwith the prefix filter specified below. Every other complete binary word is undefined. The leading zero in the constructor branch and leading one in the universal branch separate them; within each branch the $\\gamma$ fields have unique boundaries. A $\\gamma$ field is decoded by counting its initial zeros, say $z$, then reading the following $z+1$ binary digits, including their leading one. An incomplete field is undefined.\n\nThe raw transducer enumeration uses a two-sided tape with symbols $0,1,2$, where $2$ is blank. For $e\\ge1$, remove the leading one from the binary expansion of $e$ to obtain its finite table word. This is the enumeration $e=2^{|v|}+\\operatorname{value}(v)$ of every finite binary word $v$. Decode that word as a $\\gamma$ field for $s+1$, a $\\gamma$ field for $m+1$, followed by exactly $m$ rows, each with five $\\gamma$ fields\n$$\n(q+1,\\ a+1,\\ q'+2,\\ b+1,\\ d+2).\n$$\nThe domains are $s,q\\ge0$, $a,b\\in\\{0,1,2\\}$, $q'\\ge-1$ and $d\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}$. Distinct rows must have distinct pairs $(q,a)$. There are no trailing bits. The row sends state $q$ and scanned symbol $a$ to state $q'$, writes $b$ and moves the head by $d$; state $-1$ is halt. The index $e=0$, or any malformed table word, denotes initial state zero with no transitions. This explicit enumeration contains every finite deterministic transducer of this type.\n\nFor auxiliary word $z$ and program word $p$, place $\\gamma(|z|+1)zp$ in tape cells starting at zero; the remaining cells are blank, and the head starts at zero in state $s$. A bounded raw simulation performs at most the prescribed number of transitions. A missing transition is undefined. If the state reaches $-1$, its output is the finite bit word in cells $0,1,\\ldots$ up to the first blank; otherwise there is no returned word. Only finitely many cells have been written, so the output scan terminates. Cleanup to this convention is a finite computable suffix for any ordinary finite-output transducer.\n\nFor fixed $e,z$, the prefix filter starts with empty accepted and rejected sets. At stage $s=0,1,\\ldots$, enumerate every binary candidate $q$ of length at most $s$, in increasing length and then lexicographic order. Before handling each candidate advance one virtual tick, including candidates already decided. Skip previously accepted or rejected candidates. Otherwise simulate the raw machine for $s$ transitions on $(q,z)$. A candidate with no returned word stays undecided. A halting candidate is rejected if it is a prefix of an accepted word or has an accepted word as a prefix; otherwise accept it with its returned word. The universal branch on program $p$ returns its stored output when $p$ is accepted. Every stage is finite, and accepted words are pairwise prefix-incomparable by induction. When the raw halting domain is already prefix-free, the filter accepts every halting word with its original output. This proves universality without an inherited physical decoder.\n\nThe exact virtual clock convention is as follows. There is one initial tick for every submitted whole program. A constructor program has one further tick and returns its $8B$ output bits on the following transition. A universal program then uses the one-tick-per-candidate filter just specified. Parsing and each finite candidate test occur between those ticks. An invalid whole program remains undefined and advances ticks forever. Thus the bounded simulation consists of the first $k$ transitions of this explicitly defined process; it observes a returned output exactly when that return has occurred by transition $k$.\n\nTo decode a physical output, require a binary word of length divisible by eight, split it into consecutive eight-bit bytes, and apply the complete canonical descriptor grammar above. The stage-$k$ hierarchy admits a program exactly when its bounded simulation has not returned, or its returned output has a valid physical decoding. An output that is nonbinary, has incomplete bytes or fails the descriptor grammar is excluded once observed. A returned output never changes later, so the hierarchy is nested. Every bounded stage test terminates, since it performs only finitely many virtual transitions and each transition contains finite parsing, simulation and prefix-comparison operations. The source words $C(d)$ therefore decode to the complete physical evaluator just constructed, while universality, prefix-freeness and all-level admission use the same explicit presentation.\n\n---\n\n### O.5.4 Priced Prediction: Budgeted Diagonals, Reset Predictors, and Separated Reset Ledgers\n\nThis section prices prediction in running time and in registered reset heat. Proposition O.5.4a prices the SPAP diagonal in running time. Lemma O.5.4c identifies the predictor implemented by a registered finite reset, and Theorem O.5.4d proves that, when a pseudorandom generator exists, a reset with the stated efficiently simulable heat profile dissipates at least $(\\ell(n)-n)\\ln2$, less any inverse polynomial, above the entropy floor on an $\\ell(n)$-bit pseudorandom record computed from a discarded $n$-bit seed. Theorem O.5.4f bounds the separated terminal-reset ledger below by internal information minus the two conditional entropies of the retained output, and bounds the sum of the two registered local reset heats below by that ledger. Corollary O.5.4h separates deciding intersection from checking a supplied witness. Theorem O.5.4i proves that the ordinary disagreement-location minimax ledger is exactly zero, so it supplies no nonmonotone formula-depth lower-bound route. Its monotone counterpart retains an information lower bound and a monotone formula-depth upper bound; Theorem O.5.4k and Corollary O.5.4l give a near-linear monotone ledger by critical-block-sensitivity lifting. Here $H_2$ and $I_2$ denote Shannon entropy and mutual information in bits, $H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}=H_2\\ln2$, and $D_{\\mathrm{KL}}$ is relative entropy in nats.\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o54a-budgeted-spap-diagonal\"></a>**Proposition O.5.4a (Budgeted SPAP Diagonal).** Let $\\mathcal A$ be a deterministic sequential predictor that, on a binary prefix $y_{<i}=(y_1,\\ldots,y_{i-1})$, halts within $t(i)$ steps with a rational number $q_i\\in[0,1]$, its probability that $y_i=1$. Define the diagonal sequence $D_{\\mathcal A}$ by $y_i=1$ when $q_i<1/2$ and $y_i=0$ otherwise. Its first $n$ bits are computed by a multitape machine in $O\\bigl(\\sum_{i=1}^n(t(i)+i)\\bigr)$ steps, and\n$$\n-\\ln\\prod_{i=1}^n\\mathcal A(y_i\\mid y_{<i})\\ge n\\ln2,\n\\tag{O.5.4.1}\n$$\nwhere $\\mathcal A(1\\mid y_{<i})=q_i$ and $\\mathcal A(0\\mid y_{<i})=1-q_i$. Thus $\\mathcal A$ incurs log-loss at least $n\\ln2$ on a deterministic sequence whose generation costs one run of $\\mathcal A$ per symbol.\n\n*Proof.* If $q_i<1/2$, the realized bit is $1$ and receives probability $q_i<1/2$; otherwise it is $0$ and receives $1-q_i\\le1/2$. A product of $n$ factors, each at most $1/2$, is at most $2^{-n}$, which gives (O.5.4.1). The $i$-th bit is computed by copying $y_{<i}$ to the input tape of $\\mathcal A$ in $O(i)$ steps, running $\\mathcal A$ for at most $t(i)$ steps, and comparing its output, of length at most $t(i)$, with $1/2$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"remark-o54b-budgeted-diagonals-and-the-quantifier-order-of-theorem-o52e\"></a>**Remark O.5.4b (Budgeted Diagonals and the Quantifier Order of Theorem O.5.2e).** Proposition O.5.4a has the SPAP order $\\forall\\mathcal A\\,\\exists D_{\\mathcal A}$, and the defeating sequence spends the running time of the predictor it defeats. One diagonal system with a larger budget than every member of a class reverses the order. For time-constructible $t,T\\ge n$, using a uniquely decodable machine-code prefix, with $t(n)\\log t(n)=o(T(n))$, the machine that, on input $w=\\langle e\\rangle10^k$, runs the two-tape universal simulation of the $e$-th multitape machine on $w$ (Hennie and Stearns 1966), stops after $T(|w|)$ steps of its own clock, and outputs the complement of a completed simulation and $0$ otherwise, decides in time $O(T)$ a language that differs from every language decided in time $O(t)$ (Hartmanis and Stearns 1965; Hennie and Stearns 1966). With $t(n)=n^{\\lceil\\log_2n\\rceil}$ and $T(n)=2^n$ this gives $\\mathsf P\\subsetneq\\mathsf{EXP}$, a fixed-target separation of the form $\\exists L\\,\\forall\\mathcal A$. Every step remains valid when all machines share one oracle, so the fourth check in the proof of Theorem O.5.2e blocks this route as a proof of $\\mathsf P\\ne\\mathsf{NP}$.\n\n<a id=\"lemma-o54c-implied-predictor-of-a-registered-finite-reset\"></a>**Lemma O.5.4c (Implied Predictor of a Registered Finite Reset).** Let $P$ be a classical record with finite alphabet $\\mathcal P$ and let $C$ be classical side information with finite alphabet $\\mathcal C$. Fix one implementation that returns $P$ to a ready state from every initial pair $(p,c)\\in\\mathcal P\\times\\mathcal C$ and that satisfies, for every joint law of $(P,C)$ on $\\mathcal P\\times\\mathcal C$, the conditional reset hypotheses of Lemma O.5.2b with $C$ as the complete retained side-information resource, or, for a binary record, the hypotheses of Definition 28 with $R=C$, at one bath temperature $T_{\\mathrm{bath}}$. Let\n$$\n\\omega(p,c):=\\frac{\\mathbb E[Q_{\\mathrm{bath}}\\mid P=p,\\,C=c]}{k_BT_{\\mathrm{bath}}}\n$$\nbe its finite dimensionless mean heat from the initial pair $(p,c)$. Then\n$$\nZ(c):=\\sum_{p\\in\\mathcal P}e^{-\\omega(p,c)}\\le1\n\\qquad\\text{for every }c\\in\\mathcal C.\n\\tag{O.5.4.2}\n$$\nWith the implied predictor $\\hat q(p\\mid c):=e^{-\\omega(p,c)}/Z(c)$, every joint law $q$ gives\n$$\n\\frac{Q_{\\mathrm{env}}}{k_BT_{\\mathrm{bath}}}\n=H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(P\\mid C)\n+\\mathbb E_q\\Bigl[D_{\\mathrm{KL}}\\bigl(q(\\cdot\\mid C)\\,\\Vert\\,\\hat q(\\cdot\\mid C)\\bigr)\\Bigr]\n+\\mathbb E_q\\bigl[-\\ln Z(C)\\bigr].\n\\tag{O.5.4.3}\n$$\nBoth added terms are nonnegative, and the mean heat equals the conditional-entropy floor exactly when $q(\\cdot\\mid c)=\\hat q(\\cdot\\mid c)$ and $Z(c)=1$ for every $c$ with $q(c)>0$.\n\n*Proof.* The bath and auxiliary resources start in fixed states independent of $(P,C)$ and the implementation is fixed, so the mean heat conditional on an initial pair depends only on that pair, and $Q_{\\mathrm{env}}/(k_BT_{\\mathrm{bath}})=\\sum_{p,c}q(p,c)\\,\\omega(p,c)$ for every joint law $q$. Fix $c$ and run the implementation on the law that sets $C=c$ and draws $P$ from $\\hat q(\\cdot\\mid c)$. Since $\\omega(p,c)=-\\ln\\hat q(p\\mid c)-\\ln Z(c)$, its mean dimensionless heat is $H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(\\hat q(\\cdot\\mid c))-\\ln Z(c)$, while the conditional bound of Lemma O.5.2b, or Theorem 31 for a binary record, requires at least $H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(\\hat q(\\cdot\\mid c))$. Therefore $\\ln Z(c)\\le0$, which is (O.5.4.2). For a general law the same substitution gives\n$$\n\\sum_{p}q(p\\mid c)\\,\\omega(p,c)\n=H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}\\bigl(q(\\cdot\\mid c)\\bigr)\n+D_{\\mathrm{KL}}\\bigl(q(\\cdot\\mid c)\\,\\Vert\\,\\hat q(\\cdot\\mid c)\\bigr)\n-\\ln Z(c),\n$$\nand averaging over $c$ gives (O.5.4.3). Relative entropy is nonnegative and vanishes exactly at equality of laws, and $-\\ln Z(c)\\ge0$ by (O.5.4.2), which proves the equality criterion. ∎\n\nEquation (O.5.4.3) agrees with the mismatch-cost decomposition of Kolchinsky and Wolpert (2017): a registered reset implements the predictor $\\hat q$, its excess heat over the conditional-entropy floor is the expected log-loss regret of $\\hat q$ plus the normalization deficit $\\mathbb E_q[-\\ln Z(C)]$, and, at a fixed marginal law of $C$, the conditional law $\\hat q$ minimizes that excess.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o54d-pseudorandom-records-force-a-computational-reset-gap\"></a>**Theorem O.5.4d (Pseudorandom Records Force a Computational Reset Gap).** Let $G=(G_n)$ be a pseudorandom generator: a deterministic polynomial-time family $G_n:\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}^{\\ell(n)}$ with polynomial $\\ell(n)>n$ such that every uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}$ distinguisher $\\mathcal D$ has negligible advantage\n$$\n\\bigl|\\Pr[\\mathcal D(1^n,G_n(U_n))=1]-\\Pr[\\mathcal D(1^n,U_{\\ell(n)})=1]\\bigr|.\n$$\nLet the record $P_n=G_n(S_n)$ be computed from a uniform seed $S_n$ that is not retained, and let $(\\Pi_n)$ be reset implementations of the $\\ell(n)$-bit register that satisfy the hypotheses of Lemma O.5.4c with trivial side information and have mean-heat profiles $\\omega_n$. Call $(\\Pi_n)$ *efficiently simulable* if, with $\\Lambda_n:=\\ell(n)\\ln2+n$, a uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}$ machine $\\mathcal E$ satisfies\n$$\n\\Bigl|\\Pr[\\mathcal E(1^n,p)=1]-\\frac{\\min\\{\\omega_n(p),\\Lambda_n\\}}{\\Lambda_n}\\Bigr|\\le\\kappa_n\n\\qquad\\text{for every }p\\in\\{0,1\\}^{\\ell(n)},\n$$\nwith $\\kappa_n$ negligible. For every efficiently simulable family and every polynomial $r$, all sufficiently large $n$ satisfy\n$$\n\\frac{Q_{\\mathrm{env}}(n)}{k_BT_{\\mathrm{bath}}}\\ge\\ell(n)\\ln2-\\frac1{r(n)},\n\\qquad\n\\frac{Q_{\\mathrm{env}}(n)}{k_BT_{\\mathrm{bath}}}-H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(P_n)\\ge\\bigl(\\ell(n)-n\\bigr)\\ln2-\\frac1{r(n)}.\n\\tag{O.5.4.4}\n$$\nThe efficient-simulability premise is a uniform approximation condition on the clipped mean-heat profile for every record; it does not follow solely from polynomial physical duration or gate count. Pseudorandom generators of every polynomial stretch exist exactly when one-way functions exist (Håstad, Impagliazzo, Levin and Luby 1999; Goldreich 2001, Sections 3.3.2, 3.3.6 and 3.5), so (O.5.4.4) holds on the one-way-function branch.\n\n*Proof.* Write $\\omega_n'=\\min\\{\\omega_n,\\Lambda_n\\}$ and let $\\mathbb E_U$ and $\\mathbb E_G$ denote expectations over $U_{\\ell(n)}$ and over $G_n(U_n)$. By Lemma O.5.4c, $Q_{\\mathrm{env}}(n)/(k_BT_{\\mathrm{bath}})=\\mathbb E_G\\,\\omega_n$, and (O.5.4.2) with trivial side information gives\n$$\n\\sum_pe^{-\\omega_n'(p)}\\le\\sum_p\\bigl(e^{-\\omega_n(p)}+e^{-\\Lambda_n}\\bigr)\\le1+2^{\\ell(n)}e^{-\\Lambda_n}=1+e^{-n}.\n$$\nFor a law $\\mu$ and positive weights $w$ with total $W$, nonnegativity of $D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(\\mu\\Vert w/W)$ gives $\\mathbb E_\\mu[-\\ln w]\\ge H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(\\mu)-\\ln W$. With $\\mu$ uniform on $\\{0,1\\}^{\\ell(n)}$ and $w=e^{-\\omega_n'}$,\n$$\n\\mathbb E_U\\,\\omega_n'\\ge\\ell(n)\\ln2-\\ln\\bigl(1+e^{-n}\\bigr)\\ge\\ell(n)\\ln2-e^{-n}.\n$$\nSuppose the first inequality in (O.5.4.4) fails for infinitely many $n$. On those lengths $\\mathbb E_G\\,\\omega_n'\\le\\mathbb E_G\\,\\omega_n<\\ell(n)\\ln2-1/r(n)$, so\n$$\n\\Pr[\\mathcal E(1^n,U_{\\ell(n)})=1]-\\Pr[\\mathcal E(1^n,G_n(U_n))=1]\n\\ge\\frac{\\mathbb E_U\\,\\omega_n'-\\mathbb E_G\\,\\omega_n'}{\\Lambda_n}-2\\kappa_n >\\frac{1/r(n)-e^{-n}}{\\Lambda_n}-2\\kappa_n.\n$$\nSince $\\Lambda_n$ is polynomial and $\\kappa_n$ is negligible, the right side exceeds $1/(2r(n)\\Lambda_n)$ on all sufficiently large lengths of that infinite set. Then $\\mathcal E$ is a uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}$ distinguisher with non-negligible advantage, a contradiction, so the first inequality holds for all large $n$. The record takes at most $2^n$ values, so $H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(P_n)\\le n\\ln2$, which gives the second inequality. ∎\n\nWhen the seed is retained, $H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(P_n\\mid S_n)=0$ and the polynomial-time evaluator of $G_n$ supplies the compute-copy-uncompute construction of Theorem O.5.3j, which is the efficient form of the uncomputation clause in the proof of Proposition O.5.2c. Theorem O.5.4d locates the gap in the discarded seed: on a pseudorandom record, an efficiently simulable reset dissipates at least the Landauer floor of a uniformly random record of the same length, less $1/r(n)$.\n\n<a id=\"definition-o54e-separated-two-site-protocol-and-local-reset-ledger\"></a>**Definition O.5.4e (Separated Two-Site Protocol and Local Reset Ledger).** Two sites $A$ and $B$, such as the separated predictive aggregates of Section 14.3.1, hold inputs $X$ and $Y$ with joint law $\\mu$ on finite sets $\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y$. A finite protocol $\\Pi$ uses finite public coin tapes $R$ and private coin tapes $R_A$ and $R_B$, mutually independent and independent of $(X,Y)$. Each message is a function of the sender's input, its private coins, $R$ and the earlier messages; the transcript $M$ is the sequence of messages, and the output $V$ is a function of $M$ with values in a finite set $\\mathcal V$. Each site keeps a complete copy of $M$ through the start of the priced reset. Every local logical working record is a deterministic function of that site's input, its retained random tapes and $M$. A *local reset* of $\\Pi$ returns, at each site, the transcript copy and every working record to ready states under the conditional reset hypotheses of Lemma O.5.2b, retaining $C_A=(X,R,R_A,V)$ at $A$ and $C_B=(Y,R,R_B,V)$ at $B$, at bath temperatures $T_A$ and $T_B$, with no communication during the resets. The displayed $C_A,C_B$ are the complete accessible retained correlated data at their respective sites and remain unchanged; no additional readable transcript archive, surviving message copy or unpriced information sink is available. Reversible return of messages before this boundary is a different architecture. Input, random-tape and output retention, preparation, communication and control remain separately declared resources. The *reset ledger* and the *internal information* (Barak, Braverman, Chen and Rao 2013) of $\\Pi$ under $\\mu$ are, in bits,\n$$\n\\mathcal L^\\mu(\\Pi):=H_2(M\\mid X,R,R_A,V)+H_2(M\\mid Y,R,R_B,V),\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{IC}^\\mu_{\\mathrm{int}}(\\Pi):=I_2(M;X\\mid Y,R)+I_2(M;Y\\mid X,R).\n\\tag{O.5.4.5}\n$$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o54f-separated-reset-heat-and-internal-information\"></a>**Theorem O.5.4f (Separated Reset Heat and Internal Information).** For every protocol $\\Pi$, input law $\\mu$ and local reset of $\\Pi$,\n$$\n\\frac{Q_{\\mathrm{env},A}}{k_BT_A}+\\frac{Q_{\\mathrm{env},B}}{k_BT_B}\n\\ge\\ln2\\cdot\\mathcal L^\\mu(\\Pi)\n\\ge\\ln2\\Bigl[\\operatorname{IC}^\\mu_{\\mathrm{int}}(\\Pi)-H_2(V\\mid X,R,R_A)-H_2(V\\mid Y,R,R_B)\\Bigr],\n\\tag{O.5.4.6}\n$$\nand each output term is at most $\\log_2|\\mathcal V|$. Both output terms vanish when $V$ is constant on the support of $\\mu$. The second inequality holds for every $\\Pi$ and $\\mu$ without reference to a reset. Each summand on the left is nonnegative, so the total mean heat is at least $k_B\\min\\{T_A,T_B\\}\\ln2\\cdot\\mathcal L^\\mu(\\Pi)$.\n\n*Proof.* At $A$ the reset erases the transcript copy and the working records $W_A$ while retaining $C_A$, so Lemma O.5.2b gives $Q_{\\mathrm{env},A}/(k_BT_A)\\ge H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(M,W_A\\mid C_A)\\ge H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(M\\mid C_A)$; the same holds at $B$, which proves the first inequality and the nonnegativity of each summand. Because $V$ is a function of $M$,\n$$\nH_2(M\\mid X,R,R_A,V)=H_2(M\\mid X,R,R_A)-H_2(V\\mid X,R,R_A)\\ge I_2(M;Y\\mid X,R,R_A)-H_2(V\\mid X,R,R_A).\n$$\nThe private coins $R_A$ are independent of $(X,Y,R)$, so $I_2(R_A;Y\\mid X,R)=0$ and the chain rule gives\n$$\nI_2(M;Y\\mid X,R,R_A)=I_2(M,R_A;Y\\mid X,R)\\ge I_2(M;Y\\mid X,R).\n$$\nThe symmetric bound at $B$ completes (O.5.4.6). Each output entropy is at most $\\log_2|\\mathcal V|$ and is zero when $V$ is almost surely constant. ∎\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o54g-deterministic-protocols-exact-retained-output-correction\"></a>**Proposition O.5.4g (Deterministic Protocols: Exact Retained-Output Correction).** If $\\Pi$ uses no coins, then for every input law $\\mu$\n$$\n\\mathcal L^\\mu(\\Pi)=\\operatorname{IC}^\\mu_{\\mathrm{int}}(\\Pi)-H_2(V\\mid X)-H_2(V\\mid Y).\n\\tag{O.5.4.7}\n$$\nFor deterministic protocols the reset ledger therefore carries exactly the internal information of the protocol, less the entropy retained in its output.\n\n*Proof.* Without coins, $M$ is a function of $(X,Y)$, so $H_2(M\\mid X)=I_2(M;Y\\mid X)$ and $H_2(M\\mid Y)=I_2(M;X\\mid Y)$. Since $V$ is a function of $M$, $H_2(M\\mid X,V)=H_2(M\\mid X)-H_2(V\\mid X)$, and symmetrically at $B$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o54h-deciding-versus-checking-intersection\"></a>**Corollary O.5.4h (Deciding Versus Checking Intersection).** Let $X,Y\\in\\{0,1\\}^n$, and let $\\mathrm{DISJ}_n(X,Y)=1$ exactly when no coordinate has $X_i=Y_i=1$. Let $\\nu_n$ be the law with independent coordinates equal to $(0,0)$ with probability $1/2$ and to each of $(0,1)$ and $(1,0)$ with probability $1/4$.\n\n1. Every protocol $\\Pi$ that outputs $\\mathrm{DISJ}_n(X,Y)$ with error at most $\\delta<1/4$ on every input pair satisfies\n$$\n\\mathcal L^{\\nu_n}(\\Pi)\\ge\\frac n4\\bigl(1-2\\sqrt\\delta\\bigr)-2,\n$$\nso the sum of its two local reset heats under $\\nu_n$ is at least $k_B\\min\\{T_A,T_B\\}\\ln2$ times this number.\n2. A claimed common index $I$, included in $A$'s input and retained data, is checked by the deterministic protocol in which $A$ sends $I$ and $X_I$ and $B$ returns $V=X_I\\wedge Y_I$. Its transcript ledger is at most $\\log_2n+1$ bits under every joint law of $(X,Y,I)$. Under the working-record condition of Definition O.5.4e the same bound applies to the summed conditional-entropy erasure floor; it is not an upper bound on actual heat.\n\n*Proof.* Replace the public coins by a string drawn privately at $A$ and announced as the first message. The announced string is independent of $(X,Y)$, so the error on every input pair and the internal information are unchanged, and the resulting private-coin protocol has transcript $\\widetilde M=(R,M)$. Let $D=(D_1,\\ldots,D_n)$ have independent coordinates uniform on $\\{a,b\\}$; given $D_i=a$ let $X_i=0$ with $Y_i$ uniform, and given $D_i=b$ let $Y_i=0$ with $X_i$ uniform. Then $(X,Y)$ has law $\\nu_n$, $X$ and $Y$ are independent given $D$, and Bar-Yossef et al. (2004, Theorem 5.6 and Section 6) prove $I_2(X,Y;\\widetilde M\\mid D)\\ge\\frac n4(1-2\\sqrt\\delta)$ for every private-coin protocol with error at most $\\delta$. Since $\\widetilde M$ depends on $D$ only through $(X,Y)$,\n$$\nI_2(X,Y;\\widetilde M\\mid D)=I_2(X;\\widetilde M\\mid D)+I_2(Y;\\widetilde M\\mid X,D)\\le I_2(X;\\widetilde M\\mid Y,D)+I_2(Y;\\widetilde M\\mid X,D)\\le I_2(\\widetilde M;X\\mid Y)+I_2(\\widetilde M;Y\\mid X).\n$$\nThe first inequality uses $I_2(X;\\widetilde M\\mid D)\\le I_2(X;\\widetilde M,Y\\mid D)=I_2(X;\\widetilde M\\mid Y,D)$, valid because $I_2(X;Y\\mid D)=0$. The second uses $H_2(\\widetilde M\\mid X,Y,D)=H_2(\\widetilde M\\mid X,Y)$ together with $H_2(\\widetilde M\\mid Y,D)\\le H_2(\\widetilde M\\mid Y)$ and $H_2(\\widetilde M\\mid X,D)\\le H_2(\\widetilde M\\mid X)$. Hence $\\operatorname{IC}^{\\nu_n}_{\\mathrm{int}}(\\Pi)\\ge\\frac n4(1-2\\sqrt\\delta)$, and Theorem O.5.4f with $|\\mathcal V|=2$ gives item 1. In item 2, $M=(I,X_I,V)$ is determined by $A$'s retained $(X,I,V)$, so its local entropy term is zero. The other term is $H_2(I,X_I\\mid Y,V)\\le\\log_2n+1$. The working-record condition gives $H_2(M,W_A\\mid C_A)=H_2(M\\mid C_A)$ and symmetrically at $B$; excess dissipation remains unrestricted. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o54i-zero-ordinary-disagreement-ledger-and-monotone-formula-depth\"></a>**Theorem O.5.4i (Zero Ordinary Disagreement Ledger and Monotone Formula Depth).** Let $f:\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}$ be nonconstant with $n\\ge2$. In the ordinary Karchmer--Wigderson game $\\mathrm{KW}_f$, site $A$ holds $x\\in f^{-1}(1)$, site $B$ holds $y\\in f^{-1}(0)$, and the output is a coordinate $i$ with $x_i\\ne y_i$ (Karchmer and Wigderson 1990). Over finite zero-error protocols and laws on $f^{-1}(1)\\times f^{-1}(0)$, define\n$$\n\\mathcal L(f):=\\inf_\\Pi\\sup_\\mu\\mathcal L^\\mu(\\Pi),\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{IC}(\\mathrm{KW}_f):=\\sup_\\mu\\inf_\\Pi\\operatorname{IC}^\\mu_{\\mathrm{int}}(\\Pi),\n$$\nwith the output coordinate retained at both sites. Then:\n\n1. $\\mathcal L(f)=0$ and $\\operatorname{IC}(\\mathrm{KW}_f)\\le2\\log_2n$. One deterministic protocol attains zero ledger under every input law.\n2. A proposed coordinate $I$, included in $A$'s input and retained data, is checked by sending $I,x_I$ from $A$ and returning the verdict from $B$. Its transcript ledger is at most $\\log_2n+1$ bits under every joint input law. For the ordinary game the verdict is $x_I\\ne y_I$; for the monotone game it is $x_I=1$ and $y_I=0$. Under Definition O.5.4e's working-record condition this also bounds the summed conditional-entropy erasure floor, not actual implementation heat.\n3. The ordinary retained-output ledger supplies no nontrivial formula-depth lower bound: a superlogarithmic ordinary-KW ledger or internal-information bound cannot occur in this protocol class.\n4. For monotone $f$, define $\\mathcal L^+(f)$ and $\\operatorname{IC}(\\mathrm{KW}^+_f)$ analogously, requiring output $x_i=1,y_i=0$, and let $D^+(f)$ be the minimum depth of a fan-in-two monotone formula with variables at depth zero. Then\n$$\n\\max\\{0,\\operatorname{IC}(\\mathrm{KW}^+_f)-2\\log_2n\\}\n\\le\\mathcal L^+(f)\\le D^+(f).\n$$\nA monotone fan-in-two circuit family of depth $O(\\log n)$ has $\\mathcal L^+(f_n)=O(\\log n)$; consequently $\\mathcal L^+(f_n)=\\omega(\\log n)$ places the family outside monotone $\\mathsf{NC}^1$.\n\n*Proof.* For item 1, scan the coordinates in order. At coordinate $j$, $A$ sends $x_j$ and $B$ sends $y_j$; stop at the first mismatch and output $V=j$. The inputs differ, so the protocol terminates with a valid answer. If $V=i$, all earlier exchanged pairs agree and the last pair is opposite. Therefore $(X,V)$ determines the entire transcript $M$, and so does $(Y,V)$:\n$$\nH_2(M\\mid X,V)=H_2(M\\mid Y,V)=0.\n$$\nBoth ledger terms vanish under every law, and nonnegativity gives $\\mathcal L(f)=0$. As $M$ is a deterministic function of $(X,Y)$ and determines $V$,\n$$\n\\operatorname{IC}^\\mu_{\\mathrm{int}}(\\Pi)\n=H_2(M\\mid X)+H_2(M\\mid Y)\n=H_2(V\\mid X)+H_2(V\\mid Y)\\le2\\log_2n.\n$$\nThe related universal internal-information bound is Proposition 3.11 of Gavinsky et al. (2014, Section 3.3); the zero-ledger conclusion follows from the retained-output convention here. At each site the transcript can be reconstructed from its own retained input and $V$, XOR-cleared and locally uncomputed without further communication. This proves zero conditional-entropy floor, not zero heat for an arbitrary implementation.\n\nFor item 2, $M=(I,X_I,V)$ is determined by $A$'s retained $(X,I,V)$, and the other entropy term is $H_2(I,X_I\\mid Y,V)\\le\\log_2n+1$. The working-record qualification follows from $H_2(M,W\\mid C)=H_2(M\\mid C)+H_2(W\\mid M,C)$. Item 3 follows from item 1.\n\nFor item 4, Theorem O.5.4f and $\\inf_\\Pi\\sup_\\mu\\ge\\sup_\\mu\\inf_\\Pi$ give the lower bound; the two output terms total at most $2\\log_2n$. Karchmer and Wigderson (1990) give a deterministic protocol of communication cost $D^+(f)$. Expand the two local transcript entropies along its binary protocol tree. At an $A$ node, the outgoing bit is determined by $A$'s input and the prefix, so only the $B$-conditioned entropy contributes, by at most one bit; at a $B$ node the roles reverse. Summing node-reach probabilities bounds the two entropies by expected communication, hence by $D^+(f)$. Conditioning on the retained output can only reduce them. A fan-in-two depth-$d$ monotone circuit unfolds into a monotone formula of depth at most $d$, proving the last assertions. The first-disagreement scan is not generally valid for the monotone game, because its first mismatch can have the forbidden orientation. ∎\n\n<a id=\"lemma-o54j-information-transfer-along-local-reductions\"></a>**Lemma O.5.4j (Information Transfer Along Local Reductions).** Let $X'=\\varphi(X,R,R_A)$ and $Y'=\\psi(Y,R,R_B)$, where $R$, $R_A$ and $R_B$ are mutually independent and independent of $(X,Y)$, and let a protocol $\\Pi$ run on inputs $(X',Y')$ with its own independent coins, its transcript $M$ including its public coins. Then\n$$\nI_2(M;X\\mid Y,R)+I_2(M;Y\\mid X,R)\\le I_2(M;X'\\mid Y')+I_2(M;Y'\\mid X'),\n\\tag{O.5.4.8}\n$$\nwhere the right side is computed under the law of $(X',Y')$ induced by the reduction.\n\n*Proof.* Since $R_B$ is independent of $X$ given $(Y,R)$, $I_2(M;X\\mid Y,R)\\le I_2(M,R_B;X\\mid Y,R)=I_2(M;X\\mid Y,R,R_B)=H_2(M\\mid Y,R,R_B)-H_2(M\\mid X,Y,R,R_B)$. The variables $(Y,R,R_B)$ determine $Y'$, so $H_2(M\\mid Y,R,R_B)\\le H_2(M\\mid Y')$. Given $(X',Y')$, the transcript is independent of $(X,Y,R,R_A,R_B)$, so $H_2(M\\mid X,Y,R,R_B)\\ge H_2(M\\mid X,Y,R,R_A,R_B)=H_2(M\\mid X',Y')$. Hence $I_2(M;X\\mid Y,R)\\le H_2(M\\mid Y')-H_2(M\\mid X',Y')=I_2(M;X'\\mid Y')$, and the second term is bounded symmetrically. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o54k-ledger-lifting-by-critical-block-sensitivity\"></a>**Theorem O.5.4k (Ledger Lifting by Critical Block Sensitivity).** Let $S\\subseteq\\{0,1\\}^m\\times\\mathcal O$ be a search problem with a feasible output for every input and at least one *critical* input, one with exactly one feasible output. For a total function $h:\\{0,1\\}^m\\to\\mathcal O$ with $(\\alpha,h(\\alpha))\\in S$ for every $\\alpha$, let $\\mathrm{bs}(h,\\alpha)$ be the largest number of pairwise disjoint blocks $K\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$ with $h(\\alpha^K)\\ne h(\\alpha)$, where $\\alpha^K$ flips the coordinates in $K$, and let\n$$\n\\operatorname{cbs}(S):=\\min_h\\ \\max_{\\alpha\\ \\mathrm{critical}}\\ \\mathrm{bs}(h,\\alpha)\n$$\nbe the critical block sensitivity (Göös and Pitassi 2018). Let $g:\\mathcal X\\times\\mathcal Y\\to\\{0,1\\}$ be a finite two-party gadget that is *versatile*: there are $u_0,u_1\\in\\mathcal X$ and $v_0,v_1\\in\\mathcal Y$ with $g(u_\\epsilon,v_\\eta)=\\epsilon\\wedge\\eta$ for $\\epsilon,\\eta\\in\\{0,1\\}$; there are maps $\\pi:\\mathcal X\\to\\mathcal X$ and $\\sigma:\\mathcal Y\\to\\mathcal Y$ with $g(\\pi(x),\\sigma(y))=1-g(x,y)$; and there are maps $\\rho^A_s,\\rho^B_s$ indexed by shared randomness $s$ such that for every $(x,y)$ the pair $(\\rho^A_s(x),\\rho^B_s(y))$ is uniform on $g^{-1}(g(x,y))$. In the lifted problem $S\\circ g^m$, site $A$ holds $x\\in\\mathcal X^m$, site $B$ holds $y\\in\\mathcal Y^m$, and an output $o$ is feasible when $(g^m(x,y),o)\\in S$. Every zero-error protocol $\\Pi$ for $S\\circ g^m$ admits a critical $\\alpha$ such that, for the uniform law $\\mu_\\alpha$ on $(g^m)^{-1}(\\alpha)$:\n\n1. $\\Pi$ outputs the unique feasible output $o_\\alpha$ of $\\alpha$ with probability one on the support of $\\mu_\\alpha$;\n2. $\\operatorname{IC}^{\\mu_\\alpha}_{\\mathrm{int}}(\\Pi)\\ge\\operatorname{cbs}(S)/31$;\n3. $\\mathcal L^{\\mu_\\alpha}(\\Pi)\\ge\\operatorname{cbs}(S)/31$, so the sum of the two local reset heats under $\\mu_\\alpha$ is at least $k_B\\min\\{T_A,T_B\\}\\ln2\\cdot\\operatorname{cbs}(S)/31$.\n\nThe gadget $\\mathrm{VER}:\\mathbb Z_4\\times\\mathbb Z_4\\to\\{0,1\\}$, with $\\mathrm{VER}(x,y)=1$ exactly when $x+y\\bmod4\\in\\{2,3\\}$, is versatile (Göös and Pitassi 2018).\n\n*Proof.* For $\\beta\\in\\{0,1\\}^m$ let $h_\\Pi(\\beta)$ be an output of maximal probability when $\\Pi$ runs on $\\mu_\\beta$. Zero error makes every output feasible for $\\beta$, so $h_\\Pi$ is an admissible total function, and the definition of $\\operatorname{cbs}$ supplies a critical $\\alpha$ with $k:=\\mathrm{bs}(h_\\Pi,\\alpha)\\ge\\operatorname{cbs}(S)$, witnessed by disjoint blocks $K_1,\\ldots,K_k$ with $h_\\Pi(\\alpha^{K_j})\\ne h_\\Pi(\\alpha)=o_\\alpha$. Item 1 holds because $o_\\alpha$ is the only feasible output on $(g^m)^{-1}(\\alpha)$.\n\nIn unique disjointness on $k$ coordinates, $A$ holds $a\\in\\{0,1\\}^k$ and $B$ holds $b\\in\\{0,1\\}^k$ with at most one common coordinate, and the task is to decide whether they are disjoint. At each coordinate $l\\in K_j$ the parties place the pair $(u_{a_j},v_{b_j})$ and apply $(\\pi,\\sigma)$ when $\\alpha_l=1$; at each coordinate outside $\\bigcup_jK_j$ they place a fixed pair with gadget value $\\alpha_l$; they then apply independent shared-randomness self-reductions at every coordinate. Each party computes its part from its own input and shared randomness. The gadget value at $l\\in K_j$ is $\\alpha_l\\oplus(a_j\\wedge b_j)$, so the reduced input has law $\\mu_\\alpha$ when $a$ and $b$ are disjoint and law $\\mu_{\\alpha^{K_j}}$ when their common coordinate is $j$. Run $\\Pi$ five times, each run with freshly drawn self-reduction randomness and fresh coins of $\\Pi$, and answer \"disjoint\" exactly when every run outputs $o_\\alpha$. Disjoint inputs are answered correctly with probability one. On an input with common coordinate $j$, each run's input has law $\\mu_{\\alpha^{K_j}}$, under which the output $h_\\Pi(\\alpha^{K_j})\\ne o_\\alpha$ has maximal probability; each run therefore outputs $o_\\alpha$ with probability at most $1/2$, independently of the other runs, and the error is at most $2^{-5}$.\n\nAnnouncing the shared randomness as a first message, as in the proof of Corollary O.5.4h, leaves information and error unchanged. Let $U_r$ consist of the self-reduction randomness and the transcript of run $r$. The runs are independent given $(a,b)$, so $I_2(U_r;a\\mid b,U_{<r})=H_2(U_r\\mid b,U_{<r})-H_2(U_r\\mid a,b)\\le I_2(U_r;a\\mid b)$ and\n$$\nI_2(U_1,\\ldots,U_5;a\\mid b)+I_2(U_1,\\ldots,U_5;b\\mid a)\\le\\sum_{r=1}^5\\bigl[I_2(U_r;a\\mid b)+I_2(U_r;b\\mid a)\\bigr].\n$$\nUnder the hard law of Bar-Yossef et al. (2004), which is supported on disjoint pairs, each run has input law $\\mu_\\alpha$, and Lemma O.5.4j bounds its term by $\\operatorname{IC}^{\\mu_\\alpha}_{\\mathrm{int}}(\\Pi)$. Their direct-sum embedding evaluates a protocol only on pairs with at most one common coordinate, so their bound applies to this unique-disjointness protocol, and the inequality between conditional and internal information in the proof of Corollary O.5.4h gives\n$$\n5\\operatorname{IC}^{\\mu_\\alpha}_{\\mathrm{int}}(\\Pi)\\ge\\frac k4\\Bigl(1-2\\sqrt{2^{-5}}\\Bigr)\\ge0.1616\\,k.\n$$\nSince $0.1616/5\\ge1/31$ and $k\\ge\\operatorname{cbs}(S)$, item 2 follows. On the support of $\\mu_\\alpha$ the output is constant by item 1, so Theorem O.5.4f gives item 3 with both output terms equal to zero. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o54l-explicit-monotone-np-property-with-near-maximal-ledger\"></a>**Corollary O.5.4l (Explicit Monotone NP Property with Near-Maximal Ledger).** There is an explicit family of monotone functions $F_N\\in\\mathsf{NP}$ on $N$ input bits such that every zero-error protocol $\\Pi$ for $\\mathrm{KW}^+_{F_N}$ has an input law $\\mu_N$ with\n$$\n\\mathcal L^{\\mu_N}(\\Pi)=\\Omega\\!\\left(\\frac N{\\log N}\\right),\n$$\nwhile the protocol in which $A$ sends its input and $B$ names a coordinate has ledger at most $N+\\lceil\\log_2N\\rceil$ bits under every law. The monotone disagreement-location ledger $\\mathcal L^+(F_N)$ is therefore within a logarithmic factor of this trivial upper bound, and Theorem O.5.4i(4) gives $D^+(F_N)=\\Omega(N/\\log N)$.\n\n*Proof.* Göös and Pitassi (2018) take $F_N$ to be monotone satisfiability of constraint-satisfaction instances on a bounded-degree routable graph $G$ with $n$ vertices, with $N=O(n)$. They reduce the lifted Tseitin search problem $S(\\mathrm{Tse}_G)\\circ\\mathrm{VER}^{E(G)}$, with one gadget per edge variable, to $\\mathrm{KW}^+_{F_N}$: each party maps its lifted input to an input of $F_N$, a satisfiable instance at $A$ and an unsatisfiable one at $B$, and each answer of $\\mathrm{KW}^+_{F_N}$ is translated into a violated Tseitin constraint without communication. They also prove $\\operatorname{cbs}(S(\\mathrm{Tse}_G))=\\Omega(n/\\log n)$ for suitable bounded-degree expanders. Composing $\\Pi$ with this reduction gives a zero-error protocol for the lifted problem with the same transcript. Theorem O.5.4k supplies a law under which that composite has internal information at least $\\operatorname{cbs}/31$, and Lemma O.5.4j with deterministic maps transfers the bound to $\\Pi$ under the induced law $\\mu_N$. Theorem O.5.4f with $|\\mathcal V|=N$ then gives $\\mathcal L^{\\mu_N}(\\Pi)\\ge\\operatorname{cbs}/31-2\\log_2N=\\Omega(N/\\log N)$. The upper bound uses the incoming-message entropy accounting in the proof of Theorem O.5.4i(4) and the at most $N+\\lceil\\log_2N\\rceil$ communicated bits of the stated protocol. ∎\n\n<a id=\"remark-o54m-scope-of-the-ledger-and-the-internal-information-obstruction\"></a>**Remark O.5.4m (Scope of the Ledger and the Internal-Information Obstruction).** Theorem O.5.4f transfers internal-information lower bounds to the terminal local-reset ledger only after subtracting the two conditional entropies of the retained output; physical heat consequences additionally require the complete reset architecture. In the ordinary Karchmer--Wigderson game the subtraction is decisive: Theorem O.5.4i gives $\\mathcal L(f)=0$ for every $f$. The monotone orientation requirement prevents the same reconstruction argument, and Theorem O.5.4k and Corollary O.5.4l give $\\mathcal L^+(F_N)=\\Omega(N/\\log N)$, hence a monotone-depth lower bound through Theorem O.5.4i(4).\n\nThe general Karchmer--Raz--Wigderson function-composition conjecture (Karchmer, Raz and Wigderson 1995) remains a separate formula-complexity problem. Gavinsky, Meir, Weinstein and Wigderson (2017) prove its function/universal-relation analogue; their extended treatment (2014, Sections 2.6 and 3.3) principally uses external information and explicitly proves the universal $2\\log_2n$ internal-information bound for ordinary KW games. External information does not lower-bound the present local-reset ledger. For example, take $f(x)=x_n$, $X=(Z,1)$ and $Y=(Z,0)$ with uniform $Z\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n-1}$, and use the first-disagreement scan. The output $V=n$ is constant and both local ledger terms vanish, but an external observer learns $Z$, giving $I_2(M;X,Y)=n-1$. Replacing internal information by external information in Theorem O.5.4f would therefore be false.\n\n### O.5.5 Fixed-Source Prediction, Log-Loss Benchmarks, and Strong Restricted Inversion\n\nThis section connects fixed-source prediction with Definition O.5.2f and with the priced prediction of Section O.5.4, and strengthens the restricted inversion results of Section O.5.3. Proposition O.5.5a proves that a fixed sampled source whose hidden bit no uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}$ predictor guesses with non-negligible advantage, and whose records are ambiguous only with negligible probability, yields a length-preserving family satisfying (O.5.2.9). Theorem O.5.5b proves that a record ensemble is computationally indistinguishable from its uniform counterpart exactly when every bounded efficient sequential predictor has expected log-loss at least the uniform value $r\\ln2$ minus a negligible amount; it is the predictor counterpart of the reset gap of Theorem O.5.4d. Proposition O.5.5c bounds the average leave-one-out Bayes error of every record computed from an $n$-bit seed by $n/(2m)$, so the unpredictability of a pseudorandom record is computational. Proposition O.5.5c.1 bounds predictors that read few rows of a record with private linear bits. For rows $B_a\\oplus B_b\\oplus B_c\\oplus B_dB_e$ on a fixed wiring, Theorem O.5.5c.2 bounds the bias of every parity of rows through the size of the smallest stopping set, the AND-degree and the number of repeated AND pairs, and hence the advantage of every linear predictor of one row from the others; Corollary O.5.5c.3 bounds every fixed-depth polynomial-size circuit predictor through Braverman's theorem, and Proposition O.5.5c.4 proves that random wirings satisfy both hypotheses. Theorem O.5.5d gives an explicit length-preserving involution whose inversion success against every fixed-depth polynomial-size circuit family is negligible; it is a separately defined family with its own negligible bound in the sense of Corollary O.5.3g.1, and it is its own linear-time inverse. Here $h_2$ is the binary entropy in nats of Proposition O.5.2c.\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o55a-fixed-source-unpredictability-yields-a-length-preserving-one-way-function\"></a>**Proposition O.5.5a (Fixed-Source Unpredictability Yields a Length-Preserving One-Way Function).** Let one deterministic uniform polynomial-time sampler map a seed $s\\in\\{0,1\\}^n$ to a hidden bit and a record,\n$$\ns\\longmapsto\\bigl(B_n(s),Y_n(s)\\bigr),\\qquad B_n(s)\\in\\{0,1\\},\\qquad Y_n(s)\\in\\{0,1\\}^{m(n)},\n$$\nwhere $m(n)\\le n$ is computable from $1^n$ in polynomial time, and let $S\\leftarrow U_n$. Call a record $y$ *ambiguous* when its seed fiber $\\{s:Y_n(s)=y\\}$ contains two seeds with different hidden bits, and put $\\varepsilon(n):=\\Pr[Y_n(S)\\text{ is ambiguous}]$. Suppose that $\\varepsilon$ is negligible and that for every fixed uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}$ predictor $\\mathcal A$ there is a negligible function $\\nu_{\\mathcal A}$ with\n$$\n\\Pr\\bigl[\\mathcal A(1^n,Y_n(S))=B_n(S)\\bigr]\\le\\frac12+\\nu_{\\mathcal A}(n)\n\\tag{O.5.5.1}\n$$\nfor all sufficiently large $n$, the probability including the coins of $\\mathcal A$. Then the family\n$$\nf_n(s):=Y_n(s)\\,\\Vert\\,0^{\\,n-m(n)}\n$$\nhas one deterministic uniform polynomial-time evaluator, is length-preserving, and satisfies (O.5.2.9). For every fixed uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}$ inverter $\\mathcal I$ there is a fixed uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}$ predictor $\\mathcal A_{\\mathcal I}$ such that\n$$\n\\operatorname{Adv}^{\\mathrm{inv}}_{f,\\mathcal I}(n)\\le2\\nu_{\\mathcal A_{\\mathcal I}}(n)+2\\varepsilon(n)\n\\tag{O.5.5.2}\n$$\nfor all sufficiently large $n$.\n\n*Proof.* The evaluator runs the sampler and appends $n-m(n)$ zeros, so it is deterministic, uniform, polynomial-time and length-preserving. Fix $\\mathcal I$. On input $(1^n,y)$ the predictor $\\mathcal A_{\\mathcal I}$ runs $\\mathcal I$ on $(1^n,y\\Vert0^{n-m(n)})$, obtains a candidate $s'$ and checks that $|s'|=n$ and $Y_n(s')=y$. If the check passes it outputs $B_n(s')$; otherwise it outputs an independent fair bit. Under the locked law $S\\leftarrow U_n$ the check passes exactly when $\\mathcal I$ inverts, which has probability $p:=\\operatorname{Adv}^{\\mathrm{inv}}_{f,\\mathcal I}(n)$. Let $\\kappa$ be the probability that the check passes and $B_n(s')\\ne B_n(S)$. Since then $Y_n(s')=Y_n(S)$, this event requires $Y_n(S)$ to be ambiguous, so $\\kappa\\le\\varepsilon(n)$. The accuracy of $\\mathcal A_{\\mathcal I}$ is therefore\n$$\n(p-\\kappa)+\\frac{1-p}2=\\frac12+\\frac p2-\\kappa\\ge\\frac12+\\frac p2-\\varepsilon(n).\n$$\nComparing with (O.5.5.1) for $\\mathcal A_{\\mathcal I}$ gives (O.5.5.2). Its right side is negligible, so for every $c$ it is below $n^{-c}$ for all large $n$, which is (O.5.2.9) for $f$. ∎\n\nProposition O.5.5a has the fixed-source quantifier order of (O.5.2.9): the sampler is fixed before every predictor. Discharging Definition O.5.2f through it requires proofs of (O.5.5.1) and of negligible $\\varepsilon$ for one fixed sampler, which are not supplied.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o55b-uniform-entropy-log-loss-benchmark\"></a>**Theorem O.5.5b (Uniform-Entropy Log-Loss Benchmark).** Let $P_n=(H_n,S_n)$ be sampled by one uniform polynomial-time algorithm from $1^n$, with $S_n\\in\\{0,1\\}^{r(n)}$ for a polynomial $r$, and let $Q_n=(H_n,U_{r(n)})$ have the same marginal law of $H_n$ and an independent uniform record. A *bounded sequential predictor* $q$ is a uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}$ algorithm that, for each $i\\le r(n)$, receives $(1^n,H_n,S_{n,<i})$ and fresh coins $C_i$ and returns rational probabilities $q_i(0),q_i(1)\\ge2^{-p_q(n)}$ with $q_i(0)+q_i(1)=1$, for a fixed polynomial $p_q$. Its expected log-loss is\n$$\n\\mathcal L(q;P_n):=\\sum_{i=1}^{r(n)}\\mathbb E\\bigl[-\\ln q_i\\bigl(S_{n,i}\\mid H_n,S_{n,<i};C_i\\bigr)\\bigr],\n\\tag{O.5.5.3}\n$$\nand $\\mathcal L(q;Q_n)$ is defined in the same way. For a test $D$ put $\\Delta_D(n):=\\Pr[D(1^n,P_n)=1]-\\Pr[D(1^n,Q_n)=1]$, the probabilities including the coins of $D$.\n\n1. Every bounded sequential predictor satisfies $\\mathcal L(q;Q_n)\\ge r(n)\\ln2$.\n2. Let $D$ be a uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}$ test and let $\\epsilon(n)\\in(0,1/2]$ be rational and computable from $1^n$ in polynomial time, with $1/\\epsilon(n)$ bounded by a polynomial in $n$. There is a bounded sequential predictor $\\widehat q$, with all probabilities at least $1/3$ and running time polynomial in $n$, $1/\\epsilon(n)$ and the running time of $D$, such that at every $n$ with $\\Delta_D(n)\\ge\\epsilon(n)$\n$$\n\\mathcal L(\\widehat q;P_n)\\le r(n)\\ln2-\\frac{\\epsilon(n)^2}4.\n\\tag{O.5.5.4}\n$$\n3. $P_n$ and $Q_n$ are computationally indistinguishable against uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}$ tests if and only if every bounded sequential predictor $q$ satisfies\n$$\n\\mathcal L(q;P_n)\\ge r(n)\\ln2-\\nu_q(n)\n\\tag{O.5.5.5}\n$$\nfor a negligible function $\\nu_q$ and all sufficiently large $n$.\n\nFor the pseudorandom generator of Theorem O.5.4d, with $H_n=1^n$ and $S_n=G_n(U_n)$, item 3 gives $\\mathcal L(q;P_n)\\ge\\ell(n)\\ln2-\\operatorname{negl}(n)$ for every bounded sequential predictor, while the true conditional laws give log-loss $H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(G_n(U_n))\\le n\\ln2$. Theorem O.5.4d is the corresponding statement for registered resets with efficiently simulable heat profiles.\n\n*Proof.* Under $Q_n$ the bit $S_{n,i}$ is uniform and independent of $(H_n,S_{n,<i},C_i)$, so each summand of $\\mathcal L(q;Q_n)$ equals $\\mathbb E\\bigl[-\\tfrac12\\bigl(\\ln q_i(0)+\\ln q_i(1)\\bigr)\\bigr]\\ge\\ln2$, because $q_i(0)q_i(1)\\le1/4$. This proves item 1.\n\nFor item 2 write $r=r(n)$, $\\epsilon=\\epsilon(n)$, $\\alpha:=\\epsilon$, $d(h,s):=\\Pr[D(1^n,h,s)=1]$ and $\\mu(h):=2^{-r}\\sum_sd(h,s)$. The reference law\n$$\nq^*_h(s):=2^{-r}\\,\\frac{1+\\alpha d(h,s)}{1+\\alpha\\mu(h)}\n$$\nis normalized for every $h$. Since $0\\le d,\\mu\\le1$, the inequalities $\\ln(1+x)\\ge x-x^2/2$ and $\\ln(1+x)\\le x$ for $x\\ge0$ give\n$$\n\\mathbb E_{P_n}\\bigl[-\\ln q^*_{H_n}(S_n)\\bigr]\n=r\\ln2-\\mathbb E_{P_n}\\ln\\bigl(1+\\alpha d(H_n,S_n)\\bigr)+\\mathbb E\\ln\\bigl(1+\\alpha\\mu(H_n)\\bigr)\n\\le r\\ln2-\\alpha\\Delta_D(n)+\\frac{\\alpha^2}2,\n$$\nwhere the equal marginal laws of $H_n$ under $P_n$ and $Q_n$ identify $\\mathbb E\\,\\mu(H_n)$ with $\\Pr[D(1^n,Q_n)=1]$. At every $n$ with $\\Delta_D(n)\\ge\\epsilon$ the right side is at most $r\\ln2-\\epsilon^2/2$. For a prefix $v\\in\\{0,1\\}^{i-1}$ and $t\\in\\{0,1\\}$ put $\\mu_t(h,v):=2^{-(r-i)}\\sum_{u\\in\\{0,1\\}^{r-i}}d(h,vtu)$. Summing $q^*_h$ over completions gives the exact conditional law\n$$\nq^*_i(t\\mid h,v)=\\frac{1+\\alpha\\mu_t(h,v)}{2+\\alpha\\bigl(\\mu_0(h,v)+\\mu_1(h,v)\\bigr)}.\n\\tag{O.5.5.6}\n$$\nOn input $(1^n,h,v)$ the predictor $\\widehat q$ sets $K:=\\lceil(4r/\\epsilon)^2\\rceil$ and, for each $t\\in\\{0,1\\}$, runs $D$ with fresh coins on $K$ inputs $(h,vtu)$ with independent uniform $u\\in\\{0,1\\}^{r-i}$; it lets $\\widehat\\mu_t$ be the fraction of acceptances and returns the probabilities $\\widehat q_i(t)$ obtained by substituting $\\widehat\\mu_0,\\widehat\\mu_1$ for $\\mu_0,\\mu_1$ in (O.5.5.6). They are rational, sum to one and are at least $1/(2+2\\alpha)\\ge1/3$, and the running time is as stated. The derivatives of $x\\mapsto\\ln(1+\\alpha x)$ and $y\\mapsto\\ln(2+\\alpha y)$ on $[0,\\infty)$ are at most $\\alpha$ and $\\alpha/2$, so\n$$\n\\bigl|\\ln\\widehat q_i(t)-\\ln q^*_i(t\\mid h,v)\\bigr|\n\\le\\alpha|\\widehat\\mu_t-\\mu_t|+\\frac\\alpha2\\bigl(|\\widehat\\mu_0-\\mu_0|+|\\widehat\\mu_1-\\mu_1|\\bigr).\n$$\nEach $\\widehat\\mu_t$ is the mean of $K$ independent Bernoulli variables with mean $\\mu_t(h,v)$, so $\\mathbb E|\\widehat\\mu_t-\\mu_t|\\le(4K)^{-1/2}$. The coins are fresh, hence independent of $S_{n,i}$ given $(H_n,S_{n,<i})$, and the expected logarithmic error at the realized bit is at most $\\alpha K^{-1/2}$. Summing over $i$ and using the chain rule $q^*_h(s)=\\prod_iq^*_i(s_i\\mid h,s_{<i})$ gives\n$$\n\\mathcal L(\\widehat q;P_n)\\le\\mathbb E_{P_n}\\bigl[-\\ln q^*_{H_n}(S_n)\\bigr]+\\frac{r\\alpha}{\\sqrt K}\\le r\\ln2-\\frac{\\epsilon^2}2+\\frac{\\epsilon^2}4,\n$$\nwhich is (O.5.5.4).\n\nFor item 3, suppose first that every bounded sequential predictor satisfies (O.5.5.5), and let a uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}$ test $D$ have $|\\Delta_D(n)|\\ge n^{-a}$ for infinitely many $n$. One of $D$ and its complement $1-D$ has $\\Delta\\ge n^{-a}$ on infinitely many $n$, and item 2 with $\\epsilon(n)=\\min\\{1/2,n^{-a}\\}$ gives one bounded sequential predictor with $\\mathcal L(\\widehat q;P_n)\\le r(n)\\ln2-\\epsilon(n)^2/4$ on those $n$, contradicting (O.5.5.5). Conversely, let $P_n$ and $Q_n$ be indistinguishable and let $q$ be a bounded sequential predictor with $\\mathcal L(q;P_n)\\le r(n)\\ln2-n^{-a}$ for infinitely many $n$. Its total loss $\\Lambda$ lies in $[0,B(n)]$ with $B(n):=r(n)p_q(n)\\ln2$. The test $D_q$ runs $q$ on the prefixes of its input with fresh coins, computes a rational $\\widetilde\\Lambda\\in[0,B(n)]$ with $|\\widetilde\\Lambda-\\Lambda|\\le n^{-a}/8$, and accepts with a dyadic probability within $2^{-n}$ of $\\widetilde\\Lambda/B(n)$. Item 1 gives\n$$\n\\Pr[D_q(1^n,Q_n)=1]-\\Pr[D_q(1^n,P_n)=1]\\ge\\frac{n^{-a}-n^{-a}/4}{B(n)}-2^{1-n},\n$$\nwhich exceeds $n^{-a}/(2B(n))$ for all large $n$ in that infinite set, contradicting indistinguishability. ∎\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o55c-leave-one-out-predictability-of-a-seeded-record\"></a>**Proposition O.5.5c (Leave-One-Out Predictability of a Seeded Record).** Let $G:\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}^m$ be a deterministic map, $X\\leftarrow U_n$ and $Y=G(X)$. For $1\\le i\\le m$ let $e_i$ be the least probability of error of a predictor of $Y_i$ that observes the other coordinates $Y_{-i}$. Then\n$$\n\\frac1m\\sum_{i=1}^me_i\\le\\frac n{2m},\n\\tag{O.5.5.7}\n$$\nwith equality for the identity map. Consequently the Bayes predictor that receives a uniform index $J\\in\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$, independent of $X$, together with $Y_{-J}$ predicts $Y_J$ with probability at least $1-n/(2m)$. If $G=(G_n)$ is a pseudorandom generator with output length $m=\\ell(n)$, as in Theorem O.5.4d, every uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}$ algorithm that receives $(1^n,J,Y_{-J})$ predicts $Y_J$ with probability at most $1/2+\\operatorname{negl}(n)$.\n\n*Proof.* For an observed value $y_{-i}$ of positive probability let $\\pi=\\Pr[Y_i=1\\mid Y_{-i}=y_{-i}]$. The least conditional error is $\\min\\{\\pi,1-\\pi\\}$, and $h_2(\\pi)\\ge2\\ln2\\cdot\\min\\{\\pi,1-\\pi\\}$ because $h_2$ is concave, symmetric about $1/2$, and equal to $0$ at $0$ and to $\\ln2$ at $1/2$. Averaging gives $e_i\\le H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(Y_i\\mid Y_{-i})/(2\\ln2)$. Conditioning on more coordinates does not increase entropy, so the chain rule gives\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^mH_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(Y_i\\mid Y_{-i})\\le\\sum_{i=1}^mH_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(Y_i\\mid Y_1,\\ldots,Y_{i-1})=H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(Y)\\le H_{\\mathrm{Sh}}(X)=n\\ln2,\n$$\nwhich proves (O.5.5.7); for the identity map every $e_i=1/2$. The Bayes predictor with a uniform index errs with probability $m^{-1}\\sum_ie_i$. For the last statement let a uniform $\\mathsf{PPT}$ predictor $\\mathcal A$ succeed with probability $1/2+\\delta(n)$. The test that draws $J$ by rejection sampling from $\\lceil\\log_2\\ell(n)\\rceil$ fair bits per round, outputs $0$ after $n$ unsuccessful rounds, and otherwise accepts exactly when $\\mathcal A(1^n,J,y_{-J})=y_J$ stops early with the same probability $\\phi_n\\le2^{-n}$ under both laws, and when it does not stop early $J$ is uniform. On a uniform input $y_J$ is uniform and independent of $(J,y_{-J})$ and of the coins of $\\mathcal A$, so the test accepts with probability $(1-\\phi_n)/2$; on $G_n(U_n)$ it accepts with probability $(1-\\phi_n)\\bigl(1/2+\\delta(n)\\bigr)$. The difference $(1-\\phi_n)\\delta(n)$ is negligible by pseudorandomness, and so is $\\delta$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"prop-o55c1\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o55c1-adaptive-query-bound-and-full-record-prediction\"></a>**Proposition O.5.5c.1 (Adaptive Query Bound and Full-Record Prediction).** Let \\(B=(B_1,\\ldots,B_n)\\) be independent fair bits, and let \\(Y_i\\) be fixed Boolean functions of specified seed coordinates. In a set of output rows, call a seed bit private and linear for row \\(i\\) when it occurs in that row as \\(B_j\\oplus f_i(B_{-j})\\) and no other row of the set uses it. A set is peelable if one can successively remove rows with such a bit until the set is empty. If each set of at most \\(q\\) observable rows together with the target row is peelable, every predictor allowed to query at most \\(q\\) observable rows has success probability \\(1/2\\), even with adaptive queries and arbitrary computation.\n\nFor each odd \\(L\\ge3\\) this bound is sharp on a family using the same five-input predicate in every row. For independent fair bits \\(t_0,\\ldots,t_L,h,u,v\\), use the prefix and target\n$$\nY_i=t_{i-1}\\oplus t_i\\oplus h\\oplus uv\\quad (1\\le i\\le L),\\qquad\nY_*=t_0\\oplus t_L\\oplus h\\oplus uv .\n\\tag{O.5.5.7a}\n$$\nUp to \\(L-1\\) prefix queries give success exactly \\(1/2\\). The full prefix gives \\(Y_*=\\bigoplus_{i=1}^L Y_i\\) and the displayed parity is evaluated with \\(L-1\\) binary XOR operations.\n\n*Proof.* The private linear bit makes its row a uniform bit independent of all other rows in the current set, by conditioning on every other seed bit. Induction on peeling gives joint uniformity. Fix the predictor's random coins. Every possible adaptive transcript with positive probability is specified by the returned bits on a set of at most \\(q\\) distinct queried rows. Their joint uniformity with the target makes the target fair conditional on the transcript. Averaging over the transcripts and coins gives the query bound.\n\nIn (O.5.5.7a), the \\(t\\)-coordinates label the vertices of a path, and the target row adds the closing edge. Any proper subset of prefix rows together with the target is a forest, whose leaf \\(t\\)-coordinates give private linear bits until no edges remain. Summing the full prefix cancels every interior \\(t_i\\) twice and leaves \\(h\\oplus uv\\) once because \\(L\\) is odd. This proves the asserted full-record predictor. ∎\n\nThis separation is a bound on the number of available readings. Application to a physical network requires a source-specific restriction on which readings its output can depend on.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o55c2-parity-bias-of-xor-and-rows-on-a-fixed-wiring\"></a>**Theorem O.5.5c.2 (Parity Bias of XOR–AND Rows on a Fixed Wiring).** Let $B=(B_1,\\ldots,B_n)$ be independent fair bits. A *wiring* $W$ is a list of $m$ rows; row $i$ is an ordered $5$-tuple $(a_i,b_i,c_i,d_i,e_i)$ of distinct indices in $\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$, and its output is\n$$\nY_i=B_{a_i}\\oplus B_{b_i}\\oplus B_{c_i}\\oplus B_{d_i}B_{e_i}.\n\\tag{O.5.5.7b}\n$$\nThe indices $a_i,b_i,c_i$ are the *linear slots* and $d_i,e_i$ the *AND slots* of row $i$. In the terms of Proposition O.5.5c.1, a linear-slot index of row $i$ is a private linear bit of row $i$ within a set of rows exactly when no other row of the set uses it in any slot. A *stopping set* is a nonempty set of rows none of which has a private linear bit within the set. Call $W$ *$(s,\\Delta,R)$-good* when it has no stopping set of at most $s$ rows, every index lies in at most $\\Delta$ AND slots of $W$, and at most $R$ rows of $W$ have an unordered AND pair $\\{d_i,e_i\\}$ that is also the AND pair of another row. For a nonempty set $T$ of rows put $Y_T:=\\bigoplus_{i\\in T}Y_i$ and $\\operatorname{bias}(T):=\\bigl|\\mathbb E(-1)^{Y_T}\\bigr|$. Let $G_T$ be the graph on $\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$ whose edges are the AND pairs that occur in an odd number of rows of $T$, let $A_T$ be its adjacency matrix over $\\mathbb F_2$, and let $\\nu(G_T)$ be the largest number of edges of $G_T$ that form an induced matching. Then:\n\n1. A set of rows is peelable in the sense of Proposition O.5.5c.1 exactly when it contains no stopping set.\n2. Write $Y_T=f_T(B)$, where $f_T$ is a polynomial over $\\mathbb F_2$ of degree at most two without constant term. Then $\\operatorname{bias}(T)^2=2^{-\\operatorname{rank}A_T}$ if $f_T$ vanishes on $\\ker A_T$, and $\\operatorname{bias}(T)=0$ otherwise.\n3. If $T$ is not a stopping set, then $\\operatorname{bias}(T)=0$.\n4. $\\operatorname{bias}(T)\\le2^{-\\nu(G_T)}$. If every index lies in at most $\\Delta$ AND slots of $W$ and at most $R$ rows of $W$ share their AND pair with another row, then $\\operatorname{bias}(T)\\le2^{-(|T|-R)/(2\\Delta^2)}$.\n5. If $W$ is $(s,\\Delta,R)$-good, every nonempty set $T$ of rows has $\\operatorname{bias}(T)\\le2^{-(s+1-R)/(2\\Delta^2)}$, with $\\operatorname{bias}(T)=0$ when $|T|\\le s$. Consequently every *linear predictor* of the last row, which outputs $c\\oplus\\bigoplus_{i\\in T'}Y_i$ for a bit $c$ and a set $T'\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,m-1\\}$, or draws such a pair $(c,T')$ at random independently of $B$, satisfies\n$$\n\\Pr\\Bigl[c\\oplus\\bigoplus_{i\\in T'}Y_i=Y_m\\Bigr]\\le\\frac12+\\frac12\\,2^{-(s+1-R)/(2\\Delta^2)}.\n\\tag{O.5.5.7c}\n$$\n\nThe hypotheses of item 5 are invariant under reordering the rows, so (O.5.5.7c) also bounds the prediction of any row from all the others. Mossel, Shpilka and Trevisan proved an exponentially small bias for a five-local generator whose three-bit linear part and two-bit product part read independent seeds, through the same two regimes: exact balance of small sets of rows and exponential decay of the bias of large sets of products (Mossel, Shpilka and Trevisan 2006). In (O.5.5.7b) both parts read one seed, and the stopping-set condition takes the place of the independence of the seeds.\n\n*Proof.* For item 1, if a set contains a stopping set $S$, no row of $S$ is ever removed: while $S$ remains, a linear bit private within the current set would be private within $S$. If the removal of rows with private linear bits stops at a nonempty set, that set is a stopping set.\n\nFor item 2 write $f=f_T$ and $A=A_T$. An AND pair that occurs in an even number of rows of $T$ cancels, so $f(x)=\\ell(x)+\\sum_{\\{d,e\\}\\in E(G_T)}x_dx_e$ with $\\ell$ linear, and $f(x+z)=f(x)+f(z)+x^{\\mathsf T}Az$ for all $x,z\\in\\mathbb F_2^n$. Hence\n$$\n\\bigl(\\mathbb E(-1)^{f(B)}\\bigr)^2=4^{-n}\\sum_{x,z}(-1)^{f(x)+f(x+z)}=4^{-n}\\sum_z(-1)^{f(z)}\\sum_x(-1)^{x^{\\mathsf T}Az}=2^{-n}\\sum_{z\\in\\ker A}(-1)^{f(z)}.\n$$\nThe cross term vanishes on $\\ker A$, so the restriction of $f$ to $\\ker A$ is linear, and the last sum equals $|\\ker A|=2^{n-\\operatorname{rank}A}$ when $f$ vanishes on $\\ker A$ and $0$ otherwise.\n\nFor item 3, some row $i\\in T$ has a linear-slot index $v$ that no other row of $T$ uses. The index $v$ lies in no AND slot of row $i$, so $f_T$ contains $x_v$ only as a linear term and $f_T(x+e_v)=f_T(x)+1$; the involution $x\\mapsto x+e_v$ pairs the terms of $\\mathbb E(-1)^{f_T(B)}$ with opposite signs.\n\nFor item 4, let $k=\\nu(G_T)$ and fix an induced matching of $k$ edges. The principal submatrix of $A_T$ on its $2k$ endpoints is a direct sum of $k$ blocks $\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)$, so $\\operatorname{rank}A_T\\ge2k$ and item 2 gives $\\operatorname{bias}(T)\\le2^{-k}$. Distinct edges of $G_T$ at a vertex $v$ come from distinct rows of $T$ whose AND slots contain $v$, so $G_T$ has maximum degree at most $\\Delta$. A row of $T$ whose AND pair is the AND pair of no other row of $W$ contributes that pair exactly once, so distinct such rows give distinct edges and $|E(G_T)|\\ge|T|-R$. Choose edges greedily: after an edge $uv$ is chosen, discard every edge with an endpoint in $N[u]\\cup N[v]$, a set of at most $2\\Delta$ vertices, so that at most $2\\Delta^2$ edges are discarded, $uv$ among them. No later edge has an endpoint adjacent to $u$ or $v$, so the chosen edges form an induced matching with at least $|E(G_T)|/(2\\Delta^2)$ edges. Hence $\\nu(G_T)\\ge(|T|-R)/(2\\Delta^2)$.\n\nFor item 5, a set of at most $s$ rows is not a stopping set, so item 3 gives bias $0$, and a set of at least $s+1$ rows has $\\operatorname{bias}(T)\\le2^{-(s+1-R)/(2\\Delta^2)}$ by item 4. For a fixed pair $(c,T')$ put $T:=T'\\cup\\{m\\}$; then\n$$\n\\Pr\\Bigl[c\\oplus\\bigoplus_{i\\in T'}Y_i=Y_m\\Bigr]=\\Pr[Y_T=c]=\\frac12\\Bigl(1+(-1)^c\\,\\mathbb E(-1)^{Y_T}\\Bigr)\\le\\frac12+\\frac12\\operatorname{bias}(T),\n$$\nand a random pair independent of $B$ averages these bounds. ∎\n\nThe wiring (O.5.5.7a) attains (O.5.5.7c). Its $L+1$ rows contain no stopping set of at most $L$ rows, because the $t$-indices of a proper subset of rows form a forest whose leaves are private linear bits, while all $L+1$ rows carry the AND pair $uv$, so $\\Delta=R=L+1$ and the right side of (O.5.5.7c) equals $1$; the parity of the full prefix predicts the target with probability one. On this wiring the stopping-set condition holds with $s=m-1$ and linear prediction is exact, so the bound of item 5 comes from the parameters $\\Delta$ and $R$.\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o55c3-fixed-depth-circuit-predictors-on-a-wiring-without-small-stopping-sets\"></a>**Corollary O.5.5c.3 (Fixed-Depth Circuit Predictors on a Wiring without Small Stopping Sets).** Let $W$ be a wiring as in Theorem O.5.5c.2 with no stopping set of at most $s$ rows. Let $C$ be a circuit of NOT gates and unbounded fan-in AND and OR gates with inputs $Y_1,\\ldots,Y_{m-1}$ and one output, possibly nonuniform and possibly reading independent random coins, with at most $S$ gates and depth at most $d$, the depth being the largest number of AND and OR gates on a path from an input to the output. If $\\sigma\\ge\\log_2(S+5)$ and\n$$\ns\\ge3\\cdot60^{d+5}\\bigl(\\log_2(S+5)\\bigr)^{(d+3)(d+5)}\\sigma^{(d+2)(d+5)},\n\\tag{O.5.5.7d}\n$$\nthen\n$$\n\\Bigl|\\Pr\\bigl[C(Y_1,\\ldots,Y_{m-1})=Y_m\\bigr]-\\frac12\\Bigr|<15(S+5)\\cdot0.82^{\\sigma}.\n\\tag{O.5.5.7e}\n$$\nFor fixed $d$, $S$ bounded by a polynomial in $n$, and $s\\ge\\delta n$ with fixed $\\delta>0$, the choice $\\sigma=n^{1/((d+2)(d+5)+1)}$ satisfies (O.5.5.7d) for all large $n$, and the advantage is $2^{-\\Omega(n^{1/((d+2)(d+5)+1)})}$.\n\n*Proof.* By item 1 of Theorem O.5.5c.2 every set of at most $s$ rows is peelable, so by the first part of the proof of Proposition O.5.5c.1 any $\\min\\{s,m\\}$ of the coordinates of $Y=(Y_1,\\ldots,Y_m)$ are jointly uniform. If $s\\ge m$, then $Y$ is uniform and the success probability is exactly $1/2$; assume $s<m$, so that the law $\\mu$ of $Y$ is $s$-wise independent. The deviation from $1/2$ of an average over coins is at most the largest deviation at fixed coins, so let $C$ be deterministic. Adding two NOT gates, two AND gates and one OR gate that compute $(o\\wedge z_m)\\vee(\\neg o\\wedge\\neg z_m)$, where $o$ is the output of $C$ on $(z_1,\\ldots,z_{m-1})$, gives a circuit $F$ on $\\{0,1\\}^m$ with at most $S+5$ gates and depth at most $d+2$ such that $F(z)=1$ exactly when $C(z_1,\\ldots,z_{m-1})=z_m$. Under the uniform law on $\\{0,1\\}^m$ the bit $z_m$ is independent of $(z_1,\\ldots,z_{m-1})$, so $\\mathbb E F=1/2$. Braverman's theorem (Braverman 2009, Main Theorem) states: if $F$ is computed by a circuit of depth $d'$ and size $m'$, $\\sigma\\ge\\log m'$, and $\\mu$ is $r$-independent with $r\\ge3\\cdot60^{d'+3}(\\log m')^{(d'+1)(d'+3)}\\sigma^{d'(d'+3)}$, then $|\\mathbb E_\\mu F-\\mathbb E F|<0.82^{\\sigma}\\cdot15m'$. With $d'=d+2$, $m'=S+5$ and $r=s$, condition (O.5.5.7d) implies these hypotheses whether $\\log$ is read to base $2$ or base $e$, and the conclusion is (O.5.5.7e). For the last statement put $e_d:=(d+2)(d+5)$. The right side of (O.5.5.7d) is $O\\bigl((\\log n)^{(d+3)(d+5)}n^{e_d/(e_d+1)}\\bigr)$, which is below $\\delta n$ for large $n$; $\\sigma\\ge\\log_2(S+5)$ for large $n$ because $\\log_2(S+5)=O(\\log n)$; and $15(S+5)\\cdot0.82^{\\sigma}=2^{-\\Omega(\\sigma)}$ for the same reason. ∎\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o55c4-random-wirings-satisfy-the-hypotheses\"></a>**Proposition O.5.5c.4 (Random Wirings Satisfy the Hypotheses).** Fix $c\\ge1$ and put $K_c:=c\\,e^{9/2}(7/2)^{3/2}$ and $\\delta_c:=(2K_c)^{-2}$. Let $m\\le cn$, and let the $m$ rows of $W$ be independent, each a uniformly random ordered $5$-tuple of distinct indices in $\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$. Then\n$$\n\\Pr\\bigl[W\\text{ has a stopping set of at most }\\lfloor\\delta_cn\\rfloor\\text{ rows}\\bigr]\\le\\frac{12K_c^2}{n},\n\\tag{O.5.5.7f}\n$$\nand when $\\ln n\\ge2e^3c$ the wiring $W$ is $(\\lfloor\\delta_cn\\rfloor,\\lceil\\ln n\\rceil,\\lfloor\\ln n\\rfloor)$-good with probability at least $1-(12K_c^2+1)/n-4c^2/\\ln n$. Hence such wirings exist for all large $n$. On each of them every nonempty parity of rows has bias at most $2^{-(\\lfloor\\delta_cn\\rfloor+1-\\ln n)/(2\\lceil\\ln n\\rceil^2)}$ by Theorem O.5.5c.2, and Corollary O.5.5c.3 applies with $s=\\lfloor\\delta_cn\\rfloor$.\n\n*Proof.* A single row is not a stopping set, since its three linear-slot indices occur only once in it. Let $T$ be a stopping set of $s\\ge2$ rows, let $P_L$ be the set of indices that occupy a linear slot of some row of $T$, and let $P_A$ be the set of the other indices used by $T$, which occur only in AND slots. Every index of $P_L$ occurs at least twice among the $5s$ slots of $T$, and $|P_A|\\le2s$, so $2|P_L|+|P_A|\\le5s$ and $T$ uses at most $|P_L|+|P_A|\\le(5s+|P_A|)/2\\le7s/2$ indices. For $s\\le\\delta_cn$, which is at most $2n/7$ because $c\\ge1$, put $k:=\\lfloor7s/2\\rfloor\\le n$; the rows of $T$ then lie in some $k$-element set of indices, and a fixed $k$-element set contains the five indices of a uniformly random row with probability $\\binom k5/\\binom n5\\le(k/n)^5$. Since $x\\mapsto(en/x)^x$ increases on $(0,n]$, the union bound over the $\\binom ms$ sets of rows and the $\\binom nk$ sets of indices gives\n$$\n\\Pr[\\text{some }s\\text{ rows form a stopping set}]\\le\\Bigl(\\frac{ecn}s\\Bigr)^s\\Bigl(\\frac{2en}{7s}\\Bigr)^{7s/2}\\Bigl(\\frac{7s}{2n}\\Bigr)^{5s}=a_s:=\\Bigl(K_c\\sqrt{s/n}\\Bigr)^s .\n$$\nFor $s+1\\le\\delta_cn$ one has $a_{s+1}/a_s=K_c\\sqrt{(s+1)/n}\\,(1+1/s)^{s/2}\\le\\sqrt e/2$, so the sum over $2\\le s\\le\\lfloor\\delta_cn\\rfloor$ is at most $a_2/(1-\\sqrt e/2)$; since $a_2=2K_c^2/n$ and $1-\\sqrt e/2>0.175$, this proves (O.5.5.7f).\n\nAn index lies in an AND slot of a given row with probability $2/n$, independently over the rows, so it lies in at least $t$ AND slots with probability at most $\\binom mt(2/n)^t\\le(2ec/t)^t$. For $t=\\lceil\\ln n\\rceil$ and $\\ln n\\ge2e^3c$ this is at most $e^{-2t}\\le n^{-2}$, and the union over the $n$ indices shows that every index lies in fewer than $\\lceil\\ln n\\rceil$ AND slots except with probability at most $1/n$. Two distinct rows have the same unordered AND pair with probability $1/\\binom n2$, so the number $R$ of rows that share their AND pair with another row satisfies $\\mathbb ER\\le m(m-1)/\\binom n2\\le4c^2$, and Markov's inequality gives $\\Pr[R>\\ln n]\\le4c^2/\\ln n$. The union of the three exceptional events has probability at most $(12K_c^2+1)/n+4c^2/\\ln n$, which tends to $0$. The final statements are item 5 of Theorem O.5.5c.2 with $s=\\lfloor\\delta_cn\\rfloor$, $\\Delta=\\lceil\\ln n\\rceil$ and $R\\le\\ln n$, and Corollary O.5.5c.3. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o55d-strong-mathsfac0-inversion-bound-for-the-block-parity-involution\"></a>**Theorem O.5.5d (Strong $\\mathsf{AC}^0$ Inversion Bound for the Block-Parity Involution).** For $N\\ge0$ let $b=b(N)$ be the largest integer with $b(b+1)\\le N$ and put $\\rho:=N-b(b+1)$. Write $w\\in\\{0,1\\}^N$ as $w=(x_1,\\ldots,x_b,z,a)$ with blocks $x_i\\in\\{0,1\\}^b$, a mask $z\\in\\{0,1\\}^b$ and a remainder $a\\in\\{0,1\\}^{\\rho}$, and define\n$$\nF_N(x,z,a):=\\bigl(x,\\ z\\oplus(\\operatorname{PAR}(x_1),\\ldots,\\operatorname{PAR}(x_b)),\\ a\\bigr),\n\\tag{O.5.5.8}\n$$\nthe identity when $b=0$. Then:\n\n1. $F_N$ is length-preserving, computable in time $O(N)$, and an involution, $F_N\\circ F_N=\\mathrm{id}$. In particular $F_N$ is its own linear-time inverter, and the family does not satisfy (O.5.2.9).\n2. Fix a depth $d$ and a polynomial $s$. Let $C_N$ be circuits of unbounded fan-in AND and OR gates over input literals, with depth at most $d$, at most $s(N)$ gates and $N$ outputs, possibly nonuniform and possibly reading independent random coins. Then, for all sufficiently large $N$,\n$$\n\\Pr_{w\\leftarrow U_N}\\bigl[F_N\\bigl(C_N(F_N(w))\\bigr)=F_N(w)\\bigr]\\le2^{-\\lceil(\\log_2N)^2\\rceil}+2^{-\\Omega_{d,s}\\left(\\sqrt N/(\\log_2N)^{2d+2}\\right)},\n\\tag{O.5.5.9}\n$$\nwhich is negligible in $N$.\n\nItem 2 is the strong counterpart of Proposition O.5.3g: it bounds full inversion success by a negligible function instead of by $1/2+\\operatorname{negl}(N)$.\n\n*Proof.* Applying the same mask twice returns $z$, and the $b$ block parities are computed in one pass; this proves item 1.\n\nItem 2 uses Håstad's correlation bound (Håstad 2014, Corollary 3.2) in the following form: for each depth $D$ there are constants $c_D>0$ and $K_D\\ge1$ such that every circuit $C$ of depth at most $D$ with at most $T$ gates on $k$ inputs, where $T\\ge2$ and $T\\ge k\\ge K_D(\\log_2T)^{D-1}$, satisfies $\\bigl|\\mathbb E_u(-1)^{C(u)+\\operatorname{PAR}(u)}\\bigr|\\le2^{-c_Dk/(\\log_2T)^{D-1}}$.\n\nUnder $w\\leftarrow U_N$ the challenge $F_N(w)=(x,y,a)$ has $y=z\\oplus\\operatorname{PAR}(x)$ and $a$ uniform and independent of $x$. A candidate $(x',z',a')$ inverts it exactly when $x'=x$, $a'=a$ and $z'=y\\oplus\\operatorname{PAR}(x)$; dropping the conditions on $x'$ and $a'$ only enlarges the success event. The success probability is an average over the coins, $y$ and $a$, so it is at most the success at one fixed choice of them; fix such a choice. The proposed mask coordinates are then circuits in $x$, and $C_i(x):=z_i'(x)\\oplus y_i$ is the $i$th of them or its complement. Together with their complements, obtained by De Morgan duality, the $C_i$ are computed by one circuit of depth at most $d$ with at most $2s(N)$ gates.\n\nFix $1\\le t\\le b$. For nonempty $I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$ put $Q_I:=\\bigoplus_{i\\in I}C_i$. The parity of $|I|$ bits is an OR of $2^{|I|-1}$ ANDs of $|I|$ literals, so $Q_I$ is computed by a circuit of depth at most $d+2$ with at most $T:=\\max\\{N,\\,2s(N)+2^t+1\\}$ gates. Fix the blocks $x_j$ with $j\\notin I$. The restricted circuit acts on the $k=|I|b$ bits of the blocks in $I$, with $b\\le k\\le N\\le T$, and $\\sum_{i\\in I}\\operatorname{PAR}(x_i)$ is the parity of those bits. If $b\\ge K_{d+2}(\\log_2T)^{d+1}$, Håstad's bound applied to each restriction, followed by averaging over the fixed blocks, gives\n$$\n\\Bigl|\\mathbb E_x(-1)^{Q_I(x)+\\sum_{i\\in I}\\operatorname{PAR}(x_i)}\\Bigr|\\le\\varepsilon:=2^{-c_{d+2}b/(\\log_2T)^{d+1}}.\n$$\nThe indicator of joint correctness expands as\n$$\n\\mathbf 1\\{C_i(x)=\\operatorname{PAR}(x_i)\\text{ for }i\\le t\\}\n=2^{-t}\\prod_{i=1}^t\\bigl(1+(-1)^{C_i(x)+\\operatorname{PAR}(x_i)}\\bigr)\n=2^{-t}\\sum_{I\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,t\\}}(-1)^{Q_I(x)+\\sum_{i\\in I}\\operatorname{PAR}(x_i)},\n$$\nwhere the empty set contributes $1$. Hence the success probability is at most $2^{-t}\\bigl(1+(2^t-1)\\varepsilon\\bigr)\\le2^{-t}+\\varepsilon$; no independence among the errors of the circuit is used. Take $t=\\lceil(\\log_2N)^2\\rceil$, which is at most $b$ for $N\\ge2^{17}$ because $b>\\sqrt N-2$. Then $\\log_2T=O_s\\bigl((\\log_2N)^2\\bigr)$, so $b\\ge K_{d+2}(\\log_2T)^{d+1}$ for all large $N$ and $\\varepsilon=2^{-\\Omega_{d,s}(\\sqrt N/(\\log_2N)^{2d+2})}$. This proves (O.5.5.9). ∎\n\n## O.6 Conditional Temporal-Medium Interpretations for CC and Gravity\n\nTheorem O.2 supplies synchronized phase-sector minimizers and low-noise stationary concentration under its stated hypotheses. The CC and gravity readings of this synchronized medium rest, in addition, on independent response and gravitational certificates: Theorem O.6a supplies an exact bounded temporal-wave to qubit-response intertwiner on the registered CC branch, and Theorem O.6.3 supplies an explicit finite arrow--CC--gravity coexistence witness.\n\n**O.6.1 Conditional Temporal Signaling Interpretation**\n\nOn a branch carrying a stable Definition-L.1 context, Theorem 34's strict-improvement certificate, Hypothesis 3's normalized response-active context-to-control map, and Appendix L's source, carrier, and calibration records, a physical signal $N(t)$ may implement a registered temporal modulation. Theorem L.1 supplies only a maximizer in a nonempty compact admissible mapping class; it does not construct that signal. A target-law shift additionally requires a nonzero calibrated generator or instrument response, and a perspective-kernel interpretation additionally requires a normalized context-to-kernel transfer. Temporal synchronization alone supplies none of these maps.\n\n### O.6.2 Temporal Waves: Separate Gravitational and CC Branches\n\n<a id=\"definition-o1-temporal-wave\"></a>**Definition O.1 (Temporal Wave).**\nA *temporal wave* is a propagating solution for a registered local cycle-rate or phase field, such as $\\tau(x,t)$ or $\\Delta\\phi(x,t)$, under a specified evolution equation, initial/boundary data, and causal-response law. The name alone does not identify an electromagnetic or gravitational field.\n\n<a id=\"remark-o4-conditional-comparison-of-gravitational-waves-and-cc-modulation\"></a>**Remark O.4 (Conditional Comparison of Gravitational Waves and CC Modulation).**\n\n**(a) Gravitational branch.** On the independently accepted Einstein branch and its linearized transverse-traceless regime, a time-dependent retained source can generate metric perturbations satisfying the standard linearized field equation and spin-2 polarization constraints. Identifying a substrate phase disturbance with that perturbation requires an explicit injective response map preserving the equation, gauge quotient, characteristic cone, normalization, and energy flux. Without that intertwiner, the two descriptions are only interpretively compatible.\n\n**(b) CC branch.** A controlled CC modulation exists only on the response-active realization branch stated above. Its implementation energy belongs to the registered matter or nongravitational-field stress-energy ledger, but neither high complexity nor frequency decomposition proves a target response.\n\n**(c) Energy-accounting boundary.** The Einstein equation uses the certified total nongravitational stress-energy source on its branch. General relativity has no unique generally covariant local stress-energy tensor for the gravitational field itself; an Isaacson-type wave tensor requires its separate high-frequency averaging hypotheses. Consequently, implementation energy may contribute to the Einstein source only after its carrier stress-energy and non-overlap ledger are specified, and no independent `energy of consciousness` term is introduced.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o6a-exact-bounded-temporal-wave-to-qubit-response-intertwiner\"></a>**Theorem O.6a (Exact Bounded Temporal-Wave to Qubit-Response Intertwiner).** Choose Minkowski spacetime with $c>0$, $x^0=ct$ and $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$, a baseline cycle rate $\\nu_0>0$, and a real constant $\\kappa\\ne0$ with inverse units of the real scalar cycle-rate perturbation $\\phi$. Let $\\phi\\in C^2$ satisfy\n$$\n\\nu=\\nu_0+\\phi>0,\n\\qquad\n\\Box_c\\phi=J,\n\\qquad\n|\\kappa\\phi|\\le1-\\epsilon\n\\tag{O.6a.1}\n$$\non the declared domain, where $\\kappa\\ne0$ has the inverse units of $\\phi$ and $0<\\epsilon<1$. At each spacetime point define the qubit state, retained effect and response\n$$\n\\rho_\\phi=\\frac12(I+\\kappa\\phi\\,\\sigma_z),\n\\qquad\nE_+=\\frac12(I+\\sigma_z),\n\\qquad\np_\\phi(+)=\\operatorname{Tr}(E_+\\rho_\\phi)=\\frac{1+\\kappa\\phi}{2}.\n\\tag{O.6a.2}\n$$\nThen $\\rho_\\phi$ is a normalized positive state, $0<p_\\phi(+)<1$, and the pointwise response map is injective with\n$$\n\\phi=\\frac{2p_\\phi(+)-1}{\\kappa},\n\\qquad\n\\Box_c\\bigl(2p_\\phi(+)-1\\bigr)=\\kappa J.\n\\tag{O.6a.3}\n$$\nThus it preserves the field equation and its characteristic cone exactly. The scalar branch has no gauge quotient; on the response side the measured probability fixes the scalar representative by (O.6a.3).\n\nFor a normalized channel realization, set\n$$\n\\Phi_\\phi(X)=\\operatorname{Tr}(X)\\rho_\\phi,\n\\qquad\nL_\\phi:=\\Phi_\\phi-\\Phi_0.\n\\tag{O.6a.4}\n$$\nWriting $r_0(\\phi)=p_\\phi(+)$ and $r_1(\\phi)=1-p_\\phi(+)$, an explicit local Kraus realization is\n$$\nK_{ab}(\\phi)=\\sqrt{r_a(\\phi)}\\,|a\\rangle\\!\\langle b|,\n\\qquad a,b\\in\\{0,1\\},\n\\tag{O.6a.4a}\n$$\nfor which $\\sum_{a,b}K_{ab}^\\dagger K_{ab}=I$ and\n$\\sum_{a,b}K_{ab}XK_{ab}^\\dagger=\\Phi_\\phi(X)$. Hence each $\\Phi_\\phi$ is CPTP, and $L_\\phi$ is complex-linear, Hermitian-preserving and trace-annihilating as required by Definition 30. Its operational norm and retained response are\n$$\n\\|L_\\phi\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}=\\frac{|\\kappa\\phi|}{2},\n\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Tr}\\!\\left(E_+L_\\phi(\\rho)\\right)=\\frac{\\kappa\\phi}{2}\n\\tag{O.6a.4b}\n$$\nfor every input state $\\rho$, so they are nonzero exactly when $\\phi\\ne0$. A local source/control realization is obtained by assigning $J$ to the scalar carrier equation and assigning preparation of $\\rho_\\phi$ to a separate controller with degenerate qubit Hamiltonian $H_Q=0$. With scalar Lagrangian\n$$\n\\mathcal L_\\phi\n=-\\frac12\\partial_\\mu\\phi\\,\\partial^\\mu\\phi-J\\phi,\n\\tag{O.6a.5}\n$$\nthe carrier stress tensor and source-work identity are the standard scalar ones,\n$$\nT_{\\mu\\nu}^{(\\phi)}\n=\\partial_\\mu\\phi\\partial_\\nu\\phi\n-\\frac12\\eta_{\\mu\\nu}(\\partial\\phi)^2,\n\\qquad\n\\partial^\\mu T_{\\mu\\nu}^{(\\phi)}=J\\partial_\\nu\\phi.\n\\tag{O.6a.6}\n$$\nThe owner tags `scalar-source/work` and `qubit-preparation/control` are disjoint and recombine only by addition; no qubit control work is counted as scalar-wave energy. Pointwise preparation depends only on $\\phi(x)$, so the response adds no propagation edge outside the cone already fixed by (O.6a.1). The class is nonempty: any plane wave of sufficiently small amplitude with $J=0$ and $\\nu_0>\\|\\phi\\|_\\infty$ supplies a nonconstant witness.\n\n*Proof.* The eigenvalues of $\\rho_\\phi$ are $(1\\pm\\kappa\\phi)/2$, which lie in $(0,1)$ by (O.6a.1), proving positivity and normalization. Direct trace evaluation gives (O.6a.2), and $\\kappa\\ne0$ gives the inverse and wave equation in (O.6a.3). Direct multiplication verifies the two Kraus identities after (O.6a.4a), so $\\Phi_\\phi$ is CPTP. Equation (O.6a.4) is therefore a difference of trace-preserving Hermiticity-preserving linear maps and is trace-annihilating and Hermiticity preserving. The trace-norm variational formula gives (O.6a.4b). Varying (O.6a.5) gives (O.6a.1), and direct differentiation gives (O.6a.6). For the cone claim, compare two admissible $C^2$ solutions whose $\\phi$ and $\\partial_t\\phi$ data agree on $B_R(x_0)$ at $t=0$ and whose sources $J$ agree throughout $K=\\{0\\le t<R/c,\\ |x-x_0|\\le R-ct\\}$; assume $K$ lies in the declared domain with no incoming boundary data. Their difference $u$ satisfies $u_{tt}-c^2\\Delta u=0$ on $K$. For $E(t)=\\frac12\\int_{B_{R-ct}(x_0)}(u_t^2+c^2|\\nabla u|^2)\\,dx$, the local wave-energy identity gives $E′(t)=\\int_{\\partial B_{R-ct}}[c^2u_t\\partial_nu-\\frac c2(u_t^2+c^2|\\nabla u|^2)]\\,dS\\le0$, since $2c|u_t\\partial_nu|\\le u_t^2+c^2|\\nabla u|^2$. As $E(0)=0$, the matching initial values imply $u=0$ in $K$. The response in (O.6a.2) depends pointwise on $\\phi$, so it adds no propagation outside this cone. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o63-explicit-arrow-cc-gravity-coexistence-witness\"></a>**Theorem O.6.3 (Explicit Arrow--CC--Gravity Coexistence Witness).** Fix $p>q>0$ with $p+q<1$ and a registered step duration $\\tau_{\\mathrm{arr}}>0$. On the three-state ring $\\mathbb Z/3\\mathbb Z$, let\n$$\nP(i,i+1)=p,\n\\qquad\nP(i,i-1)=q,\n\\qquad\nP(i,i)=1-p-q.\n\\tag{O.6.3.1}\n$$\nIts stationary law is uniform. For a finite history $\\gamma$, let $N_+(\\gamma)$ and $N_-(\\gamma)$ count clockwise and counterclockwise moves and define\n$$\n\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{arr}}(\\gamma)\n:=(N_+(\\gamma)-N_-(\\gamma))\\ln\\frac pq.\n\\tag{O.6.3.2}\n$$\nThen, for the stationary path law, $\\log(\\Pr\\gamma/\\Pr\\gamma^R)=\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{arr}}(\\gamma)$, the mean production per step is $(p-q)\\ln(p/q)>0$, and its registered rate is this quantity divided by $\\tau_{\\mathrm{arr}}$.\n\nTake, on a disjoint factor, the finite source, control, carrier, target, battery and instrument construction of Theorem L.12.8b with $0<g\\tau_{\\mathrm{int}}<\\pi$. It has retained aggregate source $S$, control encoder $U_{\\mathrm{ctx}}$, actuation battery $B$, and response\n$$\n\\mathfrak A_{\\mathrm{G9CC}}=\\sin^2(g\\tau_{\\mathrm{int}})>0.\n\\tag{O.6.3.3}\n$$\nOn a third factor take flat spacetime with $c>0$, $x^0=ct$, metric $\\eta=\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$ and periodic spatial coordinates on a three-torus. Independently choose a nonzero torus Fourier covector $k$ and an energy-density amplitude $\\rho_0\\ne0$, and register the static conserved source perturbation\n$$\nT^{\\mathrm{src}}_{00}=\\rho_0\\cos(k\\cdot x),\n\\qquad\nT^{\\mathrm{src}}_{0i}=T^{\\mathrm{src}}_{ij}=0.\n\\tag{O.6.3.4}\n$$\nIt is a signed perturbation about a separately owned homogeneous reference and is defined before the gravitational response. Set\n$$\n\\bar h_{00}\n=\\frac{16\\pi G\\rho_0}{c^4|k|^2}\\cos(k\\cdot x),\n\\qquad\n\\bar h_{0i}=\\bar h_{ij}=0.\n\\tag{O.6.3.4a}\n$$\nThen $\\partial^\\mu T^{\\mathrm{src}}_{\\mu\\nu}=0$, $\\partial^\\mu\\bar h_{\\mu\\nu}=0$, and the linearized Lorenz-gauge equation\n$$\n\\Box\\bar h_{\\mu\\nu}\n=-\\frac{16\\pi G}{c^4}T^{\\mathrm{src}}_{\\mu\\nu}\n\\tag{O.6.3.5}\n$$\nholds. Here $\\bar h$ is dimensionless, $k$ has inverse-length units and $T^{\\mathrm{src}}$ has energy-density units.\n\nPlace all three factors on the common time coordinate $t$: update the ring at multiples of $\\tau_{\\mathrm{arr}}$, execute the finite Theorem-L.12.8b operation in its registered cycle time $\\tau_{\\mathrm{cyc}}$, and evaluate the field (O.6.3.4) continuously. Their product is a nonempty common model because the three maps act on disjoint factors and commute; no equality between $\\tau_{\\mathrm{arr}}$ and $\\tau_{\\mathrm{cyc}}$ is assumed. Its source and unit ledger has three nonoverlapping rows: $(p,q,\\tau_{\\mathrm{arr}})$ owns the dimensionless history likelihood ratio and its inverse-time rate, $(S,B)$ own the dimensionless CC response and actuation energy, and $T^{\\mathrm{src}}$ owns the energy-density-valued linearized gravitational response. The time orientation is the sign convention selected by (O.6.3.2); it is not an argument of $U_{\\mathrm{ctx}}$ and is not a term in $T^{\\mathrm{src}}$. Changing $(p,q)$ while holding the other two factors fixed changes the arrow record without changing either physical source map.\n\n*Proof.* Translation invariance of (O.6.3.1) gives the uniform stationary law. Every clockwise step contributes $\\ln(p/q)$ to the forward/reverse likelihood ratio, every counterclockwise step contributes its negative, and holds cancel, proving (O.6.3.2); stationary averaging gives the positive mean. Equation (O.6.3.3) is Theorem L.12.8b. The source is time independent and has no component with a spatial first index, so it is conserved. The response is time independent and has only a $00$ component, so it is in Lorenz gauge. Since $\\Box\\cos(k\\cdot x)=-|k|^2\\cos(k\\cdot x)$, substitution proves (O.6.3.5). Tensor-factor extension makes the three evolutions commute, and the displayed owner rows are disjoint by construction. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o64-exact-arbitrary-target-obstruction-for-the-retained-hybrid-source\"></a>**Theorem O.6.4 (Exact Arbitrary-Target Obstruction for the Retained Hybrid Source).** Keep the complete action, finite material fibre, classical scalar and gravitational functional of Definition K.5.4a and Lemma K.5.4b. Fix either original classical context and every other ready register. Replace the target-ready state by an arbitrary density operator $\\rho$ on the same two-level target; retain its gap $H_T=|e\\rangle\\langle e|$, with the natural units and numerical parameters of that definition. A full-source extension of the target-input channel means one CPTP operation with these fixed non-target preparations, whose output retains the actual quantum fibre and the classical source/geometry responses of the same action. In particular it retains the relative proper acceleration $\\ddot a/a$ already specified in Definition K.5.4a, together with its bounded continuous postprocessings. The target variable is the arbitrary input operator of (L.12.8b.5). A proposed identification of its density operators with the homogeneous mean-source preparations must preserve that input's convex mixtures. A separately supplied, input-dependent classical source preparation is an additional input, rather than a fixed-source lift of (L.12.8b.5). The following statements hold.\n\n1. **Fixed-source initial-data obstruction.** At the original $a(0)=1$, $q(0)=0$, $\\dot q(0)=v_0=10^{-4}$, let\n   $$\n   p_\\rho=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho|e\\rangle\\langle e|),\\qquad\n   E_*=11+\\frac1{e+1}.\n   $$\n   The actual material energy is $E_*+p_\\rho$. The original fixed expansion $H(0)=H_0$ satisfies the gravitational constraint exactly when $p_\\rho=0$. Since $\\rho\\ge0$, this means $\\rho=|g\\rangle\\langle g|$. Thus the same initial geometry and all original source stocks admit precisely the original target-ready state, rather than every target preparation.\n\n2. **Exact obstruction after solving the initial constraint.** Giving each input its constraint-compatible expanding geometry instead yields\n   $$\n   H_\\rho(0)=\\sqrt{\\frac{8\\pi G}{3}\n      \\left(E_*+p_\\rho+\\frac M2v_0^2\\right)+\\frac\\Lambda3},\n   \\qquad\n   A_\\rho:=\\left.\\frac{\\ddot a}{a}\\right|_0\n      =\\frac\\Lambda3-\\frac{4\\pi G}{3}\n        (E_*+p_\\rho+2Mv_0^2).\n   \\tag{O.6.4.1}\n   $$\n   Every such initial datum has a local solution of the same fully varied action. The assignment of its deterministic classical acceleration law $\\rho\\mapsto\\delta_{A_\\rho}$, and hence its complete-source assignment, is not affine. It therefore cannot be the output of one CPTP target-input channel. The obstruction already holds at preparation, before any arrow, CC, readout or reset pulse.\n\n3. **Private-coherence-preserving compensation.** Let an alternative preparation operation $\\mathcal E$ retain this original target as a quantum input, so that its target marginal is $\\rho$ for every $\\rho$, with arbitrary mathematical reference extensions. Then any CPTP $\\mathcal E$ has the form\n   $$\n   \\mathcal E(\\rho)=\\rho\\otimes\\sigma_{\\mathrm{aux}}\n   \\tag{O.6.4.2}\n   $$\n   for one input-independent auxiliary state. This includes a classical auxiliary algebra. Consequently an input-dependent compensating mean energy or deterministic geometric label cannot be prepared by such an operation while retaining arbitrary target coherence. A source specified separately for each target preparation, or a new joint encoding of target and battery, changes this target-input preparation contract.\n\n*Proof.* All pulse functions vanish in a neighbourhood of $q=0$. The bare internal Hamiltonian there is $m_*I+K$, and the only altered term in its expectation is $H_T$. It adds $p_\\rho$ to the original $E_*$. The mixed bath, positive rest stock, battery, scalar kinetic term and every installed register remain present. The same-action homogeneous restriction of Lemma K.5.4b therefore gives\n$$\n3H(0)^2=8\\pi G(E_*+p_\\rho+Mv_0^2/2)+\\Lambda,\n\\qquad\n\\dot H(0)=-4\\pi G(E_*+p_\\rho+Mv_0^2).\n\\tag{O.6.4.3}\n$$\nSubtracting the original constraint proves item 1; positivity of a two-by-two density matrix with zero excited population also sets its off-diagonal entries to zero. Adding $H(0)^2$ and $\\dot H(0)$ proves (O.6.4.1). The homogeneous scalar/material/metric equations with the fixed smooth pulse functions are a smooth finite-dimensional ODE on $a>0$; their conservation identity preserves the initial constraint. Its positive square root and compact target-state set give a common short existence interval. This proves the asserted local solution family without recalibrating a pulse or replacing the gravitational action.\n\nFor nonaffinity, take $\\rho_g=|g\\rangle\\langle g|$, $\\rho_e=|e\\rangle\\langle e|$ and $\\rho_\\lambda=(1-\\lambda)\\rho_g+\\lambda\\rho_e$, with $0<\\lambda<1$. Equation (O.6.4.1) gives three distinct accelerations and\n$$\n\\delta_{A_{\\rho_\\lambda}}\n\\ne(1-\\lambda)\\delta_{A_{\\rho_g}}\n       +\\lambda\\delta_{A_{\\rho_e}}.\n\\tag{O.6.4.4}\n$$\nA bounded continuous function that is zero at $A_{\\rho_\\lambda}$ and positive at both endpoints witnesses this inequality. For example use\n$f(x)=(x-A_{\\rho_\\lambda})^2/[s^2+(x-A_{\\rho_\\lambda})^2]$,\nwhere $s=|A_{\\rho_e}-A_{\\rho_g}|>0$ has acceleration units. These are state responses of the retained classical geometry; testing them neither installs an extra apparatus nor discards a source sector. Restriction of a CPTP output to these responses is affine, which contradicts (O.6.4.4).\n\nThe obstruction also preserves the quantum input type. The nonorthogonal preparations $|g\\rangle$ and $|+\\rangle=(|g\\rangle+|e\\rangle)/\\sqrt2$ have input trace distance $1/\\sqrt2$, unchanged by adjoining any fixed source state. Their distinct deterministic acceleration laws have total variation distance one. The continuous effect\n$$\n f(x)=\\frac{(x-A_{|+\\rangle})^2}\n {(x-A_{|+\\rangle})^2+(x-A_{|g\\rangle})^2}\n$$\ntakes respectively the values one and zero on the two output points. A quantum-channel output effect pulls back to an input effect, whose probability difference is bounded by the input trace distance. Hence the same contradiction holds without treating coherent target preparations as classical labels.\n\nFor item 3, fix any auxiliary effect $0\\le B\\le I$. Reading that effect after $\\mathcal E$ defines a CP subchannel $\\mathcal E_B$ on the retained target, and $\\mathcal E_B+\\mathcal E_{I-B}=\\mathrm{id}$. Its finite target Choi operator therefore obeys\n$$\n0\\le J(\\mathcal E_B)\\le J(\\mathrm{id})\n   =|\\Omega\\rangle\\langle\\Omega|,\n\\qquad |\\Omega\\rangle=|gg\\rangle+|ee\\rangle.\n$$\nA positive operator dominated by a rank-one operator is a scalar multiple of it. Thus $\\mathcal E_B(\\rho)=b_B\\rho$, with $b_B$ independent of $\\rho$. Varying $B$ determines one auxiliary state and every joint target/auxiliary effect, proving (O.6.4.2). The argument uses effect evaluation, so it also covers classical or infinite auxiliary algebras and introduces no physical purification or hidden trace-out. Equation (O.6.4.2) gives $\\mathcal E\\otimes\\mathrm{id}_R$ equal to identity on every target/reference state, tensored with the same $\\sigma_{\\mathrm{aux}}$. ∎\n\n**Quantum-response distinction.** Theorem O.6.4 concerns the sharp classical mean-source constitutive rule of this particular action. A fixed self-adjoint interacting Hamiltonian on a jointly quantum source has the channel $X\\mapsto U XU^\\dagger$, even when its operator equations contain nonlinear products. A fixed initial source and a retained full unitary output likewise define a CPTP target-input channel. Populating that alternative requires its actual covariant action, source selection, quantum gravitational/carrier dynamics and exhaustive energy-owner recomposition. Neither replacing $\\delta_{A_\\rho}$ by an expectation value nor tracing out an additional unregistered sector supplies those objects.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o65-conditional-sector-record-channels-and-abstract-quantum-encoding\"></a>**Theorem O.6.5 (Conditional Sector-Record Channels and Abstract Quantum Encoding).** Let the input target Hilbert space $\\mathcal H_T$ be finite-dimensional, with\n$$\nE_T=\\sum_{x\\in X}E_x\\Pi_x,\\qquad\n\\mathcal D_E(Z)=\\sum_x\\Pi_xZ\\Pi_x,\n\\tag{O.6.5.1}\n$$\nwhere the $E_x$ are distinct, the nonzero orthogonal projections $\\Pi_x$ sum to the identity, and their ranks $d_x$ need not equal one. The finite set $X$ labels input sectors. Let\n$\\Phi:\\mathcal T(\\mathcal H_T)\\to\\mathcal T(\\mathcal H_{T'}\\otimes\\mathcal H_G)$\nbe a CPTP map on trace-class operators. The output Hilbert spaces may be infinite-dimensional. Declare nonzero closed subspaces $\\mathcal K_x\\subseteq\\mathcal H_G$, with orthogonal projections $P_x$. The following are separate assumptions:\n\n- **Sector support (C):** an input density operator supported in $\\Pi_x\\mathcal H_T$ has output supported in $\\mathcal H_{T'}\\otimes\\mathcal K_x$.\n- **Exact retained distinguishability (O):** $\\mathcal K_x\\perp\\mathcal K_y$ for $x\\ne y$.\n- **In-sector retention (R), only where invoked:** $T'=T$, and fixed density operators $\\gamma_x$ on $\\mathcal K_x$ obey\n$\\Phi(\\Pi_x Z\\Pi_x)=\\Pi_xZ\\Pi_x\\otimes\\gamma_x$ for every input operator $Z$.\n\nIf the $\\mathcal K_x$ are to represent physical constraint solutions, that identification and the energy/source ownership of the retained output are additional premises. O is an exact record assumption, not a consequence of gravitation alone. Discarding an energy record or allowing overlapping total-source energy supports can invalidate it. The following conclusions hold.\n\n1. **Erasure of input cross-sector dependence.** Under C and O,\n   $$\n   \\operatorname{Tr}_G\\Phi(\\Pi_xZ\\Pi_y)=0\\quad(x\\ne y),\\qquad\n   \\operatorname{Tr}_G\\Phi=\\operatorname{Tr}_G\\Phi\\mathcal D_E.\n   \\tag{O.6.5.2}\n   $$\n   This is an input-phase-erasure statement. It does not assert output energy diagonality without additional retention assumptions: a channel can measure the input energy and prepare a fresh coherent target output. No conclusion about within-sector coherence is imposed by (O.6.5.2).\n\n2. **Pairwise quantitative bound.** For any CPTP $\\Phi$, without C, O or R, and any unit vectors $\\psi_x\\in\\Pi_x\\mathcal H_T$, $\\psi_y\\in\\Pi_y\\mathcal H_T$, put\n\n   $$\n   \\eta_z=\\operatorname{Tr}_{T'}\\Phi(|\\psi_z\\rangle\\langle\\psi_z|).\n   $$\n\n   Then\n   $$\n   \\left\\|\\operatorname{Tr}_G\\Phi(|\\psi_x\\rangle\\langle\\psi_y|)\\right\\|_1\n   \\le F(\\eta_x,\\eta_y),\\qquad\n   F(\\sigma,\\tau):=\\|\\sqrt\\sigma\\sqrt\\tau\\|_1.\n   \\tag{O.6.5.3}\n   $$\n   Under C, $F(\\eta_x,\\eta_y)\\le\\|P_xP_y\\|$; under C and O it is zero. This controls the displayed rank-one marginal transition, not a uniform diamond-norm error, a joint-output coherence error, or an approximate solution of gravitational dynamics. For infinite closed subspaces the support-angle bound can equal one even when their intersection is zero.\n\n3. **Complete classification under C and R.** These assumptions hold if and only if there are operators $G_{xy}:\\mathcal K_y\\to\\mathcal K_x$, embedded in $\\mathcal H_G$, such that\n   $$\n   \\Phi(\\Pi_xZ\\Pi_y)=\\Pi_xZ\\Pi_y\\otimes G_{xy},\\qquad\n   G_{xx}=\\gamma_x,\\qquad [G_{xy}]_{x,y}\\ge0\n   \\quad\\hbox{on }\\bigoplus_x\\mathcal K_x.\n   \\tag{O.6.5.4}\n   $$\n   The positive block operator is trace-class, with trace $|X|$; its individual embedded blocks are trace-class. Equivalently,\n   $$\n   G_{xy}=\\sqrt{\\gamma_x}\\,C_{xy}\\sqrt{\\gamma_y},\\qquad\n   [C_{xy}]\\ge0,\\qquad C_{xx}=S_x,\n   \\tag{O.6.5.5}\n   $$\n   where $S_x$ is the support projection of $\\gamma_x$, and the canonical $C$ is supported on $\\bigoplus_xS_x\\mathcal K_x$. Every off-diagonal $C_{xy}$ is a contraction. For two sectors, the one off-diagonal contraction and its adjoint completely specify positive $C$ with these diagonals. O is not required for this classification. The marginal obeys\n   $$\n   \\operatorname{Tr}_G\\Phi(\\Pi_xZ\\Pi_y)\n       =(\\operatorname{Tr}G_{xy})\\Pi_xZ\\Pi_y,\n   \\qquad |\\operatorname{Tr}G_{xy}|\\le F(\\gamma_x,\\gamma_y).\n   \\tag{O.6.5.6}\n   $$\n   Under O these coefficients vanish for $x\\ne y$, and R makes the target marginal precisely $\\mathcal D_E$. A nonzero canonical $C_{xy}$ means a nonzero channel response to the corresponding input cross block; it does not by itself imply lossless or reversible preservation.\n\n4. **Atomic classical outputs.** Suppose $\\mathcal H_G=\\ell^2(\\mathcal A)$, every output is invariant under basis dephasing $\\mathcal D_G$, and $\\mathcal K_x$ is the closed span of basis vectors indexed by pairwise disjoint sets $\\mathcal A_x$. Under C,\n   $$\n   \\Phi(\\Pi_xZ\\Pi_y)=0\\quad(x\\ne y),\\qquad\n   \\Phi=\\Phi\\mathcal D_E.\n   \\tag{O.6.5.7}\n   $$\n   This is a factorization through the energy Lüders channel. If every $d_x=1$, it is a measure-and-prepare channel. If a sector is degenerate, its normalized in-sector state can affect both its quantum output and its classical law. The assertion is for the declared atomic classical algebra; a nonatomic classical response algebra requires a separately specified instrument formulation.\n\n5. **Abstract reversible encoding.** Choose unit vectors $A_x\\in\\mathcal K_x$. Then\n   $$\n   V\\psi=\\sum_x\\Pi_x\\psi\\otimes A_x,\\qquad\n   \\Phi_V(Z)=VZV^\\dagger\n   \\tag{O.6.5.8}\n   $$\n   is an isometric CPTP encoding obeying C and R, with $\\gamma_x=|A_x\\rangle\\langle A_x|$ and $G_{xy}=|A_x\\rangle\\langle A_y|$. It is affine on every input and remains a channel with any mathematical reference extension. It preserves the input sector populations and all input information jointly. Under O its target marginal is $\\mathcal D_E$, while its full output remains recoverable. This is an operator-channel construction, not an admitted quantum gravitational source or an arrow–CC–gravity coexistence realization.\n\n*Proof.* A Stinespring isometry $V:\\mathcal H_T\\to\\mathcal H_{T'}\\otimes\\mathcal H_G\\otimes\\mathcal H_E$ exists, with $\\Phi(Z)=\\operatorname{Tr}_E VZV^\\dagger$. For a unit vector $\\psi$ in sector $x$, C gives\n$$\n\\|(I-I\\otimes P_x\\otimes I)V\\psi\\|^2\n=\\operatorname{Tr}[(I-I\\otimes P_x)\\Phi(|\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi|)]=0.\n$$\nConsequently $(I\\otimes P_x\\otimes I)V\\Pi_x=V\\Pi_x$. Thus\n$\\Phi(\\Pi_xZ\\Pi_y)=(I\\otimes P_x)\\Phi(\\Pi_xZ\\Pi_y)(I\\otimes P_y)$.\nPartial-trace cyclicity for the bounded geometry projections inserts $P_yP_x$, which is zero under O. This proves item 1 on every cross block, including all degeneracies. All traced operators are trace-class, so no finite-dimensional assumption on the output or geometry has been used.\n\nFor item 2 put $v_z=V\\psi_z$. Trace-norm duality expresses the left-hand side of (O.6.5.3) as the supremum of\n$|\\langle v_y|(U\\otimes I_{GE})v_x\\rangle|$\nover target-output contractions $\\|U\\|\\le1$. The vectors $v_x,v_y$, regarded as purifications of $\\eta_x,\\eta_y$ on G, have Hilbert–Schmidt coefficient operators $A_x,A_y$ from the conjugate purifying space to G, with $A_zA_z^\\dagger=\\eta_z$. Polar decomposition gives $A_z=\\sqrt{\\eta_z}W_z$, with $\\|W_z\\|\\le1$. Acting on the purifying factor by a contraction changes its overlap to a trace of $\\sqrt{\\eta_y}\\sqrt{\\eta_x}$ times a contraction (the transpose on the conjugate space also preserves its norm). Schatten duality therefore bounds each overlap by $\\|\\sqrt{\\eta_y}\\sqrt{\\eta_x}\\|_1$, proving (O.6.5.3). Trace-class states and these Hilbert–Schmidt operators have separable supports, so the same polar-decomposition argument applies on arbitrary output Hilbert spaces. Under C,\n$$\nF(\\eta_x,\\eta_y)\n=\\|\\sqrt{\\eta_x}P_xP_y\\sqrt{\\eta_y}\\|_1\n\\le\\|\\sqrt{\\eta_x}\\|_2\\|P_xP_y\\|\\|\\sqrt{\\eta_y}\\|_2\n=\\|P_xP_y\\|.\n$$\n\nFor necessity in item 3, R makes the target marginal of the output of every pure in-sector input $\\psi$ equal to $|\\psi\\rangle\\langle\\psi|$. Hence its Stinespring vector factors as $V\\psi=\\psi\\otimes w(\\psi)$. Comparing this equation for independent vectors and their superposition inside a sector, and using linearity of V, gives a single unit vector $w_x$ per sector. The one-dimensional case has the same form directly. Therefore\n$G_{xy}=\\operatorname{Tr}_E|w_x\\rangle\\langle w_y|$.\nC places $w_x$ in $\\mathcal K_x\\otimes\\mathcal H_E$. The vector with components $w_x$ in $(\\bigoplus_x\\mathcal K_x)\\otimes\\mathcal H_E$ shows that its partially traced block matrix is positive, with the required diagonal.\n\nConversely choose an orthonormal target basis within each sector. In the Choi operator of (O.6.5.4), define $|\\Omega_x\\rangle=\\sum_{i\\in x}|i\\rangle_{\\rm in}|i\\rangle_T$. The Choi operator is\n\n$$\n\\sum_{x,y}|\\Omega_x\\rangle\\langle\\Omega_y|\\otimes G_{xy}.\n$$\n\nOn the orthonormal vectors $\\Omega_x/\\sqrt{d_x}$ its nonzero compression is the block matrix $[\\sqrt{d_xd_y}G_{xy}]$; it vanishes on the orthogonal complement. Congruence by the invertible finite diagonal matrix $\\operatorname{diag}(\\sqrt{d_x})$ proves complete positivity exactly when $[G_{xy}]\\ge0$. This finite-input Choi criterion also holds for trace-class output: a positive Choi operator has a spectral/Kraus decomposition, with convergent trace-class sums. A positive block operator with finitely many trace-class diagonals has trace equal to the sum of their traces, here $|X|$, and so is trace-class; block compressions and embeddings preserve trace-class membership. For target cross blocks the target trace is zero; for diagonal blocks $\\operatorname{Tr}\\gamma_x=1$. Thus the map is trace preserving and satisfies C and R.\n\nFor completeness, (O.6.5.5) needs no bounded inverse of $\\gamma_x$. Set $D=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sqrt{\\gamma_x})$ on the direct sum. Block positivity implies\n$$\n|\\langle u_x,G_{xy}u_y\\rangle|^2\n\\le\\langle u_x,\\gamma_xu_x\\rangle\\langle u_y,\\gamma_yu_y\\rangle,\n\\qquad\n0\\le G\\le |X|D^2.\n$$\nConsequently the positive form $q(Du,Dv)=\\langle u,Gv\\rangle$ is well-defined and bounded on $\\operatorname{ran}D$. It extends uniquely to its closure $S=\\bigoplus_xS_x\\mathcal K_x$, giving a bounded positive C with $G=DCD$. On each diagonal its form is the identity on $S_x\\mathcal K_x$; extend it by zero on $S^\\perp$. Two-block positivity gives $\\|C_{xy}\\|\\le1$. Conversely a positive C with those diagonal blocks gives positive G with diagonal $\\gamma_x$. The two-sector assertion is the equivalence between positivity of $\\left[\\begin{smallmatrix}I&C\\\\ C^\\dagger&I\\end{smallmatrix}\\right]$ and $\\|C\\|\\le1$ on the two support spaces, which follows by completing the quadratic square. Finally cyclicity for products of Hilbert–Schmidt operators gives\n$$\n|\\operatorname{Tr}(\\sqrt{\\gamma_x}C_{xy}\\sqrt{\\gamma_y})|\n\\le\\|\\sqrt{\\gamma_y}\\sqrt{\\gamma_x}\\|_1.\n$$\nThis proves (O.6.5.6). The dense ranges of the two square roots on their supports also show that $G_{xy}=0$ if and only if the canonical $C_{xy}=0$, proving the nonzero-response statement without asserting reversibility.\n\nFor item 4, apply $\\mathcal D_G$ to the supported cross block from the first paragraph of the proof. Every surviving basis label would have to belong to both $\\mathcal A_x$ and $\\mathcal A_y$, which is impossible. The dephasing sum converges in trace norm on trace-class operators; each positive diagonal output law has countable support. If $d_x=1$, every diagonal input block is its population times a fixed rank-one projector, giving measure-and-prepare. If $d_x>1$, no such scalar reduction is imposed.\n\nFor item 5, orthogonality of the target projections and normalization of each $A_x$ give $V^\\dagger V=\\sum_x\\Pi_x=I$, even without O. The assertions about support, retention, populations and references follow directly. On the encoded range, $V^\\dagger\\Phi_V(Z)V=Z$. A globally CPTP recovery map is\n$$\n\\mathcal R(Y)=V^\\dagger YV+\n\\operatorname{Tr}[(I-VV^\\dagger)Y]\\,\\tau,\n\\tag{O.6.5.9}\n$$\nwhere $\\tau$ is any fixed target density operator. Both summands are CP and their traces sum to $\\operatorname{Tr}Y$; the second vanishes on the encoded range. Under O, tracing G kills precisely the cross-sector terms. ∎\n\n**Source-contract distinction.** In the two-level case with distinct singleton geometry labels $A_g,A_e$, item 4 gives the affine law $(1-p_\\rho)\\delta_{A_g}+p_\\rho\\delta_{A_e}$. This is admissible as an abstract sector-support channel, but it is not the mean-source law $\\delta_{A_0-cp_\\rho}$ of (O.6.4.1). For $0<p_\\rho<1$ the two laws are different; substituting the first for the second changes the constitutive/preparation class rather than solving the full-source problem of Theorem O.6.4. Similarly, the mathematical encoding (O.6.5.8) supplies no covariant quantum-source action, admitted geometry algebra, clock/controller, nonlinear constraint evolution, or exhaustive energy-owner recomposition. Preservation of input energy populations alone proves no intervention contrast of (L.12.8b.6). Its reference extensions are mathematical tests, not unowned physical spectators, and its Stinespring proof does not authorize discarding any actual source owner. The deterministic-hybrid and identity-marginal obstructions of O.6.4 are unchanged.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o66\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o66-uniform-obstruction-to-deterministic-classical-geometry-records\"></a>**Theorem O.6.6 (Uniform Obstruction to Deterministic Classical Geometry Records).** Let $\\mathcal H_T$ be a finite-dimensional target Hilbert space and let $Y$ be the measurable space of a classical geometry record, with measurable singletons. Let $\\mathcal S$ be a set of target density operators. Suppose a deterministic hybrid prescription assigns the classical law $\\delta_{g(\\rho)}$ to each $\\rho\\in\\mathcal S$. The following bounds apply to every full-source CPTP map with this classical readout, including maps with additional retained quantum, apparatus and geometry outputs.\n\n1. If $g(\\mathcal S)$ is uncountable, then every such map has worst-case total variation error exactly one for this readout. In fact its error is one for every prescribed label outside a countable subset of $Y$.\n2. For any $n\\ge2$ states $\\rho_1,\\ldots,\\rho_n\\in\\mathcal S$ with distinct prescribed labels, put $\\bar\\rho=n^{-1}\\sum_i\\rho_i$. Their mean total variation error is at least\n$$\n\\max\\left\\{0,\\,1-\\frac1n-\\frac1n\\sum_iD(\\rho_i,\\bar\\rho)\\right\\},\n\\qquad D(\\rho,\\sigma)=\\frac12\\|\\rho-\\sigma\\|_1 .\n\\tag{O.6.6.1}\n$$\n3. At fixed resolution $\\delta>0$ in a metric record space $Y$, let $B_\\delta(y)$ be the closed metric ball around $y$. If $\\rho_1,\\ldots,\\rho_n$ have prescribed labels separated by more than $2\\delta$, the lower bound (O.6.6.1) also applies to the mean probability of returning a geometry readout outside $B_\\delta(g(\\rho_i))$.\n\n*Proof.* The classical marginal of a full-source channel defines a positive operator-valued measure $E$ on the input target, with $E(Y)=I$. All spectator systems and output degrees of freedom may be traced out for this necessary condition. Let $\\rho_* = I/\\dim\\mathcal H_T$ and $\\nu_*(B)=\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho_*E(B))$. If $\\nu_*(\\{y\\})=0$, then $E(\\{y\\})=0$ by positivity and faithfulness. A probability measure has at most countably many points of positive singleton mass, since for each integer $j$ only finitely many singleton masses can be at least $1/j$. Thus only countably many labels can receive positive singleton probability for any input. For every other prescribed label $y$, the channel's probability on $\\{y\\}$ is zero, while the deterministic law puts probability one there. This proves item 1.\n\nFor items 2 and 3 let $C_i$ be the singleton or the resolution ball at the prescribed label. The sets $C_i$ are disjoint, so $\\sum_iE(C_i)\\le I$. The trace-distance effect inequality gives\n$$\n\\frac1n\\sum_i\\operatorname{Tr}(\\rho_iE(C_i))\n\\le\n\\frac1n\\sum_i\\operatorname{Tr}(\\bar\\rho E(C_i))\n+\\frac1n\\sum_iD(\\rho_i,\\bar\\rho)\n\\le\\frac1n+\\frac1n\\sum_iD(\\rho_i,\\bar\\rho).\n$$\nFor singleton records the complement probability is precisely the total variation distance to the deterministic law. For balls it is the declared resolution failure probability. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o66a\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o66a-hybrid-acceleration-at-finite-resolution\"></a>**Corollary O.6.6a (Hybrid Acceleration at Finite Resolution).** For the constraint-restored deterministic hybrid source of Theorem O.6.4, restrict to diagonal target states $\\rho_t=(1-t)|g\\rangle\\langle g|+t|e\\rangle\\langle e|$, $0\\le t\\le1$. The prescribed acceleration law is $\\delta_{A_0-ct}$, where $c=4\\pi G/3>0$ in that theorem's units. Every common CPTP geometry readout has uniform total variation error one on this family. At a positive absolute acceleration resolution $\\delta$, for every integer $n\\ge2$ and every $\\eta\\in(0,1]$ with $c\\eta>2\\delta(n-1)$, its worst-case failure probability is at least\n$$\n\\max\\{0,\\,1-1/n-\\eta/2\\}.\n\\tag{O.6.6a.1}\n$$\nFor $0<\\delta<c/2$ there is also the sharper uniform lower bound\n$$\n\\sup_t\\Pr\\bigl(|Y-(A_0-ct)|>\\delta\\,\\big|\\,\\rho_t\\bigr)\n\\ \\ge\\ \\max\\left\\{0,1-\\frac{2\\delta}{c\\ln2}\\right\\}.\n\\tag{O.6.6a.2}\n$$\nThe uniform worst-case failure can be made at most $1-\\delta/c$ by a target-independent uniform readout on the full acceleration interval. The optimal uniform success therefore scales linearly in $\\delta/c$ as $\\delta\\downarrow0$.\n\n*Proof.* The acceleration ranges over a nondegenerate interval. For (O.6.6a.1) take $t_i$ equally spaced in $[0,\\eta]$, so the resolution balls are disjoint and $\\bar\\rho=\\rho_{\\eta/2}$. Direct diagonalization gives $D(\\rho_t,\\rho_{\\eta/2})=|t-\\eta/2|\\le\\eta/2$. Apply (O.6.6.1).\n\nFor the sharper bound change the readout coordinate to $z=(A_0-Y)/c$ and put $\\epsilon=\\delta/c$. The actual output law at $\\rho_t$ is $(1-t)\\nu_0+t\\nu_1$, with arbitrary probability measures $\\nu_0,\\nu_1$ determined by the common readout. To control the worst-case success, use the probability density\n$$\n\\pi(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\ln2\\,\\max\\{t,1-t\\}},\\qquad 0<t<1 .\n$$\nLet $s(t)=\\Pr(|z-t|\\le\\epsilon\\,|\\,\\rho_t)$. Integrating first in $t$ gives\n$$\n\\int_0^1\\pi(t)s(t)\\,dt\n=\\int\\nu_0(dz)\\int_{[0,1]\\cap[z-\\epsilon,z+\\epsilon]}\n       (1-t)\\pi(t)\\,dt\n+\\int\\nu_1(dz)\\int_{[0,1]\\cap[z-\\epsilon,z+\\epsilon]}\n       t\\pi(t)\\,dt .\n$$\nBoth $(1-t)\\pi(t)$ and $t\\pi(t)$ are at most $1/(2\\ln2)$ and the window length is at most $2\\epsilon$. Hence $\\inf_t s(t)\\le 2\\epsilon/\\ln2$. The target-independent uniform readout gives at least $\\epsilon$ success for every $t$, including the endpoints. In fact, allow the geometry estimate to take values in the $\\delta$-enlargement of the acceleration interval. A target-independent uniform law there gives constant success $2\\epsilon/(1+2\\epsilon)$ for all $t$, since every successful window then lies wholly in its support. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o67\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o67-geometry-information-and-reset-constraints-of-the-original-resonant-source\"></a>**Theorem O.6.7 (Geometry Information and Reset Constraints of the Original Resonant Source).** Use the input target and finite carrier of Theorem L.12.8b, with the independent non-target preparation fixed in a context \\(c\\in\\{0,1\\}\\). Let \\(\\theta=g\\tau_{\\rm int}\\), and retain its actual target channel \\(\\Phi_c\\) in (L.12.8b.5). Every compatible additional geometry channel is a postprocessing of an explicit amplitude-damping complementary channel. In particular there is a sharp contraction for geometry distinguishability. If the full original coherent carrier–target output is retained with its unchanged channel before readout, every additional geometry channel is constant on the target inputs. At the full-contrast value, exact return of all original quantum factors to their fixed ready states cannot preserve arbitrary target/reference data through classical records alone.\n\n*The original coherent source output.* Put \\(r=\\cos\\theta\\), \\(s=\\sin\\theta\\). Omit initially pure unchanged apparatus factors for a fixed context, and write \\(V_c:\\mathbb C^2\\to\\mathcal H_a\\otimes\\mathcal H_T\\) for the actual resonant evolution on the prepared carrier. In ordered carrier and target factors the two maps are\n$$\n\\begin{array}{ll}\nV_0|g\\rangle=|0,g\\rangle,\n&V_0|e\\rangle=r|0,e\\rangle-is|1,g\\rangle,\\\\\nV_1|g\\rangle=r|1,g\\rangle-is|0,e\\rangle,\n&V_1|e\\rangle=|1,e\\rangle .\n\\end{array}\n\\tag{O.6.7.1}\n$$\nBoth maps are isometries. The absorbed ready carrier, donor, fixed context and its retained ancillary states are the actual non-target preparation of the old source. Their inclusion at this cut does not change the isometric channel on the target. The reduced target output is \\(\\Phi_c(X)=\\operatorname{tr}_a(V_c X V_c^*)\\), with the fixed ground-ready CC contrast \\(s^2\\) of (L.12.8b.6).\n\n*All compatible additional outputs.* Let \\(\\mathcal C_c\\) be any CPTP map from the original target to the old target output and an additional retained system \\(G\\). The system \\(G\\) can include classical, quantum, source or geometry records. Assume the target marginal of \\(\\mathcal C_c\\) is exactly \\(\\Phi_c\\) on every input operator. There is a CPTP map \\(\\mathcal D_c\\) from the old carrier to \\(G\\) such that\n$$\n\\mathcal C_c(X)=(\\operatorname{id}_T\\otimes\\mathcal D_c)(V_c X V_c^*).\n\\tag{O.6.7.2}\n$$\nTensor order in this formula is taken with the old target first. Take a Stinespring isometry of \\(\\mathcal C_c\\), and regard it as a dilation of \\(\\Phi_c\\) with environment \\(G\\) and the remaining dilation factor. The minimal environment of \\(\\Phi_c\\) embeds isometrically into that environment, and also into the old carrier in (O.6.7.1). On that old subspace the embedding followed by the partial trace defines \\(\\mathcal D_c\\). Complete it on the orthogonal complement by any fixed-state trace-preserving branch. Conversely every such postprocessing gives a joint channel. For normal classical outputs apply the same argument to the output operator algebra in the Heisenberg picture. The joint Stinespring representation of the tensor product has the normal representation of the target matrix factor on one tensor component, with the output algebra of \\(G\\) represented on its commutant. The embedding into this representation therefore defines the unital completely positive adjoint of \\(\\mathcal D_c\\). In particular every classical geometry POVM is a postprocessing of the old carrier channel. The statement is on complete channels and holds after adjoining an arbitrary retained input reference. This is the complementary-channel compatibility criterion applied to the literal source (Heinosaari and Miyadera 2017).\n\n*Sharp two-state bounds.* For \\(c=0\\), write the matrix entries of \\(\\rho\\) in the ordered input \\(g,e\\) basis. The complementary carrier channel is\n$$\n\\Psi_0(\\rho)=\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n\\rho_{gg}+r^2\\rho_{ee}&is\\,\\rho_{ge}\\\\\n-is\\,\\rho_{eg}&s^2\\rho_{ee}\n\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\tag{O.6.7.3}\n$$\nFor \\(c=1\\) interchange the input ground/excited names and the carrier \\(0,1\\) names; the same contraction results. Thus the complement is amplitude damping with transmitted population \\(s^2\\), together with a fixed phase choice. For two target inputs with Bloch-vector difference \\(\\Delta=(\\Delta_x,\\Delta_y,\\Delta_z)\\), contractivity and (O.6.7.2) give\n$$\n\\frac12\\|\\mathcal C_{c,G}(\\rho)-\\mathcal C_{c,G}(\\sigma)\\|_1\n\\le\n\\frac12\\sqrt{s^2(\\Delta_x^2+\\Delta_y^2)+s^4\\Delta_z^2}.\n\\tag{O.6.7.4}\n$$\nFor a classical readout use total variation on the left. In the ground/excited population test the bound is \\(s^2\\), exactly the CC contrast. For opposite equatorial pure states it is \\(|s|\\). The bounds are sharp for the given pair under this marginal condition: leave the old carrier itself as \\(G\\) to attain trace distance, or apply its two-state Helstrom effect to attain total variation. In the population case the carrier's actual occupation instrument already attains the bound, with both outcomes. This statement is an extension criterion for the original response, not a claim that the attaining effect is produced by a gravitational action.\n\nIf the output geometry is only allowed to be an atomic record of the **input** energy populations, its best possible population distinction at fixed original target marginal is still \\(s^2\\). Indeed the population Helstrom measurement is the carrier occupation measurement above. In particular disjoint input-energy geometry supports are incompatible with unchanged \\(\\Phi_c\\) unless \\(s^2=1\\). This rules out an exact classical energy-sector geometry of the support type of Theorem O.6.5 on the whole non-full-contrast resonant branch, while allowing coarser stochastic readouts.\n\n*Full original coherent output.* If the required old marginal is the full isometric channel \\(X\\mapsto V_c X V_c^*\\), not just \\(\\Phi_c\\), apply (O.6.7.2) to that channel. Its minimal environment is one-dimensional. Thus any compatible joint channel has the exact form\n$$\nX\\longmapsto V_c X V_c^*\\otimes\\sigma_{G,c},\n\\tag{O.6.7.5}\n$$\nwith \\(\\sigma_{G,c}\\) independent of the target input. It may still depend on the separately fixed context or independent arrow source. No classical or quantum geometry response to the target can be appended while preserving that full old channel. This is a conclusion about the original coherent pre-readout cut. It is stronger than preservation of the old reduced target and is not inferred merely from the instruction to retain the outputs of a modified action.\n\n*Full contrast and the exact return cut.* At \\(s^2=1\\) the reduced target outputs are constant, respectively \\(|g\\rangle\\langle g|\\) and \\(|e\\rangle\\langle e|\\). The full old carrier therefore contains the whole unknown input, by the unitary one-qubit transfer in (O.6.7.1), with fixed context-dependent phases and a possible interchange of its basis. The original target occupation readout and its copied labels at this value can be deterministic at fixed context without erasing that carrier information. The return unitary (L.12.8b.8) is certified on the original ground-ready input. It cannot return the carrier, target and battery to their same fixed pure ready states for all target inputs by its original unitary and fixed records. For example, in the \\(c=0\\) context the original return is the identity on the ground-ready reachable state. Under the excited input the old carrier is occupied at the same target readout and the original return has no borrowed source energy to restore; its original specified orthogonal-complement action leaves that distinct state. A new unitary return with a fixed ready output on those factors must deposit the original quantum information in another retained factor.\n\nMore generally, suppose an arbitrary-input return at full contrast is required to preserve the complete unknown quantum information, including arbitrary input reference entanglement. If all original quantum output factors are fixed pure ready states and every remaining output is classical, the channel is entanglement-breaking: its joint output on any reference is a sum or integral of positive reference states tensored with orthogonal classical labels. No recovery from these outputs can reconstruct a maximally entangled target/reference pair. Thus there must be a nonclassical retained output with room for a two-dimensional transmitted quantum code. In a coherent isometric return this also follows directly from orthogonality of the two carrier images. If the additional quantum output carries a reversible copy of the target information, an independently accessible appended classical label need not be nontrivial; preserving the full isometric information at an unchanged specified marginal again imposes (O.6.7.5). An entropy or heat ledger for a nonreversible arbitrary-input return must include the actual new quantum environment and correlations. The original zero conditional reset entropy on its ground-ready classical ensemble does not supply that ledger for the enlarged inputs.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o68\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o68-arbitrary-input-cc-and-quantum-geometry-on-a-regulated-bianchi-i-action\"></a>**Theorem O.6.8 (Arbitrary-Input CC and Quantum Geometry on a Regulated Bianchi-I Action).** On the explicitly supplied dust-time, diagonal Bianchi-I reduction below, there is a finite joint Hermitian source for the original arbitrary target input of (L.12.8b.5). It realizes the same full-contrast target channel, the original two-step arrow law and a retained, target-dependent anisotropic geometry output. The carrier, target and actuation battery are returned to their fixed original ready states while the unknown input remains encoded in a paid quantum factor and the geometry. The full map is CPTP and valid with arbitrary reference extensions. This is a reduced-action Galerkin source with a declared relational clock and control supplier.\n\n*Action, physical clock and marked geometric coefficient.* Work in units \\(c=1\\), with physical time and energy kept in every quantum phase. Let \\(x^i\\) be marked periodic material coordinates of total coordinate volume \\(V_0>0\\). Use the diagonal homogeneous spatial metric\n$$\nds^2=-N^2d\\lambda^2+\\sum_i e^{2\\alpha+2\\beta_i}(dx^i)^2,\\qquad\n(\\beta_1,\\beta_2,\\beta_3)\n=(b+\\sqrt3\\,d,\\ b-\\sqrt3\\,d,\\ -2b).\n\\tag{O.6.8.1}\n$$\nThe marks fix the axis periods before the comparison. The lapse and the intrinsic spatial metric are varied in the reduced action. The Einstein–Hilbert action, with its time-end boundary term, has the gravitational Lagrangian\n$$\nL_g=\\frac{3V_0e^{3\\alpha}}{8\\pi G N}\n          (-\\dot\\alpha^2+\\dot b^2+\\dot d^2)\n       -\\frac{\\Lambda V_0}{8\\pi G}Ne^{3\\alpha}.\n\\tag{O.6.8.2}\n$$\nIndeed the intrinsic curvature of the marked flat torus is zero and the extrinsic-curvature combination is \\(K_{ij}K^{ij}-K^2=6(-\\dot\\alpha^2+\\dot b^2+\\dot d^2)/N^2\\). Let \\(T\\) be the homogeneous dust proper-time field, and take its future timelike branch with canonical term \\(p_T\\dot T-Np_T\\). For example this term follows by eliminating the homogeneous dust-density multiplier after varying the pressureless action \\(\\frac12\\int\\sqrt{-g}\\,\\varrho[-g^{\\mu\\nu}\\partial_\\mu T\\partial_\\nu T-1]\\), on the future \\(\\dot T/N=1\\) branch. The relational reduction is the homogeneous dust construction (Brown and Kuchař 1995; Husain and Pawłowski 2011), with the sign of its positive density fixed below.\n\nUse the material Hilbert space and ready preparations of Definition K.5.4a for the finite arrow and CC sectors, and add one resonant store \\(J\\), initially \\(|0\\rangle_J\\), of energy \\(\\epsilon=\\hbar\\omega\\) for one excitation. Give the store a metric-dependent energy term \\(\\kappa\\,\\chi(T)f(\\alpha,b,d)n_J\\). Here \\(\\kappa>0\\) has energy units, \\(n_J=|1\\rangle\\langle1|\\), \\(\\chi\\ge0\\) is a fixed smooth clock window with support after the quantum store transfer, and\n$$\nf(\\alpha,b,d)=\\exp[2(\\alpha+b+\\sqrt3\\,d)]\n\\tag{O.6.8.3}\n$$\nis the squared first-axis length divided by its marked coordinate period squared. This is a material strain coupling. The same scalar can be written \\(g_{\\mu\\nu}e_1^\\mu e_1^\\nu\\) using the retained first material tangent. It enters the worldline-frame energy integrated over the fixed normalized material number measure. Variation gives the metric-dependent material force. In particular the derivative with respect to \\(b\\) of this term is \\(2\\kappa\\chi f n_J\\), which is anisotropic and vanishes on the unoccupied store.\n\nThe complete reduced canonical constraint, with the actual native finite matrix words entered in the material energy, is\n$$\n\\mathcal C=p_T+\n\\frac{2\\pi G}{3V_0}e^{-3\\alpha}\n            (-p_\\alpha^2+p_b^2+p_d^2)\n+\\frac{\\Lambda V_0}{8\\pi G}e^{3\\alpha}\n+H_{\\rm mat}(T)+\\kappa\\chi(T)f\\,n_J=0 .\n\\tag{O.6.8.4}\n$$\nHere a finite material state may first be treated in the normalized complex variational form \\(i\\hbar\\langle z,\\dot z\\rangle-N\\langle z,H_{\\rm mat}z\\rangle\\), with all actual factor energies and controls; a state-independent ready baseline can be added as a separate fixed material stock. The canonical Hamiltonian appearing in (O.6.8.4) is subsequently quantized as an operator on the joint finite reserve. There is no equation that substitutes a target expectation for \\(n_J\\) in that quantization. Variation of \\(p_T\\) gives \\(\\dot T=N\\), so \\(T\\) is the proper-time coordinate of the comoving branch. The lapse equation fixes the dust momentum on the constraint. The clock is supplied on this reduced branch and \\(p_T\\) is a constrained output, not a second independently specified input. The \\(T\\)-dependent controller couplings contribute \\(-\\partial_T(H_{\\rm mat}+\\kappa\\chi f n_J)\\) to \\(\\dot p_T\\). Thus even a driven window has a named clock-work coordinate in the same action.\n\n*An explicit finite geometry reserve and a nonconstant result.* Fix a rectangular coordinate box in \\((\\alpha,b,d)\\) with positive side lengths. Use the first normalized Dirichlet sine function in \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(d\\), and the first two such functions in \\(b\\). These functions give two product states \\(\\phi_1,\\phi_2\\). Compress the symmetric ordered gravity quadratic form of (O.6.8.4) on that span, using \\(p_\\alpha e^{-3\\alpha}p_\\alpha\\) for the negative term, and \\(e^{-3\\alpha}p_b^2,e^{-3\\alpha}p_d^2\\) for the other terms. This is the declared finite Galerkin ordering. The result \\(H_g\\) is diagonal in \\(\\phi_1,\\phi_2\\), with distinct energies because of the strictly positive \\(e^{-3\\alpha}p_b^2\\) coefficient and the distinct two Dirichlet eigenvalues. The finite metric and strain functions are their compressed multiplication operators. The operator \\(F=P f P\\) is positive and \\(F_{21}\\ne0\\). In the \\(b\\)-integral, \\(\\sin(\\pi x/L_b)\\sin(2\\pi x/L_b)\\) has opposite signs on the two half intervals, and its reflection on the second half has the larger strictly increasing weight \\(e^{2b}\\). The \\(\\alpha,d\\) factors have strictly positive squared-mode integrals. This proves the matrix element assertion, including its sign in the fixed sine convention.\n\nTake the same ready geometry \\(\\phi_1\\) for every input target. After the material gates finish, use the fixed short-window profile \\(\\chi(T)=\\chi_0((T-T_0)/t)\\) with \\(\\chi_0\\ge0\\) smooth and supported in \\((0,1)\\), of positive integral \\(I_0\\). Conditional on \\(n_J=0\\) the geometry remains a phase times \\(\\phi_1\\). In the other branch the Dyson expansion in the fixed finite matrix norm gives\n$$\n\\Pr(\\phi_2\\mid n_J=1)\n=\\frac{\\kappa^2 I_0^2|F_{21}|^2}{\\hbar^2}t^2+O(t^3),\n\\qquad\n\\Pr(\\phi_2\\mid n_J=0)=0 .\n\\tag{O.6.8.5}\n$$\nAny earlier purely gravitational interval preserves the ready ray. The retained geometric observable can itself be a function of the anisotropic length or its conjugate momentum. For example the compressed \\(b\\) has nonzero off-diagonal element by the same reflection argument. Its commutator with \\(H_g\\) gives a nonzero geometric momentum quadrature, measurable by its complete two-leaf finite instrument. The selected mode projector in (O.6.8.5) is another finite observable of this same marked geometry reserve. At finite \\(t>0\\) sufficiently small the entire output geometry is distinguishable by a nominated such instrument. The full geometry is kept as a quantum output, with its instrument applied only in a separately labelled response test.\n\nOn this compact finite reserve the sign of the dust momentum can be kept positive over the registered window. In place of the first \\(\\alpha\\)-sine, use a higher but single fixed \\(\\alpha\\)-sine mode. Its negative gravitational momentum term in (O.6.8.4) has expectation tending to \\(-\\infty\\) with its index, while the bounded positive potential, retained \\(b,d\\) mode terms and finite matter/control bounds stay bounded. This follows from the strict positive lower bound of \\(e^{-3\\alpha}\\) on the box and the exact squared norm of the \\(\\alpha\\)-derivative of that sine. The negative contribution is common to the two \\(b\\) modes. The earlier off-diagonal sign proof still applies because the \\(\\alpha\\)-squared integral is positive. Choose that single mode before the input so the complete finite \\(H_g+H_{\\rm mat}+\\kappa\\chi F n_J\\) is negative definite uniformly throughout the compact clock schedule. The constrained \\(p_T=-H\\) is then positive on the whole reserve. The finite native provider can realize the same conjugation by adding a fixed positive scalar to the finite controlled generator, with its actual supplied baseline and known phase. Use the unshifted operator in the constraint bookkeeping. The signed gravitational Hamiltonian is a constraint term. No uncut infinite-volume clock or local gauge-constraint quantization is assumed.\n\n*Original arrow and resonant ports on the same action.* Use the original retained arrow preparation of Definition K.5.4a and its finite update word, with \\(p=4/9,q=1/9,r=4/9\\) and two steps. The arrow coins and their purifiers and all visited ring registers remain actual factors. Its material operations have identity on the CC, store and geometry factors. They are entered in \\(H_{\\rm mat}(T)\\) on their own fixed clock intervals, so their original arrow law and positive rate are unchanged. At the stated nonoverlapping CC intervals execute the original context copy, energy-conserving carrier preparation and full resonant interaction \\(\\theta=\\pi/2\\). The geometry coupling is then zero. The actual carrier–target state at this cut is (O.6.7.1), so the old target marginal, including arbitrary target-reference inputs, is exactly the prescribed full-contrast channel. For \\(c=0\\) the target output is \\(|g\\rangle\\), for \\(c=1\\) it is \\(|e\\rangle\\), and the logical input is transferred to the carrier with its known context-dependent phase convention.\n\nUse the original occupation and retained logging cuts. At full contrast every original readout bit there is deterministic conditional on the retained context, for every input. Transfer the carrier to the fresh resonant \\(J\\) by an energy-conserving swap before the old return gate. The original target and battery in the \\(c=1\\) context then have energies \\(\\epsilon\\) and \\(E-\\epsilon\\), and their original return swap restores \\(|g,E\\rangle\\). The \\(c=0\\) context needs no battery return, and the carrier is ready in both contexts. The expected input energy above the ground, \\(\\epsilon\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho\\,|e\\rangle\\langle e|)\\), is held by \\(J\\) at this cut on the original input receipt. The known phase map can be undone by a fixed context-conditioned diagonal energy-preserving phase gate. Use this phase correction before the strain window so the two input energy states have the same \\(n_J\\) labels in both contexts. This is possible because both maps at \\(\\theta=\\pi/2\\) transfer \\(|g\\rangle,|e\\rangle\\) to \\(|0\\rangle_J,|1\\rangle_J\\) with context-dependent diagonal phases. Before clearing the four bits copy their known values into four fresh degenerate archive cells; retain the context and all four original classical values in their archives. Clear each of \\(C,M,R_C,R_M\\) by its fixed context-controlled inverse write, since all four equal \\(c\\) on the actual law. The retained context, reset bath and reset-work register are unchanged, and the conditional bit reset entropy and heat are zero. Each copy and clear is a paid reversible permutation with positive clock duration and with its complete action on complementary inputs retained.\n\nAfter those cuts execute the strain window of (O.6.8.3) on the actual stored quantum state. Its propagator on geometry and \\(J\\) is diagonal in the two store projections with separate unitary geometry branches. It retains the original input information coherently. In particular the composed map from any target state to all retained quantum and recorded outputs is CPTP, and the same construction with identity on a reference preserves the input/reference extension. The original complete coherent carrier marginal is consumed by the paid transfer, as Theorem O.6.7 requires when creating a target-dependent geometry response. The old target marginal at the prescribed original cut and the full CC contrast are unchanged. The endpoint returns all original quantum CC factors requested above, while the additional store and geometry remain part of the source output.\n\n*Complete cuts and the boundary of the clock claim.* Use the same strict order of the original context, preparation, resonant interaction, readout and logging cuts, then the store transfer, original ready return, four context clears and strain coupling. Each cut is a specified interval of the same homogeneous dust proper time \\(T\\); a registered time-dependent material profile is a function of this clock in the same action. Keep the native finite clock and controller schedule, their actual work and constrained \\(p_T\\) observable, and the final geometry state. A \\(p_T\\) measurement, if asked, is a nominated complete instrument on the reduced source, not a second simultaneous copy of a quantum observable. All non-target *independent* inputs of the reduced map are fixed. If an independent sharp initial dust-energy or a second autonomous quantum clock marginal were required, that would be a further clock-source condition not implied here. The action reduction has one clock and two anisotropy degrees of freedom, and permits a genuinely target-dependent geometry operator. It is spatially homogeneous but anisotropic, with the nonlinear \\(e^{\\pm3\\alpha}\\) constraint coefficients. It therefore goes beyond the single isotropic background of Theorem O.6.4, without supplying a spatially inhomogeneous metric field.\n\nThere is no hidden measurement of the target excitation. For a fixed classical context every old full-contrast readout is constant on the arbitrary input and can be coherently copied without disturbing it. The quantum store, geometry, reference, all classical arrow/history and CC logs and the reset bath/work registers are retained. If a classical output is requested, the complete instrument keeps both outcome leaves and an owned Stinespring record. A material gate or an output measurement extended by the identity on an independent external factor leaves that factor's nonselective marginal exactly unchanged, since the adjoint sends identity to identity. The original local CC support order of (L.12.8b.12) can be kept within its declared material tube in the reduced model. The homogeneous geometry modes are global degrees of freedom; this finite reduction does not provide local metric instruments and a spacelike-factorization theorem for inhomogeneous geometries.\n\n*Finite source inventory and recomposition.* The finite operator carrier has the \\(3^8\\cdot2^{11}\\) original arrow, context, actuation, reset and bath factor, the two-state \\(J\\), the four binary archive cells and the two Galerkin geometry states. The original Gibbs, purifying, carrier, battery and history records remain present, including unused states. For a nominated geometry effect use its own paid pointer and durable record. Before comparing outputs the declared nongravitational constitutive family consists of the material number measure, the homogeneous clock-density action, the finite Hermitian matrix coefficient functions on the material factor (including \\(J\\) and the archives), and the explicitly marked strain insertion. The original auxiliary scalar of Definition K.5.4a is replaced by the specified dust clock. The matrix units form an exhaustive Hermitian basis for this finite class. For material dimension \\(D_{\\rm mat}\\), use the \\(D_{\\rm mat}\\) diagonal matrix units and the two real Hermitian combinations for each unordered off-diagonal pair. Their coefficients are the diagonal, real and imaginary matrix entries of the independently supplied \\(H_{\\rm mat}(T)\\) in that basis. The recomposition maps these entries to their sum integrated against the fixed material number measure and inserts the clock and strain terms (O.6.8.3)–(O.6.8.4) with their original labels. It returns exactly \\(H_{\\rm mat}(T)+\\kappa\\chi F n_J\\) in the retained finite action. The energy and metric derivative use those same owners. Additional species or off-shell couplings enlarge this source class.\n\nFor each finite material slot of active dimension \\(D_i\\) and duration \\(\\tau_i>0\\), use the actual drift and the Hermitian principal generator of the compensated named endpoint. Allocate \\(D_i^2\\) real coefficients, one positive finite control slot and its separate recorded pointer or output. The standard cubic positive finite control of Definition G.8.5j.7, with \\(2\\pi\\) scalar offset, has peak at most \\(9\\pi\\hbar/(2\\tau_i)\\), integrated norm at most \\(3\\pi\\hbar\\), and gross switching bound \\(9\\pi\\hbar/\\tau_i+6\\pi\\|H_{\\rm drift,i}\\|\\), from its derivative and the drift commutator. These reserves and durations are paid, even when an equal-energy gate has zero signed endpoint work. The drift and geometry windows are separately held. In the strain window the rate of signed supplied work is exactly \\(\\kappa\\chi'(T)\\operatorname{tr}[\\rho(T)(F\\otimes n_J)]\\), bounded in absolute integral by \\(\\kappa\\|F\\|\\int|\\chi'|\\); it is accounted once by the clock/controller supply, with the final geometric energy retained. Actuation and stored input energy have their own records. The reversible context clear leaves the reset bath exactly in its initially supplied Gibbs state and the reset work store unchanged. Its driven actuator is paid separately, and the conditional reset entropy and heat are zero on the actual deterministic context law. No modular equilibrium of the driven geometry or equality with a horizon bath is asserted. This is a finite action and receipt mechanism on the stated regulated carrier. \\(\\square\\)\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o69\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o69-paid-clock-and-physical-admission-of-the-finite-bianchi-i-source\"></a>**Theorem O.6.9 (Paid Clock and Physical Admission of the Finite Bianchi-I Source).** Use the reduced Einstein–Hilbert action, positive-density dust clock and finite metric reserve of Theorem O.6.8. The qualified ready-cell and finite Hermitian-control supplier of Theorem O.7.2.3m realizes its complete finite action on an actual scheduled carrier. The strain response below is an exact finite unitary, with its two switch-work records and the actual constrained dust momentum. It has a nonconstant geometry output for arbitrary target inputs and retains the original full-contrast CC response, arrow law and complete finite return and reset ledger.\n\n*Supplied parameters and exact finite action.* Choose finite positive computable units, box limits and classical source parameters. Fix the two geometry basis vectors \\(\\phi_1,\\phi_2\\) of Theorem O.6.8 and use the same marked shape function \\(f\\) and its compression \\(F\\). The native drift on this geometry factor is the finite diagonal \\(H_g\\) derived from the gravitational constraint. Use the original material matrix words of Theorem O.6.8, including its retained classical arrow and phase records, the same actual resonant store \\(J\\), the four copied and cleared bits and the fixed bath. Its finite ready registers have their original gaps; the ready-cell provider supplies positive independent baselines on its extra cells.\n\nChoose a fixed rectangular strain window after the material return and all four clears. It has coefficient \\(\\chi=1\\) in the open hold interval and zero before and after. The two discontinuities are scheduled controller operations. This is a piecewise smooth action service with exact quench work, as admitted in Theorem O.7.2.3m. In the constraint (O.6.8.4) its literal new operator is \\(\\kappa F\\otimes n_J\\), with \\(\\kappa>0\\). During this interval the store's occupation commutes with its native drift and the strain coupling. The two geometry Hamiltonians are \\(H_0=H_g\\) and \\(H_1=H_g+\\kappa F\\). Any simultaneously held constant material or controller energy acts on its own factor and retains its ordinary phase. The strain coupling itself is one owned interaction between \\(J\\) and the already installed metric factor. The finite mode observable is read on that factor.\n\nFor a direct exact calculation write \\(H_g=\\operatorname{diag}(h_1,h_2)\\), \\(b=\\kappa F_{21}\\) and \\(d=(h_1-h_2+\\kappa(F_{11}-F_{22}))/2\\). Set \\(r=(d^2+|b|^2)^{1/2}>0\\). From \\(\\phi_1\\) the probability of finding \\(\\phi_2\\) is\n$$\nP_0=0,\\qquad P_1=\\frac{|b|^2}{r^2}\\sin^2\\left(\\frac{rt}{\\hbar}\\right).\n\\tag{O.6.9.1}\n$$\nSubtract the mean of the two diagonal entries of \\(H_1\\); the remaining Hermitian matrix squares to \\(r^2 I\\), which proves the formula. Fix the hold duration \\(t=\\pi\\hbar/(2r)\\) after the source coefficients are fixed and before the input. Its positivity and computability follow from \\(F_{21}\\ne0\\). Choosing any fixed computable \\(0<t<\\pi\\hbar/r\\) also works. The geometry in the occupied branch has a nonzero transition, and the unoccupied branch stays in its original mode. Both output vectors, including their full relative phase and the coherent store correlation, are kept. The strain window is the literal common constrained Hamiltonian on the stored target and metric. It is neither a conditional preparation of a classical geometry label nor a second metric simulation.\n\n*Native clock and chronological output.* Nominate a finite chronological word with the two original arrow updates, all original CC preparation and contrast cuts, store transfer and phase correction, context energy return, four archival copies and four reversible clears, the fixed strain hold, and its final cut. Include the original readout pointer dilation and the two arrow record cuts as actual finite operations with all their output factors. The optional two-outcome \\(\\phi_2\\) test is a further finite word after the full-source cut, with both outcomes and its complete pointer dilation. It is not required for retaining the geometry. Positive slot lengths for the ancillary gates are chosen before the run. The original resonant interval is \\(\\pi/(2g)\\); the arrow intervals keep their own registered \\(\\tau_{\\rm arr}\\). Insert the controller subintervals inside fixed common slots, or include them in the registered lengths when fixing the source schedule. This does not change \\(g\\tau_{\\rm int}=\\pi/2\\), the number of arrow steps or any readout or return order. In particular the strain hold starts only after the actual store and reset completion.\n\nUse the addressed one-hot cursor and native calibrated scheduler of Theorem O.7.2.3m. Archive the completion of each primary data, hold and readout event on its own fresh bit. Include these archive writes once in the list of \\(E\\) events. Allocate \\(E+1\\) cursor payloads and use \\(2E\\) positive-time cursor updates, with the original two flips per event. The actual retained host forecast from the calibration in that theorem controls a permutation of two physical archive addresses. The corresponding inverse address map selects the same logical clock label. Both addresses, the forecast and its controller records stay in the source, and the physical address used depends on the host outcome. The clock marks and ready arms are fixed independently of the target. Hold the rest of the physical system on its named native drift during every controller slot. Those intervals have the same dust time because the single constraint yields \\(\\dot T=N\\). On the selected gauge \\(T\\) increases by the supplier's actual calibrated duration at each interval. Thus the relational time comparison is between measured native elapsed time and the proper time of this action on that very schedule. The hardware includes the finite cursor, positive holds, ready controller and fixed marks. The constrained \\(-H(T)\\) is represented on the retained reduced system. Its measurement, if nominated, is a distinct paid output instrument.\n\n*Physical supports and source bill.* Encode the original finite material factors and the two-state geometry in named payload subspaces of the qualified ready cells of Theorem C.6c.P17a as in Theorem O.7.2.3m. Use enough payloads to embed each full finite Hilbert factor, with unused binary labels and all cell cores retained. Supply each factor's actual finite drift by its separate positive baseline and its declared matrix interaction. On the geometry factor the selected signed \\(H_g\\) term is represented by adding a fixed positive scalar to the physical generator with the corresponding common phase and paid energy baseline. Do not add that bookkeeping scalar to the unshifted gravitational constraint twice. Keep any truly added positive material baseline in \\(H_{\\rm mat}\\). For the named control slots, a joint finite prescribed interaction including the existing drift gives the actual reduced Hamiltonian; an independent second arbitrary operator with the same endpoint is not used in its gravitational variation. The strain hold has \\(\\kappa F\\otimes n_J\\) as its actual operator contribution. All other concurrent controls, when present, act on the disjoint scheduled work or cursor factors.\n\nThe full source descriptor consists of the fixed preparation matrices (including the original arrow probabilities \\(4/9,1/9,4/9\\)), the mode integrals of (O.6.8.2)–(O.6.8.3), the native endpoint matrices, positive slot lengths, clock marks, addressed support and outcome-copy maps. All coefficients are computable: the finite integrals are on bounded boxes with known smooth integrands and computable bounds, and the original finite phase gates and square-root probabilities have finite computable descriptions. The native source allows their exact mathematical specifications and certified finite physical coefficient precisions. For prescribed precision \\(2^{-n}\\), serialize separately each full ordered matrix entry, its dyadic precision, and the fixed finite recipe and addresses for generating it. Let \\(B_{\\rm data}\\) be this literal finite payload length including all logical system, controller and ready archives. Use the immutable instruction and address length of Theorem O.7.2.3m with the \\(3E\\) events, and one manifest of aliases so each payload is written once. In particular \\(E+1\\) one-hot locations, all fresh archive bits, and each \\(D_i^2\\) Hermitian real coefficient coordinates of a \\(D_i\\)-dimensional matrix profile have separate physical holdings. Allocate \\(N_{\\rm cell}=\\max(2,B_{\\rm data}+B_{\\rm control})\\) cells including their \\(75N_{\\rm cell}\\) full bit inventory, the supplier-duty cells, and the original ready/qualification, ingress and work records. The independent host qualification is that of Theorem O.7.2.3m. Its calibration is a prior task on the same installed provider, with its own fixed custody and no target-dependent commands.\n\nAt each native pulse of duration \\(\\tau_i\\) reserve its actual finite drift, controls, control capacity and elapsed exposure. With a compensated principal logarithm \\(K_i\\) including the positive \\(2\\pi\\) offset, use the peak, action and gross switching allocations from Theorem O.6.8. A clock or readout event has the actual supplier duration and its own support. Use the source's finite rated load \\(e_*/\\tau_0+\\|V_i\\|/\\tau_0+e_*/\\tau_i\\) for each emitted interaction \\(V_i\\), with \\(e_*=\\hbar/\\tau_0\\) and the fixed physical second \\(\\tau_0\\) as in Theorem O.7.2.3m. Reserve its duty-cell maximum and retained factory/switch schedule by that theorem. An interaction with many addressed factors is still one actual owned \\(V_i\\); its full support is recorded. The original \\(J\\) gap, actuation battery, bath, retained store energy, neutral pointer supplies and scalar offsets are separate energy stocks. The active and idle power integrals, actual controller calls, pure ready-cell excess and finite ROM preparation are the charged rows of the native provider. Every clock-control factor belongs to the material inventory of this action with its stated baseline and drive. In particular the new degree-of-freedom count and work stock are chosen before imposing the dust positivity margin.\n\nThe source sizing and the positive dust margin can be chosen noncircularly. Let the single \\(\\alpha\\)-sine index be \\(j\\). The negative \\(p_\\alpha\\) contribution is exactly scalar on the two geometry modes, with magnitude bounded below by a fixed positive multiple of \\(j^2\\). The other \\(\\alpha\\)-dependent multipliers and all differences of the two gravitational eigenvalues have uniform finite upper bounds on the fixed coordinate box. Moreover the positive \\(\\alpha\\)-averages in \\(F_{21}\\) are bounded away from zero. Thus the strain window, its non-scalar matrix, and the control norms for the material word have bounds independent of \\(j\\). For this sizing use the carrier generator \\(H_g\\) with its negative common scalar subtracted and keep the resulting known scalar phase. A scalar change of the generator represents the same conjugation on all quantum outputs and reference extensions. Keep this single scalar difference as a specified part of the clock calibration, with its value recoverable from \\(j\\) and the finite action. It is an energy-zero convention for the controller Hamiltonian, not an added material load in the unshifted constraint. No state-dependent shift is used.\n\nFix all other material gate data and pulse bounds, including the paid clocks and supplier duty count, with these uniform norm envelopes. A fixed-precision description of the two non-scalar finite matrices uses fixed-length padded value fields within their uniform bounds. The extra length of the positive integer \\(j\\) and its scalar-phase description is \\(O(1+\\log j)\\), or can be fixed by choosing the index from a bounded interval and using its fixed bit width. The recipe for the phase is its literal finite integral formula, and no real value is stored as an infinite bit string. Any extra physical cells for this descriptor have a fixed per-cell baseline. Thus the newly added material baseline grows at most logarithmically in \\(j\\), with all other material/control envelopes uniformly bounded. Choose \\(j\\) large enough that the complete unshifted finite constraint Hamiltonian, including these material rows, lies below a strictly negative number throughout the schedule. Then the constrained observable \\(p_T=-H\\) is positive on the full retained input range. This is a single fixed source choice before the target input. The independent clock readiness supplies no target-dependent positive-density preparation.\n\n*Work, precision and the admitted channel.* At the two strain cuts \\(T_0,T_1\\), the signed supplied work of the action's coefficient change is exactly\n$$\nW_{\\rm on}=\\kappa\\operatorname{Tr}[\\rho(T_0)(F\\otimes n_J)],\n\\qquad\nW_{\\rm off}=-\\kappa\\operatorname{Tr}[\\rho(T_1)(F\\otimes n_J)].\n\\tag{O.6.9.2}\n$$\nIt is bounded in gross magnitude by \\(2\\kappa\\|F\\|\\). During the constant window the finite constrained matter-plus-geometry Hamiltonian is conserved under its own unitary. The reduced action gives the opposite changes of \\(p_T\\) at the two switches. These are two entries of the same clock/controller energy transfer, and one does not charge both as incoming work. Held positive control, battery charges, the measured returned energy, and any thermal heat are distinct records. The reversible context clear leaves the actual bath and work register in their ready states. The clock-control supplier still owns the positive duration, work reserve and holding of those clears. They have no extra thermodynamic reset heat on the actual deterministic support.\n\nFor implemented coefficient errors choose fixed bounds \\(\\int_{I_i}\\|\\widetilde H_i-H_i\\|\\,dT\\le\\hbar\\epsilon_i\\) on the actual finite supports, using the declared matrix precision and measured interval accuracy. Variation of constants and a telescoping product give \\(\\|\\widetilde U-U\\|\\le\\sum_i\\epsilon_i\\), and the corresponding whole-source output trace error, including any reference, is at most \\(2\\sum_i\\epsilon_i\\). Norm limits retain the same complete ideal finite channel. The arrow, full-contrast CC and both strain probabilities have these same controlled errors in the precision realization, and the strain contrast stays positive at sufficiently small fixed tolerance because (O.6.9.1) is strictly positive. Exact native controls of the admitted finite branch give the exact target marginal, arrow law and reversible reset stated in Theorem O.6.8.\n\nAll outcomes may be read by complete instruments with their named environments and record factors retained. On a fixed input context the full experiment is a preparation independent of the target, followed by the displayed finite unitary or complete instrument word. It is CPTP on all target operators with reference extensions. The marked geometry, retained store, old context and histories, actual returned factors, bath, work/energy and clock outputs occur on this same action and source. The nongravitational recomposition of Theorem O.6.8 extends by the controller, duty and clock matrix units and the displayed pulse profiles with their actual owners. It is exhaustive on this declared finite native source. The finite-source realization uses an external qualified clock and the reduced, anisotropic homogeneous sector. The continuum local-net and autonomous-clock predicates of other owners are separate. \\(\\square\\)\n\n## O.7 Mathematical Emergence of the Lorentzian Signature\n\nTheorems O.3 and O.3a establish a time orientation on histories carrying their pathwise entropy-production certificates. Theorem O.7a derives Lorentzian signature on the separate branch carrying a rank-four positive Euclidean continuum tensor, a nonvanishing entropy-selected time covector whose kernel supplies the three-dimensional spatial subspace, a second-order continuum principal symbol, and a nondegenerate characteristic cone coinciding with an attained operational frontier.\n\n### <a id=\"o71-γ-convergence-of-the-spatial-sector\"></a>O.7.1 Γ‑convergence of the Spatial Sector\n\nConsider a sequence of locally-finite graphs $(G_n)$ with vertex sets $V(G_n)$ and mesh size $h_n\\to 0$ approximating a spatial slice $(M,g)$ of dimension $D$. For a discrete test field $u_n:V(G_n)\\to\\mathbb R$, a representative discrete spatial functional takes the form\n$$\nF_n(u_n)=\\sum_{(x,y)\\in E(G_n)} w_{xy}\\,\\Phi_{link}\\!\\left(\\frac{u_n(y)-u_n(x)}{h_n}\\right)+\\sum_{x\\in V(G_n)} h_n^D\\,\\mathcal{V}(u_n(x)),\n$$\nwhere $\\Phi_{link}$ is convex and encodes the spatial propagation cost density consistent with $V_{\\mathrm{prop}}$ (Definition D.1), and $\\mathcal{V}$ is the induced local term. Assume separate sequential $\\Gamma$-liminf and recovery certificates identify the continuum functional as\n$$\nF(u)=\\int_M f(x,\\nabla u)\\,d^Dx+\\int_M \\mathcal{V}(u)\\,d^Dx,\n\\qquad\nf(x,\\xi)=\\xi^T A(x)\\xi+o(|\\xi|^2),\n$$\nwith an independently certified symmetric positive-definite coefficient $A(x)$. Under equicoercivity and the remaining hypotheses of Appendix D, Theorem D.6, minimum values converge and cluster points of almost minimizers minimize $F$. The integral representation and positivity of $A$ are additional branch data. Register the spatial metric-density identification\n$$\nA(x)\\propto \\sqrt{\\det g_{ij}(x)}\\,g^{ij}(x).\n$$\nOn this identified positive-quadratic branch, the spatial geometry is represented by the effective continuum limit of the PU network's propagation sector.\n\n### O.7.2 Hyperbolic Signature Closure and Operational Speed\n\nThe Appendix O signature closure is a four-input branch theorem: a rank-four positive-definite continuum tensor, a nonvanishing time covector whose orientation is supplied by Theorem O.3's pathwise certificate and Hypothesis O.7.2.2, a second-order continuum principal symbol, and a separately accepted nondegenerate characteristic cone coinciding with an attained operational frontier. Proposition F.1 supplies its stated discrete propagation cone and Theorem 46 supplies a uniform operational causal-speed upper bound; neither establishes frontier attainment or continuum cone coincidence. The fourth input may be supplied directly as Hypothesis O.7.2.4 or, for the retained sector family covered by a finite record, by an accepted cone-saturation certificate $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{cone}}$ (Definition O.7.2.5) that explicitly includes attainment. On a branch combining Theorem Z.11's four-dimensional Euclidean carrier with a rank-four continuum realization and a separately accepted nonvanishing temporal covector, rank-nullity supplies the three-dimensional spatial kernel. These inputs then force the Lorentzian signature and normalize the characteristic speed to the separately accepted frontier.\n\n<a id=\"remark-o720-second-order-closure-versus-signature-selection\"></a>**Remark O.7.2.0 (Second-Order Closure versus Signature Selection).** When the second-order and cone-coincidence inputs are supplied by finite certificates rather than by the direct hypotheses, the covered-sector signature reading is audited by the predictive well-posedness signature certificate $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{sig}}$ of Definition 46a.1. The certificate excludes elliptic, ultrahyperbolic, constrained, higher-derivative, or nonlocal representatives that reproduce only a coarse finite-frontier behavior. Thus the finite-certificate implication used for covered sectors is\n$$\n\\mathfrak C_2\\wedge \\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{cone}}\\wedge \\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{sig}}\n\\Longrightarrow\n\\text{one-time Lorentzian branch on the tested sector}.\n$$\nThe direct Hypotheses O.7.2.1--O.7.2.4 remain the theorem-level route of Theorem O.7a.\n\n<a id=\"hypothesis-o721-rank-four-positive-continuum-tensor\"></a>**Hypothesis O.7.2.1 (Rank-Four Positive Continuum Tensor).** Assume the promoted continuum branch has a real rank-four tangent bundle on $M_{\\mathrm{reg}}$ and carries the symmetric positive-definite Cheeger tensor $g^E$ of Theorem 45.\n\n<a id=\"hypothesis-o722-entropy-selected-time-coordinate-spatial-kernel-and-propagation-identification\"></a>**Hypothesis O.7.2.2 (Entropy-Selected Time Coordinate, Spatial Kernel, and Propagation Identification).** Assume that $M_{\\mathrm{reg}}$ carries a distinguished local time coordinate $t$ with $dt_x\\ne0$ at every point and that retained histories carry Theorem O.3's independent pathwise certificate, with the future direction defined by increasing cumulative certified entropy production. Define\n$$\nS_x:=\\ker dt_x.\n$$\nThen $\\dim S_x=3$ by rank-nullity, and $g^E|_{S_x}$ is positive definite. Let $A_\\Gamma^{ij}$ be the spatial propagation coefficient produced by the $\\Gamma$-limit of §O.7.1. Require a registered common spatial frame and a specified positive density $\\rho(x)>0$ such that\n$$\nA_\\Gamma^{ij}(x)\n=\n\\rho(x)\\bigl(g^E|_{S_x}\\bigr)^{-1,ij}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.2a}\n$$\nWrite this registered coefficient as $A^{ij}$ in Hypotheses O.7.2.3--O.7.2.4. A fixed-ready-state reset and Theorem 31 may motivate the time orientation but do not construct the coordinate, discharge the pathwise certificate, or prove (O.7.2.2a); SPAP alone supplies none of them.\n\n<a id=\"hypothesis-o723-second-order-continuum-closure\"></a>**Hypothesis O.7.2.3 (Second-order continuum closure).** Any local second-order continuum closure for a scalar probe field compatible with the quadratic limit on $M_{\\mathrm{reg}}$ has principal symbol\n$$\np_x(\\xi) \\;=\\; G^{\\mu\\nu}(x)\\,\\xi_\\mu\\xi_\\nu \\;=\\; a(x)\\,\\xi_0^2 + 2\\,b^i(x)\\,\\xi_0\\xi_i + A^{ij}(x)\\,\\xi_i\\xi_j,\n$$\nwith $\\xi_0,\\xi_i$ the cotangent components in the chart of Hypothesis O.7.2.2. This second-order continuum premise is imported by Corollary 46a in §11.6.\n\n<a id=\"definition-o723a-second-order-positivity-certificate-mathfrak-c_2\"></a>**Definition O.7.2.3a (Second-Order Positivity Certificate $\\mathfrak C_2$).** A second-order positivity certificate for a retained continuum sector is a finite record\n$$\n\\mathfrak C_2\n=\n(\\mathcal K_{\\mathrm{CG}},\\;\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{Markov}},\\;\\mathcal R_{\\mathrm{KM}},\\;\\mathcal R_{\\mathrm{GKSL}},\\;\\mathcal N_{\\mathrm{jump}},\\;\\mathcal R_\\delta,\\;\\text{sector list},\\;\\text{forward lock})\n$$\nwhere $\\mathcal K_{\\mathrm{CG}}$ is the coarse-grained CP influence kernel, $\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{Markov}}$ records the Markov diffusion reduction on the diagonal probability sector, $\\mathcal R_{\\mathrm{KM}}$ records the Kramers-Moyal expansion with finite second moments and locality/tightness bounds, $\\mathcal R_{\\mathrm{GKSL}}$ records the quantum CP-to-GKSL or quantum-Fokker-Planck reduction used for off-diagonal sectors, $\\mathcal N_{\\mathrm{jump}}$ certifies that retained jump, nonlocal, fractional, or infinite-order pseudodifferential sectors are absent from the claimed local field, and $\\mathcal R_\\delta$ certifies that all discarded higher-order response data are below the operational resolution and PPI-null.\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o723b-certificate-discharge-of-second-order-closure\"></a>**Proposition O.7.2.3b (Certificate Discharge of Second-Order Closure).** An accepted $\\mathfrak C_2$ supplies differential order at most two only on its recorded reduced sectors and operational quotient. To discharge Hypothesis O.7.2.3 for a scalar propagating sector, additionally require a real local scalar differential representative with a nonzero quadratic principal part and an explicit identification of its spatial principal coefficient with $A^{ij}$ from Hypothesis O.7.2.2. Complete positivity alone supplies none of these identifications. Hyperbolicity and the two-root cone condition remain the separate Hypothesis O.7.2.4.\n\n*Proof.* On the recorded Markov/Kramers-Moyal branch, the applicable positivity theorem restricts an admitted finite truncation to order at most two unless the full infinite series is retained. The quantum reduction has only the order and domain supplied by its separate GKSL/Fokker-Planck record. The no-jump and no-nonlocal entries exclude those retained alternatives, and $\\mathcal R_\\delta$ identifies discarded response terms only modulo its declared operational quotient. With the additional real scalar representative and nonzero quadratic part, symmetrizing its quadratic coefficient gives a principal symbol $G^{\\mu\\nu}\\xi_\\mu\\xi_\\nu$. The supplied spatial-coefficient identification then writes it in the form of Hypothesis O.7.2.3. This argument does not establish the sign, nondegeneracy or attained frontier of that symbol; those remain separate branch premises. ∎\n\n**Definition O.7.2.3c (Mixing Gaussian-Scaling Audit).** Augment an accepted second-order certificate by a centered stationary increment process, diffusive rescaling, the moment and strong-mixing summability required by named CLT/Berry-Esseen results, and constants $c_2>0$, $C_m<\\infty$ satisfying\n$$\na_2(v,v)\\ge c_2\\delta^2|v|^2,\n\\qquad \\|a_m\\|\\le C_m\\delta^m\\quad(m\\ge3).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3c.1}\n$$\n\n**Proposition O.7.2.3d (Higher-Order Symbol Bound at Observed Wavenumbers).** Under Definition O.7.2.3c, for every $m\\ge3$ and\n$$\n0<|k|\\le L_{\\mathrm{obs}}^{-1},\n$$\none has\n$$\n\\frac{|a_m[k^{\\otimes m}]|}{a_2[k,k]}\n\\le\\frac{C_m}{c_2}\n\\left(\\frac{\\delta}{L_{\\mathrm{obs}}}\\right)^{m-2}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3d.1}\n$$\nThis is a deterministic higher-order-symbol estimate. A Gaussian convergence rate additionally requires a named central-limit or Berry--Esseen theorem whose moment and mixing hypotheses are all verified.\n\n*Proof.* The operator-norm bound in Definition O.7.2.3c gives\n$$\n|a_m[k^{\\otimes m}]|\n\\le\\lVert a_m\\rVert |k|^m\n\\le C_m\\delta^m|k|^m.\n$$\nThe ellipticity bound gives\n$$\na_2[k,k]\\ge c_2\\delta^2|k|^2>0.\n$$\nDivision is therefore valid and yields\n$$\n\\frac{|a_m[k^{\\otimes m}]|}{a_2[k,k]}\n\\le\n\\frac{C_m}{c_2}(\\delta|k|)^{m-2}\n\\le\n\\frac{C_m}{c_2}\n\\left(\\frac{\\delta}{L_{\\mathrm{obs}}}\\right)^{m-2},\n$$\nwhere the last inequality uses $m\\ge3$ and $|k|\\le L_{\\mathrm{obs}}^{-1}$. No probability limit follows from these symbol inequalities alone. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723e-lévy-class-trichotomy-and-quantitative-diffusive-residual\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3e (Lévy-Class Trichotomy and Quantitative Diffusive Residual).** Let the retained limit be a conservative Lévy process $X_t$ on $\\mathbb R^d$, $d\\ge1$, with $X_0=0$, stochastic continuity and stationary independent increments. Its time-homogeneous spatial convolution semigroup has\n$$\n\\mathbb E e^{ik\\cdot X_t}=e^{t\\Psi(k)},\n\\qquad\n\\Psi(k)\n=i b\\cdot k-\\frac12 k^TAk\n+\\int_{\\mathbb R^d\\setminus\\{0\\}}\n\\left(e^{ik\\cdot z}-1-ik\\cdot z\\,\\mathbf1_{|z|\\le1}\\right)\\nu(dz),\n\\tag{O.7.2.3e.1}\n$$\nwhere $A\\succeq0$ and $\\int(1\\wedge|z|^2)\\nu(dz)<\\infty$. For the displayed truncation convention the triplet is unique. The process has continuous Gaussian-plus-drift paths exactly when $\\nu=0$; $\\nu\\ne0$ retains a jump or nonlocal part. The isotropic symmetric fractional branch has $b=0$, $A=0$ and\n$$\n\\nu(dz)=c_{d,\\alpha}|z|^{-d-\\alpha}\\,dz,\\qquad\nc_{d,\\alpha}>0,\\quad 0<\\alpha<2,\n$$\ngiving $\\Psi(k)=-C_{d,\\alpha}|k|^\\alpha$ with $C_{d,\\alpha}>0$. A conservative translation-invariant Markov generator on this class, if it is a finite-order local differential operator, has order at most two.\n\nFor the quantitative diffusive subclass, let $Y_j$ be iid centered increments with covariance $\\Sigma\\succ0$ and $\\mathbb E|Y_1|^{2+\\delta}=M_{2+\\delta}<\\infty$ for $0<\\delta\\le1$. For integers $n\\ge1$ put\n$$\nX_n(t):=n^{-1/2}\\sum_{j=1}^{\\lfloor nt\\rfloor}Y_j.\n$$\nFor every finite $T>0$, $X_n$ converges in $D([0,T],\\mathbb R^d)$ to Brownian motion with covariance $\\Sigma$. For every $\\epsilon>0$,\n$$\nn\\,\\mathbb E\\!\\left[\n\\left|\\frac{Y_1}{\\sqrt n}\\right|^2\n\\mathbf1_{|Y_1|>\\epsilon\\sqrt n}\n\\right]\n\\le\n\\frac{M_{2+\\delta}}{\\epsilon^\\delta n^{\\delta/2}}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3e.2}\n$$\nIts increment sequence has zero strong-mixing coefficients at positive lags. If $\\mathbb E|Y_1|^3<\\infty$, every unit projection obeys\n$$\n\\sup_x\\left|\n\\Pr\\{u\\cdot X_n(1)\\le x\\}\n-\\Phi_{u^T\\Sigma u}(x)\n\\right|\n\\le\n\\frac{C_{\\mathrm{BE}}\\,\\mathbb E|u\\cdot Y_1|^3}\n{(u^T\\Sigma u)^{3/2}\\sqrt n}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3e.3}\n$$\nWhenever cumulants through order $m$ exist, $\\kappa_m(X_n(1))=n^{1-m/2}\\kappa_m(Y_1)$, which vanishes for $m\\ge3$.\n\n*Proof.* The Lévy--Khintchine theorem applies to the stated Lévy process and gives the exponent and triplet uniqueness. If $\\nu\\ne0$, choose a nonnegative smooth compactly supported function $f$ that vanishes near zero and satisfies $\\int f(z)\\nu(dz)>0$. Such a function exists because a nonzero Lévy measure has positive finite mass on some compact set away from zero. Its jump-generator contribution at zero is positive, whereas all derivatives and the value of $f$ vanish there. Thus this generator is not a local differential operator. If $\\nu=0$, only the first- and second-order drift and Gaussian terms remain. For the symmetric stable measure, the imaginary integral vanishes; rotation and scaling give\n$$\n-\\Psi(k)=|k|^\\alpha\n\\int_{\\mathbb R^d}(1-\\cos z_1)c_{d,\\alpha}|z|^{-d-\\alpha}\\,dz.\n$$\nThe integral is finite and positive for $0<\\alpha<2$.\n\nOn the tail event in (O.7.2.3e.2), $|Y_1|^2\\le |Y_1|^{2+\\delta}/(\\epsilon\\sqrt n)^\\delta$, proving the bound. The stated iid finite-variance hypotheses permit the functional central-limit theorem; the finite third moment permits the one-dimensional Berry--Esseen theorem for each projection. Independence makes positive-lag mixing coefficients zero. Additivity and homogeneity of cumulants give $n(n^{-1/2})^m\\kappa_m(Y_1)$, proving the final formula. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723f-stationary-gaussian-scaling-classification-and-mixture-nonentailment\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3f (Stationary Gaussian Scaling Classification and Mixture Nonentailment).** Let $(Y_j)_{j\\ge1}$ be a real centered strictly stationary Gaussian sequence, $S_0:=0$, $S_n:=\\sum_{j=1}^nY_j$ and $V(n):=\\operatorname{Var}S_n$. Assume $V(n)>0$ for $n\\ge1$ and, for some $H>0$,\n$$\n\\frac{V(\\lfloor nt\\rfloor)}{V(n)}\\longrightarrow t^{2H}\n\\qquad(n\\to\\infty)\n\\quad\\text{for every }t>0.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3f.1}\n$$\nThen:\n\n1. $H\\le1$ and $V(n)\\to\\infty$.\n2. $X_n(t):=S_{\\lfloor nt\\rfloor}/\\sqrt{V(n)}$ converges in finite-dimensional distributions to the centered Gaussian process $B_H$ with\n   $$\n   \\mathbb E\\,B_H(s)B_H(t)=R_H(s,t):=\\frac12\\left(s^{2H}+t^{2H}-|t-s|^{2H}\\right).\n   \\tag{O.7.2.3f.2}\n   $$\n3. The limit is Brownian motion exactly when $H=1/2$. For $H\\ne1/2$ its adjacent unit increments have correlation $2^{2H-1}-1\\ne0$ and its variance grows as $t^{2H}$; $H=1$ gives the ballistic process $t\\,B_1(1)$.\n4. Every $H\\in(0,1]$ occurs. For $0<H<1$ the kernel $R_H$ is positive semidefinite on $\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}$, and the increments $Y_j=B_H(j)-B_H(j-1)$ of the corresponding Gaussian sequence are strictly stationary with $V(n)=n^{2H}$ exactly. For $H=1$ take $Y_j\\equiv G\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1)$.\n5. Second-order data do not entail a Gaussian limit. Let $Z_j$ be iid $\\mathcal N(0,1)$, let $\\xi$ be independent of them with $\\Pr\\{\\xi=1\\}=\\Pr\\{\\xi=2\\}=1/2$, and put $Y_j=\\xi Z_j$. This sequence is strictly stationary and centered, has moments of every order, zero correlation at every positive lag and $\\operatorname{Var}S_n=5n/2$. Nevertheless $n^{-1/2}S_{\\lfloor n\\cdot\\rfloor}$ converges in finite-dimensional distributions to the variance mixture $\\xi B$, with $B$ a standard Brownian motion independent of $\\xi$, and\n   $$\n   \\kappa_4\\!\\left(n^{-1/2}S_n\\right)=3\\operatorname{Var}(\\xi^2)=\\frac{27}4\n   \\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge1.\n   \\tag{O.7.2.3f.3}\n   $$\n   Its empirical variance $n^{-1}\\sum_{j\\le n}Y_j^2$ converges almost surely to the nondegenerate invariant variable $\\xi^2$, so the sequence is not ergodic. The cumulant decay of Theorem O.7.2.3e rests on independence, and a Gaussian limit on a dependent sector therefore requires a hypothesis beyond stationarity, finite moments, vanishing correlations and linear variance growth, such as the mixing entries of Definition O.7.2.3c.\n\n*Proof.* (1) Minkowski's inequality in $L^2$ gives $\\sqrt{V(n)}\\le n\\sqrt{V(1)}$. Taking $t=2$ in (O.7.2.3f.1), for every $\\epsilon>0$ there is $m_0$ with $V(2m)\\ge(4^H-\\epsilon)V(m)$ for $m\\ge m_0$, hence $V(2^km_0)\\ge(4^H-\\epsilon)^kV(m_0)$. Comparison with $V(2^km_0)\\le4^km_0^2V(1)$ for all $k$ forces $4^H-\\epsilon\\le4$, and $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$ gives $H\\le1$. Taking $t=1/2$ gives $c>1$ and $m_1\\ge2$ with $V(m)\\ge cV(\\lfloor m/2\\rfloor)$ for $m\\ge m_1$. Halving $n$ until the argument first falls below $m_1$ takes at least $\\log_2(n/(m_1+1))$ steps and ends at an argument $m$ with $1\\le m<m_1$, so $V(n)\\ge c^{\\log_2(n/(m_1+1))}\\min_{1\\le m<m_1}V(m)\\to\\infty$.\n\n(2) Strict stationarity gives $S_k-S_m\\overset{d}{=}S_{k-m}$ for $k\\ge m\\ge0$, hence\n$$\n\\operatorname{Cov}(S_m,S_k)=\\frac12\\bigl(V(m)+V(k)-V(k-m)\\bigr).\n$$\nFor $0\\le s<t$, the integer $\\lfloor nt\\rfloor-\\lfloor ns\\rfloor$ differs from $\\lfloor n(t-s)\\rfloor$ by at most one, and $|\\sqrt{V(m+1)}-\\sqrt{V(m)}|\\le\\sqrt{V(1)}$. Since $V(n)\\to\\infty$, (O.7.2.3f.1) gives $V(\\lfloor nt\\rfloor-\\lfloor ns\\rfloor)/V(n)\\to(t-s)^{2H}$, so the covariance of $X_n$ converges to $R_H$ at every pair of times. The finite-dimensional laws of $X_n$ are centered Gaussian, and their characteristic functions $\\exp(-\\frac12u^{\\mathsf T}C_nu)$ converge to $\\exp(-\\frac12u^{\\mathsf T}Cu)$ with $C$ the corresponding matrix of values of $R_H$.\n\n(3) $R_{1/2}(s,t)=\\min(s,t)$ is the Brownian covariance. Conversely, $R_H(1,2)=2^{2H-1}$ equals $\\min(1,2)=1$ only for $H=1/2$. The increments $B_H(1)$ and $B_H(2)-B_H(1)$ have unit variance and covariance $R_H(1,2)-R_H(1,1)=2^{2H-1}-1$; moreover $R_H(t,t)=t^{2H}$ and $R_1(s,t)=st$.\n\n(4) For $0<H<1$ put $\\psi(x)=|x|^{2H}$. By Theorem O.7.2.3e with $d=1$ and $\\alpha=2H\\in(0,2)$, $e^{-\\lambda\\psi(x)}$ is a characteristic function for every $\\lambda>0$, hence $\\sum_{a,b}c_ac_be^{-\\lambda\\psi(x_a-x_b)}=\\mathbb E\\bigl|\\sum_ac_ae^{ix_aX}\\bigr|^2\\ge0$ for the corresponding symmetric stable variable $X$. For real $c_a$ with $\\sum_ac_a=0$ this gives $\\sum_{a,b}c_ac_b\\psi(x_a-x_b)=-\\lim_{\\lambda\\downarrow0}\\lambda^{-1}\\sum_{a,b}c_ac_be^{-\\lambda\\psi(x_a-x_b)}\\le0$. Given $t_1,\\ldots,t_m\\ge0$ and real $c_1,\\ldots,c_m$, adjoin $t_0=0$ and $c_0=-\\sum_{a\\ge1}c_a$; since $\\psi(0)=0$, the previous inequality becomes $\\sum_{a,b\\ge1}c_ac_b\\bigl(\\psi(t_a)+\\psi(t_b)-\\psi(t_a-t_b)\\bigr)\\ge0$, which is positive semidefiniteness of $2R_H$. A positive-semidefinite covariance on $\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}$ defines a centered Gaussian sequence $B_H(0),B_H(1),\\ldots$, and $B_H(0)=0$ because $R_H(0,0)=0$. The increment covariance $\\frac12\\bigl(|k+1|^{2H}-2|k|^{2H}+|k-1|^{2H}\\bigr)$ depends only on the lag $k=i-j$, so the Gaussian increment sequence is strictly stationary, and $V(n)=R_H(n,n)=n^{2H}$. For $H=1$, $V(n)=n^2$.\n\n(5) Conditional on $\\xi$, the $Y_j$ are iid $\\mathcal N(0,\\xi^2)$, so every unconditional finite-dimensional law is shift invariant; $\\mathbb EY_jY_k=\\mathbb E\\xi^2\\,\\mathbb EZ_jZ_k=0$ for $j\\ne k$, and $\\operatorname{Var}S_n=n\\mathbb E\\xi^2=5n/2$. Conditional on $\\xi$, the vector $(n^{-1/2}S_{\\lfloor nt_a\\rfloor})_a$ is centered Gaussian with covariance $\\xi^2(\\lfloor nt_a\\rfloor\\wedge\\lfloor nt_b\\rfloor)/n\\to\\xi^2(t_a\\wedge t_b)$, and bounded convergence of the conditional characteristic functions gives the limit $\\xi B$. Exactly, $n^{-1/2}S_n$ has the law of $\\xi G$ with $G\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1)$ independent of $\\xi$, so $\\mathbb E(\\xi G)^4=3\\mathbb E\\xi^4=51/2$, $\\mathbb E(\\xi G)^2=5/2$ and $\\kappa_4=51/2-3(5/2)^2=27/4=3\\operatorname{Var}(\\xi^2)$. A Gaussian law has zero fourth cumulant. Conditional on $\\xi$, the strong law of large numbers gives $n^{-1}\\sum_{j\\le n}Y_j^2\\to\\xi^2$ almost surely. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723g-full-regularly-varying-stationary-gaussian-functional-convergence\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3g (Full Regularly Varying Stationary Gaussian Functional Convergence).**\n\n#### Statement\n\nAssume precisely Theorem O.7.2.3f. For every finite $T>0$ define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX_{n}(t) &= S_{\\lfloor nt\\rfloor}/\\sqrt{V(n)}, \\\\\n\\tilde{X}_{n}(t) &= [S_{\\lfloor nt\\rfloor}+(nt-\\lfloor nt\\rfloor)Y_{\\lfloor nt\\rfloor+1}]/\\sqrt{V(n)}, \\\\\n0 &\\le t\\le T.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $B_{H}$ denote the continuous version of the centered Gaussian limit with covariance $R_{H}$. For $H=1$ this is $B_1(t)=tG$, $G\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1)$.\n\nThen:\n\n1. $\\tilde{X}_n$ converges weakly to $B_{H}$ in $C([0,T],\\mathbb{R})$, equipped with the uniform norm.\n2. $\\lVert X_n-\\tilde{X}_n\\rVert_\\infty\\to 0$ in probability.\n3. $X_{n}$ converges weakly to $B_{H}$ in $D([0,T],\\mathbb{R})$, equipped with Skorokhod $J_1$, and the laws are $C$-tight there.\n\nNo convergence rate uniform over this input class, no strong approximation on the original probability space, and no rough-path/integral enhancement is asserted. In particular “uniform norm convergence” in item 1 means weak convergence of laws in that topology, not convergence in probability to an already coupled $B_{H}$. The original step paths are not C-valued.\n\n#### Proof\n\nWrite $b(k)=\\sqrt{V(k)}$, $k\\ge 1$, $b(0)=0$, and $\\sigma=b(1)>0$. Stationarity and Minkowski give, for integers $r,s\\ge 0$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert S_{r+s}-S_{r}\\rVert_{2} &= b(s), \\\\\nb(r+s) &\\le b(r)+b(s).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTheorem O.7.2.3f already establishes $0<H\\le 1$ and the finite-dimensional limit. Fix any $h$ with $0<h<H$ (therefore $h<1$).\n\n#### 1. Uniform scale bound, without monotone variance or Potter imports\n\nThe original regular-variation condition at $t=1/2$ gives\n\n$$\nb(\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor)/b(k)\\to 2^{-H}<2^{-h}=a<1.\n$$\n\nChoose integer $N\\ge 2$ such that $b(\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor)\\le a\\,b(k)$ whenever $k\\ge N$. Put $n_j=\\lfloor n/2^j\\rfloor$, and for $n\\ge N$ let $L$ be the first $j$ for which $n_j<N$. Then $1\\le n_L<N$, and iterating gives $b(n_j)\\le a^j b(n)$ for $0\\le j\\le L$. With $b_*=\\min_{1\\le k<N}b(k)>0$,\n\n$$\nb(n)\\ge a^{-L}b_{*}=2^{hL}b_{*}\\ge b_{*}(n/N)^{h}.\n$$\n\nThe last inequality follows from $\\lfloor n/2^L\\rfloor<N$, hence $n/2^L<N$. Increasing constants over the finitely many $n<N$ gives $b(n)\\ge c_h n^{h}$ for all $n\\ge 1$.\n\nLet $M(k)=\\max_{0\\le r\\le k} b(r)$. For $k\\ge2$,\n\n$$\nM(k)\\le b(k)+M(\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor).\n$$\n\nIndeed, $r\\le\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor$ is already in the second term; for larger $r$, stationarity gives $b(r)\\le b(k)+b(k-r)$, and $k-r\\le\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor$. Set $K=\\max_{1\\le k<N}M(k)/b(k)$, finite by the original positivity assumption. Iterating the envelope bound to $n_{L}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM(n) &\\le\\sum_{j=0}^{L-1}b(n_{j})+M(n_{L}) \\\\\n&\\le\\Bigl[\\sum_{j=0}^{L-1}a^{j}+K\\,a^{L}\\Bigr]b(n) \\\\\n&\\le[1/(1-a)+K]\\,b(n).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $M(n)\\le C_h b(n)$ for all positive $n$, including the finitely many small $n$.\n\nIf $N\\le m\\le n$, choose $j$ so that $n_j\\ge m$ and $n_{j+1}<m$. All predecessors to $n_j$ are $\\ge N$, and $n_j\\le 2m-1$. Consequently\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb(m) &\\le M(n_{j})\\le C_{h}a^{j}b(n), \\\\\nn/2^{j} &< n_{j}+1\\le 2m,\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $b(m)/b(n)\\le C_h2^h(m/n)^h$. If $1\\le m<N$, the same inequality after enlarging the constant follows from $b(n)\\ge c_hn^{h}$ and boundedness of those finitely many $b(m)$. Hence\n\n$$\nb(m)/b(n)\\le A_{h}(m/n)^{h},\\qquad 1\\le m\\le n.\n\\tag{1}\n$$\n\nFor $1\\le m\\le nT$ with $m>n$, write $m=qn+r$, $0\\le r<n$. Subadditivity and (1) give $b(m)/b(n)\\le q+A_h$, bounded by a $T$-dependent constant. Since $m/n\\ge 1$, we obtain\n\n$$\nb(m)/b(n)\\le A_{h,T}(m/n)^{h},\\quad 1\\le m\\le nT.\n\\tag{2}\n$$\n\nThis derivation permits arbitrary slowly varying factors and nonmonotone variance. It uses only the half-scale limit already in the assumptions, stationarity, positivity, and $L^2$ inequalities. In particular it never silently replaces $b(n)$ by $n^{H}$.\n\n#### 2. All interpolation intervals, including below the mesh\n\nLet $\\delta=t-s>0$ with $0\\le s<t\\le T$.\n\nIf $\\delta\\ge 1/n$, decompose the polygonal increment into its full mesh-cell block of length $m$ (possibly zero) and at most two fractional edge cells. Here $m\\le n\\delta$. The $L^2$ norm of the full block is $b(m)$, and each fractional edge contributes at most $\\sigma$. Thus (2) and $\\sigma/b(n)\\le C_hn^{-h}$ imply\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\lVert\\tilde{X}_{n}(t)-\\tilde{X}_{n}(s)\\rVert_{2} \\\\\n&\\quad\\le[b(m)+2\\sigma]/b(n)\\le C_{h,T}\\delta^{h}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n$$\n\nFor $m=0$ simply omit the $b(m)$ term; there is no division by $V(0)$.\n\nIf $\\delta<1/n$, the interval meets at most two mesh cells. The coefficients multiplying the corresponding $Y$ variables are nonnegative and add up exactly to $n\\delta$. No independence is required for Minkowski, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert\\tilde{X}_{n}(t)-\\tilde{X}_{n}(s)\\rVert_{2} \\\\\n&\\le n\\delta\\sigma/b(n) \\\\\n&\\le C_{h}n^{1-h}\\delta \\\\\n&= C_{h}\\delta^{h}(n\\delta)^{1-h} \\\\\n&\\le C_{h}\\delta^{h}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n$$\n\nThis is the microscopic estimate that cannot be obtained by applying the macroscopic bound with a bare +1 floor error.\n\n#### 3. Gaussian moments and tightness\n\nEvery polygonal increment is centered Gaussian, even when its variance is zero. For any even integer $p=2k$, its $p$th absolute moment equals $c_{p}$ times the $p$th power of its $L^2$ norm, where $c_p=\\mathbb{E}|G|^p=(2k-1)!!$ for $G\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1)$. Choose $p$ so that $hp>1$. Equations (3)–(4) give, uniformly in $n$,\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{E}|\\tilde{X}_{n}(t)-\\tilde{X}_{n}(s)|^{p}\\le C_{h,T,p}|t-s|^{hp}.\n\\tag{5}\n$$\n\nThe standard Kolmogorov moment tightness criterion, with exponent $hp>1$, gives tightness of these continuous-path laws in $C([0,T])$; $\\tilde{X}_n(0)=0$ fixes the initial values. For completeness, the criterion follows by bounding dyadic-edge increments using Markov's inequality: for any $0<\\alpha<h-1/p$, the probability of a dyadic edge at level $\\ell$ exceeding $K2^{-\\alpha\\ell}$ is bounded by $CK^{-p}2^{-\\ell(hp-\\alpha p-1)}$. Summing this convergent geometric series over sufficiently fine levels and chaining continuous paths yields a uniform modulus of continuity in probability.\n\nAlso $|\\tilde{X}_n(t)-X_n(t)|$ has $L^2$ norm $\\le\\sigma/b(n)\\to 0$ at every fixed time. Therefore the existing finite-dimensional convergence passes to $\\tilde{X}_n$. The limiting Gaussian covariance admits a continuous version: $\\mathbb{E}|B_H(t)-B_H(s)|^p=c_p|t-s|^{Hp}$, and choose $Hp>1$. Tightness plus the unique continuous law determined by those finite-dimensional distributions proves item 1. No nonsingularity of finite-dimensional covariance matrices is needed.\n\n#### 4. Maximum jump and the original path topology\n\nDeterministically,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\lVert\\tilde{X}_{n}-X_{n}\\rVert_{\\infty} \\\\\n&\\quad\\le\\max_{1\\le j\\le\\lfloor nT\\rfloor+1}|Y_{j}|/b(n).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the same even $p$ with $hp>1$, stationarity and a union bound yield, for every $\\epsilon>0$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(\\lVert\\tilde{X}_{n}-X_{n}\\rVert_{\\infty}>\\epsilon) \\\\\n&\\le(\\lfloor nT\\rfloor+1)\\mathbb{E}|Y_{1}|^{p}/[\\epsilon^{p}b(n)^{p}] \\\\\n&\\le C_{\\epsilon,h,T,p}n^{1-hp}\\to 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n$$\n\nThis proves item 2 without independence or a mixing assumption. The identity time-change bounds J1 distance by uniform distance. The continuous embedding of C with uniform topology into D with J1, followed by the converging-together theorem, proves item 3. Every limiting law is supported on continuous paths, as required for C-tightness.\n\n#### 5. Boundary and degeneracy checks\n\n- $H$ may be arbitrarily small and positive: choose $h<H$ and then arbitrarily large even $p$. A fixed fourth-moment proof would lose some of this range, but Gaussianity supplies every moment automatically.\n- $H=1$: choose any $h<1$; exactly the same estimates apply and $R_1(s,t)=st$, so the continuous limit is $tG$. No exclusion of the ballistic endpoint is introduced.\n- $V(0)=0$ is handled by omitting empty blocks. Original $V(k)>0$ for every $k\\ge 1$ is used for the finite minima and denominators. The theorem does not silently add a stronger nondegeneracy assumption. Individual interpolated differences and joint Gaussian covariance matrices may be degenerate; zero Gaussian variance makes (5) trivial.\n- If one proposes changing the original hypothesis to permit $V(k)=0$ at a positive integer $k$, that is a separate formulation. Stationarity then makes every consecutive $k$-block sum zero almost surely, hence $Y_{j+k}=Y_j$ and the sums are periodic/bounded in $L^2$. Such a sequence cannot have the current positive-index divergent-variance behavior. No such extension is needed here.\n- The proof does not assume positive correlations, independent increments, Markov structure, or an absolutely continuous spectral measure.\n\nThis completes the proof.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723h-full-l2-long-memory-hermite-process-functional-class\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3h (Full L2 Long-Memory Hermite-Process Functional Class).**\n\nFix an integer $m\\ge 2$, a source Hurst parameter $H\\in(1-1/(2m),1)$, and $T<\\infty$. Set\n\n$$\nd=2-2H,\\quad c=H(2H-1),\\quad h=1-md/2=1-m(1-H).\n$$\n\nThus $0<md<1$ and $1/2<h<1$. Let $(G_j)_{j\\in\\mathbb{Z}}$ be centered stationary Gaussian with covariance\n\n$$\nr(k)=(|k+1|^{2H}-2|k|^{2H}+|k-1|^{2H})/2.\n$$\n\nIn particular $r(0)=1$. Let $F$ be any real member of $L^{2}(\\text{standard Gaussian measure})$, with mean zero and Hermite expansion in the probabilists' convention\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(x) &= \\sum_{q=m}^{\\infty}a_{q}\\mathrm{He}_{q}(x), \\\\\na_{m} &\\ne 0,\\quad\\sum_{q=m}^{\\infty}q!\\,a_{q}^{2}<\\infty.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an exact source-law and observable assumption, not an inference from response covariance. Put $Y_j=F(G_j)$, $S_k=\\sum_{j=1}^k Y_j$, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX_{n}(t) &= n^{-h}S_{\\lfloor nt\\rfloor}, \\\\\nP_{n}(t) &= n^{-h}[S_{\\lfloor nt\\rfloor}+(nt-\\lfloor nt\\rfloor)Y_{\\lfloor nt\\rfloor+1}].\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $W$ be real isonormal white noise over $L^{2}(\\mathbb{R})$, with multiple-integral normalization $\\mathbb{E}[I_q(f)I_p(g)]=1_{q=p}\\,q!\\,\\langle f,g\\rangle$. Define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb &= \\operatorname{Beta}(H-1/2,2-2H),\\quad A=\\sqrt{c/b}, \\\\\nK_{t}(x_{1},\\ldots,x_{m}) &= A^{m}\\int_{0}^{t}\\prod_{i}(u-x_{i})_{+}^{H-3/2}\\,du, \\\\\nZ(t) &= a_{m}I_{m}(K_{t}).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nValues of integrands at their singular points are immaterial. The kernels $K_{t}$ are genuine symmetric $L^{2}(\\mathbb{R}^m)$ kernels, as established below; individual functions $(u-x)_+^{H-3/2}$ are NOT asserted to lie in $L^{2}$.\n\n*Statement.* $P_{n}$ converges in distribution to $Z$ in $C([0,T])$ with the uniform topology. $X_{n}$ converges in distribution to the same continuous limit in $D([0,T])$ with the Skorokhod $J_1$ topology. In fact $\\lVert P_n-X_n\\rVert_\\infty\\to 0$ in probability. All finite-dimensional distributions converge by an explicit $L^{2}$ convergence of the leading multiple-integral kernels and an $L^{2}$-negligible full higher-chaos remainder. $Z$ is centered, non-Gaussian at every $t>0$, $h$-self-similar, and has stationary increments. Its covariance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb{E}[Z(s)Z(t)] &= [a_{m}^{2}m!\\,c^{m}/((1-md)(2-md))] \\\\\n&\\quad(s^{2h}+t^{2h}-|t-s|^{2h}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{1}\n$$\n\nConsequently $\\operatorname{Var}(S_n)/n^{2h}\\to v_m$, where\n\n$$\nv_{m}=2a_{m}^{2}m!\\,c^{m}/((1-md)(2-md)).\n\\tag{2}\n$$\n\nNormalization by $\\sqrt{\\operatorname{Var}(S_n)}$ instead of $n^{h}$ gives the variance-one Hermite process $Z/\\sqrt{v_m}$. The sign $a_{m}$ is retained.\n\n#### 2. Source construction and exact normalization\n\nWrite $g_u(x)=(u-x)_+^{H-3/2}$. For $u\\ne v$, the substitution $x=\\min(u,v)-|u-v|z$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\int_{\\mathbb{R}}g_{u}(x)g_{v}(x)\\,dx \\\\\n&\\quad=|u-v|^{-d}\\int_{0}^{\\infty}z^{H-3/2}(1+z)^{H-3/2}\\,dz \\\\\n&\\quad=b\\,|u-v|^{-d}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n$$\n\nBoth Beta parameters are positive. Nonnegative integrands permit Tonelli throughout. For bounded intervals $I,J$, the interval integrals of $g$ belong to $L^{2}$, with inner product $b\\int_I\\int_J|u-v|^{-d}\\,du\\,dv$. In particular let\n\n$$\ne_{j}(x)=A\\int_{j-1}^{j}g_{u}(x)\\,du.\n$$\n\nThen $\\langle e_i,e_j\\rangle=c\\int_{i-1}^{i}\\int_{j-1}^{j}|u-v|^{-d}\\,du\\,dv=r(i-j)$, by integrating the second derivative of $|u-v|^{2H}$. The diagonal equals $2c/((1-d)(2-d))=1$. Hence $G_j=W(e_j)$ has precisely the stipulated Gaussian law, including joint stationarity. Also $r(k)\\ge 0$, $r(k)\\le 1$, and\n\n$$\nr(k)\\sim c\\,|k|^{-d},\\quad r(k)\\le C(1+|k|)^{-d}.\n\\tag{4}\n$$\n\nFor $|k|\\ge 2$ these statements follow directly from the interval integral; bounded remaining lags are absorbed into $C$. Thus the observable is genuinely long-range: its covariance has leading term $a_m^{2}m!\\,c^m|k|^{-md}$, whose sum diverges.\n\nTonelli and (3) yield\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert K_{t}\\rVert^{2} &= c^{m}\\int_{[0,t]^{2}}|u-v|^{-md}\\,du\\,dv \\\\\n&= 2c^{m}t^{2-md}/((1-md)(2-md)).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n$$\n\nThis proves kernel existence, its nonzero norm, and its exact normalization. Mixed intervals give (1). Translation invariance and Brownian scaling of $W$ applied to this explicit kernel give joint stationary increments and joint h-self-similarity, respectively. Indeed $K_{at}(ax)=a^{1+m(H-3/2)}K_t(x)$, and $m$ white-noise factors contribute $a^{m/2}$.\n\n#### 3. Full leading-chaos kernel convergence\n\nFor unit-norm $e$, $\\mathrm{He}_m(W(e))=I_m(e^{\\otimes m})$. Rescale white noise using the unitary map $U_nf(x)=\\sqrt{n}f(nx)$. A direct change of variables gives\n\n$$\nU_{n}e_{j}(x)=A\\,n^{H}\\int_{(j-1)/n}^{j/n}g_{u}(x)\\,du.\n$$\n\nSince $mH-h=m-1$, the process $n^{-h}\\sum_{j\\le nt}\\mathrm{He}_m(G_j)$ has exactly, jointly in $t$, the law of $I_m(K_{n,t})$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nK_{n,t} &= A^{m}n^{m-1}\\sum_{j=1}^{\\lfloor nt\\rfloor} \\\\\n&\\quad\\Bigl(\\int_{(j-1)/n}^{j/n}g_{u}\\,du\\Bigr)^{\\otimes m}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n$$\n\nWe now prove $K_{n,t}\\to K_t$ in $L^{2}(\\mathbb{R}^m)$, not just convergence of $\\lVert K_{n,t}\\rVert$.\n\nFix a bounded square $[0,L]^{2}$ containing all times in question and their mesh cells. Let $q(u,v)=|u-v|^{-d}$, with any value on its measure-zero diagonal. It belongs to $L^m([0,L]^{2})$ because $md<1$. Let $Q_n^1$ and $Q_n^2$ average in the first and second variables over mesh intervals of width $1/n$ (use the shortened last cell if necessary). These are contractions on $L^m$. Moreover $Q_n^1q,\\ Q_n^2q,\\ Q_n^1Q_n^2q\\to q$ in $L^m$. To see this without a nested-partition assumption, approximate $q$ in $L^m$ by a continuous function; its mesh averages converge uniformly, and the contraction bound controls the approximation error. It follows also that their $m$-th powers converge in $L^1$, using $|a^m-b^m|\\le m|a-b|(|a|+|b|)^{m-1}$ and Hölder.\n\nPut $t_n=\\lfloor nt\\rfloor/n$. Expanding (6) and using (3) gives exactly\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert K_{n,t}\\rVert^{2} &= c^{m}\\int_{[0,t_{n}]^{2}}(Q_{n}^{1}Q_{n}^{2}q)^{m}, \\\\\n\\langle K_{n,t},K_{t}\\rangle &= c^{m}\\int_{[0,t_{n}]\\times[0,t]}(Q_{n}^{1}q)^{m}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n$$\n\nThe factors $n$ in the averages match $n^{m-1}$ in (6): the first integral weights each cell pair by $n^{-2}$, the second by $n^{-1}$. By the $L^1$ convergence just proved and absolute continuity of the integral on the shrinking endpoint strips, both expressions tend to $\\lVert K_t\\rVert^{2}$. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\lVert K_{n,t}-K_{t}\\rVert^{2}\\to 0.\n\\tag{8}\n$$\n\nThe Wiener isometry upgrades (8) to $L^{2}$ convergence of the actual random variables on a common white-noise realization, and the same holds for any finite linear combination of times. This proves joint finite-dimensional convergence of the entire leading chaos. It is stronger than convergence of covariance matrices, and does not assume moment determinacy.\n\n#### 4. The infinite higher-chaos remainder really vanishes\n\nLet $R=F-a_m\\mathrm{He}_m$ and $B=\\sum_{q>m}q!\\,a_q^{2}<\\infty$. Orthogonality and the Gaussian Hermite identity give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Cov}(R(G_{0}),R(G_{k})) &= \\sum_{q>m}q!\\,a_{q}^{2}r(k)^{q} \\\\\n&\\le B\\,r(k)^{m+1},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{9}\n$$\n\nsince $0\\le r(k)\\le 1$. If $B=0$ there is nothing to prove. Otherwise $r(k)\\to 0$ implies this covariance is $o((1+k)^{-md})$. For every $\\epsilon>0$ choose $K$ so that for $k>K$ it is at most $\\epsilon(1+k)^{-md}$. Summing covariances in a block of length $N$ then gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Var}\\Bigl(\\sum_{j=1}^{N}R(G_{j})\\Bigr)\\le C_{K}N+C\\epsilon N^{2-md}.\n$$\n\nDivide by $n^{(2h)}$, take $N=\\lfloor nt\\rfloor$, let $n\\to\\infty$ and then $\\epsilon\\to 0$. Since $2h>1$ the result is zero. Thus the full infinite remainder tends to zero in $L^{2}$ at each time and at every finite collection of times. No finite-polynomial or higher-moment hypothesis on $F$ has been silently imposed. Equations (8)-(9) establish the asserted full distributional limit.\n\nThe same calculation, or the leading-chaos norm limit plus orthogonality, proves (2).\n\n#### 5. Tightness in C and transfer to J1\n\nSet $M=\\mathbb{E}[F(G_0)^{2}]$. The Hermite expansion gives\n\n$$\n0\\le\\operatorname{Cov}(Y_{0},Y_{k})\\le M\\,r(k)^{m}\\le C(1+|k|)^{-md}.\n$$\n\nTherefore every length-N consecutive block satisfies\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{E}\\Bigl|\\sum Y_{j}\\Bigr|^{2}\\le C\\,N^{2h},\\qquad N\\ge 1.\n\\tag{10}\n$$\n\nWe prove a uniform increment bound for polygonal paths, including the often omitted submesh case:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{E}|P_{n}(t)-P_{n}(s)|^{2}\\le C_{T}|t-s|^{2h}.\n\\tag{11}\n$$\n\nIf $\\delta=t-s\\ge 1/n$, the increment consists of one full block and at most two fractional endpoint terms. Minkowski's inequality and (10) bound its $L^{2}$ norm by $Cn^{-h}(n\\delta+2)^h\\le C'\\delta^{h}$. If $\\delta<1/n$, it crosses at most two cells. Its nonnegative endpoint weights have sum $n\\delta$. Minkowski bounds its $L^{2}$ norm by $\\sqrt{M}\\,n^{-h}n\\delta$. Its square is $M\\delta^{2h}(n\\delta)^{2-2h}\\le M\\delta^{2h}$, proving (11) in this case as well.\n\nFor completeness, (11) implies the uniform tightness criterion by dyadic chaining: choose $0<\\gamma<(2h-1)/2$. Markov and a union bound over $O(2^l)$ dyadic neighboring increments show that the probability of any level-$l$ increment exceeding $\\epsilon 2^{-l\\gamma}$ is at most $C\\epsilon^{-2}2^{-l(2h-1-2\\gamma)}$. The sum over $l\\ge L$ tends to zero uniformly in $n$. On its complementary event, continuity and the geometric dyadic chain bound the modulus on distances $\\le T2^{-L}$ by $C_\\gamma\\epsilon 2^{-L\\gamma}$. Finitely many coarse levels and $P_n(0)=0$ provide the remaining bound. This proves tightness in $C([0,T])$. The same second-moment bound for $Z$ gives its continuous modification. Tightness and the finite-dimensional convergence already proved identify the unique limit $Z$ on $C$.\n\nFinally\n\n$$\n\\lVert P_{n}-X_{n}\\rVert_{\\infty}\\le n^{-h}\\max_{j\\le\\lfloor nT\\rfloor+1}|Y_{j}|.\n$$\n\nStationarity and the union bound give, for $\\epsilon>0$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&P(\\lVert P_{n}-X_{n}\\rVert_{\\infty}>\\epsilon) \\\\\n&\\quad\\le(\\lfloor nT\\rfloor+1)M/(\\epsilon^{2}n^{2h})\\to 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{12}\n$$\n\nNo independence is needed. Uniform convergence implies $J_1$ convergence; viewing the polygonal convergence in $D$ and using (12) proves the step-path assertion. We claim in-probability interpolation error for general $L^{2}$ $F$, not an unproved almost-sure bound.\n\n#### 6. Why the limit is genuinely non-Gaussian\n\nHere is an algebraic check rather than an inference from the covariance exponent. For any real symmetric $f\\in L^{2}(\\mathbb{R}^m)$, write $f\\otimes_r f$ for its $r$-fold contraction and sym for symmetrization. The Wiener product formula and isometry give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb{E}[I_{m}(f)^{4}] &= \\sum_{r=0}^{m}\\Bigl(r!\\binom{m}{r}^{2}\\Bigr)^{2} \\\\\n&\\quad(2m-2r)!\\,\\lVert\\operatorname{sym}(f\\otimes_{r}f)\\rVert^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{13}\n$$\n\nCounting which r coordinates of the first copy are paired with the first copy on the other side gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(2m)!\\,\\lVert\\operatorname{sym}(f\\otimes f)\\rVert^{2} \\\\\n&\\quad=(m!)^{2}\\sum_{r=0}^{m}\\binom{m}{r}^{2}\\lVert f\\otimes_{r}f\\rVert^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{14}\n$$\n\nThese identities follow first for finite tensors by multiplying Hermite polynomials / Wick pairings and then by $L^{2}$ contraction bounds; the finite-chaos fourth moments are finite by (13). In (14) its $r=0$ and $r=m$ terms total $2(m!)^{2}\\lVert f\\rVert^4$. The $r=m$ term of (13) adds $(m!)^{2}\\lVert f\\rVert^4$. All other terms are nonnegative. For $m\\ge 2$ and nonzero $f$, $\\lVert f\\otimes_{m-1}f\\rVert>0$: regarding $f$ as the kernel of a Hilbert-Schmidt map from $L^{2}(\\mathbb{R}^{m-1})$ to $L^{2}(\\mathbb{R})$, this contraction is the nonzero positive operator $AA^*$. Thus (14) contains a strictly positive additional term. Consequently\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{E}[I_{m}(f)^{4}]>3(\\mathbb{E}[I_{m}(f)^{2}])^{2}.\n$$\n\nApply this with $f=K_t$, which is nonzero by (5), and multiply by $a_m^4$. $Z(t)$ is not Gaussian. This also addresses odd $m$, for which symmetry makes the third moment zero.\n\n#### 7. Requested exact rank-two witness\n\nTake $H=7/8$, $m=2$ and $F(x)=x^{2}-1$. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&d=1/4,\\quad h=3/4,\\quad c=21/32, \\\\\n&b=\\operatorname{Beta}(3/8,1/4),\\quad A=\\sqrt{(21/32)/\\operatorname{Beta}(3/8,1/4)}, \\\\\n&K_{t}(x,y)=((21/32)/\\operatorname{Beta}(3/8,1/4)) \\\\\n&\\qquad\\int_{0}^{t}(u-x)_{+}^{-5/8}(u-y)_{+}^{-5/8}\\,du.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe theorem gives $n^{-3/4}$ polygonal and $J_1$ step-path convergence to $I_2(K_t)$, a Rosenblatt process with $\\operatorname{Var}(I_2(K_1))=147/64$. Its covariance is $(147/128)(s^{3/2}+t^{3/2}-|t-s|^{3/2})$. Hence dividing the sums by $(7\\sqrt{3}/8)n^{3/4}$ gives a variance-one Rosenblatt limit.\n\nThe product formula also gives the explicit third cumulant\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb{E}[I_{2}(K_{1})^{3}] &= 8c^{3}\\int_{[0,1]^{3}} \\\\\n&\\quad|u-v|^{-1/4}|v-w|^{-1/4}|w-u|^{-1/4}\\,du\\,dv\\,dw>0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOrdering the three variables and using the adjacent gaps a,b evaluates that triple integral exactly:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ &= 6\\int_{a,b>0,\\,a+b<1}(1-a-b)a^{-1/4}b^{-1/4}(a+b)^{-1/4}\\,da\\,db \\\\\n&= 6\\operatorname{Beta}(3/4,3/4)/((5/4)(9/4)) \\\\\n&= (32/15)\\operatorname{Beta}(3/4,3/4).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the variance-standardized third moment is\n\n$$\n(3\\sqrt{3}/8)\\,J=(4\\sqrt{3}/5)\\operatorname{Beta}(3/4,3/4)>0.\n\\tag{15}\n$$\n\nThis is an analytic value; it uses no finite covariance diagnostics.\n\n#### 8. Limits of entailment and residual ownership\n\nThe original Gaussian variance-regular-variation assumption does not by itself specify this nonlinear source/observable model. We explicitly strengthen the model to exact fractional Gaussian noise and a fixed $L^{2}$ function of known Hermite rank. No spectral regularity assumption is hidden: the time-domain source kernels are constructed and verified above.\n\nEven the exact response covariance cannot identify this limit among arbitrary stationary non-Gaussian sources. For the rank-two witness let $\\xi$ be independent of the entire $G$ sequence, taking $\\sqrt{1/2}$ and $\\sqrt{3/2}$ with probabilities $1/2$. Define $Y'_j=\\xi(G_j^{2}-1)$. Because $\\mathbb{E}[\\xi^{2}]=1$, $Y'$ has exactly the same covariance, the same variance at every block length, and all finite moments as $Y$. Yet its polygonal and $J_1$ limits are $\\xi I_2(K)$, by conditioning on the two $\\xi$ values. Their fourth moments differ by $\\mathbb{E}[\\xi^4]=5/4$, so their laws differ. This is a precise covariance-only nonentailment counterexample, not a claim that the exact fGn assumption is logically necessary for every possible Hermite limit theorem.\n\nCompleted scope: all centered $L^2$ fixed observables of exact unit-variance fractional Gaussian noise with Hermite rank $m\\ge 2$ and $m(2-2H)<1$, on every finite horizon, with explicit kernel, exact normalization, non-Gaussian law, full finite-dimensional distribution convergence and path tightness.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723i-critical-finite-polynomial-functional-gaussian-limit\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3i (Critical Finite-Polynomial Functional Gaussian Limit).**\n\n#### 1. Model, statement, and exact constant\n\nFix an integer $m\\ge 2$, $H=1-1/(2m)$, $T<\\infty$, and a finite polynomial in the probabilists' Hermite convention\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\sum_{q=m}^{Q}a_{q}\\mathrm{He}_{q}(x),\\qquad Q\\ge m,\\quad a_{m}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet $G_{j}$, j in Z, be centered unit-variance stationary Gaussian with exact fractional-Gaussian-noise covariance\n\n$$\nr(k)=(|k+1|^{2H}-2|k|^{2H}+|k-1|^{2H})/2.\n$$\n\nSet $c=H(2H-1)>0$ and $Y_j=F(G_j)$. For $n\\ge 2$ set $b_n=\\sqrt{n\\log n}$, $S_k=\\sum_{j=1}^kY_j$, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX_{n}(t) &= S_{\\lfloor nt\\rfloor}/b_{n}, \\\\\nP_{n}(t) &= [S_{\\lfloor nt\\rfloor}+(nt-\\lfloor nt\\rfloor)Y_{\\lfloor nt\\rfloor+1}]/b_{n}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Statement.* On every finite horizon, $P_{n}$ converges in distribution in $C([0,T])$ with its uniform topology to $\\sigma B$, where $B$ is standard Brownian motion and\n\n$$\n\\sigma^{2}=2a_{m}^{2}m!\\,[H(2H-1)]^{m}.\n\\tag{1}\n$$\n\n$X_{n}$ converges to the same limit in $D([0,T])$ with the Skorokhod $J_1$ topology, and $\\lVert P_n-X_n\\rVert_\\infty$ tends to zero in probability. Also $\\operatorname{Var}(S_n)/(n\\log n)\\to\\sigma^{2}$. The proof explicitly shows that all nontrivial contractions of each finite-dimensional leading-chaos linear combination tend to zero; covariance convergence alone is not used to infer Gaussianity.\n\nFor the concrete critical rank-two response $H=3/4$, $F(x)=x^{2}-1$, $c=3/8$ and $\\sigma^{2}=9/16$. Thus division by $(3/4)\\sqrt{n\\log n}$ yields standard Brownian motion. The prelimit response is non-Gaussian, even though its functional limit is Gaussian.\n\n#### 2. Source realization and covariance bounds\n\nThe source exists by the explicit time-domain construction of Theorem O.7.2.3h, whose source construction itself holds for every $H\\in(1/2,1)$, independently of its later long-range restriction. For self-containment: let $W$ be isonormal white noise on $L^{2}(\\mathbb{R})$, put\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&g_{u}(x)=(u-x)_{+}^{H-3/2}, \\\\\n&\\beta_{H}=\\operatorname{Beta}(H-1/2,2-2H),\\quad A=\\sqrt{c/\\beta_{H}}, \\\\\n&e_{j}(x)=A\\int_{j-1}^{j}g_{u}(x)\\,du.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $\\beta$ integral gives $\\int_{\\mathbb{R}}g_u(x)g_v(x)\\,dx=\\beta_H|u-v|^{2H-2}$ for $u\\ne v$. Tonelli implies $e_{j}$ belongs to $L^{2}$ and\n\n$$\n\\langle e_{i},e_{j}\\rangle=c\\int_{i-1}^{i}\\int_{j-1}^{j}|u-v|^{-1/m}\\,du\\,dv=r(i-j).\n$$\n\nIn particular $\\lVert e_j\\rVert=1$, so $G_j=W(e_j)$ has the prescribed law. The individual $g_{u}$ are not incorrectly treated as $L^{2}$ functions. This also proves\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&0\\le r(k)\\le 1,\\qquad r(k)\\sim c\\,|k|^{-1/m}, \\\\\n&r(k)\\le C(1+|k|)^{-1/m}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotic and bound follow by the interval integral for $|k|\\ge 2$ and a finite-lag bound elsewhere.\n\n#### 3. Exact variance growth and Brownian covariance\n\nHermite orthogonality gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Cov}(\\mathrm{He}_{q}(G_{i}),\\mathrm{He}_{p}(G_{j}))=1_{p=q}\\,q!\\,r(i-j)^{q}.\n$$\n\nFor $q=m$, $r(k)^m\\sim c^m/k$. Consequently\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{m}(N) &:= \\operatorname{Var}\\Bigl(\\sum_{j=1}^{N}\\mathrm{He}_{m}(G_{j})\\Bigr) \\\\\n&= m!\\Bigl[N+2\\sum_{k=1}^{N-1}(N-k)r(k)^{m}\\Bigr] \\\\\n&\\sim 2m!\\,c^{m}N\\log N.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n$$\n\nIndeed $\\sum_{k<N}r(k)^m\\sim c^m\\log N$ by comparison with the harmonic series, while $\\sum_{k<N}k\\,r(k)^m=O(N)$. For $q>m$, (2) gives $\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb{Z}}r(k)^q<\\infty$ and $V_q(N)\\le C_qN$. Hence (3) and orthogonality prove (1)'s variance assertion.\n\nStationary increments of these partial-sum processes give exactly\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Cov}(S_{A},S_{B})=[V(A)+V(B)-V(|A-B|)]/2.\n$$\n\nFor $A=\\lfloor ns\\rfloor$, $B=\\lfloor nt\\rfloor$, (3) gives $V(A)/(n\\log n)\\to\\sigma^{2}s$ (and similarly for $B$ and $|A-B|$); the conclusion also holds at zero. Thus the finite-dimensional covariance limit is $\\sigma^{2}\\min(s,t)$. This is only the covariance step; the next two sections establish the distributions.\n\n#### 4. Critical convolution lemma\n\nFor $0<a<1$, set $b=1-a$. For integers $i,l\\in\\{1,\\ldots,N\\}$, $h=|i-l|$, define\n\n$$\nC_{i,l}=\\sum_{j=1}^{N}(1+|i-j|)^{-a}(1+|j-l|)^{-b}.\n$$\n\nThere is a constant depending only on a such that\n\n$$\nC_{i,l}\\le C_{a}[1+\\log(N/(1+h))],\n\\tag{4}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i,l=1}^{N}C_{i,l}^{2}\\le C'_{a}N^{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n$$\n\nHere $N/(1+h)\\ge 1$, including $h=N-1$. Proof: if $h=0$, the summands are $(1+|i-j|)^{-1}$, giving $O(1+\\log N)$. If $h\\ge 1$, split $j$ into distances $\\le h/2$ from $i$, distances $\\le h/2$ from $l$, the remaining part within $O(h)$ of both endpoints, and the two exterior tails farther than a fixed multiple of $h$. The first sum is bounded by $Ch^{-b}\\sum_{k\\le h/2}(1+k)^{-a}\\le C$, the second by the analogous bound with $a,b$ interchanged. In the middle region each term is $O(h^{-1})$ and there are $O(h)$ terms. In the exterior tails the two distances are comparable, so their product is at most $C/(1+\\text{distance})$; summing from order $h$ to $N$ gives $C[1+\\log(N/(1+h))]$. This proves (4).\n\nFor (5), each distance h occurs for at most 2N ordered pairs. The sum\n\n$$\n\\sum_{h=0}^{N-1}[1+\\log(N/(1+h))]^{2}\\le C\\,N\n$$\n\nfollows by monotone integral comparison with $\\int_0^1(1+\\log(1/x))^{2}\\,dx=5$ (the endpoint contribution $(1+\\log N)^{2}$ is also $O(N)$). Multiplication by $2N$ proves (5). Small $N$ are absorbed in the constants.\n\n#### 5. Every leading-chaos contraction vanishes\n\nLet times $t_1,\\ldots,t_k\\in[0,T]$ and real coefficients $\\theta_1,\\ldots,\\theta_k$ be fixed. Their leading-chaos linear combination is $I_m(f_n)$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf_{n} &= (a_{m}/b_{n})\\sum_{j=1}^{N_{n}}w_{n,j}e_{j}^{\\otimes m}, \\\\\nw_{n,j} &= \\sum_{l}\\theta_{l}1_{j\\le\\lfloor nt_{l}\\rfloor}, \\\\\nN_{n} &= \\lfloor nT\\rfloor.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $|w_{n,j}|\\le\\sum_l|\\theta_l|$, uniformly in $n,j$. For each contraction order $p\\in\\{1,\\ldots,m-1\\}$, expansion of the tensor inner product yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\lVert f_{n}\\otimes_{p}f_{n}\\rVert^{2} \\\\\n&\\quad=a_{m}^{4}/(n\\log n)^{2}\\sum_{i,j,k,l\\le N_{n}}w_{i}w_{j}w_{k}w_{l} \\\\\n&\\qquad r(i-j)^{p}r(k-l)^{p}r(i-k)^{m-p}r(j-l)^{m-p}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n$$\n\nThis expression is nonnegative, but the weights need not be; for the bound take their absolute values. Put $a=p/m$ and $b=(m-p)/m$. By (2), each covariance product is bounded by a constant times the product of the corresponding power weights with exponents $a,a,b,b$. Fix $i,l$ and sum over $j$ and $k$. These are precisely the two convolutions of (4), one with exponents interchanged. Applying (4)-(5) gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert f_{n}\\otimes_{p}f_{n}\\rVert^{2} \\\\\n&\\le C_{T,m,\\theta}N_{n}^{2}/(n\\log n)^{2} \\\\\n&\\le C'_{T,m,\\theta}/(\\log n)^{2}\\to 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n$$\n\nFor $T=0$ or an identically zero linear combination this is trivial; small n with $N_{n}=0$ have no effect. Thus every required nontrivial contraction is controlled explicitly, including for signed Cramér-Wold coefficients.\n\nWe now invoke precisely one Gaussian-limit criterion: the original Nualart–Peccati theorem (primary reference in Section 9), Theorem 1, for a fixed chaos order $m\\ge 2$ on a separable isonormal Gaussian Hilbert space. It states that variance tending to one together with all contractions of orders $1,\\ldots,m-1$ tending to zero implies convergence to a standard normal law. The variance of $I_m(f_n)$ tends, by Section 3 applied to the leading chaos, to\n\n$$\nv=\\sigma^{2}\\sum_{u,v}\\theta_{u}\\theta_{v}\\min(t_{u},t_{v}).\n$$\n\nIf this number is positive, divide $f_{n}$ by its standard deviation and apply the criterion; (7) is preserved. If it is zero, convergence to zero follows directly in $L^{2}$. Cramér-Wold therefore proves the full multivariate Gaussian finite-dimensional limit of the leading chaos. This explicitly excludes a covariance-only argument.\n\n#### 6. All higher chaos terms disappear\n\nFor $q>m$, square summability is stronger than needed: the covariance series itself is summable by (2). Thus\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{E}\\Bigl|b_{n}^{-1}\\sum_{j\\le\\lfloor nt\\rfloor}\\mathrm{He}_{q}(G_{j})\\Bigr|^{2}\\le C_{q,T}/\\log n\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThere are only finitely many $q=m+1,\\ldots,Q$ in the present theorem; orthogonality or the triangle inequality proves their joint finite-dimensional disappearance. Combined with Section 5 this proves convergence of every finite-dimensional distribution of the full response.\n\nThis finite-dimensional argument in fact extends to a centered $L^{2}$ observable of rank $m$: if $B=\\sum_{q>m}q!\\,a_q^{2}<\\infty$, the remainder covariance is bounded by $B\\,r(k)^{m+1}$, which is summable. This observation does NOT by itself extend the path-space theorem to all $L^{2}$ observables; the tightness proof below uses a bounded finite chaos degree.\n\n#### 7. Finite-chaos fourth-moment bounds and functional tightness\n\nWe first justify the needed fourth moments without making a generic $L^{2}$-to-$L^{4}$ assertion. For a fixed order $q$ and symmetric kernel $f$, the Wiener multiplication formula and isometry give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb{E}[I_{q}(f)^{4}] &= \\sum_{p=0}^{q}\\Bigl(p!\\binom{q}{p}^{2}\\Bigr)^{2} \\\\\n&\\quad(2q-2p)!\\,\\lVert\\operatorname{sym}(f\\otimes_{p}f)\\rVert^{2} \\\\\n&\\le D_{q}\\lVert f\\rVert^{4},\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbecause symmetrization is contractive and $\\lVert f\\otimes_p f\\rVert\\le\\lVert f\\rVert^{2}$. Hence $\\lVert I_q(f)\\rVert_4\\le C_q\\lVert I_q(f)\\rVert_2$. For a sum $U$ of chaoses with orders $m,\\ldots,Q$, the triangle inequality and orthogonality imply\n\n$$\n\\lVert U\\rVert_{4}\\le\\sum_{q}C_{q}\\lVert J_{q}U\\rVert_{2}\\le C_{m,Q}\\lVert U\\rVert_{2}.\n\\tag{8}\n$$\n\nThe formula extends from finite tensors by the same bound applied to differences, giving $L^{4}$-Cauchy approximations. All linear combinations of finitely many $Y_{j}$ lie in this fixed finite sum of chaoses.\n\nFrom (2) and the finite Hermite expansion,\n\n$$\n0\\le\\operatorname{Cov}(Y_{i},Y_{j})\\le C_{F}(1+|i-j|)^{-1}.\n$$\n\nConsequently every consecutive block of length $L\\ge 1$ satisfies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb{E}\\Bigl|\\sum Y_{j}\\Bigr|^{2} &\\le C\\,L\\log(eL), \\\\\n\\mathbb{E}\\Bigl|\\sum Y_{j}\\Bigr|^{4} &\\le C\\,[L\\log(eL)]^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{9}\n$$\n\nWe prove the uniform polygonal increment estimate\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{E}|P_{n}(t)-P_{n}(s)|^{4}\\le C_{T,F}|t-s|^{2},\\qquad n\\ge 2.\n\\tag{10}\n$$\n\nLet $\\delta=t-s$. If $\\delta\\ge 1/n$, decompose the increment into a full block and at most two fractional endpoint terms. Minkowski and (9) give its $L^{4}$ norm at most\n\n$$\nC\\sqrt{(n\\delta+2)\\log(e(n\\delta+2))/(n\\log n)}.\n$$\n\nSince $n\\delta\\ge 1$ and $\\delta\\le T$, its fourth power is at most $C_T\\delta^{2}$: the ratio $\\log(e(n\\delta+2))/\\log n$ is bounded uniformly for $n\\ge 2$. If $\\delta<1/n$, at most two mesh cells are involved, and the nonnegative endpoint coefficients sum to $n\\delta$. Therefore its $L^{4}$ norm is at most $\\lVert Y_1\\rVert_4\\,n\\delta/\\sqrt{n\\log n}$, whose fourth power is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\mathbb{E}|Y_{1}|^{4}n^{2}\\delta^{4}/(\\log n)^{2} \\\\\n&\\quad=\\mathbb{E}|Y_{1}|^{4}\\delta^{2}(n\\delta)^{2}/(\\log n)^{2}\\le C\\delta^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis proves (10), including submesh increments. The fourth-moment exponent 2, not a second-moment exponent of 1, yields tightness in $C([0,T])$. Explicitly, for any $\\gamma\\in(0,1/4)$, the union bound on the $2^l$ adjacent dyadic increments of level $l$ bounds the probability that one exceeds $\\epsilon 2^{-l\\gamma}$ by $C\\epsilon^{-4}2^{-l(1-4\\gamma)}$. This is summable uniformly in $n$. Dyadic chaining and continuity give the uniform modulus-of-continuity criterion; $P_n(0)=0$ handles the starting values. Tightness together with Section 6's finite-dimensional convergence proves $P_n\\Rightarrow\\sigma B$ in $C$.\n\nFinally\n\n$$\n\\lVert P_{n}-X_{n}\\rVert_{\\infty}\\le\\max_{j\\le\\lfloor nT\\rfloor+1}|Y_{j}|/\\sqrt{n\\log n}.\n$$\n\nEven second moments suffice for this interpolation bound: stationarity, Markov and the union bound give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&P(\\lVert P_{n}-X_{n}\\rVert_{\\infty}>\\epsilon) \\\\\n&\\quad\\le(\\lfloor nT\\rfloor+1)\\mathbb{E}[Y_{1}^{2}]/(\\epsilon^{2}n\\log n)\\to 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{11}\n$$\n\nThe uniform distance dominates J1 distance. The continuous polygonal convergence therefore transfers to the step process in J1. All asserted functional conclusions follow.\n\n#### 8. Exact boundary of the result\n\nTheorem O.7.2.3f proves Gaussian-response functional convergence. That theorem assumes joint Gaussianity of the response sequence; the present $Y_j=F(G_j)$ has an explicitly nonlinear rank-$m$ response, and Gaussianity of the source does not supply Gaussianity of the response. Theorem O.7.2.3h covers the long-memory range $m(2-2H)<1$; the equality regime is the domain of this theorem. The present equality regime is therefore outside the scopes of both theorems. No claim is made that this classical probabilistic phenomenon is new to the literature; this theorem gives a complete proof for this subclass on the fGn source.\n\nThe finite polynomial assumption is used precisely in (8)-(10). For arbitrary centered $L^{2}$ observables of rank $m$, the same finite-dimensional limit and interpolation estimate follow, but this proof does not establish path tightness: a bound proportional to $|t-s|$ at second-moment order is insufficient for the chaining argument. We do not claim that $L^{2}$ functional convergence is false or that finite degree is logically necessary. It is a sufficient regularity restriction under which the full theorem is proved here.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723j-all-surviving-chaos-short-memory-functional-limit\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3j (All-Surviving-Chaos Short-Memory Functional Limit).**\n\n#### 1. Exact assumptions and result\n\nLet $(G_j)_{j\\in\\mathbb{Z}}$ be a real centered stationary jointly Gaussian sequence with $\\mathbb{E}[G_j^{2}]=1$ and correlation $r(k)=\\mathbb{E}[G_0G_k]$. Fix integers $2\\le m\\le Q$ and a finite probabilists' Hermite polynomial\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\sum_{q=m}^{Q}a_{q}\\mathrm{He}_{q}(x),\\qquad a_{m}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nAssume precisely\n\n$$\nA_{m}:=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb{Z}}|r(k)|^{m}<\\infty.\n\\tag{1}\n$$\n\nPut $Y_j=F(G_j)$, $S_N=\\sum_{j=1}^N Y_j$, and define the step and polygonal processes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX_{n}(t) &= S_{\\lfloor nt\\rfloor}/\\sqrt{n}, \\\\\nP_{n}(t) &= [S_{\\lfloor nt\\rfloor}+(nt-\\lfloor nt\\rfloor)Y_{\\lfloor nt\\rfloor+1}]/\\sqrt{n}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor every finite $T$, as $n\\to\\infty$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{n} &\\Rightarrow\\sigma B\\text{ in }C([0,T])\\text{ with the uniform topology}, \\\\\nX_{n} &\\Rightarrow\\sigma B\\text{ in }D([0,T])\\text{ with the }J_{1}\\text{ topology},\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n$$\n\nwhere B is standard Brownian motion and the exact variance coefficient is\n\n$$\n\\sigma^{2}=\\sum_{q=m}^{Q}q!\\,a_{q}^{2}\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb{Z}}r(k)^{q}.\n\\tag{3}\n$$\n\nEvery series is absolutely convergent. Each $q!\\sum_k r(k)^{q}$ is nonnegative; hence $\\sigma^{2}\\ge 0$. If $\\sigma^{2}=0$ the conclusion means the zero process. For a nondegenerate Brownian limit, the exact additional condition is $\\sigma^{2}>0$. Positivity is proved below for every fractional-Gaussian-noise source in the covered short-memory regime. No independence or mixing assumption is substituted for (1).\n\n#### 2. Isonormal representation, covariances and all surviving orders\n\nTake the closed real Gaussian Hilbert space generated by $G_{j}$ (or the completion of formal vectors $e_{j}$ with $\\langle e_i,e_j\\rangle=r(i-j)$, modulo the null space). It is separable and supports an isonormal process $W$ with $W(e_j)=G_j$ and $\\lVert e_j\\rVert=1$. Write $I_{q}$ for the multiple Wiener integrals normalized by $\\mathbb{E}[I_q(f)I_q(g)]=q!\\langle f,g\\rangle$. Then $\\mathrm{He}_q(G_j)=I_q(e_j^{\\otimes q})$.\n\nSince $|r(k)|\\le1$, assumption (1) implies $A_q=\\sum_k|r(k)|^q\\le A_m$ for every $q\\ge m$. Hermite orthogonality gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Cov}(\\mathrm{He}_{q}(G_{i}),\\mathrm{He}_{p}(G_{j}))=1_{p=q}\\,q!\\,r(i-j)^{q}.\n$$\n\nThus, writing $V_q(N)=\\operatorname{Var}(\\sum_{j=1}^N\\mathrm{He}_q(G_j))$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV_{q}(N)/N &= q!\\sum_{|k|<N}(1-|k|/N)r(k)^{q} \\\\\n&\\to v_{q}:=q!\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb{Z}}r(k)^{q}\\ge 0\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n$$\n\nby dominated convergence; nonnegativity follows because the left side is a variance. Also $V_q(N)\\le q!A_q N$. The full response has covariance\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\gamma(k)=\\sum_{q=m}^{Q}q!\\,a_{q}^{2}r(k)^{q}, \\\\\n&\\sum_{k}|\\gamma(k)|<\\infty, \\\\\n&\\operatorname{Var}(S_{N})/N\\to\\sum_{q}a_{q}^{2}v_{q}=\\sigma^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n$$\n\nAll orders in (3) survive at $\\sqrt{n}$ scale in general; the higher orders are not discarded. Stationarity gives $\\operatorname{Cov}(S_A,S_B)=[V(A)+V(B)-V(|A-B|)]/2$, and (5) yields covariance $\\sigma^{2}\\min(s,t)$ for the normalized processes. The order-$q$ component separately has covariance limit $v_q\\min(s,t)$, and different orders have zero covariance exactly. The Gaussianity of the limit still needs the contractions below.\n\n#### 3. Four-edge tail estimate for each chaos order\n\nFix an order $q\\in\\{m,\\dots,Q\\}$, a contraction order $p\\in\\{1,\\dots,q-1\\}$, and bounded deterministic weights $|w_{n,j}|\\le C_w$ supported on $j=1,\\dots,N_n$, with $N_n\\le C_T n$. Set\n\n$$\nf_{q,n}=n^{-1/2}\\sum_{j=1}^{N_{n}}w_{n,j}e_{j}^{\\otimes q}.\n$$\n\nExpanding the tensor inner product gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert f_{q,n}\\otimes_{p}f_{q,n}\\rVert^{2}\n&\\le C_{w}^{4}/n^{2}\\sum_{i,j,k,l\\le N_{n}} \\\\\n&\\quad|r(i-j)|^{p}|r(k-l)|^{p} \\\\\n&\\quad|r(i-k)|^{q-p}|r(j-l)|^{q-p}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n$$\n\nThis bound permits signed weights and signed correlations. Write $a(k)=|r(k)|^{q}$, $A=\\sum_k a(k)<\\infty$, $A(M)=\\sum_{|k|>M}a(k)$, and $\\lambda=p/q\\in(0,1)$. The summand on the right is\n\n$$\n[a(i-j)a(k-l)]^{\\lambda}[a(i-k)a(j-l)]^{1-\\lambda}.\n$$\n\nSplit the index quadruples into those for which all four edge lengths $|i-j|$, $|k-l|$, $|i-k|$, $|j-l|$ are $\\le M$, and the union of the four edge-tail events. In the first class, fixing $i$ leaves at most $(2M+1)^{2}(4M+1)$ choices for $j,k,l$. Since $|r|\\le 1$, its total contribution is at most $N_n(2M+1)^{2}(4M+1)$.\n\nFor the event $|i-j|>M$, apply Hölder with conjugate exponents $1/\\lambda$ and $1/(1-\\lambda)$ to the full quadruple sum, including this event's indicator. The first powered sum is at most\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\Bigl[\\sum_{i,j:|i-j|>M}a(i-j)\\Bigr]\\Bigl[\\sum_{k,l}a(k-l)\\Bigr] \\\\\n&\\quad\\le N_{n}^{2}A(M)A,\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the second, after dropping the event restriction, is at most $N_n^{2}A^{2}$. Thus this tail contributes at most\n\n$$\nN_{n}^{2}A^{2-\\lambda}A(M)^{\\lambda}.\n\\tag{7}\n$$\n\nThe $|k-l|$ tail has the same bound. The other two edge tails analogously contribute at most $N_n^{2}A^{1+\\lambda}A(M)^{1-\\lambda}$. Overlaps only overcount a nonnegative quantity, which is harmless. Combining (6)-(7),\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert f_{q,n}\\otimes_{p}f_{q,n}\\rVert^{2} \\\\\n&\\le C\\,[(2M+1)^{2}(4M+1)/n \\\\\n&\\qquad+A^{2-\\lambda}A(M)^{\\lambda} \\\\\n&\\qquad+A^{1+\\lambda}A(M)^{1-\\lambda}].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{8}\n$$\n\nFor each fixed $M$, take $\\limsup$ in $n$; then let $M\\to\\infty$. Since $A(M)\\to 0$, every nontrivial contraction tends to zero. The order of the two limits is essential; no unsupported quantitative rate is claimed.\n\n#### 4. Joint Gaussian finite-dimensional convergence across all orders\n\nFix times $t_1,\\dots,t_d$ and real coefficients $\\theta_1,\\dots,\\theta_d$. For each order $q$ consider the one Cramér-Wold component\n\n$$\nU_{q,n}=a_{q}n^{-1/2}\\sum_{j}\\Bigl[\\sum_{l}\\theta_{l}1_{j\\le\\lfloor nt_{l}\\rfloor}\\Bigr]\\mathrm{He}_{q}(G_{j}).\n$$\n\nIts deterministic weights satisfy Section 3's uniform bound, and its variance tends to\n\n$$\na_{q}^{2}v_{q}D_{\\theta},\\qquad D_{\\theta}=\\sum_{i,j}\\theta_{i}\\theta_{j}\\min(t_{i},t_{j})\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nIf this limit is zero, $U_{q,n}\\to 0$ in $L^{2}$. Retain the finitely many orders with a positive limit, normalize each by its own standard deviation, and form the vector of these normalized variables. Its covariance matrix is exactly the identity for every sufficiently large $n$: different chaos orders are orthogonal. Each coordinate is in a fixed Wiener chaos with order at least two. Its nontrivial contractions vanish by (8), unchanged by the bounded normalization factors.\n\nApply the precisely verified multivariate criterion of Nualart–Ortiz-Latorre, Theorem 7, implication $(\\mathrm{iii})\\Rightarrow(\\mathrm{vi})$: under covariance convergence to the identity, fixed-chaos vectors whose coordinate self-contractions all vanish converge jointly to a vector of independent standard normals. This covers different chaos orders. If only one order remains, the univariate Nualart–Peccati criterion applies; if none remain, the sum goes to zero in $L^{2}$. Hence summing the coordinates yields\n\n$$\n\\sum_{q}U_{q,n}\\Rightarrow\\operatorname{Normal}(0,\\sigma^{2}D_{\\theta}).\n$$\n\nCramér-Wold establishes the full multivariate Gaussian finite-dimensional limit with covariance $\\sigma^{2}\\min(s,t)$. This step proves joint distribution convergence, not just univariate limits plus zero covariance. Primary statements and assumptions are recorded in Section 8.\n\n#### 5. Fourth moments, submesh increments and C/J1 tightness\n\nWe use the finite-chaos fourth-moment bound explicitly. The Wiener multiplication formula gives, for each fixed q,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb{E}[I_{q}(f)^{4}] &= \\sum_{p=0}^{q}\\Bigl(p!\\binom{q}{p}^{2}\\Bigr)^{2} \\\\\n&\\quad(2q-2p)!\\,\\lVert\\operatorname{sym}(f\\otimes_{p}f)\\rVert^{2} \\\\\n&\\le D_{q}\\lVert f\\rVert^{4},\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nsince contraction norms are at most $\\lVert f\\rVert^{2}$ and symmetrization is contractive. Consequently $\\lVert I_q(f)\\rVert_4\\le C_q\\lVert I_q(f)\\rVert_2$. The triangle inequality and orthogonality then give $\\lVert U\\rVert_4\\le C_{m,Q}\\lVert U\\rVert_2$ for every $U$ in the sum of chaoses $m,\\dots,Q$. This is justified for arbitrary $L^{2}$ kernels by finite-tensor approximation and the same fourth-moment bound for differences.\n\nFor every consecutive block of $L\\ge 1$ response terms, absolute covariance summability and this bound give\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{E}\\Bigl|\\sum Y_{j}\\Bigr|^{2}\\le C\\,L,\\qquad \\mathbb{E}\\Bigl|\\sum Y_{j}\\Bigr|^{4}\\le C\\,L^{2}.\n\\tag{9}\n$$\n\nWe claim $\\mathbb{E}|P_n(t)-P_n(s)|^{4}\\le C_T|t-s|^{2}$ uniformly in $n$. For $\\delta=t-s\\ge 1/n$, decompose the increment into one full block and at most two fractional endpoints. Minkowski and (9) bound its $L^{4}$ norm by $C\\sqrt{(n\\delta+2)/n}\\le C'\\sqrt{\\delta}$. For $\\delta<1/n$, there are at most two fractional cells with nonnegative weights summing to $n\\delta$; its fourth moment is at most\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\mathbb{E}|Y_{1}|^{4}(n\\delta)^{4}/n^{2} \\\\\n&\\quad=\\mathbb{E}|Y_{1}|^{4}\\delta^{2}(n\\delta)^{2}\\le C\\delta^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{10}\n$$\n\nThus the bound is valid down to submesh scales. Dyadic chaining gives $C$-tightness: for any $0<\\eta<1/4$, Markov and a union bound over the level-$l$ dyadic intervals give the summable bound $C\\epsilon^{-4}2^{-l(1-4\\eta)}$ for any level-$l$ increment exceeding $\\epsilon 2^{-l\\eta}$. Continuity and the geometric chain give the uniform modulus criterion. The initial values vanish. The finite-dimensional limit from Section 4 and this tightness prove the first assertion of (2), including the possible zero-process limit.\n\nFinally\n\n$$\n\\lVert P_{n}-X_{n}\\rVert_{\\infty}\\le n^{-1/2}\\max_{j\\le\\lfloor nT\\rfloor+1}|Y_{j}|.\n$$\n\nFourth moments yield the union bound $C_{T,F}/(\\epsilon^{4}n)\\to 0$ for this distance to exceed $\\epsilon$. No independence is needed. Uniform distance dominates $J_1$ distance, so the polygonal convergence transfers to the step paths. This proves the entire functional theorem.\n\n#### 6. fGn specialization, including variance positivity\n\nTake the exact fGn correlation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&r_{H}(k)=(|k+1|^{2H}-2|k|^{2H}+|k-1|^{2H})/2, \\\\\n&0<H<1-1/(2m).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts centered unit-variance stationary Gaussian realization is already established in original O.7.2.3f, item 4. For $H\\ne 1/2$, Taylor's formula gives $r_H(k)\\sim H(2H-1)|k|^{2H-2}$; the same formula bounds its absolute value by $C(1+|k|)^{2H-2}$. For $H=1/2$ all nonzero-lag correlations vanish. Hence (1) holds exactly throughout this short-memory range.\n\nThe variance in (3) is strictly positive for every nonzero polynomial in the theorem:\n\n- If $H\\ge 1/2$, all correlations are nonnegative, so $\\sum_k r_H(k)^{q}\\ge r_H(0)^{q}=1$ for every $q\\ge m$.\n- If $0<H<1/2$, strict concavity of $x^{2H}$ gives $r_H(k)<0$ for $k\\ge 1$. Telescoping gives\n\n  $$\n  \\sum_{k=1}^N r_H(k)=[(N+1)^{2H}-N^{2H}-1]/2 \\to -1/2.\n  $$\n\n  Also $0<|r_H(k)|<1$ for each $k\\ge 1$ (indeed $<1/2$ from the successive positive decreasing first differences). Therefore $\\sum_{k\\ge 1}|r_H(k)|=1/2$. For even $q$, $\\sum_k r_H(k)^{q}\\ge 1$. For odd $q\\ge 3$,\n\n$$\n  \\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb{Z}}r_{H}(k)^{q}=1-2\\sum_{k\\ge 1}|r_{H}(k)|^{q}>0,\n  $$\n\n  since $|r_H(k)|^{q}<|r_H(k)|$ and the convergent sums have a strict gap already at $k=1$.\n\nSince $a_{m}\\ne 0$, at least one strictly positive contribution appears in (3). Thus every covered fGn observable has a genuinely nondegenerate Brownian functional limit.\n\n#### 7. Same-source finite-polynomial phase diagram and scope boundary\n\nCombining this theorem with the long-range component of Theorem O.7.2.3h and the critical component of Theorem O.7.2.3i gives the complete finite-polynomial response phase diagram for exact fGn, every $m\\ge 2$ and $0<H<1$:\n\n- $m(2-2H)>1$: $\\sqrt{n}$ scaling; Brownian limit with full variance (3), retaining all Hermite orders.\n- $m(2-2H)=1$: $\\sqrt{n\\log n}$ scaling; Brownian limit with variance $2a_m^{2}m![H(2H-1)]^{m}$.\n- $m(2-2H)<1$: $n^{1-m(1-H)}$ scaling; rank-$m$ non-Gaussian Hermite-process limit from the explicit kernel, with higher chaos orders negligible on that scale.\n\nEach branch includes C polygonal and J1 step convergence. This phase diagram is a concrete owner-scope consolidation, not a claim of a new classical probability theorem. The general summable-correlation finite-polynomial theorem above is proved under exactly (1), so no spectral regularity assumption is hidden.\n\nThe finite polynomial restriction is retained: this fourth-moment proof is not a proof of arbitrary-$L^{2}$ short-memory functional tightness. The finite-polynomial functional theorem alone does not supply those other retained physical-sector conclusions.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723k-infinite-chaos-lp-maximal-approximation-and-critical-tail-control\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3k (Infinite-Chaos Lp Maximal Approximation and Critical Tail Control).**\n\n#### 1. Domain and conclusions\n\nLet $(G_{j})$ be a real centered stationary jointly Gaussian sequence, $\\mathbb{E}G_j^{2}=1$, with correlation $r$. Write $\\gamma$ for standard Gaussian measure and $\\mathrm{He}_q$ for probabilists' Hermite polynomials. Fix integer $m\\ge 2$ and real $2<p<\\infty$. Let\n\n$$\nF\\in L^{p}(\\gamma),\\quad F=\\sum_{q\\ge m}a_{q}\\mathrm{He}_{q},\\quad a_{m}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nAll Hermite series in this theorem are initially $L^2$ series. Put $A_d=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb{Z}}|r(k)|^{d}$, possibly infinite. For a response $h$ define $S_k(h)=\\sum_{j=1}^k h(G_j)$, and $P_n(h)$ its polygonal partial-sum process divided by $\\sqrt{n}$. $X_n(h)$ denotes its step counterpart. A finite horizon $T\\ge 0$ is fixed.\n\nThe results are:\n\n1. If $A_m<\\infty$, there is $K_{p,m}<\\infty$, depending only on $p,m$, such that every $h\\in L^p(\\gamma)$ with all Hermite coefficients below $m$ zero satisfies\n\n   $$\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\Bigl\\lVert \\max_{0\\le k\\le N} |S_k(h)| \\Bigr\\rVert_p\\\\\n   &\\quad \\le D_p K_{p,m} \\sqrt{A_m N}\\, \\lVert h\\rVert_p,\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{1}\n   $$\n\n   $$\n   D_p = \\sqrt{2}/(1-2^{1/p-1/2}),\\quad N\\ge 1.\n   $$\n\n   Consequently\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sup_{n\\ge 1}\\lVert\\,\\lVert P_{n}(h)\\rVert_{[0,T]}\\,\\rVert_{p} \\\\\n&\\le D_{p}K_{p,m}\\sqrt{A_{m}(T+1)}\\,\\lVert h\\rVert_{p}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n$$\n\n2. There are finite polynomials $Q_{l}$ with precisely the same Hermite coefficients as $F$ through order $l$ ($l\\ge m$), no coefficients below $m$, and $\\lVert F-Q_l\\rVert_p\\le 2^{-l}$. Thus (2) proves an actual sup-over-all-$n$ approximation bound tending to zero. These are carefully chosen finite polynomials, not necessarily the ordinary Hermite partial sums.\n\n3. In this short-memory domain $P_n(F)$ converges in $C([0,T])$ to $\\sigma B$, and $X_n(F)$ converges in $D([0,T])$, $J_1$, to the same Brownian motion, with\n\n   $$\n   \\sigma^{2} = \\sum_{q\\ge m} q!\\, a_q^{2} \\sum_{k \\in \\mathbb{Z}} r(k)^q.\n   \\tag{3}\n   $$\n\n   This includes $\\sigma=0$. The ordinary Hermite cutoffs $F_{[Q]}=\\sum_{m\\le q\\le Q}a_q\\mathrm{He}_q$ satisfy the separate asymptotic maximal-tail bound\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}P(\\lVert P_{n}(F-F_{[Q]})\\rVert_{[0,T]}>\\epsilon) \\\\\n&\\le 4TA_{m}\\epsilon^{-2}\\sum_{q>Q}q!\\,a_{q}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n$$\n\n4. For exact fGn with $H=1-1/(2m)$, put $c=H(2H-1)$ and $R=F-a_m\\mathrm{He}_m$. Let $P_n^{\\mathrm{crit}}$ and $X_n^{\\mathrm{crit}}$ use denominator $\\sqrt{n\\log n}$, $n\\ge 2$. The whole infinite remainder, without a cutoff, satisfies\n\n   $$\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\bigl\\lVert \\lVert P_n^{\\mathrm{crit}}(R)\\rVert_{[0,T]} \\bigr\\rVert_p\\\\\n   &\\quad \\le D_p K_{p,m+1} \\sqrt{A_{m+1}(T+1)/\\log n}\\, \\lVert R\\rVert_p.\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{5}\n   $$\n\n   Hence $P_n^{\\mathrm{crit}}(F)$ converges in $C$ and $X_n^{\\mathrm{crit}}(F)$ in $J_1$ to $\\sigma_{c}B$, where\n\n$$\n\\sigma_{c}^{2}=2a_{m}^{2}m!\\,[H(2H-1)]^{m}.\n\\tag{6}\n$$\n\nNo differentiability of F is assumed. The Lp integrability is a genuine extra hypothesis beyond L2. Arbitrary-L2 short-memory/critical path convergence is not asserted.\n\n#### 2. The only external analytic import\n\nPrimary source: Nourdin and Nualart (2018). Use Lemmas 2.1–2.2, equation (2.8), and the bound underlying equation (3.4), pp. 6–10. They give the rank-shift representation and bounded Sobolev norms needed below; the ready-made functional theorems are not needed for the proof here.\n\nHere is the import in precise operator form. On the closed subspace $E_{d}$ of $L^p(\\gamma)$ whose coefficients of orders $0,\\dots,d-1$ vanish, set\n\n$$\nT_{d}h=\\sum_{q\\ge d}a_{q}\\mathrm{He}_{q-d}.\n$$\n\nFor $0\\le k\\le d$ the weak derivative map $h\\mapsto(T_d h)^{(k)}$ is bounded $E_d\\to L^p(\\gamma)$; denote its operator norm by $B_{p,d,k}$. The iterated Gaussian divergence satisfies\n\n$$\n\\lVert\\delta^{d}u\\rVert_{p}\\le M_{p,d}\\sum_{k=0}^{d}\\lVert D^{k}u\\rVert_{p;H^{\\otimes(d+k)}}.\n$$\n\nTake $K_{p,d}=M_{p,d}\\sum_{k=0}^d B_{p,d,k}$. These constants are finite, dimension independent, and unrelated to the polynomial degree or correlation sequence. These definitions specify the constants completely without claiming numerical sharpness.\n\nThe source states its shift lemma for exact rank $d$. The same maps on rank at least $d$ follow by adding $\\epsilon\\mathrm{He}_d$ and taking $\\epsilon$ to zero. Alternatively its proof uses only absence of orders below $d$. Its operator estimates are linear in $\\lVert h\\rVert_p$. Boundedness also follows from its all-input domain conclusion and the closed graph theorem: $L^p$ convergence implies $L^2$ convergence; the coefficient formulas identify any graph limit. There is no assumed boundedness of ordinary Hermite partial-sum operators on $L^p$.\n\n#### 3. Derivation of the uniform block bound\n\nRealize $G_i=W(e_i)$ isonormally with $\\lVert e_i\\rVert=1$ and $\\langle e_i,e_j\\rangle=r(i-j)$. For any finite index set $I$ and real weights $w_{i}$ put\n\n$$\nu=\\sum_{i\\in I}w_{i}(T_{d}h)(G_{i})\\,e_{i}^{\\otimes d}.\n$$\n\nThe imported identity gives $\\delta^d u=\\sum_i w_i h(G_i)$. For every $k\\le d$,\n\n$$\nD^{k}u=\\sum_{i}w_{i}(T_{d}h)^{(k)}(G_{i})\\,e_{i}^{\\otimes(d+k)}.\n$$\n\nBy the triangle inequality in $L^{p/2}$ and Holder for products,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert D^{k}u\\rVert_{p;H^{\\otimes(d+k)}}^{2} \\\\\n&\\le B_{p,d,k}^{2}\\lVert h\\rVert_{p}^{2} \\\\\n&\\qquad\\sum_{i,j\\in I}|w_{i}w_{j}|\\,|r(i-j)|^{d+k} \\\\\n&\\le B_{p,d,k}^{2}\\lVert h\\rVert_{p}^{2} \\\\\n&\\qquad\\sum_{i,j\\in I}|w_{i}w_{j}|\\,|r(i-j)|^{d}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese hold first for finite-polynomial approximants and then in the imported Sobolev domain; alternatively each displayed tensor derivative already exists by that domain statement. The finite sum $u$ belongs to $D^{d,p}$, hence to $\\operatorname{Dom}(\\delta^d)$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert\\sum_{i}w_{i}h(G_{i})\\rVert_{p} \\\\\n&\\le K_{p,d}\\lVert h\\rVert_{p} \\\\\n&\\qquad\\Bigl[\\sum_{i,j\\in I}|w_{i}w_{j}|\\,|r(i-j)|^{d}\\Bigr]^{1/2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{7}\n$$\n\nIn particular a consecutive block of L terms has Lp norm at most\n\n$$\nK_{p,d}\\lVert h\\rVert_{p}\\sqrt{L\\,A_{d}}.\n\\tag{8}\n$$\n\nOnly absolute correlations were used. No positivity, mixing, or source spectral regularity is required. Crucially the constant is fixed at d, not at the largest nonzero chaos order.\n\n#### 4. Explicit dyadic maximal inequality\n\nSuppose any sequence $Z_{i}$ has every consecutive length-$L$ block bounded in $L^p$ by $C\\sqrt{L}$. For $M=2^J$, partition $\\{1,\\dots,M\\}$ into its $2^j$ dyadic blocks of length $M/2^j$, for each $j=0,\\dots,J$. Each prefix is a disjoint union of at most one block at each level. Therefore, pathwise,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\max_{k\\le M}\\Bigl|\\sum_{i\\le k}Z_{i}\\Bigr| \\\\\n&\\le\\sum_{j=0}^{J}\\max_{B\\text{ at level }j}\\Bigl|\\sum_{i\\in B}Z_{i}\\Bigr|.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMinkowski and $\\lVert\\max_l|U_l|\\rVert_p\\le(\\sum_l\\lVert U_l\\rVert_p^p)^{1/p}$ give\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert\\max_{k\\le M}\\Bigl|\\sum_{i\\le k}Z_{i}\\Bigr|\\rVert_{p} \\\\\n&\\le C\\sqrt{M}\\sum_{j=0}^{J}2^{-j(1/2-1/p)} \\\\\n&\\le C\\sqrt{M}/(1-2^{1/p-1/2}).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor general $N$ choose the least power of two $M\\ge N$, so $M<2N$ unless $N=1$ (where the same weak bound holds). This proves (1). No independence or martingale property has entered. The restriction $p>2$ is exactly what makes the geometric sum converge.\n\nA polygonal path's absolute value never exceeds the larger absolute value at its two endpoints. Taking $N=\\lceil nT\\rceil$ gives (2), since $N/n\\le T+1$. The zero-horizon case is immediate. Identical maximal bounds hold for step paths.\n\n#### 5. Finite-polynomial approximation without a false Hermite-basis assumption\n\nPolynomials are dense in $L^p(\\gamma)$, $1<p<\\infty$. For completeness, if a dual element $g\\in L^{p'}(\\gamma)$ annihilates all polynomials, its bilateral Laplace transform is entire: Holder bounds $\\int|g(x)|\\exp(a|x|)\\,d\\gamma(x)$ for every finite $a$. All derivatives at zero vanish, so the transform is zero. On the imaginary axis this is the Fourier transform of the finite signed measure $g\\gamma$. Uniqueness implies $g=0$. Hahn–Banach proves density.\n\nFor fixed l define the finite-rank bounded operator\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\Pi_{\\le l}h &= \\sum_{q=0}^{l}\\Bigl[\\int h\\,\\mathrm{He}_{q}\\,d\\gamma/q!\\Bigr]\\mathrm{He}_{q}, \\\\\nC_{p,l} &= \\sum_{q=0}^{l}\\lVert\\mathrm{He}_{q}\\rVert_{p}\\lVert\\mathrm{He}_{q}\\rVert_{p'}/q!.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHolder gives $\\lVert\\Pi_{\\le l}\\rVert_{p\\to p}\\le C_{p,l}<\\infty$. Choose a polynomial $V_{l}$ with\n\n$$\n\\lVert F-V_{l}\\rVert_{p}\\le 2^{-l}/(1+C_{p,l}),\n$$\n\nand set\n\n$$\nQ_{l}=V_{l}+\\Pi_{\\le l}(F-V_{l}).\n$$\n\nThen $Q_{l}$ has all actual coefficients of F through l, and\n\n$$\n\\lVert F-Q_{l}\\rVert_{p}=\\lVert(I-\\Pi_{\\le l})(F-V_{l})\\rVert_{p}\\le 2^{-l}.\n$$\n\nIn particular $F-Q_l$ has no chaos below $l+1$. Applying (2) with the FIXED $d=m$ proves\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\sup_{n\\ge 1}P(\\lVert P_{n}(F)-P_{n}(Q_{l})\\rVert_{[0,T]}>\\epsilon) \\\\\n&\\quad\\le[D_{p}K_{p,m}\\sqrt{A_{m}(T+1)}\\,2^{-l}/\\epsilon]^{p}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{9}\n$$\n\nThis is a uniform-n, infinite-chaos approximation statement. $Q_{l}$ may have adjusted coefficients between l+1 and its finite degree. Confusing it with the canonical cutoff would invalidate the argument.\n\n#### 6. Functional convergence and canonical-cutoff asymptotics\n\nEvery $Q_{l}$ has exact rank m and finite degree. Apply the short-memory finite-polynomial theorem at\n\nTheorem O.7.2.3j.\n\nIts Brownian variance converges to (3). Indeed each chaos weight $s_q=\\sum_k r(k)^{q}$ is nonnegative and at most $A_{m}$ in absolute value, and $F-Q_l$ tends to zero in $L^2$. More explicitly, define a seminorm by $\\lVert h\\rVert_r^{2}=\\sum_{q\\ge m}q!b_q^{2}s_q$. It satisfies $\\lVert h\\rVert_r\\le\\sqrt{A_m}\\lVert h\\rVert_2$, so $|\\sigma(Q_l)-\\sigma(F)|\\le\\sqrt{A_m}\\lVert Q_l-F\\rVert_2$. Brownian laws with these coefficients converge in $C$ by coupling with the same Brownian motion.\n\nThe uniform probability approximation (9), the finite-polynomial functional limits, and this Brownian coupling imply $P_n(F)\\Rightarrow\\sigma(F)B$. One may see this directly on bounded Lipschitz test functions on $C$: split their expectation difference into the approximation term (uniformly small by (2)), the fixed-$l$ finite-polynomial limit, and the Brownian variance difference; take $n$ to infinity and then $l$ to infinity. These test functions determine weak convergence on the Polish space $C([0,T])$.\n\nAlso\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&P(\\lVert P_{n}(F)-X_{n}(F)\\rVert_{[0,T]}>\\epsilon) \\\\\n&\\quad\\le(\\lfloor nT\\rfloor+1)\\lVert F\\rVert_{p}^{p}/(\\epsilon^{p}n^{p/2})\\to 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the step conclusion follows, including $\\sigma=0$.\n\nFor each fixed $Q$ the genuine canonical remainder $R_Q=F-F_{[Q]}$ is still in $L^p$. If it is zero the bound is immediate; otherwise its actual rank $d_{Q}$ is finite and at least $Q+1$, and $A_{d_Q}\\le A_m$, so the theorem just proved applies with rank $d_Q$. Its limiting variance is at most $A_m\\sum_{q>Q}q!a_q^{2}$. The supremum norm is continuous on $C$. Portmanteau for the closed event $\\{\\lVert x\\rVert\\ge\\epsilon\\}$, followed by the $L^2$ Brownian maximal inequality $\\mathbb{E}\\sup_{t\\le T}|B_t|^{2}\\le 4T$, proves (4). Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\lim_{Q\\to\\infty}\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty} \\\\\n&\\quad P(\\lVert P_{n}(R_{Q})\\rVert_{[0,T]}>\\epsilon)=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis last order of limits is stated explicitly. It is not asserted to be a $\\sup_{\\text{all }n}$ bound for raw cutoffs under only $L^p$ integrability.\n\n#### 7. Critical entire-tail disappearance\n\nFor exact critical fGn, the source construction of Theorem O.7.2.3i gives $r(k)\\sim c|k|^{-1/m}$, $0\\le r(k)\\le 1$. Hence $A_{m+1}<\\infty$. The remainder $R=F-a_m\\mathrm{He}_m$ belongs to $L^p$ and has all coefficients below $m+1$ zero. Apply (2) at $d=m+1$ and divide by $\\sqrt{\\log n}$; this proves (5) for every $n\\ge 2$. In particular it controls every higher chaos simultaneously, with no polynomial-degree constants and no exchange of an infinite sum and a maximum.\n\nThe leading polynomial $a_m\\mathrm{He}_m$ has the critical $C/J_1$ Brownian limit from\n\nTheorem O.7.2.3i.\n\nThe probability distance from the full process tends to zero by (5); Slutsky in $C$ and the corresponding step maximal estimate prove (6). Variance convergence follows independently from orthogonality and $\\operatorname{Var}(S_n(R))\\le nA_{m+1}\\lVert R\\rVert_2^{2}$.\n\nAn explicit finite correlation-sum bound is available. The interval formula gives, for $k\\ge 2$,\n\n$$\nr(k)\\le c(k-1)^{-1/m}.\n$$\n\nConsequently\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_{m+1} &\\le 1+2r(1)^{m+1} \\\\\n&\\quad+2c^{m+1}\\zeta(1+1/m), \\\\\nr(1) &= 2^{2H-1}-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis can be inserted directly into (5).\n\nRemark. The cited Nourdin–Nualart preprint's Theorem 5.1 prints a critical variance without the square of the leading Hermite coefficient, despite allowing a general response. We do not import that displayed constant. Equation (6) is inherited from the variance computation of Theorem O.7.2.3i and has the necessary $a_{m}^{2}$ factor. Also the present critical rank $m\\ge 2$ avoids the $m=1$, $H=1/2$ white-noise exception to a logarithmic normalization.\n\n#### 8. Stronger coefficient domain for literal uniform-n Hermite truncations\n\nIf a literal ordinary cutoff with a sup-over-all-$n$ estimate is required, an explicit sufficient assumption is available without the shift-operator import. Suppose $A_m<\\infty$ and, for some $p>2$,\n\n$$\nW_{p}(F)=\\sum_{q\\ge m}|a_{q}|\\sqrt{q!}\\,(p-1)^{q/2}\\sqrt{A_{q}}<\\infty.\n$$\n\nHypercontractivity on each chaos and its block variance bound show that every length-$L$ block of $R_{Q}$ has $L^p$ norm at most $\\sqrt{L}\\,W_p(R_Q)$, where $W_p(R_Q)$ is the corresponding tail sum. Finite sums obey this estimate; the same bound for differences proves $L^p$ convergence to the $L^2$-defined $R_{Q}$. The dyadic proof in Section 4 then yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sup_{n\\ge 1}\\lVert\\,\\lVert P_{n}(R_{Q})\\rVert_{[0,T]}\\,\\rVert_{p} \\\\\n&\\le D_{p}\\sqrt{T+1}\\,W_{p}(R_{Q})\\to 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{10}\n$$\n\nAt critical fGn, assume the same weighted sum only over $q\\ge m+1$. Then (10) applies to higher-order tails with $A_{q}$ finite; dividing by $\\sqrt{\\log n}$ gives the critical bound and its $\\sup_{n\\ge 2}$ version with factor $1/\\sqrt{\\log 2}$. The leading order is retained exactly.\n\nThe hypercontractive inequality used here is $\\lVert I_q(f)\\rVert_p\\le(p-1)^{q/2}\\lVert I_q(f)\\rVert_2$; the same primary paper records it in equation (1.8). $W_p<\\infty$ is stronger than $L^p$ integrability and is explicitly not substituted for the $L^p$ theorem above.\n\n#### 10. Concrete bounded, nonpolynomial rank-two response\n\nLet $\\phi(x)=(2\\pi)^{-1/2}\\exp(-x^{2}/2)$, and\n\n$$\nF(x)=1_{|x|>1}-P(|Z|>1),\\quad Z\\text{ standard normal}.\n$$\n\nThis centered bounded even function belongs to every finite $L^p(\\gamma)$, has no odd Hermite coefficients, and\n\n$$\na_{2}=(1/2)\\mathbb{E}[1_{|Z|>1}(Z^{2}-1)]=\\phi(1)>0.\n$$\n\nThe last equality follows by integrating $(x^{2}-1)\\phi(x)=-(x\\phi(x))'$ over $[1,\\infty)$ and using symmetry. Thus its exact rank is two and it is not a finite polynomial. The short-memory result applies to every exact fGn $H<3/4$, retaining the full variance series. At $H=3/4$, (6) specializes to\n\n$$\n\\sigma_{c}^{2}=9/(32\\pi e).\n$$\n\nThe whole infinite higher-chaos remainder is bounded by (5). This example verifies that the Lp extension genuinely adds discontinuous nonpolynomial observables, without imposing differentiability or an exponential Hermite-coefficient assumption. It remains a response to an exact Gaussian source; the common physical-source realization is supplied separately below.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723l-one-correlated-driver-for-state-dependence-and-quantum-histories\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3l (One Correlated Driver for State Dependence and Quantum Histories).**\n\nAll times below are in the registered physical unit $\\tau_0=1$ second. Changing that unit transports every rate and control coefficient; it is not a second implementation clock. The literal quantitative example has $T=1$. The arguments hold on any fixed finite $T$ with the displayed $T$-dependent constants.\n\n#### 1. Retained functional classification and the new task\n\nFor exact fractional Gaussian noise $G_{j}$ with covariance\n\n$$\nr_H(k)=(|k+1|^{2H}-2|k|^{2H}+|k-1|^{2H})/2,\n$$\n\nlet $F=\\sum_{q\\ge m}a_q\\mathrm{He}_q$ be centered and have exact Hermite rank $m\\ge 2$. Theorems O.7.2.3h–O.7.2.3k imply:\n\n- If $\\sum_k|r_H(k)|^{m}<\\infty$ and $F$ is in $L^p$ for some $p>2$, the polygonal partial sums divided by $\\sqrt{n}$ converge in $C$ to $\\sigma_{F}W$. All Hermite orders enter $\\sigma_F^{2}=\\sum_{q\\ge m}q!\\,a_q^{2}\\sum_k r_H(k)^{q}$. This includes $H<1-1/(2m)$.\n- At $H=1-1/(2m)$, $F\\in L^p$, $p>2$, division by $\\sqrt{n\\log n}$ gives Brownian variance $2a_m^{2}m![H(2H-1)]^{m}$. The complete higher-chaos tail vanishes in maximal $L^p$ at $O((\\log n)^{-1/2})$.\n- If $H>1-1/(2m)$, $F\\in L^2$, division by $n^h$, $h=1-m(1-H)>1/2$, gives the non-Gaussian order-$m$ Hermite process with the explicit common-Wiener kernel in the proof of Theorem O.7.2.3h. Step versions converge in $J_1$ to the same continuous limits.\n\nNo Gaussian functional work is repeated here. In particular the first two limits can be Gaussian even though the microscopic response $F(G)$ is non-Gaussian; the last limit is genuinely non-Gaussian. Dependence and long memory are source properties, not labels inferred from a diffusion covariance.\n\nThe following results turn these driver limits into one joint source/quantum/state-dependent continuum family. Section 2 gives an explicit, independent convergence rate for a concrete long-memory member; subsequent sections do not require a rate to transfer the other already proved C limits.\n\n#### 2. Quantitative common-noise Rosenblatt witness\n\nFix $m=2$, $H=7/8$, $a=2-2H=1/4$, $h=2H-1=3/4$, $c=H(2H-1)=21/32$, and $F(z)=z^{2}-1$. Let $P_{n}$ be its polygonal partial-sum path divided by $n^{3/4}$. The accepted white-noise construction realizes $P_{n}$ and its Rosenblatt limit $Z=I_2(K)$ on one probability space. It has\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb{E}|Z(t)-Z(s)|^{2} &= (147/64)|t-s|^{3/2},\\\\\n\\operatorname{Var} Z(1) &= 147/64,\\\\\n\\mathbb{E}\\, Z(1)^3 / \\operatorname{Var}(Z(1))^{3/2} &= (4\\sqrt{3}/5)\\, \\mathrm{Beta}(3/4,3/4)>0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Claim O.7.2.3l.1.** On that explicit coupling, there is a finite computable $C_{T}$ with\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{E}\\, \\lVert P_n-Z\\rVert_{[0,T]} \\le C_T n^{-1/12}.\n$$\n\nThe slower exponent is a conservative uniform-path rate, not a claim of optimality.\n\n**Proof of the pointwise estimate.** Use the exact kernels $K_{n,t}$ from the proof of Theorem O.7.2.3h. At a mesh endpoint $t=k/n$, put $q(u,v)=|u-v|^{-a}$ on $[0,t]^{2}$ and let $Q_1,Q_2$ be averaging projections in the two variables onto $n$-mesh intervals. They are commuting orthogonal projections in $L^2$. The inner-product identities of that proof give\n\n$$\n\\lVert K_{n,t}-K_t\\rVert^{2}/c^{2}=\\lVert Q_1Q_2q\\rVert^{2}+\\lVert q\\rVert^{2}-2\\lVert Q_1q\\rVert^{2}.\n$$\n\nSymmetry of $q$ gives $\\lVert Q_1(I-Q_2)q\\rVert=\\lVert(I-Q_1)Q_2q\\rVert$. Decomposing $q$ into the four orthogonal projection components therefore reduces the right side exactly to\n\n$$\n\\lVert(I-Q_1)(I-Q_2)q\\rVert^{2}\\le\\lVert(I-Q_1)q\\rVert^{2}.\n\\tag{1}\n$$\n\nThis cancellation is the point of the common-noise coupling. Merely comparing the two variances would not prove (1).\n\nFor $0<a<1/2$, the whole-line translation identity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\int_{\\mathbb{R}}(|z|^{-a}-|z-u|^{-a})^{2}\\,dz=J_a|u|^{1-2a},\\\\\n&J_a=\\int_{\\mathbb{R}}(|z|^{-a}-|z-1|^{-a})^{2}\\,dz<\\infty.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrability follows near 0 and 1 from $2a<1$ and in the tails from the mean-value estimate $a(|z|-1)^{-a-1}$. For example\n\n$$\nJ_a \\le 8\\cdot 3^{1-2a}/(1-2a)+2a^{2}/(2a+1)\n$$\n\nis a conservative explicit bound. Jensen's inequality within each averaging cell, then integration in $v$, bounds $\\lVert(I-Q_1)q\\rVert^{2}$ by $2TJ_a n^{-(1-2a)}$. The Wiener isometry and (1) give\n\n$$\n\\sup_{\\text{mesh } t\\le T}\\lVert I_2(K_{n,t})-Z(t)\\rVert_2\\le C_T n^{-(1/2-a)}.\n$$\n\nAn arbitrary $t$ differs from its preceding mesh endpoint by at most $1/n$. The polygonal extra term has $L^2$ norm at most $\\sqrt{2}\\,n^{-h}$, and the $Z$ increment has $L^2$ norm at most $\\sqrt{147/64}\\,n^{-h}$. Consequently\n\n$$\n\\sup_{t\\le T} \\lVert P_n(t)-Z(t)\\rVert_2 \\le C_T n^{-1/4}.\n\\tag{2}\n$$\n\n**Uniform-path upgrade.** Both $P_{n}$ and $Z$ satisfy the uniform second-moment increment bound $\\lVert X(t)-X(s)\\rVert_2\\le C_T|t-s|^h$. For a dyadic partition with $K=2^L$ intervals, the expected maximum oscillation remaining below that grid is bounded by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\sum_{l\\ge L} \\Bigl[\\sum_{\\text{adjacent level-}l\\text{ intervals}} \\mathbb{E}|\\Delta X|^{2}\\Bigr]^{1/2}\\\\\n&\\quad \\le C_T \\sum_{l\\ge L} 2^{-l(h-1/2)}\\\\\n&\\quad \\le C'_T K^{-(h-1/2)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n$$\n\nContinuity and dyadic chaining justify the supremum; a general pair in one grid interval uses two chains, absorbed in $C'_T$. This is an expectation bound, not a merely pointwise Kolmogorov statement. At the $K+1$ grid points, (2) gives expected maximal difference at most $C_T\\sqrt{K+1}\\,n^{-1/4}$. Equations (2)–(3) imply\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{E}\\lVert P_n-Z\\rVert \\le C_T[\\sqrt{K+1}\\,n^{-1/4}+K^{-1/4}].\n$$\n\nChoose dyadic $K$ between $n^{1/3}$ and $2n^{1/3}$. Both terms are $O(n^{-1/12})$. This proves the theorem. All constants come from the displayed translation integral, covariance and geometric sums. ∎\n\nThis witness has stationary dependent increments and a non-Gaussian long-memory limit. The independent scale-mixture countermodel in the accepted proof has the same covariance but a different limit, so nothing here promotes this rate to covariance-only universality.\n\n#### 3. A finite correlated source with explicit errors\n\nSet $N=\\lceil nT\\rceil+1$. The $N$-vector $(G_1,\\dots,G_N)$ has computable strictly positive covariance for $H=7/8$. Positivity also follows directly from its interval white-noise kernels: on the last interval's rightmost support only the last kernel is nonzero, giving linear independence inductively. Finite box probabilities can therefore be evaluated to certified accuracy by integration of the computable nondegenerate Gaussian density, with Gaussian tail bounds. No arbitrary-real probability oracle is used.\n\nUse a rational cap $R_n\\ge\\sqrt{12\\log(n+2)}$, with $R_n\\le\\sqrt{12\\log(n+2)}+1$, and rational mesh $\\eta_n\\le n^{-4}/(1+R_n)$. Partition $[-R_n,R_n]^N$ into finitely many rational boxes; pick one rational representative in each, and add one overflow label whose declared driver is zero. Let $K_{n}$ be the number of nonoverflow boxes. Compute a certified enclosing interval $[l_j,u_j]$ for every exact box mass, with $0\\le l_j\\le p_j\\le u_j$ and width at most $2^{-b_n}$. Put $q_j=2^{-b_n}\\lfloor 2^{b_n}l_j\\rfloor$, so $0\\le p_j-q_j\\le 2^{1-b_n}$, and put all leftover mass in the overflow label. Choose $b_{n}$ so $2K_n2^{-b_n}\\le n^{-4}$. Certified interval integration constructs these enclosures and terminates; this lower-endpoint rule needs no decision about an exact dyadic boundary. A $b_n$-bit fair integer and the finite cumulative table sample $q$ exactly.\n\nOn the natural coupling, the table disagreement probability is at most $n^{-4}$; Gaussian overflow is at most $2N\\exp(-R_n^{2}/2)\\le 2N(n+2)^{-6}$. On their complement every coordinate error is $\\le\\eta_n$, so\n\n$$\n\\lVert P_n-\\hat{P}_n\\rVert\\le n^{-h}N\\eta_n(2R_n+\\eta_n).\n\\tag{4}\n$$\n\nFor the bounded uniform metric $d(x,y)=\\min(1,\\lVert x-y\\rVert)$, this proves\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\mathbb{E}\\, d(P_n,\\hat P_n)\\\\\n&\\quad \\le n^{-h}N \\eta_n(2R_n+\\eta_n)+n^{-4}+2N(n+2)^{-6}\\\\\n&\\quad = O_T(n^{-4}) + O_T(n^{-3-h}).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n$$\n\nThe finite table can be enormous; its full length, seed, workspace and retained future memory are counted. Future correlated increments are stored privately and released on the registered clock. This is a causal output source with a paid prehistory/preparation, not a memoryless source or a free Gaussian oracle. The intended latent source is classical, so sampling its seed is appropriate. It is not a substitute for coherently implementing the quantum instrument below.\n\nThe same finite-box construction works for any computable finite Gaussian covariance and computable continuous $F$ with a supplied compact modulus and tail bound. Approximation of a merely measurable $L^p$ response instead needs its actual effective $L^p$ approximation data; that is not silently inferred from membership in $L^p$. The witness $F=z^{2}-1$ has all data explicitly.\n\n#### 4. Continuous quantum source driven by that same path\n\nLet the system have finite dimension $d$. Fix Hermitian $B$, a bounded globally Lipschitz Hermitian $A(x)$, and finitely many bounded globally Lipschitz jump matrices $L_e(x)$, $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$. Write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU_x(t) &= \\exp(-i B x(t)),\\\\\n\\tilde A_x(t) &= U_x(t)^* A(x(t)) U_x(t),\\\\\n\\tilde L_{e,x}(t) &= U_x(t)^* L_e(x(t)) U_x(t),\\\\\n\\tilde D_x(t) &= \\sum_e \\tilde L_{e,x}(t)^*\\tilde L_{e,x}(t).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor each continuous path $x$ with $x(0)=0$, solve the bounded matrix ODE\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\partial_t G_x(t,s) &= (-i \\tilde A_x(t)-\\tilde D_x(t)/2)G_x(t,s),\\\\\nG_x(s,s) &= I.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIts time-ordered integral series converges. Its Hermitian part is negative semidefinite, hence $G_{x}$ is a contraction. Define the common ordered-time record space\n\n$$\nR_T=\\mathbb{C}|\\mathrm{empty}\\rangle\\oplus\\bigoplus_{m\\ge 1,e_1,\\dots,e_m}L^2(\\{0<t_1<\\dots<t_m<T\\}).\n$$\n\nFor $h=(e_1,t_1,\\dots,e_m,t_m)$, set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_x(h) &= U_x(T)G_x(T,t_m)\\tilde L_{e_m,x}(t_m)G_x(t_m,t_{m-1}) \\cdots\\\\\n&\\quad \\tilde L_{e_2,x}(t_2)G_x(t_2,t_1)\\tilde L_{e_1,x}(t_1)G_x(t_1,0),\\\\\nC_x(\\mathrm{empty}) &= U_x(T)G_x(T,0),\\\\\n(V_x u)(h) &= C_x(h)u.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n$$\n\nThe $m=0$ and $m\\ge 1$ sectors use counting mass one and ordinary ordered-simplex measure respectively; there is no additional factorial in that measure.\n\n**Claim O.7.2.3l.2.** $V_{x}$ is an isometry. Its partial trace is the finite-dimensional, pathwise completely positive evolution in the rotating frame, transported back by $U_{x}$. Every monitored history has the actual unnormalized instrument $C_x(h)\\rho C_x(h)^*$, including arbitrary initial coherences and arbitrary spectator systems. Moreover $V_{x}$ is globally Lipschitz in the uniform path norm, with an explicit finite constant.\n\n**Proof of isometry.** Let $l_e=\\sup_x\\lVert L_e(x)\\rVert$ and $\\ell=\\sum_e l_e^{2}$. The sum of squared coefficient-norm majorants is\n\n$$\n\\sum_m(\\ell T)^m/m!=\\exp(\\ell T).\n$$\n\nThis dominates the history series and its integral equation. Splitting at the final jump yields the usual variation-of-constants equation with gain $\\sum\\tilde{L}\\rho\\tilde{L}^*$ and loss $-\\{\\tilde{D},\\rho\\}/2$. Its trace derivative is zero. Thus $\\sum_h\\int C_x(h)^*C_x(h)=I$, and $V_{x}$ is an isometry. Positivity, complete positivity and spectator extension follow from the displayed actual dilation, not from a classical simulation.\n\n**Explicit stability constant.** Put $b=\\lVert B\\rVert$, $a_0=\\sup\\lVert A\\rVert$, $a_1=\\operatorname{Lip}(A)$, $l_{1,e}=\\operatorname{Lip}(L_e)$, and omit identically zero channels. Define\n\n$j_e=l_{1,e}+2b\\,l_e$,\n$j=\\max_e j_e/l_e$ (set $j=0$ if every jump channel is identically zero),\n$g=a_1+2b\\,a_0+\\sum_e l_e j_e$,\n$c_0=b+gT$.\n\nUnitary conjugation gives the indicated Lipschitz constants for the tilded coefficients; in particular $\\operatorname{Lip}(\\tilde{D})\\le 2\\sum l_e j_e$. Duhamel between two contractive no-jump propagators bounds their difference over $[s,t]$ by $g(t-s)\\lVert x-y\\rVert$. Telescoping each history product, then the final $U$, gives\n\n$$\n\\lVert C_x(h)-C_y(h)\\rVert\\le\\lVert x-y\\rVert(c_0+mj)\\prod_k l_{e_k}.\n$$\n\nAfter squaring and summing ordered histories,\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\lVert V_x-V_y\\rVert\\le C_V\\lVert x-y\\rVert, \\\\\nC_V=\\exp(\\ell T/2)\\cdot\\sqrt{(c_0+j\\ell T)^{2}+j^{2}\\ell T}.\n\\end{gathered}\n\\tag{7}\n$$\n\nThe Poisson exponential identities for sum m and sum $m^{2}$ give this exact bound. It is finite and independent of the number of ticks, the source path variation or any spectator dimension. This proof needs only continuity of x; it neither differentiates a Rosenblatt/Brownian path nor assumes bounded variation. ∎\n\nFor any $\\rho$, including a joint system–spectator state,\n\n$$\n\\lVert V_x\\rho V_x^*-V_y\\rho V_y^*\\rVert_1\\le 2\\min(1,C_V\\lVert x-y\\rVert).\n\\tag{8}\n$$\n\nThe same bound holds in diamond norm for their output channels. Measuring, discarding or retaining any fixed part of the common record is contractive.\n\n#### 5. The actual finite quantum instrument and a single refinement\n\nFor each finite sampled $\\hat{P}_n$, subdivide its $n$-mesh intervals into $n^{2}$ equal parts. The quantum tick mesh is $\\Delta_n=n^{-3}$, with an endpoint shortening/padding for general $T$. At tick $k$ use frozen tilded coefficients at its left endpoint and the complete instrument\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nK_{0,k} &= \\exp(-i \\tilde A_k \\Delta_k)\\sqrt{I-\\Delta_k \\tilde D_k},\\\\\nK_{e,k} &= \\sqrt{\\Delta_k}\\,\\tilde L_{e,k}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{9}\n$$\n\nChoose the finitely many small $n$ separately or enlarge the subdivision so $\\Delta_k\\lVert\\tilde{D}_k\\rVert\\le 1/2$. $\\sum K^*K=I$ exactly. Give every tick a fresh outcome register. The isometry $\\sum_\\alpha K_{\\alpha,k}\\otimes|\\alpha\\rangle$ extends to a finite unitary, which is implemented coherently, not by measuring the unknown input state. Apply the final $U_{\\hat{P}_n}(T)$. Intermediate physical-frame outputs apply the corresponding $U$ at the observed time; all conditional states and future coefficients can be equivalently transported in that declared frame.\n\nEmbed a discrete $m$-event record into $R_{T}$ as $\\Delta_{k_1}^{-1/2}\\cdots\\Delta_{k_m}^{-1/2}$ times the indicator of its distinct occupied cells, in its actual ordered label sector. This is an isometry. It is a comparison of discrete cell records with the continuous output space, not a claim to have physically measured a subcell event time.\n\nFor the bounded piecewise-frozen coefficients used here, the complete-record overlap estimate is as follows: each cell's vacuum expansion agrees to $O(\\Delta^{2})$, its one-jump amplitudes agree to first order, and its cross-isometry overlap is $I+O(\\Delta^{2})$. Telescoping contractive cross maps therefore gives\n\n$$\n\\lVert V_{\\text{frozen discrete}}-V_{\\text{frozen continuous}}\\rVert\\le C\\sqrt{T\\max\\Delta}.\n\\tag{10}\n$$\n\nFor completeness, the one-cell overlap is the vacuum term $I-\\Delta D+O(\\Delta^{2})$ plus the one-jump term $\\Delta\\sum L_e^*L_e+O(\\Delta^{2})$; the order-$\\Delta$ terms cancel. The full overlap differs from $I$ by at most $C\\sum\\Delta^{2}$, and the squared isometry difference is bounded by twice that. Only uniform coefficient bounds enter $C$.\n\nFreezing the polygonal path changes it by at most\n\n$$\n\\omega_{\\hat P_n}(\\Delta_n) \\le n^{-h-2} \\max_j |F(\\hat G_j)|.\n$$\n\nEquation (7), interpreted also for bounded step coefficient paths, bounds the resulting continuous-record difference by $C_{V}$ times this modulus. The quantized cap gives the deterministic bound $n^{-h-2}(R_n^{2}+1)$. Alternatively its untruncated expected bound is $O_T(n^{-h-3/2})$. The endpoint output $U$ uses the exact endpoint value; a final-cell mismatch is bounded the same way. Complete finite control errors are allocated so their isometry operator norms sum to at most $n^{-4}$. To compare the actual finite-precision leakage literally on one Hilbert space, adjoin to $R_{T}$ a common separable orthogonal failure-record space with basis labels ($n$, finite failure/output word), and embed the ideal $V_{x}$ with zero failure component. Every finite physical padding/input-flag failure output embeds there by a fixed basis map. All isometry norms in (11) and all joint tests in (12) use this augmented common space; the ideal history formulas are unchanged.\n\nThus, for the literal quantized witness, conditional on the sampled driver,\n\n$$\n\\lVert\\hat{V}_n-V_{\\hat{P}_n}\\rVert\\le C_T n^{-3/2}+C_V n^{-h-2}(R_n^{2}+1)+n^{-4}.\n\\tag{11}\n$$\n\nAll gates, outcomes, coefficients and their clock subwindows in (9) belong to the same finite program. There is no separate unaccounted Gaussian simulator that replaces quantum off-diagonal dynamics.\n\n**Claim O.7.2.3l.3 (Joint Convergence).** Let $F_{\\mathrm{test}}(x)$ be a bounded operator-valued test on the common system–record–spectator output, with $\\lVert F_{\\mathrm{test}}\\rVert\\le 1$ and $\\operatorname{Lip}(F_{\\mathrm{test}})\\le 1$ in the bounded uniform source metric. The actual finite joint source/output experiment and the continuum experiment driven by $Z$ satisfy\n\n$$\n|\\mathbb{E}\\operatorname{Tr}[F_{\\mathrm{test}}(\\hat{P}_n)\\hat{V}_n\\rho\\hat{V}_n^*]-\\mathbb{E}\\operatorname{Tr}[F_{\\mathrm{test}}(Z)V_Z\\rho V_Z^*]|\\le C_T(1+2C_V)n^{-1/12}+O_T(n^{-3/2})+O_T(n^{-4}).\n\\tag{12}\n$$\n\nTo prove this, couple $P_n,Z$ by Claim O.7.2.3l.1, couple $\\hat{P}_n,P_n$ by (5), then use (8), (11) and the test's Lipschitz bound. On the rare box/table failures use the trivial bound 2; no unbounded-tail moment is omitted. Arbitrary spectators are already covered. This establishes a quantitative common formal continuum realization in a specified weak-source/trace-norm-output topology. It does not claim total-variation convergence of atomic source laws to a continuous Gaussian law.\n\nFor the other retained driver classes, the same proof transfers their $C$ functional convergence by the bounded continuous mapping $x\\mapsto V_x$. It supplies their finite-source/control error separately from any unproved numerical rate for the inherited probability theorem.\n\n#### 6. State-dependent sectors and the exact quantum off-diagonal witness\n\n#### 6.1 General finite state dependence\n\nFor a path x, the monitored quantum jump rate is\n\n$$\n\\lambda_e(t,\\rho)=\\operatorname{Tr}[L_e(x(t))^*L_e(x(t))\\rho],\n$$\n\nand the positive-rate jump update is $L_e\\rho L_e^*/\\lambda_e$, in the physical frame. Between outcomes the unnormalized state follows (6). Zero-rate outcomes never occur; choose any normalized update there without changing a law. This is true state dependence, including pure/mixed states and rate zeros.\n\nIf $B$ and $A$ are diagonal and $L_{ij}(x)=a_{ij}(x)|j\\rangle\\langle i|$, the diagonal density sector is invariant. Its conditional population source has edge rate $|a_{ij}(x(t))|^{2}$ at current state $i$. Amplitudes may be bounded Lipschitz and vanish on whole regions. For a Brownian driver $\\theta=\\sigma W$ the joint state $(\\theta,i)$ is Markov with generator\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nGf(\\theta,i) &= (\\sigma^{2}/2)\\partial_\\theta^{2} f(\\theta,i)\\\\\n&\\quad +\\sum_j |a_{ij}(\\theta)|^{2}[f(\\theta,j)-f(\\theta,i)].\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{13}\n$$\n\nFor a Hermite driver the same conditional source exists, but the driver memory is retained; no false autonomous Markov reduction on (theta,i) is asserted. The actual finite source uses the same q-table driver and the same (9) instruments in both cases.\n\n#### 6.2 A rank-changing diffusion on the same driver\n\nRetain $Y=\\sin(\\theta)$, $\\theta=\\sigma W$, $\\theta(0)=0$. Ito's formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&dY=\\sigma\\cos(\\theta)\\,dW-(\\sigma^{2}/2)Y\\,dt,\\\\\n&\\quad d[Y]_t=\\sigma^{2}(1-Y^{2})\\,dt.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the projected generator is\n\n$$\nAf(y)=(\\sigma^{2}/2)[(1-y^{2})f''(y)-y f'(y)],\\quad y \\in [-1,1].\n\\tag{14}\n$$\n\nThis is genuinely rank-changing: its second-order coefficient vanishes at both endpoints and is positive in the interior. The boundary law is fixed by the actual sine projection, not supplied by choosing an arbitrary extension of (14). It is Markov because any two angles with the same sine differ by the sine-preserving circle symmetries, and Brownian transition laws respect those symmetries. Equivalently $\\mathbb{E}[f(\\sin(\\theta+\\sigma W_t))]$ is constant on every sine fiber, so Chapman–Kolmogorov descends. Its paths are continuous and it has no retained jump kernel.\n\n**Actual Kramers–Moyal/no-jump certificate for this branch.** Choose the literal short-memory supplier $H=1/2$ and $F(G)=G^{2}-1$, so $\\xi=G^{2}-1$ are iid, $\\mathbb{E}\\xi=0$, $\\mathbb{E}\\xi^{2}=2$ and $\\mathbb{E}\\xi^{4}=60$. For $g(\\theta)=f(\\sin(\\theta))$, $f\\in C^{3}([-1,1])$, Taylor expansion gives\n\n$$\n|n\\mathbb{E}[g(\\theta+\\xi/\\sqrt{n})-g(\\theta)]-g''(\\theta)|\\le(28/(6\\sqrt{n}))[\\lVert f'''\\rVert+3\\lVert f''\\rVert+\\lVert f'\\rVert].\n\\tag{15a}\n$$\n\nHere $\\mathbb{E}|\\xi|^{3}\\le\\mathbb{E}(G^{2}+1)^{3}=28$, and $g''=(1-y^{2})f''-yf'$. Thus the finite lifted source has an explicit vanishing higher-order response residual and the limiting generator (14) with $\\sigma^{2}=2$, including its actual coefficient and endpoint rank change. The finite sine projection need not itself be Markov; (15a) shows that its possible lift dependence is within the stated uniform remainder. Moreover\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&P\\Bigl(\\max_{j\\le nT+1}|\\xi_j|/\\sqrt{n}>\\epsilon\\Bigr)\\\\\n&\\quad \\le 60(nT+1)/(\\epsilon^{4} n^{2}),\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso no retained macroscopic jump remains. The real finite dyadic source differs in complete path law by (5), with the Brownian normalization $h=1/2$ and its corresponding source precision. This is a source-level $M_{\\mathrm{Markov}}/R_{\\mathrm{KM}}/N_{\\mathrm{jump}}$ certificate on the recorded smooth-test/finite-protocol quotient, not just an appeal to complete positivity.\n\nOn the non-Gaussian branch $Y=\\sin(Z)$, continuity follows from the same path map. Since the fixed order-two chaos has moments of every finite order, its increment moments and hypercontractivity give Holder regularity below $h=3/4$. Hence it also solves the pathwise Young relation $dY=\\cos(Z)\\,dZ$ on the lifted angle carrier. This is a continuous non-Markov-memory branch, not the local diffusion (14), and the sign/lift information is not discarded when using that relation. State-dependent nonlinear output does not erase its source memory.\n\n#### 6.3 One literal coherence/no-jump calculation\n\nTake $B=\\operatorname{diag}(1/2,-1/2)$, $A=0$ and $L=\\sqrt{r}|1\\rangle\\langle 0|$. The jump and $B$ do not commute. Conditional on $x$, the exact solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\rho_{00}(t) &= e^{-rt}\\rho_{00}(0),\\\\\n\\rho_{11}(t) &= \\rho_{11}(0)+(1-e^{-rt})\\rho_{00}(0),\\\\\n\\rho_{01}(t) &= e^{-rt/2}e^{-i x(t)}\\rho_{01}(0).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{15}\n$$\n\nThe covariance of this dissipator under $U_{x}$ makes (15) a direct instance of (6), not an additional random-phase ansatz. The vacuum/no-jump matrix and probability are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nK^{\\mathrm{empty}}_x(t) &= U_x(t)\\operatorname{diag}(e^{-rt/2},1),\\\\\np^{\\mathrm{empty}}(t) &= \\rho_{00}(0)e^{-rt}+\\rho_{11}(0).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe selected normalized state is $K^{\\mathrm{empty}}\\rho(K^{\\mathrm{empty}})^*/p^{\\mathrm{empty}}$. It is nonlinear as a function of the prior state, with survival-weighted mixture rule, while the unnormalized instrument and full channel remain affine and CP. The off-diagonal source response survives exactly in that normalized state.\n\nFor the state-dependent version $L(x)=\\sqrt{r}\\sin(x)|1\\rangle\\langle 0|$, replace $rt$ everywhere by $R_x(t)=r\\int_0^t\\sin^{2}(x(s))\\,ds$. The jump intensity can vanish and the same path simultaneously changes survival and coherence. This is a genuinely joined memory/state-dependent/off-diagonal witness, stronger than a product of independent sectors.\n\nAveraging (15) gives coherence $e^{-rt/2}\\mathbb{E}[e^{-iZ(t)}]\\rho_{01}(0)$. For a Brownian driver with variance $\\sigma^{2}t$ this is $e^{-(r+\\sigma^{2})t/2}\\rho_{01}(0)$, and the averaged quantum generator is the amplitude-damping GKSL generator minus $(\\sigma^{2}/2)[B,[B,\\cdot]]$. For a nonzero Hermite driver with exponent $h>1/2$, $\\mathbb{E}[e^{-ikZ(t)}]=1-(k^{2}\\operatorname{Var}Z(1)/2)t^{2h}+o(t^{2h})$. Its derivative at zero is zero but it is not identically one near zero. Thus it cannot satisfy the continuous multiplicative semigroup identity in $t$. Even this finite-dimensional coherence sector therefore retains non-Markov memory; calling its average a time-homogeneous GKSL semigroup would be false. The source's nonzero third cumulant additionally produces a phase response that Gaussian covariance data miss.\n\n#### 7. Retention, genuine no-jump exclusion and response-null quotient\n\n#### 7.1 Exact commuting reductions\n\nAt every finite stage retain the tuple (source label/decoded path, quantum output, recorded history). Scalar retention maps the same label to $\\sin(\\hat{P}_n)$. Quantum retention traces the source memory only when the declared protocol does not read it. Record retention applies the chosen history projection/partial trace. Their continuum maps use $(x,V_x)$ and $\\sin(x)$. Equations (5), (8), (11) show that each reduction commutes with passage to the limit, with no added error beyond its contraction/Lipschitz constant. These maps are source identities, not equalities inferred from similar covariances.\n\nIf a protocol reads both Y and quantum coherences, their correlations are retained: the conditional coherence in (15) uses the very same x that determines Y. Replacing this joint law by the product of its marginals is not an allowed retention map.\n\n#### 7.2 Two different statements involving 'no jump'\n\n(a) Vacuum selection is the instrument event $h=\\mathrm{empty}$. For general coefficients let $D_{\\mathrm{max}}=\\sup_x\\lVert\\sum L_e(x)^*L_e(x)\\rVert$. Along the no-jump evolution,\n\n$$\n\\lVert G_x(t,0)u\\rVert^{2}\\ge\\exp(-D_{\\mathrm{max}}t)\\lVert u\\rVert^{2},\n$$\n\nby differentiating the squared norm and applying Gronwall. Hence $p^{\\mathrm{empty}}\\ge\\exp(-D_{\\mathrm{max}}T)$ for every prior density and every source path. If two unnormalized selected outputs differ by trace norm $\\epsilon$, their normalized states differ by at most $2\\epsilon/p^{\\mathrm{empty}}$. The no-jump conditioning map is therefore continuous with a paid, explicit survival denominator. If parameters are allowed to send this lower bound to zero, no uniform postselection bound is claimed.\n\n(b) O.7.2.3a's $N_{\\mathrm{jump}}$ is a statement about the retained generator and memory sector. For the forward-locked Y-only Brownian protocol, (14) is the full retained generator and the jump labels/quantum carrier have no feedback into $\\theta$. Thus no retained jump/nonlocal/fractional term occurs. This is exact even when the discarded quantum instrument actually jumps. If the protocol retains i or the jump record, the jump term in (13) remains and $N_{\\mathrm{jump}}$ fails unless all retained transition rates vanish. If it retains the Hermite driver, the fractional/memory alternative remains even though its paths are continuous. Vacuum postselection does not prove $N_{\\mathrm{jump}}$ in either case.\n\nThe Brownian Y-only branch is a real local second-order scalar reduction, with nonzero quadratic coefficient on (-1,1), and an explicit endpoint-degenerate boundary law. It does not identify a spatial principal tensor or prove hyperbolicity. That extra identification is explicitly separate in O.7.2.3b.\n\n#### 7.3 Exact operational null-space certificate\n\nFreeze allowed preparations, bounded readout effects and interventions *before* declaring any data null. At a finite source stage let $E_{P}$ be the actual Heisenberg effect for protocol/outcome $P$. Then two initial operators differ by response-null $X$ exactly if $\\operatorname{Tr}(E_P X)=0$ for every allowed $P$. This is an operational quotient, not a claim that an arbitrary linear projection of states is a CPTP channel.\n\nFor a finite physical stage with a fixed finite protocol tree, enumerate its *actual admissible* instrument/control/readout branches and their products. Their effect span is a finite exact linear-algebra computation. Structural zero outcomes and clock/fresh-record constraints are retained in that enumeration. The correlated latent source is part of the source carrier and is not promoted to a freely selectable control.\n\nThere is also a useful continuous closure algorithm, with a necessary additional hypothesis: each nominated no-jump adjoint generator, jump map and Hamiltonian control used in the closure must itself be independently obtainable as an allowed short protocol (or as a derivative/linear combination of such actual protocols), and their finite concatenations must be admissible. Under this **accessible-generator hypothesis**, start with I and allowed effects and close their real Hermitian span under those actual maps. At most $d^{2}$ dimension increases occur. Exact rational/algebraic coefficients or supplied rank certificates make this an effective finite calculation. Invariance proves that all protocol effects lie in the span. Conversely, allowed short-time differentiation and concatenation put each generating-map word in the closure of the linear span of actual effects; finite dimension makes that span closed. Thus the computed span equals the operational effect span under this hypothesis.\n\nWithout the accessible-generator hypothesis, closure under a convenient larger matrix coefficient span is only an overapproximation of observable effects. Its orthogonal complement is a conservative null certificate, but may miss genuinely null directions; equality is not claimed. In particular, the ability to write a correlated path-dependent generator as a linear combination does not grant independent control over those coefficients. This construction never uses such an independence premise for the latent fGn path.\n\nFor the explicit qubit the required exact claims are proved directly, with no accessible-path hypothesis. Population-only final effects and diagonal phase controls remain diagonal for every actual source path and every actual jump word. They therefore lie in $\\operatorname{span}\\{I,Z\\}$. The population readout at any finite $T$ has effect $e^{-R_x(T)}P_0$, and averaging over the independent source has a strictly positive multiplier at least $e^{(-rT)}$; together with $I$ this spans both diagonal directions. Hence the exact effect span is $\\{I,Z\\}$, and $X/Y$ coherences are exactly null for that restricted menu.\n\nFor the full qubit menu, release the already-emitted source record at the final time and permit the paid final inverse phase $U_x(T)^*$, followed by $X$ or $Y$ readout. This is an actual finite controlled operation on the known source record, not intervention on the prior latent path. The pulled-back off-diagonal effects are $e^{-R_x(T)/2}X$ and $e^{-R_x(T)/2}Y$. Their source averages have a strictly positive multiplier at least $e^{-rT/2}$, and population readout supplies $Z$ as above. Thus the actual effect span is the entire Hermitian $2\\times 2$ space. No nonzero trace-zero coherence is null. Finite digital stages have the same conclusion for the ideal matrices; controlled physical approximation has the explicit error (S2). The constant-rate case is obtained by $R_x(T)=rT$. Adding a transverse intervention to the restricted population menu can also expand its observable span, so the old null quotient cannot be reused without rechecking the actual protocol.\n\n**Continuum response resolution is forward-locked to the topology actually proved.** Equation (12) compares source-dependent effects/interventions whose operator norms are bounded and whose Lipschitz constants in the bounded uniform source metric are declared and uniform in n. Source-independent quantum effects are included with Lipschitz constant zero. For a fixed finite outcome-adaptive protocol tree with predetermined per-branch times, bounded CP controls and such source-Lipschitz dependencies, apply (7)–(12) on each segment and telescope the CP maps. This gives the same vanishing rate with a constant depending on the fixed protocol size and declared Lipschitz bounds. It is not an unrestricted process-comb norm. Arbitrary source-dependent stopping times or discontinuous threshold tests require separate continuity/anti-concentration arguments. Continuity-set tests may receive weak convergence without the displayed quantitative rate.\n\nFor fixed $x$, a quantum trace/diamond error $\\epsilon$ controls all quantum effect probabilities by $\\epsilon$ (or $\\epsilon/2$ under the half-trace convention). Across the random source laws, only the forward-locked source-Lipschitz menu just stated receives the quantitative complete error from (12). On that declared operational quotient, it supplies $R_{\\delta}$ when its bound is $\\le\\delta$; the null certificate does not assert uniformity over all measurable source-label readouts, which could distinguish finite atomic paths from $Z$. Normalized selected protocols additionally use the survival factor above. The inverse-phase qubit readouts are globally source-Lipschitz, so both of the explicit null-space witnesses fit this menu. The exact finite-stage null-span computations remain exact for their actual finite menus, independently of which topology is later used for continuum comparison.\n\nNothing is discarded merely because its covariance is small. Source variables with no feedback and no allowed readout are null by the commuting reduction in 7.1, while the non-Gaussian memory observable and its quantum phase response remain nonnull under the full registered, source-Lipschitz continuum protocol. No stronger source total-variation claim is made.\n\n#### 8. Conclusion at the original scope\n\nThe common source is an actual finite sampled correlated register feeding an actual coherent instrument, followed by source-faithful retention and a continuous-record limit. It includes dependent non-Gaussian long memory, state-dependent/vanishing jump rates, rank-changing diffusion, and full quantum off-diagonals. Its local diffusion branch and its nonlocal/memory branches are classified differently. The construction is compatible with the retained Gaussian and non-Gaussian functional results and preserves their exact domain limits.\n\nThe finite physical admission, host qualification and clock/resource implementation are supplied by Theorem O.7.2.3m. The original target does not require every member to become a local hyperbolic scalar field, nor does Theorem O.7.2.3l claim that result.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723m-qualified-finite-common-source-actual-nd-rid-map-and-paid-clock\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3m (Qualified Finite Common Source, Actual ND-RID Map and Paid Clock).**\n\n#### 1. The explicitly nominated supplier law\n\nUse the exact ready-cell/capability construction H.1–H.8 represented by Theorem C.6c.P17a as the qualified host. Its ideal cell has 75 physical bits, one exposed payload bit, an insulated adaptive core, and an independently fixed task with Brier improvement 1/16 over the matched baseline and required margin 1/32. Its original binary-program presentation proves $C_{\\mathrm{op}}=1$ and strict aggregate/program separation. None of these facts is inferred from Hilbert dimension, gate count or an entropy.\n\nExtend the aggregate descriptor, without changing the raw ready-cell program or admitting a new program of length below four, by one fixed finite-control type:\n\nCORRELATED_INSTRUMENT_CONTROL = (finite source probability/decoding tables; finite Hermitian matrix blocks; finite addressed supports; norm bounds; positive durations; requested dyadic accuracies; fresh target-record indices; retention/readout menu).\n\nEvery field is length-delimited and finite. The grammar rejects malformed addresses, missing norm/time/accuracy bounds and reused fresh targets. It permits arbitrary finite Hermitian controls supplied with computable coefficients, as in the previously reviewed Q01/X02 supplier branch. It does not claim compilation of arbitrary complex phases into the old real gate alphabet. This is a populated new formal law, uniform in n, not an empirical fabrication claim or a source-menu promise awaiting a missing mathematical constructor.\n\nThe permitted formal branch is exactly: externally driven finite Hermitian interactions on qualified ready-cell payloads, externally supplied pure stock, retained work/record memory, a specified finite physical schedule and the resource accounting below. No autonomous/resetting/finite-power continuum premise is part of the branch.\n\n#### 2. Finite source data and normalization\n\nAt $n$, compute the finite table from Theorem O.7.2.3l, Section 3: $N$, rational cap $R_{n}$, grid $\\eta_n$, $K_n$ nonoverflow labels, $b_n$-bit probabilities $q_{j}$ and overflow $q_*=1-\\sum q_j$. Its literal table has $(K_n+1)b_n$ probability bits plus the rational coordinate representatives, their denominators and decoding/overflow rules. Every probability is nonnegative and the total is exactly one.\n\nPrepare $b_{n}$ fair bits by $b_{n}$ Hadamards on supplied pure blanks and read them into fresh owned seed records. Retain the seed, preparation qubits and all readout junk. A reversible table lookup maps the seed to its cumulative-table label $j$, retaining both the seed and $j$. All bits used by the literal table and lookup are allocated. The driver path $\\hat{P}_n$ is computed from that very label's coordinates by $F(z)=z^{2}-1$ and its nominated scaling; no separate stochastic driver is introduced later.\n\nThis construction samples a classical latent source deliberately. The subsequent quantum state is not measured to simulate its channel. The table lookup can be a fully enumerated finite Boolean reversible circuit, or one explicit permutation on the finite seed/label carrier, supplied as a finite Hermitian pulse. In either implementation the matrix/table or actual circuit description is paid and retained. Positive durations are specified for seed preparation, readout, lookup and decoding.\n\nFor the complete source ND–RID tuple, let $X=(\\text{stage }k,\\text{ retained source prefix},\\text{ sampled-label register},\\text{ actual forecast snapshot})$, and let $Y$ contain the fixed $q$ table, decoder, $T$, $n$ and arming schedule. Before label sampling the verification law is $q$. Once the full label is in $X$, the next decoded increment is deterministic conditional on that complete $X$: $V_{\\mathrm{complete}}(o|X,Y)$ is the point mass at its next decoder value, and the complete update appends that value and advances $k$. The random initial sampling and this deterministic conditional continuation are both normalized source kernels. Separately, under the declared observed filtration that reveals only the emitted prefix, the next-increment forecast law is the rational $q$-law conditioned on that prefix. Bayes conditioning is used only on positive-probability prefixes; impossible-prefix rows may be any fixed normalized law. Finite sums of dyadic weights compute these observed-filtration forecasts exactly. Internal prediction of this observer precedes the corresponding release/readout. The future-memory register is privately retained and unavailable to the declared observer protocol before release. Thus complete physical state and observed predictive state are distinguished rather than assigning a nontrivial conditional law to a fully known sampled label.\n\nFor the quantum tuple, $X=(\\rho,k,\\text{source label},\\text{retained outcomes},s_0)$. Its actual instrument is the finite dilation in §3, verification law $\\operatorname{Tr}I_\\alpha(\\rho)$, positive-probability update $I_\\alpha(\\rho)/\\operatorname{Tr}I_\\alpha(\\rho)$, registered perspective kernel $\\delta_{s0}$ and explicit tick arming. There is no attempt to copy an unknown $\\rho$ into a classical prediction register. On diagonal inputs with diagonal $B,A$ and rank-one edge matrices, this quantum tuple equals the state-dependent population kernel on the same label/clock. Off-diagonal inputs use the coherent instrument itself.\n\n#### 3. A computable coherent dilation with no rank-decision gap\n\nFor each label and tick, (9) gives the isometry\n\n$$\nV: \\mathbb{C}^d \\to \\mathbb{C}^{dr},\\quad Vu=\\sum_{\\alpha=0,\\ldots,r-1} K_\\alpha u \\otimes |\\alpha\\rangle.\n$$\n\nHere $r$ is the number of jump outcomes plus vacuum; it is unrelated to the example's scalar rate $r_{\\mathrm{rate}}$. On $\\mathbb{C}^d\\oplus\\mathbb{C}^{dr}$ use the explicit Hermitian unitary\n\n$$\nW=\\begin{pmatrix}0&V^*\\\\V&I-VV^*\\end{pmatrix}.\n\\tag{S1}\n$$\n\nIndeed $V^*V=I$, so $W^*=W$ and $W^{2}=I$. It maps an input $u$ in the first summand to $Vu$ in the output summand. This avoids selecting a Gram–Schmidt complement or deciding whether an arbitrary computable pivot is zero. Encode the finite direct-sum flag into a padded outcome register. Padding carries the identity and is excluded by preparation. The pulse\n\n$$\nH_W=(\\pi \\hbar/(2 \\tau_W))(I-W)\n$$\n\nhas norm at most $\\pi\\hbar/\\tau_W$ and $\\exp(-i\\tau_W H_W/\\hbar)=W$. Thus it implements exactly the required coherent instrument in the ideal real-coefficient model. The inverse-square-root gap $I-\\Delta D\\ge I/2$ makes the matrix square root effectively approximable; all other entries are finite computable exponentials, products and table values.\n\nTake the source-label-controlled direct sum of these $W$ blocks at each tick. Its norm bound is unchanged, but every block coefficient, support address and branch decoder is counted. Fresh flagged output registers are used at every tick. Fixed permutations route the retained system and output label to their next named roles. The final phase $U_x(T)$ is implemented by $H_{\\mathrm{phase}}=\\hbar Bx(T)/\\tau_{\\mathrm{phase}}$. Intermediate readouts are made in the corresponding physical frame by explicitly conjugating their effects or by paid $U_x(t)$ pulses; no second clock or hidden instantaneous pulse is used.\n\nA finite-coefficient implementation replaces each Hermitian matrix by a Hermitian dyadic approximation with\n\n$$\n\\sum_{\\mathrm{events}} \\tau_e \\lVert\\tilde H_e-H_e\\rVert/\\hbar \\le n^{-4}.\n\\tag{S2}\n$$\n\nDuhamel gives the same total operator-norm error. Its actual unitary is still exactly unitary, so its complete output instrument includes any small input-flag/padding leakage as an explicit failure outcome. That outcome has vanishing probability controlled by (S2); it is not silently dropped. Exactly forbidden structural blocks can be kept zero in Htilde, so a named absent jump channel is not populated by rounding. The ideal source-class identities and the finite physical approximation errors are distinguished.\n\nCoherent-output experiments retain the outcome registers without measurement. Monitored experiments additionally read them in the nominated basis, obtaining the stated instruments and normalized conditional states. They use the same source, dilation and controls. A measured history is not simultaneously claimed to be an undephased coherent history; all measured outputs are CPTP reductions of the explicitly retained dilation.\n\n#### 4. The adaptive host actually participates\n\nAt least one host core executes the full H.8 calibration schedule, including verification, discrepancy update and the retained next forecast $f_1$. Use that actual snapshot to control a nontrivial permutation $\\Pi$ of two valid source labels after sampling. Store the physical label $j'=\\Pi^{f_1}j$ and use the controlled decoder $j=\\Pi^{f_1}j'$ for all subsequent phase, jump and scalar-output tables. Retain $f_1$ and every calibration outcome. The physical source register, physical prediction table and controlled instructions change when $f_1$ changes; removing the adaptive update changes the recorded source label on the relevant histories. This is not a disconnected qualified spectator.\n\nThe decoded law and all equations in Theorem O.7.2.3l are unchanged because this is an explicitly recorded involutive frame map. No target action or desired continuum coefficient is used to choose $\\Pi$. On any finite stage with at least two positive labels choose their lexicographic first two; the Gaussian source gives them strictly positive box mass, and $b_{n}$ may be enlarged to make their lower dyadic masses positive. This finite enlargement improves every approximation bound. The two explicit boxes and their positive integration lower bounds are part of the finite input record.\n\nThe correlated source's own predictive map also changes after emitted prefixes: its correlated future law is conditional on that prefix. The host's definition-level qualification uses its independently fixed calibration, whereas the actual source forecast/verification/update tuple uses q and the emitted histories. These are distinct proofs and neither is inferred from the other.\n\n#### 5. An explicit finite inventory algorithm\n\nFor any fixed n and nominated finite observation protocol, emit the following event list in order:\n\n1. host calibration and its four independently timed fair-source preparations, ingress, scoring and retained readouts;\n2. $b_{n}$ source Hadamards and seed readouts, cumulative-table lookup and driver/conditional-table computation;\n3. the host-controlled label permutation and its retained-frame copy;\n4. $M_n=\\lceil Tn^{3}\\rceil$ coherent tick blocks, each with its fresh output flag, record routing and any nominated monitored readout;\n5. requested finite observation-frame controls/readouts and the final phase/readout.\n\nAll computation/installation is finished before the statistical window where required. Tick control, record routing, ROM delivery and cursor advancement are assigned subwindows whose sum is exactly $\\Delta_{k}$. With a fixed finite number $s_{k}$ of events in tick $k$, give each $\\tau_e=\\Delta_k/s_k$, including two positive-time clock-update events per data event. Larger supplier norms pay for smaller subwindows. Preparation/factory time occurs before $t=0$ and remains charged. Thus the clock used in the limiting paths is the actual observation time, not gate count divided after the fact by an unpaid speedup.\n\nA literal conservative storage bound is obtained by serializing rather than compressing:\n\n- source table: $(K_n+1)b_n$ bits;\n- coordinate table: the exact serialized length of $N(K_n+1)$ rational representatives;\n- source seed and retained copy: $2b_n$ bits, plus $(K_n+1)$-label address and decoded-path arithmetic workspace;\n- quantum system: $\\lceil\\log_2 d\\rceil$ bits;\n- fresh flagged quantum records: $M_{n}\\lceil\\log_2(r+1)\\rceil$ bits, plus a fresh copy for each actual monitored readout;\n- control matrices: for every tick and source label, the full finite dyadic matrix blocks of (S1), each with its actual dimension and coefficient precision; zero blocks are serialized explicitly if no compressed representation is used;\n- every readout/prediction table, arithmetic scratch reservation, frame bit, source ROM, event descriptor and installation/compilation archive.\n\nLet $B_{\\mathrm{data}}$ be the sum of these literal lengths plus the 30 calibration address entries and the chosen finite capability compiler reservations. The exact sampler/lookup and matrix-generation algorithms terminate; record their actual finite transition count and peak workspace, or a brute-force enumeration bound from their finite loops. These are determined integers at every n, not unspecified cost functions.\n\nIf $E$ is the emitted data/source/readout event count, use $E+1$ one-hot cursor bits and exactly $2E$ cursor $X$ updates. The complete run list has $3E$ events. Let $a_{\\mathrm{addr}}=\\lceil\\log_2(B_{\\mathrm{data}}+E+1)\\rceil$, enlarged to at least one, and let $p_{\\mathrm{opcode}}$ be the fixed opcode width of the extended grammar. Include the exact immutable instruction ROM length\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB_{\\mathrm{ROM}} &= 3E(p_{\\mathrm{opcode}}+3a_{\\mathrm{addr}})+B_{\\mathrm{matrix\\ payload}},\\\\\nB_{\\mathrm{control}} &= E+1+B_{\\mathrm{ROM}}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere matrix payload bytes not already counted in $B_{\\mathrm{data}}$ are added once, never twice; an event-address serialization manifest settles aliases. Allocate $N_{\\mathrm{cells}}=\\max(2,B_{\\mathrm{data}}+B_{\\mathrm{control}})$ ready cells. Every logical payload bit thereby owns its other 74 physical bits, including the cell's ROM/core. The full initial carrier is $75N_{\\mathrm{cells}}$ bits, plus only the explicitly separate fabrication archive if not already hosted.\n\nCell-ROM factory loading has exactly $16N_{\\mathrm{cells}}$ $X$ writes. Other immutable/basis loading costs its exact Hamming weight; cursor initialization costs one $X$. Supplied pure zero blanks, $b_{n}$ Hadamards, all physical readout apparatus bits, control-bank cells and retained archive are counted. No erased record is silently reused. This is an explicit finite construction/counting algorithm. It does not claim that the astronomically large complete table is printed for every $n$.\n\n#### 6. Physical energy, work, power and stock\n\nFix independent constants $\\tau_0=1$ s, $\\hbar$ with units J s, $\\beta_{\\mathrm{phys}}>0$ with units $\\mathrm{J}^{-1}$, $e_*=\\hbar/\\tau_0$, $e_b=e_*+2/\\beta_{\\mathrm{phys}}$, $v_0=10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm{m}^{3}$, $R_0=e_b/\\tau_0$ and $r_p=r_I=e_*/\\tau_0$. Use the native finite cost operators from the reviewed Hermitian supplier construction:\n\n$H_v=e_b I$, $\\hat{C}_v=3I$ on ordinary payload cells;\n$\\hat{C}_v=3P_{\\mathrm{idle}}+4P_{\\mathrm{active}}$ on each separately owned supplier-duty cell;\n$R(C)=R_0+r_p(C-1)^{2}$,\n$R_I(C)=r_I\\log_2(C/3)$,\n$D_v=[R(\\hat{C}_v)-R_0I]\\tau_0+R_I(\\hat{C}_v)\\tau_0$,\n$\\epsilon_v=H_v+D_v+(1/2)\\sum_{e\\text{ incident to }v}V_e$,\n$\\rho_v=\\epsilon_v/v_0$.\n\nThe C values are nominated finite operational cost bins, not a shortest-program oracle or an equality with register entropy. Every actual self-adjoint gate/source/readout/clock interaction $V_{e}$ is listed once, with two owning endpoint ports and its complete finite support. Summing $\\epsilon_{v}$ gives every interaction once. Additional support cells still retain their own storage/baseline costs. No nearest-neighbor or bounded-arity physical locality is asserted for this new finite supplier.\n\nFixed duty sectors contribute scalar costs that do not change the intended relative logical dynamics. Where a duty flip would fail to commute with $D_{v}$, use the complete compensated interaction\n\n$$\nV_{\\mathrm{flip}}=(\\pi \\hbar/(2\\tau_{\\mathrm{flip}}))(I-X)-D_v,\n$$\n\nwhose total local generator is $e_{b}I+(\\pi\\hbar/(2\\tau_{\\mathrm{flip}}))(I-X)$, implementing $X$ up to its recorded scalar phase. Its full norm, including $\\lVert D_v\\rVert$, is charged. For a general event whose active-support native costs are not scalar, the same known diagonal compensation is included explicitly. This prevents the false identification of an uncompensated logical pulse with the physical total generator.\n\nFor each emitted event, compute $G_e=\\lVert V_e\\rVert$ or its certified upper bound from the explicit finite matrix. Reserve the positive load\n\n$$\np_{\\mathrm{load},e}=e_*/\\tau_0+G_e/\\tau_0+e_*/\\tau_e.\n$$\n\nEach active supplier has the fixed additional rated power\n\n$$\n\\Delta_p=5r_p+r_I \\log_2(4/3)>0.\n$$\n\nReserve $A_{\\mathrm{supply}}=\\max_{\\mathrm{blocks}}\\lceil(\\sum_{\\text{active events in block}}p_{\\mathrm{load},e})/\\Delta_p\\rceil$ duty cells, including all simultaneous cursor/controller events. Add their $2A_{\\mathrm{supply}}$ explicit activation/deactivation pulses, records and storage, with the fixed switch norms above. Their schedule is serially paid and added to the finite preparation/run inventory; it is not a recursive self-powering assertion. Idle ports remain charged $R_0+4r_p$. At active count $a$ the exact rated total is\n\n$$\n(A_{\\mathrm{supply}}-a)(R_0+4r_p)+a[R_0+9r_p+r_I\\log_2(4/3)].\n$$\n\nIntegrate this once over the actual schedule. This is an explicitly nominated finite supplier power contract together with native finite energy operators; it is not a derivation of electrical generation from algorithmic complexity.\n\nEvery pure owned physical blank has free-energy excess $\\beta_{\\mathrm{phys}}^{-1}\\log 2$ relative to the dimension-matched maximally mixed state under the scalar idle Hamiltonian. Thus the supplied pure-stock excess is exactly $\\beta_{\\mathrm{phys}}^{-1}B_{\\mathrm{pure}}\\log 2$. This accounts for preparation stock, not an attained finite-time reset protocol. All seeds, purifiers, matrix/work tables, clock records and quantum output records remain owned. Repeat experiments need new stock or a separately charged actual reset; no such reset is assumed here.\n\nFor the complete driven finite network,\n\n$$\nd\\langle H_{\\mathrm{total}}(t)\\rangle/dt=\\langle\\partial_t H_{\\mathrm{total}}(t)\\rangle\n$$\n\non smooth intervals, and the switching work at a discontinuity is $\\operatorname{Tr}[\\rho(t_e)\\Delta H_{\\mathrm{total}}]$. Use the exact finite matrices/states for net work and the conservative gross reserve $2\\sum_e G_e$ for isolated rectangular interactions, adding any nonconstant duty/scalar terms once. Stored final energy is not charged a second time as extra work. Peak norms grow as $\\hbar/\\tau_e$ and the matrix/stock counts can diverge as $n$ grows. A zero ideal net rectangular-pulse quench does not make its controller, timing, preparation or standby power free.\n\nAll normative positive currency charges, if desired, are now also explicit: one fixed positive tariff per owned blank/ROM/archive bit, per delivered descriptor, per physical bit-second, and actuation $1+\\tau_e G_e/\\hbar+\\tau_0/\\tau_e$. Such tariffs supplement, and do not replace, the J/W energy balance above.\n\n#### 7. Admission and precision conclusion\n\nThe fixed extended supplier grammar constructs each finite source member from a normalized finite correlated probability table, an explicit qualified adaptive host coupling, the literal quantum isometry (S1), actual instrument probabilities/updates, fixed perspective/arming, fresh owned records and a complete positive-time resource schedule. Its computable finite matrices are supplied to the precision (S2), and Theorem O.7.2.3l, Equation (12) proves their joint retained continuum limit.\n\nThus source realization is no longer an unpopulated function $C_{\\mathrm{source}}$ or an appeal to unknown engineering. The mathematical admission is conditional on the stated new finite Hermitian-control law, which is the formal branch explicitly nominated here. This supplier branch does not assert that it was already contained in a frozen old gate menu.\n\nThe limit classification remains source-faithful: Gaussian/local diffusion and non-Gaussian memory are different branches; finite quantum off-diagonals are retained; normalization and no-jump denominators are explicit; response-null reduction uses the forward-locked protocol. No stronger all-law, scalar-spacetime, empirical manufacturing, autonomous-clock, bounded-power, thermal-FDT or uncalibrated mechanical-action claim is made.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723n-stationary-random-rate-telegraph-classification\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3n (Stationary Random-Rate Telegraph Classification).** Let $\\mu$ be a probability measure on $(0,1]$. Sample $\\Lambda\\sim\\mu$, a fair sign $\\varepsilon$, and a rate-one Poisson process $N$, independently. Retain the same $\\Lambda$ for the entire history and define\n$$\nY(t)=\\varepsilon(-1)^{N(\\Lambda t)},\\qquad\nI(t)=\\int_0^tY(s)\\,ds,\\qquad\nX_n(t)=n^{-1/2}I(nt).\n$$\nThe process $Y$ is centered, strictly stationary and bounded. Its block increments $Z_j=I(j)-I(j-1)$ are therefore centered, strictly stationary and bounded by one. For every fixed $0<T<\\infty$,\n$$\n (\\Lambda,X_n)\\Rightarrow(\\Lambda,\\Lambda^{-1/2}B)\n \\quad\\hbox{in }(0,1]\\times C([0,T]),                         \\tag{O.7.2.3n.1}\n$$\nwhere $B$ is standard Brownian motion independent of $\\Lambda$. No inverse moment of $\\Lambda$ is required. The path law on the right is Gaussian exactly when $\\mu$ is a point mass. Its increments are independent exactly in that same case. Moreover, for any deterministic positive sequence $a_n$, a nonzero finite weak limit of $I(n\\,\\cdot)/a_n$ exists exactly when $a_n/\\sqrt n\\to c\\in(0,\\infty)$; that limit is $c^{-1}\\Lambda^{-1/2}B$. If the ratio tends to infinity the limit is zero, and if it tends to zero the one-time laws are not tight.\n\nFor the family $\\mu_\\beta(d\\lambda)=\\beta\\lambda^{\\beta-1}d\\lambda$, $\\beta>0$, put $V(t)=\\mathbb E I(t)^2$ and\n$$\nc_\\beta=2^{-\\beta}\\Gamma(\\beta+1).\n$$\nThen\n$$\n\\mathbb E[Y(0)Y(t)]\\sim c_\\beta t^{-\\beta},\\qquad\n\\mathbb E[Z_1Z_{k+1}]\\sim c_\\beta k^{-\\beta},                 \\tag{O.7.2.3n.2}\n$$\nand\n$$\nV(t)\\sim\n\\begin{cases}\n\\dfrac{2c_\\beta}{(1-\\beta)(2-\\beta)}t^{2-\\beta},&0<\\beta<1,\\\\\nt\\log t,&\\beta=1,\\\\\n\\dfrac{\\beta}{\\beta-1}t,&\\beta>1.\n\\end{cases}                                                  \\tag{O.7.2.3n.3}\n$$\nConsequently\n$$\n\\frac{I(n\\,\\cdot)}{\\sqrt{V(n)}}\\Rightarrow\n\\begin{cases}\n0,&0<\\beta\\le1,\\\\\n\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\beta-1}{\\beta\\Lambda}}B,&\\beta>1,\n\\end{cases}\n\\quad\\hbox{in }C([0,T]).                                      \\tag{O.7.2.3n.4}\n$$\nThe one-time variable at $t=1$ on the left of (O.7.2.3n.4) has variance exactly one for every $n$. Thus the first line is a genuine failure of uniform integrability, rather than a variance calculation that forces a nondegenerate weak limit. For $0<\\beta\\le1$, the nondegenerate limit under $\\sqrt n$ scaling in (O.7.2.3n.1) has infinite second moment.\n\n#### Proof: stationarity and finite-dimensional convergence\n\nConditional on $\\Lambda=\\lambda$, $Y$ is the two-state continuous-time chain with generator\n$$\nQ_\\lambda=\\begin{pmatrix}-\\lambda&\\lambda\\\\\\lambda&-\\lambda\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\nand stationary initial row $\\pi=(1/2,1/2)$. This proves conditional and unconditional strict stationarity. The conditional covariance is $e^{-2\\lambda|t-s|}$.\n\nLet $D=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)$. For a fixed real $u$ and $a>0$, the conditional Feynman–Kac transition matrix for an increment of $X_n$ over a time interval of length $a$ is\n$$\n\\exp\\{na(Q_\\lambda+iuD/\\sqrt n)\\}.\n$$\nThe two eigenvalues of its exponent's matrix before multiplication by $na$ are\n$$\nr_{\\pm,n}=-\\lambda\\pm\\sqrt{\\lambda^2-u^2/n}.\n$$\nFor all sufficiently large $n$, they are distinct and real. Their spectral projectors converge respectively to\n$$\nP_+=\\mathbf1\\pi,\\qquad P_-=I-P_+.\n$$\nFurthermore $nr_{+,n}\\to-u^2/(2\\lambda)$ and $nr_{-,n}\\to-\\infty$. Hence the matrix converges to $e^{-au^2/(2\\lambda)}P_+$. The Markov property writes the joint characteristic function of successive increments over any finite partition as $\\pi$ times the ordered product of these matrices times $\\mathbf1$. Its limit is\n$$\n\\prod_j\\exp\\{-a_ju_j^2/(2\\lambda)\\}.\n$$\nThese are the independent increments of $\\lambda^{-1/2}B$. General finite-dimensional distributions follow by expressing positions as sums of increments.\n\n#### Proof: functional tightness, including intervals below the original mesh\n\nFor ordered times $0\\le s_1\\le s_2\\le s_3\\le s_4$, the independent Poisson increments give the exact identity\n$$\n\\mathbb E_\\lambda\\prod_{j=1}^4Y(s_j)\n=e^{-2\\lambda[(s_2-s_1)+(s_4-s_3)]}.\n$$\nFor $L>0$, symmetry of the four-dimensional integration cube yields\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E_\\lambda I(L)^4\n&=24\\int_{0<s_1<s_2<s_3<s_4<L}\n e^{-2\\lambda[(s_2-s_1)+(s_4-s_3)]}\\,ds_1\\cdots ds_4\\\\\n&\\le 24\\frac{L^2}{2}\n \\left(\\int_0^\\infty e^{-2\\lambda a}da\\right)^2\n=\\frac{3L^2}{\\lambda^2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFor the inequality use the coordinates $s_1,a=s_2-s_1,b=s_3-s_2,c=s_4-s_3$. At fixed $a,c$, the allowed $(s_1,b)$ triangle has area at most $L^2/2$, and extending $a,c$ to the positive quadrant increases the integral. Stationarity therefore gives, for every $s,t\\in[0,T]$,\n$$\n\\mathbb E_\\lambda|X_n(t)-X_n(s)|^4\n\\le3\\lambda^{-2}|t-s|^2.\n$$\nThis bound holds without a restriction on $|t-s|$ or $n$. For completeness, choose $0<\\alpha<1/4$ and use dyadic grids of $[0,T]$. Markov’s inequality and a union bound give a bound $C_{\\lambda,T}2^{-m(1-4\\alpha)}$ for an adjacent level-$m$ increment exceeding $2^{-m\\alpha}$. The sum of these bounds over $m\\ge M$ tends to zero uniformly in $n$. Outside that event, dyadic chaining and continuity bound the oscillation over distances at most $T2^{-M}$ by a fixed multiple of $2^{-M\\alpha}$. Together with $X_n(0)=0$, this proves conditional tightness in $C([0,T])$. Combined with the finite-dimensional calculation it proves the conditional functional limit for every fixed $\\lambda>0$.\n\nFor any bounded continuous $F$ on $(0,1]\\times C([0,T])$, apply that conditional weak convergence to $F(\\lambda,\\cdot)$ at each fixed $\\lambda$. Dominated convergence against $\\mu$ proves (O.7.2.3n.1). This step does not integrate the conditional fourth-moment bound; in particular it introduces no assumption $\\mathbb E\\Lambda^{-2}<\\infty$.\n\n#### Proof: Gaussian and independence classification\n\nAt any $t>0$, the limit characteristic function is\n$$\n\\varphi_t(u)=\\int e^{-tu^2/(2\\lambda)}\\,\\mu(d\\lambda).\n$$\nIf this is Gaussian of variance $vt$, uniqueness of Laplace transforms on positive measures gives $\\Lambda^{-1}=v$ almost surely. Conversely a fixed rate plainly gives the stated Brownian law.\n\nFor two disjoint intervals with positive lengths $a,b$, their joint characteristic function is\n$$\n\\mathbb E e^{-\\Lambda^{-1}(au^2+bv^2)/2}.\n$$\nIf the increments are independent, choose nonzero $u,v$ with $au^2=bv^2$. Then the expectation of the square of $e^{-au^2/(2\\Lambda)}$ equals the square of its expectation. This bounded random variable has variance zero. Its strictly monotone dependence on $\\Lambda$ forces a deterministic rate. This proof also covers distributions without any inverse moments.\n\n#### Proof: completeness of the normalization classification\n\nWrite $W=\\Lambda^{-1/2}B$. Fix a time $t_*$ with $0<t_*\\le T$. The law of $W(t_*)$ has an everywhere positive mixture-of-Gaussian density and no atom at zero. If $a_n/\\sqrt n\\to0$, then for each fixed $M>0$, conditional weak convergence and the absence of an atom at zero imply $\\mathbb P(|X_n(t_*)|\\le Ma_n/\\sqrt n)\\to0$; the rescaled marginals are not tight. For a positive finite limit of the ratio, multiplication in (O.7.2.3n.1) gives the stated limit. For an infinite ratio, tightness in (O.7.2.3n.1) gives convergence to zero.\n\nIf a nonzero finite weak limit exists along the full sequence, the ratio can have no subsequence tending to zero or infinity, by those conclusions. It is therefore bounded above and away from zero eventually. Any two distinct subsequential limits $c_1,c_2$ would give different one-time laws $W(t_*)/c_1$ and $W(t_*)/c_2$: for every $r>0$, $\\mathbb P(|W(t_*)|\\le r)$ is strictly increasing in $r$. This contradicts convergence of the full sequence. The ratio must converge to one positive finite constant. This proves the claimed exhaustive deterministic-normalization classification.\n\n#### Proof: covariance and all three variance regimes\n\nFor the $\\beta$ family the covariance is\n$$\nC_\\beta(t)=\\beta\\int_0^1\\lambda^{\\beta-1}e^{-2\\lambda t}d\\lambda\n=\\beta(2t)^{-\\beta}\\int_0^{2t}v^{\\beta-1}e^{-v}dv.\n$$\nThe last integral increases to $\\Gamma(\\beta)$, proving (O.7.2.3n.2) for $Y$. Also $0<C_\\beta(t)\\le c_\\beta t^{-\\beta}$ for $t>0$. The block covariance is the integral of $C_\\beta(k+v-u)$ over $u,v\\in[0,1]$; multiplying by $k^\\beta$, its integrand converges uniformly to $c_\\beta$, proving the second assertion of (O.7.2.3n.2).\n\nStationarity and Fubini give\n$$\nV(t)=2\\int_0^t(t-s)C_\\beta(s)ds.                              \\tag{O.7.2.3n.5}\n$$\nIf $0<\\beta<1$, substitute $s=tu$, divide by $t^{2-\\beta}$, and dominate by $2c_\\beta(1-u)u^{-\\beta}$. Dominated convergence and elementary integration give the first line of (O.7.2.3n.3).\n\nAt $\\beta=1$, $C_1(s)=(1-e^{-2s})/(2s)$, with the continuous extension at zero. Equation (O.7.2.3n.5) becomes\n$$\nV(t)=t\\int_0^t\\frac{1-e^{-2s}}s ds-\\int_0^t(1-e^{-2s})ds.\n$$\nThe first integral is $\\log t+O(1)$ and the second is $t+O(1)$, proving the second line of (O.7.2.3n.3). The claimed estimate of the first integral follows by splitting at one and bounding the tail integral of $e^{-2s}/s$.\n\nIf $\\beta>1$, Tonelli gives\n$$\n2\\int_0^\\infty C_\\beta(s)ds\n=\\int_0^1\\lambda^{-1}\\beta\\lambda^{\\beta-1}d\\lambda\n=\\frac{\\beta}{\\beta-1}.\n$$\nDivide (O.7.2.3n.5) by $t$ and use dominated convergence to obtain the final line. Finally, (O.7.2.3n.1) implies tightness of $X_n$; multiplying it by $\\sqrt{n/V(n)}$, whose limits are given by (O.7.2.3n.3), proves (O.7.2.3n.4). The infinite inverse moment for $\\beta\\le1$ gives the final moment assertion. ∎\n\n#### Separation from the nonlinear-fGn class\n\nFor a non-point-mass rate distribution the bounded block sequence $Z_j$ is nonergodic. Indeed, conditional on $\\Lambda=\\lambda$, its empirical second moment converges almost surely to\n$$\nf(\\lambda)=2\\int_0^1(1-s)e^{-2\\lambda s}ds,\n$$\nwhich is strictly decreasing in $\\lambda>0$. One can prove this convergence without a moment condition on the rate: for each fixed $\\lambda$, the two-state transition matrix bounds the covariance of two bounded block-square observables at separation $k\\ge2$ by $4e^{-2\\lambda(k-1)}$. The variance of their $N$-term empirical average is consequently at most $C_\\lambda/N$. Chebyshev and Borel–Cantelli along $N=m^2$ give convergence on that subsequence. Since $0\\le Z_j^2\\le1$ and $(m+1)^2/m^2\\to1$, the intervening averages differ from their adjacent square-subsequence averages by a deterministic quantity tending to zero. Disintegration in $\\lambda$ therefore yields full almost sure convergence to a shift-invariant nonconstant statistic. Ergodicity therefore fails.\n\nFor each fGn index $0<H<1$, its Gaussian covariance tends to zero. Cross-covariance decay gives mixing for finite Gaussian cylinder events by their characteristic functions, and approximation by cylinder events gives ergodicity. Every coordinatewise measurable function of that source remains ergodic. At $H=1$, the fGn sequence and every coordinatewise function of it are time-constant; the telegraph block sequence is not. Thus a nondegenerate random-rate block law is outside every coordinatewise nonlinear-fGn class treated by Theorems O.7.2.3h–k.\n\n#### Common finite formal realization on the rational-beta domain\n\nFix a positive rational $\\beta$ and a positive dyadic observation horizon $T$. The following construction supplies an effective normalized finite table converging to the entire retained source in (O.7.2.3n.1). All statements in this paragraph concern the finite Hermitian-control supplier already admitted in Theorem O.7.2.3m.\n\nChoose positive dyadic $\\eta_n,\\delta_n\\le n^{-4}$. Partition $(0,1]$ into dyadic rate cells of width at most $\\eta_n$, with dyadic midpoints $\\lambda_j>0$. Their exact masses are $b_j^\\beta-a_j^\\beta$. These are computable positive real numbers. Compute dyadic lower approximations $q_j$ with total omitted mass at most $n^{-4}$, and put that remainder on an explicit overflow label. The $q_j$ and overflow mass have a common dyadic denominator and sum exactly to one. The selected rate, the initial fair sign and all subsequent coins are retained.\n\nPartition the original time interval $[0,nT]$ into cells of length at most $\\delta_n$. Given $\\lambda_j$, use independent Bernoulli variables with success probability $\\lambda_j\\ell_k$ in a cell of length $\\ell_k$, flipping the sign at its right endpoint. Every probability is rational; dyadic endpoint partitions make it dyadic. On an overflow history use the zero path, set the retained rate $\\widehat\\Lambda_n=1$, and retain the failure record. Choose one positive rate-cell lower mass strictly positive; its two fair-sign branches then give two positive complete-table labels for the host-controlled invertible frame.\n\nHere is an explicit coupling estimate. Couple $\\Lambda$ with its rate-cell midpoint except on the table-overflow event. A rate-one marked Poisson process accepts each point when its uniform mark is below the relevant rate. A disagreement between the original and quantized rate processes over $[0,nT]$ has probability at most $nT\\eta_n$. In a cell a Poisson count of mean $a=\\lambda_j\\ell_k\\le1$ can be coupled with a Bernoulli count of parameter $a$ with failure probability at most $a^2$: the mass of two or more Poisson points is at most $a^2/2$, and $a-(1-e^{-a})\\le a^2/2$. Summing the failures gives at most $nT\\delta_n$. On successful cells, moving the unique point to the right endpoint changes the integrated path by at most $2\\delta_n$ per point. Its normalized expected uniform error is at most $2T\\sqrt n\\delta_n$. Thus the finite path $\\widehat X_n$ has vanishing bounded-uniform coupling distance from $X_n$, and $(\\widehat\\Lambda_n,\\widehat X_n)$ has the same limit as (O.7.2.3n.1).\n\nThe finite table contains exactly the sampled rate, the initial sign and the finite string of switch coins. Its table lookup and time-ordered decoder are finite algorithms. At positive-probability emitted prefixes, the predictive law is the normalized conditional law of this same table. Conditional on the entire retained seed, continuation is deterministic. These are the complete-state and observed-filtration kernels required by Theorem O.7.2.3m, rather than two distinct stochastic sources.\n\nEvery driver path $\\widehat X_n$ is $\\sqrt n$-Lipschitz in the physical observation coordinate $t=s/n$. For every finite-dimensional coefficient collection $(B,A,L_e)$ in Claim O.7.2.3l.2, its isometry map $x\\mapsto V_x$ is uniformly path-Lipschitz. Therefore (O.7.2.3n.1), the coupling estimate and bounded continuous mapping prove joint retained-source/coherent-output convergence for that entire coefficient class, including all initial coherences and spectators. Use a common quantum tick mesh $\\Delta_n\\le n^{-3}$. The path-freezing error is at most $C_V\\sqrt n\\Delta_n$, and the complete-record discretization error is at most $C\\sqrt{T\\Delta_n}$. The exact normalized Kraus operators are those of (9) in Theorem O.7.2.3l. The finite matrix approximation budget may again be $n^{-4}$.\n\nFor effective finite formal realization additionally require computable B and effective evaluations of A and every $L_{e}$, together with supplied finite norm and Lipschitz bounds. The pathwise continuity assertion above holds on the full coefficient domain of Claim O.7.2.3l.2; this algorithmic coefficient domain supplies the actual computable matrix entries required by Theorem O.7.2.3m. On it, the Hermitian isometry dilation, adaptive host-controlled invertible label frame, fresh records, positive-time event schedule and full energy/stock ledger of Theorem O.7.2.3m apply to these finite tables. The actual clock is the observation coordinate $t$, so the accelerated switching and $\\sqrt n$ path slope are paid control requirements. This establishes the common formal realization on the declared rational-beta source family and the effective coefficient domain just specified. A finite pulse norm or total stock bound uniform in $n$ is not an assumption of that supplier.\n\nTheorem O.7.2.3n classifies the retained random-rate family, including its full deterministic-normalization alternatives. The finite common-source realization covers positive rational $\\beta$ and effectively evaluable coefficient data with the stated bounds. This random-rate family keeps its exact normalization and coefficient domain. Theorems O.7.2.3t–z and Proposition O.7.2.3aa supply the general source, state and quantum classification and extend the common realization.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723o\"></a>\n**Theorem O.7.2.3o (Exact Fixed-Stationary-Source Attainable-Law Image).** Let $(\\xi_j)_{j\\ge1}$ be one real strictly stationary sequence, and define\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\nS_0=0,\\qquad S_m=\\sum_{j=1}^m\\xi_j,\\\\\nP(u)=S_{\\lfloor u\\rfloor}+(u-\\lfloor u\\rfloor)\\xi_{\\lfloor u\\rfloor+1},\\qquad\nX_n(t)=\\frac{P(nt)}{a_n}.\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\nHere $a_n>0$ is regularly varying with index $H\\in\\mathbb R$, meaning\n$$\n\\frac{a_{\\lfloor cn\\rfloor}}{a_n}\\longrightarrow c^H\\qquad(c>0).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3o.1}\n$$\nAll scales use the same source law. Let\n$$\nE=C_0([0,\\infty),\\mathbb R),\\qquad\nd(x,y)=\\sum_{m=1}^{\\infty}2^{-m}\\bigl(1\\wedge\\|x-y\\|_{[0,m]}\\bigr),\n\\tag{O.7.2.3o.2}\n$$\nwhere $C_0$ denotes continuous paths starting at zero and the norm is the uniform norm on the indicated interval. This complete bounded metric gives the compact-open topology. A continuous process $X$ is $H$-selfsimilar with stationary increments, abbreviated $H$-sssi, when its path laws satisfy\n$$\nX(c\\,\\cdot)\\overset{\\mathrm{law}}=c^HX(\\cdot),\\qquad\nX(h+\\cdot)-X(h)\\overset{\\mathrm{law}}=X(\\cdot)\n\\quad(c>0,\\ h\\ge0).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3o.3}\n$$\nThe following statements hold.\n\n1. If $X_n\\Rightarrow X$ in $E$ and $X$ is nonzero, then $H>0$ and $X$ is $H$-sssi. If $\\mathbb E|\\xi_1|<\\infty$, then $H\\le1$. Independently, $\\mathbb E|X(1)|^2<\\infty$ implies $H\\le1$.\n2. Every continuous $H$-sssi law is the whole-path limit of the fixed stationary source $\\xi_j=X(j)-X(j-1)$ under $a_n=n^H$. A finite time-one second moment gives this source finite second moments. Under this finite-second-moment assumption, the source is centered when $0<H<1$.\n3. The nonzero continuous finite-second-moment laws attainable from fixed centered stationary finite-second-moment sources under positive regularly varying scalar normalization are exactly the continuous finite-second-moment $H$-sssi laws with $0<H\\le1$. The centered endpoint witness is Theorem O.7.2.3p.\n\nThe theorem classifies the attainable laws. Deciding whether a specified dependent source has a functional limit is a separate problem. Fixed deterministic linear centering is included by setting $\\xi_j=Z_j-\\mu$ once and for all; arbitrary scale-dependent centerings $b_nt$ require their own scaling-cocycle classification. Mean-zero microscopic increments are an explicit source condition.\n\n*Proof: necessity.* Fix $c>0$ and put $m_n=\\lfloor cn\\rfloor$. The same-source definition gives the exact identity\n$$\nX_n(ct)=\\frac{a_{m_n}}{a_n}X_{m_n}\\!\\left(\\frac{cn}{m_n}t\\right).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3o.4}\n$$\nHere $m_n\\to\\infty$, $cn/m_n\\to1$ and $a_{m_n}/a_n\\to c^H$. Tightness in continuous path space gives asymptotic equicontinuity on every compact time interval. Replacing $(cn/m_n)t$ by $t$ on the right therefore changes it by a quantity tending to zero in probability uniformly on each compact. Continuous deterministic dilation and multiplication in (O.7.2.3o.4) prove the first identity of (O.7.2.3o.3).\n\nFor $h\\ge0$, put $r_n=\\lfloor nh\\rfloor$. Strict stationarity and the polygonal definition give\n$$\nX_n(r_n/n+\\cdot)-X_n(r_n/n)\\overset{\\mathrm{law}}=X_n(\\cdot).\n$$\nOn $[0,T]$, replacing $r_n/n$ by $h$ changes the left side by at most $2\\omega_{[0,h+T+1]}(X_n,1/n)$, where $\\omega$ is the uniform modulus of continuity. This tends to zero in probability, proving the second identity of (O.7.2.3o.3). The equicontinuity fact used in both steps follows directly from tightness: for any error probability choose a compact subset carrying all sufficiently late laws up to that error, use uniform equicontinuity of that compact subset, and then send the mesh to zero.\n\nIf $X(1)=0$ almost surely, selfsimilarity gives $X(t)=0$ almost surely at every positive rational $t$, and continuity makes the whole path zero. Thus $X(1)$ is nonzero with positive probability. Continuity at zero requires $t^HX(1)\\to0$ in probability as $t\\downarrow0$, forcing $H>0$.\n\nFor an integrable source, $\\mathbb E|P(nt)|\\le nt\\,\\mathbb E|\\xi_1|$. If $H>1$, choose $2<b<2^H$. Equation (O.7.2.3o.1) gives $a_n\\ge b\\,a_{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor}$ for all sufficiently large integers $n$. Repeated halving to a fixed finite range yields $a_n\\ge Cn^{\\log_2b}$ for some $C>0$, hence $n/a_n\\to0$. Every $X_n(t)$ would then tend to zero in $L^1$, contradicting the nonzero limit. If instead the limit has finite second moment, stationary increments and Minkowski give\n$$\nn^H\\|X(1)\\|_2=\\|X(n)\\|_2\\le n\\|X(1)\\|_2.\n$$\nThe norm is finite and nonzero, so $H\\le1$. This argument uses the limit's own moment assumption; it does not transfer source moments through weak convergence.\n\nThe step path $S_{\\lfloor nt\\rfloor}/a_n$ differs uniformly from $X_n$ on $[0,T]$ by at most $\\max_{j\\le\\lceil nT\\rceil}|\\xi_j|/a_n$. For $n\\ge1$, this maximum is at most $\\omega_{[0,T+1]}(X_n,1/n)$ and tends to zero in probability. Thus the step paths have the same continuous limit in the Skorokhod $J_1$ topology.\n\n*Proof: converse.* For a continuous $H$-sssi process, the sequence $\\xi_j=X(j)-X(j-1)$ is strictly stationary because stationary increments hold jointly. Selfsimilarity gives the path-law identity\n$$\nn^{-H}P(n\\,\\cdot)\\overset{\\mathrm{law}}=I_nX,\n\\tag{O.7.2.3o.5}\n$$\nwhere $I_nX$ interpolates $X$ on the mesh $k/n$. Equality of the joint laws at those countably many mesh values and measurability of interpolation give equality of the entire path laws. For every finite $T$,\n$$\n\\|I_nX-X\\|_{[0,T]}\\le\\omega_{[0,T+1]}(X,1/n)\\longrightarrow0\n\\quad\\hbox{almost surely}.\n$$\nEquation (O.7.2.3o.5) proves convergence in $E$. If $\\mathbb E|X(1)|<\\infty$, selfsimilarity gives $\\mathbb EX(t)=t^H\\mathbb EX(1)$. Stationary increments make this expectation additive, and $\\mathbb E|X(t)|=t^H\\mathbb E|X(1)|$ gives continuity at zero. Hence $\\mathbb EX(t)=t\\mathbb EX(1)$. For $H\\ne1$, the two formulas force $\\mathbb EX(1)=0$. Theorem O.7.2.3p supplies every finite-second-moment endpoint law using a centered source, completing the exact image. ∎\n\n**Scalar Gaussian and convolution-Markov subimages.** For a scalar finite-second-moment $H$-sssi law with $0<H<1$, the preceding proof gives mean zero, and stationary increments give\n$$\n\\mathbb E[X(s)X(t)]=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{2}\n\\bigl(s^{2H}+t^{2H}-|t-s|^{2H}\\bigr),\\qquad\n\\sigma^2=\\mathbb EX(1)^2.\n$$\nWhen the law is Gaussian, this covariance determines every finite-dimensional law, yielding exactly $\\sigma B_H$. The scalar fractional Brownian kernels exist by Theorems O.7.2.3f–g. At $H=1$, Theorem O.7.2.3p gives $X(t)=tV$; this law is Gaussian exactly when $V$ is Gaussian, allowing a nonzero mean and deterministic slopes. The Gaussian identification requires Gaussianity of the path law; covariance alone does not imply it. Vector Gaussian cross-covariances require separate reversibility data and are outside this scalar classification.\n\nFor the Markov subimage, require the actual time-homogeneous spatial convolution transition kernel $P_t(x,A)=\\nu_t(A-x)$ and the Markov property relative to the process's past. Each conditional future-increment distribution is then independent of the past and equals $\\nu_t$; iterated conditioning proves independent increments. Continuity and Theorem O.7.2.3e give $X(t)=bt+\\Sigma^{1/2}B(t)$, with scalar $\\Sigma\\ge0$. Selfsimilarity permits precisely nonzero centered Brownian motion at $H=1/2$, nonzero deterministic linear drift at $H=1$, and the excluded zero process. A convolution identity for one-time marginals does not supply the transition-kernel hypothesis. A nonconstant random slope has stationary increments but fails that spatial convolution transition-kernel condition. Locality and second-order scalar-spacetime closure retain the separate antecedents of Propositions O.7.2.3b and O.7.2.3d and Definition O.7.2.3c.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723p\"></a>\n**Theorem O.7.2.3p (Centered Fixed Sources for the Index-One Endpoint).** Every continuous $1$-sssi law with a finite first moment at time one is a random linear slope $X(t)=tV$. Every such scalar law with $\\mathbb EV^2<\\infty$ is attained by a fixed centered stationary finite-second-moment source. More strongly, every real random slope $V$ finite almost surely, with any moment behavior, is attained by a uniformly bounded centered stationary source under the same regularly varying index-one normalization constructed below.\n\n*Proof: random-slope rigidity.* Fix positive integers $m,N$ and define\n$$\nY_j=N\\bigl[X(j/N)-X((j-1)/N)\\bigr],\\qquad 1\\le j\\le mN.\n$$\nEvery $Y_j$ has the law of $X(1)$, and their average is $X(m)/m$, again with that law. The strictly convex function $\\phi(v)=\\sqrt{1+v^2}$ has linear growth. Taking expectations in Jensen's inequality gives equality; its nonnegative gap has expectation zero. Strict convexity forces all the $Y_j$ to coincide almost surely. The first $N$ terms sum to $NX(1)$, so every $Y_j=X(1)$. Intersect the probability-one events over integers $m,N$ and use continuity to obtain $X(t)=tX(1)$ for every $t\\ge0$ almost surely. The same argument works for finite-dimensional vectors with $\\phi(v)=\\sqrt{1+\\|v\\|^2}$.\n\n*Proof: a bounded centered source with limit $t$.* For each integer $k\\ge4$, put\n$$\nN_k=2^k,\\qquad \\ell_k=\\lfloor 2^k/k^2\\rfloor,\\qquad\nq_k=\\ell_k/N_k,\\qquad w_k=k^{-2}.\n$$\nLet independent $U_k$ be uniform on $\\mathbb Z/N_k\\mathbb Z$ and define, for every integer $j$,\n$$\nZ_j=\\sum_{k=4}^{\\infty}w_k\n\\left(q_k-\\mathbf1_{\\{(j+U_k)\\bmod N_k<\\ell_k\\}}\\right).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3p.1}\n$$\nThe series converges uniformly in $j$ and the phases because each summand has absolute value at most $w_k$ and $\\sum_{k\\ge4}w_k<\\infty$. Thus $|Z_j|\\le\\sum_{k\\ge4}w_k$. Simultaneously advancing every phase by one preserves the product uniform law, proving joint strict stationarity. Each summand has mean zero; uniform convergence permits expectation through the sum, so $\\mathbb EZ_j=0$.\n\nLet $P_Z$ be the unit-mesh polygonal interpolation of the partial sums of $Z_j$. For $n\\ge2$, define\n$$\nL_n=\\log_2n,\\qquad a_n=\\frac{n}{3L_n^3},\\qquad\nK_n=\\left\\lfloor L_n+\\frac12\\log_2L_n\\right\\rfloor,\n\\tag{O.7.2.3p.2}\n$$\nand assign any positive $a_1$. For every $c>0$, direct substitution gives $a_{\\lfloor cn\\rfloor}/a_n\\to c$.\n\nAt a fixed level $k$, each full period contains exactly $\\ell_k$ marked sites and has centered sum zero. After removing full periods from an interval of length $m$, the remaining cyclic interval has length $r<N_k$. Both its marked count and $rq_k$ lie in $[0,\\ell_k]$. Therefore, uniformly over $m\\ge0$ and every phase,\n$$\n\\left|\\sum_{j=1}^m w_k\n\\left(q_k-\\mathbf1_{\\{(j+U_k)\\bmod N_k<\\ell_k\\}}\\right)\\right|\n\\le w_k\\ell_k.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3p.3}\n$$\nInterpolation preserves this bound. Summing levels $k\\le K_n$ gives\n$$\n\\sum_{k=4}^{K_n}w_k\\ell_k\n\\le\\sum_{k=4}^{K_n}\\frac{2^k}{k^4}\n=O\\!\\left(\\frac{2^{K_n}}{K_n^4}\\right)\n=O\\!\\left(\\frac{n}{L_n^{7/2}}\\right)=o(a_n).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3p.4}\n$$\nFor the middle estimate, split the sum at $K_n/2$. The lower half is exponentially smaller, while the upper half is bounded by $16/K_n^4$ times a geometric sum.\n\nFix finite $T>0$ and put $M_n=\\lceil nT\\rceil+1$. Let $E_n(T)$ be the event that a marked block at some level $k>K_n$ meets sites $1,\\ldots,M_n$. At a given level the set of phases producing an intersection has cardinality at most $\\ell_k+M_n$. The union bound yields\n$$\n\\mathbb P(E_n(T))\\le\\sum_{k>K_n}\\left(q_k+\\frac{M_n}{2^k}\\right)\n\\le O(K_n^{-1})+O(M_n2^{-K_n})\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3p.5}\n$$\nOff this event, every level $k>K_n$ contributes the constant increment $w_kq_k$ throughout the window, giving polygonal cumulative contribution $nt\\,c_{K_n}$, where\n$$\nc_K=\\sum_{k>K}w_kq_k\n=\\sum_{k>K}\\frac1{k^4}\n+O\\!\\left(\\sum_{k>K}\\frac{2^{-k}}{k^2}\\right)\n\\sim\\frac1{3K^3}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3p.6}\n$$\nSince $K_n/L_n\\to1$, equations (O.7.2.3p.2) and (O.7.2.3p.6) give $nc_{K_n}/a_n\\to1$. Off $E_n(T)$,\n$$\n\\sup_{0\\le t\\le T}\\left|\\frac{P_Z(nt)}{a_n}-t\\right|\n\\le\\frac{\\sum_{k=4}^{K_n}w_k\\ell_k}{a_n}\n+T\\left|\\frac{nc_{K_n}}{a_n}-1\\right|\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3p.7}\n$$\nThis proves convergence in probability in $E$ to the path $t\\mapsto t$. The normalized variables have mean zero at every stage but time-one weak limit one; consequently they are not uniformly integrable. Existence of all microscopic moments does not provide that missing uniform integrability.\n\nTake $V$ independent of the phases with $\\mathbb EV^2<\\infty$ and set $Y_j=VZ_j$. Independence and centering give $\\mathbb EY_j=0$, stationarity is preserved, and\n$$\n\\mathbb EY_j^2\\le\\mathbb EV^2\\left(\\sum_{k\\ge4}w_k\\right)^2<\\infty.\n$$\nIts polygonal sum is $VP_Z$. First restrict to $|V|\\le R$ in (O.7.2.3p.7), then let $R\\to\\infty$; this proves convergence in probability in $E$ to $t\\mapsto tV$. It completes the centered finite-second-moment endpoint of Theorem O.7.2.3o.\n\n*Proof: bounded realization of arbitrary finite random slopes.* For an arbitrary real $V$ finite almost surely and independent of the phases, put\n$$\ng_k(v)=\\operatorname{sgn}(v)\\min(|v|,\\sqrt k),\\qquad\n\\widetilde Z_j=\\sum_{k=4}^{\\infty}w_kg_k(V)\n\\left(q_k-\\mathbf1_{\\{(j+U_k)\\bmod N_k<\\ell_k\\}}\\right).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3p.8}\n$$\nThis series converges uniformly in $j$, the phases and $V$, bounded by $\\sum_{k\\ge4}k^{-3/2}<\\infty$. Conditional on $V$, it is strictly stationary and centered; hence those properties also hold unconditionally, and every microscopic moment is finite. For each finite $v$, all levels $k\\ge v^2$ have $g_k(v)=v$. Conditional on $V=v$, the difference $\\widetilde Z_j-vZ_j$ is therefore a finite sum of centered periodic components. Equation (O.7.2.3p.3) bounds its cumulative polygonal sum for all microscopic times by\n$$\nC(v)=\\sum_{4\\le k<v^2}w_k|g_k(v)-v|\\ell_k<\\infty.\n$$\nSince $a_n\\to\\infty$, the normalized difference vanishes uniformly on every compact. Equation (O.7.2.3p.7) gives conditional convergence in probability to $t\\mapsto tv$ for every finite $v$. Integrating the conditional failure probabilities and applying bounded convergence gives unconditional convergence in probability in $E$ to $t\\mapsto tV$. Thus the bounded centered source class contains arbitrary heavy-tailed linear limits; the target's moment properties must be stated separately. ∎\n\n**Explicit finite prefixes and the fixed-source distinction.** The base source (O.7.2.3p.1) has uniformly computable finite block laws. For rational $T>0$ and integer $n\\ge4$, take $J_n=n$ and retain phases $U_4,\\ldots,U_{J_n}$. They require exactly\n$$\nb_{J_n}=\\sum_{k=4}^{J_n}k=\\frac{J_n(J_n+1)}2-6\n$$\nfair bits; every complete phase label has probability $2^{-b_{J_n}}$. For $N=\\lceil nT\\rceil$, compute the rational vector\n$$\nZ_j^{[J_n]}=\\sum_{k=4}^{J_n}k^{-2}\n\\left(\\frac{\\ell_k}{2^k}-\\mathbf1_{\\{(j+U_k)\\bmod2^k<\\ell_k\\}}\\right),\n\\qquad1\\le j\\le N.\n$$\nEnumerating the finite bit strings gives an exactly normalized finite dyadic probability table. It is the finite marginal construction obtained by discarding high levels of the one source, coupled through its original phases. Its coordinatewise omitted tail is at most $\\sum_{k>J_n}k^{-2}\\le J_n^{-1}$. If $P^{[J_n]}$ denotes its polygonal sum, the actual observation coordinate $t=j/n$ gives the deterministic coupling bound\n$$\n\\sup_{0\\le t\\le T}\\frac{|P_Z(nt)-P^{[J_n]}(nt)|}{a_n}\n\\le\\frac{nT}{a_nJ_n}\n=\\frac{3T(\\log_2n)^3}{n}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3p.9}\n$$\nThe normalizer is computable. Round each of the finitely many normalized vertices to dyadic precision $\\epsilon_n$, retaining zero exactly and using the same physical vertex times $j/n$. Convexity of interpolation bounds the extra uniform error by $\\epsilon_n$; $\\epsilon_n=1/n$ suffices. The complete seed and phase label can be retained privately, with prefix values released at their nominated times and positive-probability forecasts obtained by exact conditioning of this one table. Theorem O.7.2.3q supplies the paid preparation, lookup, retention and release schedule.\n\nThis increasing refinement is essential: for every fixed finite $J$, equation (O.7.2.3p.3) bounds $P^{[J]}$ for all time by $\\sum_{k=4}^{J}w_k\\ell_k$, so $P^{[J]}(n\\,\\cdot)/a_n\\to0$. A fixed truncation is a different source with a different limit.\n\nFor $Y_j=VZ_j$, a supplied effective first-moment upper bound for $V$ upgrades (O.7.2.3p.9) to an expected coupling bound after multiplication; the bare assertion $\\mathbb EV^2<\\infty$ does not supply that bound algorithmically. Alternatively a computable probability-law name for $V$ permits computable weak approximations with explicit localization. For (O.7.2.3p.8), the source-level omitted tail is uniformly bounded over $V$ by $\\sum_{k>J}k^{-3/2}\\le2/\\sqrt J$. Taking $J_n=n^2$ therefore bounds normalized truncation error by $6T(\\log_2n)^3/n$. Finite-label approximation of the remaining dependence on $V$ still requires its nominated computable or limit-computable law description. Arbitrary finite-almost-sure slope laws form a noneffective existence class; their moment properties do not provide an algorithmic law name or an effective convergence rate.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723q\"></a>\n**Theorem O.7.2.3q (Source-Faithful Finite Compilation and its Effective Boundary).** On the finite Hermitian-control supplier branch of Theorem O.7.2.3m, every fixed stationary source with uniformly computable finite-block laws and a uniformly computable positive normalizer has finite dyadic table approximants whose bounded path-law error at stage $n$ is at most $3\\cdot2^{-n}$. Whenever its normalized paths converge, these approximants have the same limit, with no convergence modulus required as input. Each stage has an exact finite sampler, retained future label, normalized prefix forecasts, participating qualified adaptive host and positive-time resource schedule. For every finite-dimensional bounded globally Lipschitz coefficient collection of Theorem O.7.2.3l, the same driver also supplies joint coherent-output convergence; effective finite implementation additionally requires computable coefficient evaluations and supplied finite norm and Lipschitz bounds.\n\nIn the unrestricted algorithmic finite-table class, the weak limit laws are exactly the limit-computable laws on $E$. A computable weak-convergence modulus for uniformly computable stage laws makes the limit computable. Theorem O.7.2.3r proves that a computable fixed stationary source can instead have a noncomputable continuum law.\n\n#### Law representations and the finite-table image\n\nA basic path is a finite rational-knot, dyadic-value polygon starting at zero and constant after an integer horizon. These form a computable dense set in $E$. Their distances in (O.7.2.3o.2) are computable: evaluate the piecewise-linear difference at the finite union of breakpoints on each relevant integer interval and sum the eventual constant geometric tail. Use the bounded Wasserstein metric\n$$\nW_d(\\mu,\\nu)=\\inf_{\\text{couplings }(X,Y)}\\mathbb E\\,d(X,Y).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3q.1}\n$$\nIt metrizes weak convergence. To see the forward implication, $W_d\\to0$ transfers integrals of bounded continuous functions by tightness of the fixed limit law and uniform continuity near a compact set carrying almost all its mass. Conversely, weak convergence permits a finite small-diameter partition covering a compact set with arbitrarily large limit mass and boundaries of zero limit mass. Match corresponding partition masses and couple the remaining mass arbitrarily. The coupling cost is bounded by the cell diameters and unmatched mass. Neither implication assumes an effective tightness modulus.\n\nA computable law is supplied by an algorithm that, on input $p$, returns a finite dyadic probability table on basic paths with $W_d$ error at most $2^{-p}$. A limit-computable law is supplied by one algorithm returning finite dyadic tables $\\nu_n\\Rightarrow\\mu$, without a supplied convergence modulus. These are law names, rather than names of individual sample paths; parameterized families require one program uniform in their declared parameters.\n\nAn algorithmic finite supplier stage gives a finite computable law, which can be replaced by a dyadic table with error $2^{-n}$. Conversely, a limit-computable law name gives exactly the tables used by the sampler below. This proves the unrestricted finite-table image. If a uniformly computable sequence has a computable modulus in $W_d$, choose a late stage with error below half the requested budget and approximate that stage to the remaining half. This computes the limit law. These representation statements classify the compiler's image; they supply no decision procedure for totality or convergence of an arbitrary sequence of programs.\n\n#### Finite blocks of one source\n\nAssume a uniform finite-block name: on input integers $(N,p)$, an algorithm returns a finite dyadic table on rational vectors in $\\mathbb R^N$ whose Wasserstein error for the bounded max metric $1\\wedge\\max_j|u_j-v_j|$ is at most $2^{-p}$ from $\\operatorname{law}(\\xi_1,\\ldots,\\xi_N)$. This is the effective source input; the exact source can be non-atomic or unbounded.\n\nAt stage $n$, take physical horizon $T_n=n$ and $N_n=n^2$ increments. On this horizon, normalized polygonal summation has uniform Lipschitz bound $N_n/a_n$ for the max-coordinate norm. Search for a positive rational lower bound on the known positive computable $a_n$, obtaining a rational\n$$\nL_n\\ge\\max(1,N_n/a_n).\n$$\nChoose $p_n$ with $L_n2^{-p_n}\\le2^{-n}$ and invoke the source algorithm at $(N_n,p_n)$. The bounded-metric estimate follows from $\\min(1,Lu)\\le\\max(1,L)\\min(1,u)$. Transform every rational vector atom to its normalized polygon and approximate each of its finitely many vertices by dyadic values with uniform error at most $2^{-n}$. Extend the paths constantly after $T_n$. The omitted part of (O.7.2.3o.2) is at most $2^{-n}$, so the resulting table $\\widehat\\nu_n$ satisfies\n$$\nW_d\\bigl(\\widehat\\nu_n,\\operatorname{law}(X_n)\\bigr)\n\\le 3\\cdot2^{-n}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3q.2}\n$$\nThus the finite approximants inherit every functional limit of the original source. Precision was chosen from a finite-stage source error budget, independently of any rate for $X_n\\Rightarrow X$.\n\nA computable fixed source and computable normalization with a functional limit therefore yield a limit-computable path law. For a computable $H$-sssi law with computable $H$, the unit-increment witness in Theorem O.7.2.3o has computable finite blocks: finite evaluations are computable continuous maps. At $H=1$, the centered source (O.7.2.3p.1) multiplied by its computable slope law also has computable blocks, using its effective amplitude tail and weak-law localization. For a limit-computable law, its unit-increment source need not be computable. The unrestricted finite-table image and the fixed-source attainable image are different classifications: a converse asserting that every limit-computable $H$-sssi law has a computable fixed stationary source remains a separate theorem. Theorem O.7.2.3r proves that the computable fixed-source image contains noncomputable path laws.\n\n#### Literal sampler, private future memory and forecasts\n\nLet a stage table have labels $j=1,\\ldots,J$, weights $q_j=m_j/2^b\\ge0$ summing exactly to one, and basic paths $p_j$. Prepare $b$ fair bits by Hadamards on supplied pure blanks and actual readout into fresh owned records. A cumulative integer lookup maps each seed in $\\{0,\\ldots,2^b-1\\}$ to its label. Implement it as a finite reversible Boolean lookup, retaining seed, label and every work record. Its unrolled circuit or complete finite permutation is an explicit finite descriptor with its own inventory.\n\nRetain all path coefficients and the union of rational knot times. At each scheduled release emit the value or increment prescribed by that same private label. The declared source-prefix observer has no direct access to unreleased future-path memory. This access restriction allows statistical dependence between released and future values: the future is a paid pre-sampled classical memory, rather than a sequence of fresh independent innovations.\n\nFor a positive-probability emitted prefix $u$, the next-symbol forecast is\n$$\nq(v\\mid u)=\n\\frac{\\sum_{j:\\,\\operatorname{prefix}(j)=uv}q_j}\n{\\sum_{j:\\,\\operatorname{prefix}(j)=u}q_j}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3q.3}\n$$\nThe numerator and denominator are finite rational sums, and the denominator is positive. Impossible-prefix rows can be assigned any fixed normalized forecast. Install and retain this observer's forecast before the corresponding release; verification appends the actual decoded symbol and updates the table by (O.7.2.3q.3). Given the complete private label, continuation instead has the point-mass law at its next decoded symbol. These are the complete-state and observed-prefix filtrations of one normalized source.\n\nEquation (O.7.2.3q.3) is specifically a source-prefix forecast. If monitored quantum records are exposed before later scalar releases, the full observer must condition on them too, using $q_j$ times the actual instrument likelihood for each label and normalizing on positive-probability histories. Alternatively the source-prefix observer can receive those quantum records after its declared forecasts. Record which observation menu is used; private-memory access alone does not imply statistical nonleakage.\n\nThe qualified host of Theorem O.7.2.3m executes its independently specified H.8 calibration and discrepancy update. Its retained forecast bit controls an invertible permutation of two positive physical label states; decoding uses the retained inverse frame. If only one path label has positive weight, split it into two identical-path labels using one explicitly counted independent fair frame bit. This is auxiliary controller randomness, not an additional microscopic increment. Host updates change the physical label, frame record and controlled instructions while preserving the decoded target path law. The original host qualification premises remain those of Theorem O.7.2.3m.\n\n#### Common coherent output on the complete coefficient domain\n\nFix finite quantum dimension $d$, Hermitian $B$, bounded globally Lipschitz Hermitian $A(x)$ and finitely many bounded globally Lipschitz jump matrices $L_e(x)$, for real $x$. For effective implementation supply computable entries/evaluations and finite computable upper bounds for their norms and Lipschitz constants. For a continuous driver $x$, define\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nU_x(t)&=e^{-iBx(t)},\\\\\n\\widetilde A_x(t)&=U_x(t)^*A(x(t))U_x(t),\\\\\n\\widetilde L_{e,x}(t)&=U_x(t)^*L_e(x(t))U_x(t),\\\\\n\\widetilde D_x(t)&=\\sum_e\\widetilde L_{e,x}(t)^*\\widetilde L_{e,x}(t),\\\\\n\\partial_tG_x(t,s)&=\n\\left(-i\\widetilde A_x(t)-\\tfrac12\\widetilde D_x(t)\\right)G_x(t,s),\n\\qquad G_x(s,s)=I.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe bounded matrix integral series converges, and its nonpositive Hermitian generator part makes $G_x$ contractive. For an ordered record $h=(e_1,t_1,\\ldots,e_m,t_m)$, set $t_0=0$ and define\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_x(h)&=U_x(T)G_x(T,t_m)\n\\prod_{k=m}^{1}\\left[\\widetilde L_{e_k,x}(t_k)G_x(t_k,t_{k-1})\\right],\\\\\nC_x(\\varnothing)&=U_x(T)G_x(T,0).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe ordered product is written left to right with decreasing $k$; its one-jump case is $U_x(T)G_x(T,t_1)\\widetilde L_{e_1,x}(t_1)G_x(t_1,0)$. Let the common record space have vacuum mass one and, for each ordered label word, ordinary Lebesgue measure on $0<t_1<\\cdots<t_m<T$. No extra factorial appears in that measure. Define $(V_xu)(h)=C_x(h)u$.\n\nPut $\\ell=\\sum_e\\sup_x\\|L_e(x)\\|^2$. The squared coefficient-norm majorants sum to $\\sum_m(\\ell T)^m/m!=e^{\\ell T}$. They dominate the history series and its integral equation. Splitting at the final jump gives gain $\\sum_e\\widetilde L_{e,x}\\rho\\widetilde L_{e,x}^*$ and loss $-\\{\\widetilde D_x,\\rho\\}/2$, whose traces cancel. Hence the integrated sum of $C_x(h)^*C_x(h)$ is $I$, and $V_x$ is an isometry. Its actual monitored instrument is $C_x(h)\\rho C_x(h)^*$, including arbitrary initial coherences and spectators; constructing it never requires measuring an unknown input state to sample a channel.\n\nFor explicit continuity put\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\nb=\\|B\\|,\\quad a_0=\\sup_x\\|A(x)\\|,\\quad a_1=\\operatorname{Lip}(A),\\quad\nl_e=\\sup_x\\|L_e(x)\\|,\\\\\nj_e=\\operatorname{Lip}(L_e)+2bl_e,\\quad\nj=\\max_{l_e>0}j_e/l_e,\\quad\ng=a_1+2ba_0+\\sum_e l_ej_e,\\quad c_0=b+gT,\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\nwith identically zero channels omitted and $j=0$ when none remain. Unitary conjugation gives the displayed tilded-coefficient Lipschitz bounds, including $\\operatorname{Lip}(\\widetilde D)\\le2\\sum_el_ej_e$. Duhamel for contractive propagators bounds their difference on $[s,t]$ by $g(t-s)\\|x-y\\|_{[0,T]}$. Telescoping each history product and the final $U$ gives\n$$\n\\|C_x(h)-C_y(h)\\|\\le\\|x-y\\|_{[0,T]}(c_0+mj)\\prod_{k=1}^ml_{e_k}.\n$$\nSquaring and summing ordered histories, the exponential-series identities for the first two Poisson moments yield\n$$\n\\|V_x-V_y\\|\\le C_V\\|x-y\\|_{[0,T]},\\qquad\nC_V=e^{\\ell T/2}\\sqrt{(c_0+j\\ell T)^2+j^2\\ell T}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3q.4}\n$$\nThe complete output channels differ in trace or diamond norm by at most $2\\min(1,C_V\\|x-y\\|_{[0,T]})$, uniformly over spectators. This proof uses continuity of the driver, with no derivative, variation or stochastic-integral convention. For effective constants use supplied rational upper bounds instead of exact suprema. Give every listed channel a positive bound unless it is explicitly certified identically zero; the same majorant proof works. The algorithm need not compute a global supremum or decide a zero channel.\n\nFor a finite table stage, compute the maximum slope $R_n$ of its finitely many polygons. Partition the physical interval into rational tick intervals including every path knot and requested protocol time, with maximum length $\\Delta_n$. Freeze the tilded coefficients at the left endpoint and use the exactly normalized instrument\n$$\nK_0=e^{-i\\widetilde A\\delta}\\sqrt{I-\\delta\\widetilde D},\\qquad\nK_e=\\sqrt\\delta\\,\\widetilde L_e,\\qquad\n\\sum_\\alpha K_\\alpha^*K_\\alpha=I,\n\\quad \\delta\\ell\\le\\tfrac12.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3q.5}\n$$\nThe square root has the explicit spectral gap $I-\\delta\\widetilde D\\ge I/2$. Let $a$ be the supplied upper bound for $\\|A\\|$ and set\n$$\nM=a+\\ell/2,\\qquad\nD_*=a^2/2+a\\ell/2+\\ell^2+M^2/2+M\\ell,\\qquad\nC=\\sqrt{2D_*}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3q.6}\n$$\nFor one frozen cell write $F=-i\\widetilde A-\\widetilde D/2$. Both $e^{F\\delta}$ and $K_0$ are contractions. Integral remainders and the scalar square-root remainder on $[0,1/2]$ give\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\|e^{F\\delta}-I-F\\delta\\|&\\le M^2\\delta^2/2,\\\\\n\\|e^{-i\\widetilde A\\delta}-I+i\\widetilde A\\delta\\|&\\le a^2\\delta^2/2,\\\\\n\\|\\sqrt{I-\\delta\\widetilde D}-I+\\delta\\widetilde D/2\\|&\\le\\ell^2\\delta^2,\\\\\n\\|K_0-e^{F\\delta}\\|&\\le\n(a^2/2+a\\ell/2+\\ell^2+M^2/2)\\delta^2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe continuous one-jump amplitude at time $s$ is $e^{F(\\delta-s)}\\widetilde L_e e^{Fs}$; its difference from $\\widetilde L_e$ is at most $M\\delta l_e$. Embed the discrete one-jump record as the normalized indicator of its cell. Its embedded coefficient is exactly the constant $\\widetilde L_e$. Thus the cell cross-isometry overlap differs from $K_0^*K_0+\\delta\\sum_e\\widetilde L_e^*\\widetilde L_e=I$ by at most $D_*\\delta^2$. Higher-jump sectors are orthogonal to the discrete image and add no cross-overlap. Telescoping the contractive system/record embeddings gives total overlap error at most $D_*\\sum_k\\delta_k^2$, and therefore isometry difference at most $C\\sqrt{T\\Delta_n}$. This holds for changing frozen coefficients. Multi-event discrete records embed as products of normalized indicators of their distinct occupied cells in the common ordered-time record space.\n\nFreezing the source path costs at most $C_VR_n\\Delta_n$ by (O.7.2.3q.4), applied also to the bounded step coefficient paths. Use the exact final endpoint phase. Choose a computable mesh making both this bound and $C\\sqrt{T\\Delta_n}$ at most $2^{-n}$. Allocate another $2^{-n}$ to matrix pulse approximation. The resulting complete-record isometry error is at most $3\\cdot2^{-n}$, chosen from finite-stage data rather than a modulus for the limit law.\n\nFor the actual matrix dilation, write $V:\\mathbb C^d\\to\\mathbb C^{dr}$ for the tick isometry and use\n$$\nW=\\begin{pmatrix}0&V^*\\\\ V&I-VV^*\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad\nW^*=W,\\quad W^2=I,\\qquad\nH_W=\\frac{\\pi\\hbar}{2\\tau}(I-W).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3q.7}\n$$\nThis acts on $\\mathbb C^d\\oplus\\mathbb C^{dr}$ and sends an input in the first summand to $Vu$ in the output summand. In positive time $\\tau$ it implements $W$, with $\\|H_W\\|\\le\\pi\\hbar/\\tau$. The finite source-label-controlled direct sum supplies the stage's blocks; fresh outcome flags and explicit permutations route every retained system and record. Padding starts in its designated input flag. Retain actual approximation leakage as named failure outcomes in a common orthogonal record space, embedding the ideal isometry there with zero failure component. Hermitian dyadic pulse approximations keep actual evolution exactly unitary and obey\n$$\n\\sum_{\\text{events }e}\\frac{\\tau_e\\|\\widetilde H_e-H_e\\|}{\\hbar}\\le2^{-n}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3q.8}\n$$\nDuhamel gives the total control error. The construction uses the explicit dilation, avoiding a pivot/rank decision or an unspecified gate-synthesis oracle. Coherent experiments retain the record without measurement; monitored experiments read the nominated basis and obtain the actual instrument probabilities and normalized positive-probability updates.\n\nIf the finite tables converge weakly to $\\mu$, equation (O.7.2.3q.4), boundedness of the actual output channels and continuous mapping prove joint source/coherent-output convergence for bounded continuous source tests and the declared complete-record trace tests. The estimates remain uniform over spectators. Together with (O.7.2.3q.2), this realizes the limit of the given fixed source. The bounded-Lipschitz finite coefficient domain includes the state-dependent and off-diagonal examples of Theorem O.7.2.3l. Theorems O.7.2.3u–z extend the coefficient, actual-state and complete-instrument classifications; Proposition O.7.2.3aa keeps this supplier and its information-access/resource contract.\n\n#### Actual clock, finite inventory and paid supplier\n\nThe supplier is exactly Theorem O.7.2.3m's externally driven finite Hermitian-control law on qualified ready cells, with supplied pure stock and retained work memory. Its resource contract allows finite interactions without a refinement-uniform support bound and permits refinement-dependent power. Autonomous control, a bounded continuum power budget and an attained reset cycle require additional suppliers.\n\nAt each stage emit a literal ordered event list containing: the host's timed calibration source/readout/update events; every source fair-bit preparation, readout and cumulative lookup; frame control, inverse-frame decoding and forecast installation; each coherent tick pulse with fresh outcome flag and record routing; each actual monitored readout; and all scalar prefix releases, observation-frame pulses, final phase and final readout. Required compilation and installation finish before the observation window, with their durations and resources charged.\n\nWithin each tick divide its actual physical duration among its finitely many scheduled events and their two cursor updates, assigning every subwindow positive duration with total exactly the tick duration. Finish the preceding subwindow before a prefix release or tick boundary. Future values can be prepared privately before release. The physical time in $P(nt)/a_n$ and (O.7.2.3q.5) is this registered observation time. Shorter subwindows require stronger paid pulses.\n\nCompute the serialized bit count $B_{\\mathrm{data}}$ for: probability numerators and denominator; all path coordinates and knots; full seed and retained copy; label/frame registers; all prefix forecast tables and snapshots; quantum system and fresh outcome/readout registers; every matrix coefficient and address; source/controller workspaces; compilation/installation archives; and the fixed host calibration data. Run the finite lookup and matrix-generation programs to completion and record their actual transition counts and peak workspace. They terminate on the supplied effective input names.\n\nIf $E_0$ is the resulting data/source/readout event count, allocate $E_0+1$ one-hot cursor bits and $2E_0$ cursor flips, giving $3E_0$ run events. Let $a_{\\mathrm{addr}}=\\max(1,\\lceil\\log_2(B_{\\mathrm{data}}+E_0+1)\\rceil)$ and let $p_{\\mathrm{opcode}}$ be the fixed supplier grammar width. The immutable instruction ROM has\n$$\nB_{\\mathrm{ROM}}=3E_0(p_{\\mathrm{opcode}}+3a_{\\mathrm{addr}})\n+B_{\\mathrm{matrix\\ payload}}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3q.9}\n$$\nInclude matrix payload here precisely when it was not counted in $B_{\\mathrm{data}}$; the serialization manifest gives each byte one owner. Add cursor and ROM sizes to the inventory. Allocate at least two complete ready cells and one cell per owned logical payload bit. Each owns its other $74$ physical bits, giving $75N_{\\mathrm{cells}}$ bits for the native carrier. Apply the same rule to separately owned supplier-duty and archive cells. Ready-cell ROM factory loading uses $16N_{\\mathrm{cells}}$ basis writes; other basis data use their actual Hamming-weight writes, and cursor initialization uses one flip. All seeds, measurement apparatus, record blanks and purifiers are owned and retained.\n\nFix $\\tau_0=1\\,\\mathrm s$, $\\hbar$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,s}$, $\\beta_{\\mathrm{phys}}>0$ in $\\mathrm J^{-1}$,\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\ne_*=\\hbar/\\tau_0,\\qquad e_b=e_*+2/\\beta_{\\mathrm{phys}},\\\\\nR_0=e_b/\\tau_0,\\qquad r_p=r_I=e_*/\\tau_0,\\qquad\nv_0=10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm m^3.\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\nOrdinary payload cells have $H_v=e_bI$ and $\\widehat C_v=3I$; separately owned duty cells have $\\widehat C_v=3P_{\\mathrm{idle}}+4P_{\\mathrm{active}}$. Use the admitted native ledger\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR(C)&=R_0+r_p(C-1)^2,&R_I(C)&=r_I\\log_2(C/3),\\\\\nD_v&=[R(\\widehat C_v)-R_0I]\\tau_0+R_I(\\widehat C_v)\\tau_0,\\\\\n\\epsilon_v&=H_v+D_v+\\tfrac12\\sum_{e\\text{ incident to }v}V_e,\n&\\rho_v&=\\epsilon_v/v_0.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{O.7.2.3q.10}\n$$\nEvery finite interaction has two named owning ports and is counted once in the summed energy. Extra support cells retain their storage and baseline costs. Compensate noncommuting duty flips or active-support native costs inside the actual interaction, for example\n$$\nV_{\\mathrm{flip}}=\\frac{\\pi\\hbar}{2\\tau_{\\mathrm{flip}}}(I-X)-D_v.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3q.11}\n$$\nThe total local generator then implements the intended flip up to its recorded scalar phase. Charge the full norm $G_e=\\|V_e\\|$, or a certified explicit upper bound, including compensation. Reserve\n$$\np_{\\mathrm{load},e}=\\frac{e_*}{\\tau_0}+\\frac{G_e}{\\tau_0}+\\frac{e_*}{\\tau_e},\n\\qquad\n\\Delta_p=5r_p+r_I\\log_2(4/3)>0.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3q.12}\n$$\nTake the maximum simultaneous load, including cursor/controller events, divide by $\\Delta_p$ and round upward to determine separately owned duty cells. Add their activation/deactivation pulses, storage and positive durations explicitly. They are externally provisioned. With $A_{\\mathrm{supply}}$ such cells and active count $a$, their exact rated total is\n$$\n(A_{\\mathrm{supply}}-a)(R_0+4r_p)\n+a\\bigl[R_0+9r_p+r_I\\log_2(4/3)\\bigr].\n$$\nIntegrate this once over the actual complete schedule. Peak norms grow inversely with event duration and can diverge under refinement.\n\nEvery supplied pure physical blank has excess free energy $\\beta_{\\mathrm{phys}}^{-1}\\log2$ relative to its dimension-matched maximally mixed reference under the scalar idle Hamiltonian. Thus the initial pure stock carries\n$$\nF_{\\mathrm{stock}}=\\beta_{\\mathrm{phys}}^{-1}B_{\\mathrm{pure}}\\log2,\n\\qquad\n\\frac{d\\langle H_{\\mathrm{total}}\\rangle}{dt}\n=\\langle\\partial_tH_{\\mathrm{total}}\\rangle\n\\quad\\hbox{on smooth intervals}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3q.13}\n$$\nAt a switch charge $\\operatorname{Tr}[\\rho\\,\\Delta H_{\\mathrm{total}}]$; a conservative gross rectangular-pulse reserve is $2\\sum_eG_e$, with independently changing native terms added once. Stored final energy is not charged again as work. All source, table, clock, control and future-prefix records remain owned. These are finite joule/watt balances at every stage; repeated use requires new stock or a separately charged actual reset. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723r\"></a>\n**Theorem O.7.2.3r (A Computable Fixed Stationary Source with a Noncomputable Brownian Limit).** There is one real centered strictly stationary Gaussian sequence $(Z_j)_{j\\in\\mathbb Z}$, with all finite moments, satisfying the following properties.\n\n1. Its covariance $C(k)$ is uniformly computable in $k\\in\\mathbb Z$, is nonnegative, satisfies $\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}C(k)\\le4/3$, and has finite block covariance matrices $C_N\\ge I_N$.\n2. On input integers $N,p\\ge1$, a terminating algorithm returns an exactly normalized finite dyadic probability table on dyadic vectors in $\\mathbb R^N$, with a coupling to $(Z_1,\\ldots,Z_N)$ whose expected maximum coordinate error is at most $2^{-p}$.\n3. Put $S_0=0$, $S_m=\\sum_{j=1}^mZ_j$, and let $P$ be their linear interpolation at integer times. Then $n^{-1/2}P(n\\,\\cdot)\\Rightarrow\\sigma B$ in $C([0,T])$ for every finite $T$ and jointly in $C_0([0,\\infty),\\mathbb R)$ with the compact-open topology. The step paths converge to the same law in $D([0,T])$ with its $J_1$ topology.\n4. Both $\\sigma^2$ and the limiting weak path law are noncomputable. The uniformly computable finite rescaled path laws have no computable convergence modulus in the bounded-metric Wasserstein representation of weak convergence specified below, or in a computably equivalent representation.\n\nThe same infinite stationary source supplies every stage. The finite tables approximate its blocks with stated errors; they are not a replacement triangular array defining a different stationary source.\n\n**Proof.**\n\n**1. Construction of the fixed source.** Fix an effective enumeration of programs with undecidable halting set $\\mathcal K$. For $e\\in\\mathcal K$, let $\\tau_e\\ge1$ be its first halting step, and set $c_e=4^{-(e+1)}$ and $L_e=2^{\\tau_e}$. Take mutually independent doubly infinite standard real Gaussian arrays $(g_j)_{j\\in\\mathbb Z}$ and $(g^e_j)_{j\\in\\mathbb Z}$ for all integers $e\\ge0$. Define\n$$\nY^e_j=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\displaystyle\\frac{\\sqrt{c_e}}{L_e}\\sum_{r=0}^{L_e-1}g^e_{j-r},&e\\in\\mathcal K,\\\\\n0,&e\\notin\\mathcal K,\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\nZ_j=g_j+\\sum_{e\\ge0}Y^e_j.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3r.1}\n$$\nEvery nonzero filter coefficient is dyadic because $\\sqrt{c_e}=2^{-(e+1)}$. Also $\\mathbb E[(Y^e_j)^2]=c_e/L_e$ for each halting index, and $\\sum_e\\mathbb E[(Y^e_j)^2]\\le\\sum_ec_e=1/3$. Thus the series converges in $L^2$. Its independent centered summands converge almost surely as well: apply the maximal inequality for independent centered partial sums to tails with summable variance, select a subsequence with summable tail-variance bounds, and apply Borel--Cantelli. Intersecting the resulting probability-one events realizes all countably many coordinates simultaneously. Finite coordinate vectors are $L^2$ limits of centered Gaussian vectors, hence are Gaussian. Each finite truncation is strictly stationary, and convergence of finite vectors preserves shift invariance. This constructs a single centered strictly stationary Gaussian process with all finite moments.\n\nIts exact covariance is\n$$\nC(k)=\\mathbf1_{\\{k=0\\}}+\n\\sum_{e\\in\\mathcal K}c_e\\frac{(L_e-|k|)_+}{L_e^2}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3r.2}\n$$\nEvery summand is nonnegative and is a covariance kernel. The independent $g_j$ component gives $C_N\\ge I_N$. For every positive integer $L$, the identity $\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}(L-|k|)_+=L^2$ and Tonelli's theorem give\n$$\n\\sigma^2:=\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}C(k)\n=1+\\sum_{e\\in\\mathcal K}c_e,\n\\qquad 1\\le\\sigma^2\\le\\frac43.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3r.3}\n$$\nThis is an ordinary summability bound; the algorithm below uses pointwise covariance estimates rather than a modulus for the covariance tail sum.\n\n**2. Uniform computation of covariance, including late halts.** Given integers $M,K_0\\ge1$, simulate each program $e=0,\\ldots,M$ for exactly $K_0$ steps. Include its covariance term precisely when it halts during this simulation, using its observed exact $\\tau_e$. Denote the resulting finite dyadic covariance by $C_{M,K_0}(k)$. A program not observed to halt can contribute at most $c_e/L_e\\le c_e2^{-K_0}$ at any lag, since $(L_e-|k|)_+/L_e\\le1$. Indices above $M$ contribute at most their respective $c_e$. Therefore, uniformly over $k\\in\\mathbb Z$,\n$$\n0\\le C(k)-C_{M,K_0}(k)\n\\le\\frac13\\,2^{-K_0}+\\frac13\\,4^{-(M+1)}\n=:v_{M,K_0}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3r.4}\n$$\nHere $\\sum_{e>M}4^{-(e+1)}=4^{-(M+1)}/3$. Choosing $M,K_0$ from a requested error computes $C(k)$ uniformly using bounded simulations. A late halt contributes small microscopic variance spread over a long range of lags, while its integrated covariance contribution remains exactly $c_e$. This accounts for the coexistence of computable pointwise covariance and a noncomputable covariance sum.\n\n**3. An explicit normalized finite-prefix table algorithm.** On input $(N,p)$ set $\\delta=2^{-p}$. Choose $M,K_0$ by finite integer search so that\n$$\nN v_{M,K_0}\\le\\frac{\\delta^2}{16}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3r.5}\n$$\nLet $Z^{\\mathrm{fin}}$ be the iid base plus the finitely many detected filter components. Couple it to $Z$ using the same Gaussian arrays. The omitted tail is centered, with coordinate variance at most $v_{M,K_0}$, so\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E\\max_{1\\le j\\le N}|Z_j-Z^{\\mathrm{fin}}_j|\n&\\le\\left(\\mathbb E\\sum_{j=1}^N|Z_j-Z^{\\mathrm{fin}}_j|^2\\right)^{1/2}\\\\\n&\\le\\sqrt{N v_{M,K_0}}\\le\\frac\\delta4.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{O.7.2.3r.6}\n$$\nWrite the retained vector as $Z^{\\mathrm{fin}}=AG$. Use $N$ base variables and $N+L_e-1$ variables for each retained filter, giving the computed finite Gaussian dimension $D=N+\\sum_{\\text{retained }e}(N+L_e-1)$. The matrix $A$ is explicitly dyadic, and every row has absolute sum at most\n$$\n1+\\sum_{\\text{retained }e}\\sqrt{c_e}\\le2.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3r.7}\n$$\nThe computation is finite, with its actual dimension determined by these expressions; its resource requirement need not be polynomial in $(N,p)$.\n\nThe following scalar subroutine produces an exactly normalized finite dyadic approximation to a standard normal variable with any requested $L^2$ error $\\varepsilon>0$.\n\n- Choose an integer $R\\ge1$ making the normal-tail bound\n$$\n\\mathbb E\\!\\left[G^2\\mathbf1_{\\{|G|>R\\}}\\right]\n\\le\\frac{2(R+1/R)e^{-R^2/2}}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}\n$$\nsmall enough. Integration by parts and $1-\\Phi(R)\\le\\varphi(R)/R$, where $\\Phi$ and $\\varphi$ are the standard normal distribution function and density, prove this bound.\n- Clip to $[-R,R]$ and round to a dyadic mesh of width $h$. The tail bound controls the clipping error in $L^2$; rounding error is at most $h$ pointwise.\n- The finitely many bin masses $p_i$ are computable Gaussian integrals, with the endpoint bins including their respective tails. Compute nonnegative dyadic lower weights $q_i\\le p_i$ having total deficit at most a chosen rational $\\eta$. Compute rational lower approximations to the masses, replace negative lower bounds by zero, and round down to a common dyadic denominator. The finite bin count specifies a sufficient precision to bound the total deficit. Place the remainder $1-\\sum_iq_i$ on the explicit atom zero.\n- Couple each retained mass to its original rounded atom and move the deficit to zero. The rounded atoms have modulus at most $R+h$, so reweighting contributes at most $(R+h)^2\\eta$ to the mean-square error. Choose clipping, rounding and reweighting to have $L^2$ errors at most $\\varepsilon/3$ each. Minkowski's inequality gives total $L^2$ error at most $\\varepsilon$.\n\nAll tail bounds, precision choices, atoms and exactly normalized dyadic weights are computed by finite procedures. Apply the subroutine independently to the $D$ coordinates, choosing $\\varepsilon\\le\\delta/(8\\sqrt D)$, and call the resulting vector $\\widehat G$. Product coupling and (O.7.2.3r.7) give\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbb E\\max_j|(AG)_j-(A\\widehat G)_j|\n&\\le2\\mathbb E\\max_i|G_i-\\widehat G_i|\\\\\n&\\le2\\sqrt D\\,\\varepsilon\\le\\frac\\delta4.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{O.7.2.3r.8}\n$$\nThe vector $A\\widehat G$ has finite dyadic atoms and dyadic product weights. Enumerating all product labels yields its literal normalized table, exact denominator and serialized length. Equations (O.7.2.3r.6) and (O.7.2.3r.8) give expected maximum error at most $\\delta/2\\le2^{-p}$. Filter lengths, product labels, bin weights and error bounds all arise from finite loops; the halting simulations terminate after the prescribed $K_0$ steps. This proves the asserted uniform finite-block name directly. The additional bound $C_N\\ge I_N$ also supplies a known positive spectral gap for any alternative principal-square-root construction.\n\n**4. Whole functional convergence.** Stationarity gives\n$$\nV(m):=\\mathbb E S_m^2\n=mC(0)+2\\sum_{k=1}^{m-1}(m-k)C(k).\n$$\nThe summability in (O.7.2.3r.3) and dominated convergence imply\n$$\n\\frac{V(m)}m\\longrightarrow\\sigma^2.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3r.9}\n$$\nFor integers $0\\le a\\le b$, $\\mathbb E[S_aS_b]=[V(a)+V(b)-V(b-a)]/2$. Together with (O.7.2.3r.9), this gives convergence of rescaled step-path covariances to $\\sigma^2\\min(s,t)$. A polygonal endpoint correction is a fraction of one $Z_j/\\sqrt n$ and has $L^2$ norm at most $\\sqrt{C(0)/n}$. Thus the polygonal finite-dimensional covariances have the same limit. The vectors are centered Gaussian, so their characteristic functions converge to those of $\\sigma B$.\n\nTo prove tightness, for $0\\le s<t\\le T$ express the polygonal increment as $n^{-1/2}\\sum_jw_jZ_j$, where $0\\le w_j\\le1$ and $\\sum_jw_j=n(t-s)$. Absolute summability and Cauchy--Schwarz yield, for any finite real weights,\n$$\n\\sum_{i,j}w_iw_jC(i-j)\n\\le\\sum_k|C(k)|\\sum_jw_j^2\n=\\sigma^2\\sum_jw_j^2.\n$$\nSince $\\sum_jw_j^2\\le\\sum_jw_j$, the increment variance is at most $\\sigma^2|t-s|$. Gaussian fourth moments then give the explicit bound, including time intervals shorter than one mesh cell,\n$$\n\\mathbb E\\left|n^{-1/2}[P(nt)-P(ns)]\\right|^4\n\\le3\\sigma^4|t-s|^2\n\\le\\frac{16}{3}|t-s|^2.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3r.10}\n$$\nFix $\\alpha\\in(0,1/4)$ and dyadic meshes of $[0,T]$. Markov's inequality, (O.7.2.3r.10) and a union bound show that the probability of any adjacent level-$r$ increment exceeding $2^{-\\alpha r}$ is at most a constant depending on $T,\\alpha$ times $2^{-r(1-4\\alpha)}$. Summing over $r\\ge R$ gives a bound tending to zero uniformly in $n$. Outside this event, continuity and dyadic chaining bound oscillations over distances at most $T2^{-R}$ by a constant times $2^{-\\alpha R}$. All paths start at zero, so this establishes tightness in $C([0,T])$. Finite-dimensional convergence identifies every subsequential limit as $\\sigma B$.\n\nUse these estimates on successive integer horizons with summable error budgets to obtain tightness and convergence in $C_0([0,\\infty),\\mathbb R)$ for the complete bounded compact-open metric\n$$\nd(x,y)=\\sum_{m\\ge1}2^{-m}\\bigl(1\\wedge\\|x-y\\|_{[0,m]}\\bigr).\n$$\nThe distance on $[0,T]$ between polygonal and step paths is bounded by $\\max_{j\\le\\lceil nT\\rceil}|Z_j|/\\sqrt n$. Continuous-limit tightness bounds this by a vanishing mesh modulus. Alternatively the direct Gaussian union bound gives\n$$\n\\mathbb P\\!\\left(\\max_{j\\le\\lceil nT\\rceil}|Z_j|>\\varepsilon\\sqrt n\\right)\n\\le2\\lceil nT\\rceil\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{\\varepsilon^2n}{2C(0)}\\right)\n\\longrightarrow0.\n$$\nHence the step paths have the same $J_1$ limit.\n\n**5. Noncomputability of the limiting variance.** Set $q=\\sigma^2-1=\\sum_{e\\in\\mathcal K}4^{-(e+1)}$. Suppose that membership in $\\mathcal K$ has been determined for indices below $e$, and subtract their finite contribution from $q$. If $e\\notin\\mathcal K$, the remainder is at most $\\sum_{j>e}c_j=c_e/3$. If $e\\in\\mathcal K$, the remainder is at least $c_e$. An approximation to $q$ with absolute error less than $c_e/6$ distinguishes these cases by comparison with $2c_e/3$. Induction would decide the halting set if $q$ were computable. Therefore $q$ and $\\sigma^2$ are noncomputable. Pointwise computability of $C(k)$ coexists with this result because a computable tail modulus for its infinite sum is absent.\n\n**6. Noncomputability of the weak law and its convergence modulus.** For the metric $d$, let $W_d(\\mu,\\nu)=\\inf\\mathbb E[d(X,Y)]$, with the infimum over couplings. A computable law has finite dyadic tables on rational-knot, dyadic-value paths, constant after an integer horizon, with algorithmically certified $W_d$ error at most $2^{-p}$ on input $p$.\n\nUse the bounded computable observable $F(x)=\\cos(x(1))$. It is $4$-Lipschitz for $d$: the $m=1$ term bounds $\\min(1,|x(1)-y(1)|)$ by $2d(x,y)$, while the cosine difference is at most $\\min(2,|x(1)-y(1)|)$. A computable weak law would therefore have computable integral of $F$, obtained from its finite tables and the Lipschitz error bound. For the asserted limit,\n$$\n\\mathbb E F(\\sigma B)=e^{-\\sigma^2/2}\n\\in[e^{-2/3},e^{-1/2}].\n$$\nThe inverse $u\\mapsto-2\\log u$ is computable on this known compact interval bounded away from zero. A computable value of this integral would compute $\\sigma^2$, contradicting step 5. Thus the limiting weak law is noncomputable. The bounded observable avoids any inference of computable second moments from a computable weak law.\n\nEvery finite rescaled path law is uniformly computable. Apply the finite-prefix algorithm on a chosen integer horizon and approximate the computable scaling factor $n^{-1/2}$ to a declared error. This is uniform over the finitely many atoms after quantization; before quantization the bound $\\mathbb E\\max_{j\\le N}|Z_j|\\le\\sqrt{4N/3}$ also supplies an explicit integrable scaling-error budget. Extend the finite polygons constantly after the chosen horizon. The omitted tail of $d$ is bounded by the corresponding geometric tail. These operations give arbitrary certified $W_d$ precision for each finite rescaled path law, uniformly in its stage index.\n\nA computable $W_d$ convergence modulus would select a stage within half any requested error of the limit; its computable finite-table approximation would meet the other half. This would give a computable limit-law name, a contradiction. Thus even this fixed source, its computable covariance, the known summability bound $4/3$ and the uniform fourth-moment estimate (O.7.2.3r.10) do not supply an effective convergence rate. ∎\n\n**Source-faithful finite physical realization.** For fixed $n,T$, apply the prefix algorithm to $N=\\lceil nT\\rceil$ increments and retain the original normalization $\\sqrt n$. To obtain a prescribed vanishing path-error budget $\\varepsilon_n$, choose expected maximum coordinate error at most $\\varepsilon_n/[2N\\max(1,n^{-1/2})]$ and a separate dyadic polygon-vertex rounding error at most $\\varepsilon_n/2$. The cumulative-coordinate error is at most $N$ times the maximum coordinate error, so the resulting finite tables converge to this source's Brownian limit.\n\nThe source-faithful compiler of Theorem O.7.2.3q and the finite Hermitian-control supplier of Theorem O.7.2.3m implement these tables with their complete paid schedules. A table with denominator $2^b$ is sampled using $b$ fair Hadamard/readout bits and a cumulative lookup, retaining seed, label and all work records. Prefix releases occur at the physical times $k/n$. Forecasts conditioned on an emitted positive-probability source prefix are obtained by rational conditioning of that same full table; conditional on the complete private label, continuation is deterministic. Source-prefix and any enlarged quantum-record filtrations follow the compiler's separate conditioning rules.\n\nPreparation may precede time zero, with its time and resources included in the inventory. Every preparation, lookup, cursor advance, release/readout and requested coherent pulse receives a positive duration in the actual schedule. Subdivide each physical interval $1/n$ among its finitely many scheduled events and charge pulse norms for those durations, retaining source, table, control, clock and future-prefix records. The explicit prefix algorithm populates the compiler's source inputs while the continuum law remains noncomputable. Theorems O.7.2.3t–z and Proposition O.7.2.3aa supply the general state-dependent and quantum-source classification, keep this example's noncomputable-limit boundary and infer no convergence modulus.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723s\"></a>\n**Theorem O.7.2.3s (Fixed Finite Quantization Changes Antipersistent Universality).** Let $(G_j)$ be scalar fractional Gaussian noise with index $0<H<1/2$, unit variance and covariance\n$$\nr(k)=\\frac{|k+1|^{2H}-2|k|^{2H}+|k-1|^{2H}}2.\n$$\nLet $Q$ be odd, finite-valued, measurable and nonzero in $L^2(\\gamma)$, where $\\gamma$ is standard Gaussian measure. Then its polygonal partial sums satisfy\n$$\nn^{-1/2}P_{Q(G)}(n\\,\\cdot)\\Rightarrow\\sigma_QB\n\\quad\\hbox{in }C_0([0,\\infty),\\mathbb R),\\qquad \\sigma_Q^2>0.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3s.1}\n$$\nThe unquantized source instead has $n^{-H}P_G(n\\,\\cdot)\\Rightarrow B_H$. Consequently $n^{-H}P_{Q(G)}(n\\,\\cdot)$ is not tight even at time one. Such fixed finite-alphabet quantizers exist arbitrarily close to the identity in one-coordinate $L^2(\\gamma)$.\n\n*Proof.* Strict concavity of $t^{2H}$ gives $r(k)<0$ for $k\\ge1$. Telescoping second differences gives\n$$\n\\sum_{k=1}^Mr(k)=\\frac{(M+1)^{2H}-M^{2H}-1}{2}\\longrightarrow-\\frac12.\n$$\nThus $\\sum_{k\\ge1}|r(k)|=1/2$ and $\\sum_{k\\in\\mathbb Z}r(k)=0$. Expand $Q(x)=a_1x+R(x)$ in probabilists' Hermite polynomials, with coefficients $a_q$. Oddness removes all even terms. A nonzero finite-valued function cannot equal a nonzero linear function Gaussian-almost-everywhere; hence $R$ is nonzero and has an odd Hermite rank $m\\ge3$. It belongs to every finite $L^p(\\gamma)$ because $Q$ is bounded and the removed term is linear. Absolute summability of $r$ supplies the rank-$m$ summability premise of Theorem O.7.2.3k. That functional theorem applies to $R$ and gives Brownian variance\n$$\n\\sigma_Q^2=\\sum_{\\substack{q\\ge3\\\\q\\text{ odd}}}q!a_q^2\n\\left(1+2\\sum_{k\\ge1}r(k)^q\\right).\n$$\nFor each such odd $q$,\n$$\n1+2\\sum_{k\\ge1}r(k)^q\n=1-2\\sum_{k\\ge1}|r(k)|^q\n\\ge1-2(1/2)^q\\ge\\frac34.\n$$\nTherefore\n$$\n\\sigma_Q^2\\ge\\frac34\\,\\mathbb E[R(G)^2]>0.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3s.2}\n$$\nThe linear contribution at the $\\sqrt n$ normalization is $a_1n^{H-1/2}$ times the tight $n^H$-normalized fGn paths, so it vanishes uniformly in probability on compact intervals. This proves (O.7.2.3s.1). At time one, multiplication by $n^{1/2-H}\\to\\infty$ makes the original $n^H$-normalized variable escape every bounded interval in probability: the limiting normal variable has no atom at zero. Thus the original normalization is not tight.\n\nTo approximate the identity, clip to a large symmetric finite interval and round to a fine symmetric dyadic mesh with an odd tie convention. Gaussian tail square-integrability and vanishing mesh give arbitrarily small $\\mathbb E|Q(G)-G|^2$. Every nontrivial such fixed quantizer has a nonzero higher Hermite component, so (O.7.2.3s.2) applies. A fixed one-coordinate approximation can therefore be arbitrarily accurate while changing long-time universality. ∎\n\nThe source-faithful error budget (O.7.2.3q.2) avoids this change by refining the growing finite source block until accumulated normalized path error vanishes. Replacing the source once and for all by one finite quantizer does not meet that budget.\n\n**Attribution.** The selfsimilar scaling principle is classical Lamperti theory (Lamperti, 1962); Theorem O.7.2.3o supplies its full path-level proof for the fixed-source, fixed-linear-centering contract and its interpolation converse. Theorem O.7.2.3p supplies the centered endpoint construction, and Theorems O.7.2.3q–r establish the effective boundary by explicit constructions. Theorem O.7.2.3s uses the functional Hermite theorem already owned by Theorem O.7.2.3k; its primary analytic source is Nourdin and Nualart (2018).\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723t\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723t-actual-growing-block-attraction-criterion\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3t (Actual Growing-Block Attraction Criterion).**\n\nThe following source-domain criterion and locally continuous coefficient extension retain the fixed-source and complete-record contracts of Theorems O.7.2.3m–s.\n\n#### 1. Source-domain-of-attraction theorem (no proposed limit is an input)\n\nLet one fixed real source have block laws $\\mu_N=\\operatorname{law}(\\xi_1,\\dots,\\xi_N)$. Stationarity, Gaussianity, mixing, moments and independence are **not** needed for this theorem. Let $a_n>0$, $S_0=0$, $S_k=\\sum_{j\\le k}\\xi_j$, and let $X_n$ interpolate $S_k/a_n$ at $k/n$. Work in $E=C_0([0,\\infty),\\mathbb{R})$ with compact-open topology. For integer $T\\ge 1$, put $N=nT$ and, for rational $\\delta>0$, define the following continuous function of the actual first $N$ source coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD(n,T,\\delta) &= (1/a_n) \\max\\{|S_j-S_i|:\\\\\n&\\qquad 0\\le i,j\\le nT,\\ |j-i|\\le\\lceil n \\delta\\rceil+2\\}.\\\\\nQ(n,T,\\delta) &= \\mathbb{E}[\\min(1,D(n,T,\\delta))].\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFor any finite tuple of nonnegative rational times `t=(t_1,...,t_k)` and rational frequencies `theta=(theta_1,...,theta_k)`, define the actual finite-block test\n\n$$\n\\Phi_n(t,\\theta) = \\mathbb{E} \\exp\\Bigl(i \\sum_r \\theta_r X_n(t_r)\\Bigr).\n$$\nOnly `mu_ceil(n max t)` is used (and the zero-length case is deterministic).\n\n**Local assertion S.** The full sequence `X_n` has a weak limit in `E` if and only if both source tests hold:\n\n- **S1 (finite rational Fourier Cauchy data):** $\\Phi_n(t,\\theta)$ is Cauchy in $\\mathbb{C}$ for every displayed rational tuple.\n- **S2 (normalized block oscillation):** for every integer $T\\ge 1$,\n  $\\lim_{\\delta\\downarrow 0,\\ \\delta\\ \\text{rational}}\\limsup_{n\\to\\infty}Q(n,T,\\delta)=0$.\n\nThese conditions mention neither a candidate limiting law nor a path-law distance, and use only explicit bounded continuous functions of the supplied growing source blocks. In particular they are a necessary/sufficient attraction criterion for every non-Gaussian dependent/long-range fixed source in the continuous-path topology. They are not a finite-time decision algorithm or a covariance criterion.\n\n**Proof.** Write $\\omega_T(x,\\delta)=\\sup_{s,t\\in[0,T],\\,|s-t|\\le\\delta}|x(t)-x(s)|$. Convex interpolation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\omega_T(X_n,\\delta) &\\le D(n,T,\\delta)\\\\\nD(n,T,\\delta) &\\le \\omega_T(X_n,\\delta+3/n).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFor the first inequality, each interpolated value is a convex combination of its two adjacent vertices; every pair of those vertices is separated by at most `ceil(n delta)+2` index units. The second follows directly from the vertex times and `ceil(n delta)<=n delta+1`.\n\nIf $X_n$ converges weakly, its laws are tight. On any compact subset of $C[0,T]$, Arzela–Ascoli gives a uniform vanishing modulus. Tightness, the second inequality, and $0\\le\\min(1,D)\\le 1$ imply S2. Continuous evaluations imply S1.\n\nConversely S2 and the first inequality imply\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\delta}\\limsup_n P(\\omega_T(X_n,\\delta)>\\eta)=0\n$$\n\nfor every $\\eta>0$, because this probability is at most $Q(n,T,\\delta)/\\min(1,\\eta)$. Since $X_n(0)=0$, this is the standard tightness criterion in $C[0,T]$. For completeness, choose decreasing meshes with summable exceptional probabilities for successively smaller oscillation tolerances; on the intersection the common modulus and the value at zero give both equicontinuity and boundedness. Finitely many early $n$ may be included in compact sets separately because each individual law is tight. Diagonalize over integer horizons to obtain tightness in $E$.\n\nEvery subsequence therefore has a weakly convergent subsubsequence. Any two such limits have equal characteristic functions on rational frequencies for every tuple of rational times, by S1. Continuity of a finite-dimensional characteristic function extends equality to real frequencies. Uniqueness of characteristic functions gives equal rational-time joint laws. Rational evaluation cylinders generate the Borel sigma-algebra of `E`, so the two path laws agree. Tightness and this unique subsequential limit imply full weak convergence. This proves both directions. No limiting law was supplied to the proof. ∎\n\n**Continuous step-version conclusion.** S2 implies $\\max_{j\\le nT}|\\xi_j|/a_n\\to 0$ in probability, since that maximum is bounded by $D(n,T,\\delta)$ for every $\\delta>0$. Thus the step sum and the polygon differ by a vanishing uniform error on every compact horizon. They have the same continuous limit in the J1 topology. This does not classify discontinuous J1 limits; those require a different oscillation criterion.\n\n**Identification and moment checks.** Once S1–S2 hold, the rational finite-dimensional limiting characteristic data uniquely identify the law. Degeneracy at the zero path holds exactly when every limiting $\\Phi(t,\\theta)=1$; it suffices to test one-time rational tuples to detect a nonzero limit. Finite second moment of a particular limiting evaluation can be tested without uniform integrability by\n\n$$\n\\sup_{M \\text{ integer}\\ge 1} \\lim_n \\mathbb{E}[\\min(M,X_n(t)^2)] < \\infty.\n$$\nEach inner limit exists by weak convergence. Monotone convergence identifies the displayed supremum with `E[X(t)^2]`. This is distinct from convergence of untruncated source moments; that stronger assertion needs uniform integrability. Regular variation and stationarity, when imposed, connect the identified law to O.7.2.3o; they do not replace S1 or S2.\n\n**Effective boundary.** With O.7.2.3q's uniform bounded-max-Wasserstein names for finite source blocks and a positive computable $a_n$, every individual $\\Phi_n$ and $Q(n,T,\\delta)$ is computable. Indeed the phase test is bounded and Lipschitz with an explicitly computable bound from $\\sum|\\theta_r|$ and $\\lceil n\\max t\\rceil/a_n$; the capped maximum has a bound $2nT/a_n$. No exact threshold probabilities or equality tests are needed. S1 and S2 are infinite quantified conditions on these computable numbers. O.7.2.3r rules out inferring a computable convergence rate or computable limit from source computability. The finite-block proof of O.7.2.3q uses only those block names and their consistency with the actual source, not stationarity; it therefore extends verbatim to the nonstationary inputs of S. Finite source tables may nevertheless be compiled to error $3\\cdot 2^{-n}$ by that proof, independently of knowing whether S1–S2 hold or how rapidly they hold.\n\nThe proof uses the classical continuous-path tightness theorem, not a new general probability principle. Primary reference: Billingsley (1999, chapter on `C`). The source-block formulation, the interpolation bounds and their application to the normalized source-faithful interface are proved above.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723u\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723u-locally-continuous-common-coherent-compiler\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3u (Locally Continuous Common Coherent Compiler).**\n\nRetain the exact O.7.2.3q coherent-history interface: one real path, fixed finite system dimension $d$, fixed Hermitian $B$, Hermitian $A(x)$, and finitely many matrices $L_e(x)$. Replace its global bounded-Lipschitz assumptions by **continuity of each matrix entry on $\\mathbb{R}$**. All matrices remain finite at every real $x$. This includes unbounded polynomial coefficients, square-root zero-rate/rank-changing coefficients, and continuous coefficients with no local Lipschitz bound.\n\n**Local assertion C.** For every $x\\in E$ and every finite $T$, O.7.2.3q's formulas define a unique contractive no-jump propagator and normalized complete-history isometry $V_x$. The map $x\\to V_x$ is continuous in operator norm on each compact time horizon. It follows that every source satisfying S1–S2 has joint source/complete-coherent-output convergence for these coefficients, uniformly over arbitrary spectator systems and arbitrary input states in the fixed finite system. All monitored instruments obtained by a fixed record measurement inherit it. No source-path derivative or stochastic-integral convention is introduced.\n\n**Detailed estimate.** Fix $R\\ge 1$; restrict to paths with uniform norms at most $R$. Choose positive finite upper bounds $a_R\\ge\\sup_{|z|\\le R}\\lVert A(z)\\rVert$ and $l_e\\ge\\max(1,\\sup_{|z|\\le R}\\lVert L_e(z)\\rVert)$, and put $\\ell=\\sum_e l_e^{2}$. Denote moduli of the coefficients on $[-R,R]$ by $w_A(r)$ and $w_e(r)$, with each tending to zero. Write $b=\\lVert B\\rVert$ and define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nz_A(r) &= w_A(r)+2b\\, a_R r\\\\\nz_e(r) &= w_e(r)+2b\\, l_e r\\\\\nz_D(r) &= 2 \\sum_e l_e z_e(r)\\\\\ng(r) &= z_A(r)+z_D(r)/2\\\\\nz(r) &= \\max_e z_e(r)/l_e \\qquad [0 \\text{ if no jump channels}]\\\\\nc(r) &= b r + T g(r).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nConjugation by `exp(-iB x(t))` gives exactly these moduli for the tilded coefficients. The tilded generator has nonpositive Hermitian part, hence its ODE has a unique contraction propagator. Contractive Duhamel gives `||G_x(t,s)-G_y(t,s)|| <= (t-s)g(r)` when `||x-y||<=r`. Telescoping the `m`-jump history product, including all no-jump intervals (whose lengths sum to `T`) and the final phase, gives\n\n$$\n\\lVert C_x(h)-C_y(h)\\rVert \\le [c(r)+m\\, z(r)] \\prod_{k=1}^m l_{e_k}.\n$$\nSquaring and integrating the ordered simplices yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\lVert V_x-V_y\\rVert &\\le \\exp(\\ell T/2)\\\\\n&\\quad \\sqrt{[c(r)+\\ell T z(r)]^2 + \\ell T z(r)^2} =: J_{R,T}(r).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis tends to zero as `r->0`; no division by a zero actual channel norm occurs. Normalization is obtained from the absolutely convergent history series with majorant `exp(ell T)` and the exact trace cancellation of gain and loss, precisely as in O.7.2.3q. The complete output-channel diamond distance is at most `2 min(1,J_(R,T)(r))`. For any convergent sequence of paths the sup norms are bounded, so the local estimate proves continuity on all of `E`. Under weak convergence, the bounded continuous map into isometries, or into their channels, gives joint convergence with the source. Alternatively couple the paths by the Skorokhod representation and use bounded convergence; the bound `2` removes any need for integrable coefficient moments. ∎\n\n**Effective finite implementation.** Require computable coefficient functions with supplied effective compact moduli and norm bounds, uniformly in rational `R`. This is an information-access condition, not inferred from continuity. At stage `n`, use the same source block table of O.7.2.3q. Its finite list of polygons gives a computable cap `R_n` and maximum slope `v_n`. Choose a tick mesh `Delta_n` so that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\Delta_n \\ell_{R_n} < 1/2,\\\\\n&J_{R_n,T}(v_n \\Delta_n) < 2^{-n},\\\\\n&C_{R_n,T} \\sqrt{T \\Delta_n} < 2^{-n},\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the frozen-coefficient constant $C$ is O.7.2.3q.6 with the local bounds. Use certified strict comparisons, with rational enclosure refinement; no equality test of computable reals is required. An effective search terminates using the supplied moduli and slack. Include every source knot and protocol time in the mesh. Use the identical exact normalized tick instruments\n$K_0=\\exp(-i\\tilde{A}\\delta)\\sqrt{I-\\delta\\tilde{D}}$, $K_e=\\sqrt{\\delta}\\,\\tilde{L}_e$, and final phase. The matrix gap is at least $I/2$. The whole-record discretization and freezing estimates remain valid with these local constants; another $2^{-n}$ pays for actual Hermitian pulse approximation. All emitted scalar paths still come from the actual source-block approximants, not a replacement limit-law sampler. Neither the source's unknown rate nor a global coefficient bound is required.\n\nUniform path-law source errors alone need not provide a numerical output rate when coefficients are unbounded. The theorem claims convergence by localization, and separately a prescribed implementation error relative to each finite source stage. A quantitative source-to-output error additionally needs a supplied path-tail bound and a compact coefficient modulus; it must not be silently inferred.\n\n#### 3. Records, conditioning, initialization and resource ownership\n\nThis extension changes coefficients and source tests, not the physical supplier. Keep O.7.2.3q's literal complete source table, fair-bit seed, unreleased future label, inverse host frame, actual finite record isometries, protocol times, pure blanks, compilation/lookup archives, ROM/cursor/duty cells and event ledger. Keep the actual physical observation clock. Every refinement has finite positive event durations, exact normalized alternatives, actual monitored readouts and paid final phase. Use the original pulse norms and gross switch-work reserves, with the larger local coefficient/mesh values. Retain all work/source records; repeated use needs fresh stock or a separately paid reset. No refinement-uniform power/support/autonomy/reset claim is added.\n\nThe initial system state remains the user's quantum input; the implementation acts coherently on it, rather than estimating it to choose a classical simulation. Any declared classical initial-state law or independent random selector must be in the supplied joint block name and charged preparation. The source-prefix forecast formula remains valid only for that filtration; if quantum records are revealed first, use the same label masses times actual instrument likelihoods. Complete-instrument convergence implies unnormalized event-output convergence. For a fixed measurable event of the retained quantum record, trace-norm convergence controls its unnormalized output; if its limiting probability is $p>0$, normalization is continuous, so posterior states converge. For events also involving the classical source, require a continuity-set premise (or a separate stronger joint convergence mode); weak source convergence does not control arbitrary source-event indicators; there is no uniform claim on histories whose probabilities vanish. An exact continuous-time history is a density, not a positive-mass atom: conditioning must use a declared disintegration or finite measurable event, not divide by zero.\n\nSTOP, archive retention, record coarsening, and fixed bounded interventions are channels on the retained record and system; compose them with the actual complete instrument. Their contraction cannot worsen the error. A discontinuous/adaptive intervention whose decision boundary has positive limiting mass requires its own convergence premise. Arbitrary postselection is not automatically uniformly stable.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723v\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723v-state-coordinate-generator-classification-and-actual-feedback-sources\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3v (State-Coordinate Generator Classification and Actual Feedback Sources).**\n\nThis separates a classification of a generator from existence/uniqueness of its process and from realization of its actual microscopic source.\n\n#### 1. Locality classifier on the recorded rich-Feller domain\n\nLet $A$ be the generator of a sub-Markov Feller semigroup on $C_0(\\mathbb{R}^d)$, with $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb{R}^d)$ contained in $D(A)$. In the fixed truncation $\\chi(z)=1_{|z|\\le 1}$, the Courrège representation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA f(x) &= -k(x)f(x)+b(x)\\cdot\\nabla f(x)+(1/2)\\operatorname{tr}[a(x)D^{2}f(x)]\\\\\n&\\quad + \\int[f(x+z)-f(x)-\\chi(z)z\\cdot\\nabla f(x)]\\, \\nu(x,dz).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $k\\ge 0$, $a$ is symmetric nonnegative, and $\\nu$ is a positive kernel on $\\mathbb{R}^d\\setminus\\{0\\}$ (equivalently $\\nu(x,\\{0\\})=0$) with $\\int(1\\wedge|z|^{2})\\nu(x,dz)<\\infty$. The representation, with its ordinary regularity/measurability conditions, is an imported theorem, not a new claim (Courrège 1965–1966). Its hypothesis is a rich Feller generator.\n\n**Exact locality test.** On this test-function domain, $A$ is local if and only if $\\nu(x,\\cdot)=0$ at every $x$. Indeed, if $f$ vanishes in a neighborhood of $x$, every displayed differential term is zero and $Af(x)=\\int f(x+z)\\nu(x,dz)$. Locality forces this to vanish for every nonnegative smooth $f$ supported away from $x$. Such functions detect every positive Radon mass away from $z=0$; exhaustion gives $\\nu=0$. The converse is immediate. On the additional no-killing convention $k=0$, a nonzero $a$ gives the second-order diffusion branch; $a=0$ leaves deterministic first order. A nonzero jump kernel is not a local second-order closure even when its moments exist. Killing is an owned loss/cemetery output, not an unrecorded normalization adjustment.\n\nThis classifies the **operator form** of the recorded rich-Feller sector, including arbitrary state-coordinate coefficients. Semigroup generation by a given measurable quadruple, the core property of Cc∞, conservativity and the boundary condition are independent fields beyond the operator form. In particular, nonexplosion is not inferred just from $k=0$. For a concrete approximation theorem, the theorem of Böttcher and Schilling (2009) requires Cc∞ to be an operator core and a uniform quadratic symbol bound. That theorem concerns its own frozen-symbol chain, which is a nominated source construction; another source with the same formal coefficients needs its own convergence proof.\n\n#### 2. Actual finite source with nonlinear state feedback\n\nFix dimensions $d,m\\ge 1$, $x_0\\in\\mathbb{R}^d$, and globally Lipschitz functions $b:\\mathbb{R}^d\\to\\mathbb{R}^d$ and $\\sigma:\\mathbb{R}^d\\to\\mathbb{R}^{d\\times m}$. They may be unbounded, degenerate, zero or rank-changing. Global Lipschitz gives constants $L,K$ with\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\n\\lvert b(x)-b(y)\\rvert+\\lVert\\sigma(x)-\\sigma(y)\\rVert_F\\le L\\lvert x-y\\rvert, \\\\\n\\lvert b(x)\\rvert^{2}+\\lVert\\sigma(x)\\rVert_F^{2}\\le K(1+\\lvert x\\rvert^{2}).\n\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nThe microscopic source at mesh $h=1/n$ is one fair-bit tape $\\varepsilon_0,\\varepsilon_1,\\dots$ with independent uniform vectors $\\varepsilon_k\\in\\{-1,+1\\}^m$ and the actual recurrence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nY_0 &= x_0,\\\\\nY_{k+1} &= Y_k+h\\, b(Y_k)+\\sqrt{h}\\, \\sigma(Y_k)\\, \\varepsilon_k.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{E1}\n$$\n\nThe source program, coefficients and $x_0$ are fixed independently of any limiting law. The output is the polygon $Y^n(t)$ through $Y_k$ at physical times $kh$. Every finite horizon uses finitely many tape bits and has a finite complete tree, including all zero or coincident-output branches. Distinct seeds need not be identified when their numerical states coincide.\n\n**Theorem.** These actual polygonal source laws converge on every compact time horizon in $C([0,T],\\mathbb{R}^d)$ to the unique nonexplosive solution of\n\n$$\ndX_t=b(X_t)\\,dt+\\sigma(X_t)\\,dW_t,\\quad X_0=x_0.\n\\tag{E2}\n$$\n\nThis is a whole coefficient class, not one driven instance. Its generator is\n\n$$\nAf=b\\cdot\\nabla f+(1/2)\\operatorname{tr}(\\sigma\\sigma^T D^{2}f).\n$$\n\n**Moment and compact-containment proof.** Let $F_k$ be the tape-prefix filtration. Conditional centering and covariance give\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{E}[Y_{k+1}-Y_k|F_k]=h\\,b(Y_k),\n$$\n\n$\\mathbb{E}[(\\sigma(Y_k)\\varepsilon_k)(\\sigma(Y_k)\\varepsilon_k)^T|F_k]=\\sigma(Y_k)\\sigma(Y_k)^T$.\nFor $h\\le 1$, the linear-growth bound and discrete Gronwall first give $\\sup_{k\\le nT}\\mathbb{E}|Y_k|^{2}\\le C_T(1+|x_0|^{2})$. Write $Y_k=x_0+\\sum_{j<k}h\\,b(Y_j)+M_k$. Doob's $L^{2}$ inequality gives $\\mathbb{E}\\max_{k\\le nT}|M_k|^{2}\\le 4\\sum_{j<nT}h\\,\\mathbb{E}\\lVert\\sigma(Y_j)\\rVert_F^{2}$. Cauchy–Schwarz bounds the maximum drift-square by $T\\sum h\\,\\mathbb{E}|b(Y_j)|^{2}$. Thus\n$\\mathbb{E}\\max_{k\\le nT}|Y_k|^{2}\\le C'_T(1+|x_0|^{2})$,\nwith constants independent of $n$. Polygonal interpolation cannot increase the maximum norm. Consequently $\\sup_n P(\\sup_{t\\le T}|Y^n(t)|>R)\\le C'_T(1+|x_0|^{2})/R^{2}$, proving compact containment in state space, not yet path-space tightness.\n\n**Tightness proof.** Stop at the first mesh point outside a ball of radius $R$ and freeze thereafter; its final increment starts inside the ball and therefore still uses bounded coefficients. For mesh-valued stopping indices $\\tau\\le nT$ and a deterministic time increment $\\delta$, conditional martingale orthogonality and Cauchy–Schwarz bound the stopped displacement second moment by $C_R[(\\delta+h)+(\\delta+h)^{2}]$. The same estimate works for bounded stopping intervals of length at most $\\delta$, by optional sampling of the martingale and its predictable quadratic variation. Aldous' stopping-time criterion gives $D$-tightness. On the event $\\sup_k|Y_k|\\le R$, every jump of the step version is bounded by $h\\sup_{|x|\\le R}|b(x)|+\\sqrt{mh}\\sup_{|x|\\le R}\\lVert\\sigma(x)\\rVert_F$, which vanishes. Compact containment then makes every subsequential limit continuous. The polygon and step versions differ by at most the largest mesh increment, so polygonal laws are tight in $C$ and share the continuous limits.\n\n**Generator identification.** For $f\\in C_c^\\infty$ and $x$ in a fixed ball, Taylor's formula at $x$, $\\mathbb{E}\\varepsilon=0$ and $\\mathbb{E}\\varepsilon\\varepsilon^T=I$ imply\n\n$$\nA_nf(x):=h^{-1}\\mathbb{E}[f(x+h\\,b(x)+\\sqrt{h}\\sigma(x)\\varepsilon)-f(x)]=b(x)\\cdot\\nabla f(x)+(1/2)\\operatorname{tr}[\\sigma\\sigma^T D^{2}f(x)]+r_n(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\sup_{|x|\\le R}|r_n(x)|\\le C_{f,R}\\sqrt{h}$. The $h\\,b\\,b^{T}$ second-moment term is included in this remainder; all local third moments are $O(h^{3/2})$. The exact discrete process\n$f(Y_k)-f(x_0)-\\sum_{j<k}h\\,A_nf(Y_j)$\nis a martingale on its actual source filtration. Stop in a ball. Along any convergent subsequence, the local uniform generator estimate and Riemann-sum convergence transfer its bounded stopped martingale identities to\n$f(X_t)-f(x_0)-\\int_0^t Af(X_s)\\,ds$.\nLocalizing to radii at which the exit map is almost surely continuous, and then increasing the radius using the displayed containment bound, yields the martingale problem for $A$. No weak convergence of input labels or sampled coefficients is substituted for this conditional identity.\n\n**Existence and uniqueness.** Global Lipschitz/linear growth gives a unique strong solution of (E2) by Picard iteration on $L^{2}$ adapted processes on short intervals and concatenation; the estimate $\\mathbb{E}\\sup_{s\\le T}|X_s|^{2}\\le C_T(1+|x_0|^{2})$ prevents explosion. The same Lipschitz estimate and Gronwall give pathwise uniqueness. The diffusion martingale problem associated with $b$ and $a=\\sigma\\sigma^T$ therefore has this unique law. One direct route constructs the Brownian driving representation of any solution's continuous local martingale with covariance $\\int a\\,ds$, adjoining independent Brownian components on the kernel of $\\sigma$ as needed, and then applies pathwise uniqueness and the standard weak/strong uniqueness implication. Thus all subsequential limits coincide, proving the full convergence. These standard martingale/SDE facts are used on the stated global Lipschitz domain; no assertion of uniqueness for arbitrary continuous $b$ or $a$ is made.\n\n#### 2a. Local coefficients, Lyapunov nonexplosion and finite-source localization\n\nThe construction also covers locally Lipschitz $b,\\sigma$ with an actual nonexplosion certificate, not only global growth bounds. Suppose $V\\in C^{2}(\\mathbb{R}^d)$, $V\\ge 1$, $V(x)\\to\\infty$ as $|x|\\to\\infty$ and $AV\\le CV$. Local Lipschitz coefficients give pathwise unique solutions up to explosion. Apply Itô's formula to $\\exp(-Ct)V(X_t)$, stopped at the first exit $\\tau_{R}$ from the radius-$R$ ball and with ordinary bounded localization. Its expectation is at most $V(x_0)$, so\n\n$$\nP(\\tau_R\\le T) \\le \\exp(CT)V(x_0)/\\inf_{|x|=R}V(x).\n$$\n\nThe right side vanishes as $R\\to\\infty$. Thus the unique local solution is global. A known certificate $V,C$ also gives an actual finite-horizon exit-error budget.\n\nChoose a smooth cutoff $\\chi_{R}$ equal to one on the radius-$R$ ball and zero outside the radius-$(R+1)$ ball. At source stage $n$ use (E1) with $b_n=\\chi_n b$ and $\\sigma_n=\\chi_n\\sigma$. These are globally Lipschitz for each fixed $n$ but their bounds need not be uniform. Do not apply the previous moment bound with $n$-dependent constants. Instead fix $R$ and compare the source stage to the Euler chain with the fixed cutoff $\\chi_{R+1}$. Couple their fair tapes. For all $n\\ge R+2$ their paths agree until the first mesh exit from the radius-$R$ ball. The fixed-cutoff convergence theorem applies; choose arbitrarily large radii $R$ for which the limiting supremum has no atom at $R$. The probability of a source exit by $T$ then converges to the corresponding probability for the fixed-cutoff diffusion. That diffusion agrees with the original solution up to this exit, and the displayed Lyapunov bound controls it. First $n\\to\\infty$ and then $R\\to\\infty$ proves convergence of the actual stage-$n$ cutoff sources to the original nonexplosive diffusion. Their coherent outputs converge by the same compact-localization argument.\n\nThe cutoff and all escape/large-excursion labels remain part of each finite source. There is no conditioning on staying in a ball. On the complement of the coupled no-exit event the outputs remain fully retained and are charged; the event probability supplies a bounded-response error, not an erased failure. Computable local coefficients, local Lipschitz bounds and cutoffs give the finite effective trees. If a numerical global tail budget is claimed, the Lyapunov certificate and its coercive lower bounds must be effective too. Mere existence of V does not give a computable inverse coercivity modulus.\n\nThis supplies a broad nonexplosive state-dependent class including superlinear coefficients satisfying the Lyapunov test.\n\n#### 3. Effective finite source, actual clock and source ownership\n\nFor effective construction require computable $x_0,b,\\sigma$, with supplied effective Lipschitz/linear-growth bounds and compact evaluation procedures. For each $n$ and finite horizon the full finite tree of (E1) is computable. Evaluate every retained vertex to certified error $\\eta_n\\to 0$ and release a dyadic approximate vertex at the same physical time $kh$. This is evaluated from the original complete seed, not propagated from a rounded previous vertex: the exact real recurrence has a finite computable description, and each of its finitely many vertices is approximated with its own certified error. The max path error is then at most $\\eta_{n}$, regardless of tree size. Retain the original seed, circuit, coefficient evaluations, error certificates and unused future labels. The new finite path law is a deterministic source-level approximation with an explicit coupling, not a supplied name for the solution law of (E2).\n\nThe finite-tape sampler and retained-label lookup service of O.7.2.3q apply directly: the source table now evaluates (E1) rather than normalized partial sums. Exactly $m\\lceil nT\\rceil$ fair bits suffice for the tape; each branch mass is $2^{-m\\lceil nT\\rceil}$. The implementation may use a table of exponential size; no efficiency bound is asserted. Every realization has finitely many preparation, computation, release, clock and archive operations. Positive-duration operations must be scheduled into the source's registered physical release intervals or prepared before the release experiment; the growing control strengths and preparation time are paid exactly as in O.7.2.3q. No refinement-uniform power, support, fixed preparation budget or autonomous-reset claim follows. Coincident numerical paths do not erase different physical seeds or their costs.\n\nAn initial law instead of $x_0$ needs its own actual source and approximation/error record. An arbitrary intervention that changes $b,\\sigma$ or the classical state must be part of the nominated source/policy and complete tree. This proof does not silently give access to an unknown quantum input or to a future classical outcome.\n\n#### 4. Common coherent output on the same state-feedback source\n\nLet $A(x)$ be Hermitian $d_q\\times d_q$ matrices and $L_e(x)$ finitely many $d_q\\times d_q$ matrices, continuous for $x\\in\\mathbb{R}^d$. Use Theorem O.7.2.3u's complete-history construction with $B=0$ (or an explicitly supplied continuous unitary frame). Its proof uses only a bound on the path range and compact coefficient moduli, and therefore extends verbatim from scalar to vector $x$ with the Euclidean metric. In particular $x\\mapsto V_x$ is locally uniformly operator-norm continuous on each compact horizon, and the complete output channel has diamond distance at most twice the isometry difference, uniformly over unknown quantum inputs and spectators.\n\nApply this to $Y^n\\Rightarrow X$. Tightness confines paths to a compact range with high probability; on that range the local modulus tends to zero. The continuous mapping theorem, or a Skorokhod coupling followed by bounded convergence, yields joint source-path and complete coherent-output convergence. Each finite seed controls the actual normalized finite tick instruments computed from its own $Y^n$; positive-time pulse approximations, record meshes and complete source errors are separately budgeted. Thus the scalar/quantum output is not realized from an unrelated sampler of the limiting diffusion. Complete records, unused tape, source controls and quantum environments remain owned.\n\nThis covers a whole nonlinear classical state-feedback diffusion class coupled to arbitrary continuous finite-dimensional coherent instruments. It does not cover a quantum measurement outcome changing the classical source unless that hybrid feedback is included in the actual joint source and proved; nor does it infer a local hyperbolic spatial field from a temporal diffusion.\n\n#### 5. Boundaries of the state-dependent classification\n\n- The global Lipschitz proof above is a concrete sufficient domain for process existence; outside it, nonexplosion and martingale-problem uniqueness require separate data.\n- The local generator $x^{2}\\partial_x$ with $x_0=1$ has deterministic trajectory $1/(1-t)$, exploding at $t=1$ despite zero killing coefficient. Stopping at explosion retains a cemetery outcome; it does not produce a conservative global solution on $\\mathbb{R}$.\n- On a state space with boundary, identical interior differential expressions can have different reflecting, absorbing or other domains. $C_c^\\infty$ of the interior need not be a core for the selected boundary semigroup. The source owns the boundary behavior.\n- Merely continuous drifts may admit nonunique ODE/martingale solutions. A selection realized by a source cannot be inferred from the formal generator alone.\n- Generator convergence plus uniqueness is a sufficient actual-source criterion, not necessary for every weakly convergent microscopic family; microscopic oscillations may prevent generator convergence while averaged laws converge. The separate exact block-test theorem is the correct general convergence criterion on its stated continuous-path domain.\n- The Courrège and Euler statements classify their displayed generator and constructive coefficient domains. The actual-source criteria of Theorems O.7.2.3t and O.7.2.3w cover source convergence outside those sufficient coefficient hypotheses; they do not infer a unique solution from an underspecified formal generator.\n\n#### Sources and import boundary\n\nThe tightness/martingale-problem framework used above is the standard one in Ethier and Kurtz (1986, Chapters 3–5). The displayed finite-source conditional moments, local generator remainder, fair-tape coupling, source-tree precision and ownership joins are derived here. Standard Lipschitz diffusion existence/uniqueness is also discussed by Stroock and Varadhan (1972).\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723w\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723w-complete-quantum-source-corner-and-tail-classification\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3w (Complete Quantum Source Corner and Tail Classification).**\n\nRetain the exact complete-record interface. This is a classification of a specified source sequence, not a replacement law sampler or a promise that every CP limit is a second-order scalar field.\n\n#### 1. Retained-output convergence from explicit finite source data\n\nFix input dimension $d<\\infty$, including every input port in the declared finite protocol. Fix one separable output/retained-record Hilbert space $K$ with a declared orthonormal basis, and nested finite-rank projections $P_k\\to I$ strongly. Every actual finite source stage supplies a CPTP map $\\Phi_n:T(\\mathbb{C}^d)\\to T(K)$ after its **declared source-faithful record embedding**. That embedding includes system, coherent history/time records, STOP/FAIL flags, and every quantum record retained by the comparison contract. It may not discard source-specific phases or coherences to force convergence. Classical private archives that are outside the observed output remain physically owned and paid; whether they are retained output is fixed before comparison, not changed with $n$.\n\nLet $\\Omega_d=d^{-1/2}\\sum_i|i,i\\rangle$ and define the normalized actual Choi operator\n\n$$\nJ_n=(\\Phi_n \\otimes \\mathrm{id}_d)(|\\Omega_d\\rangle\\langle\\Omega_d|).\n$$\nThis is mathematical notation for the actual channel's matrix coefficients, not an instruction to access an unknown source by obtaining and manipulating a free Choi state. For a known finite stage circuit/instrument, its entries are finite products/sums of the actual declared amplitudes; for an unknown black box their computability is not inferred.\n\n**Local assertion Q.** The full sequence `Phi_n` converges in diamond norm to a normal CPTP map with this complete retained output if and only if:\n\n1. **QC (finite corners):** every matrix entry $\\langle u,a|J_n|v,b\\rangle$ in the displayed countable output basis and finite input-reference basis is Cauchy;\n2. **QT (output tightness):** $\\lim_{k\\to\\infty}\\limsup_n\\operatorname{Tr}[((I-P_k)\\otimes I_d)J_n]=0$.\n\nThese conditions are finite-coordinate tests and an explicit tail test on actual finite-stage source amplitudes. They do not name an unknown target channel or use the assertion 'the channels converge' as an input.\n\n**Proof.** Necessity of QC follows from trace-norm convergence of Choi operators. For QT, choose a finite-rank projection carrying almost all of the limit Choi trace, then use trace-norm convergence. Conversely, put $R_k=P_k\\otimes I_d$. For a positive trace-one operator $J$, purification and Cauchy–Schwarz give\n\n$$\n\\lVert J-R_k J R_k\\rVert_1 \\le 2 \\sqrt{\\operatorname{Tr}[(I-R_k)J]}.\n\\tag{Q1}\n$$\nThis also follows by writing a purification as its projected and orthogonal components, comparing the two rank-one operators, and tracing the purifying system. QC makes each finite corner Cauchy in trace norm, since it is a fixed finite matrix. Given `epsilon>0`, QT chooses a corner with sufficiently small asymptotic tails; Q1 then makes the whole sequence trace-norm Cauchy. Its limit `J>=0` has trace one and `Tr_K J=I_d/d`, since these properties are closed in trace norm. The finite-input Choi reconstruction\n\n$$\n\\Phi(\\rho)=d\\, \\operatorname{Tr}_{\\mathrm{ref}}[(I \\otimes \\rho^{\\mathsf T})J]\n$$\ntherefore defines a normal CPTP map. The normalized-Choi inequalities\n\n$$\n\\lVert J_n-J\\rVert_1 \\le \\lVert\\Phi_n-\\Phi\\rVert_\\diamond \\le d\\, \\lVert J_n-J\\rVert_1\n\\tag{Q2}\n$$\nprove equivalence with diamond convergence. For the upper bound one can purify an arbitrary system–reference input and express that vector as `(I tensor A)Omega_d` with `||A||<=sqrt(d)`; convexity and a spectator isometry give the general case. Thus all input coherences and arbitrary spectator dimensions are controlled. ∎\n\n**Why both tests matter.** Sending a fixed pure input to $|n\\rangle$ makes every finite corner tend to zero while the trace escapes to infinity; QC holds and QT fails. Alternating two retained relative phases at fixed finite support satisfies QT but fails QC. A test only of record probabilities misses the latter. These are distinct obstructions, not Gaussian/covariance questions.\n\n**Effectivity.** With supplied computable finite stage amplitudes and an effective declared output embedding/basis/projection family (including computable finite projection overlaps), individual corner entries and finite-stage tails are computable. Arbitrary noneffective record embeddings do not inherit this claim. Infinite QC/QT are not automatic convergence moduli. If effective Cauchy bounds for each corner and an effective uniform-tail bound are supplied, Q1–Q2 give a computable diamond Cauchy modulus. Without them the theorem asserts existence/identification, not a universal algorithm or rate. The finite stages can still be physically implemented to individually prescribed errors under the original supplier contract.\n\n#### 2. Joint actual classical paths and retained quantum records\n\nAveraging the classical source away would lose original source/quantum correlations. To retain them, let the actual finite source supply a CP instrument over a continuous classical path space $E=C([0,T],\\mathbb{R}^r)$ together with $K$, with normalized positive Choi operator-valued measure $M_n(dx)$. Its scalar trace measure is $\\nu_n(dx)=\\operatorname{Tr}M_n(dx)$; $M_n(E)$ has reference marginal $I_d/d$. The same construction works for joint source and actual classical state-coordinate records, including source-dependent coefficients and outcomes, by including those admitted classical coordinates in $x$. A posterior density operator of an arbitrary unknown quantum input is not a freely available classical record and may not be inserted into $x$; derived posterior statistics require a declared fixed-known-input model or an actual instrument producing them. The instrument affinity constraint remains in force. Initial coordinate laws must be tight; they are not assumed to start at zero unless the actual source does.\n\nUse the following actual finite-block tests:\n\n- the scalar laws $\\nu_n$ have tight initial coordinates and, when they are grid polygons as in S, satisfy its polygonal oscillation test componentwise; for general continuous paths use instead $\\lim_{\\delta}\\limsup_n\\int\\min(1,\\omega_T(x,\\delta))\\,\\nu_n(dx)=0$;\n- $\\lim_k\\limsup_n\\operatorname{Tr}[((I-P_k)\\otimes I_d)M_n(E)]=0$;\n- for every pair of finite output/reference basis indices and every rational-time/rational-frequency tuple, the mixed corner Fourier integrals\n  $\\int\\exp(i\\sum\\theta_j\\cdot x(t_j))\\,\\langle u,a|M_n(dx)|v,b\\rangle$\n  are Cauchy.\n\n**Local assertion QJ.** These are necessary and sufficient for a unique limit instrument in the following exact topology: for every bounded continuous classical-path test $f$, the operators $\\int f(x)M_n(dx)$ converge in trace norm; equivalently their reconstructed finite-input maps converge in diamond norm. The limiting positive operator-valued measure retains every classical/quantum correlation tested by this interface. This is weak convergence in the classical path coordinate, not total variation on all path events.\n\n**Proof.** Scalar path tightness gives subsequential tightness of every finite corner's matrix-valued measures. Positivity gives, for a partition $(B_j)$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\sum_j |\\langle p|M_n(B_j)|q\\rangle| \\le\\\\\n&\\quad \\sqrt{\\langle p|M_n(E)|p\\rangle\\langle q|M_n(E)|q\\rangle} \\le 1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nMore locally the same estimate applies to any measurable subset. Thus the signed real/imaginary entry measures have uniformly bounded total variation and inherit scalar tightness. For each finite corner, extract convergent entry measures, then diagonalize over corners. Positivity and consistency survive; the output-tail condition and Q1 prevent loss of trace. They give a countably additive positive trace-class operator-valued measure `M`, with scalar trace a probability measure and reference marginal `I_d/d`.\n\nFor detail on the infinite output, decompose each positive $M_n$ with respect to its scalar trace measure (or apply the same estimates first to finite partitions). Integrating Q1 and using Cauchy–Schwarz gives, for $|f|\\le 1$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\Bigl\\lVert\\int f(x)[M_n(dx)-R_k M_n(dx)R_k]\\Bigr\\rVert_1\\\\\n&\\quad \\le 2 \\sqrt{\\operatorname{Tr}[(I-R_k)M_n(E)]}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{QJ1}\n$$\nThis justifies both countable additivity and passage from finite-corner weak convergence to trace-norm convergence of every continuous test integral. The mixed Fourier Cauchy data uniquely identify all finite corner measures: on rational evaluation cylinders they are characteristic transforms of finite complex measures, determined by real/imaginary parts and Fourier uniqueness; path tightness and continuity extend this to the path Borel sigma-algebra. Thus every subsequential instrument limit is the same. The trace-preservation constraint passes through the integral with `f=1`. Necessity follows from the assumed topology, scalar tightness and the same finite-corner/tail estimates. ∎\n\nThe source path need not be independent of the quantum input or outcomes: the reference marginal constraint is only on the whole instrument. For a truly external fixed scalar source, retain that stronger source-independence relation as an additional equality on `M_n`; it survives the limit. State dependence is therefore carried by actual source-stage joint coordinates and instrument amplitudes, not by replacing a conditional law by unrelated draws from a desired limiting distribution.\n\n**Conditioning.** A fixed measurable event in the quantum record is a bounded CP operation and obeys Q2. For classical path events require a continuity-set premise, or a separately supplied stronger convergence mode. On an event with limiting probability $p>0$, normalized posteriors converge after the unnormalized event outputs do. No uniform result is claimed for rare histories with $p\\to 0$, and a single exact continuous event time has zero mass unless the source has an atom there.\n\n#### 3. Interventions, causality and a real finite source\n\nFor a fixed finite protocol with intermediate inputs, the source supplies its actual finite causal circuit/instrument, including the sequential memory and registered intervention ports. One can collect its finite port input/reference matrix elements (its process/comb Choi coefficients) only as a mathematical description of that actual circuit. Apply the same finite-corner/tail argument to this positive process operator; use its fixed normalization constant rather than trace one. Causal partial-trace identities are linear and trace-norm closed, so the limit satisfies the same identities. For fixed finite port dimensions, contraction with any admissible tester is continuous with a finite dimension-dependent bound. Hence all adaptive finite testers and arbitrary spectators converge, not just a preselected list of outcome probabilities.\n\nIf the original protocol menu is countable, apply the tests to each finite protocol and require the **actual** cross-protocol restriction identities. Their equalities pass to limits. This classifies compatible finite-protocol responses. It does not manufacture an implementation for an arbitrary asserted family: the actual stage circuits and their source/service access are the realization input. No counterfactual intervention query is made to an unknown source. The causal source interface and actual token/STOP rules are those of Proposition 42c.13.\n\nAt a finite stage, implement the given finite coherent circuit (or its supplied finite Kraus dilation) using O.7.2.3q's Hermitian isometry dilation with exactly normalized instruments, fresh owned records and pulse-error budget. The phase/coherence data used in QC are then exactly those implemented by that stage. Where the continuous-coefficient join of Section 5 applies, classical block acquisition uses the existing actual-block name; its normalized growing-block accuracy is fixed before calling the table sampler. In the general coupled case Section 5 instead requires the actual joint circuit/service or effective joint-instrument data; the scalar block name alone is insufficient. For externally supplied quantum black-box services, use them only through the admitted causal ports; their classical matrix descriptions are not inferred from finite experiments. If no effective amplitudes or actual black-box service are supplied, the mathematical criterion remains valid but the proposed compiler has no authorized/effective source input.\n\nEach stage pays its actual source preparation/service, amplitude/coefficient computation, lookup, input encoding, interventions, coherent pulse, observer forecast, clock/cursor, STOP/FAIL routing, complete archive and fresh stock. Retain the same event order and registered physical clock. A chosen implementation error tending to zero does not change QC/QT or QJ. The physical resources may grow; no autonomous, uniformly bounded-power, or free-reset theorem is asserted.\n\n#### 4. Exact finite-dimensional Markov off-diagonal classification\n\nFor the original recorded GKSL branch, let the system have finite dimension $d$ and let the reduced evolution be a norm-continuous time-homogeneous semigroup $T_t$ with $T_0=\\mathrm{id}$. It is CPTP for all $t\\ge 0$ **if and only if** its generator has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(\\rho) &= -i[H,\\rho]+\\sum_a (L_a \\rho L_a^* - 1/2\\, \\{L_a^*L_a,\\rho\\}),\\\\\nH &= H^*,\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwith finitely many matrices `L_a` (at most `d^2-1` after removing scalar redundancy). No diagonal-state assumption is involved. An equivalent finite test on a proposed linear generator is: Hermiticity preservation, trace annihilation, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_\\perp J(L) P_\\perp &\\ge 0,\\\\\nP_\\perp &= I-|\\Omega_d\\rangle\\langle\\Omega_d|.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNecessity of conditional positivity follows by expanding the Choi matrix of $\\mathrm{id}+tL+o(t)$ and testing vectors orthogonal to $\\Omega_d$. Conversely, decompose the positive orthogonal corner into rank-one terms and reshape them into traceless jump matrices. The remaining Hermitian Choi matrix has only its row/column along $\\Omega_d$, hence represents $G\\rho+\\rho G^*$. Trace preservation forces $G+G^*=-\\sum L_a^*L_a$; its anti-Hermitian part is $-iH$, yielding the displayed form. The exact normalized tick instruments from O.7.2.3q converge to its semigroup and supply complete coherent history records, proving sufficiency without replacing amplitudes by a classical distribution.\n\nThis is the standard finite-dimensional GKSL classification, applied on its exact domain. Primary sources: Gorini et al. (1976) and Lindblad (1976).\n\n**Actual source convergence, not just generator syntax.** If the actual stage's reduced one-tick channels satisfy\n\n$$\n\\lVert\\Phi_{n,k}-\\exp(\\delta_{n,k}L(t_{n,k}))\\rVert_\\diamond\\le e_{n,k}\n$$\n\nwith $\\sum_k e_{n,k}\\to 0$, $\\max_k\\delta_{n,k}\\to 0$, and a bounded norm-continuous time-dependent GKSL coefficient record, the product telescoping inequality (all CPTP maps have diamond norm one) and Riemann approximation prove convergence to the unique time-ordered evolution. These are verifiable assumptions on the actual tick maps. For complete histories, use their actual dilation QC/QT/QJ tests or the stronger explicit isometry mesh bound from Theorem O.7.2.3u; convergence of reduced channels alone does not determine a retained unraveling. Different jump/readout dilations with the same reduced GKSL generator remain different retained sources.\n\nIf QC/QT yields a CP family that fails the semigroup law, its retained memory is not discarded or called GKSL. Whether a specified quantum-Fokker–Planck reduction exists, and its spatial differential order/domain, is exactly the separately recorded reduction in Definition O.7.2.3a. The general Q/QJ source tests still classify its complete-record convergence without pretending CP alone supplies a local scalar representative, a principal tensor, a no-jump certificate, or a cone. Those remain the explicit original C2/signature premises.\n\n**Affine boundary.** A fixed physical source acting on an arbitrary unknown input state must give affine unconditioned outputs (indeed linear maps on trace class) because probabilistic mixtures can be prepared with an inaccessible classical flag. A claimed nonlinear density-dependent unconditioned map cannot be one such channel on all inputs. Normalized conditioned histories may be nonlinear through division by their actual Born probability; the underlying instrument remains linear and is already classified by Q/QJ. Classical state-coordinate dependence of coefficients is a different matter and is retained in QJ's actual joint coordinate source.\n\n#### 5. Join rules preventing an information-access substitution\n\nA source-block name for a scalar driver alone does **not** compute arbitrary discontinuous conditional quantum instruments, and weak source approximation need not control them. There are two different admissible joins:\n\n1. **Continuous coefficient join.** Supply the coefficient evaluations and compact moduli of Theorem O.7.2.3u (or its vector corollary below). Its explicit joint stability proof transports the actual scalar/vector block approximation into the complete instrument.\n2. **General actual-instrument join.** Supply each actual finite source-stage causal circuit/service and its amplitude/instrument approximation data, including dependence on the actual finite source coordinates. Compile that complete stage to the declared finite error. QC/QT/QJ are then tests of those actual joint-stage amplitudes. They are not inferred from scalar moments or from a scalar block name alone.\n\nFor an actual finite primitive circuit with computable gates and specified source services, a finite norm-error budget follows by telescoping gate/isometry errors. A purported kernel with no such actual primitive or effective joint name is not silently promoted to a physical supplier. No conditional discontinuity is repaired by sampling an unrelated proposed limit law.\n\n**Vector continuous-coefficient corollary.** Theorem O.7.2.3u holds verbatim for paths in $C([0,T],\\mathbb{R}^r)$ with $B=0$, continuous Hermitian $A:\\mathbb{R}^r\\to M_d$ and finitely many continuous $L_e:\\mathbb{R}^r\\to M_d$. Use compact Euclidean balls and their supplied effective moduli; replace absolute scalar path distances by sup Euclidean distances. The proof uses only coefficient uniform continuity on that ball, contraction Duhamel and history-product telescoping. It therefore joins the actual state-updated finite Euler trees in Theorem O.7.2.3v to one complete coherent output, including every initial coherence, arbitrary spectator and state-dependent zero/rank-changing rate. The input's classical state coordinate is an actual supplied/prepared classical variable, not a measurement of an unknown quantum density matrix.\n\n#### 6. A declared infinite-input domain: strong channel classification without a free converter\n\nWhen the original quantum reduction explicitly has a separable infinite input carrier, choose finite-dimensional input projections $F_j$ increasing strongly to the identity and retain the actual self-adjoint/core/boundary data of any proposed generator separately. Suppose the actual source stages define normal CPTP maps on that input carrier (or a compatible family of actual encoded finite-input responses). Apply QC/QT to each restriction $T(F_jH_{\\mathrm{in}})\\to T(K)$; for QJ use its joint-instrument tests on each restriction and impose the actual restriction identities.\n\nThese tests for every $j$ are necessary and sufficient for strong channel convergence: for every fixed trace-class input, including every fixed entangled spectator state, the full output converges in trace norm (or in QJ's declared continuous-classical-test topology). Necessity follows by testing the finitely many matrix units of each finite input corner; on finite input spaces strong and diamond convergence agree. For sufficiency, the consistent finite-input limits define a trace-norm contraction on the finite-rank input algebra and hence uniquely extend to all trace class. Positivity, complete positivity at every matrix level, and trace preservation pass through this extension. Given any fixed input state, approximate its purification by its $F_j$ component and use contractivity and the same $2\\sqrt{\\text{tail}}$ comparison as Q1. First choose $j$ to control that input tail, then let $n$ tend to infinity on the finite corner. This also handles an arbitrary fixed spectator by approximating vectors with finite system support; it does not require a finite spectator dimension.\n\nNo uniform infinite-input diamond convergence is asserted. If an input-energy class is specified with a uniform finite-input-tail bound, the same argument upgrades to that energy-constrained uniform mode. If no such bound is given, it cannot be inferred from output tightness. The example with $B|k\\rangle=k|k\\rangle$ in Theorem O.7.2.3y shows the obstruction explicitly.\n\nPhysical finite realization here means the actual prepared finite encodings and their supplied source ports, plus the same paid finite isometry compiler. Extending the mathematical channel to every trace-class state is not an implemented conversion of an arbitrary infinite carrier into finite hardware. A physical device admitting such a carrier requires its own supplied/verified converter. The source classification remains exact in its stated mathematical domain without adding that unproved physical operation.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723x\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723x-recorded-quantum-fokker-planck-domain-and-actual-tick-criterion\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3x (Recorded Quantum-Fokker–Planck Domain and Actual-Tick Criterion).**\n\nThis addresses the optional quantum-Fokker–Planck reduction explicitly named in Definition O.7.2.3a, rather than treating its existence as an unpopulated assumption. It is an exact operator/domain criterion.\n\n#### Local assertion R: complete conservative resolvent criterion\n\nLet $X$ be the trace-class space $T(H)$ for a separable Hilbert space, or the predual of the classical–quantum von Neumann algebra $L^\\infty(S,\\mu)\\,\\bar{\\otimes}\\,B(H)$, on a specified sigma-finite measured classical coordinate space. In the latter case one may identify $X$ with $L^1(S,\\mu;T(H))$. The trace functional is respectively $\\operatorname{Tr}$ or $\\tau(\\rho)=\\int\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho(s)\\,\\mu(ds)$. Specify a linear operator $L:D(L)\\subset X\\to X$, including its actual domain, boundary conditions, and chosen closed extension. A formal expression on compactly supported test functions is not yet this datum.\n\nThe following are equivalent:\n\n1. $L$ is the generator of a unique strongly continuous semigroup of normal completely positive trace-preserving maps on $X$.\n2. $L$ is closed and densely defined; for every $\\lambda>0$, $\\lambda I-L$ is a bijection $D(L)\\to X$ with bounded inverse $R_\\lambda$; and the resolvent channel\n   $C_\\lambda=\\lambda R_\\lambda$\n   is completely positive and trace preserving.\n\nComplete positivity means positivity of every finite matrix amplification on the corresponding trace-class/predual space. Trace preservation means $\\tau(C_\\lambda\\rho)=\\tau(\\rho)$ for every $\\rho\\in X$; equivalently the adjoint is unital. The generator identity is $\\tau(L\\rho)=0$ on its domain, not $\\tau(L\\rho)=\\tau(\\rho)$.\n\n**Necessity.** For a strongly continuous CPTP contraction semigroup,\n\n$$\nR_\\lambda \\rho=\\int_0^\\infty \\exp(-\\lambda t)\\, T_t \\rho\\, dt\n$$\n\nis a bounded Bochner integral and the standard resolvent of its generator. Multiplication by `lambda` makes the weights a probability distribution; each finite amplification is positive and the trace is preserved. The generator is closed and densely defined.\n\n**Sufficiency.** A CPTP map is a trace-norm contraction (dual to a normal unital CP map), hence $\\lVert C_\\lambda\\rVert\\le 1$ and $\\lVert R_\\lambda^k\\rVert\\le\\lambda^{-k}$ for every positive integer $k$. The Hille–Yosida generation theorem applies to the stated closed dense operator and gives a unique strongly continuous contraction semigroup. To establish CP and conservation rather than only contractivity, use its bounded Yosida approximants\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_\\lambda &= \\lambda(C_\\lambda-I),\\\\\nT_t^{(\\lambda)} &= \\exp(t L_\\lambda)\\\\\n&= \\exp(-\\lambda t) \\sum_{k=0}^\\infty (\\lambda t)^k/k!\\, C_\\lambda^k.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvery approximant is CPTP, with literal normalization by the Poisson weights. Hille–Yosida's Yosida approximation gives strong convergence to `T_t`, uniformly for `t` in compact intervals. Apply it entrywise at each finite matrix amplification: positivity is closed and the finite block sum is norm-controlled, so the limit is completely positive. The trace is norm-continuous and therefore remains preserved. This proves the equivalence and uniqueness on the specified operator domain. ∎\n\nThe generation theorem is the classical Hille–Yosida theorem, not a new general semigroup result. The application here makes the recorded CP/conservation/domain checks explicit. A primary mathematical source for the operator-approximation framework is Trotter (1958). The finite-dimensional version reduces to the GKSL conditional-Choi test already proved in Theorem O.7.2.3w.\n\n#### Exact classification branches\n\n- A proposed quantum-Fokker–Planck differential operator with its full closed domain passes R exactly when it defines the conservative CP strongly continuous reduction on that domain.\n- Failure of a resolvent's positivity at any finite matrix level is a concrete off-diagonal/spectator failure, not repaired by positivity of diagonal probabilities.\n- A subunital/substochastic resolvent indicates trace loss. If the original protocol retains loss/STOP/cemetery output, enlarge the actual state/record space with that owned output and test the enlarged operator; do not renormalize the loss away.\n- Different closed extensions of one interior differential expression are different records. R classifies each; it does not choose one from the expression alone. The actual source and boundary operations must identify the extension.\n- Lack of a dense closed realization or failure of the positive-half-line range condition means that this proposed domain is not a conservative strongly continuous Markov reduction of the required type. It does not prove that the actual source has no non-Markov or differently-domained limit.\n- A source with memory can converge by Q/QJ yet not admit any time-homogeneous Markov reduction. That is a retained alternative, not a failed attempt to force it into GKSL/QFP.\n\nThese are necessary/sufficient tests on resolvent equations and positivity/trace data. They are not a promise of a finite-time decision algorithm, computable resolvents, a convergence modulus, or a physically accessible resolvent oracle. A mere formal series of derivatives with unspecified domain does not satisfy the input specification.\n\n#### Local assertion RS: a direct actual-tick route to a recorded reduction\n\nLet R hold, and let $D_0$ be an explicitly specified operator core for $L$. Let the actual source's one-tick reduced maps $F_n$ be CPTP contractions on the same declared input/output carrier, with $h_n>0$, $h_n\\to 0$. Assume, for every $\\rho\\in D_0$,\n\n$$\n\\lVert(F_n \\rho-\\rho)/h_n - L\\rho\\rVert_1 \\to 0.\n\\tag{RS1}\n$$\n\nThen the actual deterministic-tick products satisfy\n\n$$\nF_n^{\\lfloor t/h_n\\rfloor} \\rho \\to T_t \\rho\n$$\n\nstrongly, uniformly on compact time intervals, for every trace-class input. This is a source-level consistency condition: `F_n` is the actual supplied tick, not a sampler for a proposed target output law. The statement applies to the classical–quantum predual as well.\n\n**Proof.** Put $A_n=(F_n-I)/h_n$. Its bounded semigroup is\n\n$$\n\\exp(tA_n)=\\mathbb{E}[F_n^N],\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is Poisson with mean $t/h_n$, so it is contractive. Core convergence (RS1), the already established generator property of $L$, and the contraction bounds imply strong semigroup convergence by the Trotter–Kato core approximation theorem (equivalently strong convergence of resolvents on the dense range $(\\lambda-L)D_0$, followed by resolvent approximation). To see the dense-range step explicitly, for $y=(\\lambda-L)x$, $x\\in D_0$,\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda,A_n)y-x = R(\\lambda,A_n)(A_n-L)x,\n$$\n\nwhose norm is at most $\\lambda^{-1}\\lVert(A_n-L)x\\rVert$. Since $D_0$ is a core and $\\lambda-L$ is onto, that range is dense; contraction bounds extend resolvent convergence to all inputs. The standard contraction-semigroup approximation theorem then yields the uniform compact-time claim for the Poissonized semigroups.\n\nFor $\\rho\\in D_0$, $\\lVert A_n\\rho\\rVert$ is bounded. Contractivity and commutation with $F_n$ give\n\n$$\n\\lVert F_n^k\\rho-F_n^j\\rho\\rVert\\le|k-j|h_n\\lVert A_n\\rho\\rVert.\n$$\n\nConsequently the difference between the Poissonized and deterministic products is bounded by\n\n$$\n\\lVert A_n \\rho\\rVert\\, [\\sqrt{t h_n}+h_n],\n$$\n\nusing $\\mathbb{E}|N-t/h_n|\\le\\sqrt{t/h_n}$. This vanishes uniformly for bounded $t$. Density and contractivity extend the convergence to every input. Finite matrix amplifications and finite-rank approximation give the corresponding convergence for each fixed entangled spectator. Uniform infinite-input diamond convergence is not claimed. ∎\n\nWhen domains/embeddings vary with finite encodings, use actual specified input/output maps and the corresponding core approximation theorem, or apply the independent Q/QJ finite-input corner tests directly. Do not silently identify an infinite carrier with a finite cutoff.\n\n**Complete histories remain essential.** RS identifies the reduced semigroup. The actual dilations must independently pass Q/QJ (or the explicit complete-history bounds in Theorem O.7.2.3u). Two sources with the same reduced `L` but different retained record phases/instruments remain distinct. This is the off-diagonal requirement of the original task, not an optional strengthening.\n\n#### Actual common finite realization and cost boundary\n\nR is a mathematical classification, not a source compiler using free resolvent calls. The actual realization input is one of:\n\n- a supplied finite-stage primitive circuit/instrument with computable amplitudes and effective record embeddings, whose actual maps are tested by RS and/or Q/QJ;\n- the actual causal quantum black-box services already in the nominated source, used only at their admitted ports;\n- an effective joint finite source/instrument name with its actual stage implementation and approximation bounds, as in Theorem O.7.2.3w's two join rules.\n\nThe original O.7.2.3m–q supplier compiles those finite known isometries with fresh owned records, its Hermitian dilation, positive physical durations and explicit source/table/lookup/pulse/cursor/archive/stock/work accounting. A request to implement an arbitrary infinite-dimensional `R_lambda` without any admitted source/service/effective input is classified as an information-access failure, not silently fulfilled by inventing a law oracle.\n\nThus the joint classification separates four questions without leaving the operator domain implicit: (i) existence of an actual source's retained limit by S/Q/QJ; (ii) existence and exact CP/conservative validity of a recorded Markov/QFP reduction by R; (iii) identification with that actual source through RS or direct retained-limit comparison; and (iv) finite effective realization from the actual stage source data under m–q. Non-Markov, nonconvergent, nonconservative and noneffective branches remain explicit alternatives. None is mislabelled as a second-order local scalar field.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723y\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723y-unbounded-hamiltonian-and-source-selected-nonuniqueness\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3y (Unbounded Hamiltonian and Source-Selected Nonuniqueness).**\n\nThe following explicit unbounded-generator and nonunique-selection families witness the topology and initialization boundaries of the classification.\n\n#### 1. Unbounded self-adjoint phase generator, locally bounded quantum noise\n\nLet $H$ be a separable Hilbert space, $B$ a specified self-adjoint operator with its actual self-adjoint domain, and $U_z=\\exp(-iBz)$ its Stone group. Let $A:\\mathbb{R}\\to B(H)$ be norm-continuous and self-adjoint, and let finitely many $L_e:\\mathbb{R}\\to B(H)$ be norm-continuous. These coefficients need only be bounded on compact scalar intervals, not globally. All products involving $A,L_e$ are bounded operators; no expression such as $BA$ or $BL_e$ is assumed defined.\n\n**Local assertion U.** For every continuous scalar path, the rotating-frame history construction of O.7.2.3q defines a unique bounded no-jump propagator and a normalized isometry $V_x:H\\to H\\otimes R_T$. It retains every off-diagonal amplitude, not only the reduced master equation. If $x_n\\to x$ uniformly on $[0,T]$, then $V_{x_n}\\to V_x$ strongly. Consequently, for each fixed trace-class input state on $H\\otimes H_{\\mathrm{spectator}}$, the complete output states converge in trace norm. This conclusion includes arbitrary fixed entangled spectators. It is not unrestricted diamond-norm convergence.\n\n**Proof.** $U_{x_n(t)}\\to U_{x(t)}$ strongly, uniformly in $t$: the group orbit of each fixed vector is uniformly continuous on a compact scalar interval. Norm-continuity and local boundedness of $A,L_e$, followed by multiplication of uniformly bounded strongly continuous operators, give strong continuity of the tilded coefficients, uniformly on compact time intervals on each fixed vector. The bounded time-dependent generator has a norm-convergent Dyson series (strong integrals of its terms); the local norm majorant is $\\exp(MT)$. Its solution is unique. The real part of the generator is $-\\tilde{D}/2$, so differentiation on vectors, or the integral equation, proves contractivity. These operations never differentiate $U_x(t)$ and never apply $B$ to the evolving state.\n\nFor each fixed finite history, strong convergence follows from the Dyson series and strong convergence of the coefficient products. The history-norm majorant is $\\prod l_e$, since the no-jump propagators and final phase are contractions. Integrating its square gives $\\exp(\\ell T)$, with $\\ell=\\sum l_e^{2}$. Dominated convergence, first on finite history sectors and then on their exponentially bounded tail, yields strong convergence of $V_x$.\n\nNormalization follows by bounded-generator gain/loss trace cancellation for finite-rank input operators, extended by continuity to trace class. Equivalently the absolutely convergent history expansion gives $V_x^*V_x=I$. Strong convergence of uniformly bounded isometries gives trace-norm convergence on rank-one operators, then on all trace-class inputs by finite-rank approximation. The same proof on a tensor spectator starts with finite sums of simple tensors, which are dense; no spectator measurement or finite spectator dimension is assumed. ∎\n\n**Why unrestricted diamond convergence fails.** Take $H=\\ell^2(\\mathbb{N}_0)$, $B|k\\rangle=k|k\\rangle$, $A=L_e=0$, and at a fixed final time compare paths with $x(T)=0$ and $x_n(T)=\\pi/n$ (linear interpolation from zero). The paths converge uniformly. On $\\psi_n=(|0\\rangle+|n\\rangle)/\\sqrt{2}$, the two final pure states are orthogonal, so their channel diamond distance is $2$ for every $n$. There is no uniform error over unbounded-energy inputs, despite strong convergence for every fixed input. This is an explicit limitation of the original topology, not a failure of complete positivity.\n\nFor the phase-only case with $\\operatorname{Tr}(|B|\\rho)\\le E$, the elementary bound\n$|\\exp(-iz)-1|^{2}\\le 2|z|$\nyields, after purification,\n\n$$\n\\lVert(U_r \\otimes I)\\rho(U_r^* \\otimes I)-\\rho\\rVert_1 \\le 2 \\sqrt{2E |r|}.\n$$\nThus an energy-constrained version can restore a quantitative uniform input bound. For general noise one must supply the relevant input class and controlled truncation; an energy bound cannot be asserted without specifying its operator and propagation.\n\n#### 2. Explicit finite supplier for a genuine unbounded-operator subclass\n\nThe following supplies constructive finite-dimensional approximants.\n\nAssume $H=\\ell^2(\\mathbb{N}_0)$, $B|j\\rangle=b_j|j\\rangle$ with a computable real sequence $b_j$ (possibly unbounded), and the self-adjoint diagonal domain $\\sum_j|b_ju_j|^{2}<\\infty$. Let $A(x),L_e(x)$ have fixed finite matrix bandwidth $w$, continuous computable matrix entries, effective compact moduli, and supplied compact operator-norm bounds. They are bounded operators at every fixed $x$, though their norms may grow without bound as $|x|$ grows. Hermiticity of $A$ is a premise. The declared initial input class satisfies $\\operatorname{Tr}(G\\rho)\\le E$ for a positive diagonal compact-resolvent reference $G|j\\rangle=g_j|j\\rangle$ with a supplied effective lower tail bound $h_K=\\inf_{j\\ge K}g_j\\to\\infty$.\n\nCompress to the first $N$ levels:\n\n$$\nB_N=P_NBP_N,\n$$\n\n$A_N=P_NAP_N$, $L_{e,N}=P_NL_eP_N$, and define $D_N=\\sum_e L_{e,N}^*L_{e,N}$. Use the exactly normalized finite-dimensional history and tick constructions for these compressed operators. In particular do not substitute $P_NDP_N$ for $D_N$: that would generally break finite-instrument normalization.\n\n**Explicit truncation estimate.** Fix $R,T$ and take local bounds $a\\ge\\lVert A(x)\\rVert$, $l_e\\ge\\lVert L_e(x)\\rVert$ on $|x|\\le R$; put $\\ell=\\sum l_e^{2}$, $M=a+\\ell/2$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_Q(z) &= \\sum_{q>Q} z^q/q!,\\\\\nJ_{M_0}(z) &= \\sum_{m>M_0} z^m/m!.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nFor initial vectors in `P_K H` and any integer cutoffs `Q,M0`, take `N>=K+w(M0+2Q)+1`. The finite and infinite history isometries, embedded in the same complete record space, obey\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\lVert(V_x - V_{x,N}) P_K\\rVert\\\\\n&\\quad \\le 2 \\sqrt{\\exp(\\ell T)\\, R_Q(MT)^2 + J_{M_0}(\\ell T)}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{U1}\n$$\n\nTo prove it, expand every no-jump propagator into its Dyson series. For a fixed $m$-jump word, collect terms by total drift order $q$ across all no-jump intervals. Their norm sum is at most $\\prod l_e\\cdot(MT)^q/q!$: the interval lengths sum to $T$, and the multinomial identity applies. A drift factor has bandwidth at most $2w$; each jump factor has bandwidth at most $w$; diagonal conjugation and final phase preserve support. Thus every term with $m\\le M_0,q\\le Q$, on $P_K$, agrees exactly between the full and compressed systems. The extra margin ensures that even the two factors forming each $L^*L$ stay inside the compression. The remaining amplitude difference is at most $2\\prod l_e\\,R_Q(MT)$. Its squared integral over these history sectors is bounded by $4\\exp(\\ell T)R_Q(MT)^{2}$. On $m>M_0$, contractivity bounds each history coefficient by $\\prod l_e$; the difference contributes at most $4J_{M_0}(\\ell T)$. The sectors are orthogonal, giving (U1).\n\nFor any input, including a spectator, in the declared energy class, put $p=\\operatorname{Tr}((I-P_K)\\rho)\\le E/h_K$. On a purification decompose the system vector into its $P_K$ and complementary parts. Isometries have norm one, so\n\n$$\n\\lVert(V_x \\otimes I)\\psi-(V_{x,N} \\otimes I)\\psi\\rVert \\le \\epsilon_{\\mathrm{U1}}+2 \\sqrt{p}.\n$$\n\nFor the mathematical comparison only, extend the finite-sector channel outside `P_N` by a normalized flagged channel. This ideal comparison is not itself an implemented finite input-admission device. A literal finite physical implementation accepts explicitly prepared encoded finite-sector inputs; accepting a general infinite carrier requires a separately supplied and verified truncation/encoding instrument, whose existence is not implied by an energy bound. The complete output trace distance is at most\n\n$$\n2\\, \\epsilon_{\\mathrm{U1}}+4 \\sqrt{E/h_K}.\n\\tag{U2}\n$$\n\nThis bound is uniform over the admitted energy-constrained inputs and spectator dimensions. It preserves arbitrary off-diagonal entries within the admitted input and controls the omitted coherences via the purification tail.\n\nFor each finite actual source-table stage, compute its cap `R`, choose `K,Q,M0,N` from (U1)–(U2), then apply the locally continuous finite-dimensional compiler from Theorem O.7.2.3u. Norms of `B_N` are finite and computable from finitely many eigenvalues. For explicitly prepared encoded finite-sector inputs, all source tables, coefficient lookups, tail/failure flags, tick instruments and Hermitian dilations are literal finite data. The uniform infinite-input estimate (U2) remains an ideal comparison unless an actual truncation instrument is separately supplied. Charge the enlarged finite input preparation/encoding, source/controller/table/cursor/record/archive cells, physical durations and pulse norms under O.7.2.3q. No uniform power or free conversion of an arbitrary infinite physical carrier is claimed. The original actual source normalization and source-block limit are unchanged.\n\nThis is a nontrivial common formal continuum realization with a genuinely unbounded self-adjoint `B`, preserving coherent records. Arbitrary unbounded noise operators require actual domains, a well-defined generator and conservativity/nonexplosion as separate data. Primary context for that separate problem: Chebotarev and Fagnola (1997). No theorem from that paper is assumed to solve the omitted domain problem.\n\n#### 3. Exact nonuniqueness and initialization/selection family\n\nAn identical source and identical displayed state coefficient need not determine a continuum law. Consider the deterministic zero source and the scalar state equation\n\n$$\ny'(t)=2 \\sqrt{\\max(y(t),0)},\\quad y(0)=0.\n$$\n\nAll forward $C^1$ solutions are exactly\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&y_\\tau(t)=(t-\\tau)_+^2,\\quad \\tau \\in [0,\\infty],\\\\\n&y_\\infty(t)=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProof: the derivative is nonnegative, so $y\\ge 0$; the zero set is an initial closed interval. On any component where $y>0$, differentiation gives $(\\sqrt{y})'=1$, and continuity at its first endpoint gives the displayed solution. A positive solution cannot return to zero. Conversely each displayed function is $C^1$ and solves the equation, including at its departure time. Thus every probability law on the solution set is the pushforward of a unique delay law (with the never-departing case included). On each finite horizon, $\\tau\\to y_\\tau$ is continuous for the one-point compactification of $[0,\\infty)$.\n\nThe actual selector is operational information. A joint source/selector law can be supplied and the selector sampled/retained before the observation window, using a common source block name when correlated rather than silently assuming independence. Its computable finite approximants can be compiled through the same continuous path map. Different delay distributions yield different continuum laws with the very same zero source, coefficient and initial state. Costs of the selector, future memory and initialization are charged exactly as other source inputs. If the original input omits a selector, the correct output is a family of admissible laws, not one uniquely forecast continuation.\n\nThere is also a concrete vanishing-perturbation realization of every deterministic finite delay, avoiding an unexplained solution-selection oracle. Let\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_\\epsilon(t) &= 0 \\quad \\text{for } t\\le\\tau,\\\\\ny_\\epsilon(t) &= (t-\\tau)^2+2\\epsilon(t-\\tau) \\quad \\text{for } t>\\tau.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $t>\\tau$, $y_\\epsilon'=2(t-\\tau)+2\\epsilon=2\\sqrt{y_\\epsilon+\\epsilon^{2}}$. The actual controlled finite equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_\\epsilon' &= 2 \\sqrt{\\max(y_\\epsilon,0)+\\epsilon^2} \\cdot 1_{t>\\tau},\\\\\ny_\\epsilon(0) &= 0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe activation is a retained paid timing intervention, not part of the autonomous original equation. After activation, the equation is locally Lipschitz on the nonnegative branch and its explicit solution above is unique. Before activation the state is held at zero. On each finite horizon, $y_\\epsilon\\to y_\\tau$ uniformly with error at most $2\\epsilon T$. Its residual relative to the original autonomous coefficient is zero before activation and at most $2\\epsilon$ afterwards (away from the activation point; equations hold almost everywhere), because $\\sqrt{y+\\epsilon^{2}}-\\sqrt{y}\\le\\epsilon$. Thus the limit is a genuine solution, while the choice of solution is visibly the retained clock/controller intervention. This construction deliberately does not relabel that intervention as free spontaneous dynamics.\n\nThis exact family classifies one nonunique feedback sector and exhibits precisely why a general source-only criterion cannot select all nonlinear state-dependent continuations. It does not classify all nonunique ODEs/SDEs. Broad claims must state a selection kernel/physical discretization and then prove its tightness and its identified limiting law; mere existence of multiple formal solutions does not do that work.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723z\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723z-general-declared-record-attraction-criterion\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3z (General Declared-Record Attraction Criterion).**\n\nThis removes a potential accidental restriction to continuous-path topology. It does not alter the source's declared comparison topology or infer that a jump source has a continuous limit.\n\nLet $(E,\\rho)$ be a fixed Polish record space with a bounded complete compatible metric and a fixed countable dense set $D$. Let $\\mu_{n}$ be the laws of the actual finite source stages, after their declared record maps. Define\n\n$$\nq_{m,F}(n)=\\int \\min(1,2^m \\operatorname{dist}_\\rho(x,F))\\, \\mu_n(dx),\n$$\n\nfor $m\\ge 1$ and nonempty finite $F\\subset D$. Choose the countable algebra $A$ generated over the rationals by $1$ and the bounded continuous tents $x\\mapsto\\max(0,1-r\\rho(x,z))$, $z\\in D$, positive rational $r$. Only finite sums and products occur. The algebra separates points.\n\n**Local assertion P.** The actual source laws $\\mu_{n}$ have a full weak probability limit on E if and only if:\n\n(P1) $\\int f\\,d\\mu_n$ is Cauchy for every $f\\in A$;\n\n(P2) for every $m\\ge 1$, $\\inf_{\\text{finite nonempty }F\\subset D}\\limsup_n q_{m,F}(n)=0$.\n\nThese are bounded continuous scalar tests of actual finite source records. They contain no candidate law and no metric between probability laws.\n\n**Proof of exact tightness criterion P2.** If the laws are tight, fix $m$ and $\\varepsilon>0$. Choose compact $K$ carrying mass at least $1-\\varepsilon/2$ for every $n$. A finite subset $F$ of $D$ approximates $K$ to distance $\\varepsilon 2^{-m-1}$. Then $q_{m,F}(n)\\le\\varepsilon$ for all $n$. This gives P2.\n\nConversely suppose P2. Fix $\\varepsilon>0$. For each $m$ choose $F_{m}$ with $\\limsup_n q_{m,F_m}(n)<\\varepsilon 2^{-m-3}$. Thus all sufficiently late $n$ have $q\\le\\varepsilon 2^{-m-2}$. The finitely many earlier probability laws are individually tight. Enlarge $F_m$ by finite $D$-covers of their high-probability compact sets at sufficiently fine radius; because distance to an enlarged set cannot increase, this makes $q\\le\\varepsilon 2^{-m-2}$ for **all** $n$. On $\\{\\operatorname{dist}(x,F_m)>2^{-m}\\}$, the integrand is one. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\sup_n \\mu_n(\\operatorname{dist}(x,F_m)>2^{-m})\\le\\varepsilon 2^{-m-2}.\n$$\n\nThe set $K_\\varepsilon=\\bigcap_{m\\ge 1}\\{x:\\operatorname{dist}(x,F_m)\\le 2^{-m}\\}$ is closed and totally bounded. Completeness makes it compact. The union bound gives $\\inf_n\\mu_n(K_\\varepsilon)\\ge 1-\\sum_{m\\ge 1}\\varepsilon 2^{-m-2}>1-\\varepsilon$. Thus the full family is tight. This proof does not assume $E$ is locally compact or has a fixed countable compact exhaustion.\n\nBy Prokhorov, tightness gives subsequential weak limits. P1 makes any two such limits agree on $A$. To see uniqueness, the tents give a countable continuous point-separating map into $[0,1]^{\\mathbb{N}}$. Products of its coordinates determine every finite-dimensional pushforward distribution, since polynomials are dense on each compact cube. The pushforward measures therefore agree. The coordinate map is a Borel injection from the standard Borel space $E$, so its inverse on its image is Borel; hence the original measures agree. A tight sequence with one possible subsequential limit converges. Conversely weak convergence gives tightness and Cauchy integrals. ∎\n\n**Joint quantum version.** In QJ replace rational path Fourier functions by $f\\in A$ and replace the scalar path tightness condition by P2 on the trace laws. Use Cauchy tests for $\\int f(x)\\langle u,a|M_n(dx)|v,b\\rangle$ and the same output projection-tail test. The finite-corner complex measures inherit scalar tightness and bounded variation by positivity. Their subsequential limits are uniquely identified by the same algebra (apply its signed-measure uniqueness), and QJ's projection estimate upgrades to trace-norm convergence for every bounded continuous $f$. Thus the QJ proof extends to every fixed Polish record space without changing its physical source or quantum topology.\n\nExamples include compact-open continuous paths and an explicitly chosen Skorokhod J1 space for càdlàg records. The latter retains discontinuous paths; it is not identified with uniform convergence or with a different Skorokhod topology. On continuous polygonal sources, S1/S2 remains the sharper explicit rational-Fourier/oscillation specialization. A source can fail the continuous criterion and pass the declared càdlàg criterion, producing a retained jump alternative rather than a false Gaussian conclusion.\n\n**Effectivity and source ownership.** For a finite actual source table, these integrals are finite sums of its actual weights and record values. Computability additionally requires the supplied effective metric/record representation and effective evaluation of its functions. A causal service may supply the actual records without making exact test expectations empirically computable. Infinite P1/P2 tests are not a universal decision algorithm or rate oracle. The finite source grammar and paid implementation are those of Theorem O.7.2.3m.\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o723aa\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o723aa-exhaustion-of-the-finite-source-grammar-and-complete-classification\"></a>**Proposition O.7.2.3aa (Exhaustion of the Finite Source Grammar and Complete Classification).**\n\nThe following proposition joins the source, state and quantum classifications to the finite supplier of Theorem O.7.2.3m, without adding an information oracle or changing the physical clock.\n\n#### Source universe\n\nThe proposition concerns the classification of the non-Gaussian dependent and long-range sectors beyond the fGn functionals, of the state-dependent sector and of the quantum off-diagonal sector, and extends their common formal continuum realization to every finite descriptor of the grammar below. A classified source need not be Gaussian, Markovian, a local scalar field or hyperbolic; Definition O.7.2.3a and Proposition O.7.2.3b keep the scalar, spatial, no-jump, operational-null and cone entries separate.\n\nThe finite formal source universe is the grammar of Theorem O.7.2.3m §1:\n\n```\nCORRELATED_INSTRUMENT_CONTROL =\n (finite source probability/decoding tables;\n  finite Hermitian matrix blocks;\n  finite addressed supports;\n  norm bounds;\n  positive durations;\n  requested dyadic accuracies;\n  fresh target-record indices;\n  retention/readout menu).\n```\n\nIts literal contract permits arbitrary finite Hermitian controls with computable coefficients on the qualified ready-cell payloads, externally supplied pure stock, retained work memory, and a specified physical schedule. It rejects missing bounds, invalid addresses and reused fresh targets. It does not promise arbitrary noneffective amplitudes, unknown-service tomography, infinite input conversion, autonomous resetting or a refinement-uniform power bound. O.7.2.3q extends the actual classical source input to uniformly computable finite-block names, preserving the exact normalized growing-block error budget.\n\n**Finite descriptor exhaustion lemma.** Every valid finite descriptor in this grammar has a finite normalized quantum instrument, with computable actual matrix entries and a literal finite physical implementation under that same supplier; every state-dependent/off-diagonal finite source admitted by the grammar is therefore in the domain of Q/QJ, once its retained output embedding is fixed.\n\n**Proof by finite construction, not a source promise.**\n\n1. A descriptor contains finitely many primitive supports, source labels, pulse instructions, measurement outcomes and fresh record locations. Pad the addressed finite system and record registers to their finite tensor/direct-sum carrier. Each source probability table is either the displayed exact dyadic table or is approximated with its supplied probability precision by the lower-enclosure/overflow construction already proved in O.7.2.3l §3 and used by O.7.2.3m §2. Its actual sampler uses owned fair-bit preparations, actual readouts and reversible lookup, retaining the complete seed/work/future label. It is not a sample from an unknown continuum law.\n2. A finite Hermitian pulse $H$ of specified duration $\\tau$ has the computable unitary $\\exp(-i\\tau H/\\hbar)$. Computability follows from the finite matrix exponential series and the supplied norm bound, which gives a computable tail estimate. A dyadic Hermitian approximation remains Hermitian, hence its actual evolution is exactly unitary; Duhamel bounds its implementation error by $\\tau\\lVert\\tilde{H}-H\\rVert/\\hbar$.\n3. Unroll the finite classical-control/measurement tree. Along a branch the Kraus matrix is the ordered product of its actual unitaries, nominated measurement operators and state-preparation isometries. A finite instrument node is normalized. Induction on the tree proves the complete instrument is normalized: summing the descendants' $K^*K$ at the last node collapses to the parent's $K^*K$, and finally to $I$. STOP/FAIL and impossible outcomes remain named branches; their matrices may be zero but they are not postselected away. The instruction list is finite, so this construction terminates without deciding a noncomputable limiting event.\n4. Where the original menu retains an environment coherently, retain its actual dilation register and all complex Kraus amplitudes. Where it performs a real measurement, include its apparatus/record and the nominated dephasing/readout. A genuinely measured record is not reinterpreted as an undephased coherent alternative. Arbitrary unknown input states are never copied into a classical label. Classical state feedback uses actual classical records and lookup addresses; quantum outcome feedback uses the actual instrument branch. This induction covers every finite source-dependent, state-dependent, adaptive and off-diagonal descriptor admitted by the grammar, not just the rotating-frame example.\n5. The whole finite source may equivalently be represented by a finite isometry after retaining all its declared purification records; reduction to the nominated output is a specified CPTP map. For any known normalized finite isometry, m/q's explicit Hermitian block $W=\\begin{bmatrix} 0 & V^* \\\\ V & I-VV^* \\end{bmatrix}$ gives the common supplier construction without a rank or pivot decision. No new matrix alphabet is being admitted.\n6. Attach the same qualified host and its actually executed calibration/discrepancy update from m/q. Its retained forecast controls the invertible source-label frame and inverse decoder; if the source has one positive label, use q's counted independent frame bit to split it into two identical-output labels. This changes the physical controlled source register and instructions while preserving the decoded law, so qualification is neither inferred from Hilbert dimension nor supplied by a disconnected spectator.\n7. Retain the original event order, release times and physical observation clock. Precompilation is allowed only before the observation interval and is charged. Known internal events can use the original positive subwindows and q's finite cursor schedule; unbounded refinement-dependent pulse strength is permitted and paid, not hidden. Actual external-service latency cannot be shortened by this argument. Serialize and pay every source/table/matrix/lookup/seed/frame/forecast/tick/readout/failure/archive/cursor/ROM/duty-cell item under (O.7.2.3q.11)–(O.7.2.3q.13) and the ledger of Theorem O.7.2.3m. Fresh pure stock, owned source services, retained work, switch work and any actual reset are accounted for. This is the same finite supplier, not a new universal physical manufacturer.\n\nThese steps exhaust the finite fields of the original grammar and produce exactly the actual finite source stages to which the mathematical tests apply. ∎\n\n#### B. Common records and topology are part of the original comparison\n\nAn output embedding is not selected after seeing a limit. Preserve the actual source/record comparison fixed by the protocol. The general criterion P classifies actual records in every fixed declared Polish comparison space, including cadlag J1 records, by its bounded tent-algebra Cauchy and finite-center tightness tests. The metric is complete and compatible with that declared topology; noneffective topology does not imply an effective name. For continuous scalar/vector paths use $C([0,T],\\mathbb{R}^r)$ with uniform topology on each compact horizon. For q's quantum histories use the common direct sum of vacuum and ordered-label/time-simplex $L^2$ spaces; each finite tick record is embedded as the normalized indicator of its actual cell, with different label words and declared failure/STOP flags in orthogonal sectors. Histories occupying distinct cells have disjoint supports and unit normalized indicators, so the finite embeddings are isometries. Refinement preserves actual physical event times and amplitudes, not just jump probabilities. This is exactly q's existing record embedding.\n\nThere is no artificial separability exclusion: even if a nominated ambient output space is nonseparable, a countable sequence of finite stages occupies the closed span of the union of its finite-dimensional embedded output ranges. That span is separable and contains every possible trace-norm limit. Compatible growing finite input encodings have the same property. An abstract basis suffices for the noneffective theorem; computable overlaps/effective projections remain additional data for algorithmic claims.\n\nFor a more general valid finite descriptor, the retention/readout menu supplies its finite record labels, time bins and system encoding. Countable unions of those labels and a fixed declared system basis give a separable comparison carrier; where bins represent a common continuum record, use their actual normalized-cell embeddings rather than orthogonalizing every resolution label. Supplied compatible embedding matrices or finite overlap rules are part of the comparison record. A continuum equality needs such a common physical readout/phase identification to be well-typed. Computability of tests additionally requires effective embeddings/projections, as Q explicitly states.\n\nFinite quantum input gives diamond-norm convergence for each complete protocol. Infinite normal input, when explicitly admitted by the recorded reduction, uses compatible finite input corners and strong trace-class convergence for each fixed input/spectator. It does not acquire uniform infinite-input diamond convergence or a free physical infinite-to-finite converter. Actual finite implementations accept the prepared encodings already in the grammar. Any converter for a separate infinite carrier must itself be supplied and paid.\n\nThe continuum response menu is forward-locked: q/l promise bounded continuous/Lipschitz source-dependent tests and fixed finite admissible protocols, not all measurable source-label indicators. QJ's weak-classical/trace-quantum topology preserves exactly that distinction. Arbitrary source thresholds and stopping-time boundaries require a continuity-set/anti-concentration premise. Fixed quantum-record effects are controlled in trace norm; normalized histories require a positive limiting event probability. These are constraints of the declared topology.\n\n#### C. Exhaustive information-access cases\n\nThe classification does not assume that a scalar source name supplies an arbitrary quantum kernel.\n\n1. **Original finite computable descriptor:** the preceding exhaustion lemma supplies the actual finite instrument and paid implementation. Individual source-block/corner/tail tests are computable from its effective comparison data. Whether their infinite quantified convergence conditions hold need not be decidable.\n2. **Actual causal quantum service already supplied to the source:** wire that service only at its actual admitted ports, with its original initialization, STOP, source cost, latency and retained records. The causal-strategy representation of Proposition 42c.13 applies. Its finite response data define the mathematical Q/QJ tests, even if exact entries are not learnable from finite experiments. No counterfactual policy query or free Choi-state manipulation is authorized. The service is already the implementation; a new universal manufacturer is not claimed.\n3. **Effective joint source/instrument name:** it must come with the actual finite-stage realization data or the constructive source-access hypotheses of Proposition 42c.13. Continuous source coefficients supply one such join through Theorem O.7.2.3u. In a general discontinuous conditional instrument, a scalar bounded-Wasserstein name is insufficient: the joint instrument itself, an actual causal service, or a proved stage continuity/error bound is required. All conditional amplitudes, source correlations and lookup costs remain owned.\n4. **Only a semantic law/operator, with neither admitted effective descriptor nor actual service:** mathematical attraction/generation tests can still classify it, but there is no common effective implementation input. This is an impossibility boundary for compilers that receive no descriptor or service; constructing sources for given formal generators is a separate task. If a purported compiler receives no information distinguishing the identity qubit channel from the X channel, it produces the same output channel for both. Their diamond distance is 2, so at least one error is at least 1 by the triangle inequality. If a promised-precision preparation compiler could output a classical bit with any unspecified/noncomputable probability $p$ to error $2^{-n}$, reading its finite tables would compute $p$, again impossible. The existing computable-source/noncomputable-limit example independently rules out inferring a limit-law rate from stage computability. These boundaries do not forbid the actual accessible finite stages or their noneffective mathematical limits.\n\nThe first case is the entire explicitly nominated m/q compiler universe. The other cases preserve the original service/representation distinctions instead of silently broadening that universe. In no case is an inaccessible source replaced by an unrelated law oracle.\n\n#### D. Classification: outcome branches and evidence\n\n#### D1. Fixed-source non-Gaussian/dependent/long-range attraction\n\nFor every actual source in its declared Polish record topology, P1/P2 give necessary/sufficient actual finite-record tests for a unique full weak limit, retaining jump/cadlag alternatives as well as continuous ones. For actual fixed sources under the polygonal/continuous comparison, S1/S2 give necessary/sufficient finite-block tests for full convergence, with no candidate law input. If they hold, the limiting rational characteristic data identify the unique law; original stationarity/regular-variation assumptions invoke O.7.2.3o's exact selfsimilar stationary-increment image, and the supplied moment tests retain the finite-second-moment branch. If either S condition fails, the full sequence has no limit in the continuous topology; it can still pass P in its originally declared cadlag J1 topology and retain a jump limit. A failure of either P condition means no full limit in that declared Polish topology, not absence of subsequential or differently topologized limits. Finite coordinates can converge while oscillations fail, or tightness can hold while limiting finite distributions oscillate; both failures remain distinct. No covariance-only Gaussian inference occurs. The already accepted iid/Gaussian/fGn results are named sufficient subclasses, not the exhaustive source test.\n\n#### D2. State dependence and source selection\n\nS/QJ apply to the actual joint classical source/state path, not a guessed law for a formal state equation. They classify full convergence or its failure whether or not an auxiliary formal equation has a unique solution. A nonunique formal equation has multiple possible source-selected limits; the actual microscopic descriptor/initialization/intervention chooses its response. The exact delay family proves that this extra selector is real information, not an ignored mathematical nuisance. The Courrège theorem classifies the local/diffusive versus retained-jump/killing operator form on the recorded rich-Feller domain. The full globally Lipschitz, arbitrary-degeneracy Rademacher feedback family supplies a nontrivial actual source tree with proved tightness, generator identification, nonexplosion and uniqueness, then a common coherent output through the vector version of C. This is a whole state-dependent coefficient family, not one driven instance.\n\n#### D3. Quantum off-diagonal and Markov reductions\n\nQ/QJ, with P’s mixed-algebra extension for general Polish classical records, give necessary/sufficient finite-corner and tail tests for every actual finite descriptor's complete quantum records, with joint source correlations, arbitrary finite-input coherences/spectators and the stated strong infinite-input extension. A nonzero off-diagonal discrepancy cannot hide behind equal record probabilities. Tail failure identifies escaped response mass; corner failure identifies nonconvergent phases/amplitudes. Compatible finite-protocol causal identities pass to the limit, so permitted interventions and source timing are preserved.\n\nFor a finite time-homogeneous Markov reduction, the conditional-Choi/GKSL criterion classifies exactly the CP trace-preserving generator. The optional recorded QFP/infinite-domain branch is classified by R's resolvent equations and complete-positivity/trace conditions on the actual closed domain, with actual-source identification by RS or Q/QJ. The classical–quantum L1 resolvent branch requires its specified actual reference measure and strong continuity on that predual. It is not identified with QJ's weak classical-path topology: atomic finite paths may converge weakly to a diffuse process without total-variation/L1 convergence, and a $\\delta$ initial diffusion need not be strongly continuous in measure total variation. A valid weak path source needs no such L1 representation. This adds no positive local spatial-field premise. Non-Markov memory is a classified retained alternative. Reduced-channel convergence never identifies a full unraveling without the complete-record tests.\n\n#### D4. Common formal realization\n\nThe exhaustion lemma compiles every originally admitted finite descriptor through the same m/q supplier. Source-block approximants use growing normalized path precision; C and the Euler-tree construction prove source-faithful joint approximation for their continuous coefficient families. General descriptors use their actual joint amplitudes, not an extra law name. Complete finite isometry error budgets tend to zero independently of an unknown convergence rate, and do not alter S/Q/QJ classification. Inaccessible data fall under the proved access boundary, not an invented source. Initial conditions, source services, adaptive host, flags, clocks, archives and physical costs remain explicit.\n\n<a id=\"resolution-tv-o07-r4\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o723aa1-trace-loss-under-finite-coordinate-compression\"></a>**Proposition O.7.2.3aa.1 (Trace Loss under Finite Coordinate Compression).** On $\\ell^2(\\mathbb N_0)$ let $S|j\\rangle=|j+1\\rangle$ and set $\\mathcal L(\\rho)=S\\rho S^*-\\rho$ on the trace class. This bounded generator has a uniformly continuous completely positive trace-preserving semigroup. Let $P_n$ project onto $\\operatorname{span}\\{|0\\rangle,\\ldots,|n\\rangle\\}$, and define the compressed generator $\\mathcal L_n(\\rho)=P_n\\mathcal L(\\rho)P_n$ for states supported on $P_n$. For every $n$, its semigroup is trace-decreasing on some states. In particular the trace starting from $|n\\rangle\\langle n|$ is $e^{-t}$. Strong convergence $P_n\\to I$ and the ambient resolvent condition alone therefore leave finite-stage conservation undetermined.\n\n*Proof.* The isometry satisfies $S^*S=I$. The Poisson series $e^{-t}\\sum_{k\\ge0}(t^k/k!) S^k\\rho(S^*)^k$ is a convex combination of completely positive trace-preserving maps. The operator $P_nSP_n$ annihilates $|n\\rangle$, so $\\mathcal L_n(|n\\rangle\\langle n|)=-|n\\rangle\\langle n|$. This gives the claimed trace and applies even though the projections increase strongly to the identity. A trace-preserving finite-source construction requires a boundary prescription or an approximation that preserves trace on its own terms. ∎\n\nThe classification includes full convergence, nonconvergence and source-selected alternatives. It supplies actual-block Fourier/oscillation tests, general Polish tent-algebra/finite-center tightness tests, complete quantum finite-corner/tail tests, state-coordinate differential/jump classification, explicit nonlinear state-feedback sources, finite GKSL and recorded closed-domain QFP criteria, complete coherent instruments and a proof exhausting the finite supplier grammar of Theorem O.7.2.3m. Its finite implementations preserve the same source law, normalized alternatives, initial quantum input and spectators, host participation, physical clocks, STOP/FAIL outcomes and owned source/controller/work archives. Every source lookup, pure preparation, coefficient/matrix computation, pulse, retention and reset claim keeps the paid ledger of Theorem O.7.2.3m.\n\nThese results neither infer Gaussianity from covariance data nor turn retained memory/jumps into a local scalar field. Scalar principal-symbol identification, no-jump/operational-null certificates and cone/signature conditions remain separate premises. Mathematical classification does not provide a universal algorithm deciding convergence, a computable limiting rate, free access to unknown amplitudes or an infinite input converter. No unrestricted source-event total variation, uniform infinite-input diamond approximation, autonomous reset or refinement-uniform power bound is claimed. The inaccessible/ineffective and topology alternatives are proved boundaries of the formal realization. Scope limits: the attainable-law image of Theorem O.7.2.3o concerns fixed centered strictly stationary finite-second-moment scalar sources under positive regularly varying normalization and fixed linear centering, and general fixed sources are classified by the block tests S1–S2 and P1–P2, which are infinite conditions without a decision procedure or rate; the state-dependent sector is classified by operator form on the rich-Feller domain (the Courrège representation, imported) and by the source tests, with existence and uniqueness proved for globally Lipschitz coefficients and for locally Lipschitz coefficients with a Lyapunov certificate, and a nonunique state equation needs a source-supplied selector; the off-diagonal sector is classified by the finite-corner/tail tests, the finite-dimensional GKSL theorem (imported) and the closed-domain resolvent criterion, with strong rather than uniform diamond convergence for infinite inputs; the realization runs on the finite Hermitian-control supplier of Theorem O.7.2.3m with effective inputs (computable finite-block names, computable coefficients with moduli, finite descriptors), and a formal generator is realized when a source converging to it is supplied, such sources being constructed here for the Lipschitz/Lyapunov diffusion family, every finite-dimensional GKSL generator and the banded unbounded-diagonal family; stage resources grow with refinement, with no autonomous clock, bounded power or reset cycle. The source construction of Theorem O.7.2.3ac covers effective compact Feller transition rows, including state-coordinate jumps and killing and some non-Lipschitz diffusions. The remaining source requirements cover (a) rich-Feller state-dependent generators with a nonzero jump kernel in the state coordinate or a killing part beyond the effective compact and effectively compactified transition-row classes of Theorems O.7.2.3ac and O.7.2.3ae; (b) diffusion generators outside the globally Lipschitz and Lyapunov-certified class beyond the delay family of Theorem O.7.2.3y and the effective compact and effectively compactified transition-row classes of Theorems O.7.2.3ac and O.7.2.3ae; (c) quantum Fokker–Planck generators admitted by the resolvent criterion of Theorem O.7.2.3x beyond the banded family of Theorem O.7.2.3y, the nonbanded bounded-noise diagonal-clock family of Theorem O.7.2.3ab and the nonexplosive injective marked-jump class of Theorem O.7.2.3af. Theorem O.7.2.3ad also constructs the effective-resolvent semigroup and every fixed admitted intervention protocol, with their outcome records. A prescribed full coherent history on that branch requires its own Q/QJ source.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723ab\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723ab-coherent-finite-sources-for-nonbanded-lindblad-generators\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3ab (Coherent Finite Sources for Nonbanded Lindblad Generators).** On $\\mathcal H=\\ell^2(\\mathbb N_0)$ fix a self-adjoint diagonal clock Hamiltonian $B e_j=b_j e_j$ with computable real entries and its square-summable graph domain. Supply a bounded self-adjoint $A$ and a finite or countable family of bounded jump operators $L_e$ with computable matrix entries and $\\sum_e\\|L_e\\|^2\\le\\Lambda<\\infty$. Supply a computable bound for the tails of this sum. No band condition is imposed. Write $D=\\sum_e L_e^*L_e$. On the graph domain of the trace-class commutator of $B$, consider the bounded perturbation\n$$\n\\mathcal L(\\rho)=-i[B,\\rho]-i[A,\\rho]\n   +\\sum_e L_e\\rho L_e^*-\\tfrac12\\{D,\\rho\\}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3ab.1}\n$$\nThere is a finite tower in the Hermitian-control grammar of Theorem O.7.2.3m with physical clock, fresh stock and retained coherent histories. For every fixed trace-class input with an arbitrary fixed spectator it converges in trace norm on the full output and history at every finite observation time. Its reduced semigroup is the unique strongly continuous conservative completely positive semigroup generated by (O.7.2.3ab.1) on the stated domain. Convergence is in diamond norm on each fixed finite input corner.\n\n*Proof.* Put $U_t=e^{-itB}$ and conjugate $A,L_e$ by $U_t$ into the interaction picture. These conjugates are strongly continuous in $t$, together with their adjoints, uniformly bounded on a compact time interval, and the bound $\\Lambda$ still controls the jump sum. Let $S(t,s)$ be the no-jump propagator for $-i\\widetilde A_t-\\widetilde D_t/2$. Its Dyson series in the strong operator integrals is bounded in norm term by term by the exponential series. Differentiating $\\|S(t,s)\\psi\\|^2$ gives $-\\sum_e\\|\\widetilde L_e(t)S(t,s)\\psi\\|^2$, so $S(t,s)$ is a contraction. For each ordered jump word $(e_1,\\ldots,e_m)$ and ordered event times $(t_1,\\ldots,t_m)$ use the amplitude\n$$\nU_T S(T,t_m)\\widetilde L_{e_m}(t_m)\\cdots\n       S(t_2,t_1)\\widetilde L_{e_1}(t_1)S(t_1,0).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3ab.2}\n$$\nIntegrate its squared norm over the time simplex and sum over words and lengths. The contribution from length $m$ is bounded by $(\\Lambda T)^m\\|\\psi\\|^2/m!$. Integrating the norm-loss identity on each inter-event interval cancels its loss with the next jump gain. The unmatched term at length $m$ is bounded by $(\\Lambda T)^{m+1}\\|\\psi\\|^2/(m+1)!$, so the full history map $V_T$ is isometric. Tracing its histories gives the interaction-picture integral equation with bounded time-dependent Lindblad coefficients. Returning to the physical frame gives the variation-of-constants equation for the free trace-class group and the bounded perturbation in (O.7.2.3ab.1). Iteration of the bounded perturbation and the exponential-series bound give uniqueness. The free group has the indicated commutator generator and adding a bounded map leaves its domain unchanged. Finite matrix units form a core for the free generator by commuting coordinate compression in graph norm, hence also for (O.7.2.3ab.1).\n\nAt stage $n$, use $B_n=P_nBP_n$, $A_n=P_nAP_n$, and $L_{e,n}=P_nL_eP_n$ for the first $n$ available jump labels, where $P_n$ projects onto the first $n$ coordinates. Compute the no-jump loss from $D_n=\\sum_{e\\le n}L_{e,n}^*L_{e,n}$. In particular, a component that leaves the coordinate corner contributes neither a retained jump nor a loss at that stage. This gives an exactly normalized finite history. On each fixed vector $P_nAP_n$ and each $P_nL_eP_n$, and their adjoints, converge strongly to the corresponding operators. The uniform summable tail bound gives strong convergence of $D_n$ to $D$. The free phase factors commute with $P_n$. In the interaction picture each finite Dyson product converges on a fixed vector at each fixed tuple of times. The common exponential majorant gives strong convergence of the no-jump factors. For each word the history amplitude converges strongly, with squared norm bounded by $\\|\\psi\\|^2\\prod_i\\|L_{e_i}\\|^2$. The sum of its integrals is at most $e^{\\Lambda T}\\|\\psi\\|^2$. Dominated convergence over the ordered times, lengths and labels yields $V_{T,n}P_n\\psi\\to V_T\\psi$ in the full history space, using the same labels and times at every stage.\n\nTo implement stage $n$, use the positive clock mesh and normalized two-part tick of Theorem O.7.2.3q: the no-jump Kraus block $e^{-i\\widetilde A\\,\\delta}(I-\\delta\\widetilde D_n)^{1/2}$ and the separate blocks $\\sqrt{\\delta}\\,\\widetilde L_{e,n}$, with $\\delta\\|\\widetilde D_n\\|\\le1/2$. The coefficients at the left endpoint and the final free phase are finite computable matrices. The exact finite column isometry $W:\\mathcal H_n\\to\\mathcal H_n\\otimes\\mathcal R_n$ has a computable unitary extension on $\\mathcal H_n\\oplus(\\mathcal H_n\\otimes\\mathcal R_n)$, with block rows $(0,W^*)$ and $(W,I-WW^*)$. This matrix squares to the identity. Pad the finite direct sum to a payload register and extend by the identity. The Hermitian pulse $\\pi(I-U)/2$ implements this involution $U$ in one time unit in dimensionless clock units; its Hamiltonian is rescaled by the paid duration and the clock action unit. The input occupies the first summand with supplied ready factors and the output occupies the second, with its Kraus labels retained coherently. Embed the occupied cells by the time-cell isometry of Theorem O.7.2.3q. For fixed $n$ the matrix coefficients have computable compact moduli from the finite clock phases and supplied entries. The finite-dimensional history estimate of Theorem O.7.2.3q gives a computable mesh and total pulse-precision budget with full-history error at most $2^{-n}$ at its registered endpoints up to time $n$. For a fixed observation time use the preceding mesh endpoint, or include the time in the declared finite schedule when it is computable. The limit histories extended by no events on a vanishing final cell are strongly continuous in the endpoint by the norm majorants and strong continuity of the no-jump factors. The paid event list of Theorem O.7.2.3m includes every finite coefficient computation, matrix and addressed support, input and ready factor, pulse, positive duration, clock and retained record. The cell embeddings specify the comparison with the physical-time history. The within-cell event times are comparison coordinates.\n\nFor the declared increasing input corners, an arbitrary fixed trace-class state is handled by its finite-corner approximants; an out-of-corner input can equivalently be sent to a fixed ready state with a separate flag and the discarded subsystem retained at the input boundary. Its probability on the fixed input tends to zero. On any fixed finite corner, convergence of the isometries on a basis is operator-norm convergence, giving diamond convergence even with a spectator. Finite sums of simple vectors followed by trace-class approximation give the fixed-input result for arbitrary spectators. The same proof applies to finitely many checkpoint interventions on fixed corners by telescoping the normalized maps and recording the intervention port. The output corner and tail tests Q of Theorem O.7.2.3w follow from the resulting complete Choi-state convergence. With deterministic observation clocks, its QJ tests follow in the joint classical-clock and coherent-history dictionary. For example, $B e_j=j e_j$ and $L_1=|v\\rangle\\langle e_0|$, $v=\\sqrt{3}\\sum_{j\\ge1}2^{-j}e_j$, give a normalized nonbanded jump with a nonzero loss at the $e_0$ coordinate. The finite loss uses exactly the norm of the retained jump. ∎\n\nVerifier: actual finite-block tests and oscillation bounds; finite-corner and output-tail tests; complete positivity/trace and causal restriction identities; actual operator domains, core/resolvent or martingale checks; normalized source/instrument induction; and the serialized physical event/resource ledger. Falsifiers include replacing a source by an unrelated limit sampler, omitting a retained phase or rare-history qualification, inferring joint amplitudes from a scalar block name, losing trace through an unrecorded cutoff, or treating a nonexistent source service as free. The proofs use the named classical tightness, Courrège, GKSL and semigroup-generation theorems on their explicit domains. No common spatial hyperbolic tensor is inferred.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723ac\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723ac-finite-feedback-towers-for-effective-feller-transition-semigroups\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3ac (Finite Feedback Towers for Effective Feller Transition Semigroups).** Let $S$ be a compact metric state space with finite rational nets supplied to arbitrary accuracy and computable distances on their union. A separate isolated cemetery point may be included. Let $X$ be a càdlàg conservative Feller process on $S$, with its specified generator $\\mathcal A$, closed domain and initial law. If a sub-Markov process is used, extend it by its absorbing cemetery state and require the resulting process to be Feller on this $S$. Supply an effective weak name of the initial law and a uniform weak name of the transition probabilities $P_h(x,\\cdot)$ for rational $h>0$ and net points $x$, with a computable spatial modulus in bounded Wasserstein distance for each $P_h$. These are finite row inputs to the constructor. Equip $S$ with continuous bounded Hermitian $A(x)$ and finitely many continuous bounded matrices $L_e(x)$ of a fixed finite dimension, with computable evaluations, norm bounds and compact moduli. Then the source grammar of Theorem O.7.2.3m realizes a finite feedback tower with the original $J_1$ state-path limit $X$ and joint coherent outputs given by the time-ordered coefficients $A(X_t),L_e(X_t)$. The generator, domain, cemetery and initial law are those of the declared Feller process. Each stage has a finite physical clock and a complete resource and outcome record.\n\n*Proof.* Scale the metric to be bounded by one. Take $h_n=2^{-n}$ and horizon $T_n=n$, with $M_n=T_n/h_n$ ticks. Choose positive computable rational $\\varepsilon_n<1$ decreasing to zero. Set $a_{M_n}=\\varepsilon_n$. Working backwards, choose each positive rational $a_k\\le a_{k+1}$ so that distance at most $a_k$ implies row Wasserstein distance at most $a_{k+1}\\varepsilon_n/(4M_n)$. Use one finite net with mesh small enough to approximate each actual row at its net point to Wasserstein error at most $\\min_k a_{k+1}\\varepsilon_n/(4M_n)$. The uniform row name supplies each dyadic row on that net with this error, using a sufficiently fine finite net for the row outputs and assigning any final rounding residue to a fixed entry. The initial name gives an initial dyadic row with Wasserstein error at most $a_0\\varepsilon_n/2$. Include the cemetery point in the net, and set its row to its point mass. These choices involve only finite tables, the stated row moduli and the stage accuracy.\n\nUse the dyadic rows as actual fresh conditional transition samplers: the current state label selects the row and a fresh fair seed selects the next label. Retain the old state, seed, label and clock index at every tick. To prove convergence, couple the initial label with $X_0$ and at each time couple the exact row at $X_{kh_n}$ with the finite row at the current label. For a measurable near-optimal coupling, quantize the exact row by a fixed Borel partition of mesh $\\eta$, solve the finite transport problem to the dyadic row with a fixed lexicographic tie-break, and lift the first marginal using its conditional law in each positive-mass cell. The coefficients are measurable in the first row and the excess cost is at most $2\\eta$. On the event of current distance at most $a_k$, the triangle inequality and Markov's inequality bound the probability of violating the next threshold by $\\varepsilon_n/M_n$, with slack for the approximate coupling. The initial failure probability is at most $\\varepsilon_n$. Consequently the maximum distance at all grid times is at most $\\varepsilon_n$ except on an event of probability at most $2\\varepsilon_n$. The step path of the exact grid samples converges in $J_1$ to $X$ on any fixed dyadic horizon at which $X$ has no jump. Indeed a càdlàg path admits step approximants with finitely many partition jumps and arbitrarily small uniform error. Align those finitely many jump times with their first following grid times by an increasing piecewise-linear time change. The alignment error is at most one mesh interval, and the remaining uniform error is bounded by twice the step-approximation error. For a Feller process at any fixed positive time stochastic continuity and the càdlàg left limit imply the absence of a fixed-time jump almost surely. The preceding coupling and this path argument prove convergence of the actual state-label paths on all fixed dyadic horizons. An isolated cemetery label is part of this topology and its absorbing transition. Killing is recorded by entrance into that label.\n\nFor a bounded coefficient path use the no-jump propagator with generator $-iA(x_t)-\\sum_eL_e(x_t)^*L_e(x_t)/2$ and the history amplitude of Theorem O.7.2.3ab with identity free phase. The coefficients may be measurable in time. Its Dyson integrals converge and the norm-loss identity holds almost everywhere. The normalized complete history isometry follows by integration, with the same exponential bound on all word lengths. For $J_1$ convergence of paths the state values converge at every continuity time of the limiting path. Continuity and boundedness of the matrices give convergence of all coefficients almost everywhere and in $L^1$ on a fixed horizon. The no-jump factors converge uniformly in both endpoints by the integral Duhamel identity and the $L^1$ bound. The ordered jump amplitudes then converge at almost every time tuple. The exponential word majorant gives convergence in isometry norm on the fixed finite input space, uniformly for spectator extensions. This proves continuity of the coherent history output in the joint path limit. It also accommodates coefficient jumps at entry into the isolated cemetery, with the chosen cemetery coefficients.\n\nAt each finite stage use the actual current label to select the normalized finite tick: the no-jump column is $e^{-iA\\delta}(I-\\delta D)^{1/2}$, and the other columns are $\\sqrt{\\delta}L_e$ with $D=\\sum_eL_e^*L_e$. Refine inside the state ticks so that $\\delta\\|D\\|\\le1/2$. Retain the jump flags coherently. For each cell the overlap of the time-cell-embedded finite tick with its exact history isometry is $I+O(\\delta^2)$, uniformly over the finite table, by the no-event expansion and the sum of the one-event overlaps. Multiplication of contractions bounds the full overlap error by a constant times $\\sum\\delta^2$. Thus, as in Theorem O.7.2.3q, the mesh and pulse precision can be chosen to give isometry error at most $2^{-n}$ on $[0,T_n]$. The self-adjoint involution extension of a finite column isometry in Theorem O.7.2.3ab supplies the actual addressed pulse on fresh paid record factors. At a scheduled intervention include that checkpoint in the mesh. Finite compositions on the declared intervention menu preserve the comparison by telescoping.\n\nAt each stage the finite sampler has a dyadic row table, a fresh seed and complete normalized lookup on all supported labels. The paid event list of Theorem O.7.2.3m includes every finite row and coefficient preparation, lookup, state copy, local support, pulse, clock interval, controlled observation and retained record. Only the designated state path and coherent-history factors are compared as path and quantum outputs. The supplier retains its internal seeds and lookup data as separate finite archive factors with their physical costs. They may be included as fixed finite-stage readouts under their actual record types. The uniform finite-dimensional isometry limit gives the quantum corner and tail tests, and the coupled path convergence gives the classical Polish-record tests of Theorem O.7.2.3z and their joint form QJ. For the interval-valued continuous example below, use the polygonal interpolation of the retained labels for continuous-path tests. The coupling bounds its uniform error by the maximum grid error plus the limiting path oscillation on one cell. The coherent history still uses the actual step labels, which approach the same limit. The jump and killing branches use $J_1$.\n\nFor a jump and killing example, take $S=[0,1]\\cup\\{\\partial\\}$ with $\\partial$ isolated, and a clock of rate one with jump row $(1-x/2)\\delta_{x/2}+(x/2)\\delta_\\partial$ at $x\\in[0,1]$, and $\\delta_\\partial$ at the cemetery. Its generator is $Pf-f$, where $P$ is the displayed jump row. The jump kernel changes the state in the interval and has nonzero killing on $x>0$. It maps continuous functions to continuous functions, and its Poisson series supplies effective transition rows and spatial moduli. The generator domain is all of $C(S)$ and it includes the restrictions of smooth test functions on the interval with independent cemetery value.\n\nFor a continuous example with a non-Lipschitz diffusion coefficient, use the absorbed Brownian motion on $[0,1]$ with generator $\\tfrac12\\partial_y^2$ in the interior. Push it forward by $x=y^4$. The induced process is Feller, and in the interior its stochastic equation has drift $6\\sqrt{x}$ and diffusion coefficient $4x^{3/4}$. At each endpoint it uses the absorbing boundary law of the specified time-homogeneous generator. The transition rows in $y$ have an effective weak name and modulus. For example, uniformly approximate a Lipschitz test function by its affine endpoint interpolant plus a finite sine polynomial with zero endpoint values. Effective uniform sine approximation of the residual follows by odd periodic extension and Fejér averaging. The affine interpolant is invariant under stopped Brownian motion, and each $\\sin(k\\pi y)$ has evolution factor $e^{-k^2\\pi^2 h/2}$ by Itô's formula stopped at the first endpoint. These give computable expectation evaluations. A finite Lipschitz partition of unity with small supports turns them into a row law on a finite net with controlled Wasserstein error. Alternatively the same Brownian driving noise couples two initial points at expected distance equal to their initial separation, since absorption preserves their order and each stopped coordinate is a martingale. The map $y\\mapsto y^4$ is Lipschitz and its inverse has an effective Hölder modulus, giving the required spatial modulus in $x$. The domain of the induced generator is transported from the absorbed Brownian domain, with the stated absorbing boundary law. The construction realizes this specified source even though the globally Lipschitz Euler hypothesis for the displayed coefficients fails. A nonconstant coherent coefficient, for example $A(x)=xZ$ with a bounded jump matrix on the same clock, gives state-dependent coherent outputs in either example. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723ad\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723ad-finite-resolvent-sources-for-recorded-markov-interventions\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3ad (Finite Resolvent Sources for Recorded Markov Interventions).** Suppose the conservative resolvent criterion of Theorem O.7.2.3x holds on $X=\\mathcal T(H)$ or on a classical–quantum predual $X=L^1(S,\\mu;\\mathcal T(H))$. Supply finite channel encodings $D_n:X\\to X_n$, $I_n:X_n\\to X$ with $D_n I_n=1$, $I_nD_n\\to1$ strongly and effective names on a fixed computable dense rational test family. Here $X_n$ is a finite matrix algebra predual or a finite direct sum of those preduals. Supply each integer resolvent channel $C_j=j(j-L)^{-1}$ on those tests with trace-norm error bounds and computable finite compression. The effective encoding data include names of the embedded finite matrix-unit inputs and the compressed outputs on the dense test family. Then finite Hermitian-control sources realize the specified reduced semigroup $T_t$ strongly and uniformly on bounded time intervals, with arbitrary fixed spectators. Every fixed finite intervention protocol in the declared menu of computably compressed CPTP or complete finite-outcome instruments has convergent joint outcome and final quantum records. The corresponding finite-input corner and tail tests hold when the named outputs have a common Hilbert-space embedding preserving trace norm. The input law, closed extension, actual finite clock, intervention instruments and compressed field carrier determine the comparison.\n\n*Proof.* Write $E_n=I_nD_n$. On $\\mathcal T(H)$ an example is the compression to the first $n$ basis vectors followed by recovery of the missing weight in a fixed included basis state. It is CPTP and is the identity on the encoded corner. Its strong limit follows from trace-class finite-rank approximation and positivity of the missing trace on positive inputs. On a sigma-finite classical carrier with a computable generating sequence of refining finite-measure partitions of an exhaustion, average the operator-valued density on each occupied cell and compress its matrix part in the same way. Return the trace outside the exhaustion as a fixed reference state in one occupied cell. Embedding assigns a constant density of the corresponding trace on each cell. The maps are CPTP, the decoder is a left inverse to the embedding, and the strong limit follows first on finite cell-simple finite-matrix densities and then by contraction. All cell sizes, exhausted sets and matrix cutoffs in this example belong to the encodings.\n\nFix a dense list $(\\rho_i)$ of rational tests. At stage $j$, choose $n(j)$ so that for $i\\le j$,\n$$\n\\|E_{n(j)}\\rho_i-\\rho_i\\|_1+\n\\|E_{n(j)}C_j\\rho_i-C_j\\rho_i\\|_1<j^{-3}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3ad.1}\n$$\nThe encoded list and norm names permit a search with strict tolerances, and strong convergence gives termination. Put $E=E_{n(j)}$, $D=D_{n(j)}$, $I=I_{n(j)}$. The finite channel $\\widehat C_j=D C_j I$ has a computable matrix on the finite carrier. Indeed the compressed images of arbitrary finite inputs follow by density, contractivity and the effective encodings. Exact complete positivity and trace preservation follow from the composition. Its finite Choi blocks supply a finite isometry with computable coefficients by the positive square root and the trace identity. For a direct sum, store the classical input and output sector labels using the stipulated classical registers and compile the input-output block channels. Their trace identity is the sum over every output sector for that input sector.\n\nUse one dyadic physical tick of duration $h_j>0$ with $h_j j\\le 1/2$ and $h_jj^2\\to0$. Its finite reduced channel is $(1-h_jj)1+h_jj\\widehat C_j$. The control performs a fresh normalized dyadic choice and executes the appropriate finite isometry, including its record. The returned comparison state is the encoded system. The finite pulse and separate stocks are supplied by the involution construction of Theorem O.7.2.3m. Each stage fixes its finite horizon, addresses, strictly positive pulse intervals, fresh environment for each application and resource ledger. Coefficients can either be used as the declared computable Hermitian services or approximated with total complete-channel error at most $2^{-j}$ over the whole stage. Allocation at a shorter clock slot is metered by its actual pulse norm. Retain all records of the installed instruments and all simulator records with their declared ownership.\n\nFor convergence on $X$ use the simulated generator $B_j=j(E C_j E-1)$ and the true bounded Yosida generator $L_j=j(C_j-1)$. Both give contractive Poisson expansions. To control the Duhamel integral uniformly on the stage horizon, use a finite list along that expansion: replace (O.7.2.3ad.1) by the same inequality for the finite set $\\{C_j^k\\rho_i: 0\\le k\\le b_j,\\ i\\le j\\}$, where $b_j\\ge 4j^2+4j$. These additional norm names follow by repeated contraction and density. Then\n$$\n\\sup_{0\\le s\\le j}\\|(B_j-L_j)e^{sL_j}\\rho_i\\|_1\n\\le j^{-2} + 2j\\|\\rho_i\\|_1\\Pr\\{\\operatorname{Pois}(j^2)>b_j\\},\n\\qquad i\\le j .\n\\tag{O.7.2.3ad.2}\n$$\nThe first term follows by bounding $EC_jE-C_j$ by the two approximation errors. The tail bound uses the two contractions and stochastic monotonicity of the Poisson variable. For instance the exponential moment at base two gives $\\Pr\\{\\operatorname{Pois}(j^2)>b_j\\}\\le e^{j^2}2^{-b_j}$. Duhamel now gives convergence of $e^{tB_j}-e^{tL_j}$ on the dense list uniformly for $t\\le T$. The Yosida approximation of Theorem O.7.2.3x gives $e^{tL_j}\\to T_t$ on that interval, and contraction extends convergence to every input.\n\nThe finite products are the restriction of $(1+h_j B_j)^{\\lfloor t/h_j\\rfloor}$ to the image of the initialized encoding. For one tick, the norm difference from $e^{h_jB_j}$ is at most $\\tfrac12(h_j\\|B_j\\|)^2e^{h_j\\|B_j\\|}$. Both maps are contractions and $\\|B_j\\|\\le2j$, so the cumulative error up to $T$ is bounded by $2eT h_jj^2$, plus a final interval error at most $2jh_j$. The initialization error $\\|E\\rho-\\rho\\|_1$ vanishes by enlarging $n(j)$ so $n(j)\\ge j$. This yields the asserted physical tick limit. For a fixed spectator dimension use the finitely many block entries of an input matrix. For an infinite fixed spectator, finite-rank approximation and the completely contractive estimates give the strong result for each fixed input.\n\nFor a fixed protocol declare its checkpoints before compilation. Take a subdivided clock with a possibly shortened final interval between checkpoints, or round each fixed checkpoint with an error at most one mesh interval. Apply the encoded intervention maps $D\\mathcal V_b I$ on the corresponding branch, where the finite set of outcome maps $\\mathcal V_b$ has a CPTP sum. The same Choi construction realizes each complete instrument and retains its output label. At every step contractivity of the complete instrument into the direct sum, strong convergence of the intervening channels and convergence of $E$ give convergence in the sum of trace norms of the branch densities. A finite decision tree with outcome-dependent instruments is a composition of this type. Retained output amplitudes inside each nominated quantum output of the instrument are included in its CP map. For a fixed finite input corner the finitely many matrix entries give trace-norm Choi convergence with its reference, so (Q1)–(Q2) yield the finite-corner and tail tests. The register of the fixed protocol has a finite alphabet, and this trace-norm conclusion includes its joint outcome law. A fixed classical-coordinate observation, when used, is an actual declared finite-outcome instrument on the hybrid predual.\n\nThe dilation environments of the numerical resolvent ticks are supplied resources. A comparison that includes their full time history requires its own output embedding and history convergence. For instance, even for $C_j=1$, retaining every use of the second mixture branch as a separate jump in a fixed time interval gives a binomial count with mean asymptotic to $Tj$ and variance at most its mean. Its probability in any fixed finite-count history sector tends to zero. That representation violates the full-history tail test in a count-graded history space. The limit proved here retains the outcomes of the declared intervention instruments and the final quantum output. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723ae\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723ae-finite-feedback-on-effectively-compactified-feller-carriers\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3ae (Finite Feedback on Effectively Compactified Feller Carriers).** Let $E$ be a noncompact locally compact Polish state space whose one-point compactification $E^\\infty=E\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$ has a computable compatible metric and effective finite nets containing $\\infty$. An isolated absorbing cemetery may already belong to $E$. Let $X$ be an $E$-valued càdlàg conservative Feller process on $C_0(E)$ with the specified closed generator, domain and initial law. Supply an effective weak name of the initial law and of the compactified rational-time transition rows with a computable spatial Wasserstein modulus as in Theorem O.7.2.3ac. The state-dependent finite-dimensional coefficients $A(x),L_e(x)$ are continuous and locally bounded on $E$ and computable at the finite source labels. Then finite sources in the grammar of Theorem O.7.2.3m converge on every bounded time horizon to the original $E$-valued path in $J_1$ and to its joint coherent outputs. On an interval or Euclidean carrier with continuous limiting paths, polygonal state readouts pass the continuous-path tests as well.\n\n*Proof.* A function in $C(E^\\infty)$ has a unique decomposition $f=c+g$, with $g\\in C_0(E)$ and $c=f(\\infty)$. Define $\\widehat P_t f=c+P_tg$ on $E$ and $\\widehat P_t f(\\infty)=c$. Conservativity on $E$ makes this the original law there. At $\\infty$ the row is absorbing. The decomposition, positivity and strong continuity on $C_0(E)$ prove the Feller property on the compactification. The original path is also càdlàg there. It has no fixed-time jump, by the argument of Theorem O.7.2.3ac.\n\nUse the effective finite row construction and the coupling of Theorem O.7.2.3ac for $\\widehat P_t$. Its path argument uses no coherent coefficients. For a finite stage, treat the $\\infty$ label as an archived exceptional label and decode it to a fixed point of $E$. Use the chosen value of the coherent coefficients at that point. At other labels the state and coefficients use their actual evaluations. The finite list has a finite computable norm bound even when the coefficients are unbounded on $E$. Use the normalized ticks and positive pulse intervals of Theorem O.7.2.3ac, with the coefficient-dependent mesh and pulse precision selected anew at each stage to give conditional history-isometry error at most $2^{-n}$ on $[0,T_n]$, uniformly over that finite stage's label histories.\n\nFor a sample path of $X$ on $[0,T]$, the closure in $E$ of its range is compact. For example, every sequence of times has a monotone subsequence with values tending to a right value or a left limit, and both lie in $E$. The path therefore has positive distance from $\\infty$ in the compact metric. For a deterministic $\\eta>0$ put $K_\\eta=\\{x:d(x,\\infty)\\ge\\eta\\}$. This is compact in $E$, and the event that $X$ stays in $K_{2\\eta}$ has probability tending to one as $\\eta\\downarrow0$. On that event, a sufficiently accurate $J_1$ time change from the finite coupled path places all its values in $K_\\eta$. There the original and compactified metrics have uniformly continuous identity maps, the coefficient bounds and uniform continuity are valid, and the exceptional label never occurs. It follows from the coupling that the decoded state paths converge in probability in $J_1$ to the original path. If $E$ is Euclidean and the limiting path is continuous, this also gives uniform convergence for polygonal readouts by the uniform oscillation of the limiting path.\n\nOn the same compact sets the coefficient paths converge in $L^1$ in probability by the time-change and almost-everywhere continuity argument of Theorem O.7.2.3ac. Their exact histories converge in isometry norm in probability by the same Duhamel and ordered-word estimates. The actual conditional histories differ by at most the stage error. Exhaustion by the high-probability compact events therefore gives joint convergence of the state records and of bounded continuous test integrals of the history maps on fixed input corners. To see the tail test, first take the limiting state-integrated Choi state, whose trace is one by normalization on each history. It has vanishing finite-rank tails. Trace-norm convergence of the unweighted integrals then transfers this tail bound to the finite stages. The same estimates give the scalar tightness and the mixed finite-corner tests of QJ. The complete source ledger still retains every stage label, including the exceptional one, and all clocks and seed records.\n\nA noncompact jump-and-killing example has $E=\\mathbb Z\\sqcup\\{\\partial\\}$, nearest-neighbor symmetric jumps at total rate one, and a jump from $0$ to the isolated absorbing $\\partial$ at rate one. Uniformization by the rate-two clock gives the transition rows by a computably truncated Poisson series of finite-support iterations. Give $\\mathbb Z$ the topology of discrete labels with $|k|\\to\\infty$ represented by the extra point $\\infty$ in the compactification. For a starting label of large absolute value, reaching any fixed finite block or $\\partial$ by time $t$ requires the clock to register at least the distance to that block. The computable Poisson bound thus supplies a spatial modulus near $\\infty$. Finite labels and $\\partial$ are isolated, so the remaining modulus and nets can be chosen from finite data. The generator is the nearest-neighbor difference plus the stated killing jump on all of $C_0(E)$. The unbounded coherent Hamiltonian $A(k)=kZ$ with a fixed bounded jump matrix and nominated cemetery coefficients is covered.\n\nFor a continuous non-Lipschitz example take $X_t=Y_t^3$ on $\\mathbb R$ for Brownian motion $Y$. This is a conservative Feller process by conjugation on $C_0(\\mathbb R)$. Its closed domain is the transported Brownian domain. The interior Itô coefficients in $x$ are drift $3\\operatorname{sgn}(x)|x|^{1/3}$ and diffusion amplitude $3|x|^{2/3}$, with the Brownian conjugacy supplying the chosen passage through zero. The compactification metric can be taken from stereographic embedding of $\\mathbb R$ in the circle. Gaussian integration supplies effective row evaluations. To obtain an effective modulus, choose a large compact interval containing all compactification points at a specified positive distance from $\\infty$. For $|x|$ large, the chance that $(\\sqrt[3]{x}+Y_t-Y_0)^3$ enters that interval is bounded by a computable Gaussian tail. On bounded $x$ ranges, coupling the same Gaussian increment and using the Hölder modulus of the cube root and polynomial continuity of the cube on a bounded increment event supplies a spatial modulus, with the discarded event bounded by another Gaussian tail. This identifies every required row without a fitted diffusion coefficient or a Lipschitz assumption at zero. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723af\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723af-coherent-finite-sources-for-nonexplosive-unbounded-jump-rates\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3af (Coherent Finite Sources for Nonexplosive Unbounded Jump Rates).** Let $\\mathcal H=\\ell^2(\\mathbb N)$ and let $B e_i=b_i e_i$ be a self-adjoint diagonal clock with computable entries. For a countable declared set of jump labels let\n$$\nL_e e_i=\\ell_{e,i} e_{f_e(i)}\n$$\non the active inputs of label $e$, with $\\ell_{e,i}\\ne0$ there and $L_e e_i=0$ otherwise. Each $f_e$ is injective on its active inputs. Supply a finite effective list of the active labels, destinations and complex amplitudes at each $i$. Set $r_i=\\sum_e|\\ell_{e,i}|^2$. Assume that the classical marked continuous-time chain with these rates and destinations is nonexplosive from every basis state, counting self-jumps among its events. Then the formal generator\n$$\nL_0(\\rho)=-i[B,\\rho]+\\sum_e L_e\\rho L_e^*-\\tfrac12\\{\\operatorname{diag}(r_i),\\rho\\}\n\\tag{O.7.2.3af.1}\n$$\non the finite matrix span has a conservative trace-class closure with that span as an operator core. Finite sources in the grammar of Theorem O.7.2.3m converge to its semigroup and to the joint system and full coherent jump history, including phases and any fixed spectator. The conclusion permits genuinely unbounded jump rates and nonbanded destinations. The generator and the declared marked jump coefficients both enter the comparison.\n\n*Proof.* For $0<t_1<\\cdots<t_k<T$ and a label word $(e_1,\\ldots,e_k)$, define the amplitude\n$$\nD_{T-t_k} L_{e_k}D_{t_k-t_{k-1}}\\cdots L_{e_1}D_{t_1},\n\\qquad\nD_t e_i=\\exp\\bigl((-ib_i-r_i/2)t\\bigr)e_i .\n\\tag{O.7.2.3af.2}\n$$\nFor $k=0$ use $D_T$. Apply the amplitudes first to finite input vectors and regard their collection as one vector in the orthogonal sum over word lengths and labels, with Lebesgue measure on ordered time simplexes. For each word the final basis state is injective as a function of the initial basis state where the word is active. Therefore the squared norm for a finite input vector is the sum, over its initial basis components, of the corresponding classical probabilities weighted by their squared input amplitudes. On a basis state the squared amplitude is exactly the density of the indicated holding times and marks, multiplied by the probability of no further jump before $T$. Nonexplosion gives total mass one. Thus (O.7.2.3af.2) extends to an isometry $V_T$ on the entire Hilbert space, with time and label coherences intact. In particular this construction also includes distinct sources with the same reduced channel but different declared jump amplitudes.\n\nSplitting a history at a deterministic time identifies the two ordered-time record factors with the full ordered history, up to the null set of jumps at the splitting time. Under that identification the isometries compose in chronological order, by direct multiplication of (O.7.2.3af.2). Tracing out the history therefore gives a semigroup of trace-preserving completely positive contractions. On a finite input corner the vacuum column tends to the identity and the total nonvacuum probability tends to zero as $T\\downarrow0$. Finite-rank approximation gives strong continuity on trace class. On a matrix unit the first-order expansion of the vacuum and the one-jump terms yields (O.7.2.3af.1). The probability of two or more jumps from each basis state is $O(T^2)$, since its finitely many first destinations have finite rates. Cauchy–Schwarz gives the same order for the corresponding reduced matrix-unit terms.\n\nTo verify that the finite matrix span is a core, fix $\\lambda>0$ and suppose a bounded operator annihilates the range of $\\lambda-L_0$ under the trace pairing. Denote its paired matrix coefficients by $a_{ij}$. The annihilator equation on $|i\\rangle\\langle j|$ expresses $a_{ij}$ as the sum over labels active at both $i$ and $j$ of the paired coefficient at their destinations, integrated over the first holding time. Its kernel at time $s$ is the product of the two corresponding scalar one-jump amplitudes, conjugating the one from $j$, with the additional factor $e^{-\\lambda s}$. This is obtained by integrating the exponential from the vacuum term; its real decay parameter is $\\lambda+(r_i+r_j)/2>0$. Iterate the relation $k$ times. By Cauchy–Schwarz on the common ordered-time and label histories,\n$$\n|a_{ij}|\\le \\|a\\|_{\\rm op}\n\\left(\\mathbb E_i[e^{-\\lambda\\tau_k};\\tau_k<\\infty]\\,\n      \\mathbb E_j[e^{-\\lambda\\tau_k};\\tau_k<\\infty]\\right)^{1/2},\n\\tag{O.7.2.3af.3}\n$$\nwhere $\\tau_k$ is the time of the $k$th jump and $\\|a\\|_{\\rm op}$ is the norm of the bounded paired operator. Each expectation tends to zero by nonexplosion and bounded convergence, taking $e^{-\\lambda\\infty}=0$. The annihilator is zero, so the range is dense. The contraction-semigroup generator extending $L_0$ has a bounded resolvent at $\\lambda$. Approximation in its range gives approximation in its graph norm, proving the core statement and the unique semigroup for the resulting closed domain. Its resolvents obey Theorem O.7.2.3x.\n\nHere is an actual finite tower retaining the record. On a finite set $F_n=\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$ keep every jump whose source and destination lie in $F_n$ with its original complex amplitude. For a jump leaving $F_n$, send it into an orthogonal absorbing STOP coordinate keyed by the initial index and exit label. Use separate orthogonal record labels for these exits. This produces finitely many channels because each active source has a finite list. It gives the same no-jump rates $r_i$ before STOP and uses the diagonal clock $B$ on $F_n$. Initial states on a fixed corner are embedded as themselves when $F_n$ contains the corner. An arbitrary input can use the trace-preserving encoding of Theorem O.7.2.3ad with the outside mass sent to a separate retained input-escape coordinate. The source keeps STOP and all event marks. Common output embeddings place the stopped flags and histories in sectors orthogonal to the original system/history sector, using the original indices and exit labels.\n\nThe stopped history and (O.7.2.3af.2) agree exactly on histories that stay in $F_n$ from the input to time $T$. This event can be imposed on the amplitude by a common output projection: a given word and final index have at most one initial index and one backwards chain of visited indices, by injectivity. Hence the complementary squared norms on a fixed input corner are both bounded by\n$$\n\\max_{i\\ \\mathrm{in\\ the\\ corner}}\\Pr_i\\{\\text{the marked path exits }F_n\\text{ by }T\\}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3af.4}\n$$\nThe probability tends to zero since on every bounded time interval the chain makes finitely many jumps almost surely. For example, if a proper computable $W\\ge1$ satisfies $\\sum_e|\\ell_{e,i}|^2[W(f_e(i))-W(i)]\\le C W(i)$ with $C\\ge0$, the stopped finite-chain inequality gives the bound $e^{CT} W(i)/\\inf_{j\\notin F_n}W(j)$. It follows by applying the generator estimate to $e^{-Ct}W$ up to first exit and using the minimum value at exit. Bounded rates on each finite set and this bound also imply nonexplosion. For a fixed finite corner, (O.7.2.3af.4) bounds the squared norm of the difference of the two isometries by four times that probability. It applies with any spectator, since the good-history projection preserves the orthogonality of initial basis columns, and tensoring an operator with the identity preserves its norm.\n\nFor each $n$ the stopped finite carrier has bounded computable Hamiltonian and jump matrices. Use the finite normalized coherent tick of Theorem O.7.2.3ac, followed by the time-cell embedding and actual finite Hermitian pulses. On a growing finite time horizon choose the mesh and pulse accuracy for conditional isometry error at most $2^{-n}$ against its own exact stopped dynamics. Every mesh clock, fresh pointer, STOP record, pulse, preparation, lookup and precision budget is part of the finite source ledger. Norm convergence on fixed corners implies trace-norm Choi convergence with arbitrary fixed spectators, hence the corner and tail tests of Theorem O.7.2.3w. If a nominated classical readout measures the retained jump times and labels, its finite blocks converge by the same norm estimate and the time-cell embedding. Fixed checkpoints can be inserted in the mesh. Telescoping the finite intervention protocol and finite-rank approximation of its intermediate trace-class outputs give convergence of the joint records for every fixed complete instrument with computably compressed finite implementations. No sampling of an unknown quantum state as a classical trajectory is used.\n\nFor a nonbanded example take one label, $f(i)=2i$, $b_i=i$, $r_i=1+\\lfloor\\log_2 i\\rfloor$ and real positive $\\ell_i=\\sqrt{r_i}$. The function $W(i)=1+\\lfloor\\log_2 i\\rfloor$ is proper and the drift equals $W(i)$, since $W(2i)=W(i)+1$. The stopped bound verifies nonexplosion and yields an explicit coherent tail bound on each corner. Here $\\|L_e\\|$ is unbounded and the shift distance $|f(i)-i|$ grows without bound. ∎\n\n<a id=\"prop-o723ag\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o723ag-limit-computability-obstruction-for-uniform-finite-towers\"></a>**Proposition O.7.2.3ag (Limit-Computability Obstruction for Uniform Finite Towers).** Consider a source in the finite Hermitian-control grammar of Theorem O.7.2.3m whose stage descriptors, finite record embeddings and fixed bounded probe probabilities at a registered rational time can be computed to any rational tolerance, uniformly in stage and tolerance. If a fixed probe expectation converges, its limit is limit-computable, meaning the limit of a computable sequence of rational numbers with no assumed convergence modulus. This excludes some rich Feller jump and killing laws, some continuous non-Lipschitz diffusions, and some conservative closed-domain quantum laws from the uniform effective finite-tower image.\n\n*Proof.* For each stage $n$ compute the actual probe expectation with error at most $2^{-n}$. The rational answers form a computable sequence and have the same limit as the exact expectations. Applying a computable continuous inverse on a compact interval transfers limit-computability to the recovered parameter. In particular, if a continuous formula is invertible on a known compact parameter interval, its inverse can first be applied after clipping approximants to the image interval.\n\nChoose a real $\\theta\\in[1,2]$ that is not limit-computable. Such reals exist since there are countably many programs producing rational sequences. The following declared mathematical laws each have a fixed probe that recovers $\\theta$.\n\n1. On $\\mathbb R\\sqcup\\{\\partial\\}$ with an isolated cemetery $\\partial$, from $x$ jump to $x+1$ at rate one and to $\\partial$ at rate $\\theta$. Start at $0$. The independent exponential kill clock and rate-one Poisson motion give a conservative Feller transition law on the enlarged state space, and a sub-Markov Feller law on $C_0(\\mathbb R)$ before adding the cemetery. The generator is bounded, acts by the jump and killing formula on all of $C_0$, and in particular has the usual smooth compactly supported core. The bounded continuous function $\\mathbf1_{\\{\\partial\\}}$ has time-one expectation $1-e^{-\\theta}$. Evaluation at time one is almost surely continuous in the $J_1$ topology because the law has no jump at that fixed time.\n2. Let $X_t=(\\sqrt{\\theta}\\,W_t)^3$ for standard Brownian motion $W$ starting at zero. The full Markov family is the pushforward of Brownian motion of diffusivity $\\theta$ by cubing, and its closed generator and core are transported from Brownian motion on $C_0(\\mathbb R)$. The interior coefficients are $3\\theta\\,\\operatorname{sgn}(x)|x|^{1/3}$ and $3\\sqrt{\\theta}|x|^{2/3}$. The conjugacy supplies the passage through zero. The bounded continuous readout $\\cos(\\sqrt[3]{X_1})$ has expectation $e^{-\\theta/2}$ by the Gaussian characteristic function. It is a continuous-path probe.\n3. In the history model of Theorem O.7.2.3af take $f(i)=2i$, $b_i=i$, $r_1=\\theta$ and $r_i=1+\\lfloor\\log_2 i\\rfloor$ for $i>1$, with a single positive jump amplitude $\\sqrt{r_i}$. For the mathematical definition the rates need only be finite at each index. The drift of $W(i)=1+\\lfloor\\log_2 i\\rfloor$ is at most $2W(i)$ and proves nonexplosion. The normalization and core proof of Theorem O.7.2.3af uses only these finite rates and nonexplosion, so the same closed domain passes the conservative resolvent criterion. From $|1\\rangle\\langle1|$ the time-one probability of the final projection $|1\\rangle\\langle1|$ is $e^{-\\theta}$, since each jump strictly increases the index. This is a fixed corner test of the system output and also follows from the joint history test. The generator has an unbounded jump norm and nonbanded destinations.\n\nIn each example a source comparison preserving the stated probe would make its expectation limit-computable and hence make $\\theta$ limit-computable. This is impossible. The obstruction uses uniform effective source data and its readout. It does not assume an effective convergence rate and leaves nonuniform mathematical approximants and separately declared noneffective services outside its domain. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723ah\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723ah-finite-feedback-from-an-effective-feller-graph-core\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3ah (Finite Feedback from an Effective Feller Graph Core).** Let $E$ be an effectively compact metric state space in the sense of Theorem O.7.2.3ac, with an effective presentation of $C(E)$ by evaluation, uniform norm and uniform-continuity names. Suppose the declared closed generator $A$ gives a conservative Feller semigroup and a càdlàg process. Supply an enumerable rational linear graph core with effective $C(E)$ names of each $g$ and $Ag$, and the effective initial law. The core and its images determine effective transition rows and their spatial Wasserstein moduli by a terminating construction. Thus Theorem O.7.2.3ac gives the finite feedback tower with joint coherent histories from the core data. The result also applies on the one-point compactification of Theorem O.7.2.3ae when the core is supplied on $C_0(E)$ with the corresponding effective compactified names. The process and its closed extension are part of the generator hypothesis.\n\n*Proof.* Write $P_t$ for the conservative contraction semigroup. For each rational $\\lambda>0$ and named $f\\in C(E)$, search the core for $g$ with $\\|(\\lambda-A)g-f\\|_\\infty<\\lambda\\varepsilon$. Such a search terminates since $(\\lambda-A)$ maps the full domain onto $C(E)$ and the core is dense in graph norm. The norm inequality for the contraction resolvent certifies $\\|g-R_\\lambda f\\|_\\infty<\\varepsilon$. Thus the core provides a uniform approximation name of the resolvent on all named tests, including computable moduli inherited from the approximants.\n\nTo compute $P_t f$ for rational $t\\ge0$, first approximate $f$ by a core element $g$ in uniform norm, with a certified bound on $\\|Ag\\|_\\infty$. For $\\lambda>0$, put $C_\\lambda=\\lambda R_\\lambda$ and let $S_\\lambda(t)=\\exp(t\\lambda(C_\\lambda-I))$. This operator is the Poisson mixture of the powers of $C_\\lambda$. Its $k$th summand integrates $P_s$ against the law of a sum of $k$ independent exponential holding times of rate $\\lambda$, by the Laplace resolvent and the semigroup law. Include the zero holding time for $k=0$. The compound Poisson clock of parameter $\\lambda t$ has mean $t$ and variance $2t/\\lambda$. On the generator domain, $\\|P_sg-P_tg\\|_\\infty\\le |s-t|\\|Ag\\|_\\infty$ by integration of the semigroup derivative and contraction. Consequently\n$$\n\\|S_\\lambda(t)g-P_tg\\|_\\infty\n\\le \\sqrt{2t/\\lambda}\\,\\|Ag\\|_\\infty .\n\\tag{O.7.2.3ah.1}\n$$\nChoose $\\lambda$ from this bound, truncate the Poisson sum with its computable probability tail, and evaluate the finitely many resolvent powers by the core search. Approximation errors propagate by contraction. This produces approximating $C(E)$ names and effective uniform errors for $P_t f$.\n\nFor each mesh $\\eta>0$ choose an effective finite $\\eta/2$-net with centers $x_i$. The normalized continuous functions $\\varphi_i(x)=(\\eta-d(x,x_i))_+/\\sum_j(\\eta-d(x,x_j))_+$ form a partition of unity supported within $\\eta$ of their centers. They are effective $C(E)$ tests since the denominator is at least $\\eta/2$. The row $\\sum_i (P_t\\varphi_i)(x)\\delta_{x_i}$ has Wasserstein distance at most $\\eta$ from $P_t(x,\\cdot)$ by sending each output point to its centers with those conditional weights. Uniform evaluation bounds and dyadic simplex approximation give effective finite weak names of the rows. For the spatial modulus, approximate both rows at the same centers. Their bounded Wasserstein distance is at most $2\\eta$ plus the diameter of the carrier times half the sum of absolute differences of their weights. The effective uniform moduli of the finitely many $P_t\\varphi_i$ bound those differences. Choosing $\\eta$ and the moduli at any target tolerance gives the required spatial modulus.\n\nOn the noncompact conservative branch add the constant function to the core for $C_0(E)$ and extend the generator by $A1=0$ at the compactification point. By the decomposition in Theorem O.7.2.3ae this is a graph core for the compactified generator. Apply the same row construction there and the compact-range coherent-output comparison of that theorem on the original carrier. The proof uses the actual generator core to compute the transition tables and passes those tables to the physical source construction. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o723ai\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o723ai-failure-of-universal-realization-by-uniform-finite-sources\"></a>**Corollary O.7.2.3ai (Failure of Universal Realization by Uniform Finite Sources).** The universal generator realization claim for the effective finite Hermitian-control source towers of Theorem O.7.2.3m is false. This already holds in the rich-Feller jump-and-killing branch, for a law outside the effective transition-row classes of Theorems O.7.2.3ac and O.7.2.3ae. The counterexample has a specified smooth graph core and a unique closed conservative extension. It fails the original classical path test, so it also cannot pass the joint path and coherent-record test for the same law.\n\n*Proof.* The finite source construction of Theorem O.7.2.3m has a uniform stage and inventory algorithm. On any fixed finite input and fixed retained finite-output probe, the finite-stage expectation can be computed to rational tolerance. Its construction reads the emitted finite source table, decodes its finitely many branches, and uses the finite Hermitian pulse blocks and complete instrument maps of that stage. Approximate the finite matrix exponentials and products, sum the traces over all branches, and use the finite operator bounds to allocate the requested tolerance. Each clock and stock is already part of the finite stage. In particular a fixed marked binary output at a rational checkpoint gives such a probe directly, without requiring a computation in an abstract continuum path representation. If its expectation converges, compute the expectation at stage $n$ to error $2^{-n}$. The resulting rational sequence witnesses limit computability, with no assumed modulus for the tower's convergence. This is the finite-source application of Proposition O.7.2.3ag.\n\nFix a real $\\theta\\in[1,2]$ that is not the limit of any computable sequence of rationals. Such a real exists since the set of programs is countable. On $E=\\mathbb R\\sqcup\\{\\partial\\}$ with isolated absorbing state $\\partial$, start at zero, jump by $+1$ at rate one and jump to $\\partial$ at rate $\\theta$. The state can be constructed with a rate-one Poisson clock and an independent exponential killing time. On the Banach algebra of bounded continuous functions with $\\lim_{|x|\\to\\infty}f(x)=f(\\partial)$ its generator is\n$$\n(G_\\theta f)(x)=f(x+1)-f(x)+\\theta(f(\\partial)-f(x)),\\qquad\n(G_\\theta f)(\\partial)=0\n\\tag{O.7.2.3ai.1}\n$$\nOn the noncompact-line $C_0(\\mathbb R)$ realization before adding the cemetery, the killed semigroup and its generator are\n$$\n(T_t f)(x)=e^{-\\theta t}\\sum_{j=0}^{\\infty}e^{-t}\\frac{t^j}{j!}f(x+j),\n\\qquad (L_\\theta f)(x)=f(x+1)-(1+\\theta)f(x).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3ai.2}\n$$\nThe series defines a strongly continuous positive contraction semigroup. Its bounded generator has full domain, and $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R)$ is a graph core by uniform density. The conservative extension acts on $\\mathbb C1+C_0(\\mathbb R)$ by fixing the constants and using (O.7.2.3ai.2) on the second summand. This is the Feller algebra of the one-point compactification when $\\partial$ is the added point, with graph core $\\mathbb C1+C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R)$. The killed transition law and its absorbing extension are unique by bounded generator uniqueness. Use the isolated cemetery for the path record in the test below.\n\nThe paid output protocol reads whether the path is in the absorbing state at physical time one. Its probability is $1-e^{-\\theta}$. This is a bounded continuous state test in the disjoint-union topology, and its path evaluation at time one is almost surely continuous in $J_1$, since a jump occurs at that exact deterministic time with probability zero. Thus the original path test on the declared isolated-cemetery record forces the finite source's time-one flag probabilities to converge to $1-e^{-\\theta}$. The same conclusion follows from the joint test with the identity quantum effect. The inverse $p\\mapsto-\\log(1-p)$ is computable on the compact interval $[1-e^{-1},1-e^{-2}]$, after clipping rational approximants to that interval. A uniform effective finite tower would therefore make $\\theta$ limit-computable, a contradiction.\n\nThe law also lies outside the effective transition-row branches. On the compactified Feller carrier, use a computable continuous compactly supported function $f$ equal to one on $[0,1]$ and taking values in $[0,1]$, with $f(\\partial)=0$. Its time-one expectation from zero is $e^{-\\theta}a$, where $a=\\mathbb E f(N_1)>0$ for a unit-rate Poisson variable. The constant $a$ is computable and positive by its Poisson sum. An effective row name at that fixed input, or the effective row modulus with approximations to it, would therefore compute $\\theta$. This argument works both when the cemetery is identified with the point at infinity and when it is separately marked. The counterexample belongs to the remaining rich-Feller jump-and-killing branch. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723aj\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723aj-finite-sources-for-canonical-dyadic-stinespring-histories\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3aj (Finite Sources for Canonical Dyadic Stinespring Histories).** On the trace-class branch of Theorems O.7.2.3x and O.7.2.3ad, take $H$ with its computable orthonormal basis and assume the effective resolvent and finite-compression data of O.7.2.3ad. Fix a finite dyadic horizon in the calibrated time units and use the Stinespring history of the reduced semigroup on dyadic partitions, with refinement given by composition of consecutive dilations. There is a finite Hermitian-control tower of Theorem O.7.2.3m that retains the entire coherent environment of this history at each refinement. On each fixed finite input corner the outputs converge in trace norm, including the system and the whole embedded history. The conclusion holds with arbitrary fixed spectators and for any fixed finite list of dyadic intervention checkpoints and declared effective instruments. The corresponding tower includes those instruments and their finite record in its grammar. The result selects this history and its refinement maps as part of the comparison.\n\n*The common history.* Let $T_t$ be the conservative trace-class semigroup of the actual closed generator. For each dyadic tick length $h$, fix a Stinespring isometry $V_h:H\\to H\\otimes F_h$. Take $F_h=H\\otimes H'$ with an extra countably infinite orthogonal summand for unused environment flags, where $H'$ is a copy of $H$. Denote by $F_h^0$ the closed span of the environment coefficient vectors of $V_h$ obtained by taking input and system output matrix entries. The Stinespring representation on $H\\otimes F_h^0$ is minimal. Its iterated two-tick dilation $(V_{h/2}\\otimes I)V_{h/2}$, with the environment factors put in chronological order, realizes $T_h$. The minimality and uniqueness property therefore give an isometry $u_h^0:F_h^0\\to F_{h/2}\\otimes F_{h/2}$ satisfying\n$$\n(I_H\\otimes u_h^0)V_h=(V_{h/2}\\otimes I)V_{h/2}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3aj.1}\n$$\nFor example the isometry can be defined on the spanning coefficient vectors, since their Gram matrices are entries of the same channel. The right side uses no unused flags. Its orthogonal complement therefore contains an infinite-dimensional separable space, so $u_h^0$ extends to an isometry $u_h$ on all of $F_h$. The extension is a mathematical comparison on the unused environment subspace and acts trivially on every already retained system or reference factor. The exact coherent history is independent, up to its natural isometry, of that extension on unused flags. Tensoring $u_h$ over all ticks gives an isometric refinement of complete histories. Products of successive dyadic refinements define all comparisons and are coherent by construction. The Hilbert inductive limit is separable. In it the exact iterated isometries at every finer dyadic partition are the same map for each fixed dyadic horizon. The same is true if fixed intervention instruments are inserted at common dyadic checkpoints, since the refinement maps act on environment factors alone. This is the dyadic part of the Stinespring inclusion-system construction (Bhat and Mukherjee 2010).\n\n*Effective finite columns.* The effective resolvent data compute $T_h$ at each positive dyadic $h$ on the dense test list, in trace norm. One way to obtain an effective error on generator-domain test vectors is to take $x=C_1y$, where $C_1=(I-L)^{-1}$ and $y$ ranges over the computable dense rational tests of O.7.2.3ad. These vectors have dense linear span and computable $Lx=x-y$. For $x\\in\\operatorname{Dom}L$,\n$$\n\\left\\|e^{t j(C_j-I)}x-T_t x\\right\\|_1\n\\le \\sqrt{2t/j}\\,\\|Lx\\|_1,\\qquad C_j=j(j-L)^{-1}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3aj.2}\n$$\nIndeed $C_j$ is the exponential-time average of $T_s$. The bounded semigroup on the left is the average of $T_{S_t}$, where $S_t$ is a sum of an independent Poisson number, of mean $jt$, of independent exponential times of rate $j$. Its mean is $t$ and its variance is $2t/j$. The trajectory of $x$ is Lipschitz with constant $\\|Lx\\|_1$, which gives the estimate. The Poisson series is effectively summable on a fixed time interval. Searching the dense image of $C_1$ in trace norm and using contraction now computes $T_h$ on each named input. The same argument with finite matrix entries includes finite reference blocks.\n\nChoose a faithful diagonal density $\\tau=\\sum_i w_i|i\\rangle\\langle i|$ and normalized vector $\\xi=\\sum_i\\sqrt{w_i}|i,i\\rangle$ on $H\\otimes H'$, with $w_i=2^{-i}$ for infinite $H$ and its normalized finite restriction for finite $H$. The density $\\Gamma_h=(T_h\\otimes\\operatorname{id})(|\\xi\\rangle\\langle\\xi|)$ has an effective trace-norm name by truncating $\\xi$ and applying $T_h$ entrywise. The positive square-root purification $\\operatorname{vec}\\sqrt{\\Gamma_h}$ defines $V_h$ by its $i$th input column, obtained by taking the $i$th component on the first reference factor and dividing by $\\sqrt{w_i}$. Trace preservation gives the marginal $\\tau$ on that factor, so the columns are orthonormal. Their cross products, after tracing out the purifying factor, give exactly $T_h(|i\\rangle\\langle j|)$ by the Choi matrix entries. This verifies that the construction uses the stated channel on every input. Finite compressions compute the purification in Hilbert norm: for positive trace-class $D,E$, the Powers–Størmer inequality $\\|\\sqrt D-\\sqrt E\\|_2^2\\le\\|D-E\\|_1$ bounds the cutoff error. Hence all finite sets of columns have effective Hilbert norm names and finite output/environment tail estimates. An orthogonal unused flag is available independently of the column ranks. See Powers and Størmer (1970).\n\n*Finite stage and its clock.* At a dyadic mesh with finitely many ticks, start with a prescribed finite input corner and successively choose larger finite system projections $P_0,\\ldots,P_M$ and finite used-environment projections $Q_1,\\ldots,Q_M$ so that\n$$\n\\left\\|V_hP_{k-1}-(P_k\\otimes Q_k)V_hP_{k-1}\\right\\|\\le \\epsilon_k .\n\\tag{O.7.2.3aj.3}\n$$\nThe choices terminate by (O.7.2.3aj.2) and the effective finite-column tails. A sum of squared column-error bounds suffices for the squared operator norm. Put $A_k=(P_k\\otimes Q_k)V_h|_{P_{k-1}H}$. Complete it to a finite isometry with an orthogonal failure flag and a copy of the input space as the failure payload, by using the column $(A_k,\\sqrt{I-A_k^*A_k})$. The defect square root has norm at most $\\epsilon_k$: the omitted output is orthogonal to $A_k$ and the original column is an isometry. Embed the failure payload in the common finite system space and use one unused flag of $F_h$. The flag makes this summand orthogonal to success even if the system coordinates are reused. The completed column differs from $V_h|_{P_{k-1}H}$ by at most $\\sqrt2\\,\\epsilon_k$, since the omitted original output uses no unused flag. Keep this failure sector and its flag as part of the source. Later ticks route failed branches through a recorded inert continuation. A fixed finite system space contains every stage subspace and all payloads. Any additional unused columns are completed isometrically using extra unused finite flags. These choices require only the positive square root of a known finite positive matrix and padding.\n\nThe finite source realizes these columns by the Hermitian involution of Theorem O.7.2.3m. At each tick an actual positive-duration pulse, record routing and clock update fit in the prescribed physical tick interval. Shrinking pulse slots and the corresponding increased norms are charged, along with the initialized system, fresh flags, table entries, address stock, retained environments and control memory. The input/output summand flag in the involution can be included as a fresh finite flag of that tick. The prescribed output summand is embedded as the intended system and environment. All orthogonal summands are embedded using additional finite unused flags. Dyadic approximations to the finite Hermitian coefficients preserve unitarity of the executed pulse and carry their complete norm error, including the resulting padding amplitudes. No environment factor is reset or traced out for the coherent comparison.\n\nOn the initial input corner compare each exact stage with the padded implementation by telescoping the product from its first tick onward. The ideal comparison at tick $k$ starts in the output of the preceding finite successful columns. The error on that support is bounded by (O.7.2.3aj.3) and the completed-column estimate. Equivalently, the successful product differs from the full exact product by at most $\\sum_k\\epsilon_k$, by telescoping through the contractions $A_k$ on the already truncated input spaces. The squared total failure norm is at most $\\sum_k\\epsilon_k^2$ by the per-step defect identity. Tensoring any reference does not change these bounds. Choose the summed cutoff and physical pulse errors at most $2^{-n}$ at stage $n$ and exhaust the input by the first $n$ basis vectors. The embeddings from the first part preserve the resulting operator norm difference on every fixed corner. Arbitrary fixed trace-class input then follows by approximation and trace-norm contractivity.\n\nAt checkpoints one may retain a fixed complete finite instrument with effective finite compressions. Apply the same finite-column argument to the Stinespring column of its trace-preserving block sum with the marked output and outcome registers retained. Its labels and private environment are separate factors from the semigroup histories. For an adaptive tree, insert the fixed conditioning on each measured outcome and pad to the finitely many branches, using the largest cutoff over its finitely many nodes. On a common refinement the inserted operations are unchanged and their separate registers are not acted on by the semigroup refinement isometries. Contractivity of the complete instrument into its direct sum of outcomes gives convergence for the whole classical outcome list and the conditional unnormalized outputs. A general spectator is obtained by approximation with finite input matrices. Isometry convergence on a fixed input corner implies trace-norm convergence of its normalized Choi state on the common system/history carrier, which verifies both the finite-corner and output-tail tests of Q. For the fixed classical outcome list the trace-norm convergence verifies the corresponding QJ tests.\n\nThe finite source descriptors, coefficients and cutoff searches are effective in the named input data. The maps $u_h$ and the Hilbert inductive limit are mathematical comparison maps. This proof makes no claim of an effective name for their arbitrary unused-flag extensions, an effective all-time product-system trivialization, or a realization of an independently specified unraveling. The construction gives the canonical coherent semigroup history on dyadic checkpoints, including its quantum correlations and unused finite source outputs. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723ak\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723ak-prescribed-dyadic-histories-on-finite-named-ports\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3ak (Prescribed Dyadic Histories on Finite Named Ports).** Fix a finite dyadic horizon and a nested exhaustion by finite dyadic intervention grids. At each grid use named finite-dimensional input, output and retained-history ports with computable bases and dimensions. Include each declared classical port as a register diagonal in its named basis. On these ports prescribe a causally normalized process at every grid. A process is represented by its deterministic quantum comb, with the named coherent and classical outputs included in its output ports. The prescriptions must agree under the fixed, effectively specified restrictions from finer grids to coarser grids. A restriction inserts identity interventions at omitted checkpoints and takes the declared marginals, or uses a given equivalent completely positive wiring. The restrictions compose exactly. The comparison tests every declared finite intervention grid and every fixed spectator through its resulting retained output. Use the uniform finite Hermitian-control supplier of Theorem O.7.2.3m with its paid clock and resource records.\n\nThere is a uniform source tower for this comparison if and only if the matrices of the prescribed finite processes are uniformly limit computable. Here the condition means that a single algorithm returns a rational matrix $\\widehat R_{k,n}$ for each pair of grid index $k$ and approximation index $n$, with $\\widehat R_{k,n}\\to R_k$ for each fixed $k$. The matrices $R_k$ are normalized to trace one. No rate or modulus of convergence is needed. The realized quantities include the finite joint classical and quantum outputs for arbitrary fixed interventions on these ports. The comparison keeps every named output, including its off-diagonal coefficients when it is a coherent port.\n\n*Necessity.* At a fixed grid, finite computable process tomography reconstructs the process from a fixed finite list of probes. For example, an external tester stores each outgoing port, inserts one half of a maximally entangled pair into each incoming port, and keeps the other half and the full declared output for final state tomography. This returns the normalized comb with the fixed ordering of factors. Each finite matrix entry follows by testing the real and imaginary matrix units through Hermitian effects. For declared classical registers use their diagonal outcomes. The registers and probes have fixed names and their connection to the finite source is computable. Given a uniform source tower, compute its matrix at stage $n$ to error $2^{-n}$ using the emitted finite table, finite pulses and complete instruments. The tower's convergence for the fixed tomography probes implies convergence of these rational matrices at each fixed grid. This gives the joint algorithm in $(k,n)$.\n\n*Sufficiency.* For a grid $n$ let $\\mathscr P_n$ be its compact set of causal trace-one Choi matrices, with the declared classical ports diagonal. The defining constraints are positivity, linear partial-trace normalization for each earlier slot and the specified diagonal constraints. The finite sequential memory-channel realization of these conditions is the quantum-comb theorem (Chiribella, D'Ariano and Perinotti 2009). For each $k\\le n$ let $\\pi_{kn}$ be the declared wiring restriction, acting linearly on the full Choi matrix with the corresponding fixed normalization. Include any computable coefficients of this wiring as named data. For candidate $C\\in\\mathscr P_n$ minimize, to error at most $2^{-n}$, the loss\n$$\n\\Lambda_n(C)=\\sum_{k=1}^n 2^{-k}\n\\min\\bigl\\{1,\\|\\pi_{kn}C-\\widehat R_{k,n}\\|_{\\rm HS}^2\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3ak.1}\n$$\nThis finite choice is effective. The restrictions are explicit finite matrices, so rational coefficient approximants can be taken to give a uniform error of at most $2^{-n-3}$ in the loss over the compact trace-one matrix set. Each distance truncation is continuous and the scalar map $z\\mapsto\\min(1,z^2)$ is $2$-Lipschitz for $z\\ge0$. The resulting objective and the set of causal matrices have real polynomial descriptions with rational coefficients (Basu, Pollack and Roy 2006). Real-algebraic feasibility, bisection and sampling produce an algebraic near-minimizer $C_n$. Exact equality of any computable coefficient need not be decided.\n\nThe actual $R_n$ is a comparison in the finite problem. Consistency gives $\\pi_{kn}R_n=R_k$ for all $k\\le n$. For each fixed $k$ the corresponding loss at $R_n$ tends to zero. The sum of terms with $k>K$ is at most $2^{-K}$, independent of $n$. It follows that $\\Lambda_n(R_n)\\to0$, and therefore $\\Lambda_n(C_n)\\to0$. For every fixed $k$,\n$$\n\\|\\pi_{kn}C_n-R_k\\|_{\\rm HS}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3ak.2}\n$$\nIndeed its truncated distance to $\\widehat R_{k,n}$ is at most $2^k\\Lambda_n(C_n)$ and eventually below one. The chosen loss controls all fixed grids from a single uniform tower despite the absence of rates.\n\nRealize each $C_n$ by finite memory channels in chronological order with its declared ports. A finite algebraic realization can also be found by enumerating memory and environment dimensions and solving the real polynomial equations for finite isometry entries and their specified Choi output $C_n$. Existence of a finite memory-channel realization ensures this search terminates. Classical port outputs are produced with their diagonal instrument and their readout register retained. A private dilation environment is distinct from the named process outputs and is allocated and recorded. For each channel use the isometric-column Hermitian involution of Theorem O.7.2.3m on a fresh finite output register, and choose positive pulse durations inside the physical grid intervals. For an isometric column with implementation interval $\\tau$, its exact pulse norm is at most $\\pi\\hbar/\\tau$. Use the finite dyadic approximation rule of that theorem and allocate total accumulated operator-norm error at most $2^{-n}$. Count every register, pulse, classical readout, clock service and supplier stock as part of the finite stage. Scheduled interventions are the named tester ports. Their fixed operations and any readouts use their own declared durations, registers and stock. Use a separately owned binary routing label for the adaptive host frame map of Theorem O.7.2.3m. Record the host forecast and the physical label, and undo the frame when routing the named port. The calibration and routing records are counted. Comparison to the ideal sequential circuit follows by telescoping the actual finite unitary errors, uniformly in each fixed spectator. All final named joint outputs on a fixed grid converge in trace norm: (O.7.2.3ak.2) is convergence in a fixed finite Choi matrix space, and finite wiring is continuous in that norm. The same argument applies to a fixed adaptive intervention tree through its complete instrument with all outcomes kept. These trace-norm statements verify the finite-corner and output-tail tests on these named finite carriers.\n\n*An effective reduced law with two different history outcomes.* Use the identity reduced semigroup on a qubit. At dyadic time one emit an independent retained qubit $(|0\\rangle+e^{i\\theta}|1\\rangle)/\\sqrt2$. Every grid containing the checkpoint sees the same named output, and all other reductions and system interventions are those of the identity semigroup. The computational-basis probabilities of the history qubit are always $1/2$. Its Pauli $Y$ expectation is $\\sin\\theta$. For $\\theta\\in[0,1]$, the inverse of this sine restriction is computable and uniformly continuous. A non-limit-computable $\\theta$ therefore excludes realization by a uniform tower even though the reduced generator is the computable zero operator and has a unique conservative extension. In contrast, any limit-computable $\\theta$ is realized by this theorem. This includes noncomputable choices, for example $2\\sum_{j\\in K}4^{-(j+1)}$ for an infinite computably enumerable undecidable set $K$. Its computable finite enumerations converge. Its separated base-four digits recover $K$ from a computable value, so it is noncomputable. Thus computability of the reduced dynamics alone does not decide feasibility of an independently specified coherent output, and exact finite-grid history names are stronger than the condition needed here. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723al\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723al-canonical-dyadic-histories-for-countable-classical-quantum-carriers\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3al (Canonical Dyadic Histories for Countable Classical–Quantum Carriers).** Let $S$ be a finite or countably infinite effectively enumerated discrete label set. Let $H$ have a computable orthonormal basis and let $T_t$ be the conservative semigroup of Theorems O.7.2.3x and O.7.2.3ad on $X=\\ell^1(S;\\mathcal T(H))$. Supply the effective integer resolvents of O.7.2.3ad in the trace norm, on the dense rational tests given by finite label lists and finite matrices. On each finite dyadic horizon the prescribed input and final labels are classical. The intermediate transition environments of each grid interval are retained coherently in their fixed endpoint blocks. A finite Hermitian-control source tower realizes the resulting dyadic histories in the common carrier described below. For each fixed finite dyadic intervention protocol, the joint classical and coherent outputs converge in the sum of trace norms, with arbitrary fixed spectators and with the stated finite-corner and tail tests. The admitted interventions are completely positive classical–quantum instruments with trace-preserving sum and effective compressions on the same dense tests. The construction applies to the full conservative effective resolvent class on this carrier.\n\n*The endpoint blocks and their refinement.* Write $T_{ab,t}:\\mathcal T(H)\\to\\mathcal T(H)$ for the block from input label $b$ to output label $a$. Each block is completely positive and trace nonincreasing, $\\sum_a\\operatorname{Tr}T_{ab,t}(\\rho)=\\operatorname{Tr}\\rho$ for $\\rho\\ge0$, and the block semigroup law is\n$$\nT_{ab,t+u}=\\sum_{c\\in S}T_{ac,t}T_{cb,u}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3al.1}\n$$\nThe sum converges in trace norm on each input by positivity, preservation of the total trace, and linear decomposition of trace-class operators. For each $a,b$ and positive dyadic $h$, choose a Stinespring column $V_{ab,h}:H\\to H\\otimes F_{ab,h}$ for $T_{ab,h}$, with $F_{ab,h}$ separable and with an extra countably infinite unused orthogonal summand. Then\n$$\n\\sum_aV_{ab,h}^*V_{ab,h}=I_H\n\\tag{O.7.2.3al.2}\n$$\nin the strong operator topology, so the column that places its components in distinct output labels is an isometry. In a two-tick refinement at fixed endpoints $b,a$, compose the columns through every intermediate $c$. The outputs are in the orthogonal direct sum $\\bigoplus_c H\\otimes F_{ac,h/2}\\otimes F_{cb,h/2}$. The resulting operator is bounded by (O.7.2.3al.2), and tracing its history gives precisely $T_{ab,h}$ by (O.7.2.3al.1).\n\nThe coefficient vectors of the one-tick column generate its minimal environment subspace. They have the same Gram matrix as the corresponding vectors of the two-tick column, because the blocks realize the same map. The equality therefore gives an isometric refinement on the minimal subspace. It extends to the entire $F_{ab,h}$ by mapping its unused summand into the orthogonal complement of the two-tick coefficient vectors, which contains an infinite-dimensional unused summand. Tensor these maps on each fixed sequence of existing endpoint labels and sum them orthogonally. Coarse label sequences have orthogonal images because their old labels remain identified at the corresponding locations of the fine sequence. Successive dyadic maps give a coherent system of isometries and a separable common history space. The induced map is the identity on the system and on labels and quantum registers of any intervention at an old checkpoint. This comparison keeps the direct sum over intermediate labels as a quantum environment and reads a label as classical only where a checkpoint requires it. It agrees with the block semigroup law upon reducing to the classical final label and final system.\n\n*Computable columns and cutoffs.* The resolvent argument of (O.7.2.3aj.2) uses only the predual norm and contraction. Thus it computes $T_h$ on the named tests of $X$. In particular it computes every $T_{ab,h}$, and for fixed $b$ the sum over $a$ of the trace-norm errors is controlled by the input test accuracy. Purify the block Choi matrices against the same faithful input density as in Theorem O.7.2.3aj. Their weights need not be normalized separately. The square-root purification produces the columns $V_{ab,h}$ because it reproduces all block matrix elements, and the equality (O.7.2.3al.2) follows from the total Choi marginal. The square-root inequality from Theorem O.7.2.3aj bounds the sum of squared column errors by the sum of trace-norm errors, with the explicit fixed inverse weights for the finitely many input coordinates. This gives effective finite tails for each finite list of input columns, jointly over output labels, quantum coordinates and environment coordinates. One may also choose the label cutoff first by enumerating the trace of each positive output block until the missing trace from (O.7.2.3al.2) is below tolerance. None of these steps requires deciding a rank or a zero block.\n\nAt a given stage, start from a finite set of input labels and quantum coordinates. Choose nested cutoffs in the time sequence, with the total norm error of the omitted output and environment columns at most $2^{-n}$. The choices terminate by the column-tail bounds and the finite number of ticks and installed interventions. Complete each finite column to an isometry by adding the defect column $(I-A^*A)^{1/2}$ in an unused history flag and retaining its failure payload, as in Theorem O.7.2.3aj. It can be assigned a recorded inert continuation. Use distinct blocks for the declared classical initial labels. Exact flags separating histories are represented by finite orthogonal register coordinates. Interventions with a classical checkpoint use a copy of the label in its stipulated basis and the supplied block instrument. A record conditioned on that label is included in the output. Fresh finite registers carry all pulse and rounding flags. The supplied finite Hermitian involution realizes each column within a positive-duration subslot of the dyadic tick. At implementation duration $\\tau$ its pulse norm is bounded by $\\pi\\hbar/\\tau$. Meter each addressed Hermitian matrix, clock update, initialized label register, fresh environment, routed record and pulse tolerance. The finite totals may grow as the mesh decreases. The adaptive host frame of Theorem O.7.2.3m may be used on a paid binary route label whose physical copy is retained and whose inverse decoder routes the named ports.\n\nOn each fixed finite input corner and fixed installed protocol, telescope the column error through the exact and finite compositions. Between two interventions the refinements identify the exact maps with a single endpoint-block history. At a checkpoint the exact fixed intervention and its finite compression obey the same column-tail estimate. These estimates bound the operator-norm difference on the finite input corner by the sum of the local truncation, completion and implementation errors. Reading the stipulated classical registers contracts trace norm. Thus the corresponding finite-corner Choi densities on the common history carrier converge in the sum of trace norms over the classical outcome labels. Fixed spectators are included by tensoring the column estimates, followed by finite-rank approximation on any fixed trace-class input. The convergence gives vanishing finite-rank tails on the coherent output and vanishing tails outside finite sets of recorded labels, since both hold for the limiting trace-class densities. This verifies Q and QJ on the declared dyadic intervention records. Tracing the history at fixed times gives the stated transition blocks. A path-space comparison of readouts at all intermediate times has its own tightness requirement. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723am\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723am-prescribed-dyadic-histories-on-separable-quantum-and-countable-classical-quantum-ports\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3am (Prescribed Dyadic Histories on Separable Quantum and Countable Classical–Quantum Ports).** Fix a dyadic horizon and a nested effective exhaustion by finite dyadic intervention grids. At each grid use named separable quantum ports or countable classical–quantum ports with effective coordinate bases, including all declared quantum histories and classical readouts. A countable classical–quantum port is a trace-class direct sum over its named classical labels. Prescribe a normal causally normalized process at every grid. These processes agree under the declared composition of restrictions from finer to coarser grids. The restrictions are implemented by fixed normal causal wiring of the named ports, such as identity interventions at omitted checkpoints, and its values on finite circuits and finite tests are effectively evaluable. Any countable retained output is understood in its fixed separable carrier. The comparison takes trace-norm convergence on each fixed finite input corner and every fixed finite intervention protocol, including its recorded outcomes and fixed spectators.\n\nThere exists a uniform finite Hermitian-control source tower for this comparison if and only if the matrix coefficients of the prescribed processes at the named grids are uniformly limit computable. Equivalently, one algorithm gives convergent rational sequences for all the coefficients of the faithful weighted Choi states defined below. There is no requested convergence rate or effective output-tail bound. The tower retains all prescribed named outputs. The constructed clocks, private stocks and private resource records remain owned and counted as in Theorem O.7.2.3m. This result does not prescribe a path topology beyond the named grids.\n\n*A complete countable test.* Use, on each incoming quantum port, a faithful diagonal probability vector with explicit positive rational weights summing to one, and feed the corresponding normalized two-copy vector $\\sum_j\\sqrt{w_j}|j,j\\rangle$, keeping its reference copy. On a countable classical input use its faithful classical mixture and a copy of its label, with the quantum tester on the indicated fiber. Equivalently, use the weighted quantum tester after dephasing the classical labels on both sides. The resulting process Choi state $J_k$ is a positive trace-one state on the tensor product of the output ports and reference copies of grid $k$. The weighted construction is the normal finite-grid multipartite channel of the process evaluated on a trace-class input. It requires neither an infinite maximally entangled vector nor a finite-trace assumption on an unnormalized Choi operator. Every matrix entry of $J_k$ is a known weight times an entry of the channel on a finite matrix unit. Thus each entry can also be obtained from finitely many finite-coordinate preparations and final Hermitian tests, without preparing the faithful infinite tester as a physical resource. The weight factors are effective and nonzero on their named indices.\n\nEnumerate the real and imaginary parts of these entries as real tests $t_i$, in an effective order with known grid index $k(i)$. Each test is bounded in absolute value by one. For any fixed grid, entrywise convergence of its positive trace-one process states to the prescribed trace-one $J_k$ gives trace-norm convergence. Indeed a finite projection carrying all but $\\epsilon$ of the limit trace also carries all but $2\\epsilon$ of the approximating trace eventually, by entrywise convergence. The bound (Q1) controls the removed corner. This proves the output-tail claim without taking a trace of a merely entrywise limit.\n\nWe give the operational implication of this convergence on a fixed grid. Let $\\mathcal C_n$ and $\\mathcal C$ be the associated normal multipartite channels, with all incoming ports independent in the Choi test. Positivity and normalization identify these channels, since every finite matrix-unit image is obtained from the nonzero weighted entries. Trace-norm convergence of the weighted Choi states implies diamond convergence on every fixed finite incoming corner, by inverting the finitely many weight factors and applying (Q2). Hence the multipartite channels converge on each fixed trace-class input, including an arbitrary fixed spectator, by density and trace-norm contractivity.\n\nFor completeness the same convergence controls serial interventions in the process. For each finite-coordinate cutoff on every intervention leg use the channel\n$$\nE_P(\\rho)=P\\rho P+\\operatorname{Tr}[(I-P)\\rho]\\,\\omega_P,\n\\tag{O.7.2.3am.1}\n$$\nwhere $\\omega_P$ is a fixed ready state in its finite range. For a classical label cut the missing labels are assigned to a named included label by this same trace rule. These channels approach the identity strongly in trace norm, also on a fixed spectator. With all incoming and outgoing intervention legs compressed, the connected tester has fixed finite ports, so every test output is a bounded linear contraction or linear combination of contractions of the finite input-corner process matrices. In particular its outputs converge in trace norm, with the retained output kept.\n\nTo remove the intervention cutoffs, induct on the number of connected operations. The partially connected process state keeps all as yet unconnected inputs fed by their faithful test pairs and retains their reference copies. It also keeps the fixed tester memory. At the next intervention, the marginal on the system offered to the tester and its memory is independent of the later faithful test inputs, by causality. The induction hypothesis makes this marginal converge in trace norm. The fixed tester operation applied to that marginal gives a convergent sequence of returned-port states. Compressions (O.7.2.3am.1) before and after that tester operation therefore change the next connected process by an arbitrarily small trace-norm error uniformly at sufficiently late stages. This follows from strong convergence on the two limiting marginals and from trace-norm contractivity of the subsequent complete causal process. The cutoff tester has finite system, memory and returned-port coordinates. Connecting it to the partially connected process state is a bounded linear operation on trace-class operators: contract over the finitely many matrix units of the tested system and the finitely many weighted matrix units of the returned-port reference copy, with fixed finite inverse weights. Thus its connected output converges in trace norm. Removing the cutoffs proves the induction step. The induction begins with the unconnected Choi state tensored with the fixed initial tester state. For an instrument use its trace-preserving sum with outcome register retained. Any fixed spectator can be carried in the memory or recovered by trace-norm approximation of its input. This proves joint Q and QJ for the finite grid. On the countable classical outcomes the result includes the total variation of their marginal law.\n\n*Density of finite circuits.* For each grid $k$, finite circuits on finite coordinate cutoffs of every port are dense for any finite list of tests on its grid and coarser grids. To see this, truncate the actual grid process by finite input embeddings and finite output channels (O.7.2.3am.1). The resulting finite process is causal, since the causal identities hold after insertion of local trace-preserving channels. Its finite Choi matrices have a finite memory-channel realization by the finite comb theorem. The faithful Choi matrices of the encoded processes, regarded in the original carriers by using (O.7.2.3am.1) on input as well, converge in trace norm. This follows directly from strong convergence of the finite number of cutoff channels on the faithful tester and on the true output. The preceding operational argument now proves density also on every fixed coarser restriction, by applying the stipulated wiring as a fixed normal tester. Approximate each resulting finite causal circuit by one with algebraic isometry coefficients. Such circuits can be effectively enumerated, and they are dense. For example, approximate each finite channel by its finite Stinespring column, then approximate the column within the compact space of orthonormal columns by algebraic points, using positive square roots to normalize full-rank rational approximants. Classical block routing is kept exact and the finitely many quantum columns are approximated within their declared blocks. Errors add over the finite circuit by telescoping.\n\n*Uniform selection without a rate.* Enumerate all algebraically coded finite causal circuits, including their actual grid index $k(C)$ and finite input/output coordinate allocations. For each stage $n$ consider the first $n$ codes, together with one default finite ready circuit. Let $a_{i,n}$ be the supplied rational estimate of prescribed test $i$. For a circuit $C$ use the loss\n$$\n\\Lambda_n(C)=2^{-k(C)}+\n\\sum_{\\substack{1\\le i\\le n\\\\k(i)\\le k(C)}}2^{-i}\n\\min\\{1,|t_i(C)-a_{i,n}|\\}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3am.2}\n$$\nHere $t_i(C)$ means the actual test of the circuit after the declared restriction to grid $k(i)$. Each score is computable to any requested accuracy, and the finite comparison can pick a circuit within $2^{-n}$ of the minimum. In particular no compatibility, optimum or exact comparison of arbitrary real coefficients needs to be decided.\n\nThe resulting losses tend to zero. Given $\\epsilon>0$, choose $m$ with $2^{-m}<\\epsilon$, and a grid depth $K$ containing the first $m$ tests and satisfying $2^{-K}<\\epsilon$. Density gives one fixed coded circuit at depth $K$ whose first $m$ tests agree with the prescribed values to within $\\epsilon$. Its code is present at every sufficiently late stage. For it the first $m$ terms of the weighted loss are eventually at most $2\\epsilon$ by convergence of the estimates on that finite list, and all remaining terms have sum at most $2^{-m}$. This proves the claim. The term $2^{-k(C)}$ forces the selected grid depth to tend to infinity. Each fixed test is therefore eventually scored, and its difference from $a_{i,n}$ tends to zero. It follows that every prescribed process Choi entry converges. Both the depths and the finite coordinate allocations are chosen by the finite search. This avoids imposing a convergence modulus across growing port dimensions.\n\n*Physical source and converse.* Implement the chosen finite causal circuit by the finite Hermitian involutions of Theorem O.7.2.3m. Schedule positive pulse and routing durations in the allocated physical intervals, and use a summable total operator-norm tolerance bounded by $2^{-n}$ at stage $n$. Keep the named histories, outcome registers and all implementation ownership records. Include the adaptive host's counted frame permutation on a paid routing label. Finite stage matrices and records are computable. The total error of the implemented circuit relative to the chosen circuit is bounded in complete trace norm by twice the accumulated operator-norm tolerance, by dilation and telescoping. Thus its limits and tail tests are the ones proved above.\n\nConversely, from a uniform tower compute each fixed finite probe value to tolerance $2^{-n}$ at stage $n$ whenever the stage grid contains the probe. If a stage is too shallow, emit a fixed default for that probe. The comparison requires eventual presence of each fixed grid. The declared computable wirings and effective embeddings make the finite probe values computable as actual finite instrument records. Their convergence yields a single computable array of rational approximants to the prescribed weighted Choi coefficients. This proves necessity. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723an\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723an-full-path-and-coherent-histories-from-effective-feller-grid-laws\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3an (Full Path and Coherent Histories from Effective Feller Grid Laws).** Let $E$ be a computably presented Polish metric space with its countable dense set of named states and a bounded compatible metric. Fix a conservative càdlàg process $X$ with a specified initial law. At each fixed positive dyadic time require almost surely no jump at that time. Suppose its finite-dimensional distributions at dyadic grids have uniform effective weak names. The names may equivalently be supplied as arbitrary-accuracy finite dyadic approximations in bounded Wasserstein distance on each finite grid with the maximum coordinate metric.\n\nOn the same actual source path use continuous finite-dimensional Hermitian coefficients $A(x)$ and a fixed finite list of continuous matrices $L_e(x)$, with computable evaluations at the named states. The coherent history uses the normalized time-ordered no-jump and jump amplitudes of Theorem O.7.2.3ab on these coefficients. Then there is a uniform finite source in the grammar of Theorem O.7.2.3m. Its decoded state paths converge in $J_1$ on every fixed dyadic horizon, and its joint state/coherent outputs pass the Polish-record tests of Theorem O.7.2.3z and Q/QJ. For continuous paths in a Euclidean state space, polygonal state readouts also pass the continuous-path tests. The convergence includes each fixed finite quantum intervention protocol on the stated driver and arbitrary fixed spectators. The finite apparatus has a paid positive-duration clock and retains its source table, all routing and history records, and its separate resource inventory.\n\nIn particular the hypothesis on grid laws follows for a conservative locally compact rich-Feller process with an effective $C_0(E)$ graph core and initial law as specified below. This applies to its prescribed closed jump, killing and diffusion generators with the given conservative extension or absorbing cemetery law. The source requires neither an effective compactification nor uniform spatial moduli for the individual transition rows. The comparison is with the stipulated initial and controlled quantum-intervention law. Classical interventions that change that state law require the corresponding controlled grid laws.\n\n*The actual grid laws.* For a fixed grid, finite supported measures at named points with dyadic weights are dense in bounded Wasserstein distance. The standard effective weak representation on a computable Polish space is the one induced by such finite approximants. On a product grid use the maximum coordinate metric. It is a computable bounded compatible metric, and its named points are exactly finite words of named states. Choose at stage $n$ the mesh $h_n=2^{-n}$ on $[0,n]$ and a finite dyadic table $\\nu_n$ within $2^{-2n}$ in this metric of the actual grid law. The table can be drawn by one finite fair-bit seed and a reversible cumulative lookup. The equivalent conditional description uses the actual prefix marginals of this same table, with arbitrary normalized rows on zero-weight prefixes. Retain the seed and finite table as paid private records, and release each path coordinate at its grid time. No input from the future is passed to the released or quantum-intervention registers. This uses the actual initial and dynamical law to determine the finite table.\n\n*Path topology and tightness.* Couple the table to the true grid vector with expected maximum coordinate error at most $2^{1-2n}$. Thus its probability of maximum coordinate error above $2^{-n}$ is at most $2^{1-n}$. For any fixed dyadic $T$, eventually $T$ is a grid endpoint and the same bound holds on the restricted grid. The step interpolation of an individual càdlàg path's exact samples converges to that path in $J_1$ if it has no jump at $T$. Here is the path argument. For any positive error tolerance, a regulated càdlàg path admits a right-continuous step approximation with finitely many distinct jump times and uniformly small error. At $T$ it may be chosen with no jump when the path is continuous there. For all sufficiently fine grids, move each of its finitely many interior jump times to the first grid time at or after it. Those times preserve their order and differ by at most the grid mesh. An increasing piecewise-linear time change sends the original jump times to these grid times. The step sampling of the path is within twice the uniform approximation tolerance of the time-shifted original path. Sending the tolerance and mesh to zero proves convergence. The coupled table step paths therefore converge in probability in $J_1$ to $X$ on each fixed dyadic horizon. It follows that their laws are tight in the fixed Polish $J_1$ space and converge weakly to the actual state law. In the continuous Euclidean case, maximum grid error and the uniform modulus of continuity of each limiting sample path give the same conclusion in the uniform norm for its polygonal readout. The coherent stage still uses the actual step labels.\n\n*Joint coherent histories.* A càdlàg path's range on a finite interval has compact closure. For a converging $J_1$ sequence on that interval, time changes place all the sequence's values at distances tending uniformly to zero from limiting path values. For finitely many continuous matrix functions this gives a common finite norm bound along the sequence and convergence of coefficient values at every continuity time of the limiting path. To see the bound, use continuity and a finite subcover of neighborhoods of its compact range. This requires no assumption that a fixed neighborhood of that range is compact. The coefficient paths therefore converge in $L^1$ and almost everywhere. Duhamel's formula for the no-jump propagators and the summable ordered-word estimate of Theorem O.7.2.3ac show convergence of the complete history isometries in norm, including the output system. A bound on the finitely many matrix coefficients on that path sequence supplies the word majorant. With stochastic coupling, every subsequence has a further subsequence with this pathwise convergence almost surely. It follows that the joint path/history convergence holds in probability under the couplings, in $J_1$ for the paths and in norm for their isometries.\n\nOn each finite stage all actually used label coefficients have finite computable bounds. Refine its intervals and use the finite normalized columns of Theorem O.7.2.3ac, with fresh coherent flags, to keep the sum of cell and pulse errors below $2^{-n}$ over the stage. For each installed quantum intervention, add its checkpoint to the mesh and its own declared instrument to the actual finite protocol. The fixed exact CP compositions are contractions, so the same limiting statement follows by telescoping on each fixed protocol. Retain the coherent registers and the classical path together. The Hermitian involutions of Theorem O.7.2.3m implement the finite columns in paid positive subslots. The finite seed preparation, table lookup, state-label release, calibration, adaptive routing, and the source archives have their own addresses, finite registers and durations. The actual complete pulse error is part of the stage tolerance.\n\nFor a fixed finite input corner and a bounded continuous path test $f$, compare the trace-class output integrals of $f$ on the coupled histories. Both the scalar test and the trace-class output of the isometry converge in probability, and the trace norms are uniformly bounded. Thus the output integrals converge in trace norm. In particular the unweighted positive Choi integrals converge in trace norm. Their limit has trace one and fixed finite-rank projections exhausting the common Hilbert history carrier have vanishing tails. This transfers the tail bound to the actual finite stages by trace-norm convergence. The path tightness and mixed finite-coordinate limits are exactly the other requirements of Theorem O.7.2.3z and QJ. Inserting a fixed spectator preserves the isometry estimates. The proof applies to each fixed dyadic horizon and to both recorded survival and recorded killing when the state law has an absorbing cemetery.\n\n*Obtaining the grid law from a Feller graph core.* Here is one precise sufficient effectivity presentation. Suppose $E$ is locally compact and its $C_0(E)$ has computable uniform norm, evaluations and algebra operations on a dense rational algebra. Require that a countable named family of nonnegative compactly supported functions in that algebra supplies arbitrarily fine local bump covers as follows. For each rational $\\delta>0$ there is an effective list of pairs $(\\varphi_i,x_i)$ with $0\\le\\varphi_i\\le1$, each supported in the $\\delta$-ball about its named center $x_i$, and every point of $E$ lies in a set where some $\\varphi_i=1$. Supply an enumerable rational linear graph core for the actual generator $\\mathcal A$, with effective $C_0(E)$ names of each $g,\\mathcal A g$, and a computable expectation of each named $C_0(E)$ function under the initial probability law. A separate isolated absorbing cemetery may be included as a named point. A killed sub-Markov law is first extended by that point and uses the resulting conservative generator and its actual core.\n\nThe dense-range resolvent search of Theorem O.7.2.3ah uses only the computable uniform norm on $C_0(E)$ and the generator-core property. Its compound-Poisson estimate (O.7.2.3ah.1) therefore computes each $P_t f$ in $C_0(E)$ for dyadic $t$, without a compactification. By the Markov law, product-test expectations at $0\\le t_1<\\cdots<t_M$ are obtained by working backwards through the actual semigroup:\n$$\n\\mathbb E\\prod_{j=1}^M f_j(X_{t_j})\n=\\int P_{t_1}\\bigl(f_1 P_{t_2-t_1}(f_2\\,\\cdots P_{t_M-t_{M-1}}f_M)\\bigr)\\,d\\mu .\n\\tag{O.7.2.3an.1}\n$$\nThese expectations are computable in the uniform norm on the named product tests. They give the required atomic grid approximation by a terminating search. At accuracy $\\delta$, choose the same local bump list for all coordinates. Set $\\psi_i=\\varphi_i\\prod_{j<i}(1-\\varphi_j)$. Each $\\psi_i$ belongs to $C_0(E)$, is supported within $\\delta$ of $x_i$, and $\\sum_{i=1}^N\\psi_i=1-\\prod_{i=1}^N(1-\\varphi_i)$ increases pointwise to one. On the grid use the products of these weights to assign the corresponding coordinate centers. For each $N$ the total assigned mass is a computable finite sum by (O.7.2.3an.1), and tends to one by monotone convergence. Stop when the mass exceeds $1-\\delta$, using certified strict comparisons. Send the unassigned mass to a fixed named word. This assignment is a coupling of the actual law and an atomic measure with expected maximum grid error at most $2\\delta$ in the bounded metric. Round its finite weights to a dyadic simplex with additional Wasserstein error at most $\\delta$. Running this at a suitably small $\\delta$ gives each required grid name. The core and conservative extension identify the generator and uniqueness class. Feller stochastic continuity and the càdlàg left limits give the no-fixed-jump premise. Thus all stages use the initial law and actual semigroup obtained from the core. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723ao\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723ao-prescribed-coherent-instruments-with-a-terminal-polish-path-record\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3ao (Prescribed Coherent Instruments with a Terminal Polish Path Record).** Use the effective named dyadic intervention grids, separable quantum and countable classical–quantum intervention ports of Theorem O.7.2.3am. Let $D$ be a fixed computably presented Polish record space with a bounded complete metric $d$, a computable dense set of names, and effective evaluation of the record tents on these names. At each grid prescribe a normal causal process with a terminal classical record in $D$ and the full named coherent output in its fixed separable carrier. Concretely the process's weighted Choi state is a positive trace-class-operator-valued measure $M_k$ on $D$. Its total mass is the deterministic normalized process state, including all the system and history ports. The processes agree under the declared causal wirings, which are effective on finite circuits and finite probes. At terminal output the record names have an allowed finite decoder, and its fixed wiring at a coarser grid is effective on finite decoded outputs. The result applies in particular to continuous or Skorokhod full-path records with their named computable step or polygonal paths and the corresponding metric presentation. The terminal record can depend on the incoming quantum state and intervening operations.\n\nA uniform finite Hermitian-control source tower realizes this comparison if and only if there is one computable array converging to all the following real and imaginary prescribed tests, uniformly indexed by the grid and test:\n\n1. $\\int f(z)\\langle i|M_k(dz)|j\\rangle$ for all finite coordinate indices and for the rational tent algebra of Theorem O.7.2.3z, including its constant function.\n2. $\\int q_{m,F}(z)\\operatorname{Tr}M_k(dz)$ for every integer $m\\ge1$ and nonempty finite set of named centers $F$, where $q_{m,F}(z)=\\min(1,2^m d(z,F))$.\n\nThe tower converges on every fixed finite input corner, every fixed normal intervention protocol and arbitrary fixed spectators. Convergence is in the joint path/coherent-output topology of QJ. Both path tightness and coherent output tails are consequences of the named tests against the actual prescribed normalized process. No rate for the array or tail bounds is required.\n\n*Convergence on a fixed grid.* For a positive trace-one Choi measure, the finite-coordinate trace of the unweighted total mass increases to one. Therefore convergence of its entries against the constant function transfers its finite-rank output tails to any approximating sequence of normalized states, as in Theorem O.7.2.3am. The scalar trace law of the prescribed measure is tight because $D$ is Polish. Given $m$ and $\\epsilon$, choose a compact set of mass at least $1-\\epsilon/2$ and a finite set of dense centers within $\\epsilon 2^{-m-1}$ of it. Its prescribed $q_{m,F}$ expectation is at most $\\epsilon$. Convergence of these expectations therefore implies the P2 tightness test for the entire actual sequence. Tent entry convergence and the quantum tails now give the QJ conclusion of Theorem O.7.2.3z. The limit is exactly the prescribed measure because the tent algebra determines the finite-coordinate measures. In particular all bounded continuous path-test integrals converge in trace norm. The trace tests in item 2 cannot in general be replaced by local weak tests on bounded finite neighborhoods alone, where mass may escape.\n\nFor a fixed causal tester, first use finite coordinate cutoffs on its open ports as in Theorem O.7.2.3am. Finite links are fixed bounded linear contractions of finitely many coordinate blocks of the process matrix, with the finite inverse weights of the faithful Choi inputs. They commute with integration against the terminal path test. For a general fixed tester and each $|f|\\le1$, remove those cutoffs using the chronological induction of Theorem O.7.2.3am on the unweighted positive prefix states. The incoming and returned tester marginals there are determined by unweighted process states and converge in trace norm. The truncation error remains bounded uniformly for every such $f$: the downstream channel first produces the positive trace-class-valued outcome measure, and integration against $f$ has norm at most one on differences of Hermitian inputs, by Jordan decomposition and trace preservation. Thus each cutoff can be removed at arbitrarily small error independently of the path test. This gives the same QJ topology for the actual finite intervention protocol with its outcomes retained. The bounded input approximation also includes a fixed spectator.\n\n*Density of finite instruments.* At a fixed grid compress the exposed ports by the finite channels of Theorem O.7.2.3am. The truncated process with its terminal record remains a positive measure on finite Choi matrices. Its integrated causal normalization follows from the actual causal identities and the trace-preserving port cutoffs. For a finite list of bounded continuous path tests and a positive tolerance, quantize the terminal measure to finitely many named centers. One construction takes a compact subset carrying almost all of its scalar trace mass, uses finitely many neighborhoods on which all the chosen tests differ little from their values at their named centers, and assigns the remaining mass to a fixed named center. Every assigned matrix is the integral of the positive Choi measure on a Borel cell, so is positive. Their sum is exactly the original truncated total, preserving causal normalization. The variation of each matrix entry on a discarded set is bounded by its scalar trace mass there. Therefore this quantization approximates the finite mixed path tests. As the compact sets and quantization accuracies improve, it also gives weak path convergence and the mixed trace-norm convergence on the fixed finite carrier. Together with the port cutoffs this proves density on the original fixed grid.\n\nA finite terminal-outcome comb with positive matrices summing to its deterministic causal normalization admits a finite sequential dilation. This follows by regarding the terminal outcome as a diagonal output register and using the finite comb theorem. Approximate the finitely many isometries by algebraic columns, keeping the designated classical readout. This gives a countable effective catalogue of finite clocked candidates with finite named terminal outputs, dense for the displayed tests and every finite collection of coarser-grid tests. In the coarser comparison one uses the declared normal causal wiring on quantum ports and the prescribed continuous record map on $D$ when it changes the terminal record. If the same terminal record is kept at all grids, this map is the identity. Under a continuous record map a coarser bounded continuous test pulls back to a bounded continuous test on $D$. These are precisely the fixed readout compatibilities used in the density argument. A record wiring that is only measurable needs a separate continuity or density premise for its chosen tests.\n\n*Effective selection and implementation.* Enumerate the tests with known grid indices, using a fixed computable bound for each bounded test and dividing by that bound when necessary. At finite stage $n$ minimize the finite catalogue loss (O.7.2.3am.2) over the first $n$ candidates, with the current rational approximants to the tests. Use the missing-depth term exactly as in that theorem. Every fixed finite list can be approximated by one coded candidate at some sufficiently deep fixed grid, and this candidate remains in the finite search at all later stages. The dominated tail bound $\\sum_{i>m}2^{-i}=2^{-m}$ makes the losses tend to zero without a modulus. The depth term forces every fixed grid to be present eventually. The selected emitted instruments are causal and normalized and converge on both families of tests. The fixed-grid proof now gives the asserted joint path topology and all tester outputs.\n\nImplement each finite causal instrument with the counted finite Hermitian isometries, normalized terminal readout, stage clock, and paid routing and resource records of Theorem O.7.2.3m. Its terminal classical record is decoded by the nominated finite name. Quantum input dependence of the record is realized by the actual instrument. The tower does not sample that record independently of its own incoming quantum states or intervention outcomes. Keep all separately owned records of the implementation. Choose the finite pulse tolerances with total norm error at most $2^{-n}$, so the trace-norm perturbations do not affect any bounded test. Conversely, if a uniform tower realizes the comparison, the actual finite branch tables and fixed effective path probes compute each test to accuracy $2^{-n}$ at stage $n$. This includes the scalar trace in item 2, which is a sum over the actual finite output. The assumed joint convergence gives one uniformly convergent-in-each-coordinate rational array for the two test families. This proves necessity. ∎\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o723ap\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"proposition-o723ap-finite-encoded-tests-do-not-determine-diffuse-feedback\"></a>**Proposition O.7.2.3ap (Finite Encoded Tests Do Not Determine Diffuse Feedback).** Use a single diffuse classical intervention port with actual predual $L^1([0,1],dx)$ and its dyadic finite channel encodings from Theorem O.7.2.3ad. There is a uniform tower of complete finite two-step sources for which every fixed dyadic finite-encoded intervention, with any fixed spectator and all its outcomes, has the same limiting response as one declared normal causal process. The same is true for the prescribed tests that keep the offered port and send a fixed ready state back in its place. Nevertheless the fixed normal identity intervention on the diffuse port distinguishes the tower from that process by probability $1/2$ at every stage. The failure occurs with an unchanged, exactly uniform offered density and a single classical output bit.\n\n*Proof.* The level $n$ encoder $D_n$ integrates a density on each of the $2^n$ half-open dyadic cells $I_{n,j}$. The embedding $I_n$ assigns to label $j$ its normalized uniform density on $I_{n,j}$. Endpoint conventions have no effect in the predual. The encodings are normalized, satisfy $D_n I_n=1$, and $I_nD_n$ converges strongly by density of dyadic simple functions. For $x\\in I_{n,j}$ write $b_n(x)=j\\bmod 2$.\n\nAt stage $n$ prepare a uniform level $n$ label $J$ with $n$ fair bits. Offer the label's density under the prescribed embedding and retain $J$ privately. After the intervention, take the actual returned state through $D_n$, giving label $J'$. Release the bit\n$$\nB_n=\\mathbf{1}\\{J\\bmod 2=J'\\bmod 2\\}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3ap.1}\n$$\nThis is a normalized finite two-step circuit with a classical encoded port and reversible parity comparison, a clock with positive-duration operations, and retained seed, routing and implementation records. The fair-bit preparation and finite parity circuit use the existing grammar of Theorem O.7.2.3m. Its comparison with an arbitrary diffuse intervention $K$ uses precisely $D_n K I_n$ on the classical port, with the spectator left intact. The decoding is the density embedding already used for the mathematical comparison in Theorem O.7.2.3ad. The physical source uses only its finite label registers.\n\nThe comparison process offers the uniform density on $[0,1]$, accepts a returned state, and then outputs a fair bit independent of the return and all tester registers. It is a normal causal process. Consider a fixed finite-encoded intervention at depth $k$ on the offered port and depth $\\ell$ on the returned port. Its arbitrary finite-encoded complete instrument may depend on the input cell and may act jointly on a quantum spectator. Every returned state has density constant in the returned classical coordinate on each depth $\\ell$ cell, with possibly different operator coefficients and retained outcomes in those cells. For $n\\ge\\ell+1$, each depth $\\ell$ cell is exactly half covered by level $n$ cells of either parity. Thus for either private parity, the event $B_n=1$ gives exactly half of every returned operator-valued cell contribution and of every outcome contribution. The same holds for $B_n=0$. The offered uniform density has the exact depth $k$ uniform cell distribution for $n\\ge k$. Consequently the final bit, all tester outputs and spectator records have precisely the comparison law for $n\\ge\\max\\{k,\\ell+1\\}$. This calculation includes any fixed noncommuting spectator states because the parity comparison multiplies every classical block by the same scalar one half. It also includes any fixed finite dyadic processing of the offered register before the return, through its resulting finite instrument.\n\nFor the stop-and-store protocol, keep the offered register and return any fixed ready probability density $g\\in L^1([0,1])$ independently of it. The stored density is exactly uniform. Its joint response with $B_n$ differs in predual norm from the corresponding uniform-density and independent-fair-bit state by at most\n$$\n2\\left|\\int_0^1 \\mathbf{1}_{\\{b_n(y)=0\\}}g(y)\\,dy-\\frac12\\right|.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3ap.2}\n$$\nFor any fixed dyadic simple density the right side is zero at every sufficiently large $n$. For general $g$ it tends to zero by density in $L^1$ and the uniform bound of the parity probe. The same bound applies after adjoining fixed independent registers to the prefix test.\n\nUnder the normal identity intervention the returned density conditional on $J=j$ is exactly the offered density on $I_{n,j}$. Its encoding gives $J'=j$ with probability one. Thus $B_n=1$ surely at every stage, while its probability in the comparison process is $1/2$. All tests here use the same normalized reference measure, actual named input and return domains, and the given encoded channel interface. No point input, singular probability density, or additional classical gate is used. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723aq\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723aq-diffuse-feedback-beyond-limit-computable-encoded-probes\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3aq (Diffuse Feedback beyond Limit-Computable Encoded Probes).** Use the actual classical input and return port $L^1([0,1],dx)$, dyadic channel encodings and two-step circuits of Proposition O.7.2.3ap. There is a prescribed normal causal process whose complete responses to all fixed dyadic finite-encoded interventions have uniform limit-computable names, including finite outcome registers and effective fixed spectators, but which has no uniform finite source tower for the comparison that also admits the ordinary diffuse identity intervention. In a countable family of mixtures described below, uniform realizability for all fixed normal interventions is equivalent to limit computability of a single total weight. Only the declared offered and returned system ports, the tester's retained registers, and the final bit are compared. Implementation and private memory records are owned separately.\n\n*The mixture and its encoded responses.* Let $P_j$ be the normalized parity circuit of Proposition O.7.2.3ap at dyadic depth $j\\ge1$, regarded as a normal process using its actual density embedding on the offered port and its cell-integration channel on the returned port. Let $P_{\\rm fair}$ be the normal circuit that offers the uniform density, accepts the returned state and produces a fair bit independently of it and of all tester outputs. For nonnegative weights $p_j$ with $\\theta=\\sum_{j\\ge1}p_j\\le1$, consider the prescribed process\n$$\nP=(1-\\theta)P_{\\rm fair}+\\sum_{j\\ge1}p_jP_j.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3aq.1}\n$$\nThis is a normal causal process on the same diffuse port. It stores the branch label and the depth-$j$ cell label in countable private classical memory. The preparation has a normal joint density in the product of the Lebesgue and counting carriers, with trace one. The continuation is the countable direct sum of the normalized cell-integration and bit-comparison channels. Absolute convergence in the predual norm follows from the sum of the nonnegative weights. No point input or pointwise evaluation of a representative of a density occurs.\n\nIf the return leg of a fixed finite encoded intervention uses depth $\\ell$, every return density is constant on each level-$\\ell$ cell. Its coefficients can be operators on a spectator and outcome registers. For $j>\\ell$, the two parity classes occupy equal Lebesgue mass in every such cell. Thus either private parity produces exactly half of every returned operator block for each bit value. The outgoing marginal of $P_j$ is uniform, so after summing the hidden cell label the full tester response equals the response of $P_{\\rm fair}$, including all outcomes and spectator correlations. This applies at any fixed offered depth, since the returned parity balance is conditional on the hidden label before averaging it. Therefore the complete prescribed response on this test is exactly\n$$\nR_{\\rm fair}+\\sum_{j=1}^{\\ell}p_j(R_j-R_{\\rm fair}),\n\\tag{O.7.2.3aq.2}\n$$\nwhere each $R_j$ is the actual finite encoded response and $R_{\\rm fair}$ is its fair analogue. All branch responses are effectively computable from the finite test and the stated effective spectator input. In particular if the $p_j$ have one uniform array of rational approximants, convergent for each fixed $j$, (O.7.2.3aq.2) gives such approximants in the full decoded output norm on every encoded test, without using $\\theta$.\n\nThis also covers the extra prefix probes of Proposition O.7.2.3ap with a fixed computable ready return density $g$. For the stop-and-store output, the norm of the correction of branch $j$ from the fair output is at most $2|\\int \\mathbf{1}_{\\{b_j=0\\}}g\\,dx-1/2|$. Approximate $g$ in $L^1$ by a dyadic simple probability density at depth $m$. The correction is then bounded uniformly for every $j>m$ by twice that approximation error. The tail of the mixture has weight at most one. Thus a computable $L^1$ name of $g$ and the finite weight array give a limit-computable name in the stored predual norm, including its correlation with the bit. For instance, choose a cutoff depth for tail error $2^{-n}$ and run a finite weighted approximation to that prefix. To avoid assuming any convergence rate for the weights on a growing prefix, at stage $n$ first clip the approximated weights sequentially to total at most one. At each fixed depth the clipped weights converge to the true weights. The tail beyond any fixed depth with small uniform correction then has small contribution on both sides, regardless of its moving length. This proves convergence of the finite approximants without a rate.\n\n*Identity criterion and its converse.* The same ordinary identity channel on the stated diffuse port returns the offered density without changing it. On each $P_j$ this produces bit one surely. Consequently the prescribed identity response is\n$$\n\\Pr_P(B=1\\mid{\\rm id})=\\frac{1+\\theta}{2}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3aq.3}\n$$\nAt any actual finite stage the named identity is wired by the composition of the offered embedding and the returned encoder. On the stipulated dyadic encodings this is the computable overlap table of the finite cells. Any other effective input and output comparison for the same named identity must likewise supply its finite compressed operation, as required for fixed interventions in Theorem O.7.2.3ad. A uniform tower for this comparison gives a computable sequence of rational approximants to its actual bit expectations, to tolerance $2^{-n}$ at stage $n$. Convergence of the required identity response then makes $\\theta$ limit computable.\n\nConversely, suppose both the individual weights and $\\theta$ have the stated limit-computable names. At stage $n$ take a dyadic approximant to $\\theta$ clipped to $[0,1]$, and set the fair weight to its complement. Clip the dyadic approximants of $p_1,\\ldots,p_n$ in that order to the remaining nonnegative mass, and assign any unused mass to branch $n+1$. For each fixed $j$ the resulting weight converges to $p_j$. This follows by induction because clipping to a closed interval is continuous and the true weight lies in the limiting interval. The fair weight also converges. Since the probability vectors have total one and their limit coordinates have total one, they converge in $\\ell^1$. Indeed choose a finite subset carrying almost all limiting mass, pass to the limit on its coordinates and bound both tails by the complement.\n\nImplement each finite mixture with a finite fair-bit tape for its dyadic weights and an actual conditional parity circuit for the chosen label. A common port at the deepest used level suffices: for a shallower branch choose the offered fine label uniformly inside its sampled coarse cell, retain the coarse branch cell for the comparison, and group returned fine cells to that same coarse level. The average of the fine offered densities within a coarse cell is its original coarse density. The sum of their return integration maps is the original coarse integration map. Affinity of each actual normal intervention therefore gives the exact original response under these two replacements, also with spectator and tester registers. Every fair tape, addressed parity operation, branch record and clock interval is a paid part of the finite source. For any fixed complete tester, each normalized branch has trace norm one with all outcomes retained. Its output error is at most the $\\ell^1$ error of the mixture weights, also for a fixed arbitrary spectator. Thus the converse holds on this family for all admitted fixed normal interventions, without imposing a dyadic approximation on the intervention itself beyond the physical port encodings.\n\n*An explicit obstruction.* Let $K$ be the ordinary halting set. Let $A$ be the set of positive indices $e$ of oracle programs that halt on their own index using oracle $K$. This jump is not computable from $K$, by the relativized halting diagonal argument. At oracle stage $j$ run the first $j$ oracle programs for at most $j$ steps with the exact oracle $K$. Assign weight $4^{-e}$ at the first stage at which $e$ appears to halt. Let $p_j$ be the sum of the weights first assigned at stage $j$. For every fixed $j$ its calculation makes only finitely many oracle queries. Simulate these two finite stage computations with the ordinary finite halting approximation $K_s$, answering every query by membership in its finite enumerated set. For fixed $j$, the exact computations make only finitely many queries, and all those answers agree with $K_s$ at sufficiently late $s$. Their transcripts then agree as well. This explicitly produces the uniform rational approximant array for $p_j$, with no use of $\\theta$.\n\nHere\n$$\n\\theta=\\sum_{e\\in A}4^{-e}\\le\\tfrac13 .\n\\tag{O.7.2.3aq.4}\n$$\nThis real is not limit computable. If a computable rational sequence converged to it, $K$ could compute the real to any prescribed tolerance. To do so search for an index at which there is no later term differing by more than half the tolerance. The existence of a later violating index is a computably enumerable predicate, so $K$ can decide it, and convergence makes the search terminate. The resulting error bound follows by taking the limit. From such $K$-computable approximations one could read the digits in (O.7.2.3aq.4). After subtracting earlier digits, a zero at index $e$ leaves remainder at most $4^{-e}/3$, while a one leaves remainder at least $4^{-e}$. The separated intervals allow a uniform test with strict margin. This would decide $A$ from $K$, a contradiction. Equations (O.7.2.3aq.2)–(O.7.2.3aq.3) now give the claimed process with uniformly limit-computable finite encoded responses and no uniform full diffuse-feedback tower. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723ar\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723ar-an-effective-conservative-resolvent-with-no-càdlàg-coordinate-law\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3ar (An Effective Conservative Resolvent with No Càdlàg Coordinate Law).** There is a computably presented compact classical carrier, an explicit conservative closed generator on its diffuse $L^1$ predual, and effective resolvent and finite channel encodings as in Theorems O.7.2.3x and O.7.2.3ad, for which the actual Markov law has no càdlàg realization in the declared coordinate topology. This remains true after adjoining a quantum system with identity dynamics. The reduced semigroup and fixed strong predual intervention comparison of Theorem O.7.2.3ad apply. A full $J_1$ path comparison with the same state coordinates and Markov law is impossible.\n\n*Carrier, source and closed operator.* Let $E=\\mathbb T\\times\\{0,1\\}$ with its product topology, the circle with its usual effective metric and the bit with the discrete metric. Use normalized Lebesgue measure on the circle and fair counting probability on the bit. Fix a closed nowhere-dense set $C\\subset\\mathbb T$ of Lebesgue measure $1/2$ with a computable $L^1$ name of its indicator. Construct it by removing a central open interval of length $4^{-k}$ from each of the $2^{k-1}$ surviving intervals at round $k\\ge1$, and identify the endpoints of the original unit interval. The finite surviving unions have rational endpoints and the measure of all later deletions is $2^{-k-1}$, which gives the $L^1$ name. Their largest interval lengths tend to zero, so the remaining closed set has empty interior.\n\nDefine the Borel measure-preserving involution\n$$\nJ(x,b)=(x,b\\mathbin{\\oplus}\\mathbf{1}_C(x)).\n$$\nLet $R_t(x,b)=(x+t,b)$, with addition on the circle. The deterministic Markov coordinate law has transitions $F_t=J R_t J$, and its initial law is uniform on $E$. On densities put $Uf=f\\circ J$ and $S_tf(x,b)=f(x-t,b)$. Then $T_t=US_tU$ is the density evolution of $F_t$. The maps $T_t$ are positive isometries, preserve the integral, and are strongly continuous in $L^1$ by the same properties of $S_t$. Tensoring with the identity on any fixed finite matrix predual gives completely positive conservative contractions. The closed generator is $L=U(-\\partial_x)U$ with domain the $U$-transport of the two copies of periodic $W^{1,1}(\\mathbb T)$. The span of the transported rational trigonometric polynomials is a graph core: periodic smoothing and then trigonometric approximation approximate both a function and its weak derivative in $L^1$. The closed generator has this unique conservative semigroup by the generator uniqueness theorem.\n\nDyadic cell averaging and inclusion, preserving the bit, give the finite channel encodings of Theorem O.7.2.3ad. The isometry $U$ is effective in $L^1$. On bounded dyadic simple functions this follows by replacing the indicator of $C$ with the explicit finite unions and bounding the error by twice the product of its $L^1$ error and the uniform norm of the input. Density and the isometry extend the computation to arbitrary named inputs. Translation by a rational time is computable uniformly on finite simple functions by rational endpoint shifts and hence on arbitrary named inputs by contraction. It follows that $T_t$ is computable at each rational time, uniformly in that time and the named input. For each positive integer $j$, the normalized resolvent is the positive conservative channel\n$$\nj(j-L)^{-1}=U\\left(\\int_0^\\infty je^{-jt}S_t\\,dt\\right)U .\n\\tag{O.7.2.3ar.1}\n$$\nThe integral is effectively computable in $L^1$ on dyadic simple inputs. Truncate time with its explicit exponential tail and integrate translations on the retained interval by Riemann sums. For a finite step function the norm of its change under a time shift is bounded by the shift size times the sum of the absolute jump sizes. This gives certified norm errors for the sums. Extend by contraction to the dense rational names, apply $U$, and take the declared cell averages to obtain all finite compressions. The same construction works with finite matrix entries and finite spectators. Thus the example has the effective closed generator, resolvent and finite encodings of the registered conservative class.\n\n*No càdlàg path on the declared carrier.* The time-$t$ bit from initial $(x,b)$ is\n$$\nb_t=b\\mathbin{\\oplus}\\mathbf{1}_C(x)\\mathbin{\\oplus}\\mathbf{1}_C(x+t).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3ar.2}\n$$\nFor almost every $x\\in C$ there are dyadic times arbitrarily near zero at which $x+t\\in C$. Indeed $L^1$ continuity of translations allows a sequence of positive dyadic $t_m\\downarrow0$ such that $\\|\\mathbf{1}_C(\\cdot+t_m)-\\mathbf{1}_C\\|_1\\le2^{-m}$. The sum of the measures of the disagreement events is finite. The Borel–Cantelli lemma therefore gives $x+t_m\\in C$ for all sufficiently large $m$, for almost every $x\\in C$. On the other hand, since $C$ is closed with empty interior, for every $x\\in C$ and $\\eta>0$ the set of $t\\in(0,\\eta)$ with $x+t\\notin C$ contains an open interval. It therefore contains a dyadic time. On an event of probability $1/2$, (O.7.2.3ar.2) takes both bit values arbitrarily close to time zero along dyadics. Any process with the specified dyadic finite-dimensional distributions has the same law on that countable time skeleton and hence the same event. Every càdlàg coordinate path on $E$ has a right limit at zero, also along dyadics. This is the contradiction.\n\nThe same example rules out $J_1$ tightness of finite stage paths whose dyadic finite-dimensional distributions converge to this skeleton. A compact set of $J_1$ paths into $E$ has a uniform right-continuity modulus at zero for the discrete bit. Otherwise choose paths in the compact set with a bit change within shrinking intervals from zero and pass to a convergent subsequence. The defining time changes fix zero and approach the identity. The limit bit is constant in a sufficiently short right neighborhood of zero. Uniform convergence after time changes contradicts the bit changes. On the prescribed dyadic skeleton the event of at least one bit change in $(0,\\eta)$ has probability at least $1/2$ for every $\\eta>0$. It is a countable increasing union of finite-coordinate events, so a finite set of probes witnesses probability greater than $1/3$. Their probabilities converge for any sequence having the required finite-dimensional limits, since the bit test is clopen. Such a sequence cannot be asymptotically tight. Thus it fails the path tests even if only the dyadic marginal convergence is imposed directly. The joint QJ path test also fails upon using the identity quantum effect. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723as\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723as-dyadic-oscillation-criterion-on-the-diffuse-markov-carrier\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3as (Dyadic Oscillation Criterion on the Diffuse Markov Carrier).** Use a computably presented Polish state space $E$ with bounded complete compatible metric $d$, a named initial density and a conservative strongly continuous Markov semigroup $T_t$ on the diffuse $L^1$ predual, with the effective propagation of Theorem O.7.2.3x. Assume the named local tent functions on $E$ act by effective multiplication on the predual. Use the actual Markov grid laws of this semigroup. For rational $\\varepsilon>0$, dyadic $T>0$ and grid depth $n$, let $N_{\\varepsilon,T,n}$ count the maximum number of disjoint ordered pairs of grid times $s_1<t_1<\\cdots<s_m<t_m$ in $[0,T]$ with $d(X_{s_i},X_{t_i})>\\varepsilon$. Then a càdlàg coordinate law with the prescribed finite-dimensional distributions exists exactly when\n$$\n\\lim_{m\\to\\infty}\\sup_n\\Pr\\{N_{\\varepsilon,T,n}\\ge m\\}=0\n\\quad\\text{for every such }\\varepsilon,T.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3as.1}\n$$\nIn this case there is an effective finite $J_1$ path tower with the stipulated laws, including the coherent history driven by the continuous coefficients in Theorem O.7.2.3an. If the condition fails, every proposed sequence of decoded $J_1$ paths whose finite dyadic grid distributions tend to the prescribed laws fails tightness. The criterion concerns the named classical coordinate and its coefficient-driven history.\n\n*Grid law and finite tests.* For a finite grid, compute expectations of products of bounded named coordinate functions by alternating $T_{t_{j+1}-t_j}$ and the corresponding multiplication operators on the initial density, then integrating. To obtain uniform weak names, fix a rational radius and enumerate tents at dense named states with value one on the concentric balls of half that radius. The successive tent weights $g_i=\\varphi_i\\prod_{h<i}(1-\\varphi_h)$ have partial sum at most one and increase in sum to one. On each coordinate retain a finite collection of these weights. On the product use their products. Their finite assigned mass is computable by propagation and increases to one. Stop when this mass exceeds one minus the requested tolerance. Assign each retained product weight to the word of its named centers and the residual to any named word. Coupling with the actual grid law gives maximum-coordinate transportation cost at most the radius plus the metric bound times the residual, with an additional arbitrarily small error for dyadic weights. This yields the weak names used by Theorem O.7.2.3an.\n\nOne can replace the probabilities in (O.7.2.3as.1) by expectations of finite continuous probes. On each grid take the maximum over ordered $m$-tuples of pairs of the minimum over their pairs of $\\min(1,[d(X_s,X_t)-\\varepsilon]_+)$. Use zero when there is no $m$-tuple. The probe is bounded Lipschitz for the grid metric and has computable expectation. It is bounded above by the indicator of $N_{\\varepsilon,T,n}\\ge m$ and below by $\\min(1,\\varepsilon)$ times the indicator of $N_{2\\varepsilon,T,n}\\ge m$. Requiring the uniform tail limit for every rational threshold therefore gives the same test. This formulation requires no decision of whether a threshold carries positive mass.\n\n*Existence and uniqueness of the coordinate law.* The consistent dyadic distributions define a probability law on the countable dyadic skeleton. The events in (O.7.2.3as.1) increase with grid depth. The tail condition says that for each of the countably many horizons and thresholds the skeleton almost surely has a finite bound on its number of disjoint oscillation pairs. If the values approaching a time from one side were not Cauchy, a positive rational threshold would be exceeded by arbitrarily many disjoint pairs in nested one-sided neighborhoods. Completeness gives left and right limits everywhere on the skeleton closure. The extension by right limits is right continuous and has left limits, by the corresponding one-sided limit properties of the dense skeleton.\n\nTo check that extension agrees at the prescribed times, let $\\rho_t$ be the evolved density. Choose finitely many Borel cells of diameter at most $\\eta$ covering all but arbitrarily small $\\rho_t$ mass. For an increment $h>0$, the probability of leaving a named cell $A$ from time $t$ to $t+h$ is bounded by $\\|T_h(\\mathbf{1}_A\\rho_t)-\\mathbf{1}_A\\rho_t\\|_1$. Strong continuity makes the finite sum tend to zero. For the left increment, replace $\\rho_t$ by $\\rho_{t-h}$. The bound changes by at most twice the finite sum of the corresponding restricted norm differences, and the uncovered mass changes by at most $\\|\\rho_{t-h}-\\rho_t\\|_1$. Thus the coordinates are stochastically continuous. At each fixed dyadic time both one-sided limits agree almost surely with its prescribed value, and at every other fixed time the finite laws are determined by continuity in probability and the strong continuity of semigroup products. The extended law has no fixed-time jumps. Dyadic evaluation determines a càdlàg law, which proves uniqueness. The effective grid names and Theorem O.7.2.3an supply the tower and its driven coherent output.\n\n*Necessity and failure of tightness.* A càdlàg path on $[0,T]$ has a uniformly accurate finite step approximation and hence a finite bound on the number of disjoint pairs above any positive threshold. This bound is uniform on a compact $J_1$ set: cover it by finitely many time-change neighborhoods of paths with accurate finite step approximations, and use the fact that a time change preserves the ordering and disjointness of pairs. A fixed finite-grid event $N_{\\varepsilon,T,n}\\ge m$ is open in the grid product metric, by its strict distance tests. If (O.7.2.3as.1) fails, some positive probability lower bound occurs for arbitrarily large $m$ on suitably chosen finite grids. Weak convergence of those grid laws preserves the lower bound in the limit inferior of the sequence probabilities. Taking $m$ beyond the bound on a putative compact tightness set contradicts tightness. This also gives necessity for the single càdlàg law. See Skorokhod (1956). $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723at\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723at-diffuse-feedback-from-joint-sequential-channel-data\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3at (Diffuse Feedback from Joint Sequential Channel Data).** Fix a finite named intervention grid. At each boundary of the prescribed sequential process use its actual joint normal predual, including the process memory and each system or retained register present there. The preduals may be separable trace-class spaces or classical–quantum spaces $L^1(S,\\mu;\\mathcal T(H))$ on their named measured carriers. On each boundary supply effective finite channel encodings of Theorem O.7.2.3ad, with a dense effective family of finite rational states and a computable predual norm on their names. A port used for an external intervention and the memory it leaves untouched are stipulated tensor factors of that boundary and use product encodings. This means the prescribed joint states are normal on the declared product algebra. Use the actual joint predual whenever an output is itself specified as a single diffuse measured carrier. The theorem does not use an identification of a singular diagonal measure with a density on an independent product carrier.\n\nLet the prescribed operations be normal completely positive trace-preserving maps on these joint preduals, with any finite outcome ports retained. There is a uniform finite source tower in the grammar of Theorem O.7.2.3m converging strongly on every joint operation if and only if the output of each operation on each named dense input state has a uniform limit-computable predual-norm name. This means one algorithm gives rational finite names indexed by operation, input and approximation number, converging in predual norm for each fixed operation and input. The comparison uses the stipulated finite channel encodings. It needs neither a convergence modulus nor pointwise transition-kernel evaluations. Every such tower can be chosen to realize the corresponding external history for every fixed normal feedback intervention, including a diffuse identity or an operation that changes the subsequent classical law. It preserves arbitrary fixed spectators and all declared outcomes. If some of the process memory is private, the joint channel condition remains sufficient for the external comparison. The necessity statement concerns the declared comparison of joint operations themselves.\n\n*Finite channel density and effective choice.* At a given boundary write $D_d:X\\to X_d$ and $I_d:X_d\\to X$ for its encoder and embedding, with $D_dI_d=1$ and $E_d=I_dD_d\\to1$ strongly. For a prescribed channel $\\mathcal A:X\\to Y$ the finite channel $D_b^Y\\mathcal A I_a^X$ gives the decoded candidate $E_b^Y\\mathcal A E_a^X$. On any fixed input state $\\rho$ the difference from $\\mathcal A\\rho$ is bounded by\n$$\n\\|E_a^X\\rho-\\rho\\|_1+\\|E_b^Y\\mathcal A\\rho-\\mathcal A\\rho\\|_1.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3at.1}\n$$\nThis tends to zero by the stated encodings and contractivity. Algebraic finite channels are dense among the finite channels, including channels between finite direct sums of matrix preduals. For each input summand use a finite Stinespring isometry with a separate output-label register. Approximate its orthonormal columns by algebraic columns, for example by rational approximation followed by normalization with the positive inverse square root of their positive definite Gram matrix. Keep the output-label readout in its declared basis. The resulting finite channels preserve positivity and the total trace on every input summand. This gives an effective enumeration of algebraic finite channels and a norm-dense family at each finite size.\n\nEnumerate finite **tuples** of these algebraic channels, with one encoding choice per named wire shared by both operations incident to that wire. For each tuple require all named wire types, memory and outcome labels. Inequality (O.7.2.3at.1) at each of the finitely many operations and finite Stinespring approximation show that this joint catalogue approximates every finite collection of prescribed operation tests. Shared sizes pose no restriction, since each wire cutoff may be enlarged to serve both incident operations. Include the declared ready state as an operation from the scalar predual if it also requires approximation.\n\nEnumerate the operation/input tests as $i=1,2,\\ldots$ and let $a_{i,n}$ be the supplied named approximant to the corresponding actual output. Use the first $n$ catalogue tuples at stage $n$ and choose one within $2^{-n}$ of the minimum of\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n 2^{-i}\\min\\{1,\\|\\mathcal A_i^{(n)}(\\rho_i)-a_{i,n}\\|_1\\}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3at.2}\n$$\nHere $\\mathcal A_i^{(n)}$ is the decoded candidate operation belonging to test $i$. Include the penalty $2^{-d}$ in this loss, where $d$ is the minimum cutoff depth of the candidate's wire encodings. Each finite loss is computable by the effective encodings, finite algebraic channels and named predual norms. For a fixed finite list, a single catalogue tuple approximates all its tests to any prescribed accuracy with arbitrarily large minimum depth, by (O.7.2.3at.1). That tuple remains available at all sufficiently late stages, and the supplied names converge on that finite list. The remaining weights have arbitrarily small total, so the selected loss tends to zero. Each fixed test then converges to the prescribed output, and the selected minimum depths tend to infinity. Density and contractivity give strong convergence on every fixed joint input state. After tensoring a fixed spectator the same conclusion follows first on finite sums of elementary input tensors and then by trace-norm density. In particular the comparisons keep the correlations of the joint boundary with that spectator.\n\n*Physical finite wires and actual feedback.* The selected tuples are finite processes on a shared finite wire inventory. Realize their complete finite channels by the Hermitian isometric-column implementation of Theorem O.7.2.3m. Install the actual finite pulse durations, clock updates, private dilation registers, adaptive host routing and paid inventory. Include the finite readouts of the stipulated outcome ports. Choose total physical operator-norm error at most $2^{-n}$ over the stage, so it does not affect any fixed strong comparison.\n\nFor a fixed normal external intervention $\\mathcal V$ on its named port and tester memory, use its finite compression $D_b\\mathcal V I_a$ on its adjacent finite wires. The unaffected process memory has its actual selected finite wire. The tester memory can independently use any increasing finite encodings. The finite compression of a physically supplied effective intervention is computable from its declared compressions, and is implemented with its own instrument records and paid subslots. The mathematical convergence also holds for every fixed normal comparison map by applying its exact finite compressions. A noneffective comparison map has not thereby been supplied as a physical control service.\n\nThe decoded finite compression of the actual intervention converges strongly on each fixed port-plus-memory state by the same two-term inequality (O.7.2.3at.1). Its tensor product with the identity on the process memory has the same conclusion on any fixed joint state. Thus the selected source operations and the fixed compressed interventions converge strongly on the actual successive target states. For a finite chronology, compare the target and the simulated composition one factor at a time. The error is at most the sum of the individual decoded errors evaluated on the fixed target input to that factor, because all remaining factors are contractions and the shared encodings satisfy $D_dI_d=1$. The same bound holds with a fixed spectator, and for a complete finite-outcome tester using its block output with outcomes retained. For adaptive feedback use the single normal controlled operation on the direct sum of earlier outcome labels at its checkpoint. A returned diffuse density is routed through the actual next channel, which then supplies its conditional evolution. This proves the external history statement using the prescribed joint state at each step.\n\nConversely, a uniform tower realizing the declared joint operations has computable finite channel matrices, input encodings, and decoded output names. At each stage and on each named input compute the decoded output to predual norm error $2^{-n}$. Convergence of the actual joint operations then gives one uniform limit-computable array for all their tests. This proves necessity on the stated joint comparison. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723au\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723au-compatible-joint-path-instruments-across-dyadic-grids\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3au (Compatible Joint Path Instruments across Dyadic Grids).** Use an increasing effective exhaustion by finite dyadic grids and horizons. At each named interval prescribe a normal completely positive instrument from its entering joint system and process-memory predual to its leaving joint predual, with the complete classical coordinate segment in the stated Polish $J_1$ path space as an outcome. The integrated map is trace preserving. Use the actual joint preduals and effective finite encodings of Theorem O.7.2.3at. External intervention wires and the untouched process memory use their declared product encodings. Keep all named coherent outputs in the leaving predual. Any classical register used by a normal feedback operation is among the named strong predual ports. The separately compared path variable carries the weak Polish path topology of QJ.\n\nRequire exact consistency of the prescribed instruments under refinement. With the intervening prescribed wires joined, the fine chronological composition, with its segment records concatenated, equals the coarse instrument, including the joint memory and named outputs. The coordinate release at each refined checkpoint is a declared outcome of the operation at that checkpoint, with the initial release produced by the named preparation or the input-side readout. Jointly with the leaving density, these releases have the distribution of the prescribed path samples at those times. At an intervention checkpoint the sample used to start the next segment is supplied by its declared postintervention release operation, before later operations. The interval paths are càdlàg. Boundary values use the following segment at an intervention, and the final segment supplies the terminal value. The finite source releases the actual recorded coordinate at its clock checkpoints and decodes those releases by step interpolation, taking the sampled value at the terminal endpoint itself. The coordinate carrier has the computable bounded complete metric and named points of Theorem O.7.2.3as. Finite named coordinate labels, effective input/output encodings, and effective evaluations of the declared interval restrictions on finite circuits are part of the same named comparison.\n\nThere exists a uniform finite tower converging on all these interval instruments and on their full joint paths under every fixed finite normal intervention protocol if and only if one uniform limit-computable array supplies the following tests on each interval and each named dense rational joint input state:\n\n1. The leaving joint predual integrals against the rational tent algebra of its Polish path space, including the constant function, with convergence of their approximants in the predual norm.\n2. The scalar trace expectations of the path tightness probes $q_{m,F}$ of Theorem O.7.2.3z, with their given dense path names.\n\nThe path names can be the finite dyadic step paths with named state coordinates for the declared effective $J_1$ metric. No rate for the names or tightness is required. The statement includes arbitrary fixed spectators and a fixed finite set of normal interventions that change the subsequent classical law. If the process memory is private, the joint interval condition is sufficient for the external comparison. Necessity concerns the comparison of the named joint instruments themselves.\n\n*One interval.* For each real bounded measurable path test $f$, integrating the instrument against $f$ gives a linear map on its joint predual. On Hermitian inputs its norm is at most $\\|f\\|_\\infty$, by the Jordan decomposition and trace preservation of the total instrument. Thus convergence on the dense input list extends to each fixed input, and with any fixed spectator by finite tensor approximation. The prescribed trace law is a probability on a Polish space. Its tightness gives for each $m$ finite sets of centers with arbitrarily small prescribed $q_{m,F}$ expectation. Convergence of these expectations gives the actual P2 test of Theorem O.7.2.3z. On a compact set carrying all but arbitrarily small trace, the rational tent algebra separates points and uniformly approximates continuous tests. The approximating tests can be kept uniformly bounded on the whole path space by approximating a scalar clipping function by polynomials over the compact range of each tent polynomial. The norm of the omitted integral is at most its bound times the omitted trace mass. Consequently both test families imply convergence in the joint predual norm of the integral against every bounded continuous $f$. If the output is trace class, convergence for $f=1$ gives the finite-rank output tails. For general named diffuse outputs the same comparison retains the stipulated strong predual norm.\n\n*Finite candidate density across grids.* Fix any finite list of joint interval and input tests. Take a fine dyadic grid including all their endpoints. By exact compatibility the uncompressed fine prescription with internal wires joined has precisely the coarse joint segment instruments. For its actual finer coordinate releases, step interpolation converges almost surely in $J_1$ on each of the fixed coarse intervals as the fine mesh tends to zero. To verify this, uniformly approximate the path by a finite right-continuous step path on the interval with its actual value at the terminal endpoint. Move each of the finitely many interior step jumps to the first grid point at or after it and use an increasing piecewise-linear time change to align them. For fine enough meshes these points are distinct and interior. Both endpoints are fixed and sampled exactly. The uniform path error after the time change is bounded by twice the step-approximation error. A terminal jump causes no additional error because the sample at that endpoint is assigned separately. Since the finer releases are the samples of the same prescribed segment laws, this replacement of the record changes the weighted joint outputs by a quantity tending to zero in norm. For a positive input, the bound is the scalar trace expectation of the absolute change in the test function under this sampling. The output density and its coherent correlations have not been replaced.\n\nAt a fixed sufficiently fine grid, quantize each actual emitted classical coordinate separately to finitely many named points, using a Borel cell partition of small radius on a compact set of high probability. The finitely many trace marginals needed for the selected tests admit a common such finite quantization. The unassigned cells use one named fallback. This quantization changes the state-coordinate vector at all fine ticks by a small maximum error with high probability. The step path uses the same physical tick times, so its $J_1$ error is at most the maximum coordinate error. Continuity of the finitely many path tests and tightness then give small norm errors for their weighted output integrals. The finite assigned outcomes are genuine effects of the fine coordinate instruments and their sum is the unchanged normalized channel. Quantization is made at the actual release, so there is no use of a future readout to choose an earlier outcome.\n\nInsert effective finite channel encodings on the exposed and internal joint wires of this fixed finite prescription. For each tested input use chronological telescoping with each cutoff error evaluated on the fixed exact joint input at that wire. The errors tend to zero in predual norm by strong convergence, including the finite already retained record registers. The same bound applies to every weighted path integral with bounded test, since its norm is bounded by the complete downstream instrument. The finitely many encoded outcome operations are complete finite channels on their shared wire inventory. Approximate them by finite algebraic Stinespring columns as in Theorem O.7.2.3at. This yields an effective catalogue of finite circuits with actual named releases and coherent outputs, dense for every fixed finite list of coarse-interval tests. The cutoffs can be taken above any given depth after the fine time grid has been fixed.\n\n*Selection, clocks and arbitrary fixed feedback.* Assign a candidate both its actual grid depth and its minimum wire cutoff depth. Use the finite-catalogue weighted loss of Theorem O.7.2.3am, with a penalty tending to zero only when both depths tend to infinity. Include each test once, with the stated interval's grid index, and score it only when that interval is contained in the candidate grid. Use capped predual-norm discrepancies for the first family and capped absolute discrepancies for the second. All candidate scores are computable from their finite tables, effective restriction wires and record probes. Density gives a fixed candidate at arbitrarily large depths fitting any fixed finite initial list. It remains available at all sufficiently late stages. The geometric tail bound on the weighted loss proves convergence, and the two depth penalties ensure that every named interval eventually occurs and that the selected wire cutoffs exhaust their carriers. No rate of convergence of the prescribed names is used.\n\nImplement the selected finite channels by the normalized Hermitian isometries and paid positive subslots of Theorem O.7.2.3m. Retain the actual coordinate releases, coherent registers, outcome labels and separate resource records. Finite pulse errors can sum to at most $2^{-n}$ at stage $n$, which perturbs every bounded test by a vanishing amount. At a fixed external intervention use its actual finite compression on the common wires, as in Theorem O.7.2.3at. A physically installed intervention uses its supplied finite compressions. The strong comparison is also defined for any fixed normal intervention through those exact compression maps. In particular a diffuse return is routed to the next process operation. Its future record is produced by the operation acting on that return and the retained memory.\n\nFor a fixed protocol with finitely many checkpoints, first test the resulting joint outcome measure against a product of bounded continuous functions of its successive interval paths. This computes the chronological composition of the corresponding weighted interval maps and fixed intervention maps. The one-interval conclusion and the strong cutoff estimate of Theorem O.7.2.3at give convergence of the composition in predual norm by telescoping on the fixed target-prefix joint states. For finite adaptive outcome branches, regard the controlled operation as a normal channel on the direct sum over the earlier outcomes. The unweighted maps give convergence of the actual incoming states at each interval. Hence each interval trace-law marginal is tight, by the one-interval result and contractivity on the incoming norm errors. The joint trace laws are tight by taking products of finitely many compact sets. On such a product the algebra of product tests uniformly approximates continuous tests, and the same bounded extension and tail estimate as above give convergence of every bounded continuous integral on the product path space. This keeps the joint operator-valued path law and coherent outputs.\n\nFinally, concatenation of a fixed finite number of segments with the stated endpoint convention is continuous in $J_1$. For converging segment sequences glue the segment time changes at their fixed endpoints. On each half-open segment the uniform error estimate is inherited from its own time change, and at an interior endpoint both concatenated paths use the following segment. The endpoint at the horizon uses the final segment. The time changes approach the identity and preserve the resulting uniform state error. Thus the product-path comparison gives the full $J_1$ path and QJ comparison of the same actual tower. This includes state-law changes at the feedback checkpoints.\n\nConversely a uniform tower passing the named joint comparison gives the uniform approximant array by computing its actual finite decoded instruments, including their path probes, to accuracy $2^{-n}$. Necessity uses the named interval instruments, and asserts no ability to reconstruct private states from external readouts. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723av\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723av-no-uniformly-effective-countable-test-menu-determines-diffuse-feedback\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3av (No Uniformly Effective Countable Test Menu Determines Diffuse Feedback).** Use the diffuse classical intervention port $L^1(\\mathbb T,dx)$ with dyadic cell encodings, where $\\mathbb T$ is the circle with normalized Lebesgue measure. Fix any countable menu of normal complete intervention instruments on the offered and returned ports. Each may have retained tester registers and a fixed spectator. Assume only that the following scalar finite tables are computable uniformly in the menu index and the dyadic depth: starting from a uniform offered cell label, the joint probabilities of the offered and returned cell labels after the indicated instrument, with its outcomes and spectator traced for this table calculation. This holds in particular for a uniformly named menu of the finite compressions of Theorem O.7.2.3ad. Then one can construct a uniform tower of normalized finite two-step sources which converges to the same prescribed comparison on every instrument in the menu, including all retained tester outcomes and quantum records in joint norm. A single fixed computable rotation of the diffuse port distinguishes the tower from the comparison with probability gap one at every ideal stage. The rotation acts on the same named input and return domains.\n\n*The comparator and finite probes.* The comparison process offers the uniform density, receives the actual return, and outputs the bit zero. It is normal and causal. For a finite mesh $h$ let $D_h$ integrate over the dyadic cells and $I_h$ embed a label as uniform density in its cell. An ideal finite stage draws a uniform cell label $j$ from fair bits, offers it through $I_h$, and retains a private copy of $j$. It receives the actual intervention return through $D_h$, giving label $k$. Write $c_j,c_k$ for their cell centers. Given a fixed shift band $B\\subset(0,1)$, the released bit is\n$$\n\\mathbf 1\\{c_k-c_j\\pmod 1\\in B\\}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3av.1}\n$$\nAll preparation, encoding addresses, retained labels and the complete bit-comparison permutation are finite. An actual finite comparator with the same mesh and a constant output bit zero has the prescribed comparator's response on every fixed normal intervention. Indeed the average of the offered densities is exactly uniform, and its last channel traces the return. Thus all differences of outputs in the stated comparison concern the actual bit.\n\n*Selection of one nested shift.* Start with a nonempty closed interval $I_0$ inside $(1/4,3/4)$ with dyadic endpoints and positive length. At stage $n\\ge1$, choose more than $n2^{n+2}$ pairwise disjoint closed dyadic bands inside the interior of $I_{n-1}$. Each contains in its interior a closed dyadic child interval of positive length and diameter at most $2^{-n}$. Choose one common dyadic cell mesh $h_n\\le2^{-n}$ so that $h_n$ is smaller than the distance from each child to the complement of its band. All these choices use only dyadic endpoints and can be computed.\n\nFor any fixed menu instrument the probabilities of a one in (O.7.2.3av.1) for these different bands sum to at most one. This is the disjointness of the bands on the same offered and returned label table. Therefore some band has the sum of its probabilities for the first $n$ instruments less than $2^{-n-2}$. Each such sum is computable uniformly by the hypothesis. Search with strict rational error bounds and select a band for which the sum is less than $2^{-n}$, which terminates by the strict slack. Emit that band's finite circuit at mesh $h_n$. Set $I_n$ equal to the selected child. The nested closed intervals have diameters at most $2^{-n}$ and determine a unique computable $\\alpha\\in(1/4,3/4)$.\n\n*Complete menu convergence and failure under a fixed rotation.* For a listed instrument, sum its full tester output over both bit values. This gives the prescribed comparator's tester output, because every returned block is merely assigned to one of the two bit labels and then traced in that sum. The bit-one part is positive. If it has trace $p_n$, replacing it by bit zero changes the joint classical-bit and tester-output state by $2p_n$ in predual norm. This includes all fixed spectator and tester registers, regardless of correlations. For every listed instrument and every $n$ beyond its index, $p_n\\le2^{-n}$. Thus its complete output converges to the comparator's, not merely its bit marginal.\n\nNow use the single normal rotation $K_\\alpha$ on $L^1(\\mathbb T,dx)$, taking an offered coordinate $x$ to the returned coordinate $x+\\alpha$. In the finite comparison its actual compression is $D_{h_n}K_\\alpha I_{h_n}$. The entries of this channel are computable overlap lengths of intervals. For every offered and returned cell pair of positive probability, the circular distance from the difference of their centers to $\\alpha$ is at most $h_n$. By the child-to-band margin, this difference lies in the selected band. Equation (O.7.2.3av.1) therefore gives one with probability one at every ideal stage. The comparison gives zero. The stage algorithm has only used the supplied menu's finite tables. Its selected rotation is a computable fixed intervention of the same normal type, obtained from the shrinking intervals.\n\nThe circuits are actual finite controls in Theorem O.7.2.3m. The probability weights for the uniform offered label use finitely many fair bits. The band tests use a finite table on the cell pairs. All label and seed copies, pulse implementations, adaptive routing and positive clock intervals are supplied and counted. If ideal Hermitian coefficients are approximated by dyadics, allocate a complete stage error tending to zero. The menu convergence and the unit limiting probability gap then follow by contraction. No history or feedback outcome is replaced by a separate classical sampler. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723aw\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723aw-external-tests-for-dominated-diffuse-feedback\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3aw (External Tests for Dominated Diffuse Feedback).** Use a single offered and returned classical port on \\(\\mathbb T\\) as in Theorem O.7.2.3av, with independent uniform offer and product reference measure \\(\\lambda=dx\\,dy\\) on \\(\\mathbb T^2\\). The prescribed final decoder releases a binary record with probability \\(f(x,y)\\) of one, where \\(f:\\mathbb T^2\\to[0,1]\\) is measurable. The decoder acts only on the offered and returned coordinates and leaves the tester's other outputs alone. A stage decoder \\(f_n\\) is a finite table on product dyadic cells, with dyadic entries in \\([0,1]\\), implemented by the corresponding conditional bit preparation.\n\nThe compared interventions have a full tester-output law on the two coordinates given by a positive Bochner-integrable trace-class density \\(R(x,y)\\) relative to \\(\\lambda\\), with \\(\\int\\operatorname{Tr}R\\,d\\lambda=1\\). The density includes the tester's classical outcomes, retained quantum registers and any fixed spectator. Its first-coordinate trace marginal is uniform. The complete terminal output for the decoder is the two-block state\n\\[\n\\Xi_f(R)=\n\\left(\\int(1-f)R\\,d\\lambda\\right)\\oplus\n\\left(\\int fR\\,d\\lambda\\right).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3aw.1}\n\\]\n\n1. For any sequence of stage decoders \\(f_n\\), convergence of \\(\\int_A f_n\\,d\\lambda\\) for every product dyadic rectangle \\(A\\) yields a unique decoder \\(f\\) up to \\(\\lambda\\)-null sets with those limiting integrals. These rectangular limits are equivalent to weak-* convergence \\(f_n\\to f\\) in \\(L^\\infty(\\lambda)\\), and to trace-norm convergence of (O.7.2.3aw.1) for every dominated tester. The latter convergence is uniform on each compact set of allowed densities in the Bochner \\(L^1\\) norm. This includes a translation tester with independent noise of one fixed \\(L^1\\) density, uniformly in its translation parameter.\n2. A uniform finite source tower converging to the prescribed decoder on this class exists if and only if the rectangular masses \\(\\int_A f\\,d\\lambda\\) are uniformly limit-computable over the product dyadic rectangles. Here a limit-computable name is one algorithm returning a rational sequence for each rectangle, with its stated limit and with no convergence modulus required.\n\n*Proof.* The rectangular experiments are physical testers. Receive the offered coordinate, record its membership in a specified dyadic cell, discard that input and prepare an independent uniform return, with a second flag for its membership in a specified dyadic cell. Uniform return with a fixed dyadic flag is realized by a finite fair cell choice followed by the stipulated uniform cell encoding. The joint event that both flags and the decoder output equal one has probability \\(\\int_A f\\,d\\lambda\\) for the corresponding rectangle \\(A\\). Coarser tester and finer decoder cells use the same nested coordinate grids. Their finite tables and probabilities use exact dyadic arithmetic.\n\nThe linear span of rectangle indicators is dense in \\(L^1(\\lambda)\\), and \\(0\\le f_n\\le1\\). Thus the convergent rectangular integrals define a bounded linear functional on \\(L^1(\\lambda)\\), by approximation. On nonnegative tests its values lie between zero and the integral of the test. The \\(L^1\\) representation by its \\(L^\\infty\\) dual yields a unique representative \\(f\\) up to null sets with \\(0\\le f\\le1\\). This also proves weak-* convergence. For a simple trace-class density \\(R=\\sum_i \\mathbf1_{E_i}R_i\\) the finitely many scalar weak-* tests yield \\(\\int(f_n-f)R\\to0\\) in trace norm. For arbitrary Bochner-integrable \\(R\\) approximate by such a simple function in \\(L^1\\) norm. Since \\(|f_n-f|\\le1\\), the error in this integral is bounded by the approximation error. The two output blocks have opposite differences, so their joint difference has trace norm \\(2\\|\\int(f_n-f)R\\|_1\\). A finite \\(L^1\\) net proves uniformity on compact families. For fixed translation-noise density \\(g\\), the law \\(R_\\alpha(x,y)=g(y-x-\\alpha)\\) is continuous in \\(L^1\\) as a function of \\(\\alpha\\) by \\(L^1\\) translation continuity on the circle. Its parameter space is compact. Conversely the physical rectangular testers recover the scalar rectangular limits by measuring the joint flags.\n\nNecessity of the name condition follows by computing the rectangular tester probabilities at every stage of a uniform tower. For sufficiency enumerate product dyadic rectangles as \\(A_1,A_2,\\dots\\), and let \\(r_{j,n}\\in[0,1]\\) be clipped rational approximations converging for each fixed \\(j\\) to \\(\\int_{A_j}f\\,d\\lambda\\). At stage \\(n\\), refine the grid until it resolves all \\(A_j\\) with \\(j\\le n\\) and has depth at least \\(n\\). Among the finite tables \\(g\\) with values in \\(\\{0,2^{-n},\\dots,1\\}\\) on that grid, minimize\n\\[\n\\max_{1\\le j\\le n}2^{-j}\n\\left|\\int_{A_j}g\\,d\\lambda-r_{j,n}\\right|\n\\tag{O.7.2.3aw.2}\n\\]\nwith a fixed order for ties. Every objective value is rational and the exhaustive search terminates. Rounding the actual cell means of \\(f\\) gives a comparison table whose error in each rectangle integral is at most \\(2^{-n}\\). Its objective tends to zero: each fixed index's error tends to zero and the weight bounds the tail uniformly. The chosen table therefore converges on each fixed rectangle. Item 1 gives the asserted complete tester comparison.\n\nThe source emits a uniformly chosen offered cell, retaining its label privately. It encodes that cell by the stipulated uniform density and decodes the actual intervention return into its returned cell. A finite fair register and a reversible controlled table produce the selected bit with the dyadic probability of that cell pair. Its physical clock, source address, held labels, fair registers and outcome route belong to the finite control grammar of Theorem O.7.2.3m. Coherent tester registers pass through without entering the table. These operations implement (O.7.2.3aw.1) for the prescribed external comparison. \\(\\square\\)\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723ax\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723ax-uniform-external-approximation-of-diffuse-feedback\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3ax (Uniform External Approximation of Diffuse Feedback).** Retain the one-turn uniform offer, classical return and binary terminal decoder of Theorem O.7.2.3aw. Identify the circle with \\([0,1)\\) and use the canonical half-open dyadic cells. Let \\(D\\) be the closure, in the pointwise supremum norm on the circle, of the real dyadic step functions on the returned coordinate. Give each element of \\(D\\) its uniform-limit representative, including at the dyadic boundaries. Take the measurable binary decoder \\(f\\), with \\(0\\le f\\le1\\), in the Bochner space \\(L^1(dx;D)\\). Thus the offered coordinate is identified up to a Lebesgue-null set and the returned-coordinate representative is fixed. For a finite dyadic decoder \\(g\\), write\n\\[\n\\|f-g\\|_{\\mathrm{mix}}=\\int_0^1\\sup_{0\\le y<1}|f(x,y)-g(x,y)|\\,dx.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3ax.1}\n\\]\n\nAllow every fixed normal feedback tester on the given offered and returned \\(L^1\\) ports, with the full tester output, including a retained binary flag or a fixed spectator. A tester's joint offer–return law may be singular relative to \\(dx\\,dy\\). Its first marginal is the uniform offer and a normal returned port has an absolutely continuous unconditional returned marginal. On a scalar conditional joint law the terminal decoder performs the prescribed Bernoulli trial with parameter \\(f(x,y)\\). On a positive operator-valued tester law it multiplies the two outcome measures by \\(1-f,f\\) while leaving the tester registers untouched. Then the supremum over allowed testers of the complete terminal trace distance, using the norm \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_1\\) without the factor \\(1/2\\), is\n\\[\n2\\|f-g\\|_{\\mathrm{mix}}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3ax.2}\n\\]\nFor the lower bound it suffices to use testers whose joint laws are absolutely continuous and whose only extra output is a classical binary flag. The upper bound also applies to arbitrary joint laws with uniform first marginal and positive retained tester measures.\n\nFor this decoder class, a uniform finite tower of dyadic decoder stages converging uniformly over the above testers exists exactly when \\(f\\) has a uniform limit-computable name in (O.7.2.3ax.1). Such a name is a single algorithm producing finite dyadic tables with dyadic probabilities in \\([0,1]\\) converging in that norm, with no convergence modulus required. Tables of this kind are dense among the stated \\([0,1]\\)-valued decoders. The source clocks and paid implementation are those of Theorem O.7.2.3aw.\n\n*Proof.* Put \\(h=f-g\\). Choose an everywhere defined representative for each of \\(f,g\\) outside one fixed offer-null set, and assign any admissible decoder on that set. The joint tester law has the uniform first marginal, so this choice affects no output. For any tester, multiplying the retained positive output measure by \\(h\\) gives a signed output with trace norm bounded by the integral of \\(|h|\\) against the scalar trace measure. This follows first on simple multipliers from the triangle inequality and positivity, and extends to bounded measurable multipliers by integration. The two bit blocks have opposite signs, and the scalar trace measure has first marginal \\(dx\\). Thus the complete norm is at most \\(2\\int\\sup_y|h(x,y)|dx\\).\n\nFor the lower bound, every \\(u\\in D\\) is right-continuous on \\([0,1)\\) because its approximating half-open dyadic step functions are right-continuous and the convergence is uniform. For each fixed \\(x\\) outside the offer-null set, the supremum in (O.7.2.3ax.1) can therefore be taken over a fixed countable dense set of dyadic return points. For \\(\\epsilon>0\\), enumerate these points and choose the first one where \\(|h(x,y)|>\\sup_z|h(x,z)|-\\epsilon\\). This choice is measurable since all point evaluations on \\(D\\) are continuous. Choose the first sufficiently short dyadic interval with left endpoint at that point on which the average of \\(h(x,\\cdot)\\) differs from its value at the point by less than \\(\\epsilon\\). The integral average is measurable in \\(x\\), and the right-continuity guarantees such intervals even at dyadic boundaries. A normal tester observes the offered \\(x\\), sets its binary flag to the sign of the chosen value of \\(h\\), and returns a uniform draw in the chosen interval. The countable measurable branch partition defines a positive trace-preserving map on the \\(L^1\\) port: on each branch integrate the input density and prepare the named interval's uniform density with its flag. Its joint law has a density relative to \\(dx\\,dy\\), since all return intervals have positive length. No computability assumption on \\(f,g\\) or on this optimizing tester is required for the supremum over normal testers.\n\nKeep the flag in the terminal output. With the flag taking signed values \\(\\sigma\\in\\{-1,1\\}\\), the expectation of \\(\\sigma h\\) under the tester law is at least \\(\\int\\sup_y|h(x,y)|dx-2\\epsilon\\). Measuring the diagonal observable with value \\(\\sigma\\) on decoder output one and \\(-\\sigma\\) on decoder output zero gives a difference of twice this expectation. Its norm is one. Hence the output trace norm is at least twice the stated bound, and letting \\(\\epsilon\\) decrease to zero proves (O.7.2.3ax.2).\n\nFor density, \\(D\\) is separable because finite rational dyadic step functions are dense in it. Approximate \\(f\\) in Bochner \\(L^1\\) by finite simple functions with values in \\(D\\). Approximate each value uniformly by a dyadic step table and each measurable offer set in measure by finite dyadic unions. Refine to common grids and clip entries to \\([0,1]\\), which cannot increase distance to \\(f\\). Dyadic rounding gives the required finite probability tables. A limit-computable name supplies their paid clocked realizations through Theorem O.7.2.3aw, including the fresh fair-bit stock and the actual conditional bit table. Equation (O.7.2.3ax.2) gives uniform convergence. Conversely the emitted tables of any such uniform tower provide a mixed-norm name by the same equality. \\(\\square\\)\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723ay\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723ay-causal-external-norm-for-several-diffuse-feedback-intervals\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3ay (Causal External Norm for Several Diffuse Feedback Intervals).** Fix \\(r\\ge1\\) offered and returned \\(L^1(\\mathbb T)\\) port pairs on a declared finite clock schedule. On round \\(j\\) the source supplies a fresh uniform coordinate \\(x_j\\), independent of its complete earlier history and of the tester's earlier registers, and receives \\(y_j\\) after the tester observes the offer. It retains the full coordinate history privately. It releases one binary decoder output at the end, with conditional probability \\(f(x_1,y_1,\\ldots,x_r,y_r)\\) of one. It makes no earlier decoder-dependent release. The tester may keep arbitrary normal memory, retained output registers and a fixed spectator, and it can read the offered and returned history. Require causal normal operations, so the return at round \\(j\\) cannot depend on future offers and each returned coordinate has a normal unconditional marginal. Include the complete tester output with the terminal bit.\n\nUse the canonical half-open dyadic coordinate cells of Theorem O.7.2.3ax. Let \\(D_r\\) be the uniform closure of the finite dyadic step functions of the \\(r\\) returned coordinates, with their pointwise representatives. Let the \\([0,1]\\)-valued \\(f,g\\) lie in \\(L^1(dx_1\\cdots dx_r;D_r)\\). Denote by \\(\\|\\cdot\\|_{\\mathrm{caus}}\\) the continuous extension from finite dyadic tables of\n\\[\n\\|h\\|_{\\mathrm{caus}}\n=\\int dx_1\\,\\sup_{y_1}\\int dx_2\\,\\sup_{y_2}\\cdots\n     \\int dx_r\\,\\sup_{y_r}|h(x_1,y_1,\\ldots,x_r,y_r)|.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3ay.1}\n\\]\nFor finite tables this is a finite sequence of averages and maxima on the nested dyadic cells. The extension uses the dominating norm \\(\\int \\sup_{(y_1,\\ldots,y_r)}|h|\\,dx_1\\cdots dx_r\\). Then the exact worst-case trace-norm discrepancy of the complete terminal outputs, with no factor of \\(1/2\\) in the norm, is\n\\[\n\\sup_{\\mathcal T\\ {\\rm causal,\\ normal}}\n\\bigl\\|\\Xi_f(\\mathcal T)-\\Xi_g(\\mathcal T)\\bigr\\|_1\n=2\\|f-g\\|_{\\mathrm{caus}}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3ay.2}\n\\]\nFinite dyadic input testers with a retained sign flag suffice to take the supremum. A uniform tower of finite dyadic terminal decoders in this clocked source grammar realizes the prescribed outputs uniformly over causal normal testers exactly when \\(f\\) has a limit-computable name in (O.7.2.3ay.1). Such a name is one algorithm emitting finite dyadic tables with probabilities in \\([0,1]\\) converging in that norm. No convergence modulus is required.\n\n*Proof.* First take \\(h=f-g\\) to be a finite dyadic table on all the named coordinates. Forgetting the final tester records leaves a scalar joint law of the coordinates, with a fresh independent uniform offer at every round. Even for a quantum tester, conditioning this scalar law on the preceding coordinate history leaves the same uniform next offer. The return is a conditional probability law on the available cells, potentially depending on the whole preceding history and the current offer. The two terminal bit blocks have opposite signed differences. Positivity bounds their joint trace norm by twice the scalar expectation of \\(|h|\\). Backward conditioning at the last return bounds that expectation by the maximal final-cell value, then averages over the fresh uniform offer. Iteration gives the upper bound (O.7.2.3ay.1). The same argument uses only the causal scalar law and positivity of the retained tester measure, and so allows singular joint laws and spectators.\n\nFor equality on finite tables, compute the backward finite arrays in (O.7.2.3ay.1). At round \\(j\\) a tester records the offered cell, selects a maximizing return cell using the earlier labels and the present offer, and supplies the uniform density on that cell. This is a normal operation on the same port, implemented by the actual dyadic encode and return service. Selecting the first maximizer fixes the table in case of ties. The history is retained. At the end, the tester puts the sign of \\(h\\) at the attained cell tuple in a binary flag, with either choice at zero. The flag is computed without reading the final decoder output. The diagonal observable which equals the flag sign when the final bit is one and its opposite when the final bit is zero has operator norm one. Its expectation changes by \\(2\\,\\mathbb E|h|\\) between the two decoders. The selected normal tester therefore attains the upper bound. The same finite tester computes the sign before reading the source's terminal bit, and all its needed memories consist of the actual finite coordinate labels.\n\nDyadic tables are dense in \\(L^1(dx_1\\cdots dx_r;D_r)\\). Use simple functions in the offer tuple, approximate their offer sets by finite dyadic unions in product measure and their values uniformly by return-coordinate dyadic tables, and refine to common grids. For \\([0,1]\\)-valued decoders, clip and dyadically round the values. For a general decoder, the first-coordinate tuple marginal of every tester is the product of uniform offers. Thus replacing a decoder by an approximating table changes its complete output norm, uniformly in the tester, by at most twice their difference in the dominating norm. Also the absolute difference between the causal norms of two finite tables is at most their difference in that norm, by the triangle inequality and the nested averages and maxima. This proves that (O.7.2.3ay.1) extends uniquely and that (O.7.2.3ay.2) follows from finite-table equality. For instance all evaluations on the exceptional product-null set of offer tuples are immaterial to this comparison. The canonical return-coordinate representatives keep evaluations under normal singular feedback well defined. The extended quantity is a norm on the stated offer-almost-everywhere equivalence classes: it dominates the integral of \\(|h|\\) against independent uniform returns, and a return profile in \\(D_r\\) with a nonzero point value has positive-measure right-neighborhoods with values bounded away from zero.\n\nThe source implements any dyadic table on the finite clock schedule by an independent fair cell choice at every offer, with actual uniform cell encoding and a private held copy, followed by recording the actual returned cell of each intervention. The terminal bit uses fresh fair bits and a reversible controlled comparison on the full finite history tuple. Every source, archive, pointer, route, fresh fair register and positive-time slot is included in the finite control grammar of Theorem O.7.2.3m and the dyadic realization of Theorem O.7.2.3aw. The decoder changes neither the offers nor the preceding tester dynamics. Equation (O.7.2.3ay.2) gives sufficiency for the emitted tables of a limit-computable name and necessity for the tables of every uniformly convergent finite source tower of this type.\n\nFor strict separation from the norm that allows all return choices after seeing every offer, take \\(r=2\\), a dyadic number \\(m\\) of equal cells, and \\(h=1\\) exactly when \\(y_1\\) and the later \\(x_2\\) lie in the same cell. This gives causal norm \\(1/m\\), since \\(x_2\\) is independent of the whole first-round history, whereas the norm with the joint returned-coordinate supremum after both offer coordinates has value one. For \\(r=1\\) the formula is (O.7.2.3ax.2). \\(\\square\\)\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723az\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723az-exact-causal-comparison-with-adapted-offers-and-interim-releases\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3az (Exact Causal Comparison with Adapted Offers and Interim Releases).** Fix a finite clocked schedule of source and tester nodes on diffuse classical offer and return ports of Theorem O.7.2.3ay. Use dyadic cells on all these ports. At a source node the source emits a finite classical label or a joint finite label and offered-cell choice, with its probability table depending on the entire earlier cell and release history \\(h\\). A chosen offer cell is encoded by the stipulated uniform density within that cell. At a tester node the tester observes the history available on the named ports and chooses a return. The source records its actual dyadic cell. It retains all past labels for future controls. Every source label and offered or returned cell is available to the tester by its corresponding cut. The tester may act by causal normal instruments with private memory, retained quantum outputs and a spectator. Two sources \\(P,Q\\) may have distinct source tables \\(P_i(\\cdot\\mid h),Q_i(\\cdot\\mid h)\\) at every source node, including adapted offers and intermediate releases. Compare the complete terminal classical releases and tester outputs in trace norm without a factor \\(1/2\\).\n\nFor any node \\(i\\), history \\(h\\), and incoming nonnegative likelihood masses \\(p,q\\), define \\(B_i(h,p,q)\\) backwards by\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nB_{\\rm leaf}(h,p,q)&=|p-q|,\\\\\nB_i(h,p,q)&=\\sum_z B_{i+1}(hz,p P_i(z\\mid h),q Q_i(z\\mid h))\n   &&\\text{at a source node},\\\\\nB_i(h,p,q)&=\\max_z B_{i+1}(hz,p,q)\n   &&\\text{at a tester node}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{O.7.2.3az.1}\n\\]\nThen \\(B_1(\\varnothing,1,1)\\) is the exact supremum of the terminal trace-norm discrepancy over causal normal testers. In particular a finite deterministic cell tester with a retained history attains it. The same value compares sources using different dyadic depths by refining to a common partition and splitting every coarse offered-cell probability in the ratios of its subcell lengths. Exact rational input tables give an exactly computable rational value.\n\nThe comparison applies to diffuse kernels with the following finite-table approximation. Fix the same schedule and ordinary Borel coordinates and releases, and let source kernels depend on their full past, without hidden quantum state. At a source node, a kernel outputs a finite label, a new offer with a density relative to Lebesgue measure, or both. The tester has the same access and normal operations as above. Let \\(\\pi_n\\) record all past dyadic cells and finite labels. Suppose the joint source-label and offer kernel at each node has a dyadic-table approximation on \\(\\pi_n h\\), with uniform encoding in its offered cells and uniform conditional \\(L^1\\) error at most \\(\\epsilon_{i,n}\\) over \\(h\\). Here conditional \\(L^1\\) is the total variation norm of signed measures without the factor \\(1/2\\). If the approximating tables are uniformly named finite dyadic probability tables, then they give paid finite sources with uniform complete terminal error at most \\(\\sum_i\\epsilon_{i,n}\\) for every causal normal tester. The errors include tests with singular offer–return laws. When two diffuse sources satisfy this condition and the sums tend to zero, their worst-case distance is the limit of (O.7.2.3az.1) for their approximating tables, with error bounded by the sum of the two source error sums.\n\n*Proof.* For the finite case, retain an auxiliary copy of the whole finite history solely for the trace-norm upper bound. Start from the common initial tester state. At each source node the probability of the new finite label multiplies the corresponding block by \\(P_i(z\\mid h)\\) or \\(Q_i(z\\mid h)\\). If an offered coordinate is included, the encoded state conditional on that finite label is identical for both sources. At tester nodes the resulting trace-class output blocks for return-cell alternatives are obtained by common completely positive operations, and their traces sum to the incoming trace. Thus at a leaf the two extended states for history \\(h\\) are \\(p_h\\sigma_h\\) and \\(q_h\\sigma_h\\), with the same positive operator \\(\\sigma_h\\) obtained by the tester's conditional action maps and conditional source encodings. Here \\(p_h,q_h\\) are the products of source probabilities only. The tester maps may retain arbitrary private registers, and the factorization holds for their entire block, including all finer-coordinate or coherent records still present. The block difference has norm \\(|p_h-q_h|\\operatorname{Tr}\\sigma_h\\). At source nodes the conditional state is normalized when the incoming tester trace is one. At tester nodes, the next-cell traces are nonnegative weights of sum one. Backwards induction bounds the weighted leaf sum by (O.7.2.3az.1), which is homogeneous under a common scaling of \\(p,q\\). Tracing out the auxiliary history cannot increase the norm, giving the upper bound even when a tester elects to forget part of the history.\n\nFor the lower bound, record all emitted labels and offered cells, compute \\(p,q\\) for the given observed history, and at a tester node choose a maximizing cell in (O.7.2.3az.1). The operation returning the uniform density in that cell is normal and acts on the stipulated named port. Retain the chosen cell. The final complete retained history now separates all leaves, giving total trace norm exactly equal to the recurrence. The choices can equivalently be precomputed on the finite history tree. Both source tables and that maximizing tester have finite implementations: use fair-bit conditionals and reversible finite controls on the retained history, the prescribed cell encodings and the actual returned-cell records. A rational source table can be approximated by a dyadic one at any specified probability error. Finite source and tester operations use positive clock slots, paid controller and routing, label and pointer stocks, and retained outcomes in the grammar of Theorem O.7.2.3m. Refining an offered cell into its subcells is an exact decomposition of the same uniform density. Return labels and history controls can then be projected to their original coarser cells, proving the common-partition statement.\n\nFor the diffuse bound, fix a tester and replace the source kernels one at a time. Just before the one replaced node the two hybrid histories have the same positive measure, with all tester states retained. Conditional on each actual past, the kernel difference in the new outcome has total variation norm at most \\(\\epsilon_{i,n}\\). Multiplying by the positive past tester state and integrating gives the same bound on the output difference. This argument uses the complete past measure and imposes no product density on that past. Subsequent common causal operations are trace-norm contractions. Summing the hybrid bounds proves the claimed comparison uniformly in the tester. Applying this argument to each source in a pair and then using (O.7.2.3az.1) proves the stated computable finite comparisons and limit bound. \\(\\square\\)\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723ba\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723ba-noncompact-jump-and-killing-sources-with-an-isolated-cemetery\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3ba (Noncompact Jump and Killing Sources with an Isolated Cemetery).** Give the killed process the recorded state space $E\\sqcup\\{\\partial\\}$ with isolated absorbing cemetery $\\partial$. Under either presentation below, the specified law has uniformly effective weak names of every finite dyadic-grid distribution and has no fixed positive-time jump.\n\n1. The live space $E$ is countable discrete with effective enumeration. Supply nonnegative computable off-diagonal rates $q(x,y)$ for live $y\\ne x$ and $q(x,\\partial)$, and a computable finite total rate $\\lambda(x)=\\sum_{y\\ne x}q(x,y)+q(x,\\partial)$ for each live $x$. Supply an effective initial probability law on $E\\sqcup\\{\\partial\\}$. Assume the minimal holding-time construction is nonexplosive before absorption and has no finite explosion on surviving paths. In particular this presentation applies when the live generator with killing is rich Feller on $C_0(E)$ and its recorded extension fails to be Feller on $C_0(E\\sqcup\\{\\partial\\})$.\n2. The live space is $[0,\\infty)$, with the state-dependent jump $x\\mapsto x+u$, $u$ uniform on $[1,2]$, at rate $1+x$, and constant killing rate $\\kappa>0$ with computable $\\kappa$. Supply a computable initial probability in the weak representation on $E\\sqcup\\{\\partial\\}$. The killed sub-Markov law on $C_0(E)$ has the closed generator determined on its core $C_c^\\infty([0,\\infty))$ by\n$$\n\\mathcal A f(x)=(1+x)\\int_1^2\\bigl(f(x+u)-f(x)\\bigr)\\,du-\\kappa f(x).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3ba.1}\n$$\nHere the core consists of restrictions of smooth compactly supported functions on the real line, and determines the stated law among strongly continuous sub-Markov semigroups on $C_0(E)$. The recorded extension with isolated $\\partial$ fails to be Feller on $C_0(E\\sqcup\\{\\partial\\})$.\n\nEquip either recorded law with the continuous finite-dimensional coefficient matrices and computable named evaluations in Theorem O.7.2.3an, including specified values at $\\partial$. Its finite sources have paid clocks, controls and archives and converge to the actual joint $J_1$ state record and coherent output, including fixed finite quantum intervention protocols on the exogenous state law and fixed spectators. Interventions changing the state law require their corresponding controlled laws.\n\n*Proof for the countable presentation.* Take an increasing finite exhaustion $F_m$ of live labels. In the auxiliary finite chain keep $\\partial$ and add a distinct absorbing label $o$. From $x\\in F_m$ keep all rates to other labels of $F_m$ and the killing rate to $\\partial$, and send the residual rate\n$$\n\\lambda(x)-q(x,\\partial)-\\sum_{\\substack{y\\in F_m\\\\y\\ne x}}q(x,y)\n$$\nto $o$. Send initial mass outside $F_m\\sqcup\\{\\partial\\}$ to $o$. Its mass is computable as one minus the finite sum of the retained initial masses, since individual labels are computable clopen tests in the discrete metric. Finite matrix exponentiation gives computable auxiliary grid distributions and the probability $b_m(T)$ that the auxiliary chain is at $o$ at time $T$. Until the first omitted live label, the auxiliary and actual holding-time laws can be coupled by using identical waiting times and mapping the same destination to $o$ on exit. Thus $b_m(T)$ is the probability that the actual process starts outside the retained labels or visits an omitted live label by time $T$. The events decrease to the empty event up to null sets, since almost every sample has only finitely many live jumps on $[0,T]$. Given $\\epsilon>0$, a dovetailed search for the certified strict inequality $b_m(T)<\\epsilon$ terminates. Decode $o$ to a fixed named state. In the bounded maximum grid metric the decoded auxiliary distribution is within $\\epsilon$ of the actual grid law by the coupling. Approximate its finite probabilities by dyadic weights to any remaining tolerance. Each finite jump and the killing time have no atom at a fixed positive time, since their finite holding times are exponential conditional on the visited states. Countably many possible jump indices suffice, and nonexplosion gives the recorded càdlàg law.\n\n*Proof for the continuous-state presentation.* Before killing construct the increasing jump chain by exponential holding times and independent uniform increments. For $x\\le m$ let $\\tau_m$ be its first entrance into $(m,\\infty)$. The stopped chain has bounded rates and stopped state bounded by $m+2$. The jump drift of $V(x)=1+x$ is at most $2V(x)$. The stopped expectation inequality and Grönwall's bound give\n$$\n\\mathbb E_x V(X_{t\\wedge\\tau_m})\\le (1+x)e^{2t},\nqquad\n\\mathbb P_x(\\tau_m\\le T)\\le\\frac{(1+x)e^{2T}}{1+m}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3ba.2}\n$$\nInfinitely many jumps on $[0,T]$ would leave every finite interval, because every live increment is at least one. The estimate gives zero probability of finite explosion. Killing uses an independent exponential time of rate $\\kappa$, with an actual jump to $\\partial$. The holding-time construction and independent killing yield a càdlàg recorded path with no atom at any fixed positive jump time.\n\nFor a continuous compactly supported $f$, the killed semigroup value vanishes outside the same upper support bound by monotonicity of the live process. On each compact set, the bounded-rate cutoff semigroup is continuous by uniformization with continuous clock-step probabilities. Estimate (O.7.2.3ba.2) gives convergence of those stopped laws uniformly for bounded initial $x$, proving continuity of the unrestricted semigroup value. For $f$ supported in $[0,M]$, the probability of any event by $t$ for $x\\in[0,M]$ is at most $(1+M+\\kappa)t$. This gives strong continuity by density of $C_c(E)$ and contractivity. For each $M$ the closed subspace $B_M$ of $C_0(E)$ functions vanishing for $x\\ge M$ is invariant. Its generator is the bounded operator (O.7.2.3ba.1) on $B_M$, by a uniform first-event expansion for $x\\le M$ and the rate bound for the first two events before or upon leaving this interval. The union of the $B_M$ is a core: for any positive resolvent parameter the range of the parameter minus the generator on that union contains the union, by the invariant semigroup resolvent on each $B_M$. This range is dense, and the bounded full resolvent turns the range approximation into graph-norm approximation. Each $f\\in B_M$ can in turn be uniformly approximated by restrictions of smooth functions supported below $M+1$, with convergence in graph norm by boundedness on $B_{M+1}$. This proves the asserted rich core and the semigroup uniqueness. For the recorded extension, the semigroup applied to the $C_0$ test $1_{\\{\\partial\\}}$ has live value $1-e^{-\\kappa t}$ for every $x$ and $t>0$, which fails to vanish at infinity.\n\nFor effectivity, the initial weak name provides arbitrarily accurate finite dyadic approximants in a bounded computable metric, with $\\partial$ at distance one from live states. It also gives an effective upper tail bound: search for a continuous computable function taking values in $[0,1]$, equal to one on $\\{\\partial\\}\\cup[0,M-1]$ and zero on $[M,\\infty)$, whose initial expectation exceeds $1-\\epsilon$. Expectations of these bounded computably Lipschitz tests are computable, and the search terminates by monotone convergence. Multiply the movement rate by a computable piecewise-linear cutoff equal to one on $[0,m]$ and zero on $[m+1,\\infty)$, with $m>M$. Keep the independent killing rate, equivalently include it in the finite-rate uniformization. The localized and actual laws agree under a coupling until a live entrance into $(m,\\infty)$ or killing. For every initial live $x\\le M$, (O.7.2.3ba.2) bounds the exit probability by $(1+M)e^{2T}/(1+m)$. With the initial tail bound this gives any prescribed uniform error on the finite grid.\n\nFor completeness, the localized law has effective grid names without assuming an initial sampler or a semigroup name. Truncate the computable Poisson clock count on $[0,T]$ by a certified tail bound. On a fixed grid, clock counts per grid cell are independent Poisson variables with computable parameters. For each retained count word use its finite clock-step kernel, at each live state a mixture of killing, a uniform increment and no movement, with computable continuous weights. Compactly truncate the initial live distribution by a continuous clamp $x\\mapsto\\min(x,M)$, leaving $\\partial$ fixed, at cost at most the already bounded initial tail. The clamp is computably Lipschitz in a bounded metric obtained by truncating Euclidean distance. All finite count words then remain in a computable compact interval together with $\\partial$. Use finite weak approximants to the clamped initial law and rational quadrature for the uniform increments. For each retained count word the continuous clock-step weights on this compact set have computable moduli, as do finite compositions and products of the weights. A finite coupling by nearby initial states and uniform draws has grid error bounded by their displacement and the sum of the clock-step mixture-weight errors. Thus the finite quadratures provide certified dyadic weak names of the localized grid law. Removing the truncations with the preceding bounds computes the actual grid laws. A noncompact recorded space falls outside Theorem O.7.2.3ac. The failure of the recorded $C_0$-Feller property also rules out Theorem O.7.2.3ae and the sufficient conservative $C_0$-core construction in Theorem O.7.2.3an for this space and law.\n\nIn both presentations the grid-law and jump-time premises of Theorem O.7.2.3an now hold. Its joint law and coherent estimates apply to the same recorded path, with the cemetery treated as an ordinary absorbing state label. The proof gives no controlled grid law for classical interventions that change movement or killing, and places no claim on independently prescribed coherent histories. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o723ba1\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o723ba1-effective-killed-cores-and-recorded-survival\"></a>**Corollary O.7.2.3ba.1 (Effective Killed Cores and Recorded Survival).** Use the effective \\(C_0(E)\\) presentation, graph-core data, bump covers and initial-law names of Theorem O.7.2.3an, now for the actual live sub-Markov semigroup of a càdlàg killed process. Include the isolated absorbing cemetery in the state record and suppose there is no jump at any fixed positive dyadic time. On this source the recorded dyadic-grid laws have uniform effective weak names if and only if the live survival probabilities at the dyadic times, including time zero, are uniformly computable. Under that condition the joint path and coherent realization of Theorem O.7.2.3an applies to the recorded law.\n\n*Proof.* The live semigroup is a positive contraction on \\(C_0(E)\\). The resolvent search and the compound-Poisson error in Theorem O.7.2.3ah use the contraction property and the actual graph core. The same proof therefore computes the live sub-Markov semigroup on named \\(C_0\\) tests. In particular (O.7.2.3an.1) computes expectations of products of such tests at named live times when a cemetery value is assigned zero to each factor.\n\nEquip the recorded space with the bounded metric that places the cemetery at distance one from every live point. Its indicator is computably Lipschitz. Its expectation under a one-time grid marginal computes the probability of death at that time, proving necessity.\n\nFor sufficiency, fix a finite grid. In each possible record, a live prefix is followed by a suffix consisting entirely of cemetery labels. For a nonempty prefix use any finite product \\(F\\) of bump weights in the live coordinates, each in \\([0,1]\\). For a full live record its expectation is already computable. For a prefix ending just before time \\(t_j\\), the corresponding atom weight with death at \\(t_j\\) is \\(\\mathbb E F-\\mathbb E[F\\,\\mathbf1_{\\{X_{t_j}\\in E\\}}]\\). To compute the second term, replace the indicator by a finite coverage function \\(b_N=1-\\prod_{i\\le N}(1-\\varphi_i)\\) from the effective bump list at \\(t_j\\). The error is at most the computable difference \\(\\mathbb P(X_{t_j}\\in E)-\\mathbb E[b_N(X_{t_j})]\\), which decreases to zero by monotone convergence. Strict interval comparison finds a sufficiently accurate test. The empty-prefix case has weight \\(1-\\mathbb P(X_{t_1}\\in E)\\).\n\nChoose bump centers with mesh at most the requested spatial tolerance. For each live coordinate use the weights \\(\\psi_i=\\varphi_i\\prod_{k<i}(1-\\varphi_k)\\) of Theorem O.7.2.3an. The just-computed products and death weights assign the grid record to finite words of those centers followed by the actual cemetery label. The total assigned mass tends to one as the common number of centers increases. Search for a finite list with assigned mass greater than one minus the requested tolerance, assign the unallocated mass to a fixed named word, and round the finite probabilities to dyadic weights. The resulting coupling has bounded maximum-coordinate error from the spatial mesh, the unallocated mass, and the rounding. All three can be made arbitrarily small with a terminating algorithm. The recorded law and its own survival probabilities therefore supply the effective grid input to Theorem O.7.2.3an. \\(\\square\\)\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bb\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bb-causal-mode-and-reset-control-of-a-noncompact-killing-source\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3bb (Causal Mode and Reset Control of a Noncompact Killing Source).** Use the state space $S=[0,\\infty)\\sqcup\\{\\partial\\}$ of Theorem O.7.2.3ba with its isolated cemetery, and a computable initial weak law. Fix a finite library of modes and commands and a declared initial mode. In mode $a$ the live state jumps by a uniform increment on $[1,2]$ at rate $b_a(1+x)$ and jumps to $\\partial$ at rate $\\kappa_a$, with computable nonnegative $b_a,\\kappa_a$. The cemetery is absorbing. A command $c$ selects a mode by the declared finite table and applies a computable continuous reset $R_c:S\\to S$ with $R_c(\\partial)=\\partial$. The finite-dimensional coherent coefficients are continuous in the state in each mode and have computable evaluations at the named labels.\n\nAt distinct positive dyadic checkpoints, install any fixed finite protocol of instruments on the declared system and external control wires. At a checkpoint the actual preintervention state is released, the instrument runs, and its finite classical outcome is recorded and selects the command applied before the next interval. The recorded path value at the checkpoint is the postreset state. An instrument may depend on a finite list of exposed state releases at fixed dyadic times, including preintervention releases at its checkpoint, and on prior outcomes. For each prior outcome word require diamond-norm continuity in those state arguments of the complete finite-outcome instrument. State-independent fixed finite instruments are included. For an instrument supplied by the finite source itself, the named table evaluations are computable. A fixed instrument supplied at the declared external port is used there on the finite labels. A spectator may be kept through the protocol.\n\nThere is one uniform family of finite source transition and command controls, independent of the protocol's instrument values, with paid clocks and archives. At sufficiently fine stages each checkpoint and requested readout of a fixed protocol is a tick boundary. For every such protocol and every fixed dyadic horizon, the complete outcome word and state record converge jointly, with the path in $J_1$, together with the coherent histories and final system in the Q/QJ tests. This includes interventions whose actual quantum outcomes change subsequent movement rates, killing rates and the state coordinate itself.\n\n*Construction.* Use the live-label lattice of a finite interval $[0,M_n]$, a cemetery label, a clock mesh $h_n$ and a state accuracy $\\eta_n$. Choose $M_n\\to\\infty$, $\\eta_n\\to0$, and dyadic $h_n\\downarrow0$ with a growing horizon and $h_n$ times the maximum retained total rate at most one. Outside the retained interval use an archived overflow label with a declared cemetery decode. Obtain the initial label law from the effective weak name by a finite dyadic approximation and rounding; choose the retained intervals and accuracy so that these decoded initial laws converge weakly to the specified law. This requires no sampler for the original law.\n\nUse a fresh conditional row at each tick. In mode $a$ and live label $y$ the row chooses movement with probability $h_nb_a(1+y)$, killing with probability $h_n\\kappa_a$ and otherwise stays. For movement use a finite dyadic uniform increment and round the result to a state label with error at most $\\eta_n$ in the retained range. For each command compute its reset at the current named label to the requested accuracy and round it in the same way, using a rational approximation before rounding to avoid an equality test on computable reals. The separated cemetery component can be identified from the reset name before rounding. Use the exact cemetery value on the cemetery label. Movement and reset entries above the retained range use overflow. The total variation error in the finite row probabilities can be made at most $\\eta_n h_n$ with a finite dyadic approximation. Each row is normalized, including overflow. The protocol runs the installed instrument on its actual incoming state and released values, and the actual outcome is the input to the command table. No externally generated word or future command is an input to a transition row. For every finite table use the conditional normalized coherent ticks and fresh flags of Theorem O.7.2.3ac within each clock cell, with refinements and pulse errors chosen for total conditional history-isometry error tending uniformly to zero at that stage. Theorem O.7.2.3m implements the row selection, releases, command routing, flags and quantum pulses with positive paid subslots. Retain each sampler seed, prior label, selected mode, reset and overflow record in the finite archive. The released state paths use step decoding, with the reset value at its boundary.\n\n*Conditional path comparison.* Fix a finite word of instrument outcomes and temporarily apply its commands as a deterministic schedule in both laws. This supplies a reference path measure for each branch. Nonexplosion on every segment follows by applying the stopped estimate in Theorem O.7.2.3ba with exponent $2b_a t$ and an independent killing clock. The finitely many resets each give a finite live value or the cemetery, so the reference law has almost surely a finite number of events and bounded live range on a fixed horizon. Its random movement and killing times have no atoms at any deterministic positive checkpoint or readout time.\n\nCouple the initial states in the bounded compatible metric, so that their distance tends to zero in probability. For clarity first stop a comparison on a fixed live compact interval and after a fixed number of movement events. On an interval where both states are live and close, couple the three choices of the finite row to the exact event type in that cell. If the exact state at the beginning of the cell is $x$, the probabilities for first movement or first killing in a cell are their instantaneous rates times $h_n$ with errors $O(h_n^2)$, uniformly on the fixed compact interval. The chance of two events is $O(h_n^2)$ there: after a first live jump the state is at most two units higher, so the first two rates have a common finite bound. Include this second-event chance in the coupling error. The exact type and the finite row type can therefore be matched except with conditional probability at most\n$$\nC h_n^2+C h_n|x-y|+\\eta_nh_n,\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bb.1}\n$$\nwith a compact-dependent constant $C$, by the Lipschitz dependence of the stated rates. When movements match, couple the true uniform increment to its finite quantile approximation. The conditional law of the first increment given the first event is uniform; this coupling may be drawn before treating a second event as failure. A successful movement increases the label error by at most the increment-quantization and rounding errors. Successful killing puts both coordinates at $\\partial$. At a command the continuous reset on the compact interval carries a vanishing incoming error to a vanishing outgoing error, including the isolated cemetery case. On a compact domain its image is compact. Because the cemetery is isolated, sufficiently close images in this comparison either both lie in the live component or are both the cemetery. With finitely many resets and a bounded number of random jumps, these statements give a deterministic error tending to zero on the successful comparison from sufficiently close initial states. The sum of (O.7.2.3bb.1) over the cells consequently tends to zero. Take a slightly larger compact comparison interval than the chosen true live range. On successful histories the finite labels stay there and avoid overflow for all sufficiently fine stages. Let the compact range and event bound exhaust the reference law to remove the stops.\n\nOn a successful comparison, the movements and killing of the exact path can be aligned with their next tick boundaries. For almost every reference path the finitely many random events are separated from each other and from each fixed checkpoint, readout and horizon boundary. At sufficiently fine mesh this alignment preserves their order and does not move a random event onto one of those boundaries. The piecewise-linear time change fixes all deterministic boundaries and moves each matched event by at most $h_n$. It aligns any deterministic reset at its exact time. The resulting uniform coordinate error tends to zero in probability under the couplings. Thus the complete decoded path converges in $J_1$ and the actual pre- and postintervention releases converge at each requested checkpoint. This handles the deterministic reset even when its jump would make an unrestricted endpoint sample argument insufficient.\n\n*Outcome and coherent comparison.* On each forced word, compare the unnormalized composed branch maps. At a checkpoint use the trace-nonincreasing completely positive map for the specified outcome of its actual instrument, with the corresponding exposed releases as arguments. Between checkpoints use the actual coefficient-driven history map, including the output system and all declared coherent registers. Before multiplying the branch maps, the reference probability of the path is the deterministic-schedule law just compared. Integrating the product of unnormalized maps against that law gives exactly the corresponding actual branch output. Indeed the prefix law up to each instrument depends only on previous commands, and the next transition uses fresh draws whose conditional law is that of its selected mode. Conditioning chronologically and retaining the unnormalized instrument outputs gives the branch formula. The same factorization holds for the finite source. The deterministic-schedule laws provide the branch integrals, while the actual instrument selects its outcome and command.\n\nOn the coupled paths of a branch, the state-coefficient paths on each open interval between interventions converge in $L^1$ in probability. This follows from the aligning time changes, uniform continuity along a compact limiting path range, and the absence of fixed random jumps at the interval boundaries. The isometry estimates of Theorem O.7.2.3an then give norm convergence in probability of the conditional interval histories. State releases converge in probability, so the installed outcome maps converge in diamond norm by the stated continuity. For an externally installed instrument the same continuity applies to its actual finite-label inputs. For internal finite approximants use complete-channel errors tending to zero. Telescoping the finite sequence of contractions yields convergence in probability in trace norm of the unnormalized branch output on each fixed input corner and spectator. All these outputs have norm at most one on density inputs. Multiply by any bounded continuous test of the full path and take expectations under the couplings to obtain trace-norm convergence of the tested branch outputs. Summing over the finitely many words proves the joint probability and output assertion, including the outcome registers. The unweighted limit is normalized because each installed instrument is complete, and convergence of its finite-input Choi integrals transfers the finite-rank history tail tests as in Theorem O.7.2.3an. The weak path limits supply the remaining Polish-record tightness tests of Theorem O.7.2.3z. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bc\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bc-effective-singular-speed-diffusions-on-the-line\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3bc (Effective Singular-Speed Diffusions on the Line).** Let $m$ be a locally finite positive Borel measure on $\\mathbb R$ with full support. For each positive integer $R$, take the piecewise linear cutoff $\\phi_R$ equal to one on $[-R,R]$, vanishing outside $[-R-1,R+1]$, and linear on the two unit collars. Supply uniformly computable finite weak measure names for $\\phi_Rm$, including their masses, and a computable weak initial probability law on $\\mathbb R$. Let $X$ be the natural-scale diffusion with Brownian-local-time speed measure $m$. It is characterized by the continuous conservative local martingale problem\n\\[\n\\mathcal A f=g\\quad\\text{where}\\quad f''=2g\\,m\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bc.1}\n\\]\non each bounded open interval, with $f$ continuous, its distributional second derivative as displayed, and $g$ continuous, stopped at interior compact subintervals. The problem is unique in law for each initial distribution. No absolutely continuous part of $m$ or differentiability of its distribution function is required.\n\nThere are uniform effective weak names of all actual finite dyadic-grid laws, and of the dyadic transition row from a computably named starting point. For the continuous finite-dimensional coefficients of Theorem O.7.2.3an, supplied with computable evaluations at named states, its finite Hermitian-control grammar gives one paid source tower with continuous polygonal state convergence and the full joint state-path and coherent outputs of that theorem. It includes fixed finite quantum interventions on the autonomous state law, with the declared spectators and clock and resource records. The construction uses neither named spatial transition moduli nor a supplied effective graph core.\n\n*Local-time construction and uniqueness.* Let $B_s$ be Brownian motion with the specified initial law, with jointly continuous local time $L_s^x$ normalized by\n\n$$\n\\int L_s^x f(x)\\,dx=\\int_0^s f(B_v)\\,dv.\n$$\n\nSet\n\\[\nA_s=\\int L_s^x\\,m(dx),\\qquad C_t=A^{-1}(t),\\qquad X_t=B_{C_t}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bc.2}\n\\]\nThis is the natural-scale speed-measure construction, with ordinary Brownian motion corresponding to $m(dx)=dx$ in this convention. Its additive-functional construction and normalization are described in Cox et al. (2011, §2). On every bounded Brownian time interval, the local-time field is supported on a compact range, so $A$ is finite and continuous there. Between any two distinct times, the nonnegative continuous local-time increment has positive integral against Lebesgue measure by the occupation formula. It is positive on an open interval, to which $m$ assigns positive mass. Thus $A$ is strictly increasing. For each fixed spatial point, one-dimensional Brownian recurrence gives $L_s^x\\to\\infty$ almost surely. Fubini on a bounded interval of positive finite $m$-mass and monotone convergence give $A_s\\to\\infty$ almost surely. Thus $C$ and $X$ are continuous and conservative.\n\nFor $f''=2g\\,m$ on a bounded interval, the generalized Itô–Tanaka formula gives the drift $\\int L_{C_t}^x g(x)\\,m(dx)=\\int_0^t g(X_v)\\,dv$ while stopped inside that interval. The latter equality follows from the Stieltjes change of clock and the fact that $d_sL_s^x$ is supported where $B_s=x$. Conversely the test $f(x)=x,g=0$ forces a continuous local martingale. On a compact subinterval test a potential $f(x)=\\int |x-y|\\,m_0(dy)$, where $m_0$ is $m$ restricted to a larger compact interval in the same test domain. On the smaller subinterval $f''=2m$. Tanaka's formula and uniqueness of the continuous finite-variation part give $t=\\int L_t^y(X)m(dy)$ up to exit, with both sides stopped at that exit. The continuous local martingale time-change representation by Brownian motion gives $L_t^y(X)=L_{\\langle X\\rangle_t}^y(B)$ with the same normalization. The resulting equation identifies $\\langle X\\rangle_t$ as the inverse of (O.7.2.3bc.2). Exhausting bounded intervals gives uniqueness of the continuous conservative law. These local tests specify the generator comparison without interpreting a smooth compactly supported test as a generator-domain element for a singular speed measure.\n\n*Effective approximation of the joint Brownian clock.* Write $m_R=\\phi_Rm$, and let $\\rho_\\epsilon$ be a computable continuous nonnegative probability density supported on $[-\\epsilon,\\epsilon]$, for example the triangular density. On a rational Brownian time horizon $U>0$ define\n\\[\nA_s^R=\\int L_s^x m_R(dx),\\qquad\nA_s^{R,\\epsilon}=\\int_0^s (m_R*\\rho_\\epsilon)(B_v)\\,dv.\n\\]\nThe second clock is a computable continuous functional of a Brownian path. Its integrand is bounded, with computable evaluations and modulus from the measure name and the compact Lipschitz mollifier. The Brownian path law on $[0,U]$ is computable in the bounded uniform metric. For example its dyadic polygonal approximants have computable Gaussian finite-coordinate laws, and the Brownian bridge maximal bound on each cell and the union bound give a computable uniform-norm probability error. A computable initial law is inserted by adding an independent initial coordinate. Thus the joint law of $B,A^{R,\\epsilon}$ has a computable weak name.\n\nFor the passage to $A^R$, Tanaka's formula for $|B_s-x|$ gives the uniform estimate\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\sup_{s\\le U}|L_s^x-L_s^y|\n\\le 2|x-y|+4\\sqrt{|x-y|\\sqrt{2U/\\pi}} .\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bc.3}\n\\]\nIndeed the deterministic absolute-value differences contribute at most $2|x-y|$. The difference of the two sign stochastic integrals has quadratic variation at most $4\\int_0^U\\mathbf1_{\\{B_v\\in[x\\wedge y,x\\vee y]\\}}\\,dv$. Its expectation is at most $4|x-y|\\sqrt{2U/\\pi}$, by bounding the time-$v$ density by $(2\\pi v)^{-1/2}$, uniformly in the starting law. Doob's $L^2$ inequality then gives the second term in (O.7.2.3bc.3). By the occupation formula, $A^{R,\\epsilon}$ is obtained by replacing $L_s^x$ in $A_s^R$ by its $\\rho_\\epsilon$-average around $x$. Integrating (O.7.2.3bc.3) gives a computable upper bound for $\\mathbb E\\sup_{s\\le U}|A_s^{R,\\epsilon}-A_s^R|$, namely $m_R(\\mathbb R)$ times the right side at $|x-y|=\\epsilon$. The bound tends effectively to zero. The joint law of $B,A^R$ is therefore computable on this horizon in bounded uniform metric.\n\n*Effective inversion and grid laws.* Fix a dyadic physical horizon $T$ and an error budget $\\eta>0$. For integer $R,U$ the event\n\\[\nG_{R,U,T}=\\{\\sup_{s\\le U}|B_s|<R,\\quad A_U^R>T\\}\n\\]\nis effectively open in the joint continuous-path space. Its probability is lower semicomputable from the preceding joint law. Dovetail the tests for $R,U$ until one certifies probability greater than $1-\\eta$. This search terminates: first the true $A_U$ eventually exceeds $T$ almost surely, and for fixed $U$ the Brownian range is bounded almost surely and $A^R=A$ on the event that the range lies in $(-R,R)$. On the selected event the full and cut-off clocks agree on $[0,U]$.\n\nFor each requested positive grid time $t\\le T$, strict monotonicity and continuity of the clock allow rational brackets $s_-<s_+$ of its inverse satisfying $A_{s_-}^R<t<A_{s_+}^R$, with arbitrary small width. The joint path name can certify both inequalities and a bound on the Brownian oscillation on the bracket. This semidecides membership of the grid output in any effectively open output set whenever the output is in that set, on almost every path in the good event. For time zero take $B_0$. More explicitly, take a rational output ball whose closure lies in the proposed open set, and require that all Brownian values in each chosen bracket lie in its corresponding coordinate ball; enumerate all such certificates. This construction uses open certificates and no test for strict clock monotonicity on arbitrary input paths. The omitted mass is at most $\\eta$. Varying $\\eta$ effectively yields lower names of the full grid-law probabilities of output opens. They are probability laws, so these lower names give finite weak approximations: use rational product bump covers at decreasing mesh, find a finite union covering all but a prescribed mass, and integrate the finite partition functions by lower names for their open sublevel and superlevel sets. This is the effective weak representation in Theorem O.7.2.3an. Every search is uniform in the grid, starting law and speed-measure names. Using a point mass at a named starting point gives the transition-row statement.\n\nThe paths have no fixed-time jumps, and the joint grid names have just been obtained from the speed-measure data. Therefore Theorem O.7.2.3an supplies the same finite normalized coherent cells, positive-duration physical clock, paid random seeds and lookup tables, archived records, and block and path tests. The Brownian time used in (O.7.2.3bc.2) is a construction parameter. The actual finite clock uses the grid in the declared physical time $t$.\n\n*Singular noncompact example.* Enumerate distinct rational points $r_j$ densely in $\\mathbb R$, and take $m=\\sum_{j\\ge1}2^{-j}\\delta_{r_j}$. Its cut-off weak names are computed by truncation with geometric tail, and it has full support. On each finite time interval the physical clock measure is supported on Brownian times when $B_s$ is rational. These times have zero Lebesgue measure almost surely by the Brownian occupation formula. Under the strictly increasing clock, the corresponding set of physical times has full Lebesgue measure and zero $dC_t$-measure. Thus the nonzero quadratic-variation measure $dC_t=d\\langle X\\rangle_t$ is singular with respect to physical time. This law is not an ordinary Itô diffusion with time-absolutely-continuous quadratic variation, and in particular lies beyond the globally Lipschitz Itô source class. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bd\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bd-causal-mode-and-reset-control-of-singular-speed-diffusions\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3bd (Causal Mode and Reset Control of Singular-Speed Diffusions).** Take a finite library of computable full-support speed measures on $\\mathbb R$ as in Theorem O.7.2.3bc, a named initial mode and an effective weak initial law. Each declared command selects a mode by a finite table and applies a computable continuous reset $R_c:\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb R$ to the state. Between interventions use the natural-scale diffusion of the current mode. At each of finitely many positive dyadic intervention times install a finite-outcome instrument as in Theorem O.7.2.3bb, with diamond-norm continuity in its finitely many released state arguments for each fixed past outcome word. The actual classical outcome selects the next command. The decoded path uses the postreset state at an intervention time and exposes the prereset state to the instrument. Use the continuous finite-dimensional coherent coefficients and the named evaluations of Theorem O.7.2.3an in each mode.\n\nThere is a uniform paid finite transition and command tower, independent of the values of the installed instruments, which realizes the joint outcome word, path and coherent outputs in the tests of Theorem O.7.2.3bb for every such fixed finite protocol and dyadic horizon. It applies to the singular quadratic-variation example of Theorem O.7.2.3bc. It allows the actual instrument to change the speed measure, the state, or both.\n\n*Computable transition kernels and finite records.* For each mode and positive dyadic $h$, Theorem O.7.2.3bc computes the weak probability measure $P_h(x,\\cdot)$ uniformly from a name of the initial real $x$. Use the bounded state metric $\\min\\{1,|x-y|\\}$ and its Wasserstein metric on weak laws. A computable map on each computably compact interval is uniformly continuous there with a computable modulus: search for a finite cover by the input neighborhoods on which a desired output precision is certified, and use a Lebesgue number of the finite cover. This applies to $P_h$ for each fixed step $h$, with no uniform modulus as $h$ decreases required. It also applies to the finitely many resets. Composing a mode row with a reset gives a computable weak row, since the reset is computable continuous and the pushforward of a computable weak probability by such a map is computable. Keep the separate before- and after-reset stages and their outputs.\n\nFor a given stage choose a dyadic mesh $h$, a finite growing horizon and an error tolerance $\\varepsilon>0$. Temporarily allow a no-command choice or any library command at each tick. There are finitely many such forced schedules. For each schedule the finite joint grid law, with both before- and after-reset coordinates, is computable. To see this, iterate the named kernels, retaining the coordinates already sampled. For a bounded computable continuous test on the next product, its conditional integral is computable and continuous in the old coordinates by effective integration against the named probability, or by restricting to finite high-mass compact sets and approximating there by finite sums. The same argument applies to the initial name and deterministic reset. Therefore one can search for a common integer $R$ so that under every forced schedule all coordinates lie in $(-R,R)$ with probability greater than $1-\\varepsilon$. The probability of that finite-coordinate open event is lower semicomputable and tends to one as $R$ increases. This search uses only the finite mode and command library.\n\n*A universal finite table at each stage.* Use a retained label lattice on $[-R-1,R+1]$ and a marked overflow state. For a movement row at a label, compute a finite weak approximation and round its output to the lattice, keeping a distinct archived overflow label outside the retained range. For each reset compute an accurate rational approximation of its output before rounding or assigning overflow. A rational cutoff outside $[-R,R]$ can be used after approximation. Compute an initial dyadic row in the same way. Each table can have arbitrary additional precision before compilation. The rounded finite approximation can be coupled in the bounded state metric to the unrounded row with arbitrarily small error away from overflow. One way to implement this comparison is first to round only to a sufficiently fine finite rational net on the line for the named probability, then send its outlying named points to overflow. When the exact comparison point lies in $[-R,R]$ and its coupled named point is within a distance less than $1/2$, that point is retained.\n\nFor the error allocation, run through the finite list of movement and potential reset positions backwards. Starting with a final distance tolerance less than $\\min(\\varepsilon,1/2)$, use the compact row and reset moduli to choose a preceding tolerance at each position. For each movement make the Wasserstein errors from replacing the exact starting state by a close label and from the finite dyadic row small enough that the probability of missing the next tolerance is at most $\\varepsilon$ divided by the number of positions. This follows by a coupling for each Wasserstein comparison and Markov's inequality. Choose each reset evaluation and the state lattice fine enough for its deterministic output tolerance, and choose the initial row for the first tolerance. Use the minimum over positions and modes for any reused row. The tolerances can be made nondecreasing from the start and bounded by the final tolerance. Coupling success then persists in every position on the true compact event except for probability at most $\\varepsilon$. Adding its complement bounds the total failure probability by $2\\varepsilon$. The coupling applies to every forced schedule without adding its probabilities to those of the other schedules. Thus the same finite tables work for every word, with the maximum error over all grid, prereset and postreset coordinates bounded by the chosen tolerance on success. Execute only the actual row and command, with the instrument's observed outcome as its control. All the finite labels, row seed, clocks, coherent flags and control records are retained using the normalized conditional operations of Theorems O.7.2.3m and O.7.2.3ac.\n\n*Path and instrument limit.* Along the stages take $h\\downarrow0$, horizons tending to infinity and $\\varepsilon\\downarrow0$. Any fixed dyadic releases and intervention times eventually lie on the mesh. For a fixed forced outcome word the reference process is a concatenation of finitely many continuous diffusion paths, with specified deterministic reset times. Interpolate the finite state path linearly within each such interval, ending at its prereset label and starting the next interval at the postreset label. The compact-path modulus of continuity on each closed diffusion segment tends to zero with the mesh almost surely. The grid coupling therefore gives convergence in probability in the uniform path metric on this comparison, using the identity alignment at the deterministic reset times. It also gives convergence of all released prereset and postreset states. The finite histories may use the step values as their coefficient inputs. Their coefficient paths converge in probability in $L^1$ on each segment by continuity on the compact limiting path and the same coupling. Stage subslot and pulse errors are chosen as in Theorem O.7.2.3bb so that the physical coherent approximation has vanishing error at each stage. The isometry estimates of Theorem O.7.2.3an apply to these interval histories.\n\nAs in Theorem O.7.2.3bb, multiply the actual outcome maps in chronological order on each forced word, without normalizing the intermediate branches. Integrate that product of instrument and history maps against the forced path law for that word. It is the actual unnormalized branch output, since each transition row uses only the current state and the commands already selected. Diamond-norm continuity at the released states and the interval estimates give convergence of these products in trace norm on each fixed input and spectator, after coupling. Their norms on density inputs are bounded by one. Multiplying by a bounded continuous path test, integrating, and summing over the finitely many outcome words yields the asserted joint output and the history tail tests of Theorems O.7.2.3an and O.7.2.3bb. The theorem covers fixed finite dyadic protocols, a finite mode and reset library and the stated continuity of the instrument. Other controlled generators and general independently prescribed diffuse histories retain their tests. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723be\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723be-effective-local-martingale-problem-diffusion-compiler\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3be (Effective Local Martingale-Problem Diffusion Compiler).** Let $E=\\mathbb R^d$, with its standard computable presentation, and let $b:E\\to\\mathbb R^d$ and $a:E\\to\\operatorname{Sym}_d^+$ be computable continuous coefficients, with evaluations and moduli on rational compact boxes. On $C_c^\\infty(E)$ use\n\\[\nLf=b\\cdot\\nabla f+\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Tr}(a\\nabla^2 f).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3be.1}\n\\]\nSuppose the continuous stopped local martingale problems on bounded balls are unique in law for the indicated starting laws, including point starts, and the specified initial law $\\mu$ admits a continuous conservative solution of this local problem. Supply a computable weak name of $\\mu$. Then the full path law of this solution is computable on $C([0,\\infty),E)$. In particular its actual finite dyadic-grid laws have uniform effective weak names. The same algorithm gives a dyadic transition row from a computably named point whenever the conservative premise holds at that point.\n\nFor continuous finite-dimensional coherent coefficients with the evaluations of Theorem O.7.2.3an, these names give the paid finite Hermitian-control source of that theorem. The polygonal state readouts converge on each fixed bounded horizon and the same source gives the stated joint state-path and coherent outputs, with every fixed finite quantum intervention that preserves the autonomous state law and every fixed spectator. The compiler needs no supplied effective Lyapunov estimate, transition rows, spatial row moduli or effective generator graph core.\n\n*Reduction to bounded coefficients.* Put\n\\[\nw(x)=(1+\\|b(x)\\|+\\operatorname{Tr}a(x))^{-1}.\n\\]\nThis is positive and computable continuous. Both the drift norm and the trace covariance of the slowed operator $\\widetilde L=wL$ are at most one. For an original solution $X$, define $G_t=\\int_0^t w(X_v)^{-1}dv$ and $Y_s=X_{G^{-1}(s)}$. On every finite interval the integrand is bounded because $X$ has continuous paths. Also $G_t\\ge t$, so the time change is defined on all slowed times. The martingale identity under the inverse clock gives the continuous martingale problem for $\\widetilde L$, locally and hence globally for compactly supported tests. On a bounded ball the clock speed and its reciprocal are bounded by positive finite constants on the closure. The inverse time change sends a stopped solution of the slowed problem to one of the original stopped problem and conversely, including the constant continuation after stopping. Thus local uniqueness transfers. Any continuous conservative global slowed solution has these same stopped laws. On a finite time interval any continuous path in $E$ is bounded, so exhaustion of the balls identifies its full law with $Y$. In particular $\\widetilde L$ has exactly one conservative continuous law for the named initial law.\n\n*An effective compact container for the slowed law.* Work on the complete path space $\\mathcal X=C([0,\\infty),E)$, with metric\n\\[\nd_{\\mathcal X}(x,y)=\\sum_{j\\ge1}2^{-j}\n  \\left(\\sup_{s\\le j}\\|x(s)-y(s)\\|\\wedge1\\right).\n\\]\nThe laws use bounded Wasserstein distance for this metric. Rational polygons extended constantly after a finite time form a computable dense family of paths, and finite rational measures on them form a computable dense family of laws. The law space is complete for this bounded Wasserstein metric.\n\nUsing localized coordinate and coordinate-product tests for the bounded generator gives the semimartingale decomposition of $Y$ into drift with norm rate at most one and a continuous martingale whose covariance has trace rate at most one. For its martingale increment $M_{s+h}-M_s$, Itô's formula on the squared and fourth powers of the Euclidean norm gives\n$\\mathbb E\\|M_{s+h}-M_s\\|^2\\le h$ and $\\mathbb E\\|M_{s+h}-M_s\\|^4\\le3h^2$. Indeed the drift of the fourth power is $2\\|M-M_s\\|^2\\operatorname{Tr}\\widetilde a+4(M-M_s)^{\\mathsf T}\\widetilde a(M-M_s)\\le6\\|M-M_s\\|^2$, with $\\widetilde a=wa$. Apply the stopped formula first and use Fatou and the second-moment bound. The bounded drift and $(u+v)^4\\le8(u^4+v^4)$ then give\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\|Y_{s+h}-Y_s\\|^4\\le32h^2\\qquad(0<h\\le1).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3be.2}\n\\]\n\nTo construct a finite approximation net for the actual law at tolerance $\\epsilon$, choose an integer $J$ with the metric tail after $J$ below $\\epsilon$, and a rational initial radius $R$ of probability greater than $1-\\epsilon$ using the weak name of $\\mu$. By (O.7.2.3be.2), Markov's inequality and the union bound, the probability that any dyadic increment of length $2^{-n}$ in $[0,J]$ exceeds $2^{-n/8}$, at any $n\\ge N$, is at most\n\\[\n32J\\sum_{n\\ge N}2^{-n/2}.\n\\]\nChoose $N$ effectively so this is below $\\epsilon$. On the resulting good path set the starting radius and the scale-$N$ increments bound all scale-$N$ values. At any finer depth $M$, dyadic chaining bounds the distance to the scale-$M$ polygon on $[0,J]$ by a computable constant times $\\sum_{n\\ge M}2^{-n/8}$. To see this, approximate any time by the dyadic floors at successive depths; successive floor values differ by at most one increment at that depth, and the scale-$M$ interpolation differs from its left endpoint by at most the scale-$M$ increment. Continuity passes the sum to the actual time. Choose $M$ so the polygon error is below $\\epsilon$. Quantize its vertices on a finite rational mesh in their computably bounded box, and extend the polygon constantly beyond $J$. This gives a finite path net for the good set, with error bounded by $3\\epsilon$ in $d_{\\mathcal X}$. Couple each good path to one nearest net point, and the exceptional paths to a fixed net point. The exceptional probability is at most $2\\epsilon$, and the metric is bounded by one. Rounding the finitely many resulting weights on a sufficiently fine rational probability mesh therefore yields a computable finite list of rational laws containing a law within $6\\epsilon$ of the true law.\n\nIn particular for each $n\\ge1$ one can construct a finite law list $F_n$ with distance at most $2^{-n}$ to the unknown true law. Define the closed law container\n\\[\n\\mathcal K=\\bigcap_{n\\ge1}\n\\{\\nu:\\operatorname{dist}_{W}(\\nu,F_n)\\le2^{-n}\\}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3be.3}\n\\]\nIt is compact by total boundedness and completeness, and its finite open covers can be certified effectively as follows. Given an enumerated union $O$ of basic open balls in the law space, dovetail $n$ and finite pieces of that enumeration. For each center $v\\in F_n$, certify either that its closed ball of radius $2\\cdot2^{-n}$ is contained in one enumerated ball of $O$, by a strict triangle inequality, or that $\\operatorname{dist}_{W}(v,F_j)>2^{-j}+2\\cdot2^{-n}$ for some $j\\le n$. If all centers pass, $\\mathcal K\\subset O$. Conversely suppose $\\mathcal K\\subset O$ but no stage passes. For each $n$ choose a center not satisfying either alternative even against the whole enumeration. Their tail lies within $2^{-j}+2\\cdot2^{-n}$ of every fixed earlier $F_j$, so it is precompact. A subsequential limit lies in $\\mathcal K$, hence is interior to one enumerated ball of $O$. For sufficiently late terms that ball would satisfy the first strict certificate, a contradiction. This proves the cover semidecision used below.\n\n*Recovering the unique law from its martingale constraints.* Take a computable countable family of smooth compactly supported tests dense on each compact support in the function and its first two derivatives. One may use rational polynomials times computable smooth cutoffs on expanding rational boxes, approximating the derivatives on each box. For each such test $f$, impose, at rational $0\\le s<t$, the identities\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\nu\\left[\n\\left(f(Y_t)-f(Y_s)-\\int_s^t\\widetilde L f(Y_v)\\,dv\\right)H\n\\right]=0,\n\\tag{O.7.2.3be.4}\n\\]\nwith $H$ in a countable bounded continuous cylinder test algebra at rational times up to $s$. Use, for example, products of rational piecewise linear bump functions that generate the coordinate Borel sets. Impose the initial law using the same kind of separating tests at time zero. Each displayed functional of a path is bounded computable continuous with a computable bound. Compactly supported derivatives give a computable bound and modulus for $\\widetilde Lf$, and integration along a named continuous path is computable. Thus the constraints form an effectively closed set of laws. Monotone-class extension of the cylinder tests, continuity of paths and time integrals, and density of the smooth tests give the actual continuous martingale problem for $\\widetilde L$. The true law belongs to $\\mathcal K$, and uniqueness leaves exactly that law in $\\mathcal K$ after imposing all the constraints.\n\nFor any desired accuracy, enumerate basic balls of that radius and search for one whose union with the open complement of all the constraints covers $\\mathcal K$, using the cover semidecision. A ball containing the unique law succeeds and its center approximates that law. This computes the full slowed law without a rate of convergence for any stochastic numerical scheme.\n\n*Returning to the declared time and compiling the source.* The clock $D_s=\\int_0^s w(Y_v)dv$ is a computable continuous path functional on each finite horizon, and is strictly increasing. Under the unique slowed law it equals $G^{-1}(s)$ and tends to infinity almost surely, by the conservative construction. For each integer physical horizon $T$ and error budget $\\eta>0$, search for an integer slowed horizon $U$ such that the effectively open event $D_U>T$ has certified probability greater than $1-\\eta$. The search terminates. On this event, invert the clock by rational brackets with strict inequalities and approximate $Y$ at the bracket times using its path modulus. This computes the full rescaled path on $[0,T]$, uniformly in physical time, on the good event. For the inverse one can search rational partitions whose neighboring brackets have sufficiently small slowed-time widths, and then use continuity of $Y$. The probability of the excluded paths is at most $\\eta$. As in Theorem O.7.2.3bc, the open inverse-bracket certificates yield a computable weak path law on $[0,T]$, and the weighted metric tail gives the full path law. In particular the required grid names are computed from the actual diffusion law. Theorem O.7.2.3an applies to those names and the declared coherent coefficients, supplying its positive-duration clock and complete finite resource ledger on the original physical-time grid.\n\n*An unbounded non-Lipschitz example in two dimensions.* Take $b=0$ and $a(x)=(1+\\|x\\|^{1/2})I_2$. This is computable continuous. Its stopped local problem is the scalar positive time change of the planar Brownian problem, hence is unique by the two inverse clocks on bounded balls. To see conservative existence directly, use planar Brownian motion $\\mathcal W_s$ and the clock $\\int_0^s(1+\\|\\mathcal W_v\\|^{1/2})^{-1}dv$. Almost surely $\\sup_{v\\le n}\\|\\mathcal W_v\\|=O(n)$ as integers $n\\to\\infty$, by the Gaussian reflection bound and Borel–Cantelli. The clock therefore diverges, so its inverse gives the conservative diffusion. The covariance is not locally Lipschitz at zero. A locally Lipschitz square root would have locally Lipschitz covariance by matrix multiplication, so the example lies outside the globally Lipschitz coefficient class. It is also not a one-dimensional speed-measure process of Theorem O.7.2.3bc. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o723be1\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o723be1-controlled-effective-local-martingale-problem-diffusions\"></a>**Corollary O.7.2.3be.1 (Controlled Effective Local Martingale-Problem Diffusions).** Take a finite mode library on $\\mathbb R^d$ in the class of Theorem O.7.2.3be. Require conservative existence from every point in each mode, in addition to the stopped uniqueness and computable coefficients in that theorem. Keep a computable weak initial law with its conservative solution, a named initial mode, the finite mode and computable continuous reset commands of Theorem O.7.2.3bd on this carrier, and its fixed finite dyadic intervention and released-state continuity conditions. There is a single paid finite tower for that library which realizes the joint outcome word, state path and coherent histories for every such protocol. The actual quantum outcome can select any declared change of drift, covariance and reset.\n\n*Proof.* Theorem O.7.2.3be computes the transition row at positive dyadic time from each name of a starting point. These rows satisfy the composition law because conditioning the martingale identities at a fixed time gives the same local problem from the conditioned point, and stopped uniqueness followed by exhaustion determines its conservative law. They are computable maps into the bounded Wasserstein law space, so on each computably compact state box their uniform moduli can be obtained by finite cover, as in Theorem O.7.2.3bd. Its construction of forced finite grid laws uses these rows, the named initial law and the computable continuous resets. It therefore gives finite high-mass comparison boxes common to the finitely many schedules of each stage. The backwards error allocation and lattice coupling of that theorem apply in Euclidean dimension $d$ with the same bounded metric and finite product grids. Continuous mode paths on each segment give the same interpolation estimate, with the two labels of each reset compared at its exact checkpoint. The instrument products and paid coherent cells use the same normalized conditional operations and convergence estimates. Hence the autonomous path computability of Theorem O.7.2.3be gives a causal joint source without an assumed effective escape modulus or supplied transition rows. Other generators and independently prescribed diffuse histories retain their tests. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bf\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bf-effective-diffusion-selection-by-countable-moment-maxima\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3bf (Effective Diffusion Selection by Countable Moment Maxima).** Let $L$ have the computable continuous coefficients and compactly supported test domain of Theorem O.7.2.3be on $\\mathbb R^d$, with globally bounded drift and trace covariance and supplied computable bounds. Fix a computable weak initial law and assume that at least one conservative continuous solution exists. No uniqueness is required. In a fixed enumeration let $F_j$ be bounded computable continuous real tests on the full path space, with uniform effective bounds, separating probability measures. For example take products of rational continuous bumps at finitely many rational times. Select the solution law that first maximizes $\\int F_1$, then maximizes $\\int F_2$ over the maximizers of the first test, and continues in the fixed order. The selected law exists and is unique. If the attained successive maximum values $m_j$ have a uniform computable real name, then the entire selected law is computable. Conversely, a computable selected law gives those names uniformly. In this case the law has a paid full-path and coherent realization by Theorem O.7.2.3an. The source inputs are the selector tests and their effective maxima, in addition to the coefficient and preparation data.\n\n*Proof.* Rescale the moment constants in (O.7.2.3be.2) using the supplied global bounds. The same dyadic increment estimate and initial tightness produce the finite law nets $\\mathcal F_n$ in Theorem O.7.2.3be uniformly over every conservative solution of this martingale problem. Hence its computably compact container $\\mathcal K$ contains all those laws. The same countable identities (O.7.2.3be.4), with $L$ in place of $\\widetilde L$, give an effectively closed constraint set in $\\mathcal K$. They characterize the full solution set, since the dense tests, continuity and monotone-class argument do not require uniqueness. The resulting solution set $S_0$ is nonempty compact. The open-cover semidecision for $\\mathcal K$ also applies to every effectively closed subset, by adjoining its enumerated open complement to the cover.\n\nInductively put $m_j=\\max_{\\nu\\in S_{j-1}}\\int F_j\\,d\\nu$ and $S_j=\\{\\nu\\in S_{j-1}:\\int F_j\\,d\\nu=m_j\\}$. The test integrals are continuous, so all the $S_j$ are nonempty compact. Their intersection is nonempty, and separating tests make it a singleton. The supplied names make the equalities effectively closed. To approximate the singleton at any precision, search for a basic ball of that radius whose union with the complements of all martingale and selector equalities covers $\\mathcal K$. The cover semidecision terminates for a ball containing the singleton. This computes the selected full law. Conversely its computable weak name integrates all $F_j$ with uniform precision, so determines all the successive maxima. The law has continuous paths, effective dyadic grid laws and no fixed jumps. Theorem O.7.2.3an applies with its declared finite-dimensional coefficient names, physical clock and resource records. This statement does not infer the maxima from the existence of solutions.\n\n*An example with nonunique dynamics and derived selector values.* On the line start at zero and use the bounded computable drift $b(x)=2\\min(1,\\sqrt{x_+})$ and covariance zero. Any solution has zero quadratic variation and solves the scalar integral equation. Its paths remain nonnegative and nondecreasing, and the departure-time classification gives a unique delay $a\\in[0,\\infty]$ for each path. The path after a finite delay is $(t-a)^2$ for $a\\le t\\le a+1$, then $1+2(t-a-1)$, and is zero before departure. The case $a=\\infty$ stays at zero. The proof is separation of variables on the positive interval, joined at $x=1$ by the constant drift. Any law on delays gives a solution and these exhaust them. Take the first test $F_1(X)=\\arctan X_1$. It has a strict unique maximum at the zero-delay path. Indeed the path at time one is $(1-a)_+^2$, with the infinite delay interpreted as zero, and its unique largest value is one. The first maximum is $\\pi/4$, and all further maxima are the effective evaluations of the fixed separating tests on the computable zero-delay path. If the first test is instead $-\\arctan X_1$, the first maximum is zero and every delay at least one survives. Subsequent tests can distinguish them; for example a positive strictly increasing bounded function of $X_2$ selects exactly delay one. The selection rules here are named response tests with exactly derived values, on the same generator and preparation. They give different paid source limits. The construction extends the delay-family distinction of Theorem O.7.2.3y to an effective test for a general countable selection on the stated compact law class. Other generators, non-effective selection values and general independently prescribed histories retain their tests. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bg\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bg-effective-noncompact-finite-rate-jump-kernels\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3bg (Effective Noncompact Finite-Rate Jump Kernels).** Let $E$ be a computably presented Polish space with a bounded computable compatible metric. Adjoin an isolated absorbing cemetery $\\partial$. For each live $x$, supply a weakly computable finite nonnegative mark measure $\\nu_x$ on $E\\sqcup\\{\\partial\\}$, uniformly from a name of $x$. This means a computable map into the finite weak measure representation, including its mass $\\lambda(x)=\\nu_x(E\\sqcup\\{\\partial\\})$. Set $\\nu_\\partial=0$. Assume that the exponential holding and mark construction for these measures and the named effective weak initial law has almost surely only finitely many marks before each finite time, or before absorption. Self marks are permitted and count in this condition. Then its actual finite dyadic-grid laws have uniform effective weak names. The same conclusion holds for rows from named point starts at which the nonexplosion condition holds. No effective bound on the number of marks, rate range, or spatial containment of the original process is required.\n\nThe uniqueness class is the Markov law with these conditional holding times and destination marks and absorbing cemetery. On bounded tests for which its local drift is used, the generator is\n\\[\n\\mathcal A f(x)=\\int(f(y)-f(x))\\,\\nu_x(dy),\\qquad \\mathcal A f(\\partial)=0 .\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bg.1}\n\\]\nOn the live killed law take $f(\\partial)=0$. In particular, when the live law is rich Feller, the compiler uses its actual law without an effective graph-core presentation or named positive-time transition rows. Under the continuous coefficient and intervention hypotheses of Theorem O.7.2.3an, it supplies the paid finite path and coherent source of that theorem on the original state carrier. The result allows noncompact and nonmonotone unbounded-rate sources and arbitrary weakly computable mark laws, including state-dependent killing.\n\n*Bounded-rate approximation from the mark data.* For integer $N\\ge1$ put\n\\[\n\\nu_x^{(N)}=\\frac{N}{\\max\\{N,\\lambda(x)\\}}\\nu_x,\\qquad\nP_N(x,\\cdot)=\\left(1-\\frac{\\lambda(x)}{\\max\\{N,\\lambda(x)\\}}\\right)\\delta_x\n +\\frac{\\nu_x}{\\max\\{N,\\lambda(x)\\}} .\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bg.2}\n\\]\nThis is a computable probability kernel in the weak representation, also at zero rate and at $\\partial$. Run a rate-$N$ Poisson clock and at its times use the row $P_N$. At a live $x$ the accepted ticks, before moving to a new mark, have exponential waiting parameter $\\min\\{N,\\lambda(x)\\}$ by independent thinning, with destination $\\nu_x/\\lambda(x)$ when $\\lambda(x)>0$. A self mark simply starts a new such wait at the same state. This proves that the clocked chain is the bounded-rate holding and mark law of $\\nu^{(N)}$. The original law is likewise uniquely specified by successive exponential waits and conditional mark distributions, with no further continuation needed by nonexplosion. The formula (O.7.2.3bg.1) is its conditional drift, by stopping at bounded rates and applying the same waiting and mark identity.\n\nThe capped finite grid distributions are computable. For a fixed grid, the independent Poisson counts in its intervals have computable weights and an effective total-count tail. Conditional on those counts, the joint grid law is obtained by successive finite iteration of the computable kernel $P_N$, selecting the appropriate intermediate label at each checkpoint. Finite iteration of a weakly computable kernel with a computable initial measure gives the computable joint law of its successive labels. For instance for bounded computable continuous tests on the finite product, each backwards row integral is a computable continuous function of the preceding labels, with its original test bound. Computing its initial integral then gives the product law in the effective weak representation. This applies also to the list of all labels up to any specified tick count.\n\nFor a horizon $T$, let $G_N$ be the capped event that the initial label and every label at a Poisson tick through $T$ satisfy $\\lambda<N$, where $\\lambda(\\partial)=0$. Its probability is lower semicomputable: sum the computable Poisson count weights times the probabilities of the open condition on each finite label word. Those open probabilities are lower semicomputable in the computable joint word laws. Until the first label with rate at least $N$, the capped and original holding and mark rules are identical. They can be coupled by using the same waits and marks before reaching that label. Virtual ticks in the capped representation leave the state fixed and cannot change this exit event. Hence $\\Pr(G_N)$ is also the probability that every actual visited label through $T$ has rate less than $N$. Nonexplosion makes the list of such labels finite almost surely and each rate is finite. Therefore $\\Pr(G_N)\\to1$. For prescribed $\\eta>0$, dovetail $N$ and its open probability certificates until $\\Pr(G_N)>1-\\eta$ is certified. On this event the capped and original paths coincide through $T$, so the capped grid distribution is within $\\eta$ of the original in bounded Wasserstein distance for the maximum coordinate metric. Approximating that capped distribution by a finite dyadic law at the remaining tolerance gives the required effective name. No localization radius or quantitative rate bound for the original law enters the search.\n\nThe holding law has no jump at a fixed positive time, because every finite waiting time conditional on the past has a continuous distribution and the possible jump indices are countable. It has càdlàg paths by nonexplosion. The effective grid names therefore meet the hypotheses of Theorem O.7.2.3an, which implements the same autonomous state law with its paid physical clock, finite normalized coherent columns, classical outcomes, archives and fixed quantum intervention ports. The homogeneous Poisson clock above computes auxiliary finite laws. The physical finite clock is the declared checkpoint and pulse construction of that theorem.\n\n*A nonmonotone rich-Feller example.* Take $E=\\mathbb R$, $V(x)=\\sqrt{1+x^2}$, and\n\\[\n\\nu_x(dy)= V(x)\\,\\frac{\\mathbf1_{\\{|y-x|\\le1\\}}}{2}\\,dy+\\kappa\\delta_\\partial(dy),\n\\qquad \\kappa>0\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bg.3}\n\\]\nwith computable $\\kappa$. The effective weak names follow from computable integration on the translated unit interval. The live jump moves in both directions. First suppress killing. Its generator on live tests is $A_0 f(x)=V(x)\\int_{-1}^1[f(x+u)-f(x)]\\,du/2$. Since $V$ is one-Lipschitz, $A_0V\\le V$. Stopping on first exit from $[-R,R]$ makes the rates and stopped values bounded. The stopped drift identity and Grönwall give $\\mathbb E_x V(X_{t\\wedge\\tau_R})\\le e^tV(x)$, so $\\Pr_x(\\tau_R\\le t)\\le e^tV(x)/\\sqrt{1+R^2}$. Explosion inside a fixed compact interval is impossible by bounded rates. Exhaustion of intervals proves nonexplosion, also for any initial probability law by conditioning on its starting point. An independent exponential killing time of rate $\\kappa$ gives the killed law in (O.7.2.3bg.3).\n\nFor clarity, the live motion is Feller on $C_0(\\mathbb R)$. The bounded capped kernels are weakly continuous in the starting point, hence their finite-time expectations of bounded continuous tests are continuous by finite iteration and the Poisson tail. On compact sets of starting points, the stopped exit bound gives uniform convergence of these expectations to those of the live motion, by coupling up to the first rate cap. To see vanishing at infinity, put $W=1/V$. On $|u|\\le1$, $V(x+u)\\ge V(x)/2$, since $V(x)\\le V(x+u)+1\\le2V(x+u)$. Hence $|W(x+u)-W(x)|\\le2/V(x)^2$ and $A_0W\\le2W$. Stop first on entering a fixed compact interval $[-R,R]$ or exiting a larger one. The stopped estimate and exhaustion show $\\Pr_x(\\inf\\{s:X_s\\in[-R,R]\\}\\le t)\\le e^{2t}\\sqrt{1+R^2}/V(x)$. Compactly supported output tests consequently vanish at infinity under the semigroup. For strong continuity on a compact set of starting points, the probability of any first jump by $t$ is at most $t\\sup V$ on that set. For a compactly supported test the preceding entrance estimate is uniform over $0\\le t\\le1$ outside large compact sets. These bounds give strong continuity in the uniform norm for compactly supported tests, and density and contraction extend it to all $C_0$. Independent killing multiplies the live semigroup by $e^{-\\kappa t}$, preserving its sub-Markov Feller property.\n\nThe closed live generator has $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R)$ as a core and is $A_0-\\kappa I$ there. For the unkilled law, the finite jump range makes $A_0 f$ continuous compactly supported for any continuous compactly supported $f$. The stopped drift identity, followed by exhaustion, gives $P_t f-f=\\int_0^tP_s A_0f\\,ds$; bounded $f,A_0f$ justify the passage. Strong continuity puts such $f$ in the domain. For $f$ in the full generator domain, its pointwise generator value is the same formula: at fixed $x$, the first two marks have bounded rates up to and after the first mark since it moves by at most one. Thus the probability of two marks by $t$ is $O_x(t^2)$, and the one-mark expansion gives the pointwise derivative. Take smooth cutoffs $\\chi_R(x)=\\chi(x/R)$ with $\\chi=1$ on $[-1,1]$ and zero outside $[-2,2]$. The commutator $A_0(\\chi_R f)-\\chi_R A_0 f$ is bounded by $C\\sup_{|y|\\ge R-1}|f(y)|$, since the integral of cutoff differences is supported in the unit enlargement of the transition collars, those differences are $O(R^{-1})$, and $V=O(R)$ there. Thus $\\chi_R f\\to f$ in graph norm. Uniformly smooth each compactly supported $\\chi_R f$ on a fixed slightly larger compact support. The finite jump range and bounded rate on the enlarged support make uniform smoothing also a graph-norm approximation. This proves the asserted core, and the bounded killing term preserves it.\n\nThe recorded conservative extension on $\\mathbb R\\sqcup\\{\\partial\\}$ is not Feller on $C_0$ of that space. The compactly supported cemetery indicator evolves to the live value $1-e^{-\\kappa t}$ at every $x$, which does not vanish at infinity. The construction above uses the isolated cemetery and the original noncompact path topology nonetheless. It settles the finite-rate Markov mark class under the stated effective names and nonexplosion premise. Infinite-activity kernels and feedback that changes the autonomous law require their own construction. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bh\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bh-effective-noncompact-jump-diffusion-interlacing\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3bh (Effective Noncompact Jump–Diffusion Interlacing).** On the live carrier $\\mathbb R^d$ take the continuous diffusion of Theorem O.7.2.3be with the every-start conservative and stopped uniqueness premises of Corollary O.7.2.3be.1. Adjoin an isolated absorbing cemetery. Take a computable weak finite-measure jump kernel $x\\mapsto\\nu_x$ on this extended carrier with computable continuous total rate $\\lambda(x)=\\nu_x(\\mathbb R^d\\sqcup\\{\\partial\\})$. Self marks are allowed. Use the interlaced law in which the continuous trajectory runs between marks, the next mark occurs by its accumulated rate $\\int\\lambda(X_s)ds$, and the destination law at a mark is $\\nu_x/\\lambda(x)$ where $\\lambda(x)>0$. Start from a computable weak law and assume that on every bounded horizon the number of marks is almost surely finite. The diffusion and mark rules specify a unique conservative càdlàg law on the extended carrier. Its actual finite dyadic-grid laws are uniformly computable in the weak representation. Consequently the coherent coefficient tests of Theorem O.7.2.3an give a paid full-path and coherent source, including its fixed finite quantum protocols on the autonomous driver. The construction requires neither an effective escape or rate-tail modulus nor supplied jump–diffusion transition rows.\n\n*Interlacing and a uniform cap.* For each segment the diffusion law is unique. With an independent mean-one exponential threshold, its accumulated rate gives the next time, with no mark if the threshold is never crossed. The continuity of each diffusion path and of the rate gives finite bounded accumulated rate on every compact time interval of the segment. The probability of hitting a prescribed time exactly is zero conditionally on that segment path, since the threshold has a density. Use the destination kernel and fresh independent increments and thresholds after every mark, or stop at the cemetery. This recursion gives uniqueness in the stated holding-and-mark comparison class. Nonexplosion gives finitely many segments and no fixed positive-time jump. The live generator on compactly supported smooth tests, wherever the local martingale generator is read, is $Lf(x)+\\int(f(y)-f(x))\\,\\nu_x(dy)$, with the test's declared cemetery value. This follows by adding the compensated finite-activity jump increment to the continuous local martingale identity on bounded live sets. The theorem compares the specified interlaced law.\n\nFix a positive integer $N$. The capped law uses rate $\\lambda_N=\\min(N,\\lambda)$ and the original normalized destination where a mark occurs. Equivalently run a rate-$N$ independent homogeneous Poisson clock and apply at each candidate tick the row\n$$\nP_N(x,dy)=\\left(1-\\frac{\\lambda(x)}{\\max(N,\\lambda(x))}\\right)\\delta_x(dy)\n+\\frac{\\nu_x(dy)}{\\max(N,\\lambda(x))} .\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bh.1}\n$$\nAt the cemetery the row and the diffusion segment both stay there. This is the same finite-measure kernel construction as Theorem O.7.2.3bg, now interlaced with the diffusion. The normalization is effective even where the uncapped rate vanishes. Conditional on the continuous path before a genuine mark, Poisson thinning gives the exponential survival with accumulated capped rate. Redrawing a diffusion segment at an unchanged tick gives the same continued diffusion by the Markov property. For this property, condition the martingale identities at the given time and apply stopped uniqueness from the conditioned point, as in Corollary O.7.2.3be.1. The Poisson clock is independent of the path before each prospective segment. Conditioning on its given durations suffices for the segment construction.\n\n*Computable capped histories.* On any fixed rational horizon $T$, the Poisson count and the ordered tick times have effective laws. Given the count, the times are order statistics of independent uniform points. Truncating the count has an explicit Poisson tail. Theorem O.7.2.3be computes the full continuous segment law uniformly from the name of its initial state. One may use a full segment of length $T$, then evaluate and restrict it at the named duration, since evaluation of a continuous path at a named time is computable. The joint finite-segment law with the visited labels is thus computable for each truncated count by iterating the computable weak probability kernels, as in Theorem O.7.2.3bd. A comparison between a grid time and the tick times can be decided by approximating them until separated. Equality has probability zero, so open certificates for separation and approximation to the segment values give the effective capped grid laws. The same construction gives a lower name for the probability of the event that the maximum live rate over the finitely many continuous segments and over all posttick states is strictly less than $N$. In each continuous segment, the maximum of the computable continuous rate over its compact time interval is computable from the segment and the endpoints, by uniform approximation on that named compact path. The isolated cemetery is a separate component. The strict event therefore has open certificates on the computable segment, endpoint and time data. Truncation of the Poisson count with its known tail and enumeration over the retained counts computes its lower probability. If a live segment ends at a killing tick, include its live prereset value in this test.\n\n*Removing the cap.* The original and capped laws can be coupled identically until the first entry into $\\{x:\\lambda(x)\\ge N\\}$, whether by a continuous segment or a jump. Run their same diffusion segments, exponential thresholds and mark draws as long as the rates agree, and switch to the respective laws afterwards. For continuous segments the first crossing of the closed set is a stopping time. The survival rule before it uses the identical integrals; even if the entrance is by a jump, the destinations agree until that entrance. Equivalently one may thin common marked Poisson proposals on each bounded-rate stopped region. The two laws thus assign equal probability to the event of staying below $N$ throughout the horizon, using the same pre- and postmark convention. On each original path with finitely many marks, the union of the live continuous segment ranges and the finite endpoints is compact. Its rate maximum is finite almost surely. Hence the original probability of this event tends to one as $N\\to\\infty$. For any desired error $\\eta>0$, dovetail capped open-event certificates until one proves probability greater than $1-\\eta$. This terminates without an effective modulus from the original law. On that event the coupled capped and true grid vectors coincide. The original finite dyadic-grid laws are therefore within $\\eta$ in total variation of the selected capped laws, before the computable weak approximation of those laws.\n\nTheorem O.7.2.3an compiles these grid names on the original physical-time clock. Its construction keeps the cemetery, paid sampler, normalized coherent cells and joint output tests. The continuity needed for the coefficient comparison is that in its declared state presentation. This result includes unbounded diffusion coefficients admitted by Theorem O.7.2.3be and unbounded finite jump rates under qualitative nonexplosion, on one driver. Infinite-activity kernels and arbitrary state-law-changing interventions retain their respective tests. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bi\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bi-effective-state-dependent-compensated-infinite-activity-jumps\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3bi (Effective State-Dependent Compensated Infinite-Activity Jumps).** On $\\mathbb R^d$ consider the Brownian and compensated Poisson equation\n\\[\nX_t=X_0+\\int_0^t b(X_s)\\,ds+\\int_0^t \\sigma(X_s)\\,dW_s+\n  \\int_0^t\\int_{\\mathcal Z}g(X_{s-},z)\\,\\widetilde N(ds,dz).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bi.1}\n\\]\nThe Brownian motion, Poisson random measure of intensity $ds\\,\\nu(dz)$, and initial state have the declared independent laws. Use a computably presented mark space $\\mathcal Z$ and increasing retained mark sets $\\mathcal Z_j$ exhausting $\\mathcal Z$ up to $\\nu$-null sets. Supply uniformly computable finite weak measures $\\nu|_{\\mathcal Z_j}$ on the mark carrier, including their total masses. Supply computable continuous $b,\\sigma$ and jointly computable continuous $g(x,z)$. For each integer radius $R$, give finite computable local bounds and Lipschitz constants for $b,\\sigma$ on the closed radius-$R$ ball, and numbers $G_R,L_{g,R}$ with\n\\[\n\\sup_{\\|x\\|\\le R}\\int_{\\mathcal Z}\\|g(x,z)\\|^2\\nu(dz)\\le G_R^2,\\qquad\n\\int_{\\mathcal Z}\\|g(x,z)-g(y,z)\\|^2\\nu(dz)\n\\le L_{g,R}^2\\|x-y\\|^2\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bi.2}\n\\]\non that ball. Give a uniform effective bound $\\epsilon_{R,j}\\to0$ on $\\sup_{\\|x\\|\\le R}\\int_{\\mathcal Z\\setminus\\mathcal Z_j}\\|g(x,z)\\|^2\\nu(dz)$. The equation compensates every retained mark in this convention. Take a computable weak initial law and assume qualitative nonexplosion of the maximal solution. No effective escape modulus or initial moment bound is required.\n\nThen the actual finite dyadic-grid laws of (O.7.2.3bi.1) are uniformly computable in the effective weak representation. The path is càdlàg and has no fixed positive-time jump. The finite Hermitian-control source of Theorem O.7.2.3an therefore gives joint $J_1$ state and coherent-output convergence on the original carrier, with paid clock and retained resources, for the continuous coherent coefficients and fixed exogenous-law quantum protocols specified there. This includes infinite-activity and infinite-variation state-dependent kernels.\n\n*Local laws and retained-mark estimates.* The uniqueness class is the càdlàg solution driven by the stipulated Brownian and Poisson laws, localized to bounded live sets. The local $L^2$ Lipschitz bounds give this law by the usual stopped Picard argument, which here can be seen directly by projecting the coefficient arguments onto the closed radius-$R$ ball. The Euclidean nearest-point projection $\\pi_R$ is nonexpansive, so $b_R=b\\circ\\pi_R$, $\\sigma_R=\\sigma\\circ\\pi_R$, and $g_R(x,z)=g(\\pi_Rx,z)$ are globally bounded in their specified norms and globally Lipschitz. On a short interval the iteration of the integral equation is a contraction in expected squared supremum norm by Cauchy–Schwarz for the drift and the $L^2$ maximal inequality for the two martingales. Increments relative to the initial value have finite squared supremum norm regardless of the initial distribution. Concatenating intervals gives global projected solutions $X^R$, and the same estimates give uniqueness for each common driver. They agree with the unprojected solution until leaving the corresponding ball, including any exit jump. Nonexplosion gives a global original law. Its local generator on $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^d)$ is\n\\[\n\\mathcal A f(x)=b(x)\\cdot\\nabla f(x)\n+\\tfrac12\\operatorname{Tr}(\\sigma(x)\\sigma(x)^{\\mathsf T}\\nabla^2 f(x))\n+\\int_{\\mathcal Z}\\bigl[f(x+g(x,z))-f(x)-\\nabla f(x)\\cdot g(x,z)\\bigr]\\nu(dz).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bi.3}\n\\]\nThe integral is locally absolutely bounded by the second-order Taylor estimate and (O.7.2.3bi.2). Itô's identity gives (O.7.2.3bi.3) up to each bounded exit. This is a stated generator test domain, with uniqueness in the displayed driving-law class.\n\nLet $X^{R,j}$ have the same projected coefficients and driver as $X^R$, keeping only compensated marks in $\\mathcal Z_j$. For $T>0$ write $D(t)=\\mathbb E\\sup_{s\\le t}\\|X_s^R-X_s^{R,j}\\|^2$. The difference equation has four summands, namely the drift difference, Brownian difference, retained-mark difference, and omitted-mark integral. The squared norm is at most four times the sum of their squared norms. Cauchy–Schwarz for the drift and Doob's inequality and the two martingale isometries give\n\\[\nD(t)\\le C_{R,T}\\int_0^tD(v)\\,dv+16t\\,\\epsilon_{R,j},\n\\qquad\nC_{R,T}=4T L_{b,R}^2+16L_{\\sigma,R}^2+16L_{g,R}^2 .\n\\]\nThe Brownian coefficient norm here is Hilbert–Schmidt. Grönwall therefore gives\n\\[\n\\mathbb E\\sup_{s\\le T}\\|X_s^R-X_s^{R,j}\\|^2\n\\le 16T\\,\\epsilon_{R,j}\\,e^{C_{R,T}T}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bi.4}\n\\]\nThe same estimate without the omitted term and with zero initial difference proves pathwise uniqueness. In particular no rate estimate uniform over unbounded starting states of the original process is assumed.\n\n*Computable finite-shell laws.* For fixed $R,j$, put $\\nu_j=\\nu|_{\\mathcal Z_j}$. Its finite mass gives an independent homogeneous Poisson count on each bounded horizon. Conditional on the count, the ordered times are the order statistics of uniform times, and the independent marks have normalized measure $\\nu_j/\\nu_j(\\mathcal Z)$ when the mass is positive. Between the retained marks solve the continuous equation with coefficients $\\sigma_R$ and drift\n\\[\nb_R(x)-\\int g_R(x,z)\\nu_j(dz).\n\\]\nThis drift is computable continuous and globally bounded Lipschitz. Indeed, truncate the integrand radially at norm $M$; the discarded first moment is at most $G_R^2/M$ by (O.7.2.3bi.2), and bounded continuous integration against the computable finite mark law is effective. The Lipschitz estimate follows by Cauchy–Schwarz using $\\nu_j(\\mathcal Z)$ and the $L^2$ Lipschitz bound. The resulting continuous segment law is computable from each named start by Theorem O.7.2.3be, since the bounded Lipschitz coefficients give conservative existence and stopped uniqueness. At a mark time the state updates by the computable map $x\\mapsto x+g_R(x,z)$.\n\nFor a fixed finite count, successive joint weak integration computes the segment laws, their endpoint updates and all grid values. One may generate full continuous segments of horizon $T$ and then restrict them to the required lengths. Evaluation at a varying time is computable on continuous path space. Choosing the segment that contains a fixed grid time is an almost-sure computation, since an independent Poisson time has probability zero of equaling that checkpoint. In particular each output-open event has a lower probability name from the finite time-order and evaluation certificates. The supremum of the state norm over $[0,T]$ is the maximum of the continuous segment suprema up to their declared lengths, including both one-sided values at each mark. Each finite segment supremum is computable. Thus the probability of the strict event that this supremum is below a rational bound is lower semicomputable. The computable Poisson tail permits uniform summation of these fixed-count laws. A zero mass needs no normalized mark row: for a requested error tolerance, run the strict positive-mass test in parallel with the test that the probability of any Poisson mark is below that tolerance, using the zero-count law in the latter case. Either test supplies the required approximation. For lower probability names the zero-count contribution is available directly and a positive-count term is included once positivity of the mass is certified. These operations give the effective finite-shell grid laws and strict supremum tests from the supplied mark names alone.\n\n*Removal of the spatial projection.* Fix a bounded horizon $T$ and a desired small error $\\eta>0$. In the finite-shell laws search over $R,j$ for a certified probability greater than $1-\\eta/2$ of\n$\\sup_{s\\le T}\\|X_s^{R,j}\\|<R-1$, and simultaneously require the bound in (O.7.2.3bi.4) to be below $\\eta/4$. Such a search terminates. The original nonexplosive càdlàg path is bounded almost surely on $[0,T]$. Choose $R$ with sufficiently large probability that its range lies strictly inside radius $R-2$. On that event the projected law agrees with the original under the common driver. Then take $j$ sufficiently large in (O.7.2.3bi.4) to make the finite-shell path strictly inside radius $R-1$ with the required probability. The strict margin makes the certificate discoverable. Under the successful comparison, Markov's inequality and (O.7.2.3bi.4) show that the full projected law remains inside radius $R$ except on a set of probability at most $3\\eta/4$. There it agrees with the original law through $T$.\n\nTo compute the original grid law to arbitrary precision, choose the search tolerance sufficiently small and make the additional comparison between full projected and retained grid values using (O.7.2.3bi.4), enlarging $j$ for that second comparison if needed. The expected truncated maximum-coordinate distance is bounded by the probability of original–projected disagreement plus the square root of the retained estimate. This yields effective weak names in the maximum bounded grid metric. The compensated Poisson integral has no jump at a fixed time, since its Poisson measure assigns zero intensity to each deterministic time slice; the other components are continuous. Thus Theorem O.7.2.3an applies. The auxiliary Poisson calculations specify effective law tables. The paid finite clock and pulse operations are those of the actual Hermitian-control source in that theorem.\n\n*Rich-Feller infinite-variation example.* On the line take zero drift and Brownian coefficient, $g(x,z)=(2+\\sin x)z$, and\n\\[\n\\nu(dz)=c\\,|z|^{-1-\\alpha}\\mathbf1_{\\{0<|z|\\le1\\}}\\,dz\n\\]\nwith computable $c>0$ and rational $1<\\alpha<2$. Use $\\mathcal Z_j=\\{2^{-j}\\le |z|\\le1\\}$. The finite measures have computable integrals and masses, and the omitted uniform squared-amplitude integral is bounded by $18c\\,2^{-j(2-\\alpha)}/(2-\\alpha)$. All coefficient norms and Lipschitz bounds are global. The strong solution is conservative by the globally bounded coefficient estimates. It is rich Feller: same-driver Lipschitz estimates give continuous dependence in law on $x$; the squared expected supremum of displacement from a point start is bounded uniformly on bounded horizons, giving the $C_0$ property; the same bound tends to zero as the horizon tends to zero, giving strong continuity on $C_0$. For $f\\in C_c^\\infty$, (O.7.2.3bi.3) is continuous and compactly supported, because the jumps have absolute size at most three, and the compensated Taylor bound dominates the integrand. The integrated generator identity and strong continuity then put $f$ in the Feller generator domain. Infinite mark intensity gives infinitely many nonzero jumps on each positive interval, and $|g(x,z)|\\ge|z|$ together with the divergent first small-mark moment gives almost surely infinite total jump variation there. This example has state-dependent infinite activity, rather than a finite-rate kernel in the preceding results. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bj\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bj-controlled-infinite-activity-sources-with-additional-killing\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3bj (Controlled Infinite-Activity Sources with Additional Killing).** On $S=\\mathbb R^d\\sqcup\\{\\partial\\}$ keep the isolated absorbing cemetery. Take a finite mode library of compensated Brownian and Poisson backgrounds of Theorem O.7.2.3bi, with the stated names and conservative existence from every point. A mode may also carry an additional finite weakly computable mark measure $\\nu_x$ as in Theorem O.7.2.3bg, with computable continuous finite rate $\\lambda(x)$ and zero rate at $\\partial$. Between these additional marks run its background, and select each next mark by accumulated rate and destination law as in Theorem O.7.2.3bh. Assume these additional marks are nonexplosive for each start. A command selects a mode and applies a computable continuous reset $R_c:S\\to S$ fixing $\\partial$. Use the finite command library, initial-law names, coherent coefficient names and fixed finite dyadic intervention protocols of Theorem O.7.2.3bd. At a checkpoint the actual quantum outcome selects the command. The law releases the prereset state and records the postreset state as the new path value.\n\nFor each mode its full $J_1$ path law on rational bounded horizons, and its finite dyadic grid laws, are computable uniformly from a named point start. There is one paid finite transition and command tower for the library. It realizes the joint outcome word, original $J_1$ path and coherent outputs for every such fixed protocol, including fixed spectators. The transition and command tables do not depend on the values of the external instruments used in the protocol. The result permits state-law-changing controls of the infinite-variation example of Theorem O.7.2.3bi, as well as additional state-dependent jumps and killing in the same source.\n\n*Computable background path laws.* Use on bounded horizons the standard complete $J_1$ metric with logarithmic time distortion, truncated by one, and rational step paths with named values as dense labels. The underlying state metric is bounded and complete. We recall the effective operations needed here. A finite concatenation of named continuous paths with strictly separated named interior breakpoints is computable in this $J_1$ representation. Approximate each continuous piece uniformly and approximate the breakpoints by rational ones. Align the finitely many original and approximating breakpoints by a piecewise linear time change. As their errors go to zero with the pieces fixed, its slope ratios tend to one. This gives the required $J_1$ approximants, with the terminal value named separately. The same representation computes a path's value at a named continuity time: use increasingly accurate step names and search for a bracket on which all their values, allowing for the stated time error, have oscillation below the desired tolerance. A bracket exists at every such time. Restriction to that endpoint is also computable, by the same small-oscillation certificate and the time alignment away from it. At the two fixed endpoints the values are continuous functions of the $J_1$ path, since the time changes fix endpoints. The usual rational-step presentation and its continuity-time operations are also described by Maler (2015, §3.3).\n\nFor fixed spatial radius and retained shell, the construction in Theorem O.7.2.3bi computes the joint finite list of continuous segments and their independent ordered Poisson times and marks. The preceding concatenation gives the full finite-shell path law by an effective Poisson tail and finite joint integration. In the zero-mass case the same zero-count comparison in that theorem applies. The coupling (O.7.2.3bi.4) bounds the Wasserstein error for bounded $J_1$ by its squared-supremum bound to the power $1/2$, since the identity is an allowed time change. The open no-exit search of Theorem O.7.2.3bi selects a radius where the actual and projected paths disagree with arbitrarily small certified probability. It can use the same finite-shell supremum events and coupled error as before, and the bounded path metric now also bounds its loss. Thus it computes the full original background law, uniformly from a named start. The backgrounds have no fixed positive-time jumps, and they are Markov by uniqueness with fresh independent driving increments. In particular, conditioning at a time independent of the current unconditioned segment permits restarting its law from the endpoint.\n\n*Interlacing additional marks.* Use the finite-rate cap and row (O.7.2.3bh.1), with an independent homogeneous rate-$N$ candidate clock. Conditional on its finite count and ordered times, successively compute a background segment from the current state, its restriction and endpoint at the required time, and then its candidate-mark row. At the isolated cemetery the path and row stay there. Independence of the next candidate duration and the background implies that this restriction and evaluation occur at a continuity time almost surely. In detail, condition on the preceding segments and current start, then on the next duration. For each fixed positive duration the unconditioned background has zero probability of a jump at that time. The same argument applies to the final remaining duration conditional on the earlier candidate times. It also applies to fixed dyadic releases. Thus the oscillation and time-order certificates give the joint law of the restricted segments and endpoints. Joining the restricted paths is computable on these data, even with interior jumps. To see this, approximate each segment by named step paths and compose its internal time change with the linear alignment of the outer interval endpoints. The concatenation of these aligned time changes is an admissible time change on the full horizon, with error tending to zero. The actual postmark value starts the next segment; retain the prereset value separately whenever it is a named release. These almost-sure partial computations give lower names for all output-open probabilities and hence the effective weak probability law. The computable Poisson tail handles the count.\n\nBefore the first accepted mark, the current background path is unaffected by the proposals. Conditional on that unconditioned path, thinning gives the specified survival $e^{-\\int\\lambda_N(X_s)\\,ds}$. A candidate time is almost surely not a background jump, so the mark row uses its actual value with no competing jump convention. Ignoring virtual proposals leaves the same background law, by the Markov property and independence of candidate times. This verifies the capped law. Before leaving the rate-safe region $\\lambda<N$, the actual and capped laws can be coupled using the same background and accepted marks, exactly as in Theorem O.7.2.3bh. For an effective certificate require the supremum of the live rate on the whole path to be strictly less than $N$, including the live left limits of each segment and both sides of extra marks. Equivalently use the maximum on the compact live range closures of those segments. The supremum of a continuous named rate over a $J_1$ path with compact range closure has an effective lower name for each strict upper-bound event: sufficiently close step approximants have ranges within the prescribed spatial error after time change, and the computable rate has a uniform modulus on a compact neighborhood of their live ranges in $\\mathbb R^d$. The isolated cemetery remains distinguishable. This gives a lower probability name for the strict safe event. On it the laws agree, because their clocks and marks agree until failure of the same safety bound. Every actual finite-horizon path has a bounded live range closure almost surely by conservative backgrounds and finite additional marks. Its rate is bounded there, so the safety probabilities tend to one. Dovetail the capped open tests until one certifies any desired safety probability. As in Theorem O.7.2.3bh this bounds total variation disagreement of the entire path law before weak approximation. This proves the computability assertion for the full interlaced law and its named checkpoint distributions. Its local live generator adds the finite mark drift (O.7.2.3bg.1) to (O.7.2.3bi.3). Uniqueness refers to the driven background and the stated interlacing class.\n\n*Universal causal tables and $J_1$ limit.* Apply the finite table construction of Theorem O.7.2.3bd to the computable dyadic rows just obtained. The compact localization sets are now closed Euclidean balls together with the isolated cemetery, with computable compact moduli for each fixed step row and reset. For each stage there are finitely many forced command schedules, and their actual joint grid laws are computable by finite integration. As in that theorem, search for a common high-probability compact set and choose all row and reset tolerances backwards. The same finite rounded tables then couple each forced schedule to its true grid law with vanishing maximum-coordinate error outside vanishing exceptional mass. Overflow is a separate finite archived label and never substitutes for the actual cemetery. Execute only the current row and the command selected by the observed quantum outcome. The actual finite law retains the initial seed, row choices, release coordinates, instrument outcomes and all normalized coherent columns and pulses. Their durations and archives are those of Theorems O.7.2.3m and O.7.2.3an.\n\nFix one allowed protocol and one of its finitely many forced outcome words. The target path on each open interval between intervention times is càdlàg and almost surely has no jump at either prescribed time other than the declared reset. On the closed segment use the actual prereset endpoint for the grid path comparison. The step readouts on each such segment converge in $J_1$ by Theorem O.7.2.3an, since that segment has no terminal stochastic jump. Its time changes fix both endpoints. Concatenating these time changes gives the whole-path $J_1$ comparison with every reset fixed at its exact checkpoint and with the postreset value used there. The separate prereset outputs also converge by the grid coupling. Each segment’s coefficient paths converge in $L^1$ in probability by continuity along the compact range of its limiting path and $J_1$ convergence at continuity times. The coherent isometries therefore converge by the bound in Theorem O.7.2.3an. Chronological composition along the forced word uses its actual unnormalized quantum outcome maps. Their stated continuity in released states gives convergence on each fixed input and spectator. Integrate against the forced path law and sum over outcome words, as in Theorem O.7.2.3bd. This gives the actual outcome probabilities and complete joint path and coherent outputs, including the tightness and trace-class tail tests. The construction covers the fixed finite protocols on the given mode and reset library. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bk\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bk-count-stopped-coherent-sources-for-unbounded-quantum-jump-operators\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3bk (Count-Stopped Coherent Sources for Unbounded Quantum Jump Operators).** Let $H=\\bigoplus_{i\\ge1}H_i$ be an effective Hilbert direct sum of finite-dimensional matrix blocks. On the algebraic block span give a block-diagonal Hamiltonian $H_0=\\bigoplus_i H_{0,i}$ with computable Hermitian matrices. Give a countable marked family $L_e$, with a finite effective list of all nonzero label and output-block pairs from each input block and computable complex matrices on those pairs. Different input blocks may have the same output block under the same mark. Suppose the finite-support form $D=\\sum_eL_e^*L_e$ is block diagonal. The effective block data determine its matrices $D_i$. Let $W=\\bigoplus_i W_i I_{H_i}$, where $W_i\\ge1$ are computable, and suppose there are finite computable constants $c_0,c_1\\ge0$ with the finite-support form bounds\n\\[\nD\\preceq c_0W,\\qquad\n\\sum_e L_e^*W L_e-DW\\preceq c_1W .\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bk.1}\n\\]\nNo boundedness of $H_0$ or $D$ is required. The marked family can coherently merge or split block inputs.\n\nOn finite-support matrices define\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0(\\rho)=-i[H_0,\\rho]\n+\\sum_e L_e\\rho L_e^*-\\tfrac12(D\\rho+\\rho D).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bk.2}\n\\]\nIt has a conservative trace-class closure, with the finite matrix span as a graph core. The closure has the complete resolvents of Theorem O.7.2.3x, and these are computable on the finite rational matrix tests. The finite Hermitian-control source grammar of Theorem O.7.2.3m realizes its reduced semigroup and the full prescribed ordered-time, marked coherent history of these operators. The comparison is in trace norm on each fixed finite input corner, including fixed spectators, and applies to fixed finite protocols of effectively compressed interventions. The same construction pays for the finite clocks, coherent pulse operations, exit labels and all retained history records.\n\n*Count stopping and normalization.* Put $G=\\bigoplus_i(-iH_{0,i}-D_i/2)$, so $e^{Gt}$ is a direct sum of finite contractions and is strongly continuous. From a finite input corner, the no-mark propagator preserves its finitely many blocks, and every fixed number of marked applications has only finitely many reachable blocks and labels. For an integer $k\\ge1$ form a finite apparatus with count layers $0,\\ldots,k$. Layer zero contains the input blocks. Each next layer contains the output blocks reachable from the preceding layer. Before layer $k$, keep $G$ on each block and the entire coherent marked operator from that layer to the next for every label. Layer $k$ is absorbing. In a layer the maps are the sums of their stated input-output blocks for that label, so they preserve interference. Their total square is precisely the restriction of $D$, because every outgoing pair is included and $D$ is block diagonal. Consequently this is an ordinary finite normalized marked Lindblad source. The layer tracks only the number of marks, which is already determined by the ordered history. It does not read the block of a superposed state.\n\nOn every layer use the restriction of $W$. The value of $W$ does not change by copying a state into a different count layer. The finite stopped generator therefore has $W$-drift at most $c_1W$, by (O.7.2.3bk.1), with zero drift on the absorbing layer. For the count observable $N$ equal to the layer index, its drift is $D$ before the absorbing layer and zero there. Finite-dimensional differentiation and Grönwall give, for a unit vector $\\psi$ in the initial corner,\n\\[\n\\mathbb E_\\psi W(t)\\le e^{c_1t}\\langle\\psi,W\\psi\\rangle,\\qquad\n\\Pr_\\psi\\{\\tau_k\\le T\\}\n\\le\\frac{c_0\\langle\\psi,W\\psi\\rangle}{k}\\int_0^T e^{c_1s}\\,ds .\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bk.3}\n\\]\nHere $\\tau_k$ denotes the time the stopped model reaches layer $k$, and the probability bound follows from $\\mathbb E_\\psi N(T)\\ge k\\Pr_\\psi(\\tau_k\\le T)$. This argument tests an observable on the finite apparatus, without assuming a Markov chain on basis states.\n\nFor $0<t_1<\\cdots<t_j<T$ the declared $j$-mark column is\n\\[\ne^{G(T-t_j)}L_{e_j}e^{G(t_j-t_{j-1})}\\cdots L_{e_1}e^{Gt_1};\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bk.4}\n\\]\nthe zero-mark column is $e^{GT}$. Regard all these columns together as a vector in the orthogonal sum of ordered-time simplex spaces with the mark labels and final quantum system. The columns with $j<k$ are exactly those of the count-stopped model for the same inputs, after forgetting the duplicate count register by its known word length. Their squared norms sum to $1-\\Pr_\\psi(\\tau_k\\le T)$. Equation (O.7.2.3bk.3) proves normalization of (O.7.2.3bk.4) as $k\\to\\infty$. It defines an isometry $V_T$ on the full Hilbert space by extension from finite-support inputs. Splitting the time simplex at a deterministic time identifies the full history with the chronological product of two interval records, up to the null set of a mark at the split. Multiplication of the amplitudes then gives the isometry composition, by finite-support approximation and the isometry bound. Partial tracing yields a CPTP semigroup $T_t$. Its zero-mark column tends strongly to identity as $t\\downarrow0$, and on each input corner the squared norm of the nonzero-mark part tends to zero by (O.7.2.3bk.3) with $k=1$. Finite-rank approximation yields strong continuity in trace norm. This gives a direct block-class construction of conservativity. For related general conservativity criteria see Chebotarev and Fagnola (1997).\n\n*Closed domain and core.* On any finite input corner, the no-mark and one-mark terms give the trace-norm derivative (O.7.2.3bk.2) at zero. Indeed the no-mark terms use finite matrices, and the one-mark integrands are continuous at zero in finitely many reachable blocks. For a positive unit input the probability of two marks by time $t$ is $O(t^2)$: the first-mark hazard is bounded in the input corner and the conditional second-mark hazard is bounded on the finite set of first destinations. This follows also by integrating the first two unnormalized mark amplitudes and using contraction. Polarization gives the matrix-unit derivative. Thus the generator of $T_t$ extends $\\mathcal L_0$.\n\nTo prove a core, suppose a bounded operator $A$ annihilates the range of $\\lambda-\\mathcal L_0$ under the trace pairing, for a real $\\lambda>0$. For finite-support vectors $\\phi,\\psi$, apply the annihilator identity to $|e^{Gs}\\psi\\rangle\\langle e^{Gs}\\phi|$. Differentiate the discounted no-mark pairing and integrate on $s\\ge0$; the boundary at infinity vanishes by contraction and $e^{-\\lambda s}$. This gives\n\\[\n\\langle\\phi,A\\psi\\rangle\n=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-\\lambda s}\\sum_e\n\\langle L_e e^{Gs}\\phi,A L_e e^{Gs}\\psi\\rangle\\,ds .\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bk.5}\n\\]\nIterate this identity $k$ times, so the right side integrates the pairing of the two first-$k$-mark vectors discounted by $e^{-\\lambda\\tau_k}$. For finite $k$ all blocks and labels used from these inputs belong to finite reachable sets. The first-$k$-mark vectors have square-integrated norm at most the squared input norm, by the normalized count-stopped model, and Cauchy–Schwarz justifies the iterated integrals. For a unit input their discounted mass is at most\n$\\Pr(\\tau_k\\le T)+e^{-\\lambda T}$.\nEquation (O.7.2.3bk.3) makes its $\\limsup$ at most $e^{-\\lambda T}$ as $k\\to\\infty$. Send $T\\to\\infty$. Cauchy–Schwarz in the iterated (O.7.2.3bk.5) then gives $\\langle\\phi,A\\psi\\rangle=0$. The range is dense. The generator of $T_t$ has bounded resolvent at $\\lambda$, so density of this core range gives graph-norm density by applying the resolvent to a converging range approximation. This proves the closure and the core. Its complete resolvents follow from Theorem O.7.2.3x.\n\n*Paid finite realization.* On the first $n$ blocks, with projection $P_n$, use the internal mark $P_nL_eP_n$ under the original label. Send the exit map $(I-P_n)L_eP_n$ into a separate orthogonal absorbing copy of its output blocks, with an archived exit label for $e$. Every such list is finite. The internal and exit squares sum to $P_nDP_n$, since their destinations are orthogonal. Thus the no-mark operator on the live blocks is the exact restriction of $G$. The finite reference keeps the original mark mixing within each of these two maps. It uses a trace-preserving input encoding with the omitted initial mass recorded separately when an input lies outside the finite carrier.\n\nFix an input corner $E$ and $T$. Once the live cutoff contains every block reachable from $E$ in fewer than $k$ marks, the exact finite and infinite histories agree on the common projection onto word lengths $<k$. This projection concerns word length alone. It does not attempt to infer which intermediate blocks contributed to a coherent amplitude. Include the finite exit records in orthogonal extra sectors of the common carrier. Both histories are normalized, so their remaining squared norms coincide on every unit input in $E$ and are bounded by (O.7.2.3bk.3), with $\\langle\\psi,W\\psi\\rangle\\le\\|W|_E\\|$. The operator-norm difference of the two isometries on $E$ is at most twice the square root of that bound. Tensoring with a fixed spectator preserves the bound. Reachability and the bound give a computable cutoff choice for every fixed-corner error and finite horizon. In particular they compute the reduced resolvents on the finite rational tests by truncating the exponentially weighted time integral and evaluating finite stopped semigroups on the remaining compact time interval.\n\nFor each finite cutoff use the normalized finite coherent tick of Theorem O.7.2.3ac and its ordered-time cell embedding. Its bounded computable matrices allow clock and pulse precision with total conditional isometry error tending to zero on increasing finite horizons, as in Theorem O.7.2.3af. The resulting finite Hermitian controls retain each new flag, pulse and calibration, the exit and omitted-input records and the named history embedding. The fixed-corner norm comparison gives trace-norm convergence on every fixed input with spectator, and hence the finite-corner and trace-class tail tests on the common output. Insert the actual checkpoints of any fixed finite effectively compressed protocol and telescope its complete CP outcome maps. Finite-rank approximation on the intermediate carriers and trace-norm contraction extend the corner estimate to these intermediate states. Measuring the retained mark and time records gives their classical tests as well. No basis-trajectory sampling is used.\n\n*Noninjective unbounded example.* Take $H_i=\\mathbb C^2$, $H_{0,i}=i\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)$, $W_i=i+1$, and $C=\\operatorname{diag}(1,2)$. For each integer $j\\ge1$ give two labels $(j,+),(j,-)$, both with output block $H_j$ and inputs $H_{2j},H_{2j+1}$. Their two row blocks are respectively $\\sqrt{j/2}(C,C)$ and $\\sqrt{j/2}(C,-C)$, and they vanish on other inputs. Summing the two squares cancels the off-diagonal block terms and gives $D=jC^*C$ on both input blocks. The same cancellation holds in the weighted jump square with the multiplier $W_j$. Thus $D\\preceq 2W$, and the $W$-drift is nonpositive because $W_j\\le W_{2j},W_{2j+1}$. The no-mark Hamiltonian does not commute with $D_i$ on an active block. For a superposition of the same nonzero vector in the two inputs the minus mark cancels while the plus mark adds, so this history is genuinely sensitive to interference between distinct input blocks mapped to the same destination. The rates grow without bound and the jumps have unbounded block displacement. It lies outside the injective basis-jump class of Theorem O.7.2.3af. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bl\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bl-dominated-intermediate-releases-and-their-normal-testers\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3bl (Dominated Intermediate Releases and Their Normal Testers).** Consider a prescribed intermediate release on the actual diffuse port $L^1([0,1],dx)$ with a finite-dimensional quantum side register $H_0$. Its joint ready law is a positive integrable matrix density $F(x)$ with $\\int_0^1\\operatorname{Tr}F(x)\\,dx=1$. The stipulated dyadic encoding releases a finite cell label and decodes it as the uniform density on that cell. After the release a normal tester acts on this port, the side register and any fixed independent spectator or ready registers. Its full retained terminal carrier, including its outcomes and spectators, is separable trace class or a countable classical–quantum sum of such carriers. The source comparison requires convergence of the complete tester output in trace norm for every fixed tester of this class. The mathematical comparison uses exact cell compressions. An installed tester with supplied effective compressions uses their paid finite implementation as in Theorem O.7.2.3at.\n\n1. If an integer $M\\ge1$ is supplied with $\\operatorname{Tr}F(x)\\le M$ almost everywhere, a uniform finite Hermitian-control source tower for this comparison exists if and only if the real and imaginary entries of $\\int_I F(x)\\,dx$, for every dyadic cell $I$, are uniformly limit computable. Here this means one algorithm outputs a rational number given the cell, entry, and approximation index, with convergence at each fixed cell and entry. No rate of convergence is assumed. The finite stages may be chosen with their actual decoded trace densities uniformly bounded by $M+1$.\n2. Without the uniform bound, there is a scalar positive density with uniformly limit-computable dyadic probabilities for which no such tower exists. The failing tester is one fixed normal two-outcome measurement with uniformly computable finite compressions on every dyadic level. There is no returned system and no private source memory in this example.\n\n*Bounded-density sufficiency.* Take dyadic conditional expectations of $F$ at any finite depth. Their cells are positive matrices, their total integrated trace is one, and their pointwise traces are at most $M$. As the depth increases they converge to $F$ in matrix trace-norm $L^1$, by finite-dimensional entry approximation with dyadic simple functions. On any fixed dyadic partition, normalized positive finite matrices can be approximated by algebraic positive finite matrices. One can use rational dyadic cell weights approximating the original weights and rational or algebraic normalized states for the nonzero cells, with a normalized fallback state on a zero cell. The cell length is fixed and positive, so sufficient accuracy on the finitely many cell weights makes every decoded trace density at most $M+1/2$. Equivalently, choose the dyadic weights small enough in each cell to lie below $(M+1/2)|I|$, a finite strict-cap rounding that is possible because the original cell density bound is $M$. These finite candidates are effective. They can retain the exact trace cap $M+1$ after the paid pulse approximations by choosing the finite preparation error smaller than the remaining cap slack times the minimum cell length. The bound also follows directly for exact dyadic weight preparation with a counted finite fair-bit tape, since subsequent conditional quantum preparation is normalized in each cell.\n\nEnumerate the dyadic entry tests with their known bounds and the countable catalogue of such finite preparations. As in the finite selection of Theorem O.7.2.3am, at stage $n$ choose among the first $n$ candidates with the current rational approximants to the first $n$ tests, using geometrically decreasing test weights and capped absolute discrepancies. Every finite list of target tests can be fitted by a fixed catalogue member, by the conditional expectations and finite approximations just proved. Its score on that list converges as the supplied names converge, and the remaining geometric tail is arbitrarily small. Thus the actual selected preparations converge on each fixed dyadic matrix test. The finite clocks, fair-bit and controlled state preparations, label release, and all associated records are paid in the grammar of Theorem O.7.2.3m. Pulse errors can be chosen summable and tending to zero with the stage.\n\nTo pass to a fixed normal tester, regard its action on the diffuse coordinate and the finite input matrix as an essentially bounded measurable family of output maps. For a positive finite input matrix $A$, apply the channel to $\\mathbf1_B A$ for a measurable set $B$. In a countable basis of its retained terminal carrier this gives scalar matrix coefficient measures with bounded Radon–Nikodym densities. Positivity of all rational finite tests holds simultaneously outside one null set, and the finite sums of diagonal densities are at most $\\operatorname{Tr}A$, because the scalar output trace measure of every input set is at most $|B|\\operatorname{Tr}A$. The coefficient matrix therefore defines a positive trace-class density almost everywhere with trace bounded by $\\operatorname{Tr}A$. Finite-rank coordinate truncations converge in trace norm at each such point and are measurable. The density is thus strongly measurable in trace norm and integrates to the output measure on each input set. A countable classical–quantum sum may be embedded as the diagonal blocks of a separable trace-class carrier. Polarization in the finite input recovers the essentially bounded trace-class kernels of its matrix units. Fixed independent ready states and spectators are included in this channel before taking those entries. Complete finite outcomes can be kept as orthogonal output blocks.\n\nThus for each of the finitely many input matrix entries the bounded trace-class kernel can be approximated in $L^1(dx)$ by dyadic simple trace-class functions, using strong measurability and dyadic simple approximation of scalar coefficients. The norm error in the output integral caused by replacing one kernel by such a simple function is at most $(M+1)$ times its $L^1$ error for any actual stage. Indeed each matrix entry of a positive matrix is bounded in modulus by its trace. The same bound holds for the target. With dyadic simple kernels the output convergence is exactly convergence on a finite collection of dyadic matrix tests. Letting the kernel approximation error tend to zero proves trace-norm convergence of the complete fixed tester output. Finite stages use its actual compressed operation on the named cell labels. This compares to each fixed normal tester without asserting a uniform modulus over all testers.\n\nFor necessity, a fixed tester can record a dyadic cell event and perform any one of finitely many polarization measurements of an input matrix entry on the side register. These are effective normal instruments on all the dyadic finite encodings. A realizing uniform source has computable probabilities for these actual stage tests to any rational tolerance. Take tolerance $2^{-n}$ at stage $n$; the resulting rational sequences converge by the required comparison and give the claimed uniform limit names.\n\n*Unbounded-density obstruction.* Choose a computably enumerated dense open set $A_0\\subset(0,1)$ of mass $\\alpha\\in(0,1/4)$ with computable intersections with every dyadic cell. For example enumerate all open interiors of the basic dyadic intervals and place inside the $k$th a nonempty rational open interval of length at most $2^{-k-4}$. The union is dense, its finite subunion lengths and dyadic intersection lengths are computable, and the geometric length tail gives an effective error. This computes $\\alpha$ and the indicated intersections.\n\nUse the disjoint half-open intervals\n\\[\nI_j=[2^{-j-1},2^{-j}),\\qquad j\\ge1 ,\n\\]\nand the separate half interval $I_*=[1/2,1]$. These cover $[0,1]$ up to the endpoint zero. Assign probability $p_j=4^{-j}$ to $I_j$, and its remaining mass $2/3$ to $I_*$. Use a uniform conditional density on $I_*$. Let $A_j$ be the affine copy of $A_0$ in $I_j$ and set $A=\\bigcup_{j\\ge1}A_j$. The mass $|A\\cap I|$ is computable uniformly for every dyadic cell $I$: each of the finitely many retained affine intersections is computable from the preceding interval construction, and the omitted tail is bounded by the sum of the omitted interval lengths. In particular the normal two-outcome tester $\\mathbf1_A,1-\\mathbf1_A$ has computable compressions, with probability $|A\\cap I|/|I|$ on a uniform cell.\n\nLet $K$ be the ordinary halting set and let $J$ be its oracle halting jump, enumerated in stages with access to $K$. Start with uniform conditional density on each $I_j$. If $j$ enters $J$ at oracle stage $s$, replace that interval's conditional density by the following prescribed density. Divide $I_j$ into its relative depth-$s$ dyadic cells, place the exact original mass of each cell uniformly on one nonempty rational subinterval of its interior contained in $A_j$, and give zero mass to the rest of the cell. Such subintervals can be found effectively because $A_j$ is dense open with a known interval enumeration. These are genuine nonnegative integrable densities, preserving each relative depth-$s$ cell mass and concentrating all of branch $j$'s mass in $A_j$. The infinite disjoint sum together with the $I_*$ branch is a normalized $L^1$ density $f$. Its definition is a prescribed mathematical law, with no requirement that its full $L^1$ name be available.\n\nFor a fixed dyadic cell of absolute depth $\\ell$, each change made at stage $s\\ge\\ell$ is invisible on that cell. Indeed each relative depth-$s$ cell in any $I_j$ is also an absolute dyadic subcell of depth at least $\\ell$. To compute a probability to precision $\\epsilon$ with oracle $K$, first truncate $j$ at a computable bound with $\\sum_{j>J_0}4^{-j}<\\epsilon$, then simulate only the finitely many oracle stages $s<\\ell$ on the retained indices. Compute their exact interval overlaps, taking the uniform density for all other retained branches. This is a uniform oracle-$K$ approximation of every dyadic mass. To see the limit name explicitly, at approximation stage $n$ run the corresponding bounded-accuracy oracle computations using the finite ordinary halting approximation $K_n$, with clipped bounded outputs and a dovetailed time cutoff. For each fixed requested precision, the actual computation uses finitely many correct oracle answers, and its answer eventually stabilizes. A single rational limit sequence for each cell is obtained by selecting the largest accuracy prefix whose computations have stabilized under the currently available finite simulation, or by the ordinary limit lemma for a uniformly oracle-computable real. More explicitly, produce simultaneous approximations $a_{m,n}$ at all precisions $m\\le n$ by the bounded simulations, with any missing one set to zero. Starting from $[0,1]$, intersect successively with the closed intervals of radius $2^{1-m}$ about $a_{m,n}$, stopping at the first empty intersection, and output the midpoint of the last nonempty interval. For each fixed $m$, all computations up to $m$ eventually give values within $2^{-i}$ at precision $i\\le m$, so these intersections contain the target and the output is eventually in a radius-$2^{1-m}$ interval about its correct $m$th approximant. Hence it converges. This supplies the required single uniformly computable dyadic probe array.\n\nThe actual tester probability is instead\n\\[\n\\int_A f(x)\\,dx=\\frac{\\alpha}{3}+(1-\\alpha)\\sum_{j\\in J}4^{-j}.\n\\]\nThe digit real on the right is not computable from $K$. If it were, the $j$th digit could be recovered inductively after subtracting the preceding digits: the remaining tail after the $j$th digit is at most $4^{-j}/3$, leaving a strict gap between the two choices. This would compute the oracle halting jump $J$ from $K$, contrary to the relativized diagonal argument. Since $\\alpha$ is computable and $1-\\alpha>0$, the tester probability is likewise not computable from $K$. The limit of any computable rational sequence is computable from $K$: for each precision, the oracle decides whether some later pair in the sequence violates the specified Cauchy tolerance, and a search finds a sufficiently late term. Therefore the displayed tester probability is not limit computable.\n\nAt each finite stage on the stipulated port, the actual fixed tester is implemented by its computable compressed cell probabilities, and the finite source and tester output probabilities can be evaluated to accuracy $2^{-n}$. A source tower converging in the required tester comparison would make the displayed non-limit-computable probability a limit of those rational stage values. This is impossible. The obstruction retains absolute continuity of the offered law and uses neither a return wiring nor an unobserved source memory. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bm\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bm-multi-turn-diffuse-releases-with-quantum-feedback\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3bm (Multi-Turn Diffuse Releases with Quantum Feedback).** Fix a finite clocked schedule of turns. At each turn the source accepts its named finite-dimensional quantum input, then releases its named finite-dimensional quantum output and a classical coordinate $x_j\\in[0,1]$. A later input may be the actual quantum return of an intervention on the earlier releases. On each classical wire use the dyadic cell encodings of Theorem O.7.2.3bl, with a separate stipulated uniform decoding for each cell. The externally prescribed process is a causal comb whose normalized weighted Choi state has a positive integrable matrix density $F(x_1,\\ldots,x_k)$ with respect to product Lebesgue measure. Its prefix-causal identities concern the actual release order. In particular, tracing out future quantum outputs and integrating future releases recovers the prefix instrument tensored with the independent faithful states on unused input copies. The external comb data suffice for the construction. See Chiribella et al. (2009).\n\nSupply a nonnegative computable $L^1$ function $g$ on the classical product carrier such that $\\operatorname{Tr}F\\le g$ almost everywhere. The comparison includes every fixed causal tester whose interventions can depend measurably on all available release coordinates and earlier outcomes, with quantum returns on the named wires. Its retained terminal output is separable trace class or a countable classical–quantum sum of such spaces, including fixed spectators and outcomes. Conditional quantum operations are normal and give strongly measurable operations on each fixed trace-class input. This includes normal classical–quantum feedback with finitely many exposed outcomes and strongly measurable state-dependent instruments. A physically installed tester uses its effective finite compressions where supplied. The comparison of all fixed testers uses their exact compressions as in Theorem O.7.2.3at.\n\nOne uniform paid finite Hermitian-control tower realizes this comparison in the complete terminal trace norm if and only if the real and imaginary entries of\n\\[\n\\int_{I_1\\times\\cdots\\times I_k} F(x)\\,dx\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bm.1}\n\\]\nhave uniform limit-computable names for all product dyadic cells. The limit name is a single computable array indexed by the rectangle, entry and approximation number, convergent at each fixed index. No convergence modulus is required. The same tower supplies the joint retained output against bounded continuous tests of the classical release word, and its fixed finite-grid step path comparison uses the declared release times. It does not impose a path law at other times or require a strong retained diffuse terminal output.\n\n*Finite causal approximation.* Work with unweighted Choi coordinates when imposing causal identities, and choose the maximally mixed faithful tester on every finite input, so the conversion to normalized weighted Choi coordinates is multiplication by known rational factors. On the finite dyadic product partition of a common depth $n$, integrate $F$ on each product cell. These positive Choi matrices form a finite instrument tree with exactly the prescribed causal normalization. Indeed the prefix identities commute with the integrations on all released cells and with the sums over future cells. Causality puts each coarse cell label at its own original release, not at a later terminal lookup. Retaining the tensor product of uniform conditional cell laws on those named wires gives a decoded density equal to the dyadic conditional expectation $F^{(n)}$. The latter converges to $F$ in trace-norm $L^1$, by dyadic simple approximation of the finitely many matrix entries. Moreover\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}F^{(n)}(x)\\le g^{(n)}(x)\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bm.2}\n\\]\nfor $g^{(n)}$ the conditional expectation on this partition.\n\nFor effective enumeration, at each depth list finite rational positive Choi tuples with the exact linear causal identities. Include the condition that the trace mass of each cell $I$ is less than $\\int_I g+|I|$, certifying the strict inequality by effective integration of the named $g$. These tuples approximate the preceding finite instrument tree arbitrarily well. To verify this, first mix the given tuple by a sufficiently small positive weight with the full-rank comb that emits every cell independently and uniformly and uses full-rank depolarizing quantum outputs. Its Choi blocks are strictly positive. All the trace inequalities have positive slack for a sufficiently small mixing weight, since the original masses satisfy (O.7.2.3bm.2) and the number of cells is finite. The exact finite causal identities form an affine system with rational coefficients. Rational points are dense in its real solution space, by rational elimination and approximation of free coordinates. Approximate the mixed tuple within this space, sufficiently close to preserve strict positivity and each trace bound. This proves density of the catalogue for every fixed finite collection of dyadic matrix tests. The finite causal realization of each tuple follows from the finite comb dilation used in Theorem O.7.2.3am, with each cell as an ordinary classical outcome at its own turn.\n\nEnumerate the tests and catalogue and use the weighted finite selection of Theorem O.7.2.3am with the supplied limit names. For every fixed finite initial test list one fixed candidate fits it to arbitrary desired error. It remains available at later stages, its finite list of supplied values converges, and the remaining geometric test weights are arbitrarily small. Thus the selected decoded instruments converge on all fixed dyadic Choi entries. Implement their finite causal dilations by the counted Hermitian controls and positive physical subslots of Theorem O.7.2.3m. Choose the total pulse error at stage $j$ small enough that it is at most $2^{-j}$ in complete output trace norm and changes each actual Choi cell mass by less than the smallest cell volume. This is a computable tolerance on that finite circuit. The actual decoded stage density $F_j$ is positive and normalized, and its scalar trace density then obeys\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Tr}F_j\\le g^{(n(j))}+2 .\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bm.3}\n\\]\nThe actual stage matrix tests still converge. All finite quantum memory, previous release labels, resource records and precise clock addresses are kept in this implementation.\n\n*Convergence under fixed adaptive testers.* The family of right sides of (O.7.2.3bm.3) is uniformly integrable. Indeed write $g=g\\mathbf1_{\\{g\\le M\\}}+g\\mathbf1_{\\{g>M\\}}$. The first conditional expectation is bounded by $M$, and the second has $L^1$ norm equal to its integral, tending to zero as $M\\to\\infty$ uniformly in the partition. This also bounds the integral of $g^{(n)}+2$ on every set of small Lebesgue measure, uniformly in $n$.\n\nFix an actual tester. Expanding its finite source input and output wires in matrix units gives its complete terminal output as a finite sum of integrals of the form\n\\[\n\\int F_{ab}(x)\\,K_{ab}(x)\\,dx .\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bm.4}\n\\]\nHere the $K_{ab}$ are bounded strongly measurable functions into the retained terminal trace-class carrier, including fixed spectator and outcome registers. To verify this expansion, link the tester in chronological order to the finite Choi wires, carrying the unnormalized state of its memory. At each linkage the summation is over the finite matrix units of the source wire, with the fixed inverse weights of its faithful input copies. A test conditional on $x_1,\\ldots,x_j$ is the stipulated complete instrument on that prefix. Inserting its branch with its outcome unnormalized, and then linking the returned matrix units, is a bounded linear map. Its action on these finitely many terms is bounded by the complete trace-norm contraction and the finite inverse weights. For a general complex entry use its decomposition into Hermitian terms. Strong measurability on each fixed trace-class input and the common bound imply measurability on previously obtained varying trace-class inputs by simple-function approximation. Thus the chronological product gives the stated bounded strongly measurable kernels, with any final processing included. This also shows that (O.7.2.3bm.4) keeps the dependence of subsequent releases on actual quantum returns. It does not replace an adaptive process by independent draws from a classical history law. Finite source cells use exactly the integrated conditional tester action of the stipulated uniform decoding. Since a within-cell value is available to the tester and can be kept in its classical memory until subsequent turns, the expression is evaluated on the full decoded product coordinates with the tester acting causally on their prefixes.\n\nFor each $K_{ab}$, choose bounded dyadic simple approximants converging in measure in trace norm. They exist by strong measurability and the density of the dyadic algebra in Lebesgue measure, followed by bounded clipping of the simple values. On the set where the difference has norm at most $\\delta$, its contribution in (O.7.2.3bm.4) is at most $\\delta$ times the total trace. On the complement its norm is bounded by a common constant times the integral of the scalar trace density there. By (O.7.2.3bm.3) and uniform integrability, the latter can be made uniformly small over the actual stages. The same holds for $F$. For a fixed dyadic simple kernel the convergence is the finite set of Choi tests (O.7.2.3bm.1), so approximation and summation give convergence in the complete output trace norm. Multiplying by a bounded continuous test of the retained release word gives the same argument. Classical step-path interpolation at the fixed release times is continuous for uniform coordinate convergence, hence in $J_1$ on its image. Its laws are tight since the finite coordinate cube is compact. Positive trace-norm convergence of the integrated terminal output gives the fixed-carrier quantum tail test. These are the stated joint fixed-grid QJ comparisons.\n\n*Necessity.* A fixed finite tester can retain the chosen finite Choi preparations and outputs, and at each actual classical release record membership in its nominated dyadic cell. The complete retained prefix outcomes allow testing the product of these events at the end, together with finite Hermitian polarization tests of the quantum entries. These are effective normal tests on all the dyadic encodings. Their actual finite-stage probabilities are computable to accuracy $2^{-j}$ on stage $j$. Required convergence gives the uniform limit names for (O.7.2.3bm.1) by the same fixed finite polarization. This does not assume that the limiting target coefficients can be computed with a rate. The scalar example of Theorem O.7.2.3bl can be embedded by giving all other releases independent uniform laws and all quantum wires fixed trivial maps. Its forbidden tester reads the one obstructing release, so absolute continuity alone cannot replace the effective integrable domination in this implication. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bn\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bn-strong-diffuse-retention-in-prescribed-causal-histories\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3bn (Strong Diffuse Retention in Prescribed Causal Histories).** Use the finite clocked turn schedule, finite-dimensional quantum intervention wires and actual diffuse releases of Theorem O.7.2.3bm. A tester may use the available classical prefix and its outcomes to control its later quantum returns. Keep each released coordinate in its declared dyadic uniform-cell encoding. At the terminal comparison retain the full actual diffuse word $x=(x_1,\\ldots,x_k)$ in the strong product predual. Its reference measure is Lebesgue measure on $[0,1]^k$. The source may also have a named separable terminal quantum carrier with effective finite coordinate encodings, including a countable classical–quantum sum of such carriers. Its complete externally prescribed causal Choi density $F$ is positive, integrable in the trace norm over $x$, and normalized. It obeys the actual prefix instrument identities with the terminal quantum carrier traced out at earlier prefixes. The input data are the external Choi density and the named carrier encodings.\n\nA uniform paid finite tower realizes the strong comparison if and only if $F$ has a limit-computable name in\n\\[\nL^1\\!\\left([0,1]^k;\\mathcal T(\\mathcal H_{\\mathrm{Choi}})\\right).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bn.1}\n\\]\nA name here is a single computable sequence of rational finite-coordinate matrix-valued dyadic simple functions converging in integrated trace norm. No rate is assumed, and the simple approximants supplied in the name need not themselves be positive or causal. The strong comparison is convergence in the same $L^1$ norm on the terminal word and complete quantum output after every fixed normal causal tester of Theorem O.7.2.3bm, including fixed spectators and all its retained outcomes. The mathematical comparison uses exact tester compressions on the decoded source wires. A physically installed tester uses its declared effective conditional operations and retained memory. The source requires no integrable envelope $g$.\n\nThe strong requirement is strictly finer than the weak tester criterion of Theorem O.7.2.3bm. There is a scalar density $1/2\\le f\\le3/2$ whose dyadic masses are uniformly limit computable, but which has no name (O.7.2.3bn.1). The example therefore has a uniform tower for the weak tester comparison of Theorem O.7.2.3bm and has none for strong retention of its actual diffuse coordinate.\n\n*Norm-dense finite causal sources.* The dyadic conditional expectation on the full classical product partition preserves positivity and integrated trace. It preserves the exact finite prefix-causal identities by integrating each actual release in its cell and summing over subsequent cells. On the terminal separable carrier insert its finite trace-preserving coordinate channel as in Theorem O.7.2.3am. These channels tend strongly to the identity on trace class. Their integrated error on $F$ tends to zero by domination by $2\\operatorname{Tr}F$. Dyadic conditional expectations tend to $F$ in the same integrated norm, by density of dyadic simple finite-coordinate functions and trace-norm contraction. Thus finite causal instrument trees with cell outcomes are dense in the norm (O.7.2.3bn.1). The channel cutoffs preserve the normalized prefix identities because they act on a terminal output and are trace preserving.\n\nAt each finite level the causal identities are affine linear constraints with rational coefficients in the unweighted finite Choi convention. The normalized weighted convention for the finite incoming wires uses maximally mixed input weights. Given a finite causal instrument tree, first mix it by arbitrarily small positive weight with a full-rank depolarizing tree that emits independent uniform cell labels. Then all of its Choi blocks are strictly positive on the allowed finite output blocks. Approximate within the rational affine constraints to preserve positivity and the exact normalization. This is possible by rational linear elimination and density of rational free coordinates. For a classical–quantum terminal output the same argument is blockwise. Hence the finite rational positive Choi tuples obeying these constraints give an effective catalogue dense in (O.7.2.3bn.1). Each tuple has the finite sequential instrument realization used in Theorem O.7.2.3am. Its cell at each turn is emitted at that turn, then decoded by the stipulated uniform cell density. This source retains the cell histories and its finite physical memory in the paid inventory.\n\nGiven the supplied convergent name $A_j$ of $F$, at stage $j$ choose among the first $j$ catalogue members within $2^{-j}$ of the least $L^1$ discrepancy to $A_j$. This discrepancy is computable: refine the two finite dyadic grids and embed their finite quantum matrix supports in a common finite carrier, then sum the cell volumes times the trace norms of the difference matrices. For any tolerance, one fixed catalogue member approximates $F$ to that tolerance and remains available for all later stages. Convergence of $A_j$ and the triangle inequality show convergence of the selected densities to $F$. Install the finite instrument tree by the paid Hermitian controls and positive-duration clock of Theorem O.7.2.3m. Choose pulse errors so the complete finite process trace-norm perturbation is at most $2^{-j}$. Uniform-cell decoding is a trace-preserving channel and cannot increase this perturbation in (O.7.2.3bn.1). Thus the actual decoded stage densities $F_j$ have the same convergence.\n\n*Complete strong tester comparison.* Fix a tester in the stated class. For each classical word, link its conditional operations on the actual prefixes to the finite-dimensional source quantum wires. There is a bounded linear map $\\mathcal V_x$ from the Choi matrices with the terminal quantum carrier to the retained terminal trace class, including any fixed ready state and spectator. Its action on $F(x)$ gives the unnormalized terminal density with that word. A uniform bound on the map norm follows by expanding the finitely many source wires in matrix units. Each insertion or extraction of a matrix coordinate has norm at most one on the corresponding trace-class block. The fixed inverse Choi weights and the finite sums contribute a constant depending only on the fixed wire dimensions. The intervening complete tester operations are trace-norm contractions on Hermitian inputs, and the decomposition into real and imaginary Hermitian parts gives a fixed bound for complex terms. Earlier outcomes can be carried by their direct-sum record throughout this expansion. Thus one obtains a constant $C$ independent of $x$, the stage and the tester memory dimension. Measurability on each fixed trace-class input and the uniform bound give measurability on varying trace-class inputs by approximation by simple functions, so the linked densities are integrable. This is the same causal linkage as in Theorem O.7.2.3bm, with its unnormalized outcome branches and no replacement of the tester's earlier-coordinate memory. In particular the linked density for a finite source uses its full actual decoded word, not separate averages of later operations that forget the earlier coordinates. Consequently\n\\[\n\\int\\|\\mathcal V_x(F_j(x))-\\mathcal V_x(F(x))\\|_1\\,dx\n\\le C\\int\\|F_j(x)-F(x)\\|_1\\,dx \\longrightarrow 0 .\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bn.2}\n\\]\nThis also proves the retained word probabilities in total variation and the finite-grid path comparison after any fixed measurable decoding of that word. Fixed countable or separable outcomes have their trace-class tails because the integrated terminal densities converge in norm. The statement does not assign a prescribed path between the declared releases.\n\nConversely take the tester that supplies at each input the corresponding independent faithful finite Choi pair, retains the partner, and stores all quantum outputs and the full diffuse word. Its actual terminal density is the weighted Choi density. The stipulated strong comparison for this one tester therefore requires $F_j\\to F$ in (O.7.2.3bn.1). At an actual finite stage its positive density has a finite dyadic label table, a finite quantum coordinate representation and effective coefficients. It can be approximated in integrated trace norm by a rational dyadic finite-matrix function to accuracy $2^{-j}$, using the finite cell volumes and finite coefficient estimates. These rational approximants give the claimed name. This uses only actual stage computations and the required convergence, with no target rate.\n\n*Strictness for a bounded scalar release.* Let $K$ be the ordinary halting set and $J$ its oracle halting jump, as in Theorem O.7.2.3bl. Take the pairwise disjoint dyadic intervals $I_j=[2^{-j-1},2^{-j})$, $j\\ge1$, with the remaining interval assigned density one. If $j$ never enters $J$, also give $I_j$ density one. If $j$ enters at oracle stage $s$, give it density $1+(1/2)r_{s+1}$ in its rescaled dyadic coordinate, where $r_{s+1}$ is the function taking $+1,-1$ on the two successive halves of each relative depth-$s$ dyadic cell. The perturbation has zero integral in each such cell. This defines a positive normalized $f$ with the stated pointwise bounds. On an absolute depth-$\\ell$ dyadic cell, every change made at stage $s\\ge\\ell$ is invisible. The total contribution of the omitted $I_j$ after a finite truncation is bounded by $3/2$ times the geometric tail of their lengths. Thus the cell probability is computable with oracle $K$: simulate only finitely many stages $s<\\ell$ for the retained intervals, with effective dyadic overlaps. The explicit bounded-simulation limit construction of Theorem O.7.2.3bl gives one uniformly computable rational limit array for all these probabilities.\n\nHowever\n\\[\n\\int_{I_j}|f(x)-1|\\,dx=\n\\begin{cases}\n|I_j|/2,&j\\in J,\\\\\n0,&j\\notin J .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bn.3}\n\\]\nA limit-computable $L^1$ name would make these numbers computable with oracle $K$, uniformly in $j$. Indeed the oracle can decide whether there is a later pair of rational simple approximants whose integrated norm distance exceeds a specified rational tolerance. Searching for a tail with no such pair computes the limit to any specified accuracy. The norm after restriction to $I_j$ and subtraction of one is a computable Lipschitz functional on this presentation. The gap in (O.7.2.3bn.3) would then decide $J$ with oracle $K$, a contradiction. The weak theorem applies with computable constant envelope $3/2$. Additional trivial quantum turns and independent uniform releases embed the same distinction into the stated finite-turn class. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bo\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bo-prescribed-causal-histories-with-completed-path-segments\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3bo (Prescribed Causal Histories with Completed Path Segments).** Fix a dyadic horizon and nested effective finite checkpoint grids. At every checkpoint the source uses named finite-dimensional quantum wires. It releases, before the checkpoint intervention, a classical record of the entire completed interval in the prescribed bounded-metric Polish $J_1$ path space on that interval. The intervention may act on the offered quantum output, retain any fixed spectator and memory, and return a quantum input that changes later interval histories. The segment becomes available at its right checkpoint.\n\nFor grid $k$, write $D_k$ for the finite product of its named interval path spaces, with the maximum metric, and $M_k$ for the positive trace-class-valued weighted Choi measure on $D_k$ of the externally prescribed causal instrument. The finite incoming wires use independent maximally mixed faithful Choi tests, with their partner registers retained. The complete terminal quantum carrier may additionally be a named separable trace-class space or a countable classical–quantum direct sum. Causality means that integrating future records and tracing future outputs gives the actual prefix instrument tensored with the unused independent input partners. In particular, each segment is an actual outcome of the prefix ending at its release. No representation of the internal source memory is part of the input.\n\nAssume exact compatibility under the prescribed restrictions to coarser grids. Join the quantum wires at omitted checkpoints by their declared fixed identity or other finite causal operations. Combine the fine segment records of each retained interval by concatenation, using the following segment at an interior endpoint and the final segment at the terminal endpoint. The restrictions are effective on finite coded operations and named finite step records. This includes a deterministic jump at an old intervention checkpoint with the same endpoint convention. A tester on a fixed grid may depend on its completed segment prefix and earlier outcomes, with the complete conditional operation, including memory and retained outcomes, continuous in the segment arguments in diamond norm. Its declared returned quantum wire is routed to the actual next source operation. Mathematical comparisons use these exact fixed tester operations; physical tester services require their stated effective finite implementations.\n\nThere exists one uniform paid finite Hermitian-control tower giving the complete joint path and coherent outputs for these comparisons if and only if one computable array supplies convergent rational names of the following prescribed values, uniformly in the grid and test indices:\n\\[\n\\int_{D_k} f(z)\\langle a|M_k(dz)|b\\rangle ,\n\\qquad\n\\int_{D_k}q_{m,F}(z)\\operatorname{Tr}M_k(dz).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bo.1}\n\\]\nHere $a,b$ are finite Choi coordinates, $f$ ranges over the rational tent algebra of Theorem O.7.2.3z including the constant function, and $q_{m,F}(z)=\\min(1,2^m d(z,F))$ uses its finite named sets in $D_k$. The first tests use real and imaginary parts separately. No rate is assumed. The joint comparison is trace-norm convergence of output integrals against every fixed bounded continuous test on the segment word, and hence the joint $J_1$ and QJ comparison after concatenation. It retains the tester outputs, all the stipulated source quantum outputs and fixed spectators.\n\n*Finite-grid density and causality.* At a fixed fine grid, choose a finite list of prescribed mixed tests. The positive trace law of its weighted Choi measure is tight. For each interval choose finitely many named step paths approximating that interval's coordinate on a compact set of high trace probability. Quantize the interval independently at its actual release by assigning each Borel cell to one named path; use one fallback name for the remainder. The quantized joint Choi matrices are the positive masses of the original measure on product cells. Their normalization and prefix identities hold exactly because cell assignments at a release depend only on the corresponding completed segment. Summing over future cells and tracing future quantum outputs therefore commutes with this record quantization. A quantized segment is not selected from a later output. The product record error tends to zero in probability as the compact sets and quantization precision improve. Each bounded continuous mixed test consequently converges, with the trace law controlling the absolute matrix-entry errors. Compress the named terminal quantum carrier by the finite trace-preserving coordinate channels of Theorem O.7.2.3am. They preserve the prefix identities and approximate each positive trace-class mass in trace norm. The finite normalized causal Choi tuples admit the sequential instrument dilation of that theorem, with the selected path name as an actual classical outcome at its own checkpoint. Approximation of the finite dilation isometries by algebraic ones retains those outcome slots and gives an effective finite catalogue.\n\nThe same approximation fits any finite collection of coarser-grid tests. First take a fine prescribed grid containing those checkpoints and use its exact compatibility. Coarser quantum joins on the finite exposed wires are fixed bounded linear contractions or finite linear combinations of contractions on the Choi blocks. A coarser segment test pulls back along concatenation to a bounded continuous test of the finer segment word. Indeed glue the interval time changes at their endpoints. They approach the identity and give the same uniform spatial control on each half-open segment. At an interior endpoint both concatenated paths take the following segment's initial value, and at the terminal endpoint both take the final value. Thus the record map is $J_1$-continuous on the finite product even when a terminal value is separately recorded. Its finite step implementation is effective. Positivity, normalization and this continuity establish density for both mixed and trace probes at the chosen coarser grids.\n\n*Selecting one compatible tower.* Enumerate the real tests of (O.7.2.3bo.1), dividing each by a fixed computable bound when needed. Each has a known grid index. Use the finite catalogue selection of (O.7.2.3am.2), with the missing-depth penalty and the actual grid of the chosen finite circuit. The coarser scores are computable by the stipulated finite restrictions and path probe evaluations. Density at an arbitrarily deep fixed grid supplies one coded candidate fitting every fixed finite list to any chosen accuracy. This candidate remains available at all sufficiently late stages. Convergence of the names on that fixed list and the geometric tail of the loss prove convergence of the loss to zero. The depth penalty tends to zero, so every fixed grid is eventually present. The selected actual probabilities and entries then converge to all of (O.7.2.3bo.1).\n\nImplement these finite causal circuits by the paid Hermitian controls, finite clocks and named path-name releases of Theorem O.7.2.3m. Every segment name is a finite classical register emitted at its prescribed checkpoint. Fresh apparatus memory, path table, routing and precision records have their own paid finite inventory. The total pulse norm error may be chosen to give at most $2^{-n}$ complete trace-norm perturbation at stage $n$; this has no effect on the limiting bounded tests. The segment names are records of the prescribed comparison. The theorem does not read an unprovided live coordinate between checkpoints.\n\n*Testing the selected source with feedback.* At any fixed grid, the scalar trace probability of $M_k$ is tight. The tightness probes in (O.7.2.3bo.1) and Theorem O.7.2.3z therefore give tightness of the actual segment trace laws. The mixed tent tests, including the constant, give trace-norm convergence of all fixed bounded continuous integrals of the Choi measures by that theorem. In particular the total trace-one masses converge in trace norm, which supplies their finite-rank quantum tails.\n\nFor a fixed tester with the stated segment continuity, expand the finitely many exposed source quantum wires in matrix units, using the known finite inverse Choi weights. Connecting its complete conditional operations along a segment word gives a uniformly bounded linear map on the source terminal trace class, with the tester's own fixed ready state, memory, outcomes and spectator included. The bound depends only on the finite exposed wire dimensions, because each complete conditional map contracts trace norm on Hermitian inputs and the finite matrix extraction and insertion maps have bounded norm. Diamond continuity of the complete operations gives continuity of the linked output on every fixed matrix input as a function of the full segment word. This uses the chronological product of unnormalized outcome operations. The tester's next return, and hence the source's next conditional dynamics, can depend on the actual earlier segment; no independent sample of the future segment law is inserted.\n\nTo verify convergence after that linkage, first restrict the source Choi measures to a common finite quantum corner. The removed part of each integrated measure is uniformly small in trace norm by the positive-state corner estimate (Q1), applied to the total trace mass and its corner tail. This estimate also controls bounded weighted integrals, since the norm integral of the discarded off-corner blocks is bounded by the Cauchy–Schwarz estimate for positive block measures. Multiplying the resulting small error by the finite linkage bound leaves it small for the actual tester. On the retained finite corner the linked output is a finite sum of bounded continuous trace-class-valued functions of the segment word integrated against scalar Choi entry measures. On a compact segment set these functions admit uniformly close finite linear combinations of fixed output matrices and scalar continuous functions, by a finite continuous partition of unity. The functions can be chosen bounded on the whole product, and the trace-law tails control the complement. Each scalar integral converges by the tent and tightness criterion. Removing the two cutoffs proves trace-norm convergence of the complete linked outputs. The same proof works with any fixed bounded continuous multiplier on the segment word. This supplies the quantum tail condition for the tester's retained carrier as well. Concatenation then gives the full $J_1$ record comparison with the declared reset convention at the checkpoints.\n\nFor necessity use the finite faithful Choi tester with independent incoming halves, retained partners, and the actual successive segment records. Both classes of probes in (O.7.2.3bo.1) are bounded continuous effective tests of the named finite decoded records. An actual uniform finite tower computes each such test to any rational precision at a stage that contains its grid; one can use error $2^{-n}$ at stage $n$ and a default value at insufficient depths. Required convergence gives the indicated uniform limit names. This does not assume effective convergence of the target law. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bp\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bp-causal-quantum-feedback-with-actual-diffuse-returns\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3bp (Causal Quantum Feedback with Actual Diffuse Returns).** Fix a finite grid with $r$ turns. At turn $j$ the source offers a fresh independent uniform coordinate $x_j\\in[0,1)$ and a finite quantum output $A_j$. A tester may retain memory and a fixed spectator and return the actual coordinate $y_j\\in[0,1)$ jointly with a finite quantum input $B_j$. The source then proceeds to its next turn, with $A_{r+1}$ its terminal quantum output. Testers are normal measurable causal instruments whose actual returned coordinate has an absolutely continuous marginal on its declared port. The joint offered and returned coordinates may have a singular law. Compare the complete terminal trace norm including the tester's records.\n\nUse the unweighted deterministic comb Choi matrix $C(x,y)$ for each full word, with $x=(x_1,\\ldots,x_r)$ and $y=(y_1,\\ldots,y_r)$. Its prefix $C^{(j)}$ through output $A_j$ depends only on $x_{\\le j},y_{<j}$, and the positive matrices obey the causal identities $\\operatorname{Tr} C^{(1)}=1$ and $\\operatorname{Tr}_{A_j}C^{(j)}=I_{B_{j-1}}\\otimes C^{(j-1)}$ for $2\\le j\\le r+1$. These identities hold for every returned word outside one common offered-word null set. In $x$, the full matrix is Bochner integrable with values in the uniform trace-norm closure of finite dyadic step profiles in $y$. Use the pointwise uniform-limit representatives for the returns, with half-open cells. This lets the source act on actual singular couplings.\n\nLet $d_{\\rm ext}(C,D)$ be the supremum of terminal trace-norm differences over the stated testers. For finite dyadic causal tables it is the optimum of a finite two-outcome causal tester problem on a common cell refinement, computable for rational tables. On the stated profiles this finite distance extends continuously, with\n$$\nd_{\\rm ext}(C,D)\\le K\\int\\sup_y\\|C(x,y)-D(x,y)\\|_1\\,dx,\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bp.1}\n$$\nwhere $K$ depends only on the fixed quantum wire dimensions. A uniform paid finite tower converging uniformly over these testers exists exactly when $C$ has a limit-computable $d_{\\rm ext}$ name. Such a name is a computable sequence of rational positive normalized finite dyadic causal tables converging in $d_{\\rm ext}$, with no prescribed rate. A computable convergent sequence in the stronger norm on the right of (O.7.2.3bp.1) suffices as well.\n\n*Tester comparison.* A terminal trace-norm test is equivalent to a two-outcome measurement with signed values $\\pm1$. For fixed offered word $x$, retain the tester's return coordinates and the two final outcomes in the finite open-wire Choi representation. This gives positive matrix measures $T_{x,+}(dy)$ and $T_{x,-}(dy)$ in the dual Choi convention with probability pairing $\\operatorname{Tr}(CT)$. Positivity and countable additivity follow by composing the normal instruments and testing the resulting finite-wire matrix entries. The sum of the two integrated matrices is the deterministic tester for the fixed offered word. Its finite causal trace identities fix its trace $K$, independently of private memory dimension. The fresh independent offers supply the product Lebesgue integral in $x$. At a finite table the signed probability difference is the integral of $\\operatorname{Tr}[(C-D)(T_{x,+}-T_{x,-})]$ over the offered and returned words. This pairing is bounded by the right side of (O.7.2.3bp.1), because the sum of the two positive conditional trace measures has mass $K$. Uniform limits in the returned coordinates and Bochner approximation in the offered coordinates extend the pairing and bound. A common offered-word null set contributes zero; no absolute continuity of the conditional returned measure at fixed $x$ is needed.\n\n*Finite optimization and realization.* For two finite tables, a tester acting on cell labels can simulate the diffuse comparison by drawing each actual $x_j$ uniformly in its offered cell as that label arrives, running the prescribed instrument and returning its selected $y_j$ cell together with its actual quantum wire. It retains all sampled coordinates and memory for its terminal test. Conversely a finite tester is implemented on the diffuse ports by reading the offered cell and drawing the returned coordinate uniformly in its chosen return cell, with the same quantum outputs and memory. Both constructions preserve the two final probabilities. The finite two-outcome tester has positive effect matrices whose sum satisfies the deterministic tester trace constraints. These form a compact spectrahedron, and the signed probability difference is linear. Finite causal dilation realizes the feasible testers. For rational tables the optimum is computable, for example by bisection with semialgebraic feasibility for rational thresholds. See Chiribella et al. (2009).\n\nTo approximate a source, replace every returned coordinate by the left endpoint of its dyadic cell and average the offered coordinates on the product dyadic partition. These operations preserve positivity and prefix causality. Return sampling is a contraction in the uniform profile norm and is eventually exact on every finite dyadic step profile. It therefore converges on the uniform closure and then in the integrated norm by domination. Offer averaging converges in that norm by density of dyadic simple Bochner functions. At each finite grid mix with a strictly positive product comb to obtain strict positivity, then approximate within the rational affine prefix identities. This proves the claimed density and extension of the finite distance. The finite causal dilation of Theorem O.7.2.3am and the Hermitian controls of Theorem O.7.2.3m implement each rational table on the encoded wires. Uniform offer labels use fair bits and the stipulated uniform-cell decoding. The actual return uses its cell label and the unmeasured quantum wire. All finite clocks, controls, encodings and records enter the source ledger. Telescoping the finitely many pulse errors in complete trace norm makes their total at most $2^{-n}$ at stage $n$, uniformly over testers. Conversely each actual finite stage has an effectively named finite causal table. Approximate it by a rational positive causal table to external error $2^{-n}$ using the rational density and the finite comparison. Required convergence then gives the limit name.\n\n*One-turn exact norm and two-turn boundary.* With one turn and a trivial offered quantum wire, write the terminal channels as $\\Phi(x,y)$ and $\\Psi(x,y)$. Then\n$$\nd_{\\rm ext}(\\Phi,\\Psi)=\\int_0^1\\sup_{0\\le y<1}\\|\\Phi(x,y)-\\Psi(x,y)\\|_{\\diamond}\\,dx .\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bp.2}\n$$\nThe diamond norm of a channel difference tests a density matrix on the returned wire with an ancilla of dimension at most that of the wire. The norm bounds the error for any conditional input, including entanglement with the tester's memory. For a finite table choose on each offered cell a returned cell and a joint input attaining the maximum, return uniformly within that cell, and retain orthogonal flags for the offered cells. Their errors add, giving equality. The density argument and finite-dimensional norm equivalence extend the formula. For example, let one qubit channel act as the identity when $x,y$ share their first dyadic cell and depolarize otherwise, and let the second always depolarize. The normal return $y=x$ has a singular joint law. A Bell input with retained partner gives trace-norm difference $3/2$, from eigenvalues $3/4,-1/4,-1/4,-1/4$, whereas separable inputs give at most one by convexity.\n\nWith two turns, a pointwise channel optimum can use information the causal tester does not yet have. Take the first return to be a qubit and let all remaining intermediate quantum wires be trivial. The first source outputs the success bit of measuring the first qubit with projector $|i\\rangle\\langle i|$, where $i$ is the first bit of the independent second offer. The second source always outputs zero. The pointwise difference is two for either bit, but the tester supplies the qubit before that bit is available. Its averaged success probability is $1/2$, even with a spectator. The terminal norm is at most twice that probability, by the positive success block and trace-preserving subsequent processing. Retaining the terminal bit attains one. This demonstrates why the multi-turn comparison uses the causal optimization. $\\square$\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bq\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bq-closure-for-measurable-diffuse-quantum-returns\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3bq (Closure for Measurable Diffuse Quantum Returns).** Use the single-turn source of Theorem O.7.2.3bp with a uniform offered \\(x\\in[0,1)\\), no offered quantum wire and a joint return of \\(y\\in[0,1)\\) and the finite wire \\(B\\). The terminal quantum output is finite. Allow any Borel channel field \\(\\Phi(x,y)\\) on those wires. A normal tester may return any law with absolutely continuous unconditional \\(y\\)-marginal, may keep quantum and classical records, and uses the actual supplied coordinates. In particular \\(\\Phi\\) is specified as a Borel representative on the full pair space.\n\nFor Borel fields \\(\\Phi,\\Psi\\), the exact uniform complete output distance is\n\\[\nd_{\\rm ext}(\\Phi,\\Psi)=\n\\sup_{\\gamma_X=dx,\\ \\gamma_Y\\ll dy}\n\\int \\|\\Phi(x,y)-\\Psi(x,y)\\|_\\diamond\\,d\\gamma(x,y).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bq.1}\n\\]\nIf the supremum is restricted to \\(\\gamma\\ll dx\\,dy\\), its value is \\(\\int\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_y\\|\\Phi(x,y)-\\Psi(x,y)\\|_\\diamond\\,dx\\).\n\nFor the half-open depth-\\(k\\) dyadic cells \\(J\\) of the returned coordinate put\n\\[\n\\omega_k(x)=\\max_J\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_{(y,z)\\in J^2}\n             \\|\\Phi(x,y)-\\Phi(x,z)\\|_\\diamond .\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bq.2}\n\\]\nThe essential supremum in (O.7.2.3bq.2) is for two independent Lebesgue coordinates within \\(J\\). The field \\(\\Phi\\) is in the uniform external closure of finite dyadic channel tables if and only if both of the following conditions hold.\n\n1. \\(\\int\\omega_k(x)\\,dx\\to0\\).\n2. If \\(G(x,y)\\) is the pointwise uniform-in-\\(y\\) limit of the returned-cell averages of \\(\\Phi(x,\\cdot)\\) for almost every \\(x\\), then \\(\\Phi=G\\) off \\((N_X\\times[0,1))\\cup([0,1)\\times N_Y)\\) for some Borel Lebesgue-null sets \\(N_X,N_Y\\).\n\nThe first condition guarantees existence of \\(G\\), with a fixed constant channel used on the exceptional offered set. This is a dyadically regulated Borel channel field in the sense of Theorem O.7.2.3bp. On passing both conditions, a uniform finite paid tower of the specified dyadic stages exists exactly when \\(G\\) has the limit-computable mixed diamond norm name of that theorem. This uses no convergence modulus.\n\n*Proof of the distance formula.* For each tester the returned positive conditional states have a scalar trace law \\(\\gamma\\) on the pair space. As in Theorem O.7.2.3bp the channel difference on each state, including a retained spectator, is bounded in trace norm by its diamond distance times the trace mass. Integration gives the upper bound in (O.7.2.3bq.1). For the lower bound disintegrate any allowed \\(\\gamma\\) over its uniform first marginal. The resulting kernel is a normal return operation on the prescribed diffuse ports. Indeed, multiplying the joint law by any nonnegative integrable density of \\(x\\) still gives an absolutely continuous returned marginal. The tester may sample \\(y\\) using this kernel, prepare the joint finite return and ancilla conditional on \\(x,y\\), and retain a finite flag.\n\nFor any \\(\\epsilon>0\\), there is one finite list of return–ancilla density matrices and terminal Hermitian contractions whose paired expectations approximate the diamond distance to accuracy \\(\\epsilon\\) for every pair of channels on the fixed wires. The return ancilla dimension can be taken to be \\(\\dim B\\), by the pure-state Schmidt bound in Theorem O.7.2.3bp. Compactness of the finite-dimensional state and contraction sets, together with uniform continuity of the pairing on the compact set of channel pairs, gives the finite list. Choose the first pair attaining the list maximum at \\((x,y)\\), a Borel choice. The tester prepares its designated state and retains the pair index, so the terminal signed measurement can apply its designated contraction. The expectation difference is at least the integral in (O.7.2.3bq.1) minus \\(\\epsilon\\). The flagged preparation preserves normality because the returned scalar law remains \\(\\gamma\\). Taking suprema proves (O.7.2.3bq.1). For product-dominated laws the conditional essential supremum gives the upper bound. For the lower bound, write \\(s(x)\\) for this essential supremum and choose the conditional \\(y\\)-density to be uniform on the section where the cost exceeds \\(s(x)-\\epsilon\\). This Borel kernel is obtained by normalizing the Lebesgue section mass, which is positive. Its integral approaches \\(\\int s(x)\\,dx\\).\n\n*Proof of the closure criterion.* For a finite table \\(F\\) at returned depth \\(m\\) and any \\(k\\ge m\\), the triangle inequality within each returned cell gives\n\\[\n\\omega_k(x)\\le 2\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_y\n                  \\|\\Phi(x,y)-F(x,y)\\|_\\diamond .\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bq.3}\n\\]\nThe right integral is at most \\(2d_{\\rm ext}(\\Phi,F)\\) by the product-dominated calculation. Thus uniform dyadic approximation requires condition 1.\n\nConversely, \\(\\omega_k\\) is measurable, bounded and nonincreasing, so condition 1 gives \\(\\omega_k(x)\\to0\\) almost everywhere. Let \\(G_k(x,y)\\) average the channels \\(\\Phi(x,\\cdot)\\) in the depth-\\(k\\) cell containing \\(y\\). It is a channel field by positivity and the trace constraint. For \\(\\ell\\ge k\\) the two averages in \\(G_\\ell(x,y)\\) and \\(G_k(x,y)\\) both use subsets of the same depth-\\(k\\) cell. Averaging the two-point distance proves\n\\(\\sup_y\\|G_\\ell(x,y)-G_k(x,y)\\|_\\diamond\\le\\omega_k(x)\\).\nThe pointwise uniform-in-\\(y\\) limit \\(G\\) on this offered conull set is Borel, belongs to the regulated class, and has \\(L^1(dx)\\) error at most \\(\\int\\omega_k\\) from \\(G_k\\) in the mixed supremum norm. The same within-cell integration gives \\(\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_y\\|\\Phi(x,y)-G_k(x,y)\\|_\\diamond\\le\\omega_k(x)\\). Hence \\(\\Phi=G\\) product almost everywhere. The regulated field \\(G\\) has finite dyadic table approximants by the causal density construction of Theorem O.7.2.3bp.\n\nFor any regulated field \\(H\\), the pointwise supremum in \\(y\\) of \\(\\|G-H\\|_\\diamond\\) equals the essential supremum. The difference has the right-continuous representative on \\([0,1)\\), so each value is approached on a set of positive measure to its right. Thus the product-dominated norm of \\(\\Phi-H\\) equals the full norm \\(d_{\\rm ext}(G,H)\\). In particular\n\\[\nd_{\\rm ext}(\\Phi,G)\\le 2d_{\\rm ext}(\\Phi,H).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bq.4}\n\\]\nAny uniformly convergent finite tower therefore forces \\(d_{\\rm ext}(\\Phi,G)=0\\).\n\nBy (O.7.2.3bq.1), equality in that distance to zero implies that the Borel mismatch set is null under every coupling of \\(dx\\) and \\(dy\\), since these couplings are among the permitted tests. Kellerer’s null-set lemma says precisely that such a set is contained in a union of one vertical and one horizontal null strip. Conversely every permitted coupling ignores such strips because its first marginal is \\(dx\\) and its second is absolutely continuous. Thus distance zero is equivalent to condition 2. On passing both conditions, comparison to \\(\\Phi\\) is identical to comparison to \\(G\\) for all testers. The effective criterion and paid realization then follow from Theorem O.7.2.3bp. For Kellerer’s null-set lemma, with a direct proof, see Beiglböck et al. (2012, Appendix A).\n\n*Sharp distinctions.* For the binary replacement channel \\(f(x,y)=\\mathbf 1_{\\{x=y\\}}\\), the dyadic oscillations vanish and \\(G\\) is the constant-zero channel. The normal identity return \\(y=x\\) gives distance two to \\(G\\). Equation (O.7.2.3bq.4) gives distance at least one to every regulated channel, and the constant fair-bit channel attains one. Yet the finite tables \\(\\mathbf 1_{\\{x,y\\ {\\rm in\\ the\\ same\\ depth}\\ k\\ {\\rm cell}\\}}\\) converge pointwise to \\(f\\). For each fixed normal tester, dominated convergence in its trace law gives complete terminal convergence. This separates the fixed-tester limit from uniform comparison, even though all product-dominated tests identify \\(f\\) with zero. For a second example let \\(f(x,y)=\\mathbf 1_{\\{y>\\alpha\\}}\\) with a fixed computable non-dyadic \\(\\alpha\\in(0,1)\\). The cell containing \\(\\alpha\\) always has two positive-measure sides, and \\(\\omega_k=2\\). Equation (O.7.2.3bq.3) gives uniform gap at least one from every finite dyadic table, attained by taking the correct bits in the cells outside the crossing cell and a fair bit in that cell. \\(\\square\\)\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723br\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723br-causal-coupling-test-for-measurable-diffuse-returns\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3br (Causal Coupling Test for Measurable Diffuse Returns).** Use the fixed \\(r\\)-turn ports and fresh independent uniform offers of Theorem O.7.2.3bp. A prescribed source is a bounded Borel deterministic Choi field \\(C(x,y)\\) with the same positive prefix matrices and causal partial-trace identities, for all \\(y\\) outside a single offered-word null set. The field is specified on the actual pair space and may have arbitrary Borel return profiles. For a Borel Hermitian field \\(H\\) define\n\\[\n \\mathcal M(H)=\\sup_{\\gamma\\in\\mathcal G_r}\n                   \\int\\|H(x,y)\\|_1\\,d\\gamma(x,y),\n \\tag{O.7.2.3br.1}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathcal G_r\\) consists of the scalar laws made by drawing \\(x_j\\) fresh uniform and then sampling \\(y_j\\) from a measurable probability kernel of \\((x_{\\le j},y_{<j})\\) at each turn. Each unconditional \\(y_j\\)-marginal is absolutely continuous, as required on the declared return port. Joint laws in \\(\\mathcal G_r\\) may be singular.\n\nLet \\(D_A=\\prod_{j=1}^{r+1}\\dim A_j\\) and \\(D_B=\\prod_{j=1}^r\\dim B_j\\), in the unweighted Choi convention of Theorem O.7.2.3bp. Then\n\\[\n D_B^{-1}\\mathcal M(C-D)\\ \\le\\ d_{\\rm ext}(C,D)\\\n                 \\le\\ D_A\\mathcal M(C-D).\n \\tag{O.7.2.3br.2}\n\\]\nMoreover, a sequence \\(C_n\\) converges in complete terminal norm for every fixed normal tester exactly when \\(\\int\\|C_n-C\\|_1\\,d\\gamma\\to0\\) for each fixed \\(\\gamma\\in\\mathcal G_r\\).\n\nLet \\(P_k C\\) be the table obtained by averaging \\(C\\) on every product cell of the depth-\\(k\\) dyadic partitions of all \\(x_j,y_j\\), using product Lebesgue measure in the averages. Then \\(C\\) belongs to the uniform external closure of finite dyadic stages exactly when\n\\[\n                      \\mathcal M(C-P_kC)\\longrightarrow0.\n \\tag{O.7.2.3br.3}\n\\]\nA uniform effective paid tower exists exactly when a single algorithm outputs rational positive normalized finite causal tables \\(F_n\\) with \\(\\mathcal M(C-F_n)\\to0\\). In particular, (O.7.2.3br.3) suffices for an effective tower if the finite entries of \\(P_kC\\) are uniformly computable in \\(k\\). No modulus of convergence in (O.7.2.3br.3) is required.\n\n*Proof of the tester bounds.* The positive finite-wire tester measures \\(T_{x,+}(dy),T_{x,-}(dy)\\) from Theorem O.7.2.3bp pair with Borel matrix fields by finite entry integration. On each fixed word the pairing uses the same open-wire composition. Measurability and the finite causal trace identities allow its successive integration, with the tester at each step depending only on the available prefix. For instance a Borel source dilation can be obtained by purifying each positive prefix matrix with its canonical square root on a fixed carrier. The partial-trace identity gives two purifications of the same matrix on the earlier wires and the new incoming partner. Their purifying spaces are related by the partial isometry obtained by multiplying the two purification matrices using a Moore–Penrose inverse on the common support. Extending its action to a trace-preserving operation by preparing a fixed state on the complementary input support is Borel and does not change the purification. This factors the prescribed field into causal Borel operations. The exceptional offered set has zero mass for every tester.\n\nUse the dummy source that sends independent maximally mixed states on all \\(A_j\\) and traces out every \\(B_j\\), with the same independent classical offers. Its Choi matrix is \\(I/D_A\\) because its input factors are identities. The positive measure\n\\[\n  D_A^{-1}\\,dx\\,\\operatorname{Tr}\\bigl(T_{x,+}+T_{x,-}\\bigr)(dy)\n \\tag{O.7.2.3br.4}\n\\]\nis precisely the law of the actual classical words when the given tester acts on that dummy source. In particular it belongs to \\(\\mathcal G_r\\). Its return marginal is normal on the declared port and its causal kernels follow by disintegration of the classical law. The measure is independent of the choice of terminal two-outcome readout because summing its effects is trace preserving. The bound \\(|\\operatorname{Tr}(HU)|\\le\\|H\\|_1\\operatorname{Tr}U\\) for positive \\(U\\) proves the upper bound in (O.7.2.3br.2), already for each fixed tester with its own law (O.7.2.3br.4).\n\nFor the lower bound fix \\(\\gamma\\in\\mathcal G_r\\). The tester executes its classical causal kernels without using any quantum output. At each return it sends the input half of an independent normalized faithful Choi pair and retains the other half. It retains every \\(A_j\\) and the actual classical word. One may use \\(L^1\\) of the successive classical prefix laws of \\(\\gamma\\) for the retained classical memory. This permits singular graphs and the causal kernels send densities relative to the incoming prefix and fresh-offer law to densities relative to the next prefix law. Conditional on \\((x,y)\\), the resulting quantum state is \\(C(x,y)/D_B\\), since the faithful Choi inputs give precisely the unweighted Choi matrix divided by the product of input dimensions. The law \\(\\gamma\\) is unchanged by the quantum source. The norm of the retained difference is \\(D_B^{-1}\\int\\|C-D\\|_1\\,d\\gamma\\), by the trace-class direct integral over that classical law. This one fixed tester works for every \\(C_n\\) and \\(C\\). Together the two bounds give both (O.7.2.3br.2) and the fixed-tester criterion.\n\n*Proof of the dyadic closure test.* The averaging \\(P_k\\) preserves Choi positivity and the causal identities. In fact the average of a prefix that depends only on \\(x_{\\le j},y_{<j}\\) depends only on those cells. For a bounded Borel Hermitian field \\(H\\), the triangle inequality inside each cell gives\n\\[\n                         \\mathcal M(P_k H)\\le \\mathcal M(H).\n \\tag{O.7.2.3br.5}\n\\]\nTo verify this, fix \\(\\gamma\\) and resample its coordinates independently within their respective cells. The new law is causal. It can be produced by first drawing the actual fresh uniform offer at turn \\(j\\). Privately sample an auxiliary offer uniformly in its cell, run the original causal policy on the auxiliary offers and retained auxiliary returns, and then emit a uniform actual return in the cell of that policy's auxiliary return. The auxiliary process has law \\(\\gamma\\). Conditional on its full word, the actual coordinates are independent uniform samples in the corresponding cells. This follows by the symmetry of the two independent uniform draws in each offered cell and by the independence of the actual within-cell position from the auxiliary policy. The actual return marginals are absolutely continuous by uniform cell sampling. Thus the resampled law belongs to \\(\\mathcal G_r\\), and its integral of \\(\\|H\\|_1\\) bounds the original integral of \\(\\|P_kH\\|_1\\). Taking the supremum proves (O.7.2.3br.5).\n\nIf a finite table \\(F\\) has depth at most \\(k\\), then \\(P_kF=F\\), and\n\\[\n \\mathcal M(C-P_kC)\\le2\\mathcal M(C-F).\n \\tag{O.7.2.3br.6}\n\\]\nThis proves necessity of (O.7.2.3br.3) by taking a fixed sufficiently close finite table and then all larger depths. For sufficiency the tables \\(P_kC\\) themselves converge in \\(d_{\\rm ext}\\) by (O.7.2.3br.2). At fixed depth, rational positive tables are dense by mixing with a positive product comb and approximating in the rational affine space of causal identities, as in Theorem O.7.2.3bp. The same argument computes a rational approximation to \\(P_kC\\) when its entries are computable. The paid realization and necessity of an effective rational table name follow by applying Theorem O.7.2.3bp to the finite stages and (O.7.2.3br.2) to their errors.\n\n*An unregulated field in the uniform closure.* Take two turns, let \\(B_1\\) be a qubit, and let the intermediate offered quantum wires and \\(B_2\\) be trivial. The source applies the identity to the first returned qubit if \\(y_1<x_2\\) and applies the Pauli \\(Z\\) otherwise, sending the result on \\(A_3\\). The depth-\\(k\\) average uses the correct channel when the \\(y_1,x_2\\) cells differ, and their equal mixture when they coincide. The latter event has probability \\(2^{-k}\\) under every causal tester because \\(x_2\\) is drawn independently of the first return. There the channel difference has diamond norm one. The external error is consequently at most \\(2^{-k}\\). For equality send \\(|+\\rangle\\) on \\(B_1\\), choose uniform normal returns and retain the comparison sign from the actual \\(y_1,x_2\\). Measuring the terminal qubit in the \\(X\\) basis with that flag gives the signed discrepancy \\(2^{-k}\\). Yet for every non-dyadic fixed \\(x_2\\), the two channels occur on positive-measure subsets of the same depth-\\(k\\) return cell at every \\(k\\). Their separation is two in diamond norm. Thus the integrated return-profile oscillation does not decay, while the exact uniform causal error does. \\(\\square\\)\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bs\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bs-feedback-dependent-diffuse-offers-and-returns\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3bs (Feedback-Dependent Diffuse Offers and Returns).** Keep the fixed finite quantum wires and the actual diffuse ports of Theorem O.7.2.3br. The coordinate \\(x_j\\) now accompanies the source quantum offer \\(A_j\\) as the outcome of a Borel instrument with a density relative to \\(dx_j\\). Its law may depend on the earlier classical and quantum returns. A tester acts on each actual prefix and its retained memory, returning \\(y_j\\) and \\(B_j\\). Require normality on the named returned \\(L^1\\) ports, including when the tester is supplied independent uniform offers and independent maximally mixed quantum outputs. Retained classical records may use the joint reference law on the actual word, including singular offer–return graphs.\n\nSpecify the source by positive finite matrix Borel densities \\(C^{(j)}(x_{\\le j},y_{<j})\\) on the successive open quantum wires. Write \\(C=C^{(r+1)}(x,y)\\) for the terminal matrix with quantum output \\(A_{r+1}\\). The unweighted Choi normalization is\n\\[\n\\begin{split}\n &\\int\\operatorname{Tr}C^{(1)}(x_1)\\,dx_1=1,\\\\\n &\\int\\operatorname{Tr}_{A_j} C^{(j)}(x_{\\le j},y_{<j})\\,dx_j\n       =I_{B_{j-1}}\\otimes C^{(j-1)}(x_{<j},y_{<j-1})\n       \\quad(2\\le j\\le r),\\\\\n &\\operatorname{Tr}_{A_{r+1}} C(x,y)\n       =I_{B_r}\\otimes C^{(r)}(x_{\\le r},y_{<r}).\n\\end{split}\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bs.1}\n\\]\nThe conditional identities are required at every earlier classical prefix in the chosen representative. There is no uniform bound on the densities.\n\nUse the causal reference laws \\(\\mathcal G_r\\) of Theorem O.7.2.3br, in which each \\(x_j\\) is fresh uniform and each \\(y_j\\) follows its causal policy. These laws are used as reference measures. The source actually supplies the \\(x_j\\) instrument outcomes of (O.7.2.3bs.1). For a difference of two such terminal fields set\n\\[\n \\mathcal M(C-D)=\\sup_{\\gamma\\in\\mathcal G_r}\n                      \\int\\|C(x,y)-D(x,y)\\|_1\\,d\\gamma(x,y).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bs.2}\n\\]\nThen the two bounds of (O.7.2.3br.2) still hold, with the same dimension factors \\(D_A,D_B\\). Convergence against each fixed normal tester is equivalent to convergence of the integral in (O.7.2.3bs.2) against each fixed reference law \\(\\gamma\\).\n\nLet \\(P_k\\) average the field on each product cell of the depth-\\(k\\) dyadic partition of all the coordinates. The entire uniform closure of finite dyadic instruments with these ports is characterized by\n\\[\n                        \\mathcal M(C-P_kC)\\longrightarrow0 .\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bs.3}\n\\]\nThe corresponding paid finite tower exists effectively exactly when there is a computable sequence of rational normalized positive dyadic tables converging in (O.7.2.3bs.2). Uniformly computable entries of \\(P_kC\\) together with (O.7.2.3bs.3) suffice, with no convergence modulus.\n\n*Source construction and normalization.* To construct the Borel instrument at a prefix, use the canonical square-root purifications of the earlier Choi matrix and of the current outcome density. Include the new input’s unnormalized faithful Choi pair in the former purification. The right side of the relevant partial-trace identity in (O.7.2.3bs.1) is then the marginal of that former purification on the earlier open wires and the new input partner. In fixed bases let \\(L\\) be its coefficient matrix, with rows on that open marginal and columns on its purifying carrier. Let \\(V(u)\\) be the coefficient matrix of the current square-root purification, on the same rows and with columns on the current output and new purifying carrier. Here \\(u\\) is the new coordinate, or a single point for the terminal step. The causal identity reads\n\\[\n                  \\int V(u)V(u)^*\\,du=LL^* .\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bs.4}\n\\]\nThus \\(V(u)\\) has columns in the range of \\(L\\) for almost every \\(u\\) at this prefix. Put \\(M(u)=L^+V(u)\\) on this set and zero elsewhere. Equation (O.7.2.3bs.4) gives \\(\\int M(u)M(u)^*du=P\\), the projection onto the support of \\(L^*L\\). The matrices \\(M(u)^{\\mathsf T}\\) are the corresponding Kraus densities on the purifying input wire in the coefficient-matrix convention. Their integrated squared operators sum to \\(P^{\\mathsf T}\\). Complete the instrument on the orthogonal input support by a fixed output state and uniform outcome, which contributes zero to the prescribed Choi input. This produces exactly the current purification density, up to a coordinate-null set at each prefix. Square roots, pseudoinverses and the support test are Borel in finite dimensions. These formulas also apply to the first instrument with no quantum input. At each fresh coordinate every causal reference draws from Lebesgue measure, so the coordinate-null changes have zero joint reference mass, even for singular later returns. The resulting source has the stated weighted Choi field on the actual comparisons.\n\nFor every \\(\\gamma\\in\\mathcal G_r\\), backward integration of the scalar traces using (O.7.2.3bs.1) yields \\(\\int\\operatorname{Tr}C\\,d\\gamma=D_B\\). Indeed the last return drops out of the terminal trace, integrating each fresh \\(x_j\\) leaves the trace of the preceding prefix multiplied by \\(\\dim B_{j-1}\\), and the intervening normalized return kernels leave the reduced integrands unchanged. Tonelli applies to the nonnegative traces, so this proof does not require a bound on a density. In particular (O.7.2.3bs.2) is at most \\(2D_B\\). It also follows by taking independent uniform returns that all the product cell averages exist.\n\n*Tester comparison.* The tester matrix measures and the dummy source from Theorem O.7.2.3br use the same open wires. For a fixed tester, the sum of its signed-readout measures has trace \\(D_A\\) times its scalar reference law \\(\\gamma\\) against the dummy source \\(I/D_A\\). That law is independent of the terminal readout. The actual signed probability difference is the pairing of the two measures with \\(C-D\\) integrated over the offered coordinates. This pairing follows from the successive CP composition in (O.7.2.3bs.4), or from the same finite-wire linkage on finite measurable outcome events and integration of its matrix entries. Each actual positive event measure is absolutely continuous with respect to the dummy law. In fact the positive pairing with \\(C\\) is bounded by the pairing of the trace measure with \\(\\operatorname{Tr}C\\). The preceding trace identity makes that bound integrable, so unbounded densities still give finite pairings and normal actual returned marginals. The bound on the signed discrepancy for this tester is \\(D_A\\int\\|C-D\\|_1\\,d\\gamma\\).\n\nFor the reverse comparison fix \\(\\gamma\\in\\mathcal G_r\\) and use its causal kernels as a classical tester policy, with independent normalized faithful Choi pairs at all \\(B_j\\). The tester retains the partners, the offered quantum wires and the actual classical word. Its terminal positive density relative to \\(\\gamma\\) is \\(C/D_B\\), with total trace one by (O.7.2.3bs.1). The causal policy can use the prefix laws of the dummy reference as its retained \\(L^1\\) carriers. The actual prefix laws are absolutely continuous relative to those references, so the same kernels give normal updates for the physical source. The retained terminal difference has norm \\(\\int\\|C-D\\|_1\\,d\\gamma/D_B\\). The two tester constructions prove the uniform bounds and the asserted equivalence for each fixed tester.\n\n*Finite approximation.* Averaging all coordinates on product dyadic cells preserves (O.7.2.3bs.1). The integral in \\(x_j\\) sums the averages of its cells with their Lebesgue weights, leaving the average of the earlier prefix. This gives exactly the weighted finite normalization. For integrable Hermitian fields the resampling proof of (O.7.2.3br.5) applies by the same triangle inequality on each cell and Tonelli. In particular \\(\\mathcal M(P_kH)\\le\\mathcal M(H)\\) for differences of weighted sources. If a fixed table \\(F\\) has depth at most \\(k\\), then \\(\\mathcal M(C-P_kC)\\le2\\mathcal M(C-F)\\). Thus uniform approximation by finite tables gives (O.7.2.3bs.3), and conversely \\(P_kC\\) themselves supply approximants.\n\nOn a fixed grid multiply an offered-cell density by its cell volume to form its finite positive instrument weight. The integrated equations become the finite linear causal normalization constraints. With the same positive product comb mixing and rational affine approximation as in Theorem O.7.2.3bp, the finite tables can be made rational to any requested comparison precision. Their physical stages implement the resulting causal instruments on finite cells and finite quantum wires, with uniform actual offer decoding inside the selected cell and actual return-cell readout. These are the finite dilations and paid positive-duration schedules of Theorems O.7.2.3m and O.7.2.3bp. Pulse errors telescope in complete trace norm uniformly over testers, and every actual finite stage of this type has an effectively named normalized finite causal table. This proves the effective criterion in both directions.\n\n*Unbounded feedback-controlled example.* In two turns take the first offer uniform, with trivial quantum wire. Let the first return carry a qubit and the second return’s quantum wire be trivial. Write \\(p(u)=1/(2\\sqrt u)\\) for \\(0<u<1\\), with arbitrary finite values at both endpoints, and \\(q(u)=p(1-u)\\). These are integrable probability densities. When \\(y_1<1/2\\), release \\(x_2=u\\) together with the filtered qubit by the Kraus density \\(K(u)=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sqrt{p(u)},\\sqrt{q(u)})\\). When \\(y_1\\ge1/2\\), swap the two diagonal entries. Each instrument integrates to a trace-preserving operation. For a first returned \\(|0\\rangle\\), the actual offer density is \\(p\\) or \\(q\\) according to the feedback bit. Changing the quantum return to \\(|1\\rangle\\) exchanges those offer laws. A returned qubit coherence appears in the released quantum density multiplied by \\(\\sqrt{pq}\\) in both branches, so the instrument retains coherence. For a terminal output use a fixed state after the second return.\n\nThe first feedback bit is exactly dyadic and all other coordinate dependence is in \\(u\\). The finite weighted matrices are given by averages of \\(p,q,\\sqrt{pq}\\) over dyadic intervals. Those averages converge in \\(L^1(du)\\). Since the two branches have only finitely many coefficients, the integral of the maximal matrix error over both branches tends to zero and bounds \\(\\mathcal M(C-P_kC)\\). The reference \\(u=x_2\\) is uniform and fresh. The averages are uniformly computable from the explicit endpoint integrals for \\(p,q\\) and from effective integrable endpoint bounds for \\(\\sqrt{pq}\\). Thus (O.7.2.3bs.3) gives a paid uniform finite tower for this genuinely unbounded feedback-dependent instrument. \\(\\square\\)\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bt\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bt-exact-offer-decoders-for-singular-feedback-histories\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3bt (Exact Offer Decoders for Singular Feedback Histories).** Fix the finite quantum wires of Theorem O.7.2.3bs. At a declared turn a finite offer encoder selects a finite label and uses a fixed probability row on the actual offered coordinate for that label. Conditional on the label, the decoder draw has that row law independently of the earlier exact returned coordinates and quantum state. The row may depend on the stage and turn.\n\nThere is a normalized two-turn source with \\(x_1\\) uniform and \\(x_2=y_1\\) for which one fixed normal tester gives complete terminal trace-norm difference two from every tower of such encoders. This holds even if the offer rows are arbitrary singular probability measures. The tester returns \\(y_1\\) uniformly and independently, retains it, and retains the actual \\(x_2\\). All quantum wires can be trivial.\n\nThere is a positive criterion for a fixed adaptive decoding service. At turn \\(j\\) let \\(b_j\\) be a finite public branch determined by the earlier offered and returned dyadic cells at one fixed depth \\(k_0\\). Declare a probability measure \\(\\mu_{j,b}\\) on \\([0,1)\\) for every branch. For every dyadic cell \\(I\\) at depth \\(k\\ge k_0\\), the service provides the conditional offer row \\(\\mu_{j,b}(\\,\\cdot\\mid I)\\) when \\(\\mu_{j,b}(I)>0\\), with the exact output coordinate on the named port. Require an effective positive-cell mask and computable cell masses. The offer sampler is the declared exact decoder on the port, as uniform-cell decoding is on the Lebesgue ports. Its code, finite label, branch and calls are included in the resource description. The finite Hermitian controls below implement the cell instrument on the encoded ports.\n\nLet the positive Borel Choi prefixes satisfy (O.7.2.3bs.1) with \\(dx_j\\) replaced at the given earlier prefix by \\(\\mu_{j,b_j}\\), including the first offer, and the terminal trace equation unchanged. Normal testers are required to give absolutely continuous returned marginals on their named Lebesgue ports when tested on the adaptive reference source that selects \\(x_j\\) from \\(\\mu_{j,b_j}\\) and sends independent maximally mixed quantum outputs. Classical records can use the joint actual prefix law, including singular records.\n\nFor a field difference \\(H=C-D\\) define\n\\[\n \\mathcal M_\\mu(H)=\\sup_{\\gamma\\in\\mathcal G_\\mu}\\int\\|H(x,y)\\|_1\\,d\\gamma ,\n \\tag{O.7.2.3bt.1}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mathcal G_\\mu\\) draws each offer from \\(\\mu_{j,b_j}\\) and each return from a Borel causal policy, with absolutely continuous returned marginals. Then the bounds of (O.7.2.3br.2) hold with \\(\\mathcal M_\\mu\\) and the same dimension factors. Complete convergence for every fixed normal tester is equivalent to convergence of each of the integrals in (O.7.2.3bt.1) for its fixed reference law \\(\\gamma\\).\n\nLet \\(P_k C\\) average \\(C\\) within each nonzero joint cell of the reference with independent uniform returns. The uniform external closure of finite dyadic stages using the declared service is characterized by \\(\\mathcal M_\\mu(C-P_kC)\\to0\\). A uniform effective paid tower exists exactly when a single algorithm outputs finite normalized positive causal cell instruments whose decoded Choi tables converge in \\(\\mathcal M_\\mu\\). The finite instrument weights can be rational. Uniformly computable cell averages and this closure condition suffice without a convergence modulus.\n\n*Graph obstruction.* Under the fixed tester, write \\(Y\\) for its uniform return. At a finite stage condition on the selected offer label \\(L\\), with row \\(\\nu_L\\). The conditional probability that the decoder output equals \\(Y\\), given \\(Y,L\\), is \\(\\nu_L(\\{Y\\})\\). Every probability row has at most countably many atoms. The union of the atoms of the rows is countable, so \\(Y\\) lies in it with probability zero, regardless of the mechanism selecting \\(L\\). Thus the finite stage assigns zero probability to the Borel graph event \\(x_2=Y\\). The prescribed copy source assigns it one. The binary test of this event on the complete retained records gives trace-norm difference two. The marginal \\(x_2\\) of the target is uniform for this tester. More generally the target copy law has a normal \\(x_2\\) marginal whenever the first return is normal. This obstruction concerns the retained joint records. It applies to any countable collection of fixed rows and is unchanged by increasing the resolution of the cells. An admitted decoder that acts on the actual earlier value rather than through the stated row contract is a different source service.\n\n*Comparison with an adaptive reference.* At each actual earlier prefix use \\(\\mu_{j,b_j}\\) in the square-root instrument construction of Theorem O.7.2.3bs. The matrix support test is Borel in the prefix, and coordinate-null changes have zero mass under each reference law because its next offer is sampled from that very measure. Backward trace integration gives \\(\\int\\operatorname{Tr}C\\,d\\gamma=D_B\\) for every \\(\\gamma\\in\\mathcal G_\\mu\\), including when the densities relative to the chart are unbounded. The tester's positive finite-wire Choi measures pair with the same dummy field \\(I/D_A\\) as in Theorem O.7.2.3bs. Sampling the offers from the declared adaptive chart is part of that dummy source. Its classical trace law is therefore one fixed \\(\\gamma\\in\\mathcal G_\\mu\\), independent of the signed terminal readout. The positive event measures for the actual source are dominated by this law, with integrable density bound \\(D_A\\operatorname{Tr}C\\). The signed discrepancy is bounded by \\(D_A\\int\\|H\\|_1\\,d\\gamma\\). Conversely, for any \\(\\gamma\\in\\mathcal G_\\mu\\) run its causal classical policy, send independent normalized faithful Choi pairs and retain all outputs, partners and the actual classical word. Its retained output density is \\(C/D_B\\) relative to \\(\\gamma\\). The corresponding trace norm equals \\(\\int\\|H\\|_1\\,d\\gamma/D_B\\). This proves the two bounds and the fixed-tester equivalence.\n\n*Cell averaging.* At depth \\(k\\ge k_0\\), every joint cell determines the adaptive chart at each turn. The reference conditional within a nonzero joint cell is the product of \\(\\mu_{j,b_j}(\\cdot\\mid I_j)\\) for the offered cells and of the uniform conditional Lebesgue laws in the returned cells. Indeed the branch choices are constant on the cell. The averaging respects the integrated Choi identities by summing the next offer cells with their weights \\(\\mu_{j,b_j}(I_j)\\). Cells of zero reference mass are impossible under all causal policies with this chart and may be omitted. The resulting table is a finite positive normalized causal instrument after multiplying each current density by the corresponding offer-cell mass.\n\nFor the contraction \\(\\mathcal M_\\mu(P_kH)\\le\\mathcal M_\\mu(H)\\), fix a reference law and generate an auxiliary copy of its causal policy. Draw the actual offered coordinate from the appropriate chart, then draw the auxiliary offer independently within its cell using that chart's conditional row. Run the chosen policy on the auxiliary prefixes. Draw each actual return uniformly in the auxiliary return's cell. The actual and auxiliary prefixes have the same cells, so the same branch is used for both offers. The auxiliary process has the given reference law. Conditional on its full word the actual coordinates have the product of conditional rows described above. This follows from the two independent within-cell draws at each offer, because later chart and policy choices use only the auxiliary prefix and its common cells. The actual resampled process is causal and its returns have normal marginals. The triangle inequality inside the joint cell and Tonelli therefore give the contraction. Since \\(P_kF=F\\) for any table at depth at most \\(k\\), one obtains\n\\[\n             \\mathcal M_\\mu(C-P_kC)\\le 2\\mathcal M_\\mu(C-F).\n \\tag{O.7.2.3bt.2}\n\\]\nThis gives necessity. Sufficiency follows by using the averaged tables and the complete comparison bound.\n\nAt each finite grid the constraints on the instrument weights are rational linear equations on the positive matrices with the declared possible cells. On that support a finite independent full-rank quantum instrument with strictly positive possible cell weights supplies an interior positive point. Mixing and rational affine approximation give rational positive normalized instruments densely. The comparison error of such a finite approximation is effectively controlled by the entry errors. For example, in converting instrument weights to densities, use computed positive lower bounds on the finitely many nonzero cell masses and sum the maximum cell errors. The causal finite dilations and positive-duration paid controls of Theorem O.7.2.3m, followed by the declared exact cell decoding and actual return-cell readout, implement these stages. Telescoping bounds the full pulse error uniformly as in Theorem O.7.2.3bs. In the converse direction each actual finite stage has effective finite instrument weights. Its entries can be approximated by rational positive weights on the same support to an externally vanishing tolerance. These facts give the effective criterion and its sufficiency when the cell averages are computable.\n\n*Singular chart example.* Let \\(\\lambda_{\\rm C}\\) be the usual probability law of \\(\\sum_{n\\ge1}2\\xi_n3^{-n}\\), with independent fair bits \\(\\xi_n\\). At the second offer use the pushforward of \\(\\lambda_{\\rm C}\\) by \\(t\\mapsto t/4\\) if \\(y_1<1/2\\) and by \\(t\\mapsto 1/2+t/4\\) otherwise. Each chart is singular with respect to Lebesgue, and they have disjoint supports. The fixed mathematical decoder maps a uniform binary parameter to the ternary digits \\(0,2\\), followed by the declared affine map. Conditional cell rows use that same map on the interval of quantile parameters for the cell. Its endpoints are computable: the distribution value at a rational threshold is obtained by reading its eventually periodic ternary expansion. Convert the prefix digits \\(0,2\\) to binary digits \\(0,1\\). At the first digit \\(1\\), add half the remaining cylinder mass to the converted prefix value. If there is no such digit, convert the whole eventually periodic expansion. Either way the value at a dyadic cell endpoint, after the affine map, is a computable rational. This gives the effective zero mask and cell masses. It is the exact decoding service stipulated in the theorem.\n\nTake \\(x_1\\) uniform with trivial quantum offer, and a qubit \\(B_1\\) in the first return. In each second-offer chart its two first Cantor-digit sets, separated by the midpoint of the corresponding support interval, each have mass \\(1/2\\). On the first set apply the Kraus density \\(\\operatorname{diag}(\\sqrt{3/2},\\sqrt{1/2})\\) and on the second exchange its diagonal entries. Release the filtered qubit with \\(x_2\\). The integrated squared Kraus operator is \\(I\\). The lower first-digit set has probability \\(3/4\\) on \\(\\lvert0\\rangle\\) and \\(1/4\\) on \\(\\lvert1\\rangle\\), with the roles reversed on the upper set. The off-diagonal density multiplier is \\(\\sqrt{3}/2\\) on both parts. The first return therefore controls which singular chart is used, and its quantum state changes the digit law while preserving coherence. The density is a finite table in the stated chart, since each chart midpoint is dyadic. Its weighted instrument is finite and has the paid realization with the declared decoder. \\(\\square\\)\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bu\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bu-closure-of-singular-offer-instruments-after-a-diffuse-return\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3bu (Closure of Singular Offer Instruments after a Diffuse Return).** Consider one actual returned coordinate \\(y\\in[0,1)\\), with its named normal Lebesgue port, and a simultaneously returned finite quantum wire \\(B\\). The return may be preceded by a fixed independent offer. The source emits an actual coordinate \\(x\\in[0,1)\\) and a finite quantum wire \\(A\\). The tester may retain \\(y\\), the actual \\(x\\), every quantum output, its memory and a fixed spectator. A prescribed source is a Borel family of positive finite Choi matrix measures \\(K_y(dx)\\), satisfying \\(\\operatorname{Tr}_A K_y([0,1))=I_B\\). Borel means that \\(K_y(E)\\) is Borel in \\(y\\) for each Borel \\(E\\). No reference measure on \\(x\\) is supplied.\n\nA finite stage reads a dyadic cell of the actual \\(y\\), applies a finite quantum instrument on \\(B\\) with outcome label \\(\\ell\\), and releases \\(x\\) with the fixed row \\(\\nu_\\ell\\) of that label. Conditional on the label, the coordinate decoding uses that row independently of \\(y\\) and the quantum state. The same stage can use different quantum instruments on different return cells. The fixed probability rows may be arbitrary measures and may change between stages. The results concerning closure over such stages take these rows as exact mathematical decoders.\n\nPut \\(d=\\dim B\\). Let \\(\\|L\\|_{\\rm TV,1}\\) denote the variation of a finite Hermitian matrix measure \\(L\\) using the trace norm, namely \\(\\sup_{\\mathcal P}\\sum_{E\\in\\mathcal P}\\|L(E)\\|_1\\) over finite Borel partitions. Then the exact complete external distance between \\(K,L\\), in the convention without a factor \\(1/2\\), is\n\\[\n d_{\\rm ext}(K,L)=\\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_{y}\n    \\max_{\\rho\\in\\mathcal{D}(B\\otimes\\mathbb C^{d})}\n      \\big\\|[(K_y-L_y)\\otimes{\\rm id}](\\rho)\\big\\|_{\\rm TV,1}.\n \\tag{O.7.2.3bu.1}\n\\]\nIn (O.7.2.3bu.1) the matrix measures denote their corresponding CP operations, and the output measure includes the ancilla. The expression inside the essential supremum lies between \\(d^{-1}\\|K_y-L_y\\|_{\\rm TV,1}\\) and \\(\\|K_y-L_y\\|_{\\rm TV,1}\\).\n\n1. A sequence of finite stages converging in complete terminal norm for every fixed normal tester exists exactly when there is a probability measure \\(\\mu\\) on the offered coordinate such that \\(\\operatorname{Tr}K_y\\ll\\mu\\) for Lebesgue-almost every \\(y\\).\n2. For each depth \\(k\\) let\n   \\[\n   \\omega_k=\\max_{J}\n      \\operatorname*{ess\\,sup}_{(y,z)\\in J^2}\\|K_y-K_z\\|_{\\rm TV,1},\n   \\tag{O.7.2.3bu.2}\n   \\]\n   where \\(J\\) ranges over half-open depth-\\(k\\) dyadic cells of the returned coordinate and the essential supremum uses Lebesgue product measure. There is uniform complete convergence over all normal testers exactly when \\(\\omega_k\\to0\\). This condition implies the common domination in part 1.\n\n*Complete tester comparison.* For a Hermitian matrix measure the variation equals the integral of the trace norm of its matrix density relative to any finite scalar dominating measure. It also equals the limit of the sums over the successive dyadic partitions. To see the latter, take conditional expectations of the matrix density on these partitions and use convergence in \\(L^1\\), since dyadic intervals generate the Borel $\\sigma$ algebra. Thus all the variations under consideration are Borel in the parameters. For each fixed \\(\\rho\\) the same argument applies to the output matrix measure with an ancilla. The quantity varies continuously with \\(\\rho\\), since the difference of two normalized instruments is bounded on trace class, including the fixed finite ancilla. Its maximum can therefore be computed as a supremum over a fixed countable dense set of states for measurability purposes. The upper bound for arbitrary spectator dimension reduces to this finite ancilla bound: purify or decompose the input and use the Schmidt rank bound \\(d\\) for a pure state across \\(B\\) and its spectator, followed by convexity. Integrating against the normal returned law proves the upper side of (O.7.2.3bu.1).\n\nFor the reverse side, prepare a normal density of \\(y\\) supported on a positive-measure set where the expression is within \\(\\epsilon\\) of its essential supremum. At each such \\(y\\) choose from the fixed countable list the first state that is within \\(\\epsilon\\) of the optimum, and retain \\(y\\) and the ancilla. This is a measurable normal preparation. Its complete output difference has the integrated variation. The joint variation formula follows also by taking the increasing dyadic output partitions and integrating their variation sums, which increase to the conditional variation and are bounded. The retained exact \\(y\\) permits these conditional comparisons. Letting \\(\\epsilon\\) decrease proves (O.7.2.3bu.1). The normalized faithful Choi input produces \\((K_y-L_y)/d\\), giving the stated lower dimension bound. For the upper dimension bound a pure input is a normalized Choi vector acted on by an ancilla matrix of operator norm at most \\(\\sqrt d\\). The resulting variation is at most \\(\\|K_y-L_y\\|_{\\rm TV,1}\\). Convexity gives the bound for mixed inputs as well.\n\n*Necessity for fixed testers.* Consider one tester sending independent uniform \\(y\\) and the input half of a normalized faithful Choi pair. It retains the partner and the actual \\(x,y\\). Its target trace measure is \\(dy\\,\\operatorname{Tr}K_y(dx)/d\\). For any countable finite-stage sequence, form a probability \\(\\mu\\) by taking a strictly positive summable mixture of all its decoder rows. Every stage trace law is dominated by \\(dy\\,\\mu(dx)\\). Complete convergence for this fixed tester gives variation convergence of the trace laws, so the target is dominated by \\(dy\\,\\mu(dx)\\). Taking a Radon–Nikodym density and disintegrating it gives \\(\\operatorname{Tr}K_y\\ll\\mu\\) almost everywhere. Equality of the conditional measures follows by checking a countable generating algebra and then extending to all Borel sets. This proves necessity with one tester, regardless of the decoder rows the stages choose.\n\n*Sufficiency for fixed testers.* A common \\(\\mu\\) gives a positive finite-matrix density \\(G(y,x)\\) relative to \\(dy\\,\\mu(dx)\\). Its integral in \\(x\\) satisfies the Choi trace identity for almost every \\(y\\). On dyadic \\(y\\)- and \\(x\\)-cells take product conditional averages. Multiplying each averaged \\(x\\)-cell density by its \\(\\mu\\)-mass gives the finite positive Choi operation on that label. Summing its partial traces over labels gives \\(I_B\\). The decoder row for the label is \\(\\mu(\\cdot\\mid I)\\) on each positive \\(\\mu\\)-mass offered cell \\(I\\); zero-mass cells can be omitted. The resulting table is a normalized finite instrument of the required type. The averaged densities converge to \\(G\\) in \\(L^1(dy\\,\\mu)\\). Hence the integral in \\(dy\\) of the conditional trace variation errors tends to zero. These errors are bounded by \\(2d\\), since both Choi measures have trace \\(d\\). The same convergence holds after multiplication by any fixed integrable density of the actual returned \\(y\\), by truncation of that density and the uniform bound. The complete conditional spectator bound from (O.7.2.3bu.1) gives convergence for every fixed normal tester, with its full retained output. All comparisons use the actual \\(x\\), including for measures \\(\\mu\\) singular to Lebesgue.\n\n*Uniform criterion.* If a finite stage is constant as a Choi measure on each return cell of depth \\(m\\), then for \\(k\\ge m\\) the triangle inequality bounds \\(\\omega_k\\) by twice the essential supremum of the stage's conditional trace variation error. By the lower dimension bound this is at most \\(2d\\) times its complete uniform error. Thus \\(\\omega_k\\to0\\) is necessary.\n\nFor sufficiency let \\(K^{[k]}_y=|J|^{-1}\\int_J K_z\\,dz\\) on the depth-\\(k\\) return cell \\(J\\). For almost every \\(y\\) in each \\(J\\) the variation bound and integration in \\(z\\) give \\(\\|K_y-K^{[k]}_y\\|_{\\rm TV,1}\\le\\omega_k\\). This can be verified simultaneously on the countable dyadic output partitions and then passed to variation. Take \\(\\mu\\) to be a probability mixture with strictly positive weights of the normalized trace measures of all \\(K^{[k]}_y\\) on all their cells. For almost every \\(y\\), variation convergence to \\(K_y\\) then implies \\(\\operatorname{Tr}K_y\\ll\\mu\\). At fixed \\(k\\) only finitely many averaged Choi measures are involved. Approximate their densities relative to \\(\\mu\\) by the conditional \\(x\\)-cell averages at a sufficiently deep finite grid so all of their variation errors are small. The normalized positive finite instruments with the conditional rows of \\(\\mu\\) now approximate \\(K\\) uniformly by (O.7.2.3bu.1). This proves part 2.\n\n*Effective conditional decoders.* If the dominating measure has a declared exact cell decoder, a computable positive-cell mask and computable cell masses, the finite constructions above use that service and the paid causal instrument controls of Theorem O.7.2.3m. For part 1, uniformly computable Choi cell masses \\(\\int_J K_y(I)\\,dy\\) on the product dyadic cells suffice. At fixed depth approximate the weighted instrument within its rational affine normalization after mixing with a strictly positive instrument on its possible cells, as in Theorem O.7.2.3bt. Complete pulse errors can be taken uniformly vanishing. For uniform convergence it also suffices to supply a computable depth choice for the offered-coordinate approximation of the finitely many \\(K^{[k]}\\) to variation accuracy \\(2^{-k}\\). No rate for \\(\\omega_k\\to0\\) is needed in this construction. The qualitative necessity results hold without any decoder effectivity hypothesis.\n\n*Boundary examples.* For \\(K_y(dx)=\\delta_y(dx)\\) with trivial quantum wires, common domination would require \\(\\mu(\\{y\\})>0\\) for almost every \\(y\\), which is impossible. This recovers the graph obstruction of Theorem O.7.2.3bt. In contrast, fix two distinct points \\(a,b\\) and a computable non-dyadic threshold \\(\\alpha\\in(0,1)\\). Let a qubit instrument emit \\(\\delta_a\\) and apply the identity channel for \\(y<\\alpha\\), and emit \\(\\delta_b\\) and apply a Pauli \\(Z\\) channel otherwise. Both branches are dominated by \\((\\delta_a+\\delta_b)/2\\), so a finite tower with those two exact decoder rows converges for each fixed normal tester. On the return cell crossing \\(\\alpha\\) the two conditional instruments have disjoint offered support. Their operational distance is two and their Choi trace variation is \\(2d\\). Every finite table is constant on that cell, so its uniform error is at least one. A half mixture in the crossing cell attains one. Thus common domination alone does not give uniform convergence. \\(\\square\\)\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bv\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bv-product-domination-for-multi-turn-offer-instruments\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3bv (Product Domination for Multi-Turn Offer Instruments).** Use a fixed finite checkpoint grid. At turn \\(j\\) a source releases an actual Borel coordinate \\(x_j\\in[0,1)\\) and a finite quantum wire \\(A_j\\). At the next intervention the tester returns a finite classical label \\(b_j\\) and a finite quantum wire \\(B_j\\). After the last return the source emits a finite terminal quantum wire. A tester may retain the entire actual word and adapt its return label and quantum state to the actual prefix, with arbitrary fixed spectator and retained memory. It uses measurable causal operations. Let \\(\\mathcal B\\) be the finite set of full return-label words.\n\nThe prescribed source is given by positive finite matrix-valued Choi measures \\(C_b(dx)\\) on the full offered word \\(x=(x_1,\\ldots,x_r)\\), one for each \\(b\\in\\mathcal B\\). These measures use the unweighted Choi convention. Require the causal marginal identities. At each step, integrating out the new offer coordinate and tracing out its quantum output leaves the prior prefix Choi measure tensored with the identity on the new input wire. The prior measure depends only on the available earlier labels. At the final output the same identity holds with no new classical coordinate. The first offered prefix has total trace one. The measures, including their causal prefixes, may be singular.\n\nA finite fixed-row stage chooses an offer label in a finite causal quantum instrument at each turn and then uses one fixed probability row for its actual coordinate. The stage can retain its internal labels and quantum memory. Given the selected label, the actual coordinate is sampled independently of the past and of the quantum state. The next operation can depend on the tester's actual finite label and quantum return. Exact probability rows are admitted as mathematical decoders in the qualitative statements.\n\nPut \\(D_B=\\prod_j\\dim B_j\\) and\n\\[\n             V(C,D)=\\sum_{b\\in\\mathcal B}\\|C_b-D_b\\|_{\\rm TV,1}.\n \\tag{O.7.2.3bv.1}\n\\]\nThen for some constant \\(K\\) depending only on the fixed quantum wires and the finite label menu,\n\\[\n   \\frac{V(C,D)}{D_B|\\mathcal B|}\n          \\ \\le\\ d_{\\rm ext}(C,D)\\ \\le\\ K V(C,D).\n \\tag{O.7.2.3bv.2}\n\\]\nHere \\(d_{\\rm ext}\\) compares complete terminal trace norms uniformly over the above testers, with no factor \\(1/2\\). The lower bound is attained as a comparison by one fixed tester independent of \\(C,D\\).\n\nA sequence of finite fixed-row stages converges for every fixed normal tester if and only if a sequence converges uniformly over those testers. Both exist exactly when there are probability measures \\(\\mu_1,\\ldots,\\mu_r\\) such that\n\\[\n         \\operatorname{Tr}C_b\\ \\ll\\ \\mu_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes\\mu_r\n                   \\qquad\\text{for every }b\\in\\mathcal B .\n \\tag{O.7.2.3bv.3}\n\\]\nThe product domination is a condition on the actual joint histories. It allows singular one-coordinate laws and does not follow from domination of each separate marginal.\n\n*Tester comparison.* For the lower bound, a single tester chooses each \\(b_j\\) independently and uniformly from its menu, sends one half of an independent normalized faithful Choi pair on every \\(B_j\\), and retains the partners, every \\(A_j\\), the actual offered word and its chosen label word. The retained quantum matrix measure in block \\(b\\) is \\(C_b/(|\\mathcal B|D_B)\\). Its difference from the other source has norm (O.7.2.3bv.1) divided by \\(|\\mathcal B|D_B\\), using the direct sum of the labels and the trace variation of the actual offered word.\n\nFor the upper bound fix any causal tester and a terminal signed two-outcome readout. At each prescribed actual word the tester has a positive dual Choi effect for either readout outcome and each specified label word. This is the open-wire causal composition of its conditional quantum operations along that label branch, including its memory and fixed spectator. Joint measurability of the tester gives Borel finite matrix effects. At each fixed offered word their sum over labels and outcomes is the deterministic tester on the finite open wires. The causal trace identities bound the trace of every individual effect by a fixed finite wire constant. The signed probability difference is the sum of the integrals of the resulting signed effects against the measures \\(C_b-D_b\\). This formula is the finite Choi linkage applied successively at the actual classical outcomes, with a sum over the finite label branches. It also follows first for simple conditional tester operations, then by bounded pointwise approximation of their finite open-wire matrix elements in the integrals against the finite source measures. There is no conditional reference-density replacement for the actual offered words. Matrix trace duality bounds the result by \\(K V(C,D)\\). Taking the supremum of signed readouts gives the upper bound. This calculation applies to every fixed tester and every field pair satisfying the causal identities, even on mutually singular actual offer laws.\n\nFor completeness a family of measures with the stipulated causal identities gives a Borel source instrument tree. At each next offer, take the trace measure of its positive Choi prefix and disintegrate it over the earlier offered coordinates for the fixed finite earlier labels. The causal partial-trace identity makes the earlier trace law a fixed dimension multiple of the prior trace measure, and therefore a valid base for the disintegration. Matrix Radon–Nikodym derivatives yield a positive conditional matrix measure with that earlier trace reference. The square-root purification and Moore–Penrose construction of Theorem O.7.2.3bs implements this conditional instrument. Its integrated Choi marginal is the prior density tensored with the identity, so completion on the orthogonal purifying input support is trace preserving. Null exceptional prefixes can be assigned a fixed operation. There are only finitely many label histories, and their reference prefix laws dominate the actual laws for each tester, by the finite matrix effect bound. Thus the exceptional choices affect no tester comparison.\n\n*Necessity of joint domination.* Given a sequence of finite stages, at each turn take a probability \\(\\mu_j\\) with strictly positive mixture weights on all its decoder rows at that turn over all stages. In the fixed faithful Choi tester, each stage's offered word in each label block is dominated by the product of these measures. Indeed under this tester, condition on an external return-label word, whose labels are prepared independently of the source. At any turn the conditional next offer law is a finite mixture of the decoder rows at that turn, even if selection uses the earlier actual coordinates. Assume the law of the earlier offered prefix is dominated by the corresponding product. For a product-null next-prefix event, its next-coordinate section is \\(\\mu_j\\)-null at product-almost every earlier prefix by Fubini. It is then null for the actual next-coordinate conditional law at almost every actual earlier prefix. Induction gives domination of the full word. Thus every finite stage has joint domination. Trace-norm convergence against this one tester implies variation convergence of the scalar joint output laws, and absolute continuity relative to a fixed product measure is closed in variation. This gives (O.7.2.3bv.3).\n\n*Sufficiency and effective form.* Assume (O.7.2.3bv.3) and write \\(\\mu=\\bigotimes_j\\mu_j\\). Positivity gives a positive matrix density of each \\(C_b\\) relative to \\(\\mu\\). At a common dyadic depth \\(k\\), restrict \\(C_b\\) to all product cells to form the corresponding positive finite blocks. The causal identities persist because summing the cell labels of a future coordinate integrates it out. Cells with zero \\(\\mu_j\\)-mass can be omitted. This gives a finite causal instrument with exact normalized prefix constraints. At each turn decode its offer cell \\(I\\) by the probability row \\(\\mu_j(\\cdot\\mid I)\\). The resulting full matrix measure has exactly the cell conditional expectation of the target matrix density. These expectations converge in \\(L^1(\\mu)\\), since the increasing product dyadic partitions generate the Borel $\\sigma$ algebra. Convergence is in \\(V\\) because there are finitely many label words. The upper bound in (O.7.2.3bv.2) proves uniform complete convergence.\n\nIf the product chart has declared exact conditional decoders, computable cell masses and computable positive-cell masks, an effective paid tower on this chart exists exactly when the tuple of \\(C_b\\) has a single limit-computable name in \\(V\\) by rational positive normalized causal cell instruments decoded on this chart. In particular uniformly computable Choi cell masses suffice when (O.7.2.3bv.3) holds. On each finite grid the causal identities are rational affine constraints on the positive cell blocks, with all impossible cells omitted. A product instrument with strictly positive weights on each possible cell and full-rank quantum outputs gives an interior point relative to these blocks. Mix by a small weight with this instrument and then approximate within the rational affine constraints. This gives an effective rational positive approximation of any effectively named finite cell instrument. The cell weights and Hermitian controls use the finite causal dilation and paid positive-duration schedule of Theorem O.7.2.3m. The exact chart decoders are the supplied services; their branch, label, description and calls enter the resource account. Uniform pulse errors can be made vanishing as in Theorem O.7.2.3bp. Necessity of the limit name follows from the effective tables of the actual stages, rational approximation on their support and the fixed faithful tester lower bound in (O.7.2.3bv.2). No rate of convergence to the target is required.\n\n*Two distinct kinds of joint offer law.* With trivial wires and two turns, let the first offer \\(x_1\\) be uniform and the second repeat it exactly. Both marginals are uniform. The first and second offers form an actual joint diagonal law, which gives no mass to any countable set on the diagonal. For any product \\(\\mu_1\\otimes\\mu_2\\), the diagonal carries mass only at common atoms, a countable set. Thus the target is not product dominated. More directly the fixed tester that retains both actual coordinates and always uses the same return label sees the diagonal with probability one. For each finite fixed-row stage, the tester can instead ask whether the word lies on the diagonal away from the countable union of all stage decoder atoms. That event still has target probability one and stage probability zero at every stage. The complete norm gap is two.\n\nBy contrast, on two fixed singular nonatomic charts \\(\\mu_1,\\mu_2\\), a joint density \\(1+\\tfrac12 g_1(x_1)g_2(x_2)\\), with each \\(g_j\\) taking the values \\(\\pm1\\) on equal chart-mass dyadic halves, gives correlated actual offers. The second coordinate's law depends on the first actual half, and both marginals remain singular. The product density meets (O.7.2.3bv.3) and is already a finite cell table. A finite quantum output can retain the source's coherent instrument on the same cells. In particular the actual joint law need not have a Lebesgue density for the fixed-row criterion to pass. \\(\\square\\)\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bw\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bw-causal-cell-compression-of-arbitrary-diffuse-offer-histories\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3bw (Causal Cell Compression of Arbitrary Diffuse Offer Histories).** Fix a finite sequence of offers and returns, with finite quantum wires as in Theorem O.7.2.3bs. Each source offer includes an actual Borel coordinate \\(x_j\\in[0,1)\\). The tester returns an actual coordinate \\(y_j\\in[0,1)\\) on a normal Lebesgue port and a finite quantum wire. A prescribed source \\(S\\) is any normalized causal Borel instrument tree, possibly with private memory. Its offer kernels can be arbitrary Borel instruments, including singular conditional laws. The terminal comparison includes the actual offered and returned coordinates retained by the tester, its full memory, quantum outputs and any fixed spectator. The finite stages use dyadic return readout and fixed offer decoder rows as in Theorem O.7.2.3bt.\n\nFix a product offer chart \\(\\mu=\\bigotimes_{j=1}^r\\mu_j\\) of Borel probability measures. Its chart dummy sends independent \\(x_j\\sim\\mu_j\\) and independent maximally mixed states on the offered quantum wires. Use one common tester class throughout the comparison. The testers are causal Borel operations whose unconditional returned marginals on the named ports are normal when applied to \\(S\\) and to this chart dummy. Retained records can use their actual joint laws, including singular graphs. A finite stage on the chart uses the fixed rows \\(\\mu_j(\\,\\cdot\\mid I)\\) for its positive-mass offered cells \\(I\\). Its actual returned marginals are normal for the stated tester class.\n\nLet \\(W_S\\) be the complete Choi matrix measure of the one fixed tester sending independent uniform \\(y_j\\), supplying independent normalized faithful Choi inputs, and retaining the actual word, quantum outputs and Choi partners. Suppose its trace measure obeys\n\\[\n       \\operatorname{Tr}W_S\\ll\n                   \\Bigl(\\bigotimes_j\\mu_j\\Bigr)\\otimes\n                   \\Bigl(\\bigotimes_j dy_j\\Bigr).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bw.1}\n\\]\nFrom this one measure define \\(P_k^\\mu S\\) by the following finite prescription. Read each returned depth-\\(k\\) cell. Internally supply its uniform coordinate to the source, and bin the actual source offers at depth \\(k\\). Decode each offered cell with \\(\\mu_j(\\,\\cdot\\mid I)\\), leaving every quantum wire intact. The resulting table has the finite Choi blocks obtained by integrating \\(W_S\\) on the actual product cells and removing the known uniform-return cell factors and the faithful Choi input normalization.\n\nLet \\(d_{\\rm ext}\\) be the supremum of the full terminal trace-norm difference over the common testers, with no factor \\(1/2\\). Under (O.7.2.3bw.1), \\(S\\) belongs to the uniform external closure of finite stages on the product chart exactly when\n\\[\n                         d_{\\rm ext}(S,P_k^\\mu S)\\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bw.2}\n\\]\nEquivalently, \\(P_k^\\mu S\\) are Cauchy in the computable finite causal tester metric of Theorem O.7.2.3bp and converge to \\(S\\) for each fixed tester in the common class. For this equivalent test, computability of the finite metric refers to rational instrument blocks or effectively named blocks. If the chart has an exact named decoder, computable cell masses and a computable positive-cell mask, and the blocks of \\(W_S\\) on dyadic rectangles are uniformly computable, (O.7.2.3bw.2) yields a uniform effective paid tower. No rate in (O.7.2.3bw.2) is required.\n\nFor arbitrary varying fixed rows there is also a necessity without any decoder effectiveness hypothesis. If a countable tower converges for the fixed faithful tester, there is some product chart satisfying (O.7.2.3bw.1). The chart may be taken to be the coordinatewise strictly positive probability mixture of all stage decoder rows. If that tower converges uniformly for a common tester class admissible for this chart and closed under the wrappers, the same chart passes (O.7.2.3bw.2). This last statement concerns the qualitative exact mathematical decoders. Passing it does not supply an effective name for the chart.\n\n*Finite blocks and zero cells.* The finite instrument table is the linkage of the actual source with offer binning and uniform decoding of each input cell. Its blocks are positive. The faithful tester produces unweighted source Choi blocks divided by \\(D_B=\\prod_j\\dim B_j\\) and multiplied by the independent uniform return-cell probabilities. Thus the stated factors are removed by division by known strictly positive constants. The table has the finite causal normalization constraints, since summing the next offer labels and tracing the offered quantum output is trace preserving and leaves the previous prefix independently of the next return choice. A zero \\(\\mu_j\\)-mass cell has zero trace in the faithful measure by (O.7.2.3bw.1). The corresponding table blocks are positive and vanish. They can be omitted. For a wrapper on a general source one can formally set its zero-mass-cell decoder to a point mass at the left endpoint. For sources satisfying (O.7.2.3bw.1), those rows are never used in the compressed table against any tester, because all their finite Choi blocks vanish.\n\nA finite table decoded on this chart is of the weighted type in Theorem O.7.2.3bt. Its positive actual event measures are dominated by the same tester's chart dummy law. In particular normality on the dummy guarantees normality on every such finite stage. The finite optimizer of Theorem O.7.2.3bp still gives their exact pairwise distance with the stated common tester class. To see the upper bound, a finite tester can simulate an arbitrary tester's actual offered coordinates privately using the declared within-cell chart rows and pass only its chosen return cells. For the lower bound a finite tester lifts by applying its finite operation to the actual offer cell and returning uniformly within its selected cell. The lifted tester's returns are normal even on \\(S\\).\n\n*The causal wrapper is contractive.* Given a tester \\(T\\) for the decoded process, construct a tester \\(\\widetilde T_k\\) for the original process. Keep the original offer \\(x_j\\) private and draw a surrogate offer \\(x'_j\\) using the declared row in its cell. Run \\(T\\) on the surrogate offered history and on the actual quantum wires. Keep its returned coordinate \\(y'_j\\) in its retained records and send the source a uniform \\(y_j\\) in the cell of \\(y'_j\\), beside the same quantum input. Each step uses only the causal prefix. The return sent to the original source has an absolutely continuous marginal on every underlying source because the within-cell return decoder is uniform. In particular \\(\\widetilde T_k\\) is normal on \\(S\\) and on the chart dummy. Even when a surrogate law would give the internal \\(T\\) a singular \\(y'_j\\) marginal, this is a private Borel record of the wrapped tester and its actual port output remains normal. The wrapper subsequently drops its private original-coordinate records and retains the records of \\(T\\). The resulting complete output is exactly that of \\(T\\) on \\(P_k^\\mu S\\). The same wrapper works for every second compared source \\(D\\), so trace-norm contraction under dropping records gives\n\\[\n             d_{\\rm ext}(P_k^\\mu S,P_k^\\mu D)\n                          \\le d_{\\rm ext}(S,D).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bw.3}\n\\]\n\nA chart stage \\(F\\) of depth at most \\(k\\) is fixed by this wrapper in external comparison. Its coarse return cell is unchanged, and conditional averaging followed by decoding on the finer offered cells leaves its original chart row exactly the same. Thus\n\\[\n     d_{\\rm ext}(S,P_k^\\mu S)\n           \\le 2 d_{\\rm ext}(S,F)\\qquad (k\\ge\\operatorname{depth}F).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bw.4}\n\\]\nThis proves necessity of (O.7.2.3bw.2) for a chart tower. Sufficiency follows because the compressions are finite chart stages. For the equivalent Cauchy and fixed-tester formulation, uniform convergence implies both conditions. Conversely for large \\(k,\\ell\\) the Cauchy bound is uniform in all testers. For any one tester let \\(\\ell\\) tend to infinity in this bound using fixed-tester convergence. Taking the supremum of the resulting bound over testers gives (O.7.2.3bw.2).\n\n*Arbitrary rows and the paid criterion.* For an arbitrary sequence of fixed-row stages, choose \\(\\mu_j\\) to give every row at turn \\(j\\) positive mixture weight. The faithful tester's actual complete classical law for every stage is dominated by \\(\\mu\\otimes\\bigotimes_jdy_j\\). This follows inductively: the faithful tester gives a fresh uniform return, and conditional on the past the next offer is a mixture of the finitely many row laws, each dominated by \\(\\mu_j\\). Thus convergence in retained trace norm for the faithful tester forces (O.7.2.3bw.1). For any one fixed stage \\(F\\) from the tower, once \\(k\\) refines its return readout the wrapper only changes its offer rows. A row \\(\\nu\\ll\\mu_j\\) is replaced by the row whose density is the dyadic conditional expectation of \\(d\\nu/d\\mu_j\\). This tends in variation to \\(\\nu\\). The maximum over the finitely many rows at that stage tends to zero. Telescoping the finite operations, with the intervening tester operations trace-norm contractions, gives \\(d_{\\rm ext}(F,P_k^\\mu F)\\to0\\). Using this in the triangle inequality and (O.7.2.3bw.3) proves necessity of (O.7.2.3bw.2) for the selected chart for the common tester class in the statement.\n\nFor an effective declared chart, computable dyadic blocks of \\(W_S\\) give effective finite causal instrument tables. As in Theorem O.7.2.3bt, mix with a strictly positive instrument on the possible cells and approximate within the rational affine causal identities. The mask is used to omit impossible blocks. Choosing the rational approximations and paid pulse accuracies with vanishing uniform error gives the claimed tower. The finite dilations use the resource ledger and positive-duration controls of Theorem O.7.2.3m. The chart's exact decoded coordinate is supplied by its declared service, and each used row and invocation enters the resource account.\n\n*What independent-return domination misses.* Use two turns, let \\(x_1\\) be uniform, and let \\(x_2\\) be an independent coordinate on a fixed singular nonatomic chart. Take trivial intermediate quantum wires and output the bit \\(\\mathbf 1_{\\{x_1=y_1\\}}\\). The second return is unused. The faithful tester law is product dominated on this chart, because its \\(y_1\\) is independent uniform and the bit vanishes almost surely. The canonical compressions also output zero. The fixed normal identity return \\(y_1=x_1\\) makes the prescribed bit one and gives canonical error two. Finite tables that output one when \\(x_1,y_1\\) share the same depth-\\(k\\) cell converge for each fixed tester by bounded convergence on its actual word law, with the singular second offer decoded exactly. Their uniform error cannot tend to zero. Indeed for each finite stage at return depth \\(k\\), compare the identity tester with the tester drawing \\(y_1\\) uniformly in the same depth-\\(k\\) cell as \\(x_1\\), using identical second returns. Both have normal returns on the stated chart. Their finite-stage bit laws are identical because their return cells agree. The prescribed bit laws are the opposite deterministic bits. At least one test gives trace-norm error at least one. The constant fair-bit stage with the same offers attains one. This supplies a sharp distinction between product domination and the full causal uniform test. \\(\\square\\)\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bx\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bx-strong-completed-wiener-path-histories-across-grids\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3bx (Strong Completed Wiener-Path Histories across Grids).** Fix a bounded dyadic horizon and nested effective finite checkpoint grids, with finite quantum intervention wires and effective coarse joins as in Theorem O.7.2.3bo. At the right endpoint of each interval the source releases the actual completed continuous *increment* path, based at zero at the left endpoint. The full coordinate path is recovered by adding the successive increments to the initial point. At the right checkpoint the tester may use the completed path prefix and its retained registers to return its quantum input. Its fixed conditional operations may be arbitrary Borel measurable normal operations in the completed prefix. Compare the entire retained completed path record and coherent outputs in trace variation, including the tester's outcomes and any fixed spectator.\n\nFor grid \\(k\\) let \\(\\Lambda_k\\) be the product of the Wiener laws on the based continuous interval paths, in one fixed dimension. Assume an exact conditional decoder on these Wiener charts. It supplies the path conditioned on a positive-mass finite cylinder cell given by dyadic interior and endpoint times and rational coordinate intervals. At each turn the decoder uses only the chosen finite label on that interval. This is a declared mathematical output service, with its descriptions, labels and calls in the resource account. The finite Hermitian controls act on the encoded cells. Use the exact coarse restriction by concatenating increments and joining the omitted finite quantum wires. Assume compatibility of the prescribed external causal Choi measures under these restrictions.\n\nSuppose the prescribed Choi measures at all grids have positive matrix densities \\(F_k\\) relative to \\(\\Lambda_k\\), with the integrated causal prefix identities. Use the unweighted finite Choi convention, or its fixed faithful-input normalization. Then a single effective paid tower of finite causal cylinder instruments on these charts realizes the stated strong comparison on every grid if and only if there is one uniform limit-computable name for the array\n\\[\n                     F_k\\in L^1(\\Lambda_k;\\mathcal T_{\\rm fin}).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bx.1}\n\\]\nThe name is a computable sequence of rational finite matrix-valued cylinder tables for each grid, converging in the displayed norm. The tables in a supplied name can be arbitrary Hermitian tables. No convergence modulus in the name is required.\n\nThis criterion covers Brownian paths whose drift is selected by actual quantum intervention outcomes, as specified below. Exact deterministic step-path decoders cannot substitute for the conditional path service in the strong comparison. Even for the uncontrolled Brownian law there is one Borel path test that distinguishes every such finite deterministic step-record stage by trace-norm difference two.\n\n*Finite normalized cylinders.* In each interval choose an increasing finite generating cylinder partition, with rational box endpoints and dyadic path-evaluation times, including the interval endpoint. One can use a nested truncation and subdivision of coordinate boxes. Remove repeated observation times and the deterministic starting coordinate, and omit empty boxes. Every remaining cell has strictly positive probability under the nonsingular Gaussian law of the observations. Conditional averaging in the product partition of the intervals preserves positivity and the integrated causal prefix equations. At a given turn, summing over the next interval's cylinder cells gives the same causal marginal as integrating its entire path and tracing the new quantum output. The finite blocks are normalized causal instruments. Conversely their cylinder decoding has precisely those conditional-average densities. Conditional expectations converge in \\(L^1\\) because the cylinder partitions generate the Borel $\\sigma$ algebra of the product continuous path space. This follows by density of finite cylinder simple functions in \\(L^1\\) and trace-norm contractivity of conditional averaging.\n\nAt any fixed finite grid and partition the positive instrument weights, before division by cylinder masses, satisfy rational affine causal constraints. A product instrument assigning a strictly positive rational weight to every allowed cell and using full-rank quantum outputs is strictly positive on each of the allowed blocks. Mixing in a small amount of this instrument and approximating within the affine space gives dense rational positive normalized instruments, as in Theorem O.7.2.3bp. Their finite causal dilations and positive-duration Hermitian controls are those of Theorem O.7.2.3m. At a checkpoint release the cell label and use the declared conditional decoder on that interval. All finite control, label, clock and retained memory costs are included. Pulse errors may be made uniformly vanishing in complete trace norm because the intervening conditional decoders and tester operations are trace-preserving contractions.\n\n*Computable coarse restrictions.* On the finer Wiener product chart, a coarse increment evaluation is the sum of the completed finer interval increments and the current finer segment evaluation. The exact product chart pushes forward to the coarse Wiener chart. A finer candidate's source density is constant on its cylinder cells. Its coarse restriction consists of the same finite source blocks with the fixed coarse quantum joins, together with the pushforward of each conditional cylinder measure. The removed quantum joins act by bounded linear maps on the fixed finite matrices. Integrating a bounded scalar multiplier or comparing with a coarse simple table can be computed at finite stage as follows. Every involved finer or pulled-back coarse cell is a finite set of linear inequalities in a finite Gaussian vector. Use the independent increments between the distinct positive observation times as coordinates. Their distribution has a computable strictly positive Gaussian density, with computable tails. The boundary of each nonconstant affine inequality has measure zero, so inner and outer approximations by bounded rational boxes compute the corresponding probabilities. Constant inequalities are decided directly. The effective finite matrix operations then compute the pushforward Choi measure's pairing with all coarse cylinder tests.\n\nFor the full \\(L^1\\) restriction scores, the same calculation gives the coarse rectangle conditional expectations of the pushforward density \\(G\\). To compute its distance to a coarse simple table \\(H\\), the sequence of coarse conditional expectations gives increasing lower bounds for \\(\\|G-H\\|_{L^1}\\). The following construction also supplies upper approximations. The path chart under the fine partition can be described by the joint Gaussian law of the coarse observations and the additional fine observations used by the candidate. In fact every fine observation is a linear combination of the absolute coarse path values at the same observation time and the fine segment's initial time. Thus a finite candidate's density is already a simple cylinder function of the full coarse path. Retaining one or several coarse intervals partitions these evaluation times into their respective intervals. Every box of the fine candidate becomes a finite Boolean combination of linear Gaussian inequalities in evaluations of the coarse path. After joining omitted quantum wires, each block coefficient is a fixed effectively named finite matrix. The difference from \\(H\\) is constant on the common finite partition by those inequalities. Sum the probabilities times the finite matrix trace norms to compute its exact \\(L^1\\) score to arbitrary precision. \n\n*One tower across all grids.* Enumerate the rational normalized finite instrument candidates together with their actual grid depths. For a coarse grid \\(k\\) use the capped \\(L^1\\) discrepancy to the current approximant in (O.7.2.3bx.1), evaluated by the preceding restriction algorithm for each candidate deep enough. Use the weighted finite catalogue selection of Theorem O.7.2.3am, with its missing-depth penalty, and retain the actual grid of the selected candidate. To see density for any finite list of coarse scores, choose a fine prescribed grid containing them all. Approximate \\(F\\) there by a rational normalized fine cylinder table. Exact compatibility identifies the restrictions of \\(F\\) with the prescribed coarser densities. Joining the omitted quantum wires and pushing the classical paths forward are bounded on trace variation for each fixed fine grid. Hence the same rational table fits the finite list to any desired accuracy. Such a candidate remains available at every later stage. Convergence of the finitely many supplied names, the geometric tail of the remaining loss, and the missing-depth penalty give one tower whose actual restrictions converge in (O.7.2.3bx.1) on every grid. This argument needs neither an effective target rate nor a bound on the densities.\n\n*Complete Borel tester comparison.* On each fixed grid, expand the finite open quantum wires in matrix units. The linked tester operation for a specified path word is bounded on the input matrices by a fixed constant depending only on the wire dimensions. This uses trace-norm contraction on Hermitian inputs of each complete conditional tester operation, as in Theorem O.7.2.3bo. Its finite matrix coefficients are measurable, and all outcomes and retained memory can be included in its leaving trace class or in the actual output instrument. The trace variation difference after linking is bounded by this constant times \\(\\int\\|F_k-G_k\\|_1\\,d\\Lambda_k\\). This estimate permits arbitrary Borel path-dependent conditional operations, and comparison retains the full actual completed path record. The concatenation map preserves this bound under its pushforward, so it also gives the full continuous path and joint coherent comparison required by QJ. For necessity, apply the one fixed faithful Choi tester which retains the entire actual completed increment word and all partners. Its complete trace variation is precisely the \\(L^1\\) discrepancy in (O.7.2.3bx.1), up to the known Choi normalization. At each finite stage its restriction to any contained grid has effective cylinder coefficients and probabilities by the restriction calculation. It can be approximated to accuracy \\(2^{-n}\\) by rational cylinder box tables: Gaussian-null boundaries give convergent finite partitions, and searching finite box refinements with certified small mass of the crossing region makes the approximation effective. Stage \\(n\\) can use a default table for a grid not yet contained. Required strong convergence gives the names in (O.7.2.3bx.1).\n\n*Controlled drift and the deterministic-record obstruction.* In one dimension take an independent Brownian increment on each open checkpoint interval. Fix a finite intervention grid included in every comparison grid. Let a finite quantum instrument at each checkpoint of that fixed grid select one of finitely many computable drift values \\(v\\), applying that constant drift on the next interval. Let any coherent quantum operation on that next interval be a fixed effective channel associated to the selected mode. On an interval of length \\(h\\), the increment path with that drift has density \\(\\exp(v z(h)-v^2h/2)\\) relative to the Wiener chart. To verify it, its Gaussian joint law on any finite observation times has precisely this likelihood ratio when the endpoint is included, by multiplying the shifted independent increment densities and telescoping their linear terms. The cylinder tests determine the full path law. Multiplying the successive densities and using the finite causal quantum blocks gives the prescribed joint Choi densities. For finer grids insert no-op mode joins at the omitted checkpoints. The constant-drift exponential densities multiply to the corresponding coarse density, and the original quantum instruments still act at their actual checkpoints. This gives the required compatibility. The densities have uniform effective \\(L^1\\) approximants on each fixed grid: truncate the endpoint Gaussian variables using computable Gaussian tails under the finitely many shifted laws, then use a bounded effective uniform approximation of the exponential on the retained boxes. Finite control words and effective finite quantum blocks preserve the estimates. Thus the selected actual intervention outcome changes the future path law in the same paid tower.\n\nOn the other hand the set of continuous nonconstant paths on any positive-length interval is Borel in the full path space and has Wiener probability one. Every deterministic finite step path, including a constant path and one with a separately sampled endpoint, gives that event probability zero. One fixed tester retaining the interval record can therefore measure this event and distinguish every tower of finite deterministic step records by the maximal trace-norm difference two. This comparison concerns full retained records. The weak \\(J_1\\) tests of Theorem O.7.2.3bo remain applicable to their own convergence. \\(\\square\\)\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723by\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723by-online-drift-changes-and-a-volatility-obstruction\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3by (Online Drift Changes and a Volatility Obstruction).** Fix a dyadic horizon \\(T\\) and the one-dimensional continuous path carrier of Theorem O.7.2.3bx. On the canonical path and a private independent seed, prescribe an ordered list of at most \\(N\\) stopping rules for the observed path filtration, with command marks measurable at their stopping times. A missing command can be assigned time \\(T\\). Each command selects a drift in a fixed bounded menu, with all magnitudes at most \\(M\\). Before the first command the drift is a fixed member of the same menu. The source law is the unit-volatility weak solution with this path-dependent drift. The retained record includes the actual full path and the logical command word. The logical stopping times may also be retained as the same Borel statistics of the path and seed in each comparison. The separate physical command times of a rounded implementation are its actual dyadic ticks.\n\nLet \\(P\\) be this marked path law. For mesh \\(h\\), define \\(P_h\\) by executing each command at the first tick at or after its logical stopping time. At a tick, evaluate the prescribed logical stopping rules and marks on the completed path prefix then available, and execute commands in their original order if their physical ticks coincide. The logical rules, including dependence on previous logical marks, are evaluated on the same canonical path and seed in both laws. They have no input from physical delay times. In the full retained trace-norm convention without a factor \\(1/2\\),\n\\[\n                    \\|P-P_h\\|_{\\rm TV,1}\\le 2M\\sqrt{Nh}.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3by.1}\n\\]\nThis bound allows arbitrary Borel stopping rules. Realization of the exact rules at a physical tick requires an effective supplied service or a finite computable approximation of the rules.\n\nThere is a finite paid tower for the case where a fixed computable threshold \\(a>0\\) triggers a change from zero drift to a fixed computable bounded drift \\(v\\) at the first passage through \\(a\\). The tower uses only rounded finite path endpoint records at its actual command ticks and the declared exact conditional Wiener decoder of Theorem O.7.2.3bx. Its full retained path and finite trigger indicator converge in trace variation. The finite source can also retain the output of a fixed finite quantum instrument executed when its command is triggered, with the same fixed incoming state and spectator in the comparison. The effect of its outcome on subsequent drift may be included using a finite computable drift menu.\n\nIn contrast, let a target have volatility \\(1\\) before its first passage through \\(a\\) and \\(2\\) afterwards. For any countable sequence of stages whose volatility is piecewise constant with changes only at their finite deterministic clock ticks, there is a fixed Borel test of the retained path that gives a positive trace-norm gap independent of the stage. If the target is instead required to switch by an almost surely interior continuous stopping time, the gap is the maximal value two. These comparisons allow the stages exact continuous interval decoders. The distinction is visible in the path itself.\n\n*Delay on the same path.* Start from the product of Wiener law and the independent seed law. A bounded predictable drift \\(u\\) on the canonical path and seed gives the probability density\n\\[\n L_u=\\exp\\!\\left(\\int_0^T u_t\\,dX_t\n                            -\\tfrac12\\int_0^T |u_t|^2\\,dt\\right).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3by.2}\n\\]\nThe boundedness makes this exponential a true martingale. Under the new measure \\(X_t-\\int_0^t u_sds\\) is Brownian, and applying the inverse exponential to any weak solution gives this canonical law. The seed law is unchanged, by conditioning on the seed in the exponential construction. For two bounded predictable drifts \\(u,w\\), subtract their logarithmic densities under \\(P_u\\). The stochastic integral against its Brownian motion has mean zero, so\n\\[\n       D(P_u\\|P_w)=\\tfrac12 {\\mathbb E}_{P_u}\n                                     \\int_0^T |u_t-w_t|^2\\,dt.\n\\tag{O.7.2.3by.3}\n\\]\nThese are the bounded-drift change of measure and its entropy identity. See Girsanov's original change-of-measure theorem (Girsanov 1960).\n\nUse the predictable intervals following each logical stopping time or its rounded tick. On any one canonical path and seed the two mode lists agree except possibly on the union of the logical-to-rounded delay intervals, with length at most \\(Nh\\). This statement holds even when a later rule uses a prior logical command, since both lists use the same earlier marks on the same path. The finite energy difference is at most \\(4M^2Nh\\). Pinsker's inequality in the no-half convention gives (O.7.2.3by.1) from (O.7.2.3by.3). The logical word and logical times are common measurable functions of the canonical path and seed, so appending them is a pushforward and cannot increase the bound. The actual actuation clocks in the rounded source are separate finite records. See Fedotov et al. (2003).\n\n*Finite first-passage implementation.* Write \\(\\tau=\\inf\\{t:X_t\\ge a\\}\\wedge T\\). At each depth-\\(n\\) grid interval compute a finite rounded representation of its endpoint increment as part of its selected cylinder cell. For the analytic comparison use interval length \\(h_n\\downarrow0\\), bounded endpoint cutoff \\(R_n=n+1\\), and rounding error at most \\(h_n^2\\) inside the cutoff. The cutoff has two finite overflow cells. The actual latent path has that endpoint-cell label by the declared conditional decoder. The source adds the representative increments and retains a Boolean trigger. It executes the command at the first grid point before \\(T\\) whose sum crosses the threshold, using a rational approximation of \\(a\\) within \\(h_n\\). If there is no such point, it makes no command before \\(T\\).\n\nFor every continuous canonical path the increments are eventually inside the cutoffs. Then the maximum cumulative rounding error is at most \\((T/h_n)h_n^2=Th_n\\). For Wiener-almost every path, the approximate trigger time tends to \\(\\tau\\). Before \\(\\tau-\\epsilon\\) the continuous path has strictly positive distance from the threshold by compactness. On \\(\\{\\tau<T\\}\\), the path almost surely exceeds \\(a\\) arbitrarily soon after the first hit, by the Brownian strong Markov property and immediate positive excursions from zero. At a point of strict excess the continuity and the refining grids force a sufficiently late approximant to trigger by that point plus an arbitrarily small time. The event of a first hit exactly at \\(T\\) is null, since it implies \\(X_T=a\\). A path with no hit on \\([0,T]\\) has a strictly positive gap below \\(a\\) and eventually never triggers. Thus the trigger indicator also converges almost surely. Bounded drift gives equivalence of the target path law to Wiener law by (O.7.2.3by.2), so all these almost-sure statements hold under the target law.\n\nThe ideal grid source follows the rounded endpoint rule exactly on the canonical path. Its drift differs from the target on an interval of length at most the absolute trigger time difference. That difference tends to zero almost surely under the target law and is bounded by \\(T\\). Equation (O.7.2.3by.3) and Pinsker therefore give trace variation convergence of the full path measures, even though no effective threshold-hitting modulus was supplied. For a retained trigger bit and finite quantum instrument output, use the common-path comparison on the event where both trigger indicators agree. The instruments then have the same fixed state, spectator and outcome maps. For a finite outcome-dependent drift menu, include the outcome in a common initially latent finite register with its prescribed probability. Condition on each value in the drift comparison, then retain its prescribed quantum output precisely when the trigger fires. This reproduces the stated instrument because there is no access to the latent value before the trigger and there is no other operation on its input before it. The probability of trigger disagreement tends to zero, and the bounded density estimate gives complete trace variation convergence including the finite record and retained quantum output.\n\nAt each actual tick the finite stage knows the rounded cumulative sum, mode and trigger. Conditional on its mode, the next interval's true path law has the exponential endpoint density of Theorem O.7.2.3bx. Use a finite effective cylinder refinement of the endpoint rounding cells to approximate this density in \\(L^1\\) by its normalized conditional averages. Effective Gaussian tails and uniform approximation on bounded endpoint intervals give, for each stage and mode, arbitrarily small certified error. Its complete next-interval law, including the coarse rounding cell, is then produced by the finite instrument and the exact conditional Wiener decoder on the selected refinement cell. The finite stage uses only the coarse cell for its command decision. Telescoping the conditional errors over the finitely many actual intervals and modes controls the full retained path error. Choose the sum of table and pulse errors at most \\(2^{-n}\\). The finite row selection, trigger, outcome records, Hermitian controls and positive-duration subslots are paid as in Theorem O.7.2.3m. The actual command is computed before selecting the next interval law. No earlier path segment is selected using that future mode.\n\n*Recovering a volatility switch.* For a continuous path \\(X\\) and rational time \\(t\\), form the sum of squared increments on the dyadic partition up to \\(t\\), with a final short interval ending at \\(t\\). For the stated bounded volatility processes, the limit exists almost surely simultaneously at all rational \\(t\\) and equals \\(\\int_0^t \\sigma_s^2\\,ds\\). In fact, writing the error as the sum of the within-cell Itô integrals \\(2\\int (X_s-X_{\\rm left})\\,dX_s\\) gives a mean squared error bounded by a constant times the mesh for these continuous martingales. Summability along dyadic meshes and the probability bound by the second moment give almost-sure convergence at each rational time. The same estimate holds with bounded drift after bounding its finite variation contribution. This defines the limiting values by a Borel function of the path alone.\n\nFor the target after an interior switch time \\(\\tau\\), the rational values come from \\(t\\mapsto t+3(t-\\tau)_+\\). Its switch time is the infimum of the rational \\(t\\) at which the recovered value exceeds \\(t\\). A first-passage time on the interval before the switch has no atom at any interior deterministic time, since an atom at \\(t\\) would require the unswitched Brownian value to equal \\(a\\) at that time. It has a strictly positive probability of lying inside the horizon. Let \\(D\\) be the countable union of all stage clock ticks. The Borel test asks whether the rational recovered values equal the displayed curve for some interior \\(\\tau\\notin D\\), using the recovered infimum to specify it. It has target probability \\(\\mathbb P(\\tau<T)\\). Any piecewise constant clocked volatility curve can agree with that curve only if its nontrivial kink is at an allowed clock tick, so its probability is zero at every stage. This yields the claimed lower bound \\(2\\mathbb P(\\tau<T)\\). An almost surely interior version is obtained by taking \\(\\tau\\) as the first hit of the deterministic decreasing boundary \\(a-t/(T-t)\\) for \\(0\\le t<T\\). The boundary starts above the initial point and tends to minus infinity while the unswitched path is continuous on the closed horizon. The hit is interior almost surely. At each fixed interior time a hit requires equality to the deterministic boundary, so its distribution has no atoms. The same test yields the gap two in this version. \\(\\square\\)\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bz\"></a>\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o723bz-stopped-wiener-decoding-for-online-volatility-control\"></a>**Theorem O.7.2.3bz (Stopped-Wiener Decoding for Online Volatility Control).** Fix a positive rational threshold \\(a\\), a positive dyadic horizon \\(T\\), and a Brownian path started at zero. Let \\(\\tau\\) be its first hit of \\(a\\) before \\(T\\), with a distinguished no-hit case. A stopped prefix is the path held constant at \\(a\\) after a hit, or the unchanged path on \\([0,T]\\) in the no-hit case. Let \\(\\lambda\\) be the law of this stopped prefix together with its hit indicator and logical hit time. The latter time is a Borel function of the prefix and indicator.\n\nOn a hit, release the stopped prefix and accept a finite quantum input \\(B\\). A Borel field \\(\\mathcal I_s=(\\mathcal I_{s,c})_{c\\in\\mathcal{C}}\\) of normalized finite quantum instruments, with a finite command alphabet \\(\\mathcal{C}\\) and finite output wire \\(A\\), selects the command on the actual prefix \\(s\\). Retain the selected \\(c\\) and its quantum output. A tester may keep a fixed spectator and prepare the input by an arbitrary fixed measurable normal operation on the actual prefix and its memory. In the no-hit case take a fixed declared output with no command. After a hit, supply a fresh independent Wiener path \\(W\\) on \\([0,1]\\) and append the continuation\n\\[\n X_t=a+\\sigma_c\\sqrt{T-\\tau}\\,W\\!\\left(\\frac{t-\\tau}{T-\\tau}\\right)\n                    \\quad(\\tau\\le t\\le T),\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bz.1}\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\sigma_c>0\\) is a fixed computable finite volatility menu. The comparison retains the full actual path, logical stopping record, selected command and coherent output in complete trace variation.\n\nAdmit the following exact decoder service. A finite label describes one cylinder cell of the stopped Wiener prefix. Its decoder supplies the actual prefix with law \\(\\lambda\\) conditional on that cell, together with its logical hit flag and time. A new invocation after the command supplies the independent standard continuation \\(W\\), followed by the time and space transformation in (O.7.2.3bz.1) using the retained exact time. The service releases the stopped prefix before the command, and the continuation after it. The finite controller has access to the cylinder label, flag and finite quantum input for its finite command operation. The decoder's exact time is used for concatenation. The conditional stopped decoder and continuation can be specified from ordinary exact Wiener draws by the constructions below. They are supplied mathematical services. Their exact draws and use of logical time are part of the declared source contract. The positive-duration finite control subslots and the service invocations have their separate physical schedule and resource account.\n\nWrite \\(J(s)=\\bigoplus_c J(\\mathcal I_{s,c})\\) in the unweighted finite Choi convention on the hit set. Its no-hit block is fixed. Then a uniform effective finite paid tower using this service, with the exact prefix law \\(\\lambda\\) in the ideal decoded stages, realizes the stated strong comparison if and only if \\(J\\) has a limit-computable name in\n\\[\n                       L^1(\\lambda;\\mathcal T_{\\rm fin}).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bz.2}\n\\]\nThe name consists of rational finite matrices constant on the generating stopped-prefix cells below, converging in integrated trace norm. No convergence modulus is required. Convergence for the fixed faithful input tester that retains all records is necessary, and the resulting tower converges uniformly over the stated testers with the same input and output wires. A restriction of the final output to any smaller deterministic horizon uses the same source law and discards any command output whose logical hit is after that horizon.\n\n*Effective stopped-prefix cells.* Use a finite dyadic grid on \\([0,T]\\). Record the hit slot between its consecutive times, or the no-hit case, and a rational box for every stopped coordinate at a grid point preceding the hit slot. At and after the first grid point following a hit, the stopped coordinate is exactly \\(a\\). In the no-hit case all grid coordinates are recorded. Exclude the deterministic initial value. Choose the rational interval partitions to cover \\((-\\infty,a)\\), with a lower tail cell and finitely many subdivisions. Their finite refinements and the time grids can be taken nested and generating. The resulting $\\sigma$ algebras determine the stopped path by continuity and determine the logical hit time by the nested slots. All possible cells have positive \\(\\lambda\\)-mass. For a hit cell this follows by moving between interior points of the stipulated boxes while staying below \\(a\\) until the final designated interval, then crossing \\(a\\) strictly within it. For a no-hit cell use a path staying below \\(a\\) throughout. A sufficiently small uniform neighborhood of the chosen piecewise linear path has those same properties and positive Wiener measure. The last assertion follows from Gaussian increments and the positive probability for a Brownian bridge to stay in an open uniform neighborhood of its linear interpolant, applied on finitely many subintervals. The strict crossing in this positivity proof is a condition on the underlying Wiener path before stopping.\n\nCell probabilities are uniformly computable. Membership in a cell is almost surely decidable from an exact continuous Wiener name. At a fixed dyadic grid time the maximum is either strictly below or strictly above \\(a\\) almost surely, by the atomless Brownian maximum law for \\(a>0\\). Both strict comparisons are recognizable from uniform path approximants. Before the hit the observed coordinate is the underlying Gaussian value, and the box boundary events are null. After a hit use the deterministic stopped value, with no boundary comparison. This gives almost-sure decisions for the cell and its complement. The usual effective Wiener law on continuous paths, as in the named law constructions of Theorem O.7.2.3bj, supplies lower probability names for the two disjoint decided events. Their masses sum to one, so the cell probability is computable. The same certificate gives an almost-sure name of the stopped prefix and logical hit time: a compact interval strictly before the hit has maximum below \\(a\\), and after an interior hit the underlying Brownian path exceeds \\(a\\) arbitrarily soon. The latter fact is the Brownian strong Markov property and the immediate positive excursions from zero. A hit exactly at \\(T\\) has probability zero. Uniform continuity then computes the stopped path from the approximating times. Rejection from fresh exact Wiener draws followed by this stopping map supplies the conditional row of each positive cell. The geometric trial tail has a computable bound from any certified positive lower bound on the cell probability. The number of trials need not have a deterministic finite bound. The finite source uses the declared row service, with its precise conditional law, rather than treating those trials as a bounded number of Hermitian gates.\n\n*Strong tester bounds.* For two command fields \\(J,J'\\) the complete error for any tester is bounded above by\n\\[\n                  \\int\\|J(s)-J'(s)\\|_1\\,d\\lambda(s).\n\\tag{O.7.2.3bz.3}\n\\]\nAt each prefix the instrument with its retained classical command is a channel into a finite direct sum. On a pure input with any spectator the action of a Choi difference can be obtained by compressing its normalized faithful input on the ancilla, with operator norm at most \\(\\sqrt{\\dim B}\\) for the compressing matrix. Its resulting trace norm is therefore at most the unweighted Choi trace norm. Convexity gives the same estimate for mixed inputs, and integrating gives (O.7.2.3bz.3). For the fixed tester supplying one half of a normalized faithful Choi pair, retaining the other half and the actual prefix, the complete difference before continuation is exactly \\((\\dim B)^{-1}\\) times (O.7.2.3bz.3). The continuation adds the same stochastic decoder conditional on \\(s,c\\) to both source outputs. Adding it is trace-norm contractive. Forgetting it recovers the original retained state, so the faithful lower bound remains valid for the full path comparison. One can recover the stopped prefix and logical time from the full path and hit flag, since the first hit occurs before the change of volatility. This also verifies the lower bound if the retained full path is used in place of two separate interval records.\n\n*Finite implementation.* The conditional expectations of \\(J\\) on the finite generating cells are positive normalized instrument blocks. They converge in (O.7.2.3bz.2) by density of cell simple functions and trace-norm contractivity of averaging. Within a cell the no-hit or hit type is known. Rational instrument tables are dense among the positive normalized ones: mix each hit-cell table with a strictly positive depolarizing instrument giving positive weights to every command, and approximate within the rational affine trace-preserving equalities. For a supplied name of \\(J\\), select at stage \\(n\\) from the first \\(n\\) rational normalized instrument tables a candidate within \\(2^{-n}\\) of the least integrated trace discrepancy to the \\(n\\)-th named approximation. The distances are computable by cell masses and the finite matrix trace norms. Each fixed dense candidate remains available after its catalogue index. The triangle inequality and convergence of the supplied name show convergence to \\(J\\) without needing a target rate.\n\nThe ideal stage first draws a finite label with its computable stopped-Wiener cell probabilities and decodes the stopped prefix. It implements the selected cell's normalized finite instrument on the actual quantum return, and only then invokes the independent continuation service for that command. At a hit, the conditional continuation law is the Brownian strong Markov continuation with the selected volatility, so these operations give the claimed online law. In the no-hit case no continuation is appended. The finite sampler and instrument can be represented by computable finite Hermitian control coefficients and positive-duration subslots as in Theorem O.7.2.3m. Approximating finite cell weights and pulses to total variation and complete norm error at most \\(2^{-n}\\) makes the actual stage differ by at most that quantity. The normalized ideal table uses the exact computable cell weights. All finite control blocks, cells, command records and decoder invocations are retained and paid in the specified inventory. The controller acts after the decoder releases the prefix and before a continuation is requested. The mathematical splice is at logical time \\(\\tau\\). Its physical actuation follows release in the paid schedule. Conversely a uniform effective tower of such cell stages has effectively named finite normalized command tables. The faithful tester bound forces their convergence in (O.7.2.3bz.2), and rational approximation to vanishing error supplies the required limit name.\n\n*An exact switch with a coherent command.* Take a returned qubit, let \\(c\\in\\{0,1\\}\\) and use \\(K_0=\\operatorname{diag}(\\sqrt3/2,1/2)\\), \\(K_1=\\operatorname{diag}(1/2,\\sqrt3/2)\\) as the two Kraus operators. Their squared operators sum to \\(I\\), and each branch preserves a nonzero off-diagonal component of a coherent input. Use \\(\\sigma_0=1\\) and \\(\\sigma_1=2\\). The field is constant on the hit set, so the ideal instrument already uses a finite table with only a hit and a no-hit label. For an input \\(\\lvert1\\rangle\\) the volatility changes to \\(2\\) with probability \\(3/4\\) conditional on a hit. The hit time has no atoms in \\((0,T)\\). The independent continuation therefore gives a nondyadic volatility switch on a set of positive probability, with the exact retained path law. The quadratic-variation obstruction of Theorem O.7.2.3by still applies to deterministic-clock volatility schedules. Here the supplied decoder joins the independent continuation at the actual logical stopping time. \\(\\square\\)\n\n<a id=\"hypothesis-o724-nondegenerate-causal-cone\"></a>**Hypothesis O.7.2.4 (Nondegenerate causal cone).** Assume a separately established attained operational frontier whose speed obeys the uniform upper bound of Theorem 46 and whose discrete propagation compatibility is recorded by Proposition F.1. This frontier is nondegenerate at every point of $M_{\\mathrm{reg}}$ and coincides with the characteristic cone of $p_x$: for every nonzero spatial covector $k_i$, the polynomial\n$$\n\\omega \\;\\longmapsto\\; p_x(\\omega,k) \\;=\\; a(x)\\,\\omega^2 + 2b^i(x)\\,\\omega\\,k_i + A^{ij}(x)\\,k_i\\,k_j\n$$\nhas two distinct real roots in $\\omega$.\n\n<a id=\"definition-o725-cone-saturation-certificate-mathfrak-c_mathrmcone\"></a>**Definition O.7.2.5 (Cone-Saturation Certificate $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{cone}}$).** A cone-saturation certificate for a retained sector family is a finite proof record\n$$\n\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{cone}}\n=\n(\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{sec}},\\mathcal V_{\\mathrm{front}},\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{front}},\\mathcal N_{\\mathrm{char}},\\mathcal I_{\\mathrm{cone}},u\\text{-datum},\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{attr}},(H_{\\xi\\xi},D_{\\xi\\xi}),\\xi_{\\mathrm{res}},\\text{structural independence record}).\n$$\nHere $\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{sec}}$ lists the covered second-order symbols; $\\mathcal V_{\\mathrm{front}}$ identifies the common ND-RID protocol family and the positive speed function $c(x)$ subject to Theorem 46's upper bound; $\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{front}}$ proves that this operational frontier is attained on the covered domain; $\\mathcal N_{\\mathrm{char}}$ proves that every covered symbol has two distinct real characteristic roots for every nonzero spatial covector; and $\\mathcal I_{\\mathrm{cone}}$ proves exact equality of each covered characteristic cone with that attained operational frontier. The remaining entries retain the utilization, attractor, Hessian/noise, residual, and structural-independence data. Definition 2.4.1d governs the dependency record for the sector symbols, protocol, and error bounds.\n\nAn accepted certificate that discharges Hypothesis O.7.2.4 must certify\n$$\n\\xi_{\\mathrm{res}}=\\xi_{\\mathrm{cone}}(\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{sec}})=0\n$$\nand must contain accepted proof objects for $\\mathcal A_{\\mathrm{front}}$, $\\mathcal N_{\\mathrm{char}}$, and $\\mathcal I_{\\mathrm{cone}}$. A record proving only $0\\le\\xi_{\\mathrm{cone}}\\le\\xi_{\\mathrm{res}}$ with $\\xi_{\\mathrm{res}}>0$ is an approximate cone-audit record; it does not discharge attainment, nondegeneracy, or exact cone coincidence. The certificate covers only its listed sectors, domain, and resolution and does not discharge Hypothesis O.7.2.3.\n\n**Definition O.7.2.5a (Desynchronization Cone-Rigidity Certificate).** For a finite sector graph, one record with verified structural dependencies fixes $c_A>0$, $\\bar c_{AB}=(c_A+c_B)/2$, tolerances $\\xi_{AB}\\ge0$, and a single protocol family, resolution, and quotient map\n$$\nq_{\\mathrm{resp}}:V_{\\mathrm{sec}}\\to\\mathfrak Q_{\\mathrm{front}}.\n$$\nEvery edge obeys\n$$\n\\left|\\frac{c_A-c_B}{\\bar c_{AB}}\\right|\\le\\xi_{AB},\n\\qquad q_{\\mathrm{resp}}(A)=q_{\\mathrm{resp}}(B).\n\\tag{O.7.2.5a.1}\n$$\nAny nonadjacent numerical tolerance requires a separate path-accumulation ledger.\n\n**Proposition O.7.2.5b (Connected Response Class).** A connected component maps to one element of $\\mathfrak Q_{\\mathrm{front}}$. Connectivity alone gives no uniform numerical tolerance between nonadjacent speeds.\n\n*Proof.* Equality in a common quotient is transitive along a finite path; numerical edge errors may accumulate. ∎\n\n<a id=\"remark-o726-finite-speed-versus-cone-saturation\"></a>**Remark O.7.2.6 (Finite Speed versus Cone Saturation).** Theorem 46 supplies a uniform operational causal-speed upper bound for serialized ND-RID substrate propagation. It does not establish an attained frontier, prove that every retained continuum sector saturates a frontier, or show that all sector characteristic cones coincide. Frontier attainment and sector-cone saturation are branch statements only when directly derived or supplied by accepted records. An accepted $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{desync}}^{\\mathrm{cone}}$ supplies only the edgewise finite-response quotient statement of Proposition O.7.2.5b unless its path and sector-coverage entries also discharge the full frontier-attainment and cone-saturation record.\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o7a-hyperbolic-signature-closure\"></a>**Theorem O.7a (Hyperbolic Signature Closure).** Under Hypotheses O.7.2.1–O.7.2.4, for every $x\\in M_{\\mathrm{reg}}$:\n\n(a) *Negative time-time component.* $a(x)<0$.\n\n(b) *Lorentzian inertia.* The symmetric matrix\n$$\nG^{\\mu\\nu}(x) \\;=\\; \\begin{pmatrix} a(x) & b^j(x)\\\\ b^i(x) & A^{ij}(x)\\end{pmatrix}\n$$\nhas inertia $(1,0,3)$: exactly one negative eigenvalue and three positive eigenvalues. Equivalently, the principal symbol has Lorentzian signature $(-,+,+,+)$. ∎\n\nThe four-hypothesis package may be displayed as the antecedent\n$$\n\\mathrm{EntropyTime}\n\\,\\land\\,\n\\mathrm{PositiveSpatialBlock}\n\\,\\land\\,\n\\mathrm{SecondOrderClosure}\n\\,\\land\\,\n\\mathrm{NondegenerateCone}\n\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\n\\mathrm{LorentzianSignature}\\ (-,+,+,+),\n$$\nwith the four antecedents supplied by the rank-four continuum branch of Theorem 45, Theorem O.3's pathwise certificate together with Hypothesis O.7.2.2 (entropy-selected time, its rank-three spatial kernel, and the registered propagation-tensor identification), Hypothesis O.7.2.3 (second-order continuum closure), and Hypothesis O.7.2.4, whose operational-speed upper-bound input is supplied by Theorem 46 plus Theorem P.8.4, while its attained-frontier, nondegeneracy, and cone-coincidence inputs remain branch clauses of the characteristic principal symbol. Theorem O.7a proves the signature conclusion on this branch from all four structural inputs jointly: the Euclidean carrier dimension enters through the rank-four continuum input, and entropy-time selection through the time-coordinate input.\n\n*Proof of (a).* Fix $x$ and consider the characteristic polynomial in $\\omega$ with $k\\in S_x^*\\setminus\\{0\\}$. Hypotheses O.7.2.1–O.7.2.2 and rank-nullity give $\\dim S_x^*=3$, so the orthogonal complement $\\{k:b^ik_i=0\\}\\subset S_x^*$ is at least two-dimensional and contains nonzero $k$. For any such $k$, the polynomial simplifies to $p_x(\\omega,k)=a(x)\\omega^2+A^{ij}(x)k_i k_j$ with discriminant\n$$\n\\Delta(k) \\;=\\; -4\\,a(x)\\,A^{ij}(x)\\,k_i k_j.\n$$\n\nBy Hypotheses O.7.2.1–O.7.2.2, $A^{ij}k_ik_j>0$; by Hypothesis O.7.2.4, $\\Delta(k)>0$. Hence $a(x)<0$.\n\n*Proof of (b).* For $t\\in\\mathbb R$ and $x\\in\\mathbb R^3$, the quadratic form of $G$ is\n$$\nq(t,x)=at^2+2t\\,b^Tx+x^TAx.\n$$\nSince $A$ is positive-definite, it is invertible. Set\n$$\ny:=x+tA^{-1}b.\n$$\nThen\n$$\ny^TAy\n=x^TAx+2t\\,b^Tx+t^2b^TA^{-1}b,\n$$\nand consequently\n$$\nq(t,x)\n=y^TAy+\\bigl(a-b^TA^{-1}b\\bigr)t^2.\n$$\nThe map $(t,x)\\mapsto(t,y)$ is invertible, so it preserves the maximal dimensions of positive and negative subspaces of the quadratic form. The first term has three positive directions because $A\\succ0$. Moreover $A^{-1}\\succ0$, so\n$$\na-b^TA^{-1}b\\le a<0,\n$$\nand the second term has one negative direction. There is no zero direction. Hence $G$ has exactly one negative and three positive eigenvalues, i.e. inertia $(1,0,3)$. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o7a1-entropy-orthogonal-normal-form\"></a>**Corollary O.7a.1 (Entropy-orthogonal normal form).** Under the hypotheses of Theorem O.7a, at each $x\\in M_{\\mathrm{reg}}$ there is an orientation-preserving linear change of cotangent coordinates for which\n$$\np_x(\\xi)\n=-\\alpha(x)(\\xi_0')^2\n+\\widetilde A^{ij}(x)\\xi_i'\\xi_j',\n\\qquad\n\\alpha(x)>0,\n\\qquad\n\\widetilde A(x)\\succ0.\n$$\nAfter an orientation-preserving spatial orthonormalization,\n$$\np_x(\\xi)\n=-\\alpha(x)(\\xi_0')^2\n+\\delta^{ij}\\xi_i'\\xi_j'.\n$$\nThe algebraic normal-form construction preserves orientation. Preservation of a selected future cone is a separate restriction supplied by Hypothesis O.7.2.2; it does not follow from the determinant of the coordinate change.\n\n*Proof.* Write the quadratic form as\n$$\np_x(\\xi)\n=a\\xi_0^2+2b^i\\xi_0\\xi_i+A^{ij}\\xi_i\\xi_j,\n\\qquad\na<0,\n\\qquad\nA\\succ0.\n$$\nSet $\\alpha=-a>0$ and complete the square:\n$$\np_x(\\xi)\n=-\\alpha\\left(\\xi_0-\\alpha^{-1}b^i\\xi_i\\right)^2\n+\\left(A^{ij}+\\alpha^{-1}b^ib^j\\right)\\xi_i\\xi_j.\n$$\nThus\n$$\n\\xi_0'\n=\\xi_0-\\alpha^{-1}b^i\\xi_i,\n\\qquad\n\\xi_i'=\\xi_i,\n\\qquad\n\\widetilde A^{ij}\n=A^{ij}+\\alpha^{-1}b^ib^j.\n$$\nFor every nonzero spatial covector $v$,\n$$\nv_i\\widetilde A^{ij}v_j\n=v_iA^{ij}v_j+\\alpha^{-1}(b^iv_i)^2>0,\n$$\nso $\\widetilde A\\succ0$. The shear has determinant $1$. Choose an oriented orthonormalizing map $R\\in GL^+(3,\\mathbb R)$ with\n$$\nR^\\top\\widetilde A R=I_3;\n$$\nfor example, take the positive-definite square root and, if necessary, an orientation-preserving orthogonal factor. Extending $R$ trivially in the time coordinate gives a positive-determinant four-dimensional change of coordinates and the displayed normal form. No step proves invariance of a designated cone under the coordinate change; that restriction is imposed separately by Hypothesis O.7.2.2. ∎\n\n<a id=\"theorem-o7b-operational-speed-normalization\"></a>**Theorem O.7b (Operational Speed Normalization).** Assume Corollary O.7a.1, Hypothesis O.7.2.4 and a separately attained local operational frontier. Register its clock and spatial ruler calibration in the entropy-orthogonal, spatially orthonormal coordinates used below, transporting that calibration through the shear and spatial coordinate change. Let $c(x)>0$ denote the frontier speed in those same coordinates. Then\n$$\np_x(\\xi)=-\\frac{\\xi_0^2}{c(x)^2}+\\delta^{ij}\\xi_i\\xi_j.\n$$\nThe numerical bound of Theorem 46 transfers only when its time and spatial-distance calibration has been identified with this registered frame; on that branch,\n$$\n0<c(x)\\le\\frac{\\delta w_{\\max}}{\\tau_{\\min}}.\n$$\nNo uniform positive lower bound follows. On the additional normalized uniform-weight branch with an attained one-link frontier in the same calibration, $c(x)\\equiv c=\\delta/\\tau_{\\min}$.\n\n*Proof.* Corollary O.7a.1 gives $p_x=-\\alpha(x)\\xi_0^2+|\\xi|^2$ with $\\alpha>0$. Its dispersion relation has speed $1/\\sqrt{\\alpha(x)}$ in the declared clock and spatial norm. Cone coincidence with the operational frontier measured in that same convention gives $\\alpha(x)=1/c(x)^2$. The stated calibration identification then permits the numerical upper bound of Theorem 46. The uniform-weight equality uses its additional one-link-attainment premise. A coordinate change by itself supplies neither this calibration identification nor frontier attainment. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o7b1-derived-tangent-frame-lorentz-group-and-local-lorentz-kinematics\"></a>**Corollary O.7b.1 (Derived Tangent-Frame Lorentz Group and Local Lorentz Kinematics).** Assume the hypotheses of Theorems O.7a and O.7b and, in addition, that the spatial bundle $S=\\ker dt$ admits a registered global orientation. Then:\n\n(a) After the rescaling $\\xi_0':=\\xi_0/c(x)$, the principal symbol takes the standard Minkowski form $p_x(\\xi)=\\eta^{\\mu\\nu}\\xi_\\mu\\xi_\\nu=-\\xi_0^2+\\delta^{ij}\\xi_i\\xi_j$. Its linear isometry group is $O(1,3)$.\n\n(b) Requiring preservation of the registered spatial orientation and of the entropy-selected future cone (Hypothesis O.7.2.2) restricts $O(1,3)$ to the proper orthochronous Lorentz group $SO^+(1,3)$.\n\n(c) The orthonormal frame bundle of the emergent metric obtained by duality from $p_x$ has structure group $SO^+(1,3)$.\n\n(d) When the spin obstruction class $w_2(M_{\\mathrm{reg}})\\in H^2(M_{\\mathrm{reg}};\\mathbb Z/2)$ vanishes, spin lifts exist and each has structure group equal to the unique connected double cover $\\mathrm{Spin}(1,3)\\cong SL(2,\\mathbb C)$. The group factor is therefore fixed on the spin-admissible branch, but the global spin structure need not be unique.\n\nThese conclusions concern the principal symbol, causal cone, and tangent-frame bundle. Lorentz covariance of the full realized matter dynamics additionally requires a common cone for every response-active sector, covariant lower-order and interaction terms, and compatible tensor or spinor representations; it does not follow from the tangent-frame result alone.\n\n*Proof.* (a) Theorem O.7b gives\n$$\np_x(\\xi)=-\\frac{1}{c(x)^2}\\xi_0^2+\\delta^{ij}\\xi_i\\xi_j.\n$$\nWriting $\\xi_0':=\\xi_0/c(x)$ gives\n$$\np_x(\\xi)=-(\\xi_0')^2+\\delta^{ij}\\xi_i\\xi_j.\n$$\nThe linear transformations preserving this quadratic form are, by definition, the elements of $O(1,3)$.\n\n(b) For $\\Lambda\\in O(1,3)$, the sign of $\\det\\Lambda$ records four-dimensional orientation, while the sign of $\\Lambda^0{}_0$ distinguishes preservation from reversal of the chosen time cone. After the future time orientation is fixed by $\\Lambda^0{}_0>0$, the condition $\\det\\Lambda=1$ is equivalent to preservation of the induced spatial orientation. The registered spatial orientation imposes $\\det\\Lambda=1$, and Hypothesis O.7.2.2 imposes $\\Lambda^0{}_0>0$. Their intersection is the identity component $SO^+(1,3)$.\n\n(c) An orthonormal frame at $x$ is an ordered basis whose Gram matrix is $\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,1,1)$. Two such frames differ by an element of $O(1,3)$. Restricting to frames with the chosen spatial orientation and future time orientation restricts every transition function to $SO^+(1,3)$; hence the oriented, time-oriented orthonormal frame bundle has that structure group.\n\n(d) On the branch with the selected time line and oriented rank-three spatial bundle $S=\\ker dt$, the tangent bundle splits as $TM_{\\mathrm{reg}}\\cong\\mathbf 1\\oplus S$. The Whitney product formula gives $w_2(TM_{\\mathrm{reg}})=w_2(S)$. Milnor (1963b, p. 200) states that an oriented $SO(3)$ bundle has a spin lift exactly when $w_2=0$; his cohomological description and exact sequence make the nonempty set of lifts an $H^1(M_{\\mathrm{reg}};\\mathbb Z/2)$-torsor. The inclusion $SO(3)\\hookrightarrow SO^+(1,3)$ is a homotopy equivalence by the rotation–boost decomposition, so extension of this lift supplies the corresponding Lorentzian spin frame lift. The connected double cover is $\\operatorname{Spin}^+(1,3)\\cong SL(2,\\mathbb C)$. Thus every lift has this structure group, whereas the global lift is unique only when the stated torsor has one element. ∎\n\n<a id=\"corollary-o7b2-no-riemannian-replacement-of-the-causal-cone\"></a>**Corollary O.7b.2 (No Riemannian Replacement of the Causal Cone).** Under Hypotheses O.7.2.1–O.7.2.4, a positive-definite four-dimensional principal symbol cannot represent the nondegenerate characteristic cone and attained operational frontier of Hypothesis O.7.2.4. If $H^{\\mu\\nu}$ is positive-definite, then\n$$\nH^{\\mu\\nu}\\xi_\\mu\\xi_\\nu>0\n$$\nfor every nonzero covector $\\xi$, so the characteristic set $H^{\\mu\\nu}\\xi_\\mu\\xi_\\nu=0$ is empty away from the zero covector and contains no nondegenerate two-sheet causal cone. Therefore a Riemannian symbol can enter the PU stack only as a Euclidean auxiliary description satisfying a separate reconstruction gate, such as the reflection-positivity gate of Appendix F; it cannot be the physical causal principal symbol of the regular branch.\n\n*Proof.* The first claim is the definition of positive-definiteness applied to the quadratic symbol. A nondegenerate operational causal frontier requires nonzero characteristic covectors separating future and past propagation directions. A positive-definite symbol has no such nonzero null covectors, so it cannot satisfy Hypothesis O.7.2.4. Theorems O.7a and O.7b then leave only the Lorentzian signature branch under the stated hypotheses. ∎\n\nThe tangent-frame Lorentz-kinematics component of premise (T5) of §12 follows on the branch of Corollary O.7b.1, including Hypotheses O.7.2.1–O.7.2.4 and the registered spatial orientation. For covered retained sectors, an accepted $\\mathfrak C_{\\mathrm{cone}}$ may supply Hypothesis O.7.2.4 without discharging the other branch premises. Full matter-dynamical Lorentz covariance and metric universality retain the additional common-cone, interaction, representation, and boundary-sufficiency records stated in (T5).\n\n## O.8 Conclusion\n\nThe framework obtains coherent timing and a preferred statistical direction on branches carrying the relevant synchronization and history records, and Lorentzian signature on its four-hypothesis branch. Connections to consciousness and gravity are made through the additional physical models of Section O.6.\n\n**Technical ledger.**\n\nThe framework obtains temporal coherence and a probabilistic forward orientation on explicitly certified branches.\n*   **Temporal Coherence:** Under the statistical and ledger premises of Theorem O.1 and the connected low-noise detailed-balance hypotheses of Theorem O.2, stationary measures concentrate near synchronized phase configurations.\n*   **The Arrow of Time:** Under the pathwise guarantee-level certificate of Theorem O.3a, a cycle satisfies $P_R/P_F\\le e^{-h_{\\min}}$; the factor $1/2$ requires the separate condition $h_{\\min}\\ge\\ln2$. The registered binary-support and reset-cost ledgers do not by themselves establish this stochastic pathwise bound; SPAP alone does not require a physical state merge.\n*   **Retained history and receiver-pattern scope:** Theorem O.4 distinguishes recurrence of an accessible state from deletion of a forward-locked retained history. Proposition O.4.2 grades the availability of Appendix M's receiver-pattern descriptors, not the existence of a thermodynamic arrow. External evaluation and integration-cost conclusions retain the model-access, decision, insulation, and pattern-specific reduction certificates of Theorems M.10.3 and M.10.5. An external controller may still restore a selected accessible state without erasing the forward-locked retained history (Corollary O.4.1).\n\nThe temporal-coherence and pathwise-arrow results can be combined with the CC and gravity sectors only on their common certified branch. A CC modulation requires the independent context-to-response and energy-accounting data of Appendices L and S. A gravitational-wave identification additionally requires the Appendix-B source tensor, the operational-continuum/AQFT bridge, the metric equation, and a linearized transverse-traceless propagation theorem. Appendix O supplies temporal ordering and, on Hypotheses O.7.2.1–O.7.2.4, the Lorentzian signature of Theorem O.7a.\n";
